window.BANK_I=[{"id":"I|L1|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Enterobius — mode of infection","s":"A 7-year-old boarding-school pupil has anal itching that wakes her at night, and several dorm-mates have the same complaint. A sticky-tape swab shows colourless eggs flattened on one side, each holding a larva. Using the lecturer's definition of mode of infection (the act plus the infective stage), which answer is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":75,"c":"<b>Enterobius — mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Person-to-person spread and air-borne transmission","B":"Ingestion of unembryonated eggs from fingers","C":"Penetration of perianal skin by hatched larvae","D":"Ingestion or inhalation of embryonated eggs","E":"Ingestion of eggs that embryonate in the soil"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"These are the modes of transmission, meaning the channels. The wrap-up table on slide 82 copies them into the mode-of-infection column by mistake. Slide 80, and the definition on slide 23 (method or verb plus infective stage), show why they do not count as a mode of infection.","B":"Wrong stage. The infective stage is the embryonated egg. Eggs are laid unembryonated but contain a larva within 4–6 hours (slide 74).","C":"This distorts retroinfection. In retroinfection the eggs hatch on the perianal skin and the larvae go back into the bowel through the anus. They do not penetrate skin. Skin penetration is the route of the filariform larvae of hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Correct. This is Enterobius vermicularis (D-shaped egg containing a larva, on perianal tape). Its modes of infection on slide 80 are ingestion of embryonated eggs, inhalation of embryonated eggs, and retroinfection. Each one is a verb plus the infective stage.","E":"An egg that embryonates in soil fits Ascaris and Trichuris. Enterobius eggs embryonate on the perianal skin within 4–6 hours and never need soil."},"img":"slides_i/L1_75.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A stem asks for the MODE OF INFECTION, not the mode of transmission. Which answer has the right shape?","o":{"B":"Soil-transmitted","E":"Food-borne","D":"Skin penetration by the filariform (L3) larva","A":"Faecal–oral","C":"Person-to-person contact"},"r":{"B":"Also a transmission channel, and it describes a group of worms rather than an act.","E":"'Food-borne' is a mode of TRANSMISSION. Examiners set one stem for each in the same paper, with overlapping options, so writing this when asked for infection scores nothing.","D":"Correct. Mode of INFECTION names the precise act plus the NAMED STAGE. Everything else here names a channel.","A":"A route, not an act plus a stage.","C":"A channel. It is the right transmission answer for Enterobius but still not a mode of infection."},"t":"Mode of infection","sl":82,"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Strongyloides — autoinfection","s":"A 62-year-old man left rural north-east Thailand 35 years ago and has lived in a city flat ever since. He has recurrent itchy, fast-moving linear wheals across the buttocks that fade within hours, and persistent eosinophilia. His fresh stool contains larvae with a short buccal cavity. What best explains how the infection has lasted so long without further contact with soil?","lec":"L1","sl":53,"c":"<b>Strongyloides — autoinfection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Embryonated eggs on the perianal skin are carried back to the mouth on the fingers","B":"Eggs laid on the perianal skin hatch there and the larvae crawl back into the rectum","C":"Filariform larvae lie dormant in skeletal muscle and are released as immunity wanes","D":"Larviparous females release larvae that re-invade the jejunal mucosa and mature there","E":"Rhabditiform larvae turn filariform inside the host and re-penetrate gut wall or skin"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Hand-to-mouth spread of embryonated eggs is how Enterobius, and the Taenia solium egg, reinfect. Strongyloides is not acquired by ingesting eggs.","B":"This describes Enterobius retroinfection. Enterobius passes eggs, not larvae, and its eggs are collected on perianal tape.","C":"Larvae resting in muscle describes Trichinella (L2). No intestinal nematode in this lecture does this.","D":"This is the Capillaria philippinensis mechanism, where larviparous females cause autoinfection in the small intestine (slide 62). Strongyloides does not work this way.","E":"Correct. Larva currens (fast-moving, short-lived tracks) and rhabditiform larvae with a short buccal cavity in fresh stool point to Strongyloides stercoralis. In autoinfection, L1 becomes L3 either in the intestine (internal autoinfection) or on the perianal skin (external autoinfection). The L3 then re-enters and the cycle keeps going for decades (slide 53, slide 59)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which intestinal nematode is the classic cause of autoinfection, and what makes it possible?","o":{"C":"Trichuris trichiura — its eggs are infective as soon as they are laid","D":"Ascaris lumbricoides — its eggs embryonate in the bowel lumen","E":"Strongyloides stercoralis — rhabditiform larvae can moult to infective filariform larvae inside the gut","A":"Hookworm — filariform larvae re-penetrate the perianal skin","B":"Necator americanus — adults migrate upward from the caecum"},"r":{"C":"Trichuris eggs also need a soil period; they are not infective when passed.","D":"Ascaris eggs must embryonate in SOIL for two to three weeks; an egg swallowed straight from the perianal region cannot infect.","E":"Correct, and it is why strongyloidiasis can persist for decades and can hyperinfect under corticosteroids.","A":"Hookworm larvae must develop in soil first — this is the plausible-sounding but wrong route.","B":"Necator is a hookworm and behaves as in D; adult migration is not an infection route."},"t":"Autoinfection","sl":53,"img":"slides_i/L1_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Trichuris — habitat and mode of infection","s":"A 6-year-old from a village where night soil is spread on the vegetable plots has intermittent bloody mucoid stools. Stool microscopy shows yellow-brown, barrel-shaped eggs with a clear plug at each pole and an undivided granular content. Where does the adult worm live, and how was it acquired?","lec":"L1","sl":29,"c":"<b>Trichuris — habitat and mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Caecal mucosa of the large intestine; ingestion of embryonated eggs","B":"Lumen of the large intestine; ingestion or inhalation of embryonated eggs","C":"Caecal mucosa of the large intestine; ingestion of unembryonated eggs","D":"Lumen of the small intestine; ingestion of embryonated eggs","E":"Jejunal mucosa; ingestion of fish containing third-stage larvae"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. A barrel-shaped, bile-stained egg with mucoid bipolar plugs is Trichuris trichiura. It lives in the large intestine, mainly in the mucosa of the caecum (slide 35), and infects by ingestion of embryonated eggs from soil.","B":"This is Enterobius, which lives in the lumen of the large intestine and is acquired by ingestion or inhalation of embryonated eggs.","C":"Right habitat, wrong stage. The egg in the stool is the unembryonated diagnostic stage. It must embryonate in soil before it can infect.","D":"This is Ascaris, which lives in the lumen of the small intestine.","E":"This is Capillaria philippinensis. Its egg is peanut-shaped with flattened plugs and a striated shell."},"img":"slides_i/L1_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A stool contains a barrel-shaped egg with a transparent plug at each end. Which worm, and what clinical sign accompanies a heavy infection?","o":{"D":"Enterobius vermicularis — perianal itching","B":"Ascaris lumbricoides — intestinal obstruction","A":"Trichuris trichiura — rectal prolapse","C":"Hookworm — iron-deficiency anaemia","E":"Capillaria philippinensis — protein-losing enteropathy"},"r":{"D":"Enterobius eggs are asymmetrically flattened on one side and are collected by perianal SCOTCH TAPE, not from stool.","B":"Ascaris eggs are round with a thick mamillated albuminous coat.","A":"Correct. The barrel shape with bipolar mucoid plugs is unmistakable, and heavy whipworm infection in a child classically causes rectal prolapse.","C":"Hookworm eggs are thin-walled, transparent ovals containing 4–8 cells.","E":"Capillaria eggs are described as PEANUT-shaped with flattened bipolar plugs — similar to Trichuris but with a striated shell and flatter plugs, and this is the deliberate near-miss."},"t":"Egg morphology","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L1_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Egg morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Enterobius — specimen and stage","s":"Four children at a kindergarten scratch their bottoms at night and sleep poorly. The first child's stool was examined twice by formalin–ether concentration and was negative both times. Which specimen should be taken next, and what will it most likely show?","lec":"L1","sl":74,"c":"<b>Enterobius — specimen and stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Perianal tape swab; barrel-shaped eggs with a mucoid plug at each pole","B":"Perianal tape swab; colourless eggs flattened on one side, containing a larva","C":"Night blood film; sheathed larvae circulating in the peripheral blood","D":"Fresh stool smear; rhabditiform larvae with a large genital primordium","E":"Third stool concentration; colourless eggs flattened on one side, with a larva"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right specimen, wrong egg. A barrel-shaped egg with bipolar plugs is Trichuris, which is found in stool.","B":"Correct. For Enterobius, eggs are rare in faeces but easy to collect with a Scotch-tape perianal swab (slide 77). The egg is D-shaped (plano-convex), colourless and embryonated (slide 74). Enterobius is the exception to the rule that stool examination is the best test for intestinal nematodes.","C":"The night timing is a trap. Nocturnal peripheral blood sampling is used for microfilariae (L2). The pinworm is nocturnal because the female migrates to the anus at night.","D":"Rhabditiform larvae in stool point to Strongyloides. There is nothing in these children to suggest it.","E":"Right egg, wrong specimen. Repeating stool concentration does not help, because the gravid female lays her eggs on the perianal skin, not in the bowel lumen."},"img":"slides_i/L1_74.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A mother brings two children with anal itching that is worse at night. What specimen should be collected, and how?","o":{"C":"A perianal skin scraping in 10% KOH","A":"A formed stool sample for concentration technique","E":"Three stool samples on alternate days","B":"A perianal scotch-tape (cellophane) swab taken in the early morning before washing","D":"A duodenal aspirate"},"r":{"C":"KOH is for fungal elements and for Paragonimus sputum, not for pinworm eggs.","A":"Stool examination is insensitive for Enterobius precisely because the eggs are not deposited in the gut lumen.","E":"Repeat stools raise sensitivity for other nematodes but not for this one.","B":"Correct. The female Enterobius migrates out at night and lays eggs on perianal skin, so the eggs are on the SKIN and not usually in stool. Take the tape before bathing or defaecation.","D":"A duodenal aspirate is used for Strongyloides and Giardia."},"t":"Diagnosis","sl":77,"img":"slides_i/L1_77.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Ascaris — route to the lung","s":"A 4-year-old has fever, dry cough, wheeze and eosinophilia. About two months later her stool contains round, yellow eggs with a thick, bumpy outer coat. How did the parasite reach her lungs?","lec":"L1","sl":19,"c":"<b>Ascaris — route to the lung</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Rhabditiform larvae moulted in her bowel and re-entered the blood to reach the lungs","B":"Filariform larvae penetrated the skin of her feet and travelled in the blood to the lungs","C":"Adult worms crawled up the oesophagus and were aspirated into the airways","D":"Eggs shed by adult worms in the bowel were carried in portal blood to the lungs","E":"Larvae hatched from swallowed eggs in her gut and travelled in the blood to the lungs"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"L1 larvae turning into L3 larvae inside the bowel is Strongyloides autoinfection. Ascaris cannot autoinfect.","B":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae is how hookworm and Strongyloides reach the lung. It does not fit an albumin-coated egg.","C":"Adult ascarids can wander ectopically (nose, mouth, bile duct), but the lung phase is caused by larvae, not adults.","D":"Eggs carried in the blood to other organs is a schistosome idea. Ascaris eggs simply pass out in the faeces.","E":"Correct. The egg with a thick, bumpy albumin coat is a fertilised Ascaris egg. She swallowed embryonated eggs, the larvae hatched, entered the circulation and migrated to the lungs (slide 19), which caused Loeffler's syndrome (slide 20). They were then coughed up and swallowed, and matured in the small intestine."},"img":"slides_i/L1_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which group of intestinal nematodes migrates through the lungs, and what symptom can that produce?","o":{"B":"Only hookworm — pleural effusion","D":"Trichuris, Enterobius and Capillaria — cough and wheeze","A":"Only Ascaris — haemoptysis","E":"Ascaris, hookworm and Strongyloides — cough and wheeze (Löffler-type pneumonitis)","C":"None of the intestinal nematodes enters the lung"},"r":{"B":"Hookworm is in the club, but the symptom is cough/wheeze, and it is not alone.","D":"None of these three migrates through the lung; they stay in the gut.","A":"Ascaris is in the club but is not alone.","E":"Correct. These three make the classic heart–lung migration, which is why an intestinal worm can present with respiratory symptoms. Remembering the trio prevents you leaping to a lung fluke.","C":"The opposite of the truth, and the reason respiratory symptoms are such a productive distractor."},"t":"Life cycle","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L1_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Life cycle</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Hookworm — cause of anaemia","s":"A 35-year-old rubber tapper who works barefoot is tired and pale; haemoglobin is 8 g/dL with microcytic, hypochromic red cells. Stool shows colourless, thin-shelled oval eggs containing 4–8 cells. Which mechanism explains the anaemia?","lec":"L1","sl":40,"c":"<b>Hookworm — cause of anaemia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Adults in the small intestine compete with the host for dietary vitamin B12","B":"Adults attached to the duodenal and jejunal mucosa cause chronic blood loss","C":"Adults burrowing into jejunal villi cause a protein-losing enteropathy","D":"Adults threaded into the caecal mucosa cause petechial bleeding and dysentery","E":"Larvae crossing the alveoli cause haemoptysis and loss of red cells"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Vitamin B12 competition causes a megaloblastic anaemia. That belongs to Diphyllobothrium (L3) and does not fit microcytic red cells.","B":"Correct. An egg with 4–8 cells and a thin, transparent shell is the hookworm egg. The adults live in the mucosa and submucosa of the duodenum and jejunum (slide 48), and the intestinal blood loss causes iron-deficiency anaemia (slide 44).","C":"Villous destruction with protein-losing enteropathy is Capillaria philippinensis.","D":"This is Trichuris, which lives in the caecal mucosa and has a barrel-shaped egg.","E":"The pulmonary phase causes Loeffler's syndrome (cough, wheeze, fever). It is not the cause of chronic iron-deficiency anaemia."},"img":"slides_i/L1_40.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about hookworm is correct?","o":{"E":"Both infective and diagnostic stages are the adult worm","A":"The infective stage is the egg, swallowed from contaminated vegetables","D":"The infective stage is the rhabditiform larva, which penetrates skin","C":"The diagnostic stage is the filariform larva in stool","B":"The infective stage is the filariform (L3) larva, which penetrates skin; the diagnostic stage is the egg in stool"},"r":{"E":"Adults are never an infective or diagnostic stage here.","A":"Hookworm eggs are not infective; they must hatch and develop in soil.","D":"The RHABDITIFORM larva is the feeding, non-infective stage; the FILARIFORM is the infective one.","C":"Finding larvae rather than eggs in fresh stool points to Strongyloides, which is exactly the discrimination the papers set.","B":"Correct, and the pairing is the template for the whole lecture: infective stage and diagnostic stage are different objects, in different places."},"t":"Hookworm","sl":43,"img":"slides_i/L1_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Hookworm</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Strongyloides — infective vs diagnostic stage","s":"A technician receives a freshly passed stool from a patient with larva currens. The direct smear shows motile larvae with a short buccal cavity and a bulb-shaped oesophagus. She asks whether the larvae she is looking at could infect her through the skin. Which answer is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":51,"c":"<b>Strongyloides — infective vs diagnostic stage</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"No; these larvae can infect people only if swallowed on contaminated vegetables","B":"Yes; these are third-stage larvae, the same stage that is picked up from soil","C":"Yes; these are filariform larvae, which can penetrate intact skin on direct contact","D":"No; these are rhabditiform larvae, which must first moult to the filariform stage","E":"No; these larvae must first develop inside a small freshwater fish to infect"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Strongyloides is not acquired by ingestion. Its mode of infection is skin penetration by filariform larvae.","B":"L3 is the filariform larva. The larva described here is L1.","C":"The bulb-shaped oesophagus rules out filariform larvae, which have a long, elongated oesophagus.","D":"Correct. A bulbous (rhabditiform) oesophagus means the L1 rhabditiform larva, which is the diagnostic stage in stool (slide 51). Only the L3 filariform larva, with its long oesophagus and notched tail, is infective (slide 52).","E":"Development in a small freshwater fish belongs to Capillaria, whose infective stage is the third-stage larva in fish."},"img":"slides_i/L1_51.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 45-year-old man has abdominal pain and chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool examination shows LARVAE, not eggs. What is the most likely agent, and why is the finding decisive?","o":{"B":"Enterobius vermicularis — larvae hatch on perianal skin","E":"Hookworm — its eggs hatch rapidly once passed","A":"Trichuris trichiura — its larvae emerge in the rectum","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides — larvae return to the gut after lung migration","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis — its eggs hatch in the bowel, so larvae rather than eggs appear in fresh stool"},"r":{"B":"Enterobius eggs are on perianal skin and larvae are not the stool finding.","E":"Hookworm eggs hatch only after some hours outside the body — in a fresh specimen you see eggs.","A":"Trichuris passes eggs, and its larvae are not found in stool.","C":"Ascaris passes eggs.","D":"Correct. This is the single most useful discriminator in L1: EGGS in fresh stool = hookworm; LARVAE in fresh stool = Strongyloides."},"t":"Strongyloides","sl":56,"img":"slides_i/L1_56.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Strongyloides</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Capillaria — infective stage","s":"A 40-year-old man from Si Sa Ket has had three months of watery diarrhoea with loud borborygmi. He has lost 10 kg and now has pitting oedema with a low serum albumin. His stool contains larvae and small, slender adult worms as well as eggs. What is the infective stage of this parasite, and where is it acquired?","lec":"L1","sl":64,"c":"<b>Capillaria — infective stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Third-stage larva, in raw small freshwater fish","B":"Filariform larva, in moist faecally polluted soil","C":"Metacercaria, in undercooked cyprinoid freshwater fish","D":"Embryonated egg, on raw vegetables grown in soil","E":"Rhabditiform larva, passed in another person's stool"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Chronic watery diarrhoea, borborygmi, weight loss and protein-losing enteropathy (hypoalbuminaemic oedema) point to Capillaria philippinensis. In severe cases the stool shows adults and larvae as well as eggs (slide 66). The infective stage is the third-stage larva in small freshwater fish (slide 64, slide 69). Past-paper keys (BM29) take this clinical picture over Strongyloides even when larvae are seen.","B":"Seeing larvae in stool makes Strongyloides tempting, but Strongyloides does not produce protein-losing enteropathy, and adult worms are not a Strongyloides stool finding in this lecture.","C":"A metacercaria in cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis viverrini (L4). The dish is similar but the stage is different.","D":"Embryonated eggs on vegetables are how Ascaris and Trichuris infect.","E":"The rhabditiform larva is a diagnostic stage (Strongyloides), not an infective one."},"img":"slides_i/L1_64.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient from the north-east has months of diarrhoea, weight loss and protein-losing enteropathy. Stool shows peanut-shaped eggs with flattened bipolar plugs and a striated shell. What is the mode of infection?","o":{"B":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","E":"Ingestion of embryonated eggs from contaminated vegetables","A":"Ingestion of small freshwater fish containing third-stage larvae","C":"Ingestion of cyprinoid fish containing metacercariae","D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing encysted larvae"},"r":{"B":"That is hookworm or Strongyloides.","E":"That is Ascaris, Trichuris or Enterobius.","A":"Correct. Capillaria philippinensis is acquired from small raw freshwater fish, and the stage named is the THIRD-STAGE LARVA. It also autoinfects, which is why the illness is protracted and severe.","C":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis viverrini (L4) — same meal, different stage, different organ. Read the stage, not the fish.","D":"Encysted larvae in pork is Trichinella (L2)."},"t":"Capillaria","sl":63,"img":"slides_i/L1_63.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Capillaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Control vs prevention — soil-transmitted","s":"In a village where most adults carry hookworm, the district health office wants a measure that stops the parasite spreading through the community, rather than one that protects a single person from catching it. Which measure fits the lecturer's definition of control?","lec":"L1","sl":47,"c":"<b>Control vs prevention — soil-transmitted</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Washing hands thoroughly before meals","B":"Wearing shoes whenever walking outdoors","C":"Wearing gloves whenever handling soil","D":"Sanitary disposal of faeces in toilets","E":"Washing vegetables well before eating"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Hand-washing is prevention, used for Ascaris, Trichuris and Enterobius.","B":"Shoes are prevention (slide 47) because they protect the wearer. The BM29 key specifically warns against mixing up control and prevention with this option.","C":"Gloves are also prevention for hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Correct. The slides list sanitary disposal, treatment of infected individuals and not using faeces as fertiliser as control, because they stop spread. Past-paper keys give 'use a toilet' for control of soil-transmitted helminths.","E":"Washing vegetables is prevention against the egg-ingested worms (Ascaris, Trichuris)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_47.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pair of measures controls SOIL-TRANSMITTED helminths specifically?","o":{"D":"Using a sanitary toilet and wearing shoes","C":"Cooking fish thoroughly and avoiding pickled crab","A":"Treating the patient and all household contacts","B":"Boiling drinking water and chlorinating pools","E":"Controlling the snail intermediate host"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and it is the answer key's own pairing. A toilet keeps eggs out of the soil; shoes keep filariform larvae out of your feet.","C":"That prevents food-borne trematodes and Capillaria, not soil-transmitted nematodes.","A":"That is the Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana answer — person-to-person spread, not soil.","B":"Water treatment addresses protozoa, not soil-transmitted helminths.","E":"Snail control belongs to the trematodes in L4."},"t":"Control","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L1_34.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Learning-guidance grid — matching worms","s":"Two worms from this lecture share the same infective stage, the same mode of transmission and the same mode of infection, yet one lives in the small intestine and the other in the large intestine. Which pair is it?","lec":"L1","sl":82,"c":"<b>Learning-guidance grid — matching worms</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Trichuris trichiura and Enterobius vermicularis","B":"Ascaris lumbricoides and Trichuris trichiura","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides and Enterobius vermicularis","D":"Capillaria philippinensis and Trichuris trichiura","E":"Hookworm and Strongyloides stercoralis"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Both live in the large intestine, and Enterobius spreads person-to-person or through the air rather than through soil.","B":"Correct. For both worms the infective stage is the embryonated egg, transmission is through soil, and infection is by ingestion of embryonated eggs. Ascaris lives in the lumen of the small intestine and Trichuris in the large intestine (caecal mucosa).","C":"The habitats differ, but so does the mode of transmission: Enterobius is person-to-person, air-borne or by retroinfection, not soil.","D":"Capillaria's infective stage is a third-stage larva in fish, and its transmission is food-borne.","E":"These two do share all three (filariform larva, soil, skin penetration), but both live in the small intestine."},"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"What would happen if a person accidentally swallowed an Ascaris lumbricoides egg taken directly from the perianal area, without a soil period?","o":{"A":"Visceral larva migrans, because the larva hatches immediately","C":"Intestinal infection, because Ascaris eggs are infective as soon as they are passed","B":"Nothing — the egg is not yet embryonated and therefore not infective","E":"Cutaneous larva migrans at the site of contact","D":"Autoinfection with adult worms in the colon"},"r":{"A":"Hatching requires an embryonated egg.","C":"That is Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana, whose eggs are infective when laid.","B":"Correct. Ascaris eggs need two to three weeks in SOIL to embryonate. Freshly passed eggs are harmless, which is exactly why Ascaris is NOT on the autoinfection list.","E":"Cutaneous larva migrans is caused by animal hookworm larvae penetrating skin.","D":"Ascaris cannot autoinfect, for exactly the reason in A."},"t":"Ascaris","sl":18,"img":"slides_i/L1_18.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Ascaris</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Strongyloides — hyperinfection treatment","s":"A 55-year-old woman on long-term prednisolone for rheumatoid arthritis develops abdominal pain, diarrhoea, cough and wheeze. Her stool and sputum both contain very large numbers of larvae; there is no sepsis, meningitis or other organ involvement. Which treatment matches the lecture?","lec":"L1","sl":55,"c":"<b>Strongyloides — hyperinfection treatment</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Albendazole for the patient, repeated after two weeks","B":"Ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day for two days, then stop","C":"Ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day until stool stays negative for two weeks","D":"Mebendazole for her and the whole household, repeated in two weeks","E":"Albendazole with supportive fluid and electrolyte replacement"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Albendazole is listed only for uncomplicated disease.","B":"The 2-day course is the regimen for uncomplicated strongyloidiasis only.","C":"Correct. Massive numbers of larvae in the bowel and lungs of a patient on corticosteroids is hyperinfection (slide 55). For hyperinfection and dissemination, the slides give ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day until stool examination stays negative for 2 weeks (slide 57).","D":"Treating the whole family and retreating after two weeks is the Enterobius plan.","E":"Supportive fluid and electrolyte care alongside albendazole or mebendazole is the Capillaria plan."},"img":"slides_i/L1_55.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What is the best general method for diagnosing an intestinal nematode infection?","o":{"A":"Serology","D":"Blood film","C":"Stool examination","B":"Muscle biopsy","E":"Skin snip"},"r":{"A":"Serology is a fall-back where no egg can be found — for example in extrapulmonary paragonimiasis or trichinellosis.","D":"Blood films are for the blood and tissue nematodes (microfilariae) and for malaria.","C":"Correct, and it is the defining property of the whole lecture — these worms live in the gut lumen, so their eggs or larvae come out in the stool. L2's worms invert exactly this.","B":"Muscle biopsy is the Trichinella method (L2).","E":"Skin snips are used for Onchocerca, not for gut nematodes."},"t":"Diagnosis","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L1_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q8","t":"Autoinfection","s":"Which of the following parasites can cause autoinfection?","lec":"L1","sl":82,"c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q8 · BM28 Q37 — 'which parasite can manifest autoinfection?'</span>","o":{"E":"Paragonimus spp.","D":"Taenia saginata","C":"Strongyloides stercoralis","B":"Ascaris lumbricoides","A":"Opisthorchis viverrini"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Paragonimus needs two intermediate hosts outside the body.","D":"Only T. SOLIUM autoinfects, and it does so as cysticercosis; saginata does not.","C":"Correct, and the usual key. The full autoinfection list the BM28 answer key gives is: Strongyloides stercoralis, Enterobius vermicularis, Taenia SOLIUM (as cysticercosis), Hymenolepis nana, Capillaria philippinensis — and the BM30 key adds Cryptosporidium.","B":"Ascaris eggs must embryonate in soil first, so they cannot re-infect the host who passed them.","A":"Opisthorchis needs a snail and then a fish."},"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q37"]},{"id":"I|L1|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q3","t":"Strongyloides","s":"A 45-year-old man with abdominal pain and chronic watery diarrhoea is found to have LARVAE in his faeces. What is the most likely causative agent?","lec":"L1","sl":56,"c":"<b>Strongyloides</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q3 — larvae in faeces with chronic watery diarrhoea</span>","o":{"E":"Trichuris trichiura","B":"Hookworm","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides","A":"Enterobius vermicularis"},"a":"D","r":{"E":"Trichuris passes barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs.","B":"Hookworm passes eggs; larvae appear only if the specimen has been left standing for hours, which the question does not say.","D":"Correct. Strongyloides eggs hatch inside the bowel, so a FRESH stool contains rhabditiform LARVAE rather than eggs.","C":"Ascaris passes round mamillated eggs.","A":"Enterobius eggs are on perianal skin and are collected by tape."},"img":"slides_i/L1_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L1|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q20","t":"Hookworm","s":"A 40-year-old man presents with dyspnoea, cough and abdominal pain. Stool examination reveals an oval, thin-walled, transparent egg containing 4–8 cell stages. What is the mode of infection of this parasite?","lec":"L1","sl":40,"c":"<b>Hookworm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q20 · BM30 — 4–8 cell egg with respiratory symptoms</span>","o":{"C":"Ingestion of vegetables contaminated with unembryonated eggs","B":"Ingestion of small freshwater fish containing metacercariae","D":"Ingestion of mountain crabs containing metacercariae","E":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","A":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing cysticerci"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Unembryonated eggs are never infective — this option is wrong on the stage alone.","B":"A fish/metacercaria option points to Opisthorchis (L4).","D":"The mountain-crab option is the designed trap: respiratory symptoms tempt you towards Paragonimus. But Ascaris, hookworm and Strongyloides ALL migrate through the lung, so cough does not mean lung fluke.","E":"Correct. The egg described is a HOOKWORM egg, and hookworm is acquired by filariform larvae penetrating the skin.","A":"That is Taenia solium (L3)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_40.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-hook"]},{"id":"I|L1|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q5","t":"Capillaria","s":"A patient has had chronic diarrhoea for three months. Stool examination shows a peanut-shaped egg. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L1","sl":63,"c":"<b>Capillaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q5 · BM28 · BM30 formative — 'peanut-like egg', chronic diarrhoea</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of raw beef containing cysticerci","D":"Ingestion of embryonated eggs from soil-contaminated vegetables","B":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","C":"Ingestion of cyprinoid fish containing metacercariae","E":"Ingestion of small freshwater fish containing third-stage larvae"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Cysticercus bovis in beef is Taenia saginata.","D":"That route belongs to Ascaris, Trichuris and Enterobius.","B":"That is hookworm and Strongyloides.","C":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis viverrini — the same meal with a different stage, which is how this distractor works.","E":"Correct. The 'peanut-like' egg is Capillaria philippinensis, and the BM28 answer key states the mode of infection in exactly these words."},"img":"slides_i/L1_63.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM28 Q-cap","BM30 Q-cap"]},{"id":"I|L1|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-trich","t":"Trichuris","s":"A child presents with rectal prolapse. Stool examination shows barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar mucoid plugs. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L1","sl":29,"c":"<b>Trichuris</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 · BM30 formative — barrel egg, rectal prolapse</span>","o":{"A":"Trichuris trichiura","C":"Capillaria philippinensis","D":"Ascaris lumbricoides","B":"Trichinella spiralis","E":"Taenia solium"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Barrel-shaped with bipolar plugs, yellow-brown, plus rectal prolapse in a child is the whipworm.","C":"Capillaria is the deliberate near-miss — also bipolar, but PEANUT-shaped with FLATTENED plugs and a striated shell, and it gives protein-losing enteropathy rather than prolapse.","D":"Ascaris eggs are round with a thick mamillated coat.","B":"Trichinella is diagnosed from muscle, not stool, and gives myositis rather than prolapse.","E":"Taenia eggs are spherical with radial striation."},"img":"slides_i/L1_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q-trich"]},{"id":"I|L1|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q1","t":"Control","s":"How are soil-transmitted helminths controlled?","lec":"L1","sl":34,"c":"<b>Control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q1 — control of soil-transmitted helminths</span>","o":{"C":"Using sanitary toilets and wearing shoes","B":"Treating the patient and the whole family","E":"Boiling drinking water","D":"Eliminating the snail intermediate host","A":"Cooking freshwater fish thoroughly"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. A toilet stops eggs reaching the soil, and shoes stop filariform larvae reaching skin. The two measures cover both halves of the transmission route.","B":"That is the correct answer for Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana, whose route is person-to-person.","E":"Water treatment matters for protozoa.","D":"Snail control belongs to the trematodes in L4.","A":"Cooking fish prevents Capillaria and Opisthorchis, but those are not soil-transmitted."},"img":"slides_i/L1_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q7","t":"Enterobius","s":"A mother brings her two children to hospital with itchy anal symptoms. How should this infection be controlled?","lec":"L1","sl":78,"c":"<b>Enterobius</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q7 — mother with two itchy-bottomed children</span>","o":{"D":"Cook food thoroughly","E":"Use a sanitary toilet","B":"Boil water before bathing","A":"Wear shoes","C":"Treat the infected patient and the whole family"},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Pinworm is not food-borne.","E":"Sanitation matters for soil-transmitted worms, whose eggs need a soil period. Pinworm does not use the soil.","B":"Bathing water is not the route.","A":"Shoes prevent skin penetration by hookworm and Strongyloides.","C":"Correct. Enterobius eggs are infective the moment they are laid and spread by hand, bedding and dust, so a household or institution must be treated together — otherwise reinfection is immediate."},"img":"slides_i/L1_78.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q19","t":"Diagnosis","s":"Which is the best method for diagnosing an intestinal nematode infection?","lec":"L1","sl":22,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q19 — best method for diagnosing intestinal nematodes</span>","o":{"A":"Muscle biopsy","B":"Blood examination","D":"Stool examination","E":"Urine filtration","C":"Sputum examination"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Muscle biopsy is for Trichinella.","B":"Blood is the specimen for the filarial worms and for malaria.","D":"Correct — and this is the one-line definition of the lecture. These worms live in the gut lumen, so eggs or larvae leave in the stool.","E":"Urine filtration is for Schistosoma haematobium.","C":"Sputum is for Paragonimus."},"img":"slides_i/L1_22.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q1","t":"Ascaris egg","s":"A stool examination shows an egg whose outermost layer is a coarse, bumpy ALBUMINOUS coat. Which parasite is this?","lec":"L1","sl":17,"c":"<b>Ascaris egg</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q2 · BM31 SUM1 Q1 — 'albuminous coated egg' (asked in three cohorts running)</span>","o":{"E":"Capillaria philippinensis","C":"Trichuris trichiura","A":"Ascaris lumbricoides","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis","B":"Trichinella spiralis"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Capillaria is peanut-shaped with FLATTENED bipolar plugs and a striated shell.","C":"Trichuris is barrel-shaped with a clear mucoid plug at each pole. No albuminous coat.","A":"Correct. The fertilised Ascaris egg has THREE shell layers and the outermost is the mammillated albuminous coat, which is what makes it look lumpy and bile-stained yellow-brown. One word — albuminous — settles this item.","D":"Strongyloides is not diagnosed on eggs at all — you find rhabditiform LARVAE in stool.","B":"Trichinella passes no egg in stool; the diagnostic stage is encysted larva in muscle."},"img":"slides_i/L1_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q2"]},{"id":"I|L1|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q2","t":"Larvae in stool","s":"A stool examination shows LARVAE (no eggs). Which infection does this indicate?","lec":"L1","sl":52,"c":"<b>Larvae in stool</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q3 · BM31 SUM1 Q2 — 'larva found in stool'. NOTE: BM31's key records that the lecturer allowed two answers on this item and said it might be dropped.</span>","o":{"D":"Necator americanus","E":"Strongyloides stercoralis","C":"Trichuris trichiura","A":"Hymenolepis nana","B":"Taenia saginata"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Hookworm passes unembryonated eggs. Larvae appear only if the specimen has stood for a day — which is the near-miss the examiner plants, and is why BM31's lecturer conceded two answers.","E":"Correct. Strongyloides eggs hatch INSIDE the bowel wall, so a freshly passed stool already contains rhabditiform larvae. This is the single most repeated one-line fact in the block.","C":"Trichuris passes barrel-shaped eggs.","A":"Hymenolepis nana passes eggs with polar filaments.","B":"Taenia passes eggs with radial striation, or proglottids."},"img":"slides_i/L1_52.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q3"]},{"id":"I|L1|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 1","t":"Ascaris — obstruction","s":"A 5-year-old girl has severe abdominal pain and constipation. An X-ray shows PARTIAL INTESTINAL OBSTRUCTION of the small bowel, and multiple large worms are seen. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"<b>Ascaris — obstruction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Lect 1 & Lect 2 — the 5-year-old with partial intestinal obstruction, asked twice in the same paper</span>","o":{"B":"Ingestion of raw vegetables contaminated with embryonated eggs","A":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae","C":"Ingestion of water contaminated with unembryonated eggs","E":"Ingestion of cyprinoid fish containing third-stage larvae","D":"Autoinfection by embryonated eggs"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. A bolus of large worms obstructing a child's small bowel is Ascaris, and Ascaris is acquired by swallowing the EMBRYONATED egg off soil-contaminated food.","A":"Skin penetration is hookworm and Strongyloides — neither forms a worm bolus.","C":"The stage is the trap. An UNembryonated egg is not infective; Ascaris eggs need 2–3 weeks in soil to embryonate.","E":"Cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis (L4).","D":"Ascaris cannot autoinfect, precisely because the egg must embryonate in soil first."},"img":"slides_i/L1_18.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM32 Lect 2"]},{"id":"I|L1|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q28","t":"Ascaris — why soil matters","s":"What would happen if a person accidentally swallowed an Ascaris lumbricoides egg taken directly from a patient's perianal skin, without the egg having spent time in soil?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"<b>Ascaris — why soil matters</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q28 — 'what happens if you swallow an Ascaris egg straight from the perianal area, without it passing through soil?'</span>","o":{"E":"Cysticercosis in muscle and brain","B":"Ascariasis, because all helminth eggs are infective when passed","A":"Nothing — the egg is unembryonated and therefore not infective","D":"Cutaneous larva migrans at the site of contact","C":"Autoinfection with hyperinfection syndrome"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Cysticercosis follows ingesting Taenia solium eggs, which are infective immediately.","B":"False for Ascaris and Trichuris; true for Enterobius, whose eggs ARE infective within hours — which is the contrast the examiner is testing.","A":"Correct, and this is the whole point of the question: Ascaris eggs leave the body UNEMBRYONATED and need 2–3 weeks in warm moist soil to become the infective embryonated egg. This is exactly why Ascaris is a soil-transmitted helminth and why it cannot autoinfect.","D":"Creeping eruption follows skin penetration by animal hookworm larvae.","C":"Autoinfection belongs to Strongyloides, Enterobius, Capillaria and T. solium."},"img":"slides_i/L1_18.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q32","t":"Host terminology","s":"What is the role of a RESERVOIR host?","lec":"L1","sl":69,"c":"<b>Host terminology</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q32 — 'what is the function of a reservoir host?'</span>","o":{"B":"The host that harbours the larval or asexual stages of the parasite","E":"The host in which the parasite reaches sexual maturity and reproduces","C":"An animal that harbours the same parasite as humans and maintains it in nature as a source of human infection","A":"A host in which the parasite cannot complete its life cycle and is a dead end","D":"An arthropod that carries the parasite on its body surface without biological development"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"That is the INTERMEDIATE host.","E":"That is the DEFINITIVE host.","C":"Correct. For Capillaria philippinensis the reservoir is fish-eating birds; for Trichinella it is pigs and wild boar.","A":"That is the ACCIDENTAL (dead-end) host — humans for Angiostrongylus and Toxocara.","D":"That is a MECHANICAL transmitter, e.g. the cockroach."},"img":"slides_i/L1_69.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L1|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q26","t":"Strongyloides — three phases","s":"A patient has creeping eruption of the skin, dyspnoea, and abdominal pain — all at once. What would stool examination show?","lec":"L1","sl":54,"c":"<b>Strongyloides — three phases</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q26 — creeping eruption + dyspnoea + abdominal pain; what is found in faeces?</span>","o":{"D":"Encysted larvae","E":"Unembryonated eggs with a thin transparent shell","A":"Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs","C":"Embryonated eggs with polar filaments","B":"Rhabditiform larvae"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Encysted larvae are found in MUSCLE in trichinellosis, never in stool.","E":"Thin-shelled unembryonated eggs are hookworm — and hookworm gives creeping eruption and cough too, but it cannot autoinfect, so the three phases do not run together.","A":"Trichuris — no skin or lung phase at all.","C":"Hymenolepis nana (L3).","B":"Correct. Skin + lung + gut simultaneously is the signature of Strongyloides autoinfection, and Strongyloides is the one intestinal nematode whose stool stage is a LARVA."},"img":"slides_i/L1_54.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L1|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Rhabditiform larva — hookworm vs Strongyloides","s":"A stool sat on the ward overnight before reaching the laboratory and now contains rhabditiform larvae. Because hookworm eggs can hatch in a delayed specimen, the technologist has to decide which worm the larvae belong to. Which combination of features identifies Strongyloides?","lec":"L1","sl":41,"c":"<b>Rhabditiform larva — hookworm vs Strongyloides</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Short buccal cavity and large genital primordium","B":"Filariform oesophagus and a notched tail tip","C":"Long buccal cavity and small genital primordium","D":"Long buccal cavity and large genital primordium","E":"Short buccal cavity and small genital primordium"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct (slide 51). The Lab 1 lecturer stressed that the genital primordium is the more practical of the two features to read.","B":"These are filariform (L3) features. The stem describes rhabditiform larvae, which have a bulbous oesophagus.","C":"This is the hookworm rhabditiform larva (slide 41), the exact reverse of Strongyloides.","D":"A mixed combination. The long buccal cavity belongs to hookworm.","E":"A mixed combination. The small primordium belongs to hookworm."},"img":"slides_i/L1_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A fresh stool sample examined within an hour of passing contains actively moving rhabditiform larvae. You must decide between hookworm and Strongyloides stercoralis. Which single pair of features settles it?","o":{"A":"Strongyloides has a SHORT buccal cavity and a LARGE genital primordium; hookworm has a LONG buccal cavity and a SMALL genital primordium","C":"Strongyloides has a LONG buccal cavity and a SMALL genital primordium; hookworm is the reverse","B":"Strongyloides has a filariform oesophagus at this stage; hookworm has a rhabditiform one","E":"Strongyloides has a notched tail at this stage; hookworm has a pointed tail","D":"Strongyloides larvae are motile and hookworm larvae are not"},"r":{"A":"Correct — and the mnemonic that survives exam pressure is that Strongyloides is the one in a hurry: short mouth, big gonad, ready to become infective inside you (autoinfection).","C":"This is the deliberate inversion. It is the single most common way to lose this mark.","B":"Both are RHABDITIFORM larvae here, so both have the bulbous rhabditiform oesophagus — that feature cannot discriminate.","E":"Pointed vs notched tail is real, but it is the FILARIFORM discriminator, not the rhabditiform one. The stem says rhabditiform.","D":"Both are motile in a fresh sample. Motility tells you the sample is fresh, not which worm it is."},"t":"Larva discriminator — buccal cavity","sl":41,"img":"slides_i/L1_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Larva discriminator — buccal cavity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Harada–Mori — back to the stool","s":"A Harada–Mori filter-paper culture set up from a barefoot farmer's stool yields long, slender larvae with an elongated oesophagus and a pointed tail. What would direct microscopy of his fresh stool most likely have shown?","lec":"L1","sl":40,"c":"<b>Harada–Mori — back to the stool</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Rhabditiform larvae with a long buccal cavity","B":"Barrel-shaped eggs with a plug at each pole","C":"Thin-shelled oval eggs containing 4–8 cells","D":"Filariform larvae with a pointed tail","E":"Rhabditiform larvae with a short buccal cavity"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Hookworm rhabditiform larvae appear only after the eggs hatch outside the body, for example in a delayed specimen or in culture. A fresh stool shows eggs.","B":"The barrel-shaped egg with polar plugs is Trichuris, which is not cultured this way.","C":"Correct. A filariform larva with a pointed tail is hookworm (slide 42). The hookworm diagnostic stage in fresh stool is the unembryonated egg: thin-shelled and transparent, with 4–8 cells (slide 40, slide 45).","D":"The filariform larva is the infective stage, found in soil or produced by culture. It is not what fresh stool shows.","E":"Rhabditiform larvae with a short buccal cavity in fresh stool would mean Strongyloides, and its filariform tail is notched, not pointed."},"img":"slides_i/L1_40.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A Harada–Mori culture set up from a patient's stool is read on day 7 and grows long slender larvae with an elongated oesophagus. The tail tip is notched. What has the culture recovered, and what does that imply for treatment?","o":{"C":"Strongyloides filariform larvae — ivermectin is the drug of choice, not albendazole alone","B":"Hookworm filariform larvae — albendazole or mebendazole will clear it","D":"Strongyloides rhabditiform larvae — the notch is a rhabditiform feature","E":"Hookworm rhabditiform larvae — culture converts filariform back to rhabditiform","A":"Trichuris larvae — Trichuris is the only nematode cultured by Harada–Mori"},"r":{"C":"Correct. NOTCHED tail = Strongyloides; pointed tail = hookworm. Strongyloides matters more because of autoinfection, and the deck gives ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day.","B":"Hookworm filariform larvae have a POINTED tail. The drug half would be right, which is exactly why this is the planted near-miss.","D":"Long and slender with an elongated oesophagus is filariform by definition; rhabditiform larvae are short with a bulb.","E":"Culture drives development FORWARD (rhabditiform → filariform), never backwards.","A":"Trichuris is diagnosed from unembryonated eggs in stool; it has no larval culture step in this deck."},"t":"Larva discriminator — tail","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L1_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Larva discriminator — tail</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Hookworm — order of clinical phases","s":"A migrant labourer who walked barefoot through wet fields develops a series of problems over the following months, ending with iron-deficiency anaemia. Which sequence, with the stage responsible for each phase, matches the lecture?","lec":"L1","sl":43,"c":"<b>Hookworm — order of clinical phases</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cough from migrating larvae, then itchy rash from filariform larvae, then anaemia from adults","B":"Itchy rash from filariform larvae, then anaemia from adults, then cough from migrating adults","C":"Itchy rash from filariform larvae, then cough from migrating larvae, then anaemia from eggs","D":"Itchy rash from filariform larvae, then cough from migrating larvae, then anaemia from adults","E":"Itchy rash from rhabditiform larvae, then cough from migrating larvae, then anaemia from adults"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The larvae must enter through the skin before they can reach the lungs.","B":"The lung phase comes before the adults settle in the gut, and it is caused by larvae, not adults.","C":"Eggs cause no disease. Blood loss comes from the attached adult worms.","D":"Correct. The phases are cutaneous (ground itch where the filariform larvae enter), then pulmonary (Loeffler's syndrome from larvae passing through the lungs), then intestinal (adult worms causing blood loss and iron-deficiency anaemia) (slide 44, slide 43).","E":"The rhabditiform larva is not infective and never penetrates skin."},"img":"slides_i/L1_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient has fever, dry cough, wheeze and marked eosinophilia. The physician suspects a migrating helminth larva in the lung. Which group of intestinal nematodes can produce this picture?","o":{"D":"Ascaris lumbricoides, hookworm and Strongyloides stercoralis — all three send larvae through the lung","E":"Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichiura and Enterobius vermicularis","B":"Hookworm, Trichuris trichiura and Capillaria philippinensis","C":"Strongyloides stercoralis and Enterobius vermicularis only","A":"Every intestinal nematode in this lecture, because all nematode larvae migrate"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Loeffler's syndrome needs a lung-migrating larva, and only these three do it — Ascaris after the egg hatches, hookworm and Strongyloides after skin penetration.","E":"Trichuris and Enterobius have NO migratory phase at all; their larvae develop in the gut lumen.","B":"Trichuris and Capillaria both stay in the bowel. Capillaria's damage is villous destruction, not pneumonitis.","C":"Enterobius does not migrate through the lung. Strongyloides alone is an incomplete answer.","A":"The whole point of the wrap-up slide is that they do NOT all migrate — this option is the trap for anyone pattern-matching 'nematode = migration'."},"t":"Loeffler's syndrome — which worms","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L1_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Loeffler's syndrome — which worms</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Severe strongyloidiasis — disseminated vs hyperinfection","s":"A kidney-transplant recipient on tacrolimus and prednisolone develops fever, neck stiffness and Escherichia coli meningitis; his stool contains abundant larvae with a short buccal cavity. How is this form of the disease classified, and why did a gut bacterium reach the meninges?","lec":"L1","sl":50,"c":"<b>Severe strongyloidiasis — disseminated vs hyperinfection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Disseminated disease; adult worms in the bowel wall released bacterial toxins","B":"Disseminated disease; the steroids alone let gut bacteria cross the bowel wall","C":"Hyperinfection; larvae invading the brain caused an eosinophilic meningitis","D":"Disseminated disease; migrating larvae carried gut bacteria to other organs","E":"Hyperinfection; migrating larvae carried gut bacteria to other organs"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Adult worms stay in the small intestine. Spread is caused by migrating larvae.","B":"The steroids are the trigger for uncontrolled larval proliferation. The bacteria are carried by the larvae, which is the point of the slide.","C":"Eosinophilic meningitis from larvae in the CNS is Angiostrongylus (L2). The organism here is a Gram-negative gut bacterium.","D":"Correct (slide 55). Disseminated strongyloidiasis is spread to other organs, with microbial sepsis because the larvae carry organisms, leading to acute meningitis. Hyperinfection is uncontrolled larval proliferation limited to the bowel and often the lungs.","E":"The mechanism is right but the label is wrong. Once disease reaches organs beyond the bowel and lungs, it is disseminated."},"img":"slides_i/L1_50.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A man on high-dose prednisolone for lupus develops abdominal pain, cough and then Gram-negative bacteraemia with meningitis. Stool shows abundant rhabditiform larvae. What has happened, and what is the correct course of ivermectin?","o":{"D":"Disseminated strongyloidiasis — ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day continued until stool examination stays negative for 2 weeks","B":"Hyperinfection strongyloidiasis — a single 2-day course of ivermectin 200 µg/kg/day","C":"Disseminated strongyloidiasis — albendazole for 3 days, because ivermectin does not cross into the CNS","A":"Loeffler's syndrome from Ascaris — albendazole single dose","E":"Hookworm autoinfection — mebendazole for 3 days"},"r":{"D":"Correct on both halves. Spread BEYOND the normal migration route (here to the meninges, with larvae carrying enteric bacteria) is DISSEMINATED disease, and the deck's regimen is ivermectin continued until stool is negative for two weeks.","B":"Hyperinfection is massive larval proliferation confined to bowel and lung. Once there is bacterial meningitis the disease has disseminated, and the 2-day uncomplicated course is far too short.","C":"The species and the mechanism are right but the drug is wrong — the deck gives ivermectin for severe disease, and the sepsis is caused by bacteria the larvae carry, not by larvae in the CSF.","A":"Ascaris produces Loeffler's syndrome but cannot autoinfect and does not cause Gram-negative sepsis.","E":"Hookworm does NOT autoinfect — its filariform larvae must develop in soil first. This is the classic hookworm/Strongyloides swap."},"t":"Severe strongyloidiasis","sl":53,"img":"slides_i/L1_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Severe strongyloidiasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Ascaris — unfertilised egg","s":"The stool of a child who vomited a large, creamy-white roundworm contains an elongated, irregular egg about 90 µm long, with a thin shell, an albuminous coat and refractile granules inside. What is this egg, and what does it mean for transmission?","lec":"L1","sl":17,"c":"<b>Ascaris — unfertilised egg</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Unfertilised egg; it cannot develop into the infective stage","B":"Embryonated egg; it is already infective when it is passed","C":"Fertilised egg; it becomes infective after weeks in the soil","D":"Decorticated egg; it becomes infective after weeks in the soil","E":"Unfertilised egg; it becomes infective after weeks in the soil"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The unfertilised egg measures 78–105 µm, is oval and irregular, and contains refractile granules (slide 17). The life cycle marks it as one that 'will not undergo further development' (slide 19), so it is diagnostic only.","B":"The embryonated egg is the infective stage, but it is found in soil, not in stool.","C":"The fertilised egg is 45–84 µm, oval to round, and holds a single unembryonated cell mass, not granules.","D":"A decorticated egg has lost its albuminous layer, but this egg still has its coat.","E":"Correct egg type, wrong fate. No embryo can develop in an unfertilised egg."},"img":"slides_i/L1_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A stool report describes an Ascaris egg that is oval with an irregular outline, has a thin chitinous shell with an albuminous coat, is colourless-to-pale, and contains refractile granules rather than a single cell mass. Which egg is this, and can it infect the next person?","o":{"A":"An unfertilised egg — it can never become infective, because there is no embryo to develop","C":"A fertilised egg — it becomes infective after 2–3 weeks in soil","E":"A decorticated egg — it is infective as soon as it is passed","D":"An embryonated egg — it is the infective stage and is found in stool","B":"A fertilised egg that has already embryonated in the bowel lumen"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Refractile granules and the irregular, longer (78–105 µm) shape mark the UNFERTILISED egg. It is a diagnostic finding only — nothing develops inside it.","C":"Fertilised eggs are rounder (45–84 µm), yellow-brown, and carry a single unsegmented cell, not granules.","E":"Decorticated simply means the albuminous layer has been LOST. This stem explicitly says the albuminous coat is present, and decorticated eggs are not infective either.","D":"The embryonated egg is the infective stage but it is found in SOIL, not in stool — the deck states this on both p.18 and p.23.","B":"Ascaris eggs cannot embryonate in the bowel. That requirement for a soil period is exactly why Ascaris cannot autoinfect."},"t":"Ascaris egg types","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L1_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Ascaris egg types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Hookworm — species and egg report","s":"An adult worm removed at duodenal endoscopy has a buccal capsule armed with two pairs of teeth. The patient's earlier stool report had simply read 'hookworm egg'. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":39,"c":"<b>Hookworm — species and egg report</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is Ancylostoma duodenale; the report should have named it from egg size","B":"It is Strongyloides stercoralis; its eggs hatch before they reach the stool","C":"It is Ancylostoma duodenale; the two human species' eggs cannot be told apart","D":"It is Necator americanus; the two human species' eggs cannot be told apart","E":"It is Necator americanus; the report should have named it from egg size"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The size ranges are printed on slide 40, but the slide marks them indistinguishable, so size is not used.","B":"Strongyloides has no toothed buccal capsule, and it does not produce 'hookworm eggs'.","C":"Correct. Two pairs of teeth means Ancylostoma duodenale, while Necator has a pair of cutting plates (slide 39). The eggs of the two species are marked 'indistinguishable' and are reported as 'hookworm egg' (slide 40).","D":"Necator has cutting plates, not teeth.","E":"Wrong species, and wrong about egg size."},"img":"slides_i/L1_39.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"An adult worm recovered at endoscopy has a buccal capsule bearing TWO PAIRS OF TEETH. A colleague asks whether the stool egg count could have told you the same thing. What is the correct pair of statements?","o":{"C":"The worm is Ancylostoma duodenale; the eggs of the two human hookworms are indistinguishable, so stool is reported simply as 'hookworm egg'","A":"The worm is Necator americanus; the eggs are indistinguishable, so stool is reported as 'hookworm egg'","E":"The worm is Ancylostoma duodenale; its eggs are larger (60–80 × 35–40 µm) and can be told apart from Necator on size","D":"The worm is Necator americanus; its cutting plates correspond to a thin-shelled 4–8 cell egg unique to the species","B":"The worm is Ancylostoma caninum, an animal hookworm, so no eggs will ever appear in human stool"},"r":{"C":"Correct on both halves. TEETH = Ancylostoma, cutting PLATES = Necator, and the deck states plainly that the eggs are *indistinguishable.","A":"Necator has a pair of cutting PLATES, not teeth. The second half is right, which is what makes this the near-miss.","E":"The size ranges do overlap and the deck marks them *Indistinguishable — quoting the numbers as if they discriminated is the trap.","D":"Wrong species for teeth, and the 4–8 cell thin-shelled egg is shared by both human hookworms.","B":"A. caninum is an animal hookworm causing creeping eruption, but the stem describes a worm recovered from the human gut, and 'no eggs ever' overstates the point being tested."},"t":"Hookworm species pair","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L1_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Hookworm species pair</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Capillaria — supportive treatment","s":"A fisherman from Buriram has had profuse watery diarrhoea for two months and is now cachectic, dehydrated, hypokalaemic and oedematous. His stool shows eggs with flattened bipolar plugs and a pitted shell. Besides albendazole or mebendazole, what does the lecture say he also needs, and why?","lec":"L1","sl":63,"c":"<b>Capillaria — supportive treatment</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Treatment of the whole family, because the eggs are infective within hours","B":"Enterotomy, because aggregated adult worms obstruct the small intestine","C":"Fluid and electrolyte support, because damaged villi cause heavy losses","D":"Prolonged ivermectin, because the larvae carry gut bacteria to other organs","E":"Iron replacement, because the adults feed on blood from the jejunal mucosa"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Treating the whole family is for Enterobius, whose eggs embryonate within 4–6 hours.","B":"Enterotomy to remove a worm mass is for Ascaris intestinal obstruction (slide 24).","C":"Correct. The peanut-shaped, pitted egg is Capillaria philippinensis. The adults burrow into the mucosa and flatten or destroy the villi, which causes malabsorption and fluid and electrolyte loss (slide 65). Slide 67 adds supportive treatment to maintain fluid and electrolyte balance.","D":"Prolonged ivermectin is for severe strongyloidiasis.","E":"Blood loss causing iron-deficiency anaemia is hookworm."},"img":"slides_i/L1_63.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A man from north-east Thailand has had watery diarrhoea and borborygmi for three months, has lost 10 kg, and now has pitting oedema. Stool shows peanut-shaped eggs with striated shells, and also larvae and adult worms. Why are larvae and adults appearing in the stool, and what explains the oedema?","o":{"C":"The female is larviparous, so autoinfection multiplies the worm burden in the small intestine; villous destruction causes a protein-losing enteropathy with hypoalbuminaemia","D":"The female is oviparous only, so the larvae come from eggs that hatched in the bowel; the oedema is from iron-deficiency anaemia","B":"Adults and larvae in stool mean the patient has Strongyloides co-infection; the oedema is renal","E":"The worms have migrated ectopically from the bile duct; the oedema is from portal hypertension","A":"The larvae are third-stage larvae from undercooked fish passing through unchanged; the oedema is nutritional only"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Capillaria philippinensis females are both oviparous AND larviparous — the larviparous ones drive internal autoinfection, so the burden escalates and villi are flattened or destroyed, giving hypoalbuminaemia and pitting oedema.","D":"The deck lists both reproductive modes; restricting it to oviparous removes the mechanism the question is testing. Iron-deficiency anaemia is the HOOKWORM story.","B":"Strongyloides gives rhabditiform larvae, not peanut-shaped striated eggs, and does not cause protein-losing enteropathy.","E":"Ectopic migration into the biliary tree is ASCARIS. Capillaria stays in the small bowel.","A":"Third-stage larvae are what you SWALLOW in the fish; they do not simply pass through, and calling the oedema purely nutritional misses the hypoalbuminaemia mechanism."},"t":"Capillaria — autoinfection","sl":62,"img":"slides_i/L1_62.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Capillaria — autoinfection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Enterobius — why treat the household","s":"After a child with anal itching is treated, the doctor also treats both parents and a sibling who have no symptoms, and repeats the course two weeks later. Which feature of this parasite's life cycle best justifies that plan?","lec":"L1","sl":74,"c":"<b>Enterobius — why treat the household</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Family members share raw fish carrying the larvae, so all are exposed together","B":"Adult worms live for years, so relapse comes from worms the first dose failed to kill","C":"Eggs passed in the stool need weeks in soil, so the household yard stays contaminated","D":"Larvae passed in the stool penetrate the skin of relatives who share a bathroom","E":"Eggs laid on perianal skin are infective within hours and spread by hands and dust"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Raw fish is the source of Capillaria.","B":"The slide gives avoiding re-infection as the reason for retreatment, not drug failure against long-lived adults.","C":"A soil period applies to Ascaris and Trichuris.","D":"Larvae in stool that penetrate skin describes Strongyloides and hookworm (their filariform larvae).","E":"Correct. Enterobius eggs are laid on the perianal skin and embryonate within 4–6 hours (slide 74). They then spread person-to-person and through the air (slide 75), so the whole family is treated and retreated after two weeks to avoid re-infection (slide 78)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_74.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 6-year-old has perianal itching worst at night. Three routine stool concentrations are negative. Which single statement about this infection is correct?","o":{"E":"The diagnostic stage is collected from PERIANAL SKIN by Scotch tape, because the embryonated egg is both the diagnostic and the infective stage and is deposited there","B":"Stool concentration is the correct test and three negatives exclude the diagnosis","D":"The infective stage is an unembryonated egg that must spend 2–3 weeks in soil","A":"Retroinfection means the gravid female crawls back up the bowel to lay eggs in the caecum","C":"Treatment is a single dose for the child alone; the family need not be treated"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and it is why the stool was negative — eggs are RARE in faeces. Enterobius is unusual in that one stage is both diagnostic and infective.","B":"This is the reasoning error the stem sets up: negative stool does not exclude enterobiasis, it is the expected result.","D":"Enterobius eggs contain a fully developed larva when laid — infective almost immediately. The soil period belongs to Ascaris and Trichuris.","A":"Retroinfection is the hatched LARVAE re-entering through the anus from the perianal skin, not the adult female reversing direction.","C":"The deck is explicit: treat the whole family and retreat after two weeks."},"t":"Enterobius — three routes","sl":75,"img":"slides_i/L1_75.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Enterobius — three routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Trichuris — anatomy to pathology","s":"A 5-year-old has bloody mucoid diarrhoea, tenesmus and a prolapsed rectum studded with small whip-shaped worms. Which statement correctly links the worm's structure to the bleeding?","lec":"L1","sl":28,"c":"<b>Trichuris — anatomy to pathology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its burrowing into small-bowel villi flattens them and causes mucosal bleeding","B":"Its buccal capsule with teeth grips the mucosa and draws blood from its vessels","C":"Its thin anterior part is embedded in large-bowel mucosa, causing petechial haemorrhage","D":"Its larvae crossing the colonic wall towards the lungs leave bleeding tracks","E":"Its thick posterior part is embedded in large-bowel mucosa, causing petechial haemorrhage"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Destruction of small-bowel villi is Capillaria.","B":"A toothed buccal capsule belongs to hookworm (Ancylostoma).","C":"Correct. The narrow anterior three-fifths of the body is embedded in the mucosa of the large intestine and caecum and causes petechial haemorrhage (slide 28, slide 31). Heavy infection gives dysentery and rectal prolapse.","D":"Trichuris has no lung migration. It hatches and develops directly in the gut.","E":"This reverses the anatomy. The thin 'whip' end is the one that burrows."},"img":"slides_i/L1_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 5-year-old presents with bloody mucous diarrhoea and tenesmus, and a prolapsed rectum studded with small whip-like worms. Which statement links this worm's anatomy to what you are seeing?","o":{"C":"The narrow anterior three-fifths is threaded into the caecal and colonic mucosa, causing petechial haemorrhage — hence dysentery and, with a heavy burden, prolapse","E":"The thick posterior three-fifths anchors in the mucosa while the thin anterior end hangs free in the lumen","B":"The worm sucks blood from submucosal vessels through a buccal capsule, which is why the stool is bloody","A":"The adult lives in the small intestine and the blood comes from villous destruction","D":"Prolapse occurs because the worms obstruct the lumen as an aggregated mass"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The deck specifies the narrow portion is 3/5 of the body and that it is EMBEDDED in large-bowel mucosa, causing petechial haemorrhage.","E":"The proportions are inverted. The thin end is the anterior, burrowing end — that is what 'whipworm' describes: thin lash, thick handle.","B":"Trichuris has no blood-sucking buccal capsule; that is hookworm. The bleeding here is mucosal petechiae.","A":"Trichuris lives in the LARGE intestine, mainly caecal mucosa. Villous destruction in the small bowel is Capillaria.","D":"Aggregated worm masses causing obstruction is ASCARIS. Trichuris prolapse is from straining with heavy mucosal infestation."},"t":"Trichuris — anatomy to sign","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L1_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Trichuris — anatomy to sign</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Strongyloides — culture when smears are negative","s":"A patient with recurrent larva currens and eosinophilia has three negative direct stool smears. Which additional laboratory method from the lecture is designed to recover this parasite, and what stage does it yield?","lec":"L1","sl":56,"c":"<b>Strongyloides — culture when smears are negative</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Agar plate culture of stool, yielding filariform larvae","B":"Harada–Mori culture of stool, yielding rhabditiform larvae","C":"Perianal sticky-tape swab, yielding embryonated eggs","D":"Colonoscopy, yielding adults embedded in the mucosa","E":"Agar plate culture of stool, yielding adult worms"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 56 lists culture methods (Harada–Mori and agar plate) for Strongyloides, and the culture yields filariform larvae.","B":"Harada–Mori is also a valid method here, but it yields filariform larvae. Rhabditiform larvae are what direct stool examination shows.","C":"The perianal tape swab is for Enterobius.","D":"Colonoscopy showing adults in the mucosa is for Trichuris (slide 32).","E":"Culture recovers larvae, not adult worms."},"img":"slides_i/L1_56.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the culture methods in this lecture is correct?","o":{"A":"Harada–Mori culture is used for both hookworm and Strongyloides and grows filariform larvae; the agar plate method is listed only for Strongyloides","D":"Harada–Mori is used only for hookworm; Strongyloides is cultured only on agar plates","C":"Both methods recover adult worms, which is how the two species are told apart","E":"Culture is used to embryonate Trichuris and Ascaris eggs so their infectivity can be confirmed","B":"Culture replaces stool microscopy because larvae are never seen directly in fresh stool"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The deck lists Harada–Mori under BOTH worms and adds the agar plate method under Strongyloides only.","D":"Half right. Harada–Mori appears under Strongyloides too, so restricting it to hookworm loses the mark.","C":"Both methods recover LARVAE. Species is then called on tail shape (pointed vs notched).","E":"No egg-embryonation culture appears anywhere in this lecture; Trichuris and Ascaris are diagnosed on eggs seen directly.","B":"Strongyloides rhabditiform larvae are exactly what you look for in direct stool examination — culture supplements it, it does not replace it."},"t":"Culture methods","sl":45,"img":"slides_i/L1_45.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Culture methods</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Diagnostic stage — lab reports","s":"Five laboratory reports each record the diagnostic stage found for a different patient. Which report is consistent with the lecture's learning-guidance tables?","lec":"L1","sl":80,"c":"<b>Diagnostic stage — lab reports</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Child who vomited a roundworm; embryonated eggs in fresh stool","B":"Child with rectal prolapse; embryonated eggs in fresh stool","C":"Man who eats raw small fish; embryonated eggs in fresh stool","D":"Farmer with iron-deficiency anaemia; filariform larvae in fresh stool","E":"Child with nocturnal anal itching; embryonated eggs on a perianal tape"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Ascaris eggs in stool (fertilised, unfertilised or decorticated) are unembryonated. The embryonated egg is the infective stage in soil.","B":"Trichuris eggs are unembryonated in stool. They embryonate in soil.","C":"Capillaria eggs are unembryonated in stool (slide 69).","D":"In hookworm the stool shows unembryonated 4–8-cell eggs. The filariform larva is the infective stage, found in soil.","E":"Correct. Enterobius is the one worm whose diagnostic stage is the embryonated egg, found on perianal skin (slide 74, slide 80)."},"img":"slides_i/L1_80.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which worm is correctly paired with the stage you would REPORT from the specimen (its diagnostic stage)?","o":{"E":"Strongyloides stercoralis — rhabditiform larva","B":"Hookworm — filariform larva","A":"Ascaris lumbricoides — embryonated egg","C":"Trichuris trichiura — embryonated egg","D":"Enterobius vermicularis — unembryonated egg"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Strongyloides is the one worm whose LARVA, not egg, appears in stool — the eggs hatch inside the bowel.","B":"Hookworm's diagnostic stage is the unembryonated 4–8 cell EGG. The filariform larva is its INFECTIVE stage, found in soil.","A":"Ascaris eggs are passed UNembryonated (fertilised, unfertilised or decorticated). The embryonated egg is the infective stage in soil.","C":"Trichuris is the same trap: unembryonated egg in stool, embryonated egg in soil.","D":"Enterobius is the reverse trap — its egg is EMBRYONATED when laid, which is why it is both diagnostic and infective."},"t":"Diagnostic stage — the grid","sl":82,"img":"slides_i/L1_82.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Diagnostic stage — the grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"Capillaria — host roles","s":"A nematode whose peanut-shaped, pitted-shell eggs appear in the stool of patients with protein-losing enteropathy is kept circulating in nature by fish-eating birds, even in years when few people are infected. Which assignment of host roles is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":64,"c":"<b>Capillaria — host roles</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Humans definitive; birds intermediate; small freshwater fish reservoir","B":"Humans definitive; small freshwater fish intermediate; birds reservoir","C":"Humans intermediate; birds definitive; small freshwater fish reservoir","D":"Birds definitive; small freshwater fish intermediate; humans accidental","E":"Humans intermediate; small freshwater fish definitive; birds reservoir"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Bird and fish are swapped. The larva develops in the fish that people eat.","B":"Correct (slide 64, slide 69). Humans host the sexually reproducing adult (definitive host). The fish carries the third-stage larva (intermediate host). Fish-eating birds maintain the parasite without harm (reservoir host).","C":"Humans are the definitive host, and the fish is the intermediate host.","D":"Humans are not accidental here. The worm matures and produces eggs in the human gut.","E":"Humans harbour the adult, so they cannot be the intermediate host."},"img":"slides_i/L1_64.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"For Capillaria philippinensis, which assignment of host roles is correct?","o":{"B":"Definitive host humans; intermediate host small freshwater fish; reservoir host fish-eating birds","C":"Definitive host fish-eating birds; intermediate host humans; reservoir host small freshwater fish","D":"Definitive host humans; intermediate host fish-eating birds; reservoir host small freshwater fish","E":"Definitive host small freshwater fish; humans are an accidental host only","A":"There is no intermediate host — the infection is soil-transmitted like the other intestinal nematodes"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The fish carries the third-stage larva (intermediate), the bird maintains the parasite in nature without harm (reservoir), and the adult worm reproduces sexually in the human gut (definitive).","C":"Humans harbour the sexually reproducing adult, so humans cannot be the intermediate host.","D":"The roles of bird and fish are swapped. The larva develops in the FISH — that is what you eat.","E":"Humans are the definitive host here; calling them accidental is the Angiostrongylus/Gnathostoma pattern, not this one.","A":"Capillaria is the one FOOD-BORNE worm in this lecture — the deliberate exception to the soil-transmitted group."},"t":"Host terminology — Capillaria","sl":64,"img":"slides_i/L1_64.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Host terminology — Capillaria</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Ascaris — prevention vs control","s":"A 6-year-old vomits a 25-cm, creamy-white cylindrical worm whose mouth has three lips. Her mother asks what the child herself can do to avoid catching it again. Which measure is listed as prevention for this worm?","lec":"L1","sl":16,"c":"<b>Ascaris — prevention vs control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Treatment of infected individuals","B":"Washing vegetables before eating","C":"Wearing shoes when walking outdoors","D":"Cooking small freshwater fish well","E":"Not using human faeces as fertiliser"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"This is also a control measure.","B":"Correct. A creamy-white worm with three lips is Ascaris lumbricoides. Prevention on slide 25 is hand-washing and washing vegetables before eating, because the worm is caught by ingesting embryonated eggs.","C":"Shoes prevent skin penetration by hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Cooking small freshwater fish prevents Capillaria.","E":"This is a control measure for the community, not personal prevention."},"img":"slides_i/L1_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Five patients each need ONE piece of prevention advice. Which pairing is right?","o":{"B":"A rice farmer with iron-deficiency anaemia and hookworm eggs in stool — wear shoes and gloves when working the soil","E":"A child with pruritus ani and Enterobius — wear shoes outdoors","C":"A man with chronic watery diarrhoea and Capillaria — wash vegetables before eating","D":"A woman vomiting a large cylindrical worm (Ascaris) — cook small freshwater fish thoroughly","A":"A child with rectal prolapse and Trichuris — avoid undercooked pork"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Hookworm enters by SKIN PENETRATION from soil, so the advice must block skin–soil contact.","E":"Enterobius is person-to-person, air-borne and retroinfection — the advice is short fingernails and handwashing, plus treating the whole family.","C":"Capillaria is food-borne from raw small freshwater FISH; washing vegetables is the advice for the egg-ingested worms.","D":"Ascaris is acquired by swallowing embryonated eggs from soil — wash hands and vegetables. The fish advice belongs to Capillaria.","A":"Pork appears nowhere in this lecture; Trichuris is acquired by ingesting embryonated eggs from soil."},"t":"Prevention — matched to route","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L1_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Prevention — matched to route</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L1|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Ascaris — ectopic migration","s":"A 9-year-old with a heavy roundworm burden (stool full of round, yellow eggs with a bumpy coat) develops colicky right-upper-quadrant pain and jaundice. What is the most likely explanation, and which investigation from the lecture would show it?","lec":"L1","sl":20,"c":"<b>Ascaris — ectopic migration</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"An aggregated worm mass blocked the jejunum; plain abdominal X-ray","B":"Larvae in the lungs caused Loeffler's syndrome; chest radiograph","C":"Larvae migrating through the liver caused hepatitis; liver biopsy","D":"Adult worms migrated into the common bile duct; ultrasound","E":"Eggs embolised to portal venules, as in schistosomiasis; CT scan"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Intestinal obstruction from worm masses is real, but it causes bowel obstruction, not obstructive jaundice.","B":"Loeffler's syndrome causes cough, wheeze and fever, not jaundice.","C":"Larval migration is not the cause of biliary jaundice, and liver biopsy is not a method in this lecture.","D":"Correct. Ectopic ascariasis includes migration of adult worms into the common bile duct (slide 20). Ultrasound, CT, MRI and endoscopy are listed as diagnostic methods (slide 22).","E":"Egg embolisation is the schistosome mechanism. Ascaris eggs leave in the faeces."},"img":"slides_i/L1_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A child with a heavy Ascaris burden develops right upper quadrant pain and jaundice. Which statement best fits?","o":{"D":"Adult worms have migrated ectopically into the common bile duct; ultrasound or CT is the imaging that demonstrates it","B":"Larvae migrating through the liver have caused Loeffler's syndrome; chest radiography is the investigation","A":"An aggregated mass of worms is obstructing the small bowel; stool examination will be negative","C":"The eggs have embolised to the liver, as in schistosomiasis; a liver biopsy is needed","E":"This is impossible — Ascaris adults remain in the small intestinal lumen throughout"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The deck names ectopic ascariasis to nose, mouth and common bile duct, and lists endoscopy, CT, ultrasound and MRI as diagnostic methods alongside stool.","B":"Loeffler's syndrome is the LUNG phase and presents with cough and wheeze, not obstructive jaundice.","A":"Obstruction is real in heavy infection but presents as small-bowel obstruction, not jaundice — and stool would still show eggs.","C":"Egg embolisation to the liver is the SCHISTOSOMA mechanism, from a different lecture.","E":"Ectopic migration of adults is explicitly listed, so 'impossible' is false — this is the option for anyone who only memorised 'lumen of small intestine'."},"t":"Ectopic ascariasis","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L1_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Ectopic ascariasis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233901039},{"id":"I|L2|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Why stool fails — two reasons","s":"Two patients are on the same ward. Patient 1 ate raw Pila snails and now has severe headache, neck stiffness and eosinophils in the CSF. Patient 2 ate raw wild boar at a feast in Nan; after a bout of diarrhoea he now has facial oedema and aching muscles. The intern sends stool from both for formalin-ether concentration, and both are negative. Which explanation of the two negative results matches the lecture?","lec":"L2","sl":2,"c":"<b>Why stool fails — two reasons</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In patient 1 the larva cannot develop into an egg-laying adult in a human; in patient 2 the diagnostic larvae have encysted in striated muscle","B":"In patient 1 the human is a definitive host whose adult worms stay in the brain; in patient 2 the human is an accidental host whose larva never matures","C":"In patient 1 the adult females lay their eggs into the CSF instead of the gut; in patient 2 the encysted larvae lie in the intestinal mucosa","D":"In patient 1 the adults sit in the pulmonary arteries, so larvae leave in sputum; in patient 2 the adults live in muscle and never enter the gut","E":"In patient 1 the human is a paratenic host, so larvae are passed intermittently; in patient 2 the eggs are too scanty to detect until the muscle phase"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Patient 1 = Angiostrongylus (snail + CSF eosinophils); the human is an accidental host, so the third-stage larva never becomes an egg-laying adult and nothing reaches the stool. Patient 2 = Trichinella (wild boar, enteric phase then facial oedema and myalgia); the human IS a definitive host, but the diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in striated muscle (muscle biopsy). These are exactly the two reasons behind slide 2's 'cannot be diagnosed by stool examination'.","B":"Host status is swapped: Angiostrongylus → human accidental; Trichinella → human definitive (slide 32 lists humans among its definitive hosts).","C":"No adult Angiostrongylus develops in a human, so there are no eggs anywhere. For Trichinella, the adults are in the intestinal mucosa but the ENCYSTED larvae are in striated muscle — the option puts the right stage in the wrong tissue.","D":"The pulmonary arteries are the habitat in the RAT, not the human. Trichinella adults live in the small-intestinal mucosa (slide 32), so the second half is also inverted.","E":"The human is an ACCIDENTAL host of Angiostrongylus, not paratenic (slide 7 defines paratenic as a host in which the parasite does not change stage at all; accidental = it grows but never reaches adulthood). Trichinella females produce larvae, and nothing diagnostic appears in stool at any phase."},"img":"slides_i/L2_2.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"What single property separates every worm in this lecture from every worm in L1, and what does it change about diagnosis?","o":{"A":"They live in tissue, blood or the nervous system rather than the gut lumen, so stool examination is useless","B":"They are all transmitted by mosquitoes rather than by soil, so blood films replace stool","C":"They are all acquired from raw food, so a dietary history replaces the laboratory","E":"They are all zoonotic, so animal serology replaces human testing","D":"They are all treated with ivermectin, so the diagnosis rarely matters"},"r":{"A":"Correct — and the BM33 deck says so by re-showing L1's chart with 'diagnosed by stool examination' under one column and this lecture under the other. No adult lays eggs into the bowel lumen, so every diagnostic method has to change: blood film, muscle biopsy, serology, CSF.","B":"Only the filarial worms are mosquito-borne; Gnathostoma, Angiostrongylus and Trichinella are food-borne.","C":"Three of the five are food-borne, but the filarial worms are not.","E":"Several are zoonotic, but that is not what unites them and it does not change the specimen.","D":"Treatment differs between them, and it is not the organising idea."},"t":"Orientation","sl":2,"img":"slides_i/L2_2.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Orientation</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Angiostrongylus — vegetable route","s":"A 45-year-old vegetarian from Khon Kaen, who has never eaten snails or any raw animal food, develops severe headache, neck stiffness and photophobia. Her CSF shows markedly raised eosinophils. Her home garden is overrun with giant African snails, and she eats its lettuce raw every day. What was her most likely mode of infection?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — vegetable route</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of raw vegetables contaminated with embryonated eggs","B":"Skin penetration by third-stage larvae while handling garden snails","C":"Ingestion of raw water plants carrying encysted metacercariae","D":"Ingestion of raw vegetables contaminated with third-stage larvae","E":"Ingestion of first-stage larvae passed onto vegetables in rat faeces"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Embryonated eggs on vegetables is the Ascaris / Trichuris route (L1). No egg of Angiostrongylus is ever infective to humans.","B":"Skin penetration by L3 from a handled meal is Gnathostoma's food-handler route; it is not a listed route for Angiostrongylus.","C":"Metacercariae on water plants is Fasciolopsis buski (L4) — a trematode, and an intestinal one.","D":"Correct. Headache + neck stiffness + CSF eosinophils in the North-east = Angiostrongylus cantonensis. Besides raw snails, slugs and paratenic hosts, the slide's mode of infection explicitly includes 'contaminated vegetables containing third stage larvae' — larvae shed from the snail onto the leaves.","E":"First-stage larvae in rat faeces infect the SNAIL, not people; they must develop to L3 inside the mollusc first (slide 17 life cycle)."},"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient develops severe headache, neck stiffness and paraesthesiae after eating raw Pila snails. CSF shows eosinophilic pleocytosis. What is the parasite, and what is the human's host status?","o":{"C":"Wuchereria bancrofti — the human is the definitive host","B":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis — the human is the definitive host and adults develop in the lung","E":"Gnathostoma spinigerum — the human is the definitive host","A":"Trichinella spiralis — the human is an accidental host","D":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis — the human is an accidental host in which the larva can never mature"},"r":{"C":"Wuchereria is mosquito-borne and causes lymphatic obstruction.","B":"The lung stage belongs to the RAT's cycle, not the human's.","E":"Gnathostoma also makes humans accidental hosts, but its signature is migratory swelling, and the snail history points to Angiostrongylus.","A":"Trichinella comes from raw pork or wild boar and lodges in striated muscle.","D":"Correct. The rat is the definitive host; in a human the larva migrates to the CNS, cannot complete development and dies there, producing eosinophilic meningitis."},"t":"Angiostrongylus","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Trichinella — why the illness has two phases","s":"A 35-year-old man from Chiang Rai ate raw wild-boar larb at a funeral. Within days he had nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea; about two weeks later his face and eyelids swelled and his limb muscles became painful and firm. Which sequence of events in his body explains this two-phase illness?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"<b>Trichinella — why the illness has two phases</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Encysted larvae freed in the stomach → adults in the lymphatics → microfilariae in the blood → encystment in striated muscle","B":"Encysted larvae freed in the small intestine → adults in the mucosa → new larvae carried in the circulation → encystment in striated muscle","C":"Third-stage larvae penetrate the stomach wall → wander through subcutaneous tissue → reach the eyelids → encyst in the orbit","D":"Eggs hatch in the small intestine → larvae penetrate the gut wall → adults mature in striated muscle → eggs are passed in stool","E":"Larvae freed in the small intestine → migration to the lungs → coughed up and swallowed → adults settle in the mucosa"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Lymphatics and microfilariae belong to W. bancrofti / B. malayi.","B":"Correct. Raw boar + GI upset then (≈2 weeks) facial/periorbital oedema and myalgia = Trichinella spiralis. The CDC cycle on slide 27: encysted larva ingested → released in small intestine → adults in small intestine → larvae deposited in mucosa → circulation → encysted larva in striated muscle. The adults explain phase 1 (GI), the migrating/encysting larvae explain phase 2 (oedema, myalgia, pseudohypertrophy, nurse cell).","C":"Migration through subcutaneous tissue is Gnathostoma's pattern (migratory swelling); the stomach wall is its habitat in cats and dogs.","D":"Trichinella never produces eggs that reach the stool, and the adults live in the gut mucosa, not in muscle — the option inverts adult and larval sites.","E":"A lung migration then swallowing is the Ascaris / hookworm / Strongyloides pattern from L1; Trichinella has no lung phase."},"img":"slides_i/L2_27.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A man develops diarrhoea, then muscle swelling and tenderness in the limbs with periorbital and facial oedema, two weeks after a wedding feast of raw wild boar. What is the diagnostic stage, and in what specimen?","o":{"E":"Larvae in cerebrospinal fluid","A":"Adult worms in stool","C":"Microfilariae on a night blood film","D":"Eggs in stool by concentration technique","B":"Encysted larvae in striated muscle, found on muscle biopsy"},"r":{"E":"CSF larvae would point to Angiostrongylus.","A":"Adults are not recovered from stool in practice, and the gut phase is brief.","C":"Microfilariae belong to the filarial worms.","D":"Trichinella females are viviparous — they release larvae, not eggs, and nothing appears in stool.","B":"Correct. Trichinella's adults are transient in the gut; the larvae encyst in striated muscle inside a nurse cell, and that is what a biopsy shows. The sequence — enteric phase, then myositis with periorbital oedema — is the classic story."},"t":"Trichinella","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L2_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Trichinella</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Brugian filariasis — diagnostic stage","s":"A 42-year-old rubber tapper from Narathiwat has had recurrent painful lymph nodes in the groin and, over three months, swelling of the right leg below the knee with thickened, darkened skin. Her genitalia are normal. What is the diagnostic stage of the most likely parasite, and where is it found?","lec":"L2","sl":34,"c":"<b>Brugian filariasis — diagnostic stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Microfilariae in peripheral blood","B":"Encysted larvae in striated muscle","C":"Adult worms in the lymphatic system","D":"Third-stage larvae in peripheral blood","E":"Third-stage larvae in the swollen skin"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Southern Thailand (Chumphon → Narathiwat) + lymphadenitis + lymphoedema below the knee with the genitals spared = Brugia malayi. Its diagnostic stage is the microfilaria in peripheral blood, sought on thick and thin blood smears.","B":"Encysted larvae in striated muscle is Trichinella's diagnostic stage.","C":"The lymphatic system is the adults' HABITAT, not the diagnostic stage (slide 38 lists them separately).","D":"Third-stage larvae are the INFECTIVE stage — in the mosquito, entering the bite wound. Swapping infective and diagnostic stages is the classic distractor.","E":"L3 larvae in skin/tissue is the Gnathostoma pattern (larva recovered from a lesion)."},"img":"slides_i/L2_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 50-year-old man in Narathiwat has fever and enlarged lymph nodes. What is the mode of transmission of the most likely parasite?","o":{"B":"Ingestion of undercooked freshwater fish","A":"The bite of an infected Mansonia mosquito, which deposits L3 larvae that penetrate the bite wound","D":"Skin penetration by cercariae in water","C":"Injection of sporozoites by a female Anopheles","E":"Ingestion of embryonated eggs from soil"},"r":{"B":"Fish-borne infections give hepatobiliary or intestinal disease, not lymphadenopathy.","A":"Correct. Southern Thailand plus fever with lymphadenopathy points to Brugia malayi, whose vector is Mansonia. Note the verb: filarial L3 larvae are DEPOSITED on the skin and penetrate the bite wound — they are not injected.","D":"Cercarial penetration is Schistosoma (L4).","C":"Sporozoite INJECTION is Plasmodium — and malaria characteristically has neither rash nor lymphadenopathy, which is what separates it here.","E":"Soil-transmitted eggs give intestinal nematodes."},"t":"Filariasis","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Gnathostoma — definite diagnosis","s":"A 28-year-old woman from Nakhon Nayok often eats undercooked frog and snakehead fish. For a year she has had painless swellings that appear on her cheek, fade over a few days, then reappear on her forearm. Her blood eosinophil count is raised. According to the lecture, which finding would make the definite diagnosis?","lec":"L2","sl":9,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — definite diagnosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Eggs found in stool by a concentration method","B":"A larva recovered from one of the skin lesions","C":"A positive immunoblot for specific antibody","D":"Microfilariae on a thick and thin blood smear","E":"Encysted larvae in a biopsy of skeletal muscle"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Humans are an accidental host, so the third-stage larva never becomes an adult and no eggs are produced — stool examination is not helpful (slide 9's boxed note).","B":"Correct. Raw frog/fish + intermittent migratory swelling = Gnathostoma spinigerum. Slide 9: 'Definite diagnosis is the identification of the larvae from the lesion.' Everything else on that slide (history, eosinophilia, CSF, immunoblot/ELISA, PCR, imaging) is supportive.","C":"Immunoblot / ELISA IS on Gnathostoma's slide — but as a supporting laboratory investigation. The lecture reserves 'definite diagnosis' for finding the larva itself.","D":"Blood smears for microfilariae are for W. bancrofti / B. malayi.","E":"Encysted larvae in muscle biopsy is Trichinella."},"img":"slides_i/L2_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which clinical picture is characteristic of Gnathostoma spinigerum, and how is it acquired?","o":{"B":"Migratory subcutaneous swelling, from third-stage larvae in raw freshwater fish, eel or frog","E":"Fixed subcutaneous nodules, from filariform larvae penetrating skin","A":"Eosinophilic meningitis, from metacercariae in raw crab","D":"Lymphoedema of the leg, from L3 larvae deposited by a mosquito","C":"Periorbital oedema with myositis, from encysted larvae in pork"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The larva wanders, so the swelling MOVES — the single most useful clinical clue. Cyclops is the first intermediate host and a fish, eel or frog the second.","E":"A fixed nodule is not the pattern, and skin penetration is not the route.","A":"Eosinophilic meningitis is Angiostrongylus, and the meal is a snail, not a crab.","D":"Lymphoedema is filarial.","C":"That is Trichinella."},"t":"Gnathostoma","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L2_7.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Gnathostoma</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"W. bancrofti microfilaria → which patient","s":"A thick blood film shows a sheathed microfilaria about 280 × 9 µm with a gently curved body, a short empty head space, separate nuclei that can be counted individually, and no nuclei at the tip of the tail. Which patient is this film most likely to have come from?","lec":"L2","sl":35,"c":"<b>W. bancrofti microfilaria → which patient</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A rubber tapper in Narathiwat with leg swelling below the knee and normal genitalia","B":"A villager in Khon Kaen with headache, neck stiffness and CSF eosinophils","C":"A fisherman in Chumphon with swelling of the forearm below the elbow","D":"A farmer in Chiang Mai with periorbital oedema and myalgia after raw pork","E":"A migrant worker in Tak with swelling of the leg and the scrotum"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Narathiwat, below-knee swelling and spared genitals is the Brugia malayi picture — its microfilaria is smaller (175–230 × 5–6 µm), tightly coiled, with a long head space and two terminal nuclei.","B":"North-east + meningism + CSF eosinophils = Angiostrongylus; no blood-film stage.","C":"Chumphon and 'below the elbow' are also Brugia malayi (Chumphon → Narathiwat; leg below knee / arm below elbow).","D":"Periorbital oedema + myalgia after raw pork = Trichinella, diagnosed by muscle biopsy, not a blood film.","E":"Correct. 240–300 × 7.5–10 µm, gently curved, separate countable nuclei, small head space, no terminal nuclei = Wuchereria bancrofti. In Thailand it sits in the border provinces (Tak, Kanchanaburi, Ratchaburi, Ranong), and bancroftian disease mostly affects the lower limbs but also the arm, breast and genitalia."},"img":"slides_i/L2_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which mosquito genus transmits which filarial species in Thailand?","o":{"E":"Mansonia transmits Brugia malayi; Culex, Aedes and Anopheles transmit Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Anopheles transmits Brugia malayi; Mansonia transmits Wuchereria bancrofti","C":"Aedes transmits Brugia malayi; Culex transmits Wuchereria bancrofti","A":"All four genera transmit both species equally","B":"Only Culex transmits either species"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and this single split is what every vector-matching question in the archive is built on. Brugia malayi is also concentrated in southern Thailand, matching Mansonia's distribution.","D":"Reversed — and this is the commonest wrong pairing offered.","C":"Aedes carries W. bancrofti, not B. malayi.","A":"The species split is the examinable point.","B":"Culex is one of three W. bancrofti vectors."},"t":"Filariasis","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L2_33.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Gnathostoma — ocular form vs Trichinella","s":"A 50-year-old man has swelling around one eye and progressive blurring of vision in that eye. Over the past year he has had a swelling that moved slowly across his chest wall, and he often eats undercooked eel. He has no muscle pain. What is the diagnostic stage of the causative parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — ocular form vs Trichinella</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Microfilaria in peripheral blood","B":"Unembryonated egg in the stool","C":"Third-stage larva in human tissue","D":"Adult worm in the stomach wall","E":"Encysted larva in striated muscle"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Microfilariae in blood are the filarial diagnostic stage.","B":"Unembryonated eggs pass in the faeces of the DEFINITIVE host (cat/dog) in the CDC cycle, never in the accidental human host.","C":"Correct. Undercooked eel + migratory swelling + periorbital swelling with visual loss = ocular gnathostomiasis (slide 8). The diagnostic stage is the third-stage larva found in the human, the accidental host.","D":"The stomach wall (inside a tumour) is the adult's habitat in cats, dogs and wild felines — the adult never forms in a human.","E":"Periorbital oedema is also a Trichinella sign, which is the bait — but Trichinella comes from pork/boar, brings myalgia, and does not migrate. Its diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in muscle."},"img":"slides_i/L2_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"When should blood be drawn to look for microfilariae of Wuchereria bancrofti, and why?","o":{"E":"Immediately after exercise, which mobilises microfilariae from the lungs","D":"At midday, because the microfilariae accumulate in peripheral blood during activity","A":"At any time, because the microfilariae are present continuously","C":"At night, because the microfilariae show nocturnal periodicity matching the vector's biting time","B":"Only after treatment, which releases microfilariae into the circulation"},"r":{"E":"Exercise is not the trigger.","D":"The opposite of nocturnal periodicity.","A":"If that were true the timing question would not exist.","C":"Correct. The microfilariae appear in peripheral blood when the vector feeds, so a daytime film can be falsely negative in a genuinely infected patient. This is the one timing fact the papers set.","B":"Treatment kills microfilariae rather than releasing them, and diagnosis precedes treatment."},"t":"Diagnosis","sl":40,"img":"slides_i/L2_40.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Filariae — what the mosquito does","s":"A Mansonia mosquito feeds at night on a man in Narathiwat whose blood contains sheathed microfilariae with two terminal nuclei. Two weeks later the same mosquito feeds on his neighbour. Which account of the parasite's passage through the mosquito and into the neighbour is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Filariae — what the mosquito does</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The microfilariae develop into adult worms in the mosquito, and these release larvae onto the skin","B":"The microfilariae develop into third-stage larvae in the mosquito, and these penetrate the bite wound","C":"The microfilariae develop into third-stage larvae in the mosquito, and these are injected into a capillary","D":"The microfilariae survive unchanged in the mosquito, and are injected into the neighbour's bloodstream","E":"The microfilariae develop into sporozoites in the salivary glands, and these are injected with saliva"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Adult filarial worms live only in the definitive host's lymphatics, never in the mosquito.","B":"Correct. The mosquito is the intermediate host: ingested microfilariae shed their sheaths, penetrate the midgut, develop L1 → L3 in the thoracic muscles and migrate to the proboscis. The lecture's mode of infection is 'third stage larvae penetrate into wound during mosquitoes biting'.","C":"Right stage, wrong verb. 'Injection' belongs to malaria sporozoites (and Leishmania promastigotes); filarial L3 are deposited and actively penetrate the bite wound — the lecturer's precision trap.","D":"Microfilariae are what the mosquito TAKES UP (the diagnostic stage in human blood); they must develop inside it before they are infective.","E":"Sporozoites in salivary glands, injected with saliva, is Plasmodium (L7)."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Three parasites enter through the skin by three different mechanisms. Which set is correctly matched?","o":{"C":"All three are injected by their arthropod vector","A":"Plasmodium sporozoites penetrate intact skin; filarial larvae are injected; Schistosoma cercariae are deposited","B":"Plasmodium sporozoites are INJECTED; filarial L3 larvae are DEPOSITED and penetrate the bite wound; Schistosoma cercariae PENETRATE INTACT SKIN","D":"All three penetrate intact skin from water","E":"Plasmodium is deposited; filarial larvae penetrate intact skin; Schistosoma is injected"},"r":{"C":"Schistosoma has no arthropod vector at all.","A":"Every verb is misassigned.","B":"Correct, and the papers use these three verbs as distractors for each other — BM31's Leishmania question offered 'injection of sporozoite during mosquito's blood meals' as a wrong option in a non-malaria stem.","D":"Only Schistosoma does this.","E":"All three are wrong."},"t":"Verbs","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Verbs</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Gnathostoma — role of the chicken","s":"A woman who carefully avoids raw fish and frogs develops an itchy swelling on her abdomen that creeps to a new site every few days; her eosinophil count is high. Her only risky food is lightly grilled free-range chicken, eaten weekly. In the life cycle of the parasite she most likely has, what role does the chicken play?","lec":"L2","sl":7,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — role of the chicken</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Second intermediate host","B":"Definitive host","C":"First intermediate host","D":"Paratenic host","E":"Accidental host"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The second intermediate hosts are freshwater fish, eels and frogs — the larva develops (early L3 → advanced L3) there.","B":"The definitive hosts are cats, dogs and wild felines, with the adult in a stomach-wall tumour.","C":"The first intermediate host is Cyclops.","D":"Correct. Migratory (creeping) swelling + eosinophilia = Gnathostoma spinigerum. Birds, chicken and snakes are its paratenic hosts: they eat an infected fish/frog and simply carry the third-stage larva without it developing further — slide 7's Thai definition: the parasite does not change stage from the one the host took in.","E":"The accidental host is the human — the parasite grows but never reaches adulthood."},"img":"slides_i/L2_7.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which parasite has the human as an ACCIDENTAL host?","o":{"A":"Brugia malayi","B":"Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides","E":"Trichuris trichiura"},"r":{"A":"Same for B. malayi.","B":"Humans are the definitive host of W. bancrofti — the adults live in human lymphatics and reproduce there.","D":"Correct. The rat is the definitive host; in a human the larva reaches the CNS and dies without maturing. Gnathostoma is the other accidental-host parasite in this lecture.","C":"Humans are the definitive host of Ascaris.","E":"Humans are the definitive host of Trichuris."},"t":"Angiostrongylus","sl":23,"img":"slides_i/L2_23.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q23","t":"Trichinella","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with diarrhoea, muscular hypertrophy, tenderness in the extremities and facial oedema. He ate raw wild boar two weeks ago at a relative's wedding. What is the diagnostic stage?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"<b>Trichinella</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q23 — raw wild boar, myositis, facial oedema</span>","o":{"A":"Rhabditiform larvae in stool","B":"Adult worms in stool","C":"Microfilariae in blood","E":"Embryonated eggs in stool","D":"Encysted larvae in muscle"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Rhabditiform larvae in stool is Strongyloides (L1).","B":"The intestinal phase is brief and adults are not the diagnostic finding.","C":"Microfilariae belong to the filarial worms.","E":"Trichinella females release LARVAE, not eggs, and nothing reaches the stool.","D":"Correct. Trichinella larvae encyst inside a nurse cell in striated muscle; muscle biopsy is the diagnostic specimen, and the answer key describes the nurse cell as oval with pink calcification."},"img":"slides_i/L2_27.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q12","t":"Filariasis","s":"A 50-year-old man from Narathiwat has fever and enlarged lymph nodes. What is the mode of transmission of the most likely parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q12 — Narathiwat, fever with lymphadenopathy</span>","o":{"D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork","E":"Injection of sporozoites during an Anopheles blood meal","A":"Ingestion of raw freshwater fish","B":"Skin penetration by cercariae in fresh water","C":"The bite of an infected Mansonia mosquito"},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Pork gives Taenia solium or Trichinella.","E":"Malaria is the designed near-miss for any 'fever in an endemic area' stem — but malaria characteristically has NO rash and NO lymphadenopathy, which is exactly why this stem mentions nodes.","A":"Fish-borne parasites give hepatobiliary or intestinal disease.","B":"Cercarial penetration is Schistosoma.","C":"Correct. Southern Thailand plus fever and lymphadenopathy is Brugia malayi, whose vector is Mansonia."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-angio","t":"Angiostrongylus","s":"A patient has a history of eating Pila spp. (apple snail). What infection is most likely?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 เฉลย SUM1 — history of eating Pila spp. (หอยเชอรี่)</span>","o":{"B":"Fasciolopsis buski","A":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","D":"Gnathostoma spinigerum","E":"Opisthorchis viverrini","C":"Paragonimus spp."},"a":"A","r":{"B":"F. buski comes from raw aquatic PLANTS such as water chestnut.","A":"Correct. The apple snail carries the third-stage larva of A. cantonensis, and the human is an accidental host in whom the larva reaches the CNS and causes eosinophilic meningitis.","D":"Gnathostoma comes from raw fish, eel or frog and gives migratory swelling.","E":"O. viverrini comes from raw cyprinoid fish and goes to the bile ducts.","C":"Paragonimus comes from raw or pickled crab and goes to the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-accidental","t":"Accidental host","s":"Which of the following parasites has the human as an ACCIDENTAL host?","lec":"L2","sl":23,"c":"<b>Accidental host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — 'which parasite has a human as an accidental host?'</span>","o":{"D":"Brugia malayi","A":"Necator americanus","C":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","B":"Taenia saginata","E":"Paragonimus spp."},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Humans are the definitive host of B. malayi.","A":"Humans are the definitive host of hookworm — adults live and reproduce in the human gut.","C":"Correct. The rat is the definitive host; in a human the larva cannot mature and dies in the CNS.","B":"Humans are the definitive host of T. saginata.","E":"Humans are the definitive host of Paragonimus — the adults live in the human lung."},"img":"slides_i/L2_23.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-infstage","t":"Infective stage","s":"Which statement about the infective stage is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":38,"c":"<b>Infective stage</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — Wuchereria vs skin-penetrating larvae</span>","o":{"A":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects by skin penetration of filariform larvae from soil","D":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects when the mosquito injects microfilariae directly into the blood","E":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects when the mosquito deposits third-stage larvae that enter through the bite wound","B":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects by ingestion of embryonated eggs","C":"Wuchereria bancrofti infects by ingestion of larvae in undercooked fish"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"That is hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Microfilariae are what the mosquito takes UP from the human, not what it puts back.","E":"Correct, and the formative's own key makes the contrast explicit: Schistosoma, hookworm and Strongyloides have larvae that PENETRATE SKIN, whereas Wuchereria has larvae DEPOSITED BY THE VECTOR at the bite site.","B":"Filarial worms have no egg stage outside the female.","C":"No fish stage exists in this cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L2_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L2|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-larva","t":"Larval stages","s":"Which larval stage is the INFECTIVE stage of hookworm?","lec":"L2","sl":16,"c":"<b>Larval stages</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — filariform vs rhabditiform larva</span>","o":{"B":"Rhabditiform larva","C":"Filariform larva","D":"Third-stage larva in a fish","E":"Encysted larva in muscle","A":"Microfilaria"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"The rhabditiform larva is the feeding, non-infective stage — it is what hatches from the egg in the soil, and what you find in fresh stool in strongyloidiasis.","C":"Correct. The filariform (L3) larva is the slender, non-feeding, skin-penetrating stage.","D":"That is Capillaria or Gnathostoma.","E":"That is Trichinella.","A":"That is the filarial worms' blood stage, which infects the MOSQUITO rather than a human."},"img":"slides_i/L2_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L2|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q25","t":"Gnathostoma","s":"A patient has recurrent, INTERMITTENT MIGRATORY SWELLING of the face and limbs. He regularly eats improperly cooked eel. What is the most likely parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q25 (improperly cooked eel + recurrent facial swelling) · BM28 SUM1 Q35 (intermittent migratory swelling)</span>","o":{"C":"Paragonimus spp.","E":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","D":"Trichinella spiralis","B":"Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Gnathostoma spinigerum"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Paragonimus goes to the lung.","E":"Angiostrongylus also comes from undercooked food but goes to the CNS — eosinophilic meningitis, not skin swellings.","D":"Trichinella causes FIXED periorbital oedema and myalgia after raw pork or wild boar; it does not migrate from place to place. That fixed-versus-migratory contrast is the point of the item.","B":"Opisthorchis stays in the bile ducts.","A":"Correct. 'Migratory swelling that comes and goes' = cutaneous gnathostomiasis; the third-stage larva wanders through subcutaneous tissue. Eel, snakehead fish, frog and chicken are the classic sources."},"img":"slides_i/L2_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q35"]},{"id":"I|L2|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q9","t":"Angiostrongylus — CSF","s":"A 30-year-old man has severe headache and neck stiffness. He ate Pila spp. (apple snail). CSF analysis shows a raised EOSINOPHIL count. Which parasite is responsible?","lec":"L2","sl":18,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — CSF</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q9 — Pila spp. snail + severe headache + neck stiffness + eosinophils in CSF</span>","o":{"D":"Capillaria philippinensis","E":"Trichinella spiralis","C":"Enterobius vermicularis","B":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis","A":"Gnathostoma spinigerum"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Capillaria is an intestinal worm causing chronic watery diarrhoea.","E":"Trichinella affects striated muscle, not meninges.","C":"Enterobius stays in the large bowel.","B":"Correct. Eosinophilic meningitis + apple snail is Angiostrongylus, the rat lungworm. Humans are an ACCIDENTAL host, so the immature worm dies in the CNS and no eggs or larvae are ever found in stool — and larvae are only rarely seen in CSF.","A":"Gnathostoma is the other cause of eosinophilic meningitis — but its history is eel or raw fish, and it usually gives migratory swellings or radiculomyelitis rather than this snail history. Both are acceptable neurological causes; the SNAIL decides it."},"img":"slides_i/L2_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L2|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q47","t":"Trichinella — source","s":"A 62-year-old man has fever and muscle pain. A MUSCLE BIOPSY shows a coiled larva inside a nurse cell. Where did he acquire it?","lec":"L2","sl":26,"c":"<b>Trichinella — source</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q47 — 62-year-old with fever and muscle pain; muscle biopsy shown; what is the source?</span>","o":{"E":"Eating poorly cooked pork or wild boar","D":"Eating unwashed vegetables","B":"Smoking","A":"Walking barefoot on contaminated soil","C":"Drinking untreated water"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. A larva encysted in striated muscle is Trichinella spiralis, and the infective stage is the ENCYSTED LARVA in raw or undercooked pork, wild boar or other game meat.","D":"Vegetables carry Ascaris and Trichuris eggs — intestinal, never in muscle.","B":"Smoking is a distractor with no parasitic route.","A":"Skin penetration is hookworm and Strongyloides.","C":"Waterborne routes give protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba."},"img":"slides_i/L2_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM31 Q21"]},{"id":"I|L2|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"B. malayi microfilaria → vectors","s":"A night blood film shows a sheathed microfilaria about 200 × 5 µm, kinked into tight coils, with a long empty head space, a crowded nuclear column and two isolated nuclei at the tail tip. Which mosquito genera does the lecture list as vectors of this species?","lec":"L2","sl":36,"c":"<b>B. malayi microfilaria → vectors</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Anopheles and Aedes","B":"Mansonia and Culex","C":"Culex and Aedes","D":"Mansonia and Anopheles","E":"Culex, Mansonia, Anopheles and Aedes"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Aedes belongs to the W. bancrofti list.","B":"Culex is a W. bancrofti vector, not a B. malayi one.","C":"Culex and Aedes appear only in the W. bancrofti list.","D":"Correct. 175–230 × 5–6 µm, tightly coiled, long head space, packed nuclei, two terminal nuclei = Brugia malayi. The learning-guidance table lists its intermediate hosts as Mansonia spp. and Anopheles spp. (BM33 added Anopheles).","E":"This is the full W. bancrofti list (and slide 37's combined filarial list) — the species-specific table on slide 43 narrows B. malayi to two genera."},"img":"slides_i/L2_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A night blood film from a patient in Narathiwat shows a sheathed microfilaria, tightly coiled, with a long empty head space, a nuclear column so densely packed that individual nuclei cannot be resolved, and TWO isolated nuclei at the very tip of the tail. Which species is it, and what does that predict about the limb involvement?","o":{"D":"Brugia malayi — disease below the knee and below the elbow, with the genitalia typically spared","C":"Wuchereria bancrofti — disease in the lower limbs, arm, breast and genitalia","E":"Brugia malayi — genital elephantiasis is the hallmark","A":"Wuchereria bancrofti — the two terminal nuclei are its defining feature","B":"Neither: terminal nuclei mean the microfilaria is unsheathed and therefore not a lymphatic filaria"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Two isolated TERMINAL nuclei + tightly coiled + long head space = B. malayi, and the deck says Brugian filariasis spares the genitals and affects leg below knee / arm below elbow.","C":"W. bancrofti has NO terminal nuclei, a SMALL head space, and separated nuclei that can be counted individually. The clinical half describes bancroftian disease correctly, which is the bait.","E":"Species right, clinics wrong — genital involvement is rare in Brugian filariasis. This is the commonest way to half-answer this item.","A":"Straight inversion of the tail feature. W. bancrofti's tail 'exhibits no terminal nuclei'.","B":"Both species are SHEATHED; terminal nuclei and sheathing are independent features."},"t":"Microfilaria discriminator","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L2_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Microfilaria discriminator</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Two eosinophilic-CNS worms — definitive hosts","s":"Two patients have eosinophils in the CSF. Patient 1, from Udon Thani, developed severe headache and neck stiffness after a raw snail salad. Patient 2 has had intermittent migratory swellings for months and now has radiculomyelitis with a subarachnoid haemorrhage. Which pairing gives the definitive host of each patient's parasite?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"<b>Two eosinophilic-CNS worms — definitive hosts</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Patient 1 — rodents; patient 2 — pigs and wild boar","B":"Patient 1 — cats and dogs; patient 2 — rodents","C":"Patient 1 — rodents; patient 2 — humans","D":"Patient 1 — humans; patient 2 — cats and dogs","E":"Patient 1 — rodents; patient 2 — cats and dogs"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Pigs and wild boar are definitive hosts of Trichinella, which causes myalgia and periorbital oedema, not migratory swelling with CNS haemorrhage.","B":"Swapped. The 'rat lungworm' is Angiostrongylus; the cat/dog stomach-wall worm is Gnathostoma.","C":"Humans are an accidental host of Gnathostoma, which is why the larva keeps wandering.","D":"Humans are accidental hosts of Angiostrongylus — the larva never matures in them.","E":"Correct. Patient 1 = Angiostrongylus cantonensis (snail, eosinophilic meningitis): definitive host rodents, adults in the pulmonary arteries. Patient 2 = Gnathostoma spinigerum (migratory swelling, then haemorrhagic neurological disease): definitive hosts cats, dogs and wild felines. Humans are accidental hosts of both."},"img":"slides_i/L2_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Two patients both have eosinophils in the CSF. Patient 1 ate raw snails and has severe headache with neck stiffness. Patient 2 ate raw eel, has had intermittent migratory swellings for months, and now has radiculomyelitis with a subarachnoid haemorrhage. Which pairing is correct?","o":{"E":"Patient 1 Angiostrongylus cantonensis, patient 2 Gnathostoma spinigerum","C":"Patient 1 Gnathostoma spinigerum, patient 2 Angiostrongylus cantonensis","D":"Both are Angiostrongylus cantonensis; migratory swelling is an early phase of the same disease","A":"Both are Gnathostoma spinigerum; snails are its second intermediate host","B":"Patient 1 Angiostrongylus cantonensis, patient 2 Trichinella spiralis"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Snail + eosinophilic meningitis = rat lungworm. Migratory swelling + haemorrhagic CNS disease = Gnathostoma, which is the one that bleeds.","C":"The exposures are swapped. Snails are Angiostrongylus's intermediate host; eels are Gnathostoma's second intermediate host.","D":"Intermittent migratory swelling is a GNATHOSTOMA sign and appears nowhere under Angiostrongylus.","A":"Gnathostoma's second intermediate hosts are freshwater fish, eels and frogs — not snails.","B":"Trichinella causes myalgia and periorbital oedema, not CSF eosinophilia with subarachnoid haemorrhage."},"t":"Eosinophilic CNS disease — which worm","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L2_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Eosinophilic CNS disease — which worm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Gnathostoma — food-handler route","s":"A restaurant cook who never eats raw food has had, for months, an itchy swelling that moves along her forearm, vanishes, then reappears near the elbow. She fillets snakehead fish and eels every day, bare-handed. Her blood eosinophils are 18%. Which mode of infection best explains her illness?","lec":"L2","sl":7,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — food-handler route</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Skin penetration by cercariae released from the eels into water","B":"Penetration of a mosquito bite wound by third-stage larvae","C":"Ingestion of eggs carried from her hands to her mouth","D":"Skin penetration by third-stage larvae from the fish she prepares","E":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae from soil in the kitchen yard"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Cercarial skin penetration from water is Schistosoma (L4); eels carry Gnathostoma L3, not cercariae.","B":"L3 entering a mosquito bite wound is the filarial route.","C":"Humans never meet a Gnathostoma egg — eggs are passed by cats and dogs and hatch in water; the human is infected only by L3.","D":"Correct. Migratory swelling + eosinophilia + contact with fish/eels = Gnathostoma. Slide 7 lists three modes of infection: ingestion of raw second intermediate/paratenic hosts, skin penetration in a food handler by third-stage larvae from the infected meal, and drinking water containing L3 in Cyclops.","E":"Filariform larvae from soil penetrating skin is hookworm / Strongyloides (L1)."},"img":"slides_i/L2_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A restaurant cook who says she never eats raw food develops intermittent migratory swelling of the forearm. Which statement explains how she could have acquired Gnathostoma spinigerum?","o":{"D":"Third-stage larvae from the infected meal she was preparing penetrated her skin directly while she handled it","E":"She must have eaten the food; skin penetration is not a recognised route for this worm","B":"Filariform larvae in soil penetrated her feet, as in hookworm","A":"Eggs on her hands were swallowed after handling raw fish","C":"A mosquito inoculated third-stage larvae while she worked"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The deck lists THREE routes: ingestion of the raw second intermediate or paratenic host, SKIN PENETRATION in a food handler from the infected meal, and drinking water containing third-stage larvae in Cyclops.","E":"This is the answer of someone who memorised only 'food-borne'. The food-handler route is explicitly on the slide.","B":"Filariform larvae in soil is hookworm/Strongyloides. Gnathostoma's infective stage is the THIRD-STAGE larva in an animal host.","A":"Gnathostoma is never acquired from eggs — humans are an accidental host and no egg stage reaches us.","C":"Mosquito inoculation is the FILARIAL route (W. bancrofti, B. malayi), not Gnathostoma."},"t":"Gnathostoma — three modes of infection","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L2_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Gnathostoma — three modes of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Angiostrongylus — CSF without larvae","s":"A 30-year-old man from Khon Kaen develops severe headache and neck stiffness two weeks after a party where raw apple snails were served. Lumbar puncture shows raised opening pressure and many eosinophils, but no larvae are seen despite careful examination. Which interpretation is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":19,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — CSF without larvae</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The result means the larvae have matured, so eggs should be sought in stool","B":"The result means the larvae have encysted, so a muscle biopsy is required","C":"The result points to a fungus, because parasitic meningitis lacks eosinophils","D":"The result excludes the diagnosis, because the diagnostic stage is larvae in CSF","E":"The result supports the diagnosis, because larvae are rarely detected in CSF"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The larva never matures in a human (accidental host), so there are never eggs in stool.","B":"Encysted larvae in muscle belong to Trichinella.","C":"Eosinophilic pleocytosis is precisely the hallmark of this parasite (slide 20).","D":"This is the over-literal reading of 'diagnostic stage = L3 in CSF'; absence of larvae does not exclude it.","E":"Correct. Raw Pila snails + meningism + CSF eosinophilia = Angiostrongylus. The diagnostic stage is formally L3 in CSF, but slide 19 warns 'Larvae are rarely detected in the CSF', so the diagnosis rests on history, CSF eosinophil count, blood eosinophilia, immunoblot/ELISA and PCR."},"img":"slides_i/L2_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why does the lecture group these worms as ones that 'cannot be diagnosed by stool examination'?","o":{"E":"In Gnathostoma and Angiostrongylus humans are an ACCIDENTAL host, so the third-stage larva never matures into an egg-laying adult; in Trichinella and the filariae the diagnostic stage sits in muscle or blood, not in the gut lumen","C":"Because their eggs are too small to be seen by routine concentration techniques","B":"Because all four are transmitted by vectors, and vector-borne parasites never appear in stool","D":"Because all of them live exclusively in the lymphatic system","A":"Because their adults live in the small intestine but release larvae only into the bloodstream"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and the two halves have different reasons — accidental-host biology for the first two, anatomical location of the diagnostic stage for the others.","C":"Size is irrelevant; there are no eggs to find. This is the trap for anyone who assumes a technique problem.","B":"Only the filariae are vector-borne. Gnathostoma, Angiostrongylus and Trichinella are all food-borne.","D":"Only W. bancrofti and B. malayi are lymphatic. Trichinella encysts in striated muscle; Angiostrongylus larvae end up in CSF.","A":"Trichinella's adult IS in the small intestine, but the point is that its diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in muscle — the option generalises a half-truth to all four."},"t":"Why stool is useless","sl":2,"img":"slides_i/L2_2.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Why stool is useless</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Angiostrongylus — management","s":"A 33-year-old man from Surin has had a pounding headache, neck stiffness and blurred vision for five days, beginning two weeks after he ate raw golden apple snails. CSF: opening pressure raised, 40% eosinophils. The intern wants to start a worm-killing drug immediately. Which plan follows the lecture?","lec":"L2","sl":21,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — management</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Albendazole for two weeks, to kill the larvae before they reach the brain","B":"Diethylcarbamazine, with supportive care and repeated lumbar puncture","C":"Albendazole or mebendazole combined with a course of prednisolone","D":"Supportive care, lumbar puncture to relieve pressure, and corticosteroids","E":"Surgical removal of the worm, followed by albendazole or ivermectin"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The reflex answer the lecture explicitly rejects — and the larvae are already in the CNS.","B":"DEC is the filariasis drug.","C":"Albendazole/mebendazole + prednisolone is the Trichinella regimen (slide 30).","D":"Correct. Snails + meningism + CSF eosinophils (+ eye complications) = Angiostrongylus. Slide 21: self-limited illness; supportive treatment; lumbar puncture to relieve intracranial pressure; corticosteroids to reduce inflammation; 'antihelminthic drugs are usually not used' (dying larvae in the CNS worsen the inflammation).","E":"Surgical removal + albendazole/ivermectin is Gnathostoma's treatment list (slide 10)."},"img":"slides_i/L2_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A man with confirmed eosinophilic meningitis from Angiostrongylus cantonensis has severe headache. Which management plan matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"Supportive care, repeated lumbar puncture to relieve intracranial pressure and corticosteroids; anthelmintics are usually not given","E":"High-dose albendazole immediately, to kill the larvae before they burrow further","B":"Ivermectin plus surgical removal of the worm from the CSF","C":"Diethylcarbamazine, as for the other tissue nematodes","A":"No treatment of any kind, because the illness is self-limited"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the reasoning matters: killing larvae inside the CNS worsens the inflammatory response, so the deck steers to pressure relief and steroids.","E":"This is the reflex answer and the deck explicitly contradicts it — 'antihelminthic drugs are usually not used'.","B":"Surgical removal is mentioned for GNATHOSTOMA, and even there it is noted as difficult. Larvae are rarely even detected in CSF.","C":"DEC is the filariasis drug.","A":"The illness is self-limited, but the deck still lists active supportive measures — LP and corticosteroids. 'No treatment of any kind' overshoots."},"t":"Angiostrongylus — treatment trap","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L2_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — treatment trap</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Trichinella — expected laboratory profile","s":"Two weeks after a village feast of raw wild boar in Nan, a 40-year-old man has fever, swollen eyelids and painful, firm calf muscles. Which set of laboratory results would the lecture lead you to expect?","lec":"L2","sl":29,"c":"<b>Trichinella — expected laboratory profile</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Blood eosinophils 30–80%, with raised CK, LDH and AST","B":"Eggs on stool concentration, with raised ALT and bilirubin","C":"Microfilariae on a night film, with a raised blood eosinophil count","D":"Blood eosinophils below 5%, with raised CK, LDH and AST","E":"CSF eosinophils raised, with normal CK and a normal muscle biopsy"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Raw boar + periorbital oedema + myalgia/pseudohypertrophy = Trichinella. Slide 29 lists eosinophilia in blood 30–80% and raised muscle enzymes LDH, CK (CPK) and AST (GOT), alongside muscle biopsy and serology.","B":"Stool eggs with a cholestatic picture suggests a liver fluke (Opisthorchis, L4); Trichinella produces nothing in stool.","C":"Microfilariae belong to lymphatic filariasis.","D":"The enzymes are right, but trichinellosis gives marked eosinophilia, not a normal count.","E":"CSF eosinophilia is the Angiostrongylus / Gnathostoma finding; in trichinellosis the muscle is the tissue involved."},"img":"slides_i/L2_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A man from the North of Thailand who ate raw boar two weeks ago now has fever, periorbital oedema and severe myalgia. Which set of investigations would confirm the diagnosis?","o":{"A":"Muscle biopsy showing encysted larvae, ELISA/immunoblot serology, eosinophilia of 30–80%, and raised LDH, CK and AST","D":"Stool examination for encysted larvae, plus eosinophilia","E":"CSF analysis for eosinophils and larvae, plus raised CK","C":"Thick and thin blood smears for larvae, plus serology","B":"Colonoscopy with biopsy of the small-bowel mucosa, where the adult worms live"},"r":{"A":"Correct — all four elements are on the slide, and the muscle enzymes are the detail most people omit because the myalgia feels self-explanatory.","D":"Encysted larvae are in MUSCLE, not stool. Trichinella cannot be diagnosed from stool at all.","E":"CSF eosinophilia belongs to Angiostrongylus and Gnathostoma.","C":"Blood smears for larvae is the FILARIASIS method; Trichinella larvae are not in peripheral blood.","B":"The adults are in small-intestinal mucosa, but the deck's diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in striated muscle — biopsying the gut is not the listed method."},"t":"Trichinella — laboratory panel","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L2_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Trichinella — laboratory panel</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Gnathostoma — four rows of hooklets","s":"A worm is removed from a migrating subcutaneous swelling in a man who eats undercooked eel. Under the microscope it has a cephalic bulb bearing four rows of hooklets. Which interpretation is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":5,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — four rows of hooklets</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is a young adult, since the third-stage larva carries eight rows","B":"It is a third-stage larva, the furthest stage this worm reaches in a human","C":"It is an adult, whose eggs could be found in this patient's stool","D":"It is an adult female, since only females carry a cephalic bulb","E":"It is a third-stage larva, which would have become an adult in his stomach wall"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Inverted counts: adult = 8 rows, L3 = 4 rows.","B":"Correct. Eel + migratory swelling = Gnathostoma. Four rows of cephalic hooklets = third-stage larva; eight rows = adult. Humans are accidental hosts, so the L3 cannot develop into an adult — it wanders instead, which is why the swelling migrates.","C":"Four rows is the larva, and in a human no adult (and no egg) ever forms.","D":"Both the adult and the L3 carry a cephalic bulb; the row count, not the bulb, separates them.","E":"The stomach-wall tumour is the adult's habitat in cats, dogs and wild felines, not in humans."},"img":"slides_i/L2_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A worm is teased out of a migrating skin swelling and shows a cephalic bulb bearing FOUR rows of hooklets. What is it, and why is that consistent with the host?","o":{"B":"A third-stage larva — humans are an accidental host, so the parasite cannot develop past L3 into the 8-row adult","E":"An adult worm — the adult carries 4 rows and the larva 8","D":"A third-stage larva that will mature into an adult in the human stomach wall within weeks","C":"An adult female, identified by the cephalic bulb, which only adults possess","A":"An encysted larva, as in Trichinella"},"r":{"B":"Correct on both halves. FOUR rows = L3; EIGHT rows = adult; and the adult only forms in cats, dogs and wild felines, where it lives in a tumour in the stomach wall.","E":"The counts are inverted — this is the whole discriminator, so inverting it is the intended miss.","D":"Humans are accidental hosts. The larva wanders but never matures, which is why stool is useless and why the swellings keep migrating.","C":"BOTH the adult and the L3 have a cephalic bulb; only the hooklet row count separates them.","A":"Encystment in muscle is Trichinella; Gnathostoma larvae migrate rather than encyst."},"t":"Gnathostoma — hooklet rows","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L2_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Gnathostoma — hooklet rows</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Gnathostoma — control vs prevention","s":"After a cluster of patients with intermittent migratory swellings in a district where snakehead fish, eels and frogs are eaten half-cooked, the provincial office asks for a control measure — one aimed at the parasite's source rather than at protecting individual people. Which measure fits the lecture?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"<b>Gnathostoma — control vs prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Closed-system livestock farming","B":"Wearing gloves while preparing fish","C":"Treating infected cats and dogs","D":"Drinking sterile water in the district","E":"Eradicating rats, snails and slugs"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Closed-system livestock farming is the Trichinella control measure.","B":"Gloves when cooking is on the same slide, but under PREVENTION (protects the individual food handler).","C":"Correct. Migratory swelling after fish/eel/frog = Gnathostoma. Slide 11 lists one control measure — treatment of infected cats and dogs (the definitive hosts that shed the eggs).","D":"Drinking sterile water is also Gnathostoma PREVENTION (avoids L3 in Cyclops).","E":"Eradicating rats, snails and slugs is the Angiostrongylus control measure."},"img":"slides_i/L2_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the mosquito vectors of lymphatic filariasis is correct?","o":{"C":"Wuchereria bancrofti is carried by Culex, Mansonia, Anopheles and Aedes, whereas Brugia malayi is carried by Mansonia and Anopheles","D":"Both species are carried by the same four genera, which is why they overlap geographically","B":"Brugia malayi is the broader of the two, adding Culex and Aedes to Mansonia and Anopheles","E":"Both are carried only by Anopheles, which is also the malaria vector","A":"Aedes carries Brugia malayi; Culex carries Wuchereria bancrofti; the other genera are not vectors"},"r":{"C":"Correct — the wider vector range is part of why bancroftian filariasis is pan-tropical while Brugian filariasis is confined to South and East Asia.","D":"The deck's table gives DIFFERENT lists for the two species; the difference is the examinable point.","B":"The breadth is inverted. W. bancrofti has the longer list.","E":"Anopheles carries both, but it is not the only vector of either.","A":"Aedes is listed for W. bancrofti, not B. malayi, and cutting the lists to one genus each discards Mansonia, which matters for Brugia."},"t":"Filarial vectors","sl":37,"img":"slides_i/L2_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Filarial vectors</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Lymphatic filariasis — drug","s":"A woman from Pattani has recurrent lymphangitis and swelling of the leg below the knee. A night blood film shows sheathed, tightly coiled microfilariae about 190 × 5 µm with two terminal nuclei. Which drug does the lecture list for this infection?","lec":"L2","sl":41,"c":"<b>Lymphatic filariasis — drug</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Diethylcarbamazine","C":"Ivermectin","D":"Prednisolone","E":"Albendazole"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Praziquantel is the trematode / cestode drug (L3, L4, L9).","B":"Correct. Southern Thailand + below-knee lymphoedema + two terminal nuclei = Brugia malayi. The lecture's treatment slide for lymphatic filariasis lists diethylcarbamazine (DEC) plus supportive treatment — no other drug.","C":"Ivermectin is on Gnathostoma's treatment slide, not the filariasis one.","D":"Prednisolone accompanies albendazole/mebendazole in trichinellosis.","E":"Albendazole is listed for Gnathostoma and (with mebendazole) Trichinella in this lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L2_41.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which set of host roles for Angiostrongylus cantonensis is correct?","o":{"B":"Rodents definitive (adults in the pulmonary arteries); Pila spp. and Achatina fulica intermediate; freshwater shrimp, crabs, frogs and monitors paratenic; humans accidental","D":"Humans definitive; rodents accidental; snails intermediate; crabs paratenic","C":"Rodents definitive; freshwater shrimp intermediate; snails paratenic; humans accidental","E":"Rodents definitive (adults in the stomach wall); Cyclops intermediate; snails paratenic","A":"Snails definitive; rodents intermediate; humans accidental"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the common name 'rat LUNG worm' encodes the habitat you are asked for.","D":"Humans never support the adult — that is precisely why the larva ends up lost in the CNS.","C":"Snail and shrimp roles are swapped. The larva DEVELOPS in the snail (intermediate); the shrimp merely carries it unchanged (paratenic).","E":"Stomach-wall habitat and Cyclops belong to GNATHOSTOMA. This option imports that worm's life cycle wholesale.","A":"A snail cannot be the definitive host; no sexual reproduction occurs there."},"t":"Angiostrongylus — host roles","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — host roles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Lymphatic filariasis — control programme","s":"A district in Chumphon with many cases of below-the-knee lymphoedema wants to cut transmission across the whole community rather than protect individual residents. Which pair of measures does the lecture give as control for this disease?","lec":"L2","sl":42,"c":"<b>Lymphatic filariasis — control programme</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mass treatment, and reducing the numbers of mosquito larvae and pupae","B":"Closed-system livestock farming, and thorough cooking of meat","C":"Bed nets and repellents, and treating infected cats and dogs","D":"Building latrines, and wearing shoes when working in the fields","E":"Mass treatment, and eradicating the rats, snails and slugs nearby"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Chumphon + below-knee lymphoedema = Brugian filariasis. Slide 42: prevention = prevent mosquito bites; control = mass treatment and vector control that reduces mosquito larvae and pupae (the aquatic stages).","B":"Closed-system farming and cooking belong to Trichinella.","C":"Bed nets/repellents are individual prevention against bites; treating cats and dogs is Gnathostoma control.","D":"Latrines and shoes are soil-transmitted helminth measures (L1).","E":"Mass treatment fits, but rats, snails and slugs are the Angiostrongylus control targets."},"img":"slides_i/L2_42.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about Trichinella spiralis in humans is correct?","o":{"A":"Humans are a definitive host — adults live in small-intestinal mucosa — yet the diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in striated muscle, not anything in stool","D":"Humans are an accidental host, like in gnathostomiasis, so the larva never matures","E":"Humans are an intermediate host and pigs are the definitive host","B":"The adult lives in striated muscle and releases larvae into the gut","C":"Both the infective and the diagnostic stage are the encysted larva in pork"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the subtlety of this worm: being a definitive host does NOT mean stool is diagnostic, because it is the larva you detect and it has left the gut.","D":"The learning-guidance slide lists humans among the definitive hosts alongside pigs, boar, bears, wild felines and foxes.","E":"Inverted, and it collides with the slide's list.","B":"The reverse: adults in the intestinal mucosa, larvae encysted in muscle.","C":"Infective stage = encysted larvae in PORK; diagnostic stage = encysted larvae in HUMAN striated muscle. Same stage, different host — and the exam asks them separately."},"t":"Trichinella — who is the definitive host","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L2_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Trichinella — who is the definitive host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Angiostrongylus — adult habitat","s":"A 25-year-old man from Udon Thani has severe headache, neck stiffness and a seizure after eating raw snails at a drinking party; his CSF contains many eosinophils. In the natural life cycle of this parasite, where do the adult worms live?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"<b>Angiostrongylus — adult habitat</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In the subarachnoid space of humans","B":"In the stomach wall of cats and dogs","C":"In the pulmonary arteries of rats","D":"In the intestinal mucosa of pigs","E":"In the lymphatic vessels of humans"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Larvae reach the human CNS but never mature there — humans are accidental hosts, so there is no adult habitat in people.","B":"The stomach wall of cats and dogs is Gnathostoma's habitat.","C":"Correct. Snails + eosinophilic meningitis = Angiostrongylus cantonensis, the 'rat lung worm': adults live in the pulmonary arteries of rats (definitive host), and first-stage larvae leave in rat faeces to infect snails.","D":"The small-intestinal mucosa is Trichinella's habitat (pigs, boar, humans).","E":"Human lymphatics house adult W. bancrofti and B. malayi."},"img":"slides_i/L2_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A district is running a lymphatic filariasis programme. Which combination matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Diethylcarbamazine with supportive treatment for cases; prevention by avoiding mosquito bites; control by mass treatment and vector control aimed at larvae and pupae","D":"Albendazole alone for cases; control by eradicating rats and snails","B":"Ivermectin for cases; prevention by wearing shoes","E":"Praziquantel for cases; control by sanitary disposal of faeces","A":"Corticosteroids for cases; control by closed-system livestock farming"},"r":{"C":"Correct on all three limbs, and note the vector control targets the AQUATIC stages, not the adult mosquito.","D":"Eradicating rats and snails is Angiostrongylus control.","B":"Wearing shoes blocks soil-penetrating larvae — hookworm and Strongyloides, from L1.","E":"Praziquantel and sanitary disposal belong to the fluke and soil-transmitted lectures.","A":"Closed-system livestock farming is the TRICHINELLA control measure."},"t":"Filariasis — treatment and control","sl":41,"img":"slides_i/L2_41.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Filariasis — treatment and control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Trichinella — human host status","s":"A hunter from Phrae shared raw wild-boar meat with his family. Two weeks later he has periorbital oedema and aching, swollen muscles; eosinophils are 45% and CK is raised. Which statement about the human's role in this parasite's life cycle is correct?","lec":"L2","sl":32,"c":"<b>Trichinella — human host status</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Paratenic host, carrying the larva unchanged from the meat eaten","B":"Accidental host, in which the larva does not develop into an adult","C":"Definitive host, with adults living in the small-intestinal mucosa","D":"Intermediate host, with the adults found in pigs and wild boar","E":"Definitive host, with adults living in the striated muscle"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The larva does not stay unchanged — it becomes an adult in the human gut, whose offspring encyst in muscle.","B":"Accidental host describes Gnathostoma and Angiostrongylus, not Trichinella.","C":"Correct. Raw boar + periorbital oedema + myalgia + eosinophilia + raised CK = Trichinella spiralis. The learning-guidance slide lists humans among the definitive hosts (with pigs, boar, bears, wild felines, foxes); the habitat is the mucosa of the small intestine — yet the diagnostic stage is the encysted larva in striated muscle, so stool still does not help.","D":"Humans are definitive hosts; pigs and boar are also definitive hosts (and the source of infective meat).","E":"Striated muscle holds the encysted LARVAE; the adults are in the intestinal mucosa."},"img":"slides_i/L2_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which Thai geographical pairing matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Brugia malayi from Chumphon down to Narathiwat; Wuchereria bancrofti in the border provinces such as Tak, Kanchanaburi, Ratchaburi and Ranong","D":"Wuchereria bancrofti from Chumphon down to Narathiwat; Brugia malayi in the border provinces","E":"Trichinella spiralis is concentrated in the North-east and Angiostrongylus in the North","A":"Angiostrongylus cantonensis does not occur in Thailand","B":"Gnathostoma spinigerum is restricted to Africa"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The southern peninsula belt is Brugian; the western border provinces are Bancroftian, tied to migrant populations.","D":"Straight swap of the two distributions.","E":"Inverted: Trichinella is the NORTH, Angiostrongylus the NORTH-EAST.","A":"The deck names Thailand's North-east region specifically for Angiostrongylus.","B":"Gnathostoma is Southeast Asia, South and Central America, and some parts of Africa — Southeast Asia first."},"t":"Geography as a clue","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L2_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Geography as a clue</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L2|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Trichinella — treatment","s":"A 38-year-old man from Chiang Mai has fever, facial oedema, myalgia and 50% eosinophils two weeks after eating home-made fermented raw pork sausage (naem) at a festival. Which treatment does the lecture list for his infection?","lec":"L2","sl":30,"c":"<b>Trichinella — treatment</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Albendazole or mebendazole, with prednisolone","B":"Lumbar puncture and steroids, with no anthelmintic","C":"Praziquantel, with prednisolone","D":"Ivermectin, with surgical removal of the cysts","E":"Diethylcarbamazine, with supportive care"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Raw pork + facial oedema + myalgia + marked eosinophilia = Trichinella. Slide 30: albendazole, mebendazole; prednisolone (to damp the inflammatory reaction to larvae in muscle).","B":"Supportive care, LP and steroids without anthelmintics is the Angiostrongylus plan.","C":"Praziquantel is for flukes and tapeworms, not nematodes.","D":"Ivermectin (with surgical removal of the worm) is on Gnathostoma's slide; encysted muscle larvae are not excised.","E":"DEC is the lymphatic-filariasis drug."},"img":"slides_i/L2_30.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about trichinellosis management and control is correct?","o":{"A":"Albendazole or mebendazole is given together with prednisolone, and control rests on proper cooking plus closed-system livestock farming","E":"Albendazole is given alone; steroids are contraindicated because they cause hyperinfection","D":"No drug is used because the larvae are already encysted; only analgesia is given","C":"Ivermectin plus surgical excision of encysted larvae","B":"Diethylcarbamazine, because the larvae are tissue-dwelling"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The steroid is there to damp the inflammatory reaction to dying larvae in muscle.","E":"Corticosteroid-driven hyperinfection is the STRONGYLOIDES warning from L1. In trichinellosis prednisolone is part of the regimen.","D":"The deck lists active drug treatment, so 'no drug' is wrong.","C":"Excision of encysted muscle larvae is not a listed treatment.","B":"DEC belongs to filariasis."},"t":"Trichinella — control","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L2_30.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Trichinella — control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233904954},{"id":"I|L3|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Cysticercosis — the stage she swallowed","s":"A 32-year-old lifelong vegetarian has new-onset seizures, and MRI shows several small cystic brain lesions, each containing a scolex. Her family's cook, who eats raw pork larb, recently passed white segments; an Indian-ink injection of one showed uterine branches <15. Which stage did she most likely ingest?","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis — the stage she swallowed</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Embryonated egg of Taenia saginata","B":"Cysticercus cellulosae of Taenia solium","C":"Embryonated egg of Taenia solium","D":"Cysticercoid larva of Hymenolepis nana","E":"Cysticercus bovis of Taenia saginata"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The right stage from the wrong species. The host table (slides 16 and 20) prints a dash for T. saginata in the cysticercosis row: its eggs do not develop in humans. Uterine branches <15 also makes the cook's worm T. solium, not T. saginata (>15).","B":"The lecturer's own trap (BM30 formative feedback). Cysticercus cellulosae in pork is the infective stage of TAENIASIS solium — it grows into an adult worm in the gut, not cysts in the brain. And a vegetarian never ate it.","C":"Correct. Brain cysts containing a scolex = cysticercosis, and slide 12 is explicit: cysticercosis comes from ingestion of T. solium EGGS. The cook's segment (uterine branches <15) is T. solium, so his eggs on her food are the source. She never needed to eat pork.","D":"The cysticercoid is the Hymenolepis larva in beetles/fleas; it gives an intestinal adult, not brain cysts.","E":"Cysticercus bovis in beef gives taeniasis saginata (an adult in the gut). It never causes human cysticercosis."},"img":"slides_i/L3_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient has a painless cystic lesion removed surgically; inside is a larva with a scolex and hooks. What was the mode of infection?","o":{"A":"Ingestion of raw pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae","C":"Ingestion of fruit or vegetables contaminated with Taenia solium EGGS","D":"Ingestion of raw beef containing Cysticercus bovis","B":"Ingestion of undercooked fish containing plerocercoid larvae","E":"Skin penetration by filariform larvae"},"r":{"A":"The designed trap, and the single commonest error in the whole lecture. Raw pork containing a cysticercus gives you an ADULT WORM in the gut — taeniasis, not cysticercosis. Same worm, opposite end of the cycle.","C":"Correct. Cysticercosis is what happens when a human swallows the EGG and becomes an accidental intermediate host — the larva then encysts in tissue.","D":"T. saginata gives taeniasis only. Humans are not a permissive intermediate host for it, and the learning-guidance table prints a dash in that cell.","B":"Plerocercoid in fish is Diphyllobothrium latum, which gives an adult worm.","E":"No cestode enters through skin."},"t":"Taeniasis vs cysticercosis","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L3_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Taeniasis vs cysticercosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Taeniasis solium — mode of infection","s":"A 28-year-old man from Nan, who eats both raw beef and raw pork dishes at village festivals, notices flat white segments in his stool. One segment injected with Indian ink shows uterine branches <15, and the stool also contains dark-brown radially striated eggs. What was his mode of infection?","lec":"L3","sl":9,"c":"<b>Taeniasis solium — mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of undercooked beef containing Cysticercus bovis","B":"Ingestion of vegetables contaminated with Taenia solium eggs","C":"Ingestion of food contaminated by insects carrying cysticercoids","D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae","E":"Ingestion of raw freshwater fish containing plerocercoid larvae"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Beef with Cysticercus bovis gives taeniasis saginata, whose gravid proglottid has uterine branches >15. The mixed diet is there so the branch count has to decide.","B":"The reverse of the classic trap. Swallowing T. solium eggs gives CYSTICERCOSIS (larvae in tissue), not segments and eggs in the stool.","C":"Insects carrying cysticercoids transmit Hymenolepis (both species), whose eggs are not radially striated.","D":"Correct. Two steps: uterine branches <15 = T. solium; segments and eggs in stool = an ADULT worm in the gut = taeniasis solium, whose infective stage is Cysticercus cellulosae in undercooked pork (slides 12, 16, 20).","E":"Plerocercoid in freshwater fish is Diphyllobothrium latum — operculated eggs and proglottids wider than long; no Indian-ink branch count applies."},"img":"slides_i/L3_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A gravid proglottid is injected with Indian ink and shows 20 uterine branches on one side of the main stem. Which species, and what is the safe way to remember it?","o":{"C":"Hymenolepis nana — the count applies to all cyclophyllideans","A":"Taenia solium — pork worms have more branches (>15)","B":"Taenia saginata — but only if the count is under 15","E":"Diphyllobothrium latum — its proglottids are wider than long","D":"Taenia saginata — cow is bigger than pig, so beef gets the bigger number (>15)"},"r":{"C":"The count is a Taenia discrimination only.","A":"Reversed. T. solium has FEWER than 15.","B":"The threshold direction is wrong.","E":"D. latum's proglottids are indeed wider than long, but the branch count is not how it is identified, and it is not the organism described.","D":"Correct. >15 = T. saginata, <15 = T. solium. Everything about saginata is 'more' — longer worm, more proglottids, more branches."},"t":"Species identification","sl":18,"img":"slides_i/L3_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Species identification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Hymenolepis diminuta — mode of infection","s":"A 2-year-old living in a rat-infested shophouse, with cockroaches in the rice store, has mild diarrhoea. Stool shows spherical, yellowish-brown eggs about 65 µm across, each containing a hooked embryo but no polar filaments. How did this child most likely acquire the infection?","lec":"L3","sl":24,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis diminuta — mode of infection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Swallowing an insect that carried cysticercoid larvae","B":"Auto-infection by eggs hatching inside the child's own gut","C":"Swallowing undercooked pork that carried cysticerci","D":"Swallowing embryonated eggs from another child's hands","E":"Swallowing embryonated eggs in food soiled by rat faeces"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Spherical, yellowish-brown, 60–70 µm, no polar filaments = H. diminuta (slide 24). Its only mode of infection is ingestion of insects (beetles, fleas, cockroaches) carrying the cysticercoid — the human is an accidental host (slides 26–28).","B":"Auto-infection in the intestine belongs to H. nana only (slide 28).","C":"Cysticerci in pork are Taenia solium; the egg here is not a Taenia egg (those are 31–43 µm, dark brown, radially striated).","D":"Direct egg-to-mouth, person-to-person spread is H. NANA's route. H. diminuta's egg must first be eaten by an arthropod.","E":"The designed trap: the rat is the definitive host, so rat droppings do contain eggs — but the egg is not infective to a mammal until an insect has turned it into a cysticercoid."},"img":"slides_i/L3_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which larval stage goes with which host?","o":{"B":"All four stages occur in the same intermediate host","E":"Cysticercoid in mammal muscle; cysticercus in an insect; plerocercoid in Cyclops","D":"Cysticercus in Cyclops; plerocercoid in an insect; procercoid in fish","A":"Cysticercus in mammal muscle (Taenia); cysticercoid in an insect (Hymenolepis); procercoid in Cyclops and plerocercoid in fish (Diphyllobothrium)","C":"Sparganum in mammal muscle; cysticercus in fish; procercoid in an insect"},"r":{"B":"Each stage is host-specific, which is exactly what the options test.","E":"The first two are swapped and the third is wrong.","D":"All three are misassigned.","A":"Correct. Two memory hooks: the '-oid' in cysticercoid carries the I of Insect; and 'Cyclops gets the PRO, the fish gets the PLERO'.","C":"Sparganum is the plerocercoid of Spirometra in HUMAN tissue, not in mammal muscle as part of a normal cycle."},"t":"Larval stages","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L3_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Larval stages</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Hymenolepis nana — infective stages","s":"A 6-year-old has abdominal pain, diarrhoea and anal pruritus. Perianal tape tests are negative, but stool shows colourless oval eggs about 40 µm, each with a hooked embryo and fine filaments arising from both poles of the inner membrane. Which stages of this parasite are infective to humans?","lec":"L3","sl":24,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis nana — infective stages</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Unembryonated egg and cysticercoid larva","B":"Rhabditiform larva and filariform larva","C":"Embryonated egg and cysticercus larva","D":"Embryonated egg and cysticercoid larva","E":"Oncosphere and cysticercoid larva"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"All cyclophyllidean eggs, including Hymenolepis, are passed already embryonated. The unembryonated egg is Diphyllobothrium latum's.","B":"Rhabditiform/filariform larvae are Strongyloides (L1), which the anal-pruritus and auto-infection clues are meant to tempt you towards.","C":"Cysticercus is the Taenia larva (in cattle/pig muscle). Hymenolepis forms the smaller, solid cysticercoid.","D":"Correct. Colourless, 30–50 µm, polar filaments = H. nana; anal pruritus is listed for heavy H. nana infection (slide 29). The 2569 life-cycle slide states two infective stages: the embryonated egg and the cysticercoid (in beetles and fleas).","E":"The oncosphere (hexacanth embryo) is what hatches from the egg inside the gut; the stage the slide labels infective is the whole embryonated egg."},"img":"slides_i/L3_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which set of statements about the two Hymenolepis species is correct?","o":{"A":"Neither autoinfects; both spread only through insects","B":"H. diminuta autoinfects and spreads person-to-person; H. nana needs an intermediate host","C":"Both autoinfect, and both require an insect intermediate host","D":"H. nana autoinfects, spreads person-to-person and needs no intermediate host; H. diminuta does none of these","E":"H. nana requires an intermediate host and H. diminuta spreads person-to-person"},"r":{"A":"Nana autoinfects.","B":"Completely reversed.","C":"Only nana autoinfects, and only diminuta needs the insect.","D":"Correct, and every line is the opposite of what intuition suggests. The dwarf tapeworm has the smaller egg but the extra features — and the 2569 slide adds that H. nana has TWO infective stages: the embryonated egg AND the cysticercoid.","E":"Reversed."},"t":"Hymenolepis","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L3_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Hymenolepis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Taenia saginata — proglottid and scolex","s":"A man who eats both raw beef and raw pork, but has never eaten raw fish, is treated with a single dose of praziquantel; segments and the scolex are recovered. His physician notes that for this species the magnesium sulphate purge on the treatment slide was not needed. Which pair of findings did the laboratory report?","lec":"L3","sl":8,"c":"<b>Taenia saginata — proglottid and scolex</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Uterine branches >15 and a scolex without hooks","B":"Uterine branches >15 and a scolex with rostellar hooks","C":"Uterine branches <15 and a scolex with rostellar hooks","D":"A rosette uterus and a scolex with two sucking grooves","E":"Uterine branches <15 and a scolex without hooks"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. MgSO4 is added only in taeniasis SOLIUM (slide 19), so this worm is T. saginata: gravid proglottid with uterine branches >15 (write it exactly like that — 'more than 15' alone was marked zero) and a scolex with four suckers and no rostellum or hooks (slide 8).","B":">15 branches is right, but rostellar hooks belong to T. solium.","C":"Both findings are T. solium — the species for which MgSO4 IS required.","D":"Rosette uterus and two bothria are Diphyllobothrium latum (slides 32, 35), excluded by the diet and the lack of any fish exposure.","E":"Unarmed scolex is right for T. saginata, but <15 branches is T. solium."},"img":"slides_i/L3_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which egg description belongs to which cestode?","o":{"A":"Radial striation = Taenia spp.; 4–8 polar filaments = H. nana; operculum with a terminal knob at 58–76 µm = D. latum","B":"Radial striation = H. nana; polar filaments = Taenia; operculum = H. diminuta","E":"Polar filaments = Taenia; radial striation = D. latum; operculum = H. nana","C":"All cestode eggs are operculated","D":"Radial striation = D. latum; operculum = Taenia; polar filaments = H. diminuta"},"r":{"A":"Correct. And note what radial striation does NOT give you: it identifies the GENUS only. The species must come from the meal, a proglottid or PCR.","B":"All three are misassigned.","E":"All three are misassigned.","C":"D. latum is the ONLY operculated cestode egg. In L4 almost every fluke egg is operculated, which is where the confusion is engineered.","D":"All three are misassigned."},"t":"Egg morphology","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L3_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Egg morphology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Diphyllobothrium vs Opisthorchis egg","s":"A 45-year-old who ate raw freshwater fish during two years in Japan has vague abdominal pain. Stool shows an oval, thin-shelled egg about 65 × 45 µm with a lid at one pole and a small knob at the other. A trainee reports it as Opisthorchis viverrini. Apart from its size, which feature of this egg separates it from the O. viverrini egg?","lec":"L3","sl":36,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium vs Opisthorchis egg</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"An unembryonated interior","B":"A shell without radial striations","C":"An operculum at one pole","D":"A small abopercular knob","E":"An oval outline"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The egg is Diphyllobothrium latum (58–76 × 40–51 µm, Japan, raw freshwater fish), which is passed UNEMBRYONATED (slides 36, 38). The O. viverrini egg (L4) is about half the size and already embryonated. The lecturer warned that both eggs share an operculum and a knob.","B":"Neither egg has radial striations (that is Taenia), so this cannot separate them.","C":"Shared: O. viverrini is also operculated — that is exactly the trap she flagged.","D":"Shared: O. viverrini also has a small knob opposite the operculum.","E":"Both eggs are oval; shape does not separate them."},"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Two operculated eggs with a terminal knob are shown. One is 58–76 × 40–51 µm and unembryonated; the other is 25 × 12 µm and embryonated with a prominent shoulder. What are they, and what actually decides it?","o":{"D":"Both are D. latum at different maturities","C":"The big one is Opisthorchis viverrini and the small one is D. latum — shape decides","A":"The big unembryonated one is Diphyllobothrium latum, the small embryonated one is Opisthorchis viverrini — SIZE decides, because both have an operculum and a knob","E":"Both are Opisthorchis; the size difference is an artefact of focusing","B":"The presence of a knob identifies D. latum uniquely, so size is irrelevant"},"r":{"D":"They are different organisms in different lectures, and only one is embryonated when passed.","C":"Reversed. D. latum is the big one; O. viverrini is 25 x 12 micrometres.","A":"Correct, and the lecturer flagged this cross-lecture trap herself: 'remember this size — in the afternoon you will meet an egg that also has an operculum and a knob, but is half as big.' Both features are shared, so MEASURE.","E":"The size difference is real and is the discriminator.","B":"Both eggs carry a knob, which is precisely why the knob cannot decide it. Spirometra mansoni is a third look-alike, effectively identical to D. latum but present in Thailand."},"t":"Operculated eggs","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Operculated eggs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Why MgSO4 is added","s":"A patient whose expelled segment showed uterine branches <15 is given praziquantel as a single dose, followed by magnesium sulphate. What is the purpose of the magnesium sulphate for this species?","lec":"L3","sl":19,"c":"<b>Why MgSO4 is added</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"To flush out the paralysed worm before its scolex can reattach to the intestinal wall","B":"To limit the inflammation around dying cysticerci that are already in the brain","C":"To flush out eggs freed from disintegrating proglottids before they cause cysticercosis","D":"To raise praziquantel absorption by drawing water into the small-bowel lumen","E":"To relieve the constipation that the adult worm causes in symptomatic patients"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The incomplete answer. Clearing the worm is true of any purge, but it does not explain why the slide restricts MgSO4 to T. solium.","B":"Inflammation around dying brain cysts is a neurocysticercosis-treatment issue (steroids, L9), not the purpose of a gut purge in taeniasis.","C":"Correct. Uterine branches <15 = T. solium. Slide 19: 'MgSO4 (prevent cysticercosis in taeniasis solium)'. Treatment breaks up the gravid proglottids and frees eggs, and T. solium eggs are the infective stage for cysticercosis — so the purge must evacuate them. The answer has to reach cysticercosis to earn the mark.","D":"MgSO4 is a purgative here, not an absorption enhancer.","E":"Constipation is one of the mild symptoms of taeniasis (slide 17), but the slide gives MgSO4 a specific purpose: preventing cysticercosis."},"img":"slides_i/L3_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does the 2569 treatment slide add magnesium sulphate for taeniasis SOLIUM only?","o":{"B":"To increase praziquantel absorption","A":"Simply to expel the dead worm from the bowel","C":"Praziquantel disintegrates gravid proglottids and frees eggs, and T. solium eggs are infective for cysticercosis — the purgative evacuates them before they can be re-ingested","D":"To prevent the meningitis-like reaction of praziquantel","E":"To treat the constipation that taeniasis causes"},"r":{"B":"MgSO4 is a purgative, not an absorption enhancer.","A":"The incomplete version, which is exactly what loses the mark.","C":"Correct, and the answer has to reach cysticercosis to earn the mark. This is the whole reason solium is treated differently from saginata.","D":"That is prevented with a corticosteroid.","E":"Taeniasis is usually asymptomatic or causes mild non-specific symptoms."},"t":"Treatment","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L3_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Treatment</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Diphyllobothrium — cause of the anaemia","s":"A 50-year-old Thai man who worked for three years in Finland, often eating raw freshwater fish, has fatigue, eosinophilia and a macrocytic anaemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. He later passes a chain of segments that are wider than long. What is the mechanism of his anaemia?","lec":"L3","sl":39,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — cause of the anaemia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Iron deficiency from blood loss at the attachment site","B":"Haemolysis triggered by antibodies to worm antigens","C":"Vitamin B12 deficiency as the worm takes up the vitamin","D":"Marrow suppression by toxins from dead proglottids","E":"Folate deficiency from damage to the jejunal villi"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Iron deficiency from blood loss is the hookworm story (L1) and gives a microcytic, not macrocytic, picture.","B":"Haemolysis is not a mechanism the lecture gives for any cestode.","C":"Correct. Proglottids wider than long + raw freshwater fish abroad (no report in Thailand) = Diphyllobothrium latum. Slide 39: megaloblastic anaemia (vitamin B12 deficiency), with the hypersegmented neutrophil pictured.","D":"No marrow-toxin mechanism is taught; D. latum anaemia is a vitamin deficiency.","E":"Folate deficiency also gives hypersegmented neutrophils, which is why it is tempting — but the slide names vitamin B12."},"img":"slides_i/L3_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which combination points to Diphyllobothrium latum?","o":{"E":"Megaloblastic anaemia, a barrel-shaped egg, and a Thai rice-farming history","C":"Megaloblastic anaemia with hypersegmented neutrophils, a large operculated egg, and a travel history outside Thailand","B":"Iron-deficiency anaemia, an operculated egg, and raw crab consumption","A":"Megaloblastic anaemia, radially striated eggs, and raw beef consumption","D":"Eosinophilia, a non-operculated egg with a lateral spine, and freshwater swimming"},"r":{"E":"A barrel egg is Trichuris (L1) and causes no anaemia of this type.","C":"Correct. The worm competes for vitamin B12, giving a megaloblastic picture — and the slide states twice that there is NO REPORT IN THAILAND, so a travel history matters.","B":"Hookworm causes iron deficiency; raw crab is Paragonimus.","A":"Radial striation is Taenia, which does not cause megaloblastic anaemia.","D":"A lateral spine on a non-operculated egg is Schistosoma mansoni (L4)."},"t":"Diphyllobothrium","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L3_32.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Human as accidental host","s":"Child 1 develops seizures from brain cysts after eating snacks made by a vendor whose passed segments show uterine branches <15. Child 2, from a house with rats and cockroaches, passes spherical yellowish-brown eggs about 65 µm without polar filaments. According to the lecture's host tables, what role does the human play in each child's infection?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"<b>Human as accidental host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Definitive host in child 1; accidental host in child 2","B":"Accidental host in both children","C":"Definitive host in both children","D":"Accidental host in child 1; definitive host in child 2","E":"Intermediate host in child 1; definitive host in child 2"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Reversed for child 1: a human carrying brain cysts is not harbouring the adult worm.","B":"Correct. Child 1 has cysticercosis from T. solium eggs — the host table lists 'Accidental host (Cysticercosis): Humans' (slides 16, 20). Child 2 has H. diminuta, whose definitive host is the rat, with 'Accidental host = Human' (slides 26, 27).","C":"Humans are definitive hosts in TAENIASIS (adult in the gut) and for H. nana — neither is what these children have.","D":"Child 1 is right, but for H. diminuta the rat is the definitive host and the human is accidental.","E":"Child 2 is wrong (accidental, not definitive). Note the slides label the human in cysticercosis as the accidental host."},"img":"slides_i/L3_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"For which parasite is the human an ACCIDENTAL intermediate host, and why is that different from being an accidental host of Angiostrongylus?","o":{"E":"Both Taenia species equally","D":"Taenia saginata — humans cannot digest beef cysticerci","C":"Hymenolepis diminuta — humans are the definitive host of nana instead","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum — humans replace the fish","B":"Taenia solium in cysticercosis — the larva develops normally, just in the wrong mammal, whereas the Angiostrongylus larva can never mature at all"},"r":{"E":"Only T. solium forms cysticerci in humans; saginata does not.","D":"Humans do become infected from beef cysticerci — that is taeniasis saginata, with the human as definitive host.","C":"Humans are an accidental DEFINITIVE host for H. diminuta, which is a different role again, and the statement about nana is not the contrast asked for.","A":"Humans are the definitive host of D. latum.","B":"Correct, and the distinction is worth holding because the exam uses the word 'accidental' for both. In cysticercosis the cysticercus forms exactly as it would in a pig; in angiostrongyliasis the larva reaches a dead end."},"t":"Host roles","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L3_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Host roles</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q17","t":"Taenia species","s":"A patient gives a history of eating raw beef, and stool examination shows an egg with radial striation. What is the infection?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Taenia species</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q17 — raw beef plus radially striated egg</span>","o":{"C":"Hymenolepis diminuta","A":"Taenia solium","D":"Hymenolepis nana","E":"Taenia saginata","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"H. diminuta eggs are brown, spherical, 60–70 µm and have no filaments.","A":"T. solium would need a history of raw PORK.","D":"H. nana eggs are colourless with 4–8 polar filaments.","E":"Correct — and note carefully how the question is built: radial striation gives you only 'Taenia spp.', and the word BEEF is what finishes it.","B":"D. latum eggs are operculated with a terminal knob, and come from raw freshwater fish."},"img":"slides_i/L3_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-cysti","t":"Cysticercosis","s":"A patient has a painless cystic structure removed; it contains a larva with a scolex and hooks. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 — painless cyst with scolex and hooks; mode of infection</span>","o":{"E":"Ingest fruit or vegetables contaminated with Taenia solium eggs","A":"Ingest raw pork with Cysticercus cellulosae","D":"Ingest raw beef with Cysticercus bovis","B":"Ingest fruit contaminated with Taenia saginata eggs","C":"Ingest undercooked insect containing cysticercoid larvae"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Cysticercosis follows swallowing the EGG, not the larva.","A":"The trap that sits in this option list every time: raw pork with a cysticercus gives an adult worm — taeniasis.","D":"Beef cysticerci give taeniasis saginata.","B":"T. saginata eggs swallowed by a human do nothing — humans are not a permissive intermediate host, and the learning-guidance table prints a dash in that cell.","C":"Cysticercoid in an insect is Hymenolepis."},"img":"slides_i/L3_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q34"]},{"id":"I|L3|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q37","t":"Hymenolepis nana","s":"A 10-year-old boy living in an orphanage has diarrhoea, weight loss and malnutrition. Stool shows an oval transparent egg, 45 × 35 µm, with 4–8 polar filaments. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L3","sl":24,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis nana</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q37 · BM29 SUM1 Q37 — orphanage, 4–8 polar filaments</span>","o":{"B":"Hymenolepis nana","A":"Hymenolepis diminuta","C":"Hookworm","E":"Strongyloides stercoralis","D":"Enterobius vermicularis"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Polar filaments are unique to H. nana among the cestodes — and the orphanage is the second clue, because nana spreads person-to-person and needs the whole institution treated.","A":"H. diminuta eggs are brown, spherical, 60–70 µm and have NO polar filaments; it also needs an insect intermediate host and does not autoinfect.","C":"Hookworm eggs are thin-walled ovals with 4–8 CELLS — note how close that wording is to '4–8 polar filaments'.","E":"Strongyloides gives larvae, not eggs, in fresh stool.","D":"Enterobius eggs are D-shaped and collected from perianal skin."},"img":"slides_i/L3_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q37"]},{"id":"I|L3|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q37","t":"Diphyllobothrium","s":"A 50-year-old man has eosinophilia and megaloblastic anaemia. Stool shows a yellow-brown egg, 40 × 60 µm, oval with an operculum and a terminal knob. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L3","sl":36,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q37 — megaloblastic anaemia, operculum + terminal knob, 40×60 µm</span>","o":{"B":"Eating raw freshwater fish containing plerocercoid larvae","D":"Eating raw freshwater fish containing metacercariae","C":"Eating food contaminated with sparganum","A":"Eating undercooked beef containing cysticerci","E":"Eating undercooked insects containing cysticercoid larvae"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Megaloblastic anaemia plus a large operculated egg with a terminal knob is Diphyllobothrium latum, whose infective stage in fish is the PLEROCERCOID.","D":"Metacercaria belongs to the FLUKES — the same fish, a different stage, and the reason this option is here. Read the stage, not the fish.","C":"Sparganum is the plerocercoid of Spirometra in human tissue; it is a distractor in D. latum questions and never the answer for D. latum itself.","A":"Cysticercus in beef is T. saginata, which causes no megaloblastic anaemia.","E":"Cysticercoid in insects is Hymenolepis diminuta."},"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q39"]},{"id":"I|L3|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-auto","t":"Autoinfection","s":"Which cestode can cause autoinfection?","lec":"L3","sl":25,"c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 — autoinfection item with H. diminuta offered as the distractor</span>","o":{"D":"Spirometra spp.","A":"Hymenolepis diminuta","C":"Taenia saginata","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum","E":"Hymenolepis nana"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Spirometra needs Cyclops and a second intermediate host.","A":"H. diminuta is the deliberate split inside this question: it needs an insect intermediate host and cannot autoinfect.","C":"T. SOLIUM can autoinfect (as cysticercosis) but saginata cannot.","B":"D. latum needs a copepod and then a fish.","E":"Correct. H. nana's embryonated egg is infective the moment it is laid, so it can re-infect the same host — which is also why it spreads person-to-person and why a household or institution must be treated together."},"img":"slides_i/L3_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L3|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q34","t":"Indian ink — uterine branches","s":"An actively motile proglottid is expelled from the rectum. An Indian-ink injection shows 18 UTERINE BRANCHES. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Indian ink — uterine branches</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q34 — active proglottid from the rectum, Indian ink shows 18 uterine branches</span>","o":{"D":"Taeniasis saginata","A":"Taeniasis solium","E":"Cysticercosis","C":"Diphyllobothriasis","B":"Hymenolepiasis"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The lecture gives one number: MORE than 15 branches = T. saginata, FEWER than 15 = T. solium. 18 is more than 15. The extra clue agrees — only saginata's proglottids crawl out actively on their own.","A":"T. solium has fewer than 15 branches, and its proglottids are passed passively in stool.","E":"Cysticercosis is the LARVAL tissue disease from swallowing T. solium EGGS. There are no proglottids in the rectum in cysticercosis — this option confuses the adult tapeworm with the larva.","C":"Diphyllobothrium has a rosette-shaped central uterus, not lateral branches, and its egg is operculate with a terminal knob.","B":"Hymenolepis proglottids disintegrate in the bowel and are not recovered intact."},"img":"slides_i/L3_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L3|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-taenia","t":"Taeniasis vs cysticercosis","s":"A 40-year-old man has a history of eating UNDERCOOKED PORK, and a Taenia-type organism is recovered on stool examination. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"<b>Taeniasis vs cysticercosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative · parasitology — undercooked pork, organism recovered in stool; the lecturer's written feedback stressed the Taeniasis / Cysticercosis distinction</span>","o":{"B":"Cysticercosis","A":"Taeniasis solium","C":"Taeniasis saginata","E":"Gnathostomiasis","D":"Paragonimiasis"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"This is the distinction the examiner wrote feedback about. Cysticercosis is what happens when a human swallows the EGG and becomes the intermediate host — larvae in brain, eye and muscle, and NOTHING in the stool. Same worm, opposite stage, opposite direction.","A":"Correct. Pork + adult worm in the gut = intestinal taeniasis from T. solium. The infective stage swallowed was the CYSTICERCUS CELLULOSAE in the muscle of the pig.","C":"Beef, not pork.","E":"Gnathostoma comes from eel or freshwater fish and gives migratory swellings.","D":"Paragonimus comes from crab and goes to the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L3_16.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L3|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Taeniasis — complications of the adult worm","s":"A 40-year-old passes a worm after treatment; its scolex bears four suckers and a rostellum with hooks. His stool had shown radially striated eggs, and he has no neurological, eye or skin findings. Which complication does the lecture list for this intestinal infection itself?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"<b>Taeniasis — complications of the adult worm</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Acute appendicitis","B":"Cholangitis from wandering proglottids","C":"Megaloblastic anaemia","D":"Seizures from brain cysts","E":"Rectal prolapse"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Armed scolex = T. solium adult in the gut = taeniasis solium. Slide 17: most are asymptomatic; symptoms are mild and non-specific, with intestinal obstruction and appendicitis as the listed complications.","B":"Cholecystitis/cholangitis from migrating proglottids is listed for heavy Diphyllobothrium latum infection (slide 39).","C":"Megaloblastic anaemia (vitamin B12) is Diphyllobothrium latum.","D":"Seizures come from cysticercosis — the LARVA in tissue after swallowing eggs — not from the adult worm; and he has no neurological findings.","E":"Rectal prolapse is Trichuris trichiura (L1)."},"img":"slides_i/L3_17.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A whole tapeworm is expelled after treatment. The scolex carries four suckers PLUS a rostellum armed with a double crown of hooks. Which species is it, and what must you now warn the patient about?","o":{"A":"Taenia solium — this patient can also develop cysticercosis by swallowing its eggs, including from their own hands","D":"Taenia saginata — the rostellar hooks are its identifying feature","C":"Taenia solium — but cysticercosis is impossible in someone who already carries the adult worm","B":"Taenia saginata — the patient risks cysticercosis from eating undercooked beef","E":"Diphyllobothrium latum — the hooks are the bothria seen end-on"},"r":{"A":"Correct. ARMED scolex (rostellum + hooks) = T. solium, and the same person's faecal–oral contamination is exactly how autoinfective cysticercosis happens.","D":"T. saginata is UNARMED — four suckers, no rostellum, no hooks. This is the single most tested morphological pair in the lecture.","C":"Species right, warning wrong, and dangerously so — carrying the adult is a RISK FACTOR for cysticercosis, not protection from it.","B":"Wrong species, and cysticercosis never comes from beef. Cysticercus bovis in beef causes taeniasis saginata, full stop.","E":"Bothria are sucking GROOVES on an elongated scolex, not hooks, and D. latum has no rostellum."},"t":"Scolex discriminator","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L3_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Scolex discriminator</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Cysticercosis vs taeniasis — naming the disease","s":"A 38-year-old pig farmer who often eats undercooked pork has several painless, firm nodules under the skin of his arms. Excision shows fluid-filled bladders, each holding a single invaginated scolex armed with hooks. Repeated stool examinations are negative. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L3","sl":11,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis vs taeniasis — naming the disease</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Gnathostomiasis","B":"Taeniasis saginata","C":"Trichinellosis","D":"Cysticercosis","E":"Taeniasis solium"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Gnathostoma (L2) gives intermittent migratory swellings, not fixed cysts containing a scolex.","B":"T. saginata has an unarmed scolex, its larva lives in cattle, and humans never develop its cysticerci.","C":"Trichinella (L2) also comes from pork and lodges in muscle, but as a coiled larva in a nurse cell, not a bladder with a scolex.","D":"Correct. A bladder with one hooked scolex is a cysticercus (Cysticercus cellulosae) lying in tissue — subcutaneous cysticercosis (slides 11, 14, 15). It came from swallowing T. solium eggs, whatever his pork history.","E":"The trap the lecturer wrote feedback about. The pork history tempts you, but taeniasis means an adult worm in the gut with eggs/segments in stool — his stool is negative."},"img":"slides_i/L3_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient presents with new-onset seizures and brain imaging shows multiple cystic lesions. Which single statement about how this was acquired is correct?","o":{"D":"He swallowed Taenia solium EGGS; in cysticercosis the human is an accidental host, and the egg — not the cysticercus — is the infective stage","E":"He ate undercooked pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae, which migrated to the brain instead of the gut","C":"He swallowed Taenia saginata eggs; both Taenia species cause cysticercosis","B":"He ate undercooked beef containing Cysticercus bovis","A":"He swallowed Taenia solium eggs, which makes him a definitive host carrying an adult worm in the brain"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the host-role half matters: for taeniasis the human is the DEFINITIVE host; for cysticercosis the human steps into the pig's place as an ACCIDENTAL (intermediate-type) host.","E":"Eating the cysticercus gives you the ADULT worm in the gut — that is taeniasis solium. It never disseminates.","C":"T. saginata eggs do NOT cause human cysticercosis; the slide leaves that cell of the table blank deliberately.","B":"Beef and C. bovis give taeniasis saginata only.","A":"The egg hatches into a larva that encysts — no adult worm forms, so he is not a definitive host here."},"t":"Cysticercosis — what you must swallow","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L3_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Cysticercosis — what you must swallow</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Hymenolepis nana — modes of transmission","s":"Eleven children and one carer at a Bangkok day-care centre are found to pass transparent oval eggs, 30–50 µm, with 4–8 polar filaments around a hooked embryo. Which pair lists this parasite's modes of transmission as given in the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":28,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis nana — modes of transmission</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Food-borne and person-to-person","B":"Soil-transmitted and person-to-person","C":"Vector-borne and transplacental","D":"Vector-borne and person-to-person","E":"Food-borne and vector-borne"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Polar filaments = H. nana. Slide 28 gives it two modes of transmission: food-borne AND person-to-person — which is why it clusters in orphanages and day-care centres (slide 29). H. diminuta is food-borne only.","B":"Hymenolepis eggs are infective as soon as they are passed — no soil maturation (that is Ascaris/Trichuris, L1).","C":"Neither vector-borne nor transplacental transmission is taught for any cestode.","D":"Person-to-person is right, but there is no vector-borne mode.","E":"Beetles and fleas are INTERMEDIATE hosts that are eaten, not vectors that bite; the slide calls the route food-borne."},"img":"slides_i/L3_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A stool smear from a child in an orphanage shows a small colourless egg, about 40 µm, containing a hexacanth embryo, with delicate filaments arising from the poles of the inner membrane. Which species is it, and what does that mean for the other children?","o":{"A":"Hymenolepis nana — it transmits person-to-person and can autoinfect, so the whole group is at risk without any insect involved","D":"Hymenolepis diminuta — the polar filaments are its hallmark","C":"Hymenolepis nana — but transmission requires eating a beetle or flea, so the other children are not at risk","B":"Hymenolepis diminuta — humans are the definitive host and spread it directly","E":"Taenia spp. — the radial striations have been mistaken for polar filaments"},"r":{"A":"Correct. 4–8 POLAR FILAMENTS = H. nana, and it is the one cestode in the lecture with direct person-to-person spread plus intestinal autoinfection — which is why the deck cites Thai orphanage outbreaks.","D":"H. diminuta has NO polar filaments and is bigger (60–70 µm) and yellowish-brown.","C":"Species right, epidemiology wrong. Insect ingestion is ONE of H. nana's routes; the embryonated egg alone is also infective.","B":"For H. diminuta the definitive host is the RAT and the human is accidental — direct human spread does not occur.","E":"Taenia eggs are 31–43 µm with a thick radially striated shell and are dark brown — no filaments, and they would be reported as 'Taenia spp.'."},"t":"Hymenolepis egg pair","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L3_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Hymenolepis egg pair</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Taeniasis — prevention vs control","s":"In a village where several adults pass segments with uterine branches <15 and some villagers have seizures from brain cysts, the district team asks which measure the lecture lists under control (rather than prevention) for this parasite. Which is it?","lec":"L3","sl":19,"c":"<b>Taeniasis — prevention vs control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Controlling rodents, fleas and beetles","B":"Avoiding food contaminated with insects","C":"Freezing meat below −10 °C for 24–48 hours","D":"Not using human faeces as fertiliser","E":"Avoiding raw or undercooked pork"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Rodent and insect control is the Hymenolepis control list (slide 31).","B":"Avoiding food contaminated with insects is Hymenolepis prevention (slide 31).","C":"Freezing below −10 °C for 24–48 h is the Diphyllobothrium prevention measure for fish (slide 42), not a Taenia control measure.","D":"Correct. T. solium (branches <15; cysticercosis in the village). Slide 19 splits it: prevention = avoid raw pork (solium) / raw beef (saginata); control = treat infected individuals, use a toilet, do not use faeces as fertiliser.","E":"True for T. solium, but the slide files it under PREVENTION (the individual's act), not control."},"img":"slides_i/L3_19.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"You are treating confirmed taeniasis solium with a single dose of praziquantel. Which additional step does the lecture specify, and why?","o":{"D":"Give MgSO4 — a purge that clears the disintegrating worm and its eggs quickly, reducing the risk of precipitating cysticercosis","C":"Give MgSO4 to improve praziquantel absorption","E":"Give albendazole as well, because praziquantel does not kill the scolex","B":"Give a corticosteroid, because dying worms in the gut cause anaphylaxis","A":"Nothing extra — the MgSO4 step applies to taeniasis saginata, where the proglottids are motile"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Praziquantel breaks the worm up and releases eggs into the bowel; a purge gets them out before they can hatch and cause autoinfective cysticercosis.","C":"MgSO4 is a purgative here, not an absorption enhancer.","E":"Albendazole is not listed for intestinal taeniasis in this deck — it belongs to the tissue-cyst setting.","B":"Steroids belong to neurocysticercosis management, not to clearing the intestinal worm.","A":"The slide attaches MgSO4 specifically to taeniasis SOLIUM, because only T. solium eggs are dangerous to the person passing them."},"t":"Praziquantel and the MgSO4 trap","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L3_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Praziquantel and the MgSO4 trap</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Diphyllobothrium — first intermediate host","s":"Colonoscopy in a man who lived in Japan reveals a very long, ribbon-like worm; the recovered scolex is elongated, with two slit-like sucking grooves and no hooks. Which stage of this worm develops in its first intermediate host?","lec":"L3","sl":37,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — first intermediate host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Plerocercoid larva in a Cyclops copepod","B":"Cysticercoid larva in a grain beetle","C":"Procercoid larva in a Cyclops copepod","D":"Coracidium in a freshwater snail","E":"Procercoid larva in a small freshwater fish"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Right host, wrong stage — the plerocercoid forms in the second intermediate host, the fish. 'Cyclops gets the pro, the fish gets the plero.'","B":"Cysticercoids in insects belong to Hymenolepis, a cyclophyllidean with four suckers.","C":"Correct. Two bothria (sucking grooves) = the pseudophyllidean Diphyllobothrium latum. Its 1st IH is Cyclops, holding the procercoid; the 2nd IH is freshwater fish, holding the plerocercoid, which is the infective stage for humans (slides 37–38).","D":"The coracidium swims free in water and is eaten by the copepod; there is no snail in the cestode cycles (snails are the fluke hosts of L4).","E":"Fish is the SECOND intermediate host, and the stage that matters there is the plerocercoid."},"img":"slides_i/L3_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A traveller returning from Japan has macrocytic anaemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. Stool shows an oval operculate egg about 65 µm long with a small knob at the abopercular end. Which pair of statements is correct?","o":{"C":"Diphyllobothrium latum; the egg is UNembryonated when passed, and the anaemia is megaloblastic from the worm competing for vitamin B12","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum; the egg is embryonated when passed, and the anaemia is iron-deficiency from chronic blood loss","E":"Taenia saginata; the operculum is the characteristic feature of taeniid eggs","A":"Hymenolepis nana; the knob is a polar filament seen end-on, and the anaemia is nutritional","D":"Diphyllobothrium latum; the anaemia is haemolytic and the egg is indistinguishable from Taenia spp."},"r":{"C":"Correct on both. D. latum is the only cestode in this lecture with an OPERCULATED, UNEMBRYONATED egg, and the B12 story explains the hypersegmented neutrophils.","B":"Both halves are wrong in the plausible direction — the egg is unembryonated, and the anaemia is B12-related, not iron-related.","E":"Taeniid eggs are round, thick-shelled with radial striations and embryonated — no operculum anywhere.","A":"H. nana eggs are 30–50 µm with polar FILAMENTS, not a shell knob, and no operculum.","D":"Species right, but the mechanism is B12 competition, and D. latum eggs resemble SPIROMETRA, not Taenia."},"t":"Diphyllobothrium — egg and anaemia","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L3_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — egg and anaemia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Pseudophyllidean proglottid → expected egg","s":"A chain of proglottids passed by a traveller is examined: each segment is wider than long, with a common genital pore in the middle of the segment and a rosette-shaped uterus. What would a stool examination of this patient most likely show?","lec":"L3","sl":34,"c":"<b>Pseudophyllidean proglottid → expected egg</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"An embryonated egg with a thick, radially striated shell","B":"An embryonated egg with an operculum and prominent shoulders","C":"An embryonated colourless egg with 4–8 polar filaments","D":"An unembryonated barrel-shaped egg with bipolar plugs","E":"An unembryonated egg with an operculum and a small knob"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Thick radially striated embryonated egg = Taenia spp., whose proglottids are longer than wide with a branched uterus.","B":"Embryonated operculated egg with prominent shoulders = Opisthorchis viverrini (L4) — a fluke, with no proglottids at all.","C":"Colourless egg with polar filaments = Hymenolepis nana.","D":"Unembryonated barrel-shaped egg with bipolar plugs = Trichuris trichiura (L1), a nematode.","E":"Correct. Proglottid wider than long, central common genital pore, rosette uterus = Diphyllobothrium latum (slides 34–35; Taenia's pore is lateral). Its diagnostic stage is the unembryonated, operculated egg with a small knob (slides 36, 41)."},"img":"slides_i/L3_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which set of features identifies a PSEUDOPHYLLIDEAN tapeworm?","o":{"E":"A scolex bearing two sucking grooves (bothria), proglottids wider than long with a rosette-shaped uterus and a common genital pore on the flat surface","B":"A scolex with four cup-shaped suckers and a gravid proglottid whose uterine branches are counted with Indian ink","D":"A scolex with an armed rostellum and eggs containing a hexacanth embryo","C":"A scolex with bothria and an embryonated egg with radial striations","A":"A strobila with a lateral genital pore and cysticercoid larvae in insects"},"r":{"E":"Correct — all three features belong to Diphyllobothrium latum, the only pseudophyllidean the lecture covers in depth.","B":"Four suckers and uterine-branch counting are CYCLOPHYLLIDEAN (Taenia).","D":"An armed rostellum is T. solium — cyclophyllidean. Hexacanth embryos occur in both groups, so that half cannot discriminate.","C":"Bothria are right, but radially striated embryonated eggs are taeniid; D. latum's egg is thin, smooth, operculate and unembryonated.","A":"A lateral genital pore and cysticercoid larvae in insects describe Hymenolepis — cyclophyllidean."},"t":"Pseudophyllidean vs Cyclophyllidean","sl":4,"img":"slides_i/L3_4.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Pseudophyllidean vs Cyclophyllidean</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Hymenolepis nana — auto-infection sequence","s":"A 7-year-old's stool repeatedly shows transparent oval eggs, 30–50 µm, with polar filaments. He has had no contact with insects or other children for months, yet his worm burden keeps rising. According to the life cycle, what happens next after an egg hatches inside his own intestine?","lec":"L3","sl":25,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis nana — auto-infection sequence</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The oncosphere leaves in stool and must be eaten by a flea or beetle","B":"The oncosphere enters a mesenteric venule and encysts as a cysticercus","C":"The oncosphere attaches to the ileal mucosa and grows directly into an adult","D":"The oncosphere enters an intestinal villus and becomes a cysticercoid","E":"The oncosphere migrates through the lungs and is swallowed back to the gut"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The insect route exists for H. nana but is not needed — and it is the ONLY route for H. diminuta. It cannot explain rising burden with no insect contact.","B":"Blood-borne spread and a cysticercus in tissue is T. solium cysticercosis, not Hymenolepis.","C":"It skips a stage: there is always a cysticercoid (in the villus) between the oncosphere and the adult.","D":"Correct. Slide 25, step 9: if eggs remain in the intestine they release the hexacanth embryo, which penetrates an intestinal villus; the cysticercoid develops in the villus, then emerges as a scolex and grows to an adult in the ileum. That is H. nana auto-infection.","E":"Lung migration is the Ascaris/hookworm/Strongyloides pattern (L1); no cestode in this lecture migrates through the lungs."},"img":"slides_i/L3_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the two Hymenolepis species?","o":{"D":"H. nana can be acquired by swallowing the embryonated egg, by swallowing an infected beetle or flea, or by autoinfection; H. diminuta is acquired only by swallowing an insect carrying a cysticercoid, and the human is an accidental host","C":"Both species require an insect intermediate host, and neither autoinfects","E":"H. diminuta autoinfects while H. nana does not, which is why H. diminuta is commoner in children","A":"H. nana requires a rat as definitive host; humans are accidental for both species","B":"Both are acquired by eating undercooked pork containing cysticercoid larvae"},"r":{"D":"Correct. H. nana is the flexible one — that is why it spreads in crowded settings and why it is the only cestode here that autoinfects.","C":"H. nana does NOT require an insect; the egg alone is infective. And autoinfection is one of its defining features.","E":"Inverted on both counts. H. nana is the one found in Thai orphanage children.","A":"Humans and rodents are both definitive hosts for H. nana; only for H. diminuta is the human accidental.","B":"No Hymenolepis species comes from pork. Pork is T. solium."},"t":"Hymenolepis — routes compared","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L3_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Hymenolepis — routes compared</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Diphyllobothrium — prevention","s":"A Bangkok restaurant plans to serve raw perch imported from Europe, where customers have been diagnosed with a tapeworm that causes megaloblastic anaemia and that the lecture says has no report in Thailand. Short of cooking, which treatment of the fish does the lecture recommend?","lec":"L3","sl":42,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Freeze below −10 °C for 24–48 hours","B":"Cold-smoke at 30 °C for 24 hours","C":"Chill at 0–4 °C for 7 days","D":"Freeze below −4 °C for 6 hours","E":"Marinate in lime juice for 48 hours"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Megaloblastic anaemia + no report in Thailand = Diphyllobothrium latum. Slide 42 prevention: consume cooked freshwater fish, or freeze fish below −10 °C for 24–48 hours — both the temperature and the duration are examinable.","B":"Cold-smoking at low temperature does not kill the larvae and is not a listed measure.","C":"Refrigeration does not kill plerocercoid larvae.","D":"Too warm and far too short to kill plerocercoids; not on the slide.","E":"Acid marination (as in koi pla-style dishes) does not kill the larvae; the slide offers only cooking or freezing."},"img":"slides_i/L3_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A sushi restaurant asks how to make its freshwater fish safe from Diphyllobothrium latum. Which advice matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Cook it, or freeze it below −10 °C for 24–48 hours","D":"Freeze it below 0 °C for 2 hours","B":"Soak it in vinegar or lemon juice, which kills plerocercoid larvae","C":"Nothing is needed, because D. latum has never been reported in Thailand","E":"Salt it heavily, as for Opisthorchis"},"r":{"A":"Correct — the temperature AND the duration are both on the slide, and freezing is the practical option for a raw-fish menu.","D":"Too warm and far too brief; this is the number most people misremember.","B":"Acid marination does not kill plerocercoids; the deck offers only cooking and freezing.","C":"It is true that there is no report in Thailand, but imported fish and travel make the advice relevant — and the question asks what makes fish safe, not where the worm lives.","E":"Salting is not a listed measure for any of these, and Opisthorchis is a fluke from a different lecture."},"t":"Diphyllobothrium — prevention","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L3_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Reporting a Taenia egg","s":"A butcher who eats both raw pork and raw beef dishes has stool showing round, dark-brown eggs about 35 µm, with a thick radially striated shell around a hooked embryo. No segments have been passed. How should the laboratory report this finding?","lec":"L3","sl":10,"c":"<b>Reporting a Taenia egg</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Taenia saginata egg","B":"Hymenolepis diminuta egg","C":"Taenia spp. egg","D":"Taenia solium and T. saginata eggs","E":"Taenia solium egg"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"In the past-paper version the diet (raw beef only) made T. saginata the answer. Here the diet is mixed, so the egg alone cannot name a species.","B":"H. diminuta eggs are 60–70 µm, spherical, yellowish-brown and not radially striated.","C":"Correct. 31–43 µm, dark brown, thick shell with radial striation, hexacanth larva = Taenia egg, and slide 10 says report as 'Taenia spp. egg' — the eggs of the two species cannot be told apart. With a mixed diet and no segment, species needs a gravid proglottid (uterine branches >15 / <15) or PCR (slide 18).","D":"Asserting a mixed infection is equally unsupported — the egg gives the genus only.","E":"Nothing in the egg identifies T. solium; radial striation is shared by both species."},"img":"slides_i/L3_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A stool contains dark-brown, thick-shelled, radially striated eggs containing a hexacanth embryo. How should the laboratory report this, and how could the species be settled?","o":{"C":"Report 'Taenia spp. egg' — the eggs of T. solium and T. saginata are indistinguishable — and settle the species on a gravid proglottid stained with Indian ink, or by PCR","B":"Report 'Taenia solium egg' — the radial striations are specific to T. solium","A":"Report 'Taenia saginata egg' — only T. saginata sheds eggs into stool; T. solium sheds proglottids only","E":"Report 'Taenia spp. egg' — the species can never be determined by any method","D":"Report 'cysticercus' — an embryonated egg in stool means cysticercosis"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and both species-level methods are named on the slides: >15 uterine branches = T. saginata, <15 = T. solium, or PCR.","B":"Radial striations belong to BOTH species — that is precisely why the report must stay at genus level.","A":"Both species shed eggs and proglottids. This is a fabricated distinction.","E":"The first half is right but the second is false — the deck gives two ways to reach species.","D":"Eggs in stool mean intestinal TAENIASIS. Cysticercosis is a tissue disease and is not diagnosed from stool."},"t":"Reporting a Taenia egg","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L3_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Reporting a Taenia egg</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Cysticercosis — distribution of cysts","s":"A 30-year-old woman, whose husband passes segments with uterine branches <15, develops the larval tissue infection that follows swallowing his worm's eggs. Which distribution of this disease matches the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":15,"c":"<b>Cysticercosis — distribution of cysts</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Brain 30–50%, subcutaneous tissue 40–60%","B":"Brain 13–46%, eye 60–90%","C":"Brain 60–90%, eye 13–46%","D":"Muscle 70–95%, brain 10–20%","E":"Subcutaneous tissue 60–90%, eye under 5%"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Invented figures; the slide gives no percentage for subcutaneous cysts.","B":"The two numbers swapped — the brain is the dominant site.","C":"Correct. Uterine branches <15 = T. solium; swallowing its eggs = cysticercosis. Slide 15 (new in 2569): neurocysticercosis 60–90%, ocular cysticercosis 13–46%, plus subcutaneous (and intramuscular) forms. The ranges overlap because one patient can have more than one site.","D":"Muscle-dominant encystment is the Trichinella pattern (L2), not cysticercosis.","E":"Subcutaneous cysts are recognised but are not the commonest form."},"img":"slides_i/L3_15.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the distribution of cysticerci in human cysticercosis matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"The central nervous system is involved in 60–90% of cases, the eye in 13–46%, with subcutaneous and intramuscular cysts also recognised","E":"Subcutaneous cysts are the commonest presentation, with CNS involvement in under 10%","B":"Only the CNS is ever involved, which is why the disease is called neurocysticercosis","A":"The liver and lung are the commonest sites, as in hydatid disease","D":"The cysts lodge only in striated muscle, as in trichinellosis"},"r":{"C":"Correct — and the overlap of the two percentage ranges tells you many patients have both.","E":"Inverted. Subcutaneous cysts are the least clinically dangerous and are not the commonest.","B":"Neurocysticercosis is the commonest FORM, not the only one — the slide lists ocular, subcutaneous and intramuscular as well.","A":"Liver and lung is the ECHINOCOCCUS/hydatid pattern, which is not this organism.","D":"Muscle-only encystment is TRICHINELLA, from L2."},"t":"Cysticercosis — where the cysts go","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L3_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Cysticercosis — where the cysts go</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Beef vs pork tapeworm compared","s":"Worm X was acquired from raw beef larb and worm Y from raw pork. Which comparison of the two adult worms matches the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":7,"c":"<b>Beef vs pork tapeworm compared</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"X and Y are the same length and differ in scolex alone","B":"X is the longer worm and has more uterine branches","C":"X is the longer worm, but Y has more uterine branches","D":"Y is the longer worm and has more uterine branches","E":"Y is the longer worm, but X has more uterine branches"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"They differ in length, proglottid number and uterine branches as well as the scolex.","B":"Correct. Beef = T. saginata: 4–12 m, 1,000–2,000 proglottids, uterine branches >15. Pork = T. solium: 2–7 m, 800–1,000 proglottids, uterine branches <15 (slides 7, 9). The beef worm is 'more' on every count.","C":"Length right, but T. solium has FEWER (<15) uterine branches.","D":"Reversed on both counts.","E":"Branches right, but T. solium is the shorter worm (2–7 m)."},"img":"slides_i/L3_7.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which pairing of adult worm dimensions is correct?","o":{"B":"Taenia saginata 4–12 m with 1,000–2,000 proglottids; Taenia solium 2–7 m with 800–1,000 proglottids","E":"Taenia solium 4–12 m with 1,000–2,000 proglottids; Taenia saginata 2–7 m with 800–1,000","D":"Both species reach 10 m and carry about 3,000 proglottids","C":"Taenia saginata is the shorter of the two because cattle are smaller intermediate hosts than pigs","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum is the shortest human tapeworm at 2–7 m"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The BEEF tapeworm is the bigger animal — a useful way to hold it, since it is also the one with more uterine branches.","E":"Straight swap of the two sets of numbers.","D":"10 m and ~3,000 proglottids are DIPHYLLOBOTHRIUM's figures, imported here as a distractor.","C":"T. saginata is the longer species, and the reasoning offered is invented.","A":"D. latum is the LONGEST human tapeworm, over 10 m."},"t":"Taenia — size and proglottid count","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L3_7.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Taenia — size and proglottid count</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Hymenolepis nana — treatment and control","s":"A 9-year-old boarding-school pupil passes transparent oval eggs about 40 µm, each with 4–8 polar filaments around a hooked embryo; the school kitchen has rats and flour beetles. Which plan matches the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":31,"c":"<b>Hymenolepis nana — treatment and control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Praziquantel 10–15 mg/kg once at bedtime; add a magnesium sulphate purge","B":"Niclosamide as a single dose; freeze kitchen food below −10 °C first","C":"Praziquantel 40 mg/kg once at bedtime; control snails in school ponds","D":"Praziquantel 25 mg/kg three times in one day; control rodents and insects","E":"Praziquantel 10–15 mg/kg once at bedtime; control rodents and insects"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Right drug and dose, but the MgSO4 purge belongs to taeniasis SOLIUM only (slide 19).","B":"Niclosamide is listed for Taenia and D. latum, and freezing is the D. latum fish measure.","C":"40 mg/kg single bedtime dose is the L4 community dose for opisthorchiasis; snails are fluke hosts, not cestode hosts.","D":"25 mg/kg three times in one day is the L4 individual dose for liver fluke.","E":"Correct. H. nana (polar filaments). Slide 31: praziquantel 10–15 mg/kg orally in a single dose before bedtime; control = treat infected people, use toilet, no faeces as fertiliser, control rodents and insects (fleas and beetles)."},"img":"slides_i/L3_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which management plan for hymenolepiasis matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"Praziquantel 10–15 mg/kg orally as a single dose before bedtime, with control of rodents, fleas and beetles alongside treating infected people","C":"Niclosamide for 7 days, with control aimed at cattle and pigs","D":"Albendazole 400 mg for 3 days, with freezing of fish as prevention","A":"Praziquantel plus MgSO4 purge, to prevent autoinfection with cysticerci","B":"Diethylcarbamazine, because the larval stage is a cysticercoid"},"r":{"E":"Correct on drug, dose, timing and the control measures, which target the insect intermediate hosts and the rodent reservoir.","C":"Niclosamide appears for Taenia and D. latum; cattle and pigs are Taenia's intermediate hosts, not Hymenolepis's.","D":"Fish freezing is D. latum prevention.","A":"MgSO4 is specific to taeniasis solium; Hymenolepis autoinfection involves cysticercoids in the bowel, not tissue cysticerci.","B":"DEC is the filariasis drug from L2."},"t":"Hymenolepis treatment and control","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L3_30.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Hymenolepis treatment and control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L3|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Diphyllobothrium — heavy infection","s":"A 60-year-old who ate raw freshwater fish for years in Finland carries a heavy burden of a tapeworm whose segments are wider than long. He now develops fever, right-upper-quadrant pain and jaundice. What is the most likely explanation according to the lecture?","lec":"L3","sl":39,"c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — heavy infection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Larvae migrating from the liver to the lungs","B":"Larval cysts developing in the liver","C":"Proglottids migrating into the bile ducts","D":"Adult flukes lodged in the intrahepatic ducts","E":"Eggs trapped in the portal venules"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Liver-to-lung larval migration is the Ascaris/hookworm pattern (L1); D. latum stays in the small intestine.","B":"Tapeworm larval cysts in the liver are not part of the D. latum cycle — humans are its definitive host, harbouring only the adult.","C":"Correct. Proglottids wider than long + raw fish abroad = Diphyllobothrium latum. Slide 39, heavy infections: intestinal obstruction, and migration of proglottids can cause cholecystitis and cholangitis.","D":"Adult flukes in the bile ducts are Opisthorchis viverrini (L4) — raw cyprinoid fish in Thailand, not a tapeworm with proglottids.","E":"Eggs trapped in portal venules is Schistosoma (L4)."},"img":"slides_i/L3_39.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Beyond anaemia, which complications of heavy diphyllobothriasis does the lecture name?","o":{"C":"Intestinal obstruction, and cholecystitis or cholangitis from migrating proglottids","D":"Rectal prolapse and dysentery","E":"Loeffler's syndrome from larvae in the lung","A":"Seizures from cysts in the brain","B":"Protein-losing enteropathy with pitting oedema"},"r":{"C":"Correct. A worm over 10 m long can obstruct, and its proglottids can wander into the biliary tree.","D":"Rectal prolapse and dysentery are TRICHURIS, from L1.","E":"D. latum has no pulmonary migration; Loeffler's belongs to Ascaris, hookworm and Strongyloides.","A":"Brain cysts are T. solium cysticercosis.","B":"Protein-losing enteropathy is CAPILLARIA philippinensis, from L1."},"t":"Diphyllobothrium — heavy infection","sl":39,"img":"slides_i/L3_39.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Diphyllobothrium — heavy infection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233907432},{"id":"I|L4|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Schistosoma — the life cycle that breaks the pattern","s":"A 40-year-old rice farmer from Leyte, the Philippines, has chronic diarrhoea and hepatosplenomegaly. Stool shows a round, non-operculated embryonated egg about 85 µm across with a small lateral knob. Which statement about the life cycle of this parasite is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":62,"c":"<b>Schistosoma — the life cycle that breaks the pattern</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its eggs are passed unembryonated and must develop in water before a miracidium hatches","B":"It needs a snail and then a freshwater fish, in whose muscle the metacercaria develops","C":"Its cercariae leave the snail and encyst on aquatic plants as metacercariae before being eaten","D":"The miracidium that hatches in fresh water penetrates human skin and matures in the veins","E":"In one snail host it goes from sporocyst to cercaria, with no redia or metacercaria"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Schistosome eggs are passed already embryonated — the miracidium is inside (slides 63–64). Passed-unembryonated eggs belong to Paragonimus and Fasciolopsis.","B":"Snail (Bithynia) → cyprinoid fish with metacercariae is Opisthorchis viverrini (slide 14). Schistosoma has no second intermediate host.","C":"That is Fasciolopsis buski: cercariae encyst on water chestnut / water caltrop / water mimosa as metacercariae (slides 48–49). Schistosomes never form a metacercaria.","D":"The miracidium infects the SNAIL. The stage that penetrates human skin is the fork-tailed cercaria (slide 62).","E":"Correct. Round egg + small lateral knob + Philippines = Schistosoma japonicum. Schistosomes use ONE snail (here Oncomelania) and no second intermediate host; inside it the miracidium becomes sporocysts and then fork-tailed cercariae — the teaching document states there is no redia and no metacercaria. The fork-tailed cercaria is the infective stage."},"img":"slides_i/L4_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"In how many ways does Schistosoma break the pattern of every other fluke in this lecture?","o":{"E":"Dioecious; one intermediate host only; no redia and no metacercaria; non-operculated embryonated egg with a spine; acquired by skin penetration in water","C":"Dioecious; two intermediate hosts; operculated egg; acquired by ingestion","A":"Monoecious; one intermediate host; non-operculated egg; acquired by ingestion","B":"Dioecious; one intermediate host; operculated egg; acquired by skin penetration","D":"Monoecious; two intermediate hosts; non-operculated egg; acquired by skin penetration"},"r":{"E":"Correct — five separate breaks, and worth writing out from memory. If a stem mentions a male holding a female in a GYNECOPHORIC CANAL, no other trematode in the syllabus can be the answer.","C":"It has only ONE intermediate host (a snail) and its egg is not operculated.","A":"It is dioecious, not monoecious.","B":"Its egg is NOT operculated — that is the point.","D":"It is dioecious with one intermediate host."},"t":"Schistosoma","sl":59,"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Opisthorchis — how the fluke causes cholangiocarcinoma","s":"A 62-year-old man from Udon Thani who has eaten pla-som and koi pla since childhood is found on ultrasound to have advanced periductal fibrosis and a perihilar mass. Which sequence is proposed in the lecture for how his tumour arose?","lec":"L4","sl":20,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis — how the fluke causes cholangiocarcinoma</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fluke secretory products and inflammatory free radicals damaged bile-duct cell DNA","B":"Eggs lodged in portal venules formed granulomas that progressed to pipestem periportal fibrosis","C":"Migrating juvenile flukes penetrated hepatocytes and left necrotic tracks that later scarred","D":"Immune complexes were deposited in the hepatic sinusoids during the acute febrile phase","E":"Adult worms blocked the common bile duct, and retained bile salts transformed the hepatocytes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Raw cyprinoid fish + periductal fibrosis + perihilar mass = Opisthorchis viverrini → cholangiocarcinoma. Slide 20 gives three converging arms: mechanical damage by the sucker → periductal fibrosis; excretory–secretory (ES) antigen products; and immunopathology → ROS/RNS free radicals (+ nitrosamine) — all ending in DNA damage of the bile-duct epithelium. The teaching document names cathepsin F and B1 in the ES products.","B":"Egg granulomas in portal venules with pipestem (Symmers') fibrosis is hepatosplenic SCHISTOSOMIASIS (slide 67) — it causes portal hypertension, not bile-duct cancer.","C":"O. viverrini metacercariae excyst in the duodenum and crawl up the bile duct; they do not burrow through hepatocytes. The cancer is of bile-duct epithelium, not hepatocytes.","D":"Immune-complex illness in the acute phase is Katayama fever of schistosomiasis (slide 65).","E":"Heavy infection can cause obstructive jaundice (slide 19), but the lecture's model is DNA damage in the cholangiocytes, and the tumour is of the bile duct, not of hepatocytes."},"img":"slides_i/L4_20.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 60-year-old man from Khon Kaen has obstructive jaundice and a mass-forming liver tumour. Stool shows a 25 × 12 µm embryonated operculated egg with a prominent shoulder and a terminal knob. What is the infective stage of this parasite?","o":{"A":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish","B":"Cercaria penetrating intact skin","D":"Embryonated egg ingested from contaminated vegetables","C":"Plerocercoid larva in freshwater fish","E":"Filariform larva penetrating the skin"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and note what the question actually asks. The vignette identifies Opisthorchis viverrini; the question then asks about its STAGE. Read the last line of the stem before you read the options.","B":"Cercarial penetration is Schistosoma.","D":"Embryonated eggs from vegetables is the intestinal nematode route.","C":"Plerocercoid in fish is Diphyllobothrium latum (L3) — the same meal with a different stage.","E":"Filariform larvae belong to hookworm and Strongyloides."},"t":"Opisthorchis","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L4_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Opisthorchis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Paragonimus — acute phase, final habitat","s":"Six weeks after a hiking trip in Phitsanulok, where he ate crushed raw mountain crab in chilli paste, a 28-year-old man has fever, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, urticaria and marked eosinophilia. If he is not treated, where will the adult worms of this parasite finally settle?","lec":"L4","sl":36,"c":"<b>Paragonimus — acute phase, final habitat</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In the vesical venous plexus around the bladder","B":"In the mesenteric veins of the small intestine","C":"In the lung parenchyma, inside a fibrous cyst","D":"In the intrahepatic bile ducts, held by suckers","E":"In the duodenal and jejunal mucosa"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The vesical venous plexus is S. haematobium (slide 61).","B":"Mesenteric veins of the small intestine are S. japonicum / S. mekongi, acquired by skin penetration in water, not by eating.","C":"Correct. Mountain crab = Paragonimus (ปู → ปอด on slide 72). The gut symptoms, urticaria and eosinophilia are the ACUTE phase (slide 36), when the juvenile excysts in the small intestine and migrates through the gut wall, peritoneum and diaphragm; the adults then settle in the lung, usually in pairs inside a host fibrous capsule (teaching doc). Do not let the abdominal presentation pull you to an intestinal fluke.","D":"Bile duct is Opisthorchis viverrini, acquired from raw cyprinoid FISH (ปลา → ตับ).","E":"Duodenum/jejunum is Fasciolopsis buski, acquired from raw water plants (กระจับ → ลำไส้)."},"img":"slides_i/L4_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Four parasites are acquired from 'raw freshwater food'. Which matching is correct?","o":{"A":"Metacercaria on water plants = Paragonimus; metacercaria in crab = F. buski","E":"Metacercaria in crab = O. viverrini; metacercaria in fish = Paragonimus; plerocercoid on plants = F. buski","B":"All four are acquired as metacercariae from fish and cannot be separated","C":"Metacercaria in cyprinoid fish = O. viverrini; metacercaria in crab = Paragonimus; metacercaria on water plants = F. buski; plerocercoid in fish = D. latum","D":"Plerocercoid in crab = Paragonimus; metacercaria in fish = D. latum"},"r":{"A":"Reversed.","E":"Every pairing is shuffled.","B":"The food source is precisely what separates them.","C":"Correct, and these are not interchangeable 'raw aquatic food' — they lead to completely different ORGANS. Plant goes to the gut, fish goes to the bile duct, crab goes to the lung.","D":"Both are wrong; the stage names belong to the other organisms."},"t":"Same meal, different worm","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L4_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Same meal, different worm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Paragonimus — does pickling make crab safe?","s":"A 50-year-old woman from Saraburi eats som tam topped with freshwater crab that was soaked in brine for about an hour. Months later she has a chronic cough with blood-tinged sputum. Which counselling statement about her food is correct?","lec":"L4","sl":35,"c":"<b>Paragonimus — does pickling make crab safe?</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Brining kills the metacercariae but not the eggs, and the eggs in crab meat are infective","B":"Brining kills the metacercariae, so her infection must have come from raw fish instead","C":"The crab is only the first intermediate host, so the snails it ate are what infected her","D":"Brief brining leaves the metacercariae in the crab alive, so the crab must be cooked","E":"The crab carries cercariae that penetrate the mouth, which no amount of cooking prevents"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The infective stage in the crab is the metacercaria. Paragonimus eggs are passed unembryonated in sputum/faeces and infect nothing until a miracidium hatches in water to find a snail.","B":"The crab is the risk, not a hidden fish. Raw cyprinoid fish would give Opisthorchis, a bile-duct fluke that does not cause a cough.","C":"Crab / crayfish is the SECOND intermediate host; the first is a freshwater snail (slide 34, 40).","D":"Correct. Freshwater crab + chronic cough with blood-tinged sputum = Paragonimus (NL slide 41 uses exactly 'blood-tinged sputum'). The teaching document states that short pickling of crab in brine or vinegar does NOT kill the metacercariae; prevention is appropriate cooking of crab, mountain crab and crayfish (slide 39).","E":"Cercariae penetrating the mouth and lips is the Schistosoma route (slide 69). Cooking the crab does prevent paragonimiasis."},"img":"slides_i/L4_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 50-year-old woman regularly eats ปูดอง (brief-pickled raw crab) in papaya salad. Is this safe, and what infection is she at risk of?","o":{"B":"Not safe, but the risk is Opisthorchis viverrini","E":"Safe — the acid in vinegar kills the metacercaria within minutes","A":"Safe — Paragonimus is acquired only from freshwater fish","D":"Not safe — brief pickling in brine or vinegar does not kill the metacercaria, so she is at risk of paragonimiasis","C":"Not safe, but the risk is Fasciolopsis buski"},"r":{"B":"O. viverrini comes from cyprinoid FISH.","E":"The specific misconception the lecturer wrote the slide to correct.","A":"Paragonimus comes from crab.","D":"Correct, and the teaching document says so explicitly. Salting, pickling and fermentation do NOT kill metacercariae — pla-ra, undercooked pla-som and ปูดอง are all full exposures.","C":"F. buski comes from raw aquatic PLANTS."},"t":"Paragonimus","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L4_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Paragonimus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Stool methods — which detects the most light O. viverrini infections","s":"After a mass-treatment campaign in a Khon Kaen village, most remaining liver-fluke infections are light, with few eggs per gram of stool. The team can examine one stool sample per villager. By the lecturer's own comparison study, which method will detect the most of these infections?","lec":"L4","sl":22,"c":"<b>Stool methods — which detects the most light O. viverrini infections</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mini Parasep commercial kit","B":"Kato-Katz thick smear","C":"Formalin ethyl-acetate concentration","D":"Direct simple smear in saline","E":"Cellophane tape (Scotch tape) method"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The commercial Mini Parasep kit reached only 32.4%.","B":"Kato-Katz is also a concentration-type method on slide 22, but it detected only 68.3% in her study.","C":"Correct. Her study of low-intensity O. viverrini infection ranked sensitivity FECT 91% > Kato-Katz 68.3% > Mini Parasep kit 32.4% > direct simple smear 12.4% (teaching doc; stated in class as 'FECT is the best'). Low egg output is exactly when a concentration step matters.","D":"The direct simple smear found only 12.4% — the classic trap option for 'best stool method'.","E":"The cellophane-tape (perianal) method is for Enterobius eggs laid on the perianal skin (L1), not for eggs passed in faeces."},"img":"slides_i/L4_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which specimen goes with which fluke?","o":{"B":"Paragonimus → stool; S. haematobium → stool; Opisthorchis → sputum","C":"Paragonimus → sputum (morning, 10% KOH); Schistosoma haematobium → urine by filtration; Opisthorchis → stool by concentration technique","E":"Paragonimus → urine; S. haematobium → sputum; Opisthorchis → blood","D":"All three are diagnosed on stool examination","A":"All three are diagnosed on serology only"},"r":{"B":"Stool is second-line and often negative for Paragonimus, and it is the wrong specimen for S. haematobium.","C":"Correct. Sending a stool for a bladder fluke is a designed distractor, and for Opisthorchis a DIRECT simple smear is only about 12% sensitive — so if the options offer 'direct simple smear' against 'formalin ethyl-acetate concentration', take the concentration technique.","E":"All three are misassigned.","D":"The specimen choice is exactly what is being tested.","A":"Serology is the fall-back for EXTRAPULMONARY paragonimiasis, where there are no eggs to find."},"t":"Specimens","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L4_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Specimens</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Opisthorchis — reservoir hosts in control","s":"In a village beside Nong Han wetland, every person passing small operculated embryonated eggs with a prominent shoulder and posterior knob is treated, yet within two years the prevalence has returned. Apart from people, which animals must the programme also treat because they maintain this parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":28,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis — reservoir hosts in control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Tigers and other wild carnivores","B":"Domestic dogs and cats","C":"Rodents and monkeys","D":"Cattle and water buffaloes","E":"Pigs kept beside the ponds"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Tigers and other carnivores are definitive hosts of Paragonimus (slide 40).","B":"Correct. Shoulder + knob = Opisthorchis viverrini. Slide 28 lists dogs and cats as its reservoir hosts, and the control list (slide 24) is: sanitary disposal, treatment of infected individuals AND reservoir hosts, and decreasing the snail hosts — the One Health approach in her teaching document.","C":"Rodents and monkeys are listed as definitive hosts of S. mansoni (slide 61).","D":"Cattle and buffaloes are the natural hosts of Fasciola (the ruminant liver fluke) — not in this life cycle.","E":"Pigs are a definitive host of Fasciolopsis buski (slide 48, 53), and domestic pigs are a reservoir of S. mekongi (slide 61)."},"img":"slides_i/L4_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which egg discrimination are you actually expected to make on morphology alone?","o":{"A":"No egg discriminations are expected; all species come from the history","C":"Fasciolopsis buski versus Fasciola hepatica — the operculum is larger in Fasciola","D":"Fasciola versus Echinostoma — the shell is thicker in Echinostoma","E":"Schistosoma japonicum versus S. mekongi — the spine is longer in mekongi","B":"Opisthorchis viverrini versus the small intestinal flukes — shoulder and knob prominent = Opisthorchis; both faint or absent = small intestinal fluke"},"r":{"A":"Too absolute: the O. viverrini versus small-intestinal-fluke discrimination IS expected.","C":"You are NOT expected to resolve this group. Report 'large intestinal fluke egg' and take the species from province and diet — BM28 asked exactly this and keyed Fasciola and Fasciolopsis as having identical eggs.","D":"Same group, same answer — not resolvable from the egg.","E":"These are separated by SIZE (51–73 vs 70–100 µm) and geography, not by spine length.","B":"Correct. This is the one pair the lecturer expects you to resolve from the egg itself."},"t":"Egg groups","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Egg groups</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Fasciolopsis — why the face and legs swell","s":"A 12-year-old girl from Ayutthaya who peels raw water caltrops with her teeth has abdominal discomfort, diarrhoea and swelling of the face and legs. Stool shows a 150 × 80 µm thin-walled, unembryonated egg with an indistinct operculum. To what does the lecture attribute her swelling?","lec":"L4","sl":50,"c":"<b>Fasciolopsis — why the face and legs swell</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Allergy to worm metabolites absorbed from the gut","B":"Immune complexes formed as larvae migrate towards the lung","C":"Portal hypertension from egg granulomas trapped in the liver","D":"Right heart failure from eggs lodged in pulmonary arterioles","E":"Hypoalbuminaemia from bile-duct fibrosis and liver failure"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Water caltrop + Ayutthaya + large unembryonated egg = Fasciolopsis buski (girls and children 10–14 are the high-prevalence group, slide 46). Slide 50: 'toxic or allergic metabolites — absorption of worm metabolites by the host: allergic reaction, swelling of the legs and face'. (The teaching document adds protein loss in heavy infection, but the slide's stated mechanism is the allergic one.)","B":"Larvae migrating to the lung is Paragonimus (or the L1 nematodes); F. buski metacercariae stay in the small intestine.","C":"Portal hypertension from egg granulomas is hepatosplenic schistosomiasis (slide 67).","D":"Pulmonary hypertension from lodged eggs is pulmonary schistosomiasis (slide 67).","E":"Bile-duct fibrosis belongs to Opisthorchis, and F. buski lives in the small intestine, not the liver."},"img":"slides_i/L4_50.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A 50-year-old woman from Suphan Buri eats uncooked water chestnut and presents with diarrhoea plus facial and leg oedema. What is the parasite, and what is the oedema?","o":{"D":"Opisthorchis viverrini — the oedema is from hepatic failure","A":"Fasciolopsis buski — the oedema is caused by the parasite (protein loss and malabsorption), not by heart or kidney disease","E":"Schistosoma mekongi — the oedema is from portal hypertension","C":"Paragonimus — the oedema is from pleural effusion","B":"Fasciola hepatica — the oedema is nephrotic"},"r":{"D":"Opisthorchis comes from fish, and its disease is biliary.","A":"Correct. Suphan Buri and raw water chestnut are the two clues, and the oedema is a recognised feature of heavy F. buski infection — not a competing diagnosis.","E":"Schistosoma is acquired from water contact, not a meal.","C":"Paragonimus comes from crab and affects the lung.","B":"Fasciola is a liver fluke of herbivores; the province and food here point to F. buski, and its eggs are indistinguishable anyway."},"t":"Fasciolopsis","sl":44,"img":"slides_i/L4_44.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Fasciolopsis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Opisthorchis + MASLD + SARS-CoV-2 (new 2569 slide)","s":"A 45-year-old man from Kalasin who eats koi pla and a diet high in fat and fructose is found to have small shouldered, knobbed eggs in his stool; later he also catches COVID-19. Which statement matches the lecturer's own research shown on the new coinfection slide?","lec":"L4","sl":21,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis + MASLD + SARS-CoV-2 (new 2569 slide)</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Coinfected patients had higher interferon-γ levels than those with SARS-CoV-2 alone","B":"The fluke plus that diet promoted fatty liver disease through inflammation and fibrogenesis","C":"The fluke plus that diet caused hepatocellular carcinoma instead of cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Coinfected patients cleared SARS-CoV-2 faster because fluke antigens boosted Th1 immunity","E":"The fluke protected the liver from steatosis because adult worms consume lipid in the bile"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Slide 21, right graph: IFN-γ was highest with SARS-CoV-2 alone; the OV + SARS-CoV-2 group was significantly LOWER than SARS-CoV-2 alone.","B":"Correct. Her 2025 Acta Tropica paper (slide 21, left): a high-fat/high-fructose diet plus O. viverrini infection promotes metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) via inflammation, fibrogenesis and metabolic dysfunction — the HFF+OV panel shows the most steatosis and fibrosis.","C":"The paper is about steatotic liver disease, not a switch in cancer type; the fluke-associated cancer remains cholangiocarcinoma.","D":"Nothing on the slide shows faster clearance, and the coinfection group had lower (not boosted) IFN-γ, a Th1 cytokine.","E":"The opposite: the HFF + OV combination had the worst steatosis, not protection."},"img":"slides_i/L4_21.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Why is 'praziquantel' almost never a discriminating answer in this lecture?","o":{"B":"Because it treats all nine flukes — so a treatment question is really asking about the DOSE or regimen","C":"Because praziquantel is ineffective against trematodes","D":"Because albendazole has replaced it for all trematodes","E":"Because it is contraindicated in Schistosoma infection","A":"Because it is used only for the blood flukes"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the doses genuinely differ: 25 mg/kg three times daily for one day (Opisthorchis, Fasciolopsis), the same for two days (Paragonimus), 40 mg/kg (S. mansoni, S. haematobium) and 60 mg/kg (S. japonicum, S. mekongi).","C":"It is the drug of choice for all of them.","D":"Albendazole is the nematode and cysticercosis drug.","E":"Schistosomiasis is a core praziquantel indication.","A":"It covers the liver, lung and intestinal flukes too."},"t":"Treatment","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Treatment</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q42","t":"Opisthorchis","s":"Koi Pla (ก้อยปลา) is a famous dish in north-eastern Thailand. What is the most common long-term complication of eating such food when it is contaminated with a parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":9,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q42 — ก้อยปลา and its long-term complication</span>","o":{"E":"Prolapse of the rectal muscle","C":"Liver abscess with brownish (anchovy) pus","B":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Pyelonephritis","A":"Chondrosarcoma"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Rectal prolapse is heavy Trichuris trichiura infection (L1).","C":"Anchovy pus in a liver abscess is EXTRAINTESTINAL AMOEBIASIS from Entamoeba histolytica (L5) — a liver lesion, but a different organism entirely.","B":"Correct. Koi pla is raw cyprinoid fish, so the parasite is Opisthorchis viverrini, and chronic infection of the bile ducts leads to cholangiocarcinoma — typically after more than twenty years.","D":"Not a recognised complication of any fluke here.","A":"Not associated with parasitic infection."},"img":"slides_i/L4_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q14","t":"Egg groups","s":"A 40-year-old man from Suphan Buri has an egg in his stool measuring 140 × 85 µm, with a small operculum and a smooth thin wall. What is the most likely parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":47,"c":"<b>Egg groups</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 Q14 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q14 — 140 × 85 µm egg from Suphan Buri</span>","o":{"B":"Paragonimus spp.","D":"Opisthorchis viverrini","C":"Fasciolopsis buski","E":"Taenia solium","A":"Taenia saginata"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Paragonimus eggs are 80–120 µm with a flattened operculum and a thickened posterior end, and the history would be raw crab.","D":"O. viverrini eggs are 25 × 12 µm — five times smaller — and are embryonated with a prominent shoulder.","C":"Correct. The size puts the egg in the large-intestinal-fluke group, which is morphologically indistinguishable — and SUPHAN BURI, an endemic province, is what finishes the question.","E":"Taenia eggs are spherical, 31–43 µm, with radial striation and no operculum.","A":"Same as D."},"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q14","BM29 Q14"]},{"id":"I|L4|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q-eggs","t":"Egg groups","s":"Which pair of parasite eggs have essentially identical features?","lec":"L4","sl":47,"c":"<b>Egg groups</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 — 'which parasite eggs have identical features?'</span>","o":{"C":"Opisthorchis and Schistosoma","A":"Fasciola and Fasciolopsis","E":"Paragonimus and Taenia","B":"Schistosoma mansoni and S. haematobium","D":"Opisthorchis and Diphyllobothrium"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Schistosoma eggs are non-operculated with a spine — nothing like Opisthorchis.","A":"Correct, and the lecture makes the point directly: the large operculated eggs of Fasciola hepatica, F. gigantica, Fasciolopsis buski and Echinostoma are reported simply as 'large intestinal fluke egg'. The species comes from province and diet. On an ADULT specimen, however, you can decide: F. buski has NO cephalic cone, Fasciola has one.","E":"Completely different sizes and structures.","B":"These are separated by spine position: lateral (mansoni) versus terminal (haematobium).","D":"These differ about five-fold in size, though both are operculated with a knob — which is the L3/L4 cross-lecture trap, not an identity."},"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q22","t":"Amoebic vs fluke liver disease","s":"Which long-term complication is correctly matched to its parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":20,"c":"<b>Amoebic vs fluke liver disease</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q22 — 'cirrhosis / colon cancer / liver abscess / bladder cancer / cholangiocarcinoma'</span>","o":{"D":"Cholangiocarcinoma — Opisthorchis viverrini","B":"Bladder cancer — Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Liver abscess with anchovy pus — Opisthorchis viverrini","E":"Colon cancer — Schistosoma haematobium","C":"Cirrhosis — Paragonimus spp."},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Chronic biliary infection and inflammation over decades leads to cholangiocarcinoma — which is why north-east Thailand has the world's highest incidence.","B":"Bladder cancer is associated with Schistosoma HAEMATOBIUM, which lives in the vesical plexus.","A":"Anchovy-pus liver abscess is Entamoeba histolytica (L5).","E":"S. haematobium affects the urinary tract; the intestinal schistosomes are mansoni, japonicum and mekongi.","C":"Paragonimus lives in the lung."},"img":"slides_i/L4_20.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-inf","t":"Infective stage","s":"A patient from Khon Kaen has cholangitis and obstructive jaundice; stool shows an operculated egg with a shoulder and a knob. Which of the following is the INFECTIVE stage of this parasite?","lec":"L4","sl":17,"c":"<b>Infective stage</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 — full Opisthorchis vignette then 'which is the infective stage?'</span>","o":{"A":"Metacercaria","E":"Cercaria","B":"Embryonated egg","C":"Filariform larva","D":"Rhabditiform larva"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The vignette names the organism; the question asks about its stage — and for Opisthorchis viverrini the infective stage is the metacercaria encysted in cyprinoid fish.","E":"The cercaria is what leaves the snail and encysts in the fish; it is not what infects a human. For Schistosoma, however, the cercaria IS the infective stage.","B":"The embryonated egg is the DIAGNOSTIC stage here, and the infective stage for the SNAIL.","C":"Filariform larvae belong to hookworm and Strongyloides.","D":"Rhabditiform larvae belong to Strongyloides."},"img":"slides_i/L4_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q-inf"]},{"id":"I|L4|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-schisto","t":"Schistosoma","s":"A 50-year-old man has chronic diarrhoea and intestinal bleeding. Stool shows a non-operculated embryonated egg with a lateral spine. What is the parasite, and how was it acquired?","lec":"L4","sl":66,"c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM30 · BM31 — chronic diarrhoea with intestinal bleeding, non-operculated egg</span>","o":{"D":"Schistosoma mansoni, acquired when cercariae penetrated intact skin during freshwater contact","B":"Schistosoma haematobium, acquired by drinking contaminated water","C":"Opisthorchis viverrini, acquired from raw cyprinoid fish","E":"Fasciolopsis buski, acquired from raw water plants","A":"Paragonimus spp., acquired from raw crab"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. A LATERAL spine on a non-operculated egg is S. mansoni, and schistosomes are the only water-borne, skin-penetrating flukes. Note that the mouth and lips count as skin — drinking contaminated water can infect you.","B":"S. haematobium has a TERMINAL spine and appears in URINE with haematuria.","C":"Opisthorchis eggs are small and operculated, and the disease is biliary.","E":"F. buski eggs are large and operculated.","A":"Paragonimus eggs are operculated and appear in sputum."},"img":"slides_i/L4_66.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L4|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q24","t":"Paragonimus","s":"A man has a chronic cough with haemoptysis, and his sputum is RUSTY-BROWN. How did he acquire this infection?","lec":"L4","sl":37,"c":"<b>Paragonimus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 (rusty-brown sputum, mode of infection) · BM31 SUM1 Q24 (raw mountain crab, clinical manifestation) · BM28 SUM1 Q30 — asked in three cohorts</span>","o":{"B":"Eating raw cyprinoid fish containing metacercariae","A":"Eating water caltrop containing metacercariae","D":"Skin penetration by cercariae in fresh water","E":"Eating raw or pickled freshwater (mountain) crab containing metacercariae","C":"Inhaling eggs in dust"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Cyprinoid fish is Opisthorchis, which goes to the bile duct, not the lung.","A":"Water caltrop and other water plants carry Fasciolopsis buski metacercariae, which stay in the intestine.","D":"Cercarial skin penetration is Schistosoma.","E":"Correct. Rusty-brown sputum is Paragonimus, the lung fluke, and its second intermediate host is the freshwater/mountain CRAB (or crayfish). Thai 'ปูดอง' — pickled crab — is the classic exposure.","C":"No trematode is acquired by inhalation. Note the classic trap: this picture is mistaken for pulmonary TB, and many patients are treated for TB first."},"img":"slides_i/L4_37.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-para","BM28 Q30"]},{"id":"I|L4|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q30","t":"Schistosoma haematobium","s":"A patient presents with HAEMATURIA. An oval egg with a TERMINAL SPINE is recovered. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L4","sl":63,"c":"<b>Schistosoma haematobium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q30 — haematuria with an oval egg bearing a TERMINAL spine</span>","o":{"A":"Penetration of the skin by filariform larvae","E":"Penetration of the skin by cercariae","B":"Penetration of the skin by rhabditiform larvae","C":"Drinking water contaminated with mature cysts","D":"Eating freshwater plants contaminated with metacercariae"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Filariform larvae are hookworm and Strongyloides — an intestinal worm, no haematuria.","E":"Correct. Terminal spine + haematuria = Schistosoma haematobium, and every schistosome is acquired when the fork-tailed CERCARIA penetrates intact skin during contact with fresh water. Learn the spine table: haematobium TERMINAL, mansoni LATERAL, japonicum/mekongi small LATERAL knob.","B":"The rhabditiform larva is a feeding, non-infective stage and never penetrates skin. This option is wrong on the stage alone.","C":"Mature cysts are protozoa (Entamoeba, Giardia).","D":"Freshwater plants carry Fasciolopsis buski."},"img":"slides_i/L4_63.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L4|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 3","t":"Schistosoma mansoni","s":"A 55-year-old woman is admitted with abdominal pain, dysentery and mild hepatomegaly. Stool examination shows an oval egg with a prominent LATERAL SPINE. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L4","sl":63,"c":"<b>Schistosoma mansoni</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Lect 3 — 55-year-old woman, dysentery with mild hepatomegaly, egg with a LATERAL spine</span>","o":{"B":"Fasciolopsis buski","E":"Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Paragonimus spp.","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Schistosoma mekongi"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Fasciolopsis is a very large (140 × 85 µm) operculate egg with no spine.","E":"Opisthorchis is a small OPERCULATE egg with a shoulder and a posterior knob, and the disease is biliary, not dysenteric.","A":"Paragonimus eggs are operculate and appear in SPUTUM.","C":"Correct. A large LATERAL spine on a stool egg is S. mansoni, which lives in the inferior mesenteric vein — hence bloody diarrhoea plus liver involvement from eggs embolising to the portal tracts.","D":"S. mekongi is the Mekong species and its egg carries only a SMALL LATERAL KNOB, not a prominent lateral spine — that size difference is the planted near-miss, and it is the species you would expect in a Thai patient. The question hands you 'prominent lateral spine' to force mansoni."},"img":"slides_i/L4_63.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-ov-prev","t":"Opisthorchis — prevention","s":"A 40-year-old man has indigestion, abdominal pain and diarrhoea. Stool analysis shows an ELECTRIC-LIGHT-BULB shaped egg, operculated, with a shoulder and one posterior knob. What is the most appropriate PREVENTION?","lec":"L4","sl":13,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative · parasitology — electric-light-bulb egg, operculate with shoulder and one posterior knob; what is the prevention?</span>","o":{"A":"Consistent use of footwear","B":"Appropriate cooking of freshwater fish","E":"Use of molluscicides","C":"Sanitary disposal of faeces","D":"Washing vegetables before eating"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Footwear prevents hookworm and Strongyloides.","B":"Correct. The egg is Opisthorchis viverrini, acquired by eating raw cyprinoid fish (koi pla). Prevention acts on the person's own exposure — cook the fish.","E":"Molluscicides attack the FIRST intermediate host (Bithynia snail). The examiner's feedback made the distinction explicit: prevention stops this person being infected; CONTROL breaks transmission in the community. Snail killing and sanitary disposal are control.","C":"Sanitary disposal is control of the egg-shedding end of the cycle, not prevention for this patient.","D":"Vegetables carry Ascaris, Trichuris and Fasciolopsis, not Opisthorchis."},"img":"slides_i/L4_13.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L4|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Light-bulb egg without shoulder or knob — where does the adult live?","s":"A 35-year-old man from Nan who often eats koi pla has mild diarrhoea. His stool contains operculated, embryonated, light-bulb-shaped eggs 26 × 12 µm; the operculum sits flush with no raised rim, and no distinct posterior knob is seen. Where do the adults of this parasite live?","lec":"L4","sl":58,"c":"<b>Light-bulb egg without shoulder or knob — where does the adult live?</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In the vesical venous plexus","B":"In the small intestine","C":"In the intrahepatic bile ducts","D":"In the lung parenchyma","E":"In the mesenteric veins"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The vesical venous plexus is S. haematobium, whose egg has a terminal spine and is found in urine.","B":"Correct. Same meal, same size and same light-bulb outline as O. viverrini — but the egg LACKS the prominent shoulder and posterior knob, so it is a small intestinal fluke egg (Haplorchis spp., family Heterophyidae; slide 58). Heterophyids are acquired from raw fish and live in the small intestine. Shoulder + knob obvious = Opisthorchis; faint/absent = small intestinal fluke — the one fluke-egg discrimination she expects you to make.","C":"The bile duct is where O. viverrini lives — the trap for anyone who stops at 'koi pla + light-bulb egg'.","D":"The lung is Paragonimus, whose egg is 68–118 µm and unembryonated.","E":"Mesenteric veins are Schistosoma, whose eggs are non-operculated."},"img":"slides_i/L4_58.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A stool contains a tiny operculated embryonated egg about 28 µm long with an 'electric light bulb' outline. Under oil it LACKS a prominent shoulder around the operculum and LACKS a distinct posterior knob. What should be reported?","o":{"B":"Small intestinal fluke egg (Haplorchis spp.) — the absent shoulder and knob separate it from Opisthorchis viverrini","A":"Opisthorchis viverrini egg — the electric-light-bulb appearance is specific to it","D":"Large intestinal fluke egg — Fasciolopsis buski has the same outline at this size","E":"Paragonimus spp. egg — the operculum and the small size fit","C":"Schistosoma mekongi egg — its minute lateral knob is easily missed"},"r":{"B":"Correct. This is the highest-yield egg pair in the lecture: BOTH are bulb-shaped, embryonated and operculate, and only the SHOULDER and the posterior KNOB discriminate. Their absence means a heterophyid.","A":"O. viverrini has a well-developed operculum WITH a shoulder and a posterior knob — the stem removes exactly those two features.","D":"F. buski eggs are enormous (135–190 µm), unembryonated, and have a small indistinct operculum.","E":"Paragonimus eggs are 68–118 µm, UNembryonated, and have a thickened posterior end rather than a knob.","C":"Schistosome eggs are NON-operculated — that alone excludes every schistosome here."},"t":"Electric-light-bulb egg pair","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L4_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Electric-light-bulb egg pair</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Terminal-spined egg — which snail?","s":"A 23-year-old volunteer returns from two months working on irrigation canals in Egypt with painless haematuria. Urine sediment shows a non-operculated embryonated egg 150 µm long with a spine at one end. Which snail transmitted this infection?","lec":"L4","sl":61,"c":"<b>Terminal-spined egg — which snail?</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Neotricula aperta","B":"Biomphalaria spp.","C":"Bulinus truncatus","D":"Bithynia spp.","E":"Oncomelania spp."},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Neotricula aperta transmits S. mekongi in the Mekong basin.","B":"Biomphalaria transmits S. mansoni, whose egg has a LATERAL spine and is found in faeces — the Egypt near-miss.","C":"Correct. Terminal spine + urine + painless haematuria = Schistosoma haematobium, whose only intermediate host is Bulinus truncatus (slide 61). Egypt fits both haematobium and mansoni, so geography alone cannot decide — the spine position does.","D":"Bithynia is the first intermediate host of Opisthorchis viverrini.","E":"Oncomelania transmits S. japonicum (China, Japan, Philippines, Indonesia)."},"img":"slides_i/L4_61.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A urine specimen contains a non-operculated embryonated egg with a TERMINAL spine. Which trio is correct for this species?","o":{"C":"Schistosoma haematobium — snail host Bulinus truncatus, adults in the vesical venous plexus, presenting with painless haematuria and a risk of bladder cancer","D":"Schistosoma mansoni — snail host Biomphalaria spp., adults in the mesenteric veins of the large intestine, presenting with intestinal bleeding","B":"Schistosoma haematobium — snail host Biomphalaria spp., adults in the mesenteric veins, presenting with bloody diarrhoea","A":"Schistosoma japonicum — snail host Oncomelania spp., adults in the mesenteric veins of the small intestine, presenting with haematuria","E":"Schistosoma mekongi — snail host Neotricula aperta, adults in the bladder plexus, presenting with haematuria"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the chain is worth memorising as a unit: terminal spine → urine → Bulinus → bladder plexus → painless haematuria → bladder cancer.","D":"S. mansoni has a LATERAL spine and is found in FAECES, not urine. Everything else in the option is internally consistent, which is what makes it the near-miss.","B":"Species right, snail and habitat imported from S. mansoni — the classic half-right answer.","A":"S. japonicum has a small LATERAL KNOB, not a terminal spine, and causes intestinal, not urinary, disease.","E":"S. mekongi has a MINUTE lateral knob and lives in mesenteric veins; no schistosome but haematobium occupies the bladder plexus."},"t":"Schistosome egg + snail + organ","sl":61,"img":"slides_i/L4_61.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Schistosome egg + snail + organ</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Schistosoma — infection without swimming","s":"A 9-year-old boy from Khong Island in southern Laos has never swum in the Mekong, but his family drinks untreated river water. He has hepatosplenomegaly, and his stool shows round non-operculated eggs about 60 µm across with a minute lateral knob. How did this parasite most likely enter his body?","lec":"L4","sl":64,"c":"<b>Schistosoma — infection without swimming</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Swallowed embryonated eggs hatched in his intestine","B":"Swallowed cercariae matured in his intestinal lumen","C":"Miracidia in the water penetrated his stomach wall","D":"Cercariae penetrated his oral and lip mucosa","E":"Swallowed metacercariae excysted in his duodenum"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Schistosome eggs hatch into miracidia that infect SNAILS; humans are not infected by eggs.","B":"Cercariae do not develop in the gut lumen; they penetrate tissue, become schistosomula and travel in the blood to the mesenteric veins.","C":"Miracidia infect the snail intermediate host, never a human.","D":"Correct. Mekong + minute lateral knob + smallest egg (51–73 µm) = Schistosoma mekongi. Every schistosome enters by penetration of fork-tailed cercariae, and slide 69 says to drink safe water because the mouth or lips can be infected — so drinking is still a penetration route, not ingestion of a stage that develops in the gut.","E":"Metacercariae belong to the food-borne flukes; schistosomes have no metacercarial stage."},"img":"slides_i/L4_64.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about how the flukes in this lecture are acquired is correct?","o":{"D":"Opisthorchis, Paragonimus and Fasciolopsis are all food-borne and their infective stage is the METACERCARIA; Schistosoma is water-borne and its infective stage is the FORK-TAILED CERCARIA, which penetrates skin","E":"All four are food-borne, and all four are acquired as metacercariae","B":"Schistosoma is food-borne from raw snails; the other three are acquired by skin penetration","C":"All four infect by skin penetration; only the vehicle differs","A":"Schistosoma's infective stage is the miracidium, which penetrates the skin during wading"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and it is the single structural fact that organises the whole lecture — one fluke behaves differently from the rest in stage, route AND transmission.","E":"Schistosoma has no metacercarial stage at all; it never encysts on food.","B":"Precisely inverted.","C":"Only Schistosoma penetrates skin. Opisthorchis, Paragonimus and Fasciolopsis are swallowed.","A":"The miracidium is what hatches from the egg and infects the SNAIL. The stage that infects humans is the fork-tailed cercaria."},"t":"Infective stage — the odd one out","sl":59,"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Infective stage — the odd one out</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Praziquantel — community regimen for opisthorchiasis","s":"Stool screening in a Sakon Nakhon village finds 28% of adults passing small operculated eggs with a shoulder and a posterior knob. The district plans to treat the whole community in a single visit. Which regimen from the lecture fits this plan?","lec":"L4","sl":24,"c":"<b>Praziquantel — community regimen for opisthorchiasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Praziquantel 10–15 mg/kg once, before bedtime","B":"Praziquantel 25 mg/kg three times in one day","C":"Praziquantel 40 mg/kg once, before bedtime","D":"Praziquantel 25 mg/kg three times daily for 2 days","E":"Albendazole 400 mg once daily for 3 days"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"10–15 mg/kg single dose at bedtime is the Hymenolepis regimen from L3 (slide 31 there).","B":"25 mg/kg three times for one day is the regimen for an individual patient (slide 24) — right drug, wrong setting.","C":"Correct. Shoulder + knob = Opisthorchis viverrini. Slide 24 (new 2569): in the community, praziquantel 40 mg/kg orally as a single dose before bedtime — one supervised dose is what a mass campaign can deliver.","D":"25 mg/kg three times daily for TWO days is the Paragonimus regimen in her teaching document.","E":"Albendazole is a nematode / neurocysticercosis drug; praziquantel is used for every fluke in this lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which praziquantel regimen for opisthorchiasis matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"An individual patient gets 25 mg/kg orally three times in one day; a community mass-treatment programme uses 40 mg/kg as a single dose before bedtime","D":"An individual patient gets 40 mg/kg as a single dose; community programmes use 25 mg/kg three times a day for three days","B":"Both settings use 25 mg/kg as a single dose","A":"Both settings use 10–15 mg/kg as a single dose before bedtime","E":"Individuals get albendazole 400 mg daily for three days; only communities receive praziquantel"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The logic is practical: a single supervised dose is what a mass campaign can deliver, so the dose is raised to compensate.","D":"The two regimens are swapped, and the duration is stretched — the commonest way this is misremembered.","B":"25 mg/kg is a THREE-times-a-day dose, not a single dose.","A":"10–15 mg/kg at bedtime is the HYMENOLEPIS regimen from L3.","E":"Albendazole is not the drug for flukes; praziquantel is used in both settings."},"t":"Praziquantel dosing — Opisthorchis","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Praziquantel dosing — Opisthorchis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Opisthorchis — primary vs secondary prevention grid","s":"A 52-year-old woman from Roi Et has twice been treated for a fluke whose small eggs carry a shoulder and a posterior knob. She now attends regular abdominal ultrasound so that a bile-duct tumour can be found early. In the lecture's prevention grid, what does this ultrasound represent?","lec":"L4","sl":24,"c":"<b>Opisthorchis — primary vs secondary prevention grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Primary prevention of opisthorchiasis","B":"Control of opisthorchiasis in the community","C":"Secondary prevention of opisthorchiasis","D":"Primary prevention of cholangiocarcinoma","E":"Secondary prevention of cholangiocarcinoma"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Primary prevention of opisthorchiasis is appropriate cooking of freshwater (cyprinoid) fish — and 'cook cyprinoid fish' is the precise wording she marks; generic slogans such as 'กินร้อน ช้อนกลาง ล้างมือ' score zero.","B":"Control is sanitary disposal, treatment of infected people and reservoir hosts, and decreasing snail hosts — community measures, not imaging of one person.","C":"Secondary prevention of opisthorchiasis is stool examination every 6 months in endemic areas plus treatment of infected individuals.","D":"Primary prevention of CCA is treating opisthorchiasis — which is what her two previous courses of praziquantel were.","E":"Correct. Slide 24 splits prevention in two. Cholangiocarcinoma: primary = treatment of opisthorchiasis patients; secondary = ultrasound or CT scan. Imaging aims to catch the complication early, so it is secondary prevention of the cancer."},"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"The deck separates prevention of opisthorchiasis from prevention of cholangiocarcinoma. Which cell of that grid is stated correctly?","o":{"E":"Secondary prevention of cholangiocarcinoma = ultrasound or CT surveillance, because the aim is to catch the tumour early rather than to stop the fluke","B":"Primary prevention of cholangiocarcinoma = cooking freshwater fish properly","D":"Secondary prevention of opisthorchiasis = ultrasound or CT surveillance","A":"Primary prevention of opisthorchiasis = treating patients who already have opisthorchiasis","C":"There is no secondary prevention for either, because cholangiocarcinoma takes more than 20 years to develop"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Secondary prevention always means detecting the complication early; for CCA that is imaging.","B":"Cooking the fish is primary prevention of the INFECTION. Primary prevention of CCA is treating opisthorchiasis patients.","D":"Secondary prevention of the infection is six-monthly stool examination in endemic areas plus treating those found positive.","A":"Treating infected individuals is primary prevention of the CANCER, not of the infection.","C":"The 20-year latency is exactly why surveillance imaging is worth doing; the deck lists secondary prevention for both."},"t":"Opisthorchis — the prevention grid","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L4_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Opisthorchis — the prevention grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Paragonimus — the TB mimic: which specimen confirms it","s":"A 38-year-old man from Nakhon Nayok has had cough, chest pain, night sweats and weight loss for four months, with blood-tinged sputum. Two sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli are negative. He catches crabs by the waterfalls and eats them raw in som tam. Which specimen and finding would confirm the diagnosis?","lec":"L4","sl":37,"c":"<b>Paragonimus — the TB mimic: which specimen confirms it</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Early-morning sputum with embryonated eggs bearing a knob","B":"Urine sediment with non-operculated eggs bearing a spine","C":"Early-morning sputum with unembryonated operculated eggs","D":"Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid with fork-tailed cercariae","E":"Stool concentrate with embryonated eggs bearing a shoulder"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Right specimen, wrong egg: Paragonimus eggs are passed UNembryonated and have a thickened posterior end, not a knob.","B":"Non-operculated spined eggs in urine are S. haematobium.","C":"Correct. Chronic TB-like picture (slide 37) + raw freshwater crab = pulmonary paragonimiasis. Slide 38: sputum examination for unembryonated eggs, detected in MORNING sputum; the egg is operculated with a thickened posterior end (slide 33). The teaching document adds digestion in 10% KOH and centrifugation.","D":"Fork-tailed cercariae are the free-swimming schistosome stage in water; they are never recovered from the lung.","E":"An embryonated egg with a shoulder is O. viverrini, a bile-duct fluke that does not cause a TB-like illness. (Paragonimus eggs can appear in stool if sputum is swallowed, but they are unembryonated.)"},"img":"slides_i/L4_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A man from Nakhon Nayok has months of cough, chest pain, night sweats, weight loss and rusty-brown sputum. Two sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli are negative. What is the next best test and what would it show?","o":{"C":"Examine a MORNING sputum sample for unembryonated operculate eggs with a thickened posterior end — Paragonimus, acquired from raw mountain crab","D":"Examine urine for non-operculated eggs with a terminal spine","B":"Examine stool for embryonated eggs with a shoulder and a posterior knob","A":"Serology only, because Paragonimus eggs never appear in sputum","E":"Bronchoalveolar lavage for fork-tailed cercariae"},"r":{"C":"Correct on all three: the specimen (morning sputum), the egg description, and the exposure. Eggs also appear in stool because the patient swallows sputum.","D":"That describes S. haematobium in urine — wrong organ, wrong egg, wrong disease.","B":"Shoulder + knob + embryonated is OPISTHORCHIS, a biliary fluke — it does not cause a TB-like lung picture.","A":"Serology is listed, but specifically for EXTRAPULMONARY paragonimiasis. Sputum microscopy is the first-line test here.","E":"Cercariae are the schistosome stage and are never found in sputum."},"t":"Paragonimus — the TB mimic","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L4_33.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Paragonimus — the TB mimic</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Paragonimus — extrapulmonary disease needs serology","s":"A 30-year-old man from Phetchabun who eats raw freshwater crab develops new seizures. CT shows cystic lesions in the cerebral cortex, and repeated sputum and stool examinations find no eggs. Which investigation does the lecture list for this form of his disease?","lec":"L4","sl":37,"c":"<b>Paragonimus — extrapulmonary disease needs serology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Urine antigen strip for the liver fluke","B":"Formalin ethyl-acetate stool concentration","C":"Repeated early-morning sputum microscopy","D":"Cerebrospinal fluid examination for larvae","E":"Serology for antigen and antibody"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The urine OV-RDT detects O. viverrini antigen (slide 22), a bile-duct fluke from fish.","B":"Stool concentration finds swallowed Paragonimus eggs only in pulmonary disease — and it is the best method for O. viverrini, not this case.","C":"Morning sputum is the test for PULMONARY disease, and it has already been negative.","D":"CSF examination for larvae belongs to eosinophilic meningitis from Angiostrongylus (L2), acquired from snails, not crab.","E":"Correct. Raw crab + brain lesions = extrapulmonary paragonimiasis; the brain is the most common extrapulmonary site (slide 37). With no adult in the lung there are no eggs to find, so slide 38 lists serological techniques (Ag & Ab detection) specifically for extrapulmonary paragonimiasis."},"img":"slides_i/L4_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A stool shows a very large (about 160 µm), symmetrically oval, thin-walled, brown-yellow UNembryonated egg with a small indistinct operculum. How should this be reported and why?","o":{"E":"'Large intestinal fluke egg' — Fasciolopsis buski, Fasciola spp. and Echinostoma spp. eggs are morphologically similar, so species cannot be given from the egg alone","D":"'Fasciolopsis buski egg' — the indistinct operculum is unique to this species","B":"'Small intestinal fluke egg' — size is not a reportable criterion","C":"'Opisthorchis viverrini egg' — all operculate eggs in Thai stool are reported this way","A":"'Schistosoma mansoni egg' — the thin wall and large size fit"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The deck states the similarity twice, once under F. buski and once under Echinostoma, and gives the genus-level report explicitly.","D":"The indistinct operculum is shared across the group — it is a reason NOT to name the species.","B":"Small intestinal fluke eggs are 22–32 µm and embryonated; this one is five times larger and unembryonated.","C":"O. viverrini eggs are 25 µm and embryonated. Reporting by habit rather than by morphology is exactly the error being tested.","A":"Schistosome eggs are NON-operculated and carry a spine or knob."},"t":"Large intestinal fluke egg — report it right","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L4_47.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Large intestinal fluke egg — report it right</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Schistosoma — Katayama fever mechanism","s":"A 26-year-old Thai tourist swam in Lake Malawi and had an itchy papular rash that evening. Five weeks later she develops fever, urticaria, cough and marked eosinophilia, while stool and urine still show no eggs. What is the mechanism of her current illness?","lec":"L4","sl":65,"c":"<b>Schistosoma — Katayama fever mechanism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hypersensitivity to cercariae penetrating the skin surface","B":"Allergy to metabolites absorbed from adult intestinal worms","C":"Antigen–antibody complexes in a host with no prior exposure","D":"Granulomas around eggs trapped in the liver and gut wall","E":"Larvae migrating through the alveoli on their way to the gut"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"That was the rash on the evening of the swim — swimmer's itch. The question asks about the illness five weeks later.","B":"Absorbed worm metabolites causing allergic swelling is Fasciolopsis buski (slide 50).","C":"Correct. Freshwater lake + immediate itchy rash (swimmer's itch / cercarial dermatitis) = schistosome cercariae. Fever, urticaria and eosinophilia 2–8 weeks later in a non-immune person is Katayama fever, 'caused by Ag–Ab complex' (slide 65).","D":"Egg granulomas and fibrosis are CHRONIC schistosomiasis (slides 65, 67); no eggs are yet being passed.","E":"Larvae passing through the alveoli to reach the gut (Loeffler's syndrome) is the Ascaris / hookworm story from L1."},"img":"slides_i/L4_65.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A traveller who swam in Lake Malawi develops an itchy rash within hours, then six weeks later has fever, urticaria and eosinophilia. What are these two events?","o":{"C":"Cercarial dermatitis (swimmer's itch) at penetration, then Katayama fever — an antigen–antibody complex illness appearing 2–8 weeks later in a non-immune host","B":"Cercarial dermatitis, then chronic hepatosplenic schistosomiasis with portal hypertension","A":"Ground itch from hookworm larvae, then Loeffler's syndrome","D":"Larva currens from Strongyloides, then hyperinfection","E":"Katayama fever at penetration, then cercarial dermatitis as the eggs begin to be laid"},"r":{"C":"Correct on mechanism and on timing — the 2–8 week window and the immune-complex mechanism are both stated on the slide.","B":"Hepatosplenic disease is CHRONIC, driven by egg granulomas and fibrosis over years, not six weeks.","A":"Ground itch and Loeffler's are the hookworm story from L1; this patient's exposure is freshwater swimming.","D":"Larva currens is Strongyloides, and there is no soil contact in the history.","E":"The sequence is inverted — the dermatitis is immediate, the fever is delayed."},"t":"Katayama fever","sl":65,"img":"slides_i/L4_65.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Katayama fever</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Fasciolopsis — why the pig farm matters","s":"In an Ayutthaya district, children who eat raw water chestnuts pass large unembryonated eggs with an indistinct operculum. Treating every infected child has not stopped new cases, and the ponds where the chestnuts grow receive run-off from pig pens. Why does the pig run-off matter?","lec":"L4","sl":48,"c":"<b>Fasciolopsis — why the pig farm matters</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pigs are definitive hosts, so their faeces add eggs to the ponds","B":"Pigs are reservoir hosts that carry metacercariae in their muscle","C":"Pigs shed cercariae in their urine, which encyst on the plants","D":"Pigs are second intermediate hosts that carry cysts in their gut","E":"Pigs are first intermediate hosts in which the rediae multiply"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Raw water chestnut + central Thailand + large unembryonated egg = Fasciolopsis buski. Humans AND pigs are its definitive hosts (slides 48, 53), and slide 46 links prevalence to growing water plants and feeding pigs on them — pig faeces reaching the pond seed the snails. This is the NL slide 54 scenario.","B":"Pigs harbour ADULT worms in the small intestine; metacercariae sit on the plant surface, and eating pork does not transmit F. buski.","C":"Cercariae come from the snail, not from a mammal, and no stage leaves in urine.","D":"The second intermediate hosts are the aquatic plants themselves — water chestnut, water caltrop, water mimosa.","E":"The first intermediate host is a freshwater snail (Segmentina spp., slide 53)."},"img":"slides_i/L4_48.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A woman from Suphan Buri who eats raw water caltrop grown in a pond beside a pig farm has abdominal discomfort and now facial and leg swelling. Which chain is correct?","o":{"A":"Fasciolopsis buski — freshwater snail (Segmentina) 1st IH, the aquatic plant itself is the 2nd IH carrying metacercariae, humans and PIGS are definitive hosts, and the swelling reflects an allergic reaction to absorbed worm metabolites","E":"Fasciolopsis buski — the pig is the intermediate host and the plant merely carries eggs; the swelling is from protein loss","B":"Opisthorchis viverrini — the plants are contaminated by fish-eating cats; the swelling is from hypoalbuminaemia","D":"Schistosoma mekongi — pond water is the exposure and the swelling is from portal hypertension","C":"Paragonimus westermani — crabs in the same pond are the source and the swelling is allergic"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and the pig detail is the point of the vignette: pigs are a true definitive host, which is why a pig farm beside the pond sustains transmission.","E":"The pig is a DEFINITIVE host, not an intermediate one, and a plant carries metacercariae, not eggs.","B":"Opisthorchis comes from raw cyprinoid FISH, not aquatic plants.","D":"Schistosome infection is by skin penetration while in the water, not by eating plants, and the history here is eating.","C":"Paragonimus comes from crab or crayfish and lodges in the lung."},"t":"Fasciolopsis — the whole chain","sl":46,"img":"slides_i/L4_46.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Fasciolopsis — the whole chain</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Schistosoma — what causes the portal hypertension","s":"A 50-year-old rice farmer from Mindoro, the Philippines, has splenomegaly, ascites and oesophageal varices, yet his liver enzymes are near normal. Stool shows round non-operculated eggs about 85 µm across with a small lateral knob. What produces his portal hypertension?","lec":"L4","sl":67,"c":"<b>Schistosoma — what causes the portal hypertension</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Periductal fibrosis from flukes grazing the bile ducts","B":"Immune complexes deposited during the acute phase","C":"Granulomas and fibrosis around liver-trapped eggs","D":"Hepatocyte necrosis caused by migrating juvenile worms","E":"Adult worms mechanically obstructing the portal vein"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Periductal fibrosis from sucker damage is Opisthorchis (slide 20).","B":"Immune complexes are the mechanism of acute Katayama fever (slide 65), not of chronic portal hypertension.","C":"Correct. Philippines + small lateral knob = S. japonicum. In schistosomiasis the disease is the host reaction to EGGS: eggs swept to the liver induce granuloma and fibrosis, raising portal venous pressure → hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, ascites (slide 67). Hepatocytes are relatively spared, which is why the enzymes can be near normal (teaching doc: periportal / Symmers' pipestem fibrosis).","D":"No schistosome stage destroys hepatocytes by migration; the lesion is periovular granuloma.","E":"The adults live in the mesenteric veins but do not physically block the portal vein; the fibrosis around eggs does."},"img":"slides_i/L4_67.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about chronic schistosomiasis is correct?","o":{"C":"Eggs trapped in the liver induce granuloma and fibrosis, raising portal venous pressure and producing hepatomegaly, splenomegaly and ascites; eggs reaching the lung can cause pulmonary hypertension","B":"The adult worms themselves obstruct the portal vein, which is what raises the pressure","E":"Liver damage comes from the fluke feeding on hepatocytes, as in Opisthorchis","D":"Ascites in schistosomiasis is caused by protein-losing enteropathy","A":"Pulmonary involvement occurs only in S. haematobium, because it is the species closest to the venous return"},"r":{"C":"Correct. In schistosomiasis the pathology is the immune reaction to trapped EGGS, not to the worms — that is the concept the question is built on.","B":"Adults live in the veins but do not mechanically obstruct the portal system; the fibrosis around eggs does.","E":"Opisthorchis damages BILE DUCT epithelium; no schistosome feeds on hepatocytes.","D":"Protein-losing enteropathy is Capillaria philippinensis, from L1.","A":"Pulmonary schistosomiasis is listed generally, not restricted to one species."},"t":"Where the eggs jam","sl":66,"img":"slides_i/L4_66.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Where the eggs jam</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Echinostoma — how its egg is reported","s":"After praziquantel, a man from Udon Thani passes several flukes about 1 cm long whose heads carry a collar with a horseshoe-shaped row of spines around the oral sucker. Had his stool been examined before treatment, how should the eggs have been reported?","lec":"L4","sl":56,"c":"<b>Echinostoma — how its egg is reported</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fasciolopsis buski egg","B":"Medium intestinal fluke egg","C":"Echinostoma spp. egg","D":"Small intestinal fluke egg","E":"Large intestinal fluke egg"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Same problem — the egg does not allow F. buski to be named; the adult here is not F. buski (no collar spines, >2 cm).","B":"'Medium' classifies the adult worm, not the egg; there is no 'medium intestinal fluke egg' report in the lecture.","C":"The species cannot be named from the egg, because Echinostoma, F. buski and Fasciola eggs look alike.","D":"Small intestinal fluke eggs are the 22–32 µm light-bulb eggs of Haplorchis (slide 58).","E":"Correct — and it is a precision trap. The 1-cm adult with horseshoe collar spines is Echinostoma spp., a MEDIUM intestinal fluke by adult size (0.5–2 cm, slides 43, 55). But its egg is similar to those of Fasciolopsis buski and Fasciola spp., so slide 56 says 'Report: Large intestinal fluke egg'. The size class of the adult does not carry over to the egg report."},"img":"slides_i/L4_56.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"An adult intestinal fluke about 1 cm long has a head collar bearing a horseshoe-shaped row of spines around the oral sucker. How is it classified and named?","o":{"E":"A MEDIUM intestinal fluke, Echinostoma spp. — the collar spines are its defining feature","A":"A LARGE intestinal fluke, Fasciolopsis buski — collar spines are present in all large flukes","C":"A SMALL intestinal fluke, Haplorchis spp. — spines mark the heterophyids","D":"A liver fluke, Opisthorchis viverrini — the collar is the ventral sucker seen obliquely","B":"A blood fluke, Schistosoma japonicum — the spines correspond to the egg's lateral knob"},"r":{"E":"Correct on both counts. The size band (0.5–2 cm) and the horseshoe collar spines are the two things the slide gives you.","A":"F. buski is a LARGE fluke, over 2 cm, and has no collar spines.","C":"Haplorchis species are under 0.5 cm — this worm is twice that at minimum.","D":"Opisthorchis is a leaf-like liver fluke with no collar spines at all.","B":"Schistosomes are blood flukes living in veins and have no circumoral disc."},"t":"Echinostoma — the collar","sl":43,"img":"slides_i/L4_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Echinostoma — the collar</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Paired worms in the veins — what are their eggs like?","s":"At autopsy of a Cambodian fisherman, pairs of worms are found in the mesenteric veins: a slender worm lies in a ventral groove running along a shorter, stouter one. Which description fits the eggs this parasite passes?","lec":"L4","sl":59,"c":"<b>Paired worms in the veins — what are their eggs like?</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Operculated and still unembryonated","B":"Operculated with a thickened posterior end","C":"Non-operculated and still unembryonated","D":"Operculated, embryonated, with a shoulder","E":"Non-operculated and already embryonated"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Operculated and unembryonated fits Fasciolopsis / the large intestinal fluke egg.","B":"Operculated with a thickened posterior end is Paragonimus (slide 33).","C":"Right about the operculum, wrong about development: schistosome eggs already contain a miracidium when passed.","D":"Operculated, embryonated, with a shoulder is O. viverrini.","E":"Correct. A female held in the male's gynecophoric canal (slide 59) means Schistosoma — the only dioecious fluke in the lecture (the others, e.g. O. viverrini, are monoecious). All schistosome eggs are non-operculated and embryonated, with the miracidium inside (slides 63–64). In Cambodia this would be S. mekongi."},"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which feature distinguishes adult Schistosoma from the other trematodes in this lecture?","o":{"E":"The sexes are separate, and the male carries the slender female in a ventral gynecophoric canal; the other flukes are monoecious (hermaphroditic)","A":"Schistosoma is monoecious while the other flukes have separate sexes","B":"Schistosoma has no suckers, which is how it stays in the bloodstream","C":"Schistosoma has a stichosome oesophagus, unlike the other flukes","D":"Schistosoma is leaf-shaped with diagonal multilobed testes"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Schistosoma is the only dioecious fluke here, and the gynecophoric canal — the groove in which the male holds the female — is what the adult-worm slide is drawn to show.","A":"Inverted. Opisthorchis is explicitly labelled MONOECIOUS on its morphology slide; Schistosoma is the exception with separate sexes.","B":"Both oral and ventral suckers are labelled on the schistosome slide.","C":"A stichosome oesophagus belongs to Capillaria philippinensis, a nematode from L1.","D":"Leaf-shaped with diagonal multilobed testes and columnar vitelline glands is OPISTHORCHIS VIVERRINI."},"t":"Schistosome adult anatomy","sl":59,"img":"slides_i/L4_59.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Schistosome adult anatomy</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Schistosomiasis — prevention for one person vs control","s":"A Thai nurse will spend a year at a clinic beside Lake Victoria, where many patients pass faecal eggs with a lateral spine. Which measure from the lecture is prevention for her personally, rather than a control measure?","lec":"L4","sl":69,"c":"<b>Schistosomiasis — prevention for one person vs control</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Joining community mass treatment with praziquantel","B":"Wearing shoes whenever walking on moist soil","C":"Cooking freshwater fish thoroughly before eating","D":"Boiling bathing water for one minute before use","E":"Eliminating the snail hosts along the lakeshore"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Community mass drug treatment is listed under CONTROL on slide 69 — the prevention/control split was made explicit in the BM30 formative feedback.","B":"Footwear prevents hookworm and Strongyloides, whose larvae live in soil (L1).","C":"Cooking freshwater fish prevents opisthorchiasis (and the L3 fish tapeworm); schistosomes are not eaten.","D":"Correct. Lateral spine in faeces = S. mansoni, acquired by cercarial skin penetration. Slide 69 prevention: avoid swimming/wading in fresh water in endemic areas, boil water before bathing for 1 minute to kill cercariae, drink safe water.","E":"Snail control/elimination is also CONTROL on slide 69."},"img":"slides_i/L4_69.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which prevention advice for schistosomiasis matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"Avoid swimming and wading in freshwater in endemic areas, boil water for one minute before bathing to kill cercariae, and drink safe water because the mouth and lips can be a portal","A":"Cook freshwater fish thoroughly and freeze it below −10 °C","C":"Wear shoes and gloves when working in soil","E":"Avoid raw crab and crayfish","B":"Take praziquantel prophylactically before every freshwater exposure"},"r":{"D":"Correct on all three points, including the detail that drinking unsafe water matters because cercariae can penetrate the oral mucosa.","A":"Cooking and freezing fish is Diphyllobothrium and Opisthorchis advice; schistosomes are not eaten.","C":"Shoes and gloves block soil-dwelling nematode larvae, not water-dwelling cercariae.","E":"Raw crab is the PARAGONIMUS exposure.","B":"Praziquantel is used for treatment and for community mass drug administration, not as pre-exposure prophylaxis."},"t":"Schistosomiasis prevention","sl":69,"img":"slides_i/L4_69.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Schistosomiasis prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Food → organ summary slide, entered from the egg","s":"A stool from a woman in Nakhon Pathom contains a 160 × 85 µm, thin-walled, symmetrically oval unembryonated egg with an indistinct operculum. Using the lecturer's closing summary slide, which exposure-to-habitat pair explains her infection?","lec":"L4","sl":72,"c":"<b>Food → organ summary slide, entered from the egg</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cercarial skin penetration → blood vessels","B":"Raw cyprinoid fish → small intestine","C":"Raw cyprinoid fish → bile duct","D":"Raw water caltrop → small intestine","E":"Raw mountain crab → lung"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Skin penetration → blood is Schistosoma, whose eggs are non-operculated with a spine or knob.","B":"Fish → small intestine is the heterophyid small intestinal flukes, whose eggs are 22–32 µm light bulbs.","C":"Fish → bile duct is O. viverrini, whose egg is 25 × 12 µm and embryonated.","D":"Correct. A 135–190 µm unembryonated egg with an indistinct operculum is the large intestinal fluke egg; in central Thailand that is Fasciolopsis buski, from raw water chestnut / water caltrop (แห้ว กระจับ → ลำไส้, slide 72). Every option is a true pair; only the egg decides.","E":"Crab → lung is Paragonimus, whose egg is 68–118 µm with a thickened posterior end and is found mainly in sputum."},"img":"slides_i/L4_72.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which food-to-organ pairing from the wrap-up slide is correct?","o":{"D":"Raw crab → lung (Paragonimus); raw cyprinoid fish → bile duct (Opisthorchis); raw water chestnut or caltrop → small intestine (Fasciolopsis); skin penetration in water → blood vessels (Schistosoma)","B":"Raw crab → bile duct; raw fish → lung; water chestnut → blood vessels; skin penetration → small intestine","C":"Raw crab → small intestine; raw fish → lung; water chestnut → bile duct; skin penetration → blood vessels","A":"All four are acquired by eating, and all four end up in the intestine","E":"Raw fish → blood vessels; raw crab → small intestine; water chestnut → lung; skin penetration → bile duct"},"r":{"D":"Correct — this single slide is the fastest way to answer any 'which food / which organ' stem in the paper.","B":"Crab and fish are swapped, and the last two are scrambled.","C":"Crab and water chestnut are swapped; crab is the lung fluke.","A":"Schistosoma is NOT acquired by eating, and each fluke has its own habitat.","E":"Every pairing here is wrong."},"t":"Food → organ mapping","sl":72,"img":"slides_i/L4_72.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Food → organ mapping</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L4|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Urine OV-RDT (new 2569 slide)","s":"A screening team in Kalasin finds that many villagers refuse to collect stool but will give urine. Which test shown in the lecture could screen them for the liver fluke acquired from koi pla, and what does it detect?","lec":"L4","sl":22,"c":"<b>Urine OV-RDT (new 2569 slide)</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"An OV-RDT strip detecting anti-fluke IgG","B":"Urine filtration detecting the fluke eggs","C":"OV-RDT strip detecting parasite antigen","D":"Urine ELISA detecting cercarial antibody","E":"Urine sediment detecting shouldered eggs"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The strip detects ANTIGEN. Antibody detection for O. viverrini is ELISA on serum/blood (slide 22).","B":"O. viverrini eggs leave in bile and faeces, never in urine; urine filtration is the S. haematobium method.","C":"Correct. Slide 22 (new 2569): 'Urine OV strip: OV-RDT (Ag detection)' — her petty-patent application 2303000815; the teaching document gives a sensitivity of 94.2% and specificity of 93.2%. The same slide shows a LAMP kit (OV-V-Tect) with a lateral-flow dipstick that reads O. viverrini positive and minute intestinal fluke negative.","D":"Humans are not infected by cercariae of O. viverrini (they infect fish); the urine test is an antigen test.","E":"Shouldered eggs are found in stool, not urine."},"img":"slides_i/L4_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which laboratory approach to opisthorchiasis is listed in the lecture?","o":{"C":"A urine OV-RDT strip that detects Opisthorchis ANTIGEN, used alongside stool microscopy, ELISA antibody detection and PCR","A":"Urine microscopy for Opisthorchis eggs, which are excreted renally","B":"Sputum examination, because the fluke migrates through the lung","D":"Blood film examination for microfilaria-like larvae","E":"Only stool microscopy — no serological or molecular test exists for this fluke"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the distinction matters: the urine test detects ANTIGEN, not eggs. The slide even carries the Thai petty-patent number.","A":"Opisthorchis eggs are shed in bile into the gut and appear in STOOL; they are not filtered into urine.","B":"The lung route belongs to Paragonimus.","D":"Blood films are for filariasis and malaria.","E":"ELISA and PCR are both listed explicitly."},"t":"Urine OV-RDT","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L4_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Urine OV-RDT</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233909975},{"id":"I|L5|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Mode of infection — E. histolytica","s":"A 32-year-old man has had lower abdominal pain, tenesmus and mucous bloody stools for a week. A fresh saline mount shows amoebae with finger-like pseudopodia moving rapidly in one direction, several of them containing red blood cells. On the short-answer paper you are asked for this organism's mode of infection. Which answer earns the mark?","lec":"L5","sl":11,"c":"<b>Mode of infection — E. histolytica</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of mature cysts with four nuclei in contaminated food or water","B":"Ingestion of trophozoites carrying red cells in contaminated food or water","C":"Ingestion of immature cysts with one or two nuclei in contaminated food or water","D":"Ingestion of mature cysts with a kidney-shaped macronucleus in contaminated food or water","E":"Ingestion of mature cysts with eight nuclei in contaminated food or water"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Finger-like pseudopodia, rapid unidirectional movement and ingested RBCs = Entamoeba histolytica. Its infective stage is the mature cyst with 4 nuclei (p.11, p.14, p.20). The lecturer said a bare 'ingestion of cysts' scores zero — the maturity (4 nuclei) is the answer.","B":"The trophozoite is the stage seen in the stool and the one that invades, but it is not the infective stage — the slides give only the mature cyst.","C":"Fewer than 4 nuclei = immature cyst, which is not infective (p.11: '<4 nuclei = immature cyst').","D":"A cyst with a kidney-shaped macronucleus is Balantidium coli (p.27).","E":"Eight nuclei is the mature cyst of Entamoeba coli (p.49), the non-pathogenic look-alike."},"img":"slides_i/L5_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Asked for the mode of infection of Entamoeba histolytica, which answer scores full marks?","o":{"E":"Ingestion of cysts","A":"Ingestion of the MATURE cyst with 4 nuclei from contaminated food or water","D":"Ingestion of trophozoites","B":"Faecal–oral transmission","C":"Ingestion of the immature cyst"},"r":{"E":"This is the answer she marks as worth zero. It names the object without naming the stage that matters.","A":"Correct. The lecturer said in class, in so many words, that the short form scores nothing: 'อย่าตอบมาแค่ cyst ไม่ได้คะแนน'. An immature cyst is not infective, so the maturity IS the answer.","D":"Trophozoites are destroyed by gastric acid and are never infective by ingestion — except for Acanthamoeba, which does not use the gut at all.","B":"A route, not a mode of infection.","C":"An immature cyst is not infective, which is exactly why 'mature' must appear."},"t":"Mode of infection","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L5_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Bloody mucoid diarrhoea — three-way answer","s":"A 28-year-old returns from a rural district with lower abdominal pain, tenesmus and mucous bloody diarrhoea. The referral note says only 'dysentery — stool positive for parasites', with no description of what was seen. Which statement about identifying the cause is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Bloody mucoid diarrhoea — three-way answer</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Two protozoa fit this stool, so a history of pig contact must decide between them","B":"Giardia and Entamoeba histolytica fit this stool, so the motility must decide","C":"Entamoeba histolytica and E. coli fit this stool, so the number of cyst nuclei must decide","D":"Three organisms in the block fit this stool, so the parasite's morphology must decide","E":"One organism in the block fits this stool, so treatment can start without the morphology"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"E. histolytica and Balantidium are the two protozoa, but Trichuris is left out, and a pig history only supports Balantidium — the organism seen decides.","B":"Giardia gives watery/fatty diarrhoea WITHOUT blood or mucus (p.62).","C":"Entamoeba coli is non-pathogenic and causes no dysentery (p.45).","D":"Correct. The lecturer counted them out loud: Trichuris trichiura (L1), Entamoeba histolytica and Balantidium coli all give bloody mucoid diarrhoea (dysentery = mucous bloody diarrhoea = bloody stool), and she warned you cannot answer from the stool alone. Look at the organism: RBCs inside an amoeba → E. histolytica; huge ciliated cell with kidney-shaped macronucleus → Balantidium; barrel egg with bipolar plugs → Trichuris.","E":"The commonest misreading — dysentery is not synonymous with amoebiasis."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Three organisms in this block produce bloody mucoid diarrhoea. Which feature identifies which?","o":{"D":"Ingested red blood cells inside the organism = E. histolytica; a large ciliated cell with a kidney-shaped macronucleus = Balantidium coli; a barrel egg with bipolar plugs = Trichuris trichiura","B":"Cilia = E. histolytica; ingested red cells = Balantidium; barrel egg = Giardia","E":"Falling-leaf motility = E. histolytica; ingested red cells = Trichuris; cilia = Balantidium","C":"All three are separable from the stool description alone","A":"Ingested red blood cells = Balantidium; kidney-shaped macronucleus = E. histolytica"},"r":{"D":"Correct. She counted these three off in class and warned that you cannot answer from the stool description — you must look at the ORGANISM in the figure. Note also that the papers rotate the wording: dysentery, mucous bloody diarrhoea and bloody stool all mean the same thing.","B":"Every feature is misassigned; Giardia causes no bloody diarrhoea at all.","E":"Falling-leaf motility is GIARDIA, which is the odd one out here.","C":"The explicit point of her warning was the opposite.","A":"Reversed."},"t":"Bloody diarrhoea","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Bloody diarrhoea</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Acanthamoeba — infective stage and route","s":"An otherwise healthy 21-year-old who wears soft contact lenses and rinses her lens case under the tap develops severe eye pain and photophobia. A corneal scraping shows cysts with a wrinkled outer wall and a polyhedral inner wall. Which stage started this infection, and how did it get in?","lec":"L5","sl":36,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — infective stage and route</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mature cyst — inhaled through the nose, then carried to the eye in blood","B":"Trophozoite — swallowed in tap water, then carried to the eye in blood","C":"Trophozoite — placed directly on the cornea by the lens","D":"Mature cyst — swallowed in tap water, then carried to the eye in blood","E":"Double-walled cyst — placed directly on the cornea by the lens"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The nasal cavity is a real portal, but for GAE, not keratitis — and the entering stage is still the trophozoite.","B":"Right stage, wrong route: entry is directly through the eye, nose or a skin lesion, not the gut.","C":"Correct. Wrinkled ectocyst + polyhedral endocyst = Acanthamoeba keratitis. For Acanthamoeba the TROPHOZOITE is the infective stage (p.36, p.39), entering through the eye via a soft contact lens (p.38). It is the one organism in this lecture where 'mature cyst' is the wrong answer — nothing has to survive a stomach.","D":"This is the faecal–oral template of the other four organisms; Acanthamoeba is not acquired by swallowing.","E":"The cyst is a diagnostic stage (p.37) and survives in the lens case, but the slides name the trophozoite as infective."},"img":"slides_i/L5_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which organism in this lecture has the TROPHOZOITE as its infective stage, and why?","o":{"A":"Entamoeba coli — it is non-pathogenic and therefore not destroyed","E":"Entamoeba histolytica — its trophozoite is acid-resistant","D":"Balantidium coli — its cilia protect it from gastric acid","B":"Giardia intestinalis — its adhesive disc lets it attach before acid exposure","C":"Acanthamoeba — it enters through the eye, nose or broken skin, so nothing has to survive a stomach"},"r":{"A":"Non-pathogenic does not mean acid-resistant; E. coli also infects as a cyst.","E":"E. histolytica trophozoites are destroyed by gastric acid.","D":"Balantidium is acquired as a mature cyst.","B":"Giardia is acquired as a mature cyst.","C":"Correct, and this is the one place 'mature cyst' is the WRONG answer. 'Cyst = infective' is a rule about the FAECAL–ORAL ROUTE, not about protozoa in general — learn the reason and you cannot get it backwards."},"t":"Acanthamoeba","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L5_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Amoebic liver abscess — which test","s":"A 45-year-old man has fever, right upper quadrant pain and hepatomegaly; ultrasound shows a single liver abscess, and aspiration yields thick brown material the colour of shrimp paste. He had mucous bloody diarrhoea two months ago, but three stool examinations are now negative. Which additional test does the lecture list specifically for this form of the disease?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Amoebic liver abscess — which test</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"FECT concentration of a fourth stool sample","B":"Modified AFB stain of a fresh stool sample","C":"Trichrome-stained stool smear for chromatoid bodies","D":"Serum ELISA for the anti-amoebic antibody titre","E":"Iodine wet mount of the aspirate for mature cysts"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"FECT raises the yield of cysts in intestinal disease, but repeating stool tests does not address the extraintestinal site.","B":"Modified acid-fast staining is for the coccidian oocysts of L6 (Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora).","C":"A permanent stain of stool identifies intestinal E. histolytica, but stool is negative here and chromatoid bodies belong to the cyst.","D":"Correct. Anchovy (กะปิ-coloured) pus + RUQ pain = extraintestinal amoebiasis (liver abscess) from Entamoeba histolytica. The organism has left the bowel, so stool is often negative; p.17 lists 'ELISA for E. histolytica titer (Extraintestinal amoebiasis)'.","E":"Cysts form only in the bowel lumen; in the liver, trophozoites are found at the EDGE of the lesion (p.16), not cysts in the pus."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"How do the two clinical forms of amoebiasis differ, and what does each require?","o":{"D":"Lower abdominal pain with tenesmus = intestinal amoebiasis (stool); right upper quadrant pain with hepatomegaly = liver abscess (serology + imaging, and drainage)","A":"Lower abdominal pain = liver abscess; right upper quadrant pain = colitis","B":"Both present identically and are distinguished only by serology","E":"Lower abdominal pain = Balantidium; right upper quadrant pain = E. histolytica","C":"Both are diagnosed by stool examination alone"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Same organism, different disease, different specimen, different management. In extraintestinal disease the stool is often NEGATIVE because the organism has left the bowel.","A":"Reversed anatomy.","B":"The stem chooses the site deliberately, and the site is the discriminator.","E":"Both forms are E. histolytica; Balantidium causes colitis only.","C":"Stool is often negative in liver abscess — that is the trap."},"t":"Amoebiasis sites","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L5_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Amoebiasis sites</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Giardia — mode of infection","s":"A 6-year-old boy has had cramping abdominal pain and foul-smelling, greasy, watery diarrhoea without blood for a month, and he is losing weight. A fresh saline mount shows pear-shaped organisms tumbling like falling leaves. Which mode of infection earns the mark?","lec":"L5","sl":59,"c":"<b>Giardia — mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Drinking water contaminated with mature cysts containing four nuclei","B":"Drinking water contaminated with immature cysts containing two nuclei","C":"Swimming in water where trophozoites enter through the nasal cavity","D":"Drinking water contaminated with trophozoites bearing four pairs of flagella","E":"Eating food contaminated with mature cysts containing eight nuclei"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Child + chronic steatorrhoea without blood + falling-leaf motility = Giardia intestinalis. Infective stage = mature cyst with 4 nuclei (p.61, p.66); mode of infection = ingestion of mature cysts from contaminated food or water (p.60). BM30 Q11 keyed exactly this ('drinking water contaminated by mature cyst').","B":"Giardia cysts have 2–4 nuclei; only the 4-nucleus cyst is mature and infective (p.59).","C":"Entry of trophozoites through the nasal cavity is the Acanthamoeba (GAE) route (p.38).","D":"The trophozoite (2 nuclei, 4 pairs of flagella) is the stage that lives in the small bowel and is diagnostic, not infective.","E":"Eight nuclei is the mature cyst of Entamoeba coli (p.49)."},"img":"slides_i/L5_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 5-year-old has foul-smelling, fatty, watery diarrhoea with no blood and no mucus. Stool shows an organism with falling-leaf motility. What is it, and where does it live?","o":{"D":"Entamoeba histolytica — the large intestine","A":"Giardia intestinalis — the SMALL intestine, although it encysts in the large intestine","E":"Balantidium coli — the large intestine","C":"Trichomonas vaginalis — the urogenital tract","B":"Acanthamoeba spp. — free-living in water"},"r":{"D":"E. histolytica gives bloody mucoid diarrhoea with rapid unidirectional motility.","A":"Correct. The absence of blood and mucus is the ANSWER, not a caveat, and steatorrhoea is the word to hunt for. Giardia is the odd one out among today's gut protozoa — everything else lives in the colon. Note also the three synonyms: G. intestinalis = G. duodenalis = G. lamblia.","E":"Balantidium gives bloody mucoid diarrhoea, and it is a huge ciliated cell.","C":"Trichomonas gives greenish-yellow vaginal discharge with jerky motility.","B":"Acanthamoeba causes keratitis or encephalitis, not diarrhoea."},"t":"Giardia","sl":58,"img":"slides_i/L5_58.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Giardia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Balantidium — infective vs diagnostic stage","s":"A 50-year-old farmer has chronic diarrhoea that has become mucous and bloody. His stool contains very large oval organisms, well over 50 µm long, coated with short hair-like structures and showing a kidney-shaped nucleus. Which pairing of infective and diagnostic stage is correct for this organism?","lec":"L5","sl":26,"c":"<b>Balantidium — infective vs diagnostic stage</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Infective: trophozoite; diagnostic: cyst and trophozoite","B":"Infective: trophozoite; diagnostic: trophozoite","C":"Infective: mature cyst; diagnostic: trophozoite","D":"Infective: mature cyst; diagnostic: cyst and trophozoite","E":"Infective: mature cyst; diagnostic: cyst"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Trophozoite-infective with both stages diagnostic is the Acanthamoeba pattern (p.39).","B":"Trophozoite as the only stage (infective and diagnostic) is the Trichomonas vaginalis pattern (p.6).","C":"Half right — the slides list both cysts and trophozoites in faeces as diagnostic.","D":"Correct. Cilia + kidney-shaped macronucleus + 50–200 µm = Balantidium coli, the largest protozoan in humans. Infective stage = mature cyst; diagnostic stage = cysts AND trophozoites (p.29, p.33).","E":"Half right — the trophozoite is also diagnostic."},"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which feature steers a bloody-diarrhoea stem towards Balantidium coli rather than Entamoeba histolytica?","o":{"C":"Bloody mucoid stool","B":"A flask-shaped ulcer on biopsy","E":"Lower abdominal pain with tenesmus","A":"A cyst as the infective stage","D":"A history of pig contact or pig farming — Balantidium is the zoonotic one of the pair"},"r":{"C":"Shared by both, and by Trichuris.","B":"The flask-shaped ulcer is classically E. histolytica, but her Balantidium slide prints it too — so it does NOT decide. Look at the organism.","E":"Shared by both.","A":"Both are acquired as mature cysts, and for both the diagnostic stage is cyst AND trophozoite.","D":"Correct. The pig is the discriminator, because the two organisms give the same stool and the same site."},"t":"Balantidium","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Balantidium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Acanthamoeba keratitis — prevention","s":"A soft contact-lens wearer is treated for a painful, sight-threatening keratitis; the corneal scraping showed trophozoites with spiny projections and a large karyosome, plus double-walled cysts. Which instruction from the lecture's prevention list targets how she acquired it?","lec":"L5","sl":36,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba keratitis — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Keep chlorine levels and pH adequate in the pools where she swims","B":"Boil tap water before drinking and wash vegetables before eating","C":"Clean the lenses with lens solution, never with tap water","D":"Wear the lenses in hot tubs only if the water is well chlorinated","E":"Rinse the lens case in tap water and let it air-dry between uses"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Pool chlorination and pH is the past-paper answer for the free-living amoeba acquired while swimming (the child with fever after a pool) — not the lens route.","B":"That is the drinking-water/food advice for Giardia and E. histolytica (p.19, p.65); Acanthamoeba is not swallowed.","C":"Correct. Spiny acanthopodia, large karyosome, double-walled cyst = Acanthamoeba keratitis. The AK prevention slide: clean lenses with solution, never use tap water with lenses; do not sleep, swim, shower or use a hot tub while wearing them; wash hands before handling them (p.43). Tap water is safe to drink but still carries free-living amoebae.","D":"The slide says do not use a hot tub, swim or shower while wearing lenses at all.","E":"Tap water must not touch the lenses or their case — this is exactly how the organism reaches the eye."},"img":"slides_i/L5_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A contact-lens wearer develops photophobia and severe keratitis. A cyst is seen with a wrinkled outer wall and a polyhedral inner wall. What is the organism, and what is the key preventive instruction?","o":{"C":"Acanthamoeba — never rinse or store lenses in tap water; use lens solution only","A":"Naegleria fowleri — avoid swimming in warm fresh water","B":"Balamuthia mandrillaris — avoid soil contact","E":"Entamoeba histolytica — boil drinking water","D":"Giardia intestinalis — filter drinking water"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The two-layered cyst — wrinkled ECTOCYST outside, polyhedral ENDOCYST inside — is Acanthamoeba. Municipal tap water is safe to DRINK and still contains free-living amoebae, so the instruction is absolute: solution only.","A":"Naegleria causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis after warm freshwater exposure, not keratitis.","B":"Balamuthia causes granulomatous encephalitis.","E":"E. histolytica is a gut and liver pathogen.","D":"Giardia causes diarrhoea."},"t":"Acanthamoeba","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L5_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Acanthamoeba — AK vs GAE","s":"Two patients grow the same free-living amoeba with double-walled cysts. Patient 1 is a healthy 20-year-old soft contact-lens wearer with a painful corneal ulcer. Patient 2 is a kidney-transplant recipient with months of headache, confusion and focal weakness. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":38,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — AK vs GAE</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Both patients acquired it by swallowing mature cysts that excysted in the small intestine","B":"Patient 1 needs a lumbar puncture and brain CT or MRI to confirm the diagnosis","C":"Patient 1's disease is the form seen mainly in immunocompromised hosts, with about 100% mortality","D":"Patient 2's organism entered through the nose or skin, and this form has about 100% mortality","E":"Patient 2's organism entered through the cornea and then spread onward to the brain"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Acanthamoeba is never swallowed — trophozoites enter through the eye, nose or skin (p.38).","B":"LP and CT/MRI are the GAE work-up; AK is confirmed by corneal scraping for trophozoites and cysts (p.42).","C":"The host groups are swapped: AK affects healthy lens wearers; immunocompromise and 100% mortality belong to GAE.","D":"Correct. Granulomatous amoebic encephalitis (GAE): passes through the nasal cavity or skin, chronic, commonly in immunocompromised patients, mortality rate 100% (p.40).","E":"The cornea is the portal for keratitis (AK), not for GAE."},"img":"slides_i/L5_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which host factor separates Acanthamoeba keratitis from granulomatous amoebic encephalitis?","o":{"A":"Both occur only in contact-lens wearers","C":"Keratitis occurs in the immunocompromised; GAE occurs in healthy people","E":"Both occur only in the immunocompromised","D":"Keratitis occurs in healthy contact-lens wearers; GAE occurs in the immunocompromised","B":"Host status has no bearing on which syndrome develops"},"r":{"A":"GAE has no lens association.","C":"Reversed.","E":"Keratitis typically affects healthy lens wearers.","D":"Correct. If a stem mentions HIV, transplantation or chemotherapy, it is steering you to GAE.","B":"Host status is exactly what the stem uses to point you."},"t":"Acanthamoeba","sl":40,"img":"slides_i/L5_40.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Measuring a cyst — size decides","s":"In a trichrome-stained stool smear viewed under the ×100 oil-immersion objective, a spherical cyst spans 30 divisions of the ocular micrometer. Four nuclei are visible, but the chromatoid bars cannot be made out. How should the cyst be reported?","lec":"L5","sl":11,"c":"<b>Measuring a cyst — size decides</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Acanthamoeba double-walled cyst","B":"Entamoeba coli cyst","C":"Mature Entamoeba histolytica cyst","D":"Mature Giardia intestinalis cyst","E":"Balantidium coli cyst"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The Acanthamoeba cyst is 10–20 µm with ONE nucleus and a wrinkled/polyhedral double wall (p.37).","B":"Correct. At ×100 oil, 1 division = 1 µm (the lecturer's measuring rule), so the cyst is 30 µm. Only Entamoeba coli (cyst 10–35 µm, 1/2/4/8 nuclei, larger than E. histolytica) fits a 30 µm spherical cyst; with 4 nuclei it is simply not yet at its mature count of 8.","C":"The count of 4 is the trap. The E. histolytica cyst is 10–15 µm (p.11; ~10–20 on p.53) — a 30 µm cyst is too big, whatever the nuclear count.","D":"The Giardia cyst is oval, 8–19 × 7–10 µm, with axonemes and median bodies (p.59).","E":"The Balantidium cyst is 40–65 µm with a single kidney-shaped macronucleus and a thick wall (p.27)."},"img":"slides_i/L5_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which observation belongs to which preparation?","o":{"A":"All three observations can be made on any preparation","C":"Motility is best seen in iodine; nuclei are best seen in saline","E":"The chromatoid body is only visible on permanent stain","B":"Motility is a saline-only observation; the chromatoid body is seen in saline and the same material is called a glycogen mass in iodine; nuclei are essentially an iodine or permanent-stain observation","D":"Permanent stain is the correct first-line investigation in a general hospital"},"r":{"A":"Each preparation shows different things, which is exactly the point of the question.","C":"Iodine KILLS the organism, so motility is lost; nuclei are the thing iodine reveals.","E":"It is visible in a saline wet mount, where it is called a chromatoid body.","B":"Correct. The same structure has two names depending on the well — chromatoid body in saline, glycogen mass in iodine — which is why the question 'how do you identify it on an unstained wet mount?' has a different answer from 'how do you identify it definitively?'.","D":"A permanent stain is not available in a general hospital, so it is never the right first-line answer to 'how would you investigate this patient?'."},"t":"Laboratory","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L5_10.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Laboratory</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Amoebic dysentery — treatment","s":"A 50-year-old man has colicky lower abdominal pain, tenesmus and frequent mucous bloody stools. An iron-haematoxylin smear shows trophozoites with a single nucleus containing a central karyosome and evenly arranged peripheral chromatin, with red cells in the cytoplasm. Which drug is the treatment?","lec":"L5","sl":9,"c":"<b>Amoebic dysentery — treatment</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Metronidazole","B":"Co-trimoxazole","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Pyrimethamine","E":"Albendazole"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Centric karyosome + regular peripheral chromatin + ingested RBCs = Entamoeba histolytica (p.9). Specific treatment = metronidazole (or tinidazole), followed by a luminal agent for invasive amoebiasis (p.18). The lecturer: 'NL ออกนะ' — this exact NL-style item is her slide 23.","B":"Co-trimoxazole is used for the coccidia (L6), e.g. Cystoisospora and Cyclospora.","C":"Praziquantel is for flatworms (cestodes, trematodes).","D":"Pyrimethamine belongs to Toxoplasma / malaria (L7).","E":"Albendazole is an anthelminthic — it would suit Trichuris, the helminth member of the dysentery triad, not this amoeba."},"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does the presence of ingested red blood cells inside an amoeba matter so much?","o":{"B":"Because ingested red cells distinguish the cyst from the trophozoite","C":"Because red cells inside the organism indicate a liver abscess rather than colitis","E":"Because red cells in the stool are what define dysentery","D":"Because only Balantidium ingests red cells","A":"Because Entamoeba dispar is morphologically identical to E. histolytica but non-pathogenic, and does not ingest red cells — so the red cells prove you are looking at the pathogen"},"r":{"B":"Only the trophozoite feeds, but that is not why the feature matters.","C":"Erythrophagocytosis is a feature of invasive intestinal disease; in liver abscess the stool is often negative altogether.","E":"Red cells IN THE STOOL is just bleeding, which any dysentery gives you. Red cells INSIDE THE ORGANISM is the diagnosis — the distinction the question is built on.","D":"Balantidium does not characteristically show erythrophagocytosis.","A":"Correct. The lecturer said she will not set E. dispar directly but that you should know it exists — and its relevance is exactly this: it is why erythrophagocytosis is a diagnostic feature and not just a descriptive one."},"t":"Entamoeba dispar","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Entamoeba dispar</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q21","t":"Amoebic liver abscess","s":"A 50-year-old patient has right upper quadrant pain and bloody mucoid diarrhoea. Aspiration of a liver abscess yields anchovy-paste pus, and the organism shows amoeboid movement. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":22,"c":"<b>Amoebic liver abscess</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q21 · BM30 Q21 — anchovy pus with amoeboid movement</span>","o":{"E":"Entamoeba coli","B":"Entamoeba histolytica","C":"Acanthamoeba spp.","D":"Capillaria philippinensis","A":"Balantidium coli"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"E. coli is non-pathogenic and never causes invasive disease. Its cyst is LARGER than E. histolytica's and has more nuclei.","B":"Correct. Anchovy-paste pus is the classic description of an amoebic liver abscess, and this is extraintestinal amoebiasis.","C":"Acanthamoeba causes keratitis, encephalitis or cutaneous disease, never a liver abscess.","D":"Capillaria is an intestinal nematode causing chronic diarrhoea and protein loss (L1).","A":"Balantidium causes colitis but not liver abscess."},"img":"slides_i/L5_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q21"]},{"id":"I|L5|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q11","t":"Giardia","s":"A patient presents with foul-smelling diarrhoea. Stool examination reveals an organism with falling-leaf movement. What is the most likely causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":58,"c":"<b>Giardia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q44 · BM29 SUM1 Q11 — foul-smelling stool, falling-leaf movement</span>","o":{"B":"Giardia intestinalis","C":"Entamoeba histolytica","E":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","D":"Cystoisospora belli","A":"Balantidium coli"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Falling-leaf motility plus foul-smelling, fatty, NON-bloody diarrhoea is Giardia. Note that motility is a saline-wet-mount observation and is destroyed by iodine.","C":"E. histolytica shows rapid unidirectional movement and gives BLOODY mucoid diarrhoea.","E":"Cyclospora is a coccidian oocyst with no motility (L6).","D":"Cystoisospora is likewise a non-motile oocyst (L6).","A":"Balantidium moves by cilia and gives bloody diarrhoea."},"img":"slides_i/L5_58.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q44"]},{"id":"I|L5|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q-dys","t":"Trophozoite with RBC","s":"A patient has dysentery, tenesmus and lower abdominal pain. What must be found on stool examination to make the diagnosis?","lec":"L5","sl":9,"c":"<b>Trophozoite with RBC</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 · BM31 · BM30 — dysentery + tenesmus, 'what must you find?'</span>","o":{"C":"An embryonated egg","A":"A sporulated oocyst 4–6 µm","D":"A spore 20–30 µm","E":"A trophozoite with red blood cells inside it","B":"A cyst 2 µm"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"An embryonated egg belongs to the helminths.","A":"A 4–6 µm sporulated oocyst is Cryptosporidium (L6), which causes WATERY diarrhoea WITHOUT blood, in the small bowel.","D":"No organism in the block matches that description; the L6 sizes are planted to tempt you.","E":"Correct, and this item has appeared three years running. The tell is 'dysentery' plus 'tenesmus' — blood and large bowel — which is Entamoeba histolytica.","B":"A 2 µm cyst points at Microsporidia's spore size (L6), again in the wrong clinical setting."},"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q-dys","BM30 Q-dys"]},{"id":"I|L5|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q29","t":"Acanthamoeba","s":"A 25-year-old woman has photophobia and visual loss. A cyst is seen with two layers, the outer one wrinkled. What is the infection?","lec":"L5","sl":37,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q29 · BM29 Q31 · BM30 — photophobia with a wrinkled two-layer cyst</span>","o":{"E":"Acanthamoeba spp.","C":"Naegleria fowleri","D":"Balamuthia mandrillaris","A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Strongyloides stercoralis"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. The double-walled cyst — wrinkled ectocyst, polyhedral endocyst — plus eye involvement is Acanthamoeba keratitis, and the risk factor asked about in the follow-up item is CONTACT LENSES.","C":"Naegleria causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis after warm freshwater swimming; the prevention answer for that item is chlorination and pH adjustment.","D":"Balamuthia causes granulomatous encephalitis, not keratitis.","A":"E. histolytica does not infect the eye.","B":"A nematode, not an amoeba."},"img":"slides_i/L5_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q-acanth","BM29 Q31"]},{"id":"I|L5|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q15","t":"Trichomonas","s":"A 35-year-old woman has greenish-yellow vaginal discharge and a haemorrhagic 'strawberry' cervix. What is the appropriate control measure, and what would you see in the discharge?","lec":"L5","sl":54,"c":"<b>Trichomonas</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q15 · BM28 Q45 — strawberry cervix, greenish-yellow discharge</span>","o":{"C":"Treat the patient only; the discharge shows a mature cyst","A":"Treat both the patient and her partner; the discharge shows a trophozoite with jerky motility","D":"Treat the patient and her whole family; the discharge shows falling-leaf motility","B":"Boil drinking water; the discharge shows a ciliated organism","E":"No treatment is needed; the condition is self-limiting"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Treating one partner guarantees reinfection.","A":"Correct. Trichomonas vaginalis is sexually transmitted, so the partner must be treated or reinfection is certain — and the organism has NO cyst stage, so the trophozoite is both the infective and the diagnostic stage, seen with a characteristic jerky motility.","D":"Family treatment is the Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana answer.","B":"Trichomonas is not water-borne.","E":"It is a treatable, transmissible infection."},"img":"slides_i/L5_54.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM28 Q45"]},{"id":"I|L5|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-pool","t":"Free-living amoeba","s":"A child developed high fever and headache three days after swimming in a pool, and a free-living amoeba was identified. What is the appropriate prevention?","lec":"L5","sl":43,"c":"<b>Free-living amoeba</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM28 Q43 — child who swam in a pool, prevention</span>","o":{"B":"Never swim again","D":"Drink only clean water","E":"Maintain adequate chlorine levels and adjust the pH of the pool water","C":"Avoid swimming during the summer","A":"Avoid swimming in cold fresh water"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Absolute avoidance is not a practical or examinable control measure.","D":"Naegleria enters through the nose during swimming, not by drinking.","E":"Correct, and it is the answer key's own wording. ⚠ Note the contrast with L6: Cryptosporidium is RESISTANT to chlorine and needs FILTRATION instead. Same-sounding question, opposite answer — read which organism the stem gives you.","C":"Season is not the control point.","A":"The organism favours WARM fresh water, so this is the wrong way round."},"img":"slides_i/L5_43.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["BM30 Q-pool","BM28 Q43"]},{"id":"I|L5|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q25","t":"Balantidium coli","s":"A patient has bloody mucoid diarrhoea and abdominal pain. Stool examination shows a large oval trophozoite covered in short hair-like structures, with a KIDNEY-SHAPED MACRONUCLEUS. What is the organism?","lec":"L5","sl":26,"c":"<b>Balantidium coli</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 (dysentery, trophozoite with cilia) · BM31 SUM1 Q25 · BM30 Formative — the only ciliate in the syllabus, asked in three cohorts</span>","o":{"E":"Balantidium coli","C":"Entamoeba histolytica","D":"Entamoeba coli","B":"Giardia intestinalis","A":"Acanthamoeba spp."},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct, and it is decided by two words. Balantidium coli is the ONLY ciliate that infects humans, so 'cilia' alone excludes everything else; the kidney-shaped macronucleus confirms it. It is also the largest protozoan in the syllabus at 50–200 µm.","C":"E. histolytica is the other dysentery protozoan, but it moves with a pseudopodium and contains ingested RED CELLS — no cilia, no macronucleus.","D":"E. coli is non-pathogenic and causes no dysentery.","B":"Giardia has flagella and shows falling-leaf motion with foul-smelling fatty stool, not dysentery.","A":"Acanthamoeba is free-living and causes keratitis or encephalitis, never colitis. Note the exam's three-way dysentery list: Trichuris, Entamoeba histolytica, Balantidium coli."},"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-balant","BM30 Q-balant"]},{"id":"I|L5|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q40","t":"Giardia — axoneme","s":"A patient has watery diarrhoea with FAT in the stool. Examination of the specimen shows AXONEMES. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":57,"c":"<b>Giardia — axoneme</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q40 — watery diarrhoea with fat in the stool, AXONEME seen on examination</span>","o":{"D":"Cryptosporidium spp.","C":"Microsporidia","E":"Cystoisospora belli","A":"Trichomonas vaginalis","B":"Giardia intestinalis"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Cryptosporidium is a 4–6 µm acid-fast oocyst, and its diarrhoea is watery WITHOUT fat.","C":"Microsporidia spores are 1–2 µm with a coiled polar filament, seen on gram-chromotrope.","E":"Cystoisospora is a 25 × 15 µm ellipsoidal oocyst on modified acid-fast stain.","A":"Trichomonas is urogenital — vaginal discharge, not steatorrhoea.","B":"Correct. Axonemes — the internal rods of the flagella running down the midline — are a Giardia structure and are visible in BOTH the trophozoite and the cyst. Steatorrhoea follows because Giardia coats the duodenal mucosa and blocks fat absorption."},"img":"slides_i/L5_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L5|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q5 (58)","t":"Extra-intestinal amoebiasis","s":"What is the most common EXTRA-INTESTINAL manifestation of Entamoeba histolytica infection?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Extra-intestinal amoebiasis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 58 & 60 — most common extra-intestinal form of invasive amoebiasis</span>","o":{"A":"Cutaneous amoebiasis","B":"Brain abscess","D":"Pleural effusion","E":"Peri-anal ulceration","C":"Liver abscess"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Skin is the rarest site of all. The slide ranks them and the examiner asks for the top of the list.","B":"Brain involvement happens but is rare, and it is what the NEXT item turns on.","D":"Pleura and lung come second, usually by direct extension from a liver abscess.","E":"Peri-anal ulceration is listed on the slide but is uncommon.","C":"Correct, and by a wide margin. Trophozoites travel up the portal vein, so the liver is hit first — right upper quadrant pain, fever, hepatomegaly, and the 'anchovy-sauce' pus that is really necrotic liver, not true pus."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 Q5 (60)"]},{"id":"I|L5|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q2 (60)","t":"Brain lesions — which amoeba","s":"Which of the following does NOT commonly produce a space-occupying lesion in the brain?","lec":"L5","sl":34,"c":"<b>Brain lesions — which amoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 60 Q2 — which organism does NOT produce a space-occupying brain lesion?</span>","o":{"E":"Toxoplasma gondii","D":"Acanthamoeba spp.","B":"Balamuthia mandrillaris","A":"Naegleria fowleri","C":"Entamoeba histolytica"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Toxoplasma in an immunocompromised host is THE classic ring-enhancing brain mass (L7).","D":"Acanthamoeba causes granulomatous amoebic encephalitis (GAE), a slow mass lesion.","B":"Balamuthia mandrillaris also causes GAE.","A":"Naegleria fowleri causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis (PAM) — fulminant, after swimming in warm fresh water.","C":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. E. histolytica CAN reach the brain, but cerebral amoebiasis is rare — its signature lesion is in the LIVER. Everything else on this list is defined by what it does to the CNS."},"img":"slides_i/L5_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q13 (57)","t":"Amoebic colitis vs shigellosis","s":"Which finding is NOT typical of acute amoebic colitis?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Amoebic colitis vs shigellosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q13/16 — which finding is NOT typical of acute amoebic colitis?</span>","o":{"A":"Absence of severe dehydration","C":"Insidious onset over days to weeks","D":"Lower abdominal pain","E":"Tenesmus","B":"Abundant faecal leukocytes"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Correct of amoebiasis: stool volume is small and bloody rather than voluminous, so severe dehydration is uncommon.","C":"Amoebiasis creeps up over days to weeks, unlike the abrupt onset of bacillary dysentery.","D":"Lower abdominal pain is expected — the ulcers are in the caecum and sigmoid.","E":"Tenesmus is expected, and is one of the three keywords the exam reuses.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer, and this is the single fact that separates amoebic from bacillary dysentery at the bench. Amoebic trophozoites LYSE neutrophils, so faecal leukocytes are scanty. Abundant faecal leukocytes point to Shigella."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q4 (60)","t":"Trichomonas — morphology","s":"A 25-year-old woman has a yellow vaginal discharge and a history of several sexual partners in the past two weeks. What is the characteristic appearance of the causative agent?","lec":"L5","sl":54,"c":"<b>Trichomonas — morphology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 60 Q4 — yellow discharge with multiple sexual partners; what does the organism look like?</span>","o":{"D":"Pear-shaped with two anterior nuclei and a large adhesive disc","A":"Pear-shaped with an undulating membrane and jerky motility","E":"Pear-shaped with a sucking disc and falling-leaf motion","B":"Round with four to eight ingested red cells","C":"Oval with cilia and a kidney-shaped macronucleus"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"Two anterior nuclei and an adhesive disc are Giardia, which is intestinal.","A":"Correct. Trichomonas vaginalis exists ONLY as a trophozoite — there is no cyst — and it is recognised by the undulating membrane running down one side plus its twitching, jerky movement on a wet mount.","E":"'Sucking disc' plus falling-leaf motion is Giardia again, described a second way — the examiner plants Giardia twice because both organisms are pear-shaped flagellates.","B":"Ingested red cells are Entamoeba histolytica.","C":"Cilia and a kidney-shaped macronucleus are Balantidium coli."},"img":"slides_i/L5_54.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L5|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"E. coli trophozoite in a dysenteric stool","s":"A patient has had five days of tenesmus and mucous bloody diarrhoea. A trichrome smear shows only amoebic trophozoites whose single nucleus has an eccentric karyosome and irregularly clumped peripheral chromatin; none contains red cells. What is the correct interpretation?","lec":"L5","sl":45,"c":"<b>E. coli trophozoite in a dysenteric stool</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Amoebic dysentery confirmed, since any amoeba in a bloody stool is the pathogen","B":"A free-living amoeba that has colonised the colon of a weakened host","C":"The pathogenic amoeba caught early, before it has begun to ingest red cells","D":"The pathogenic amoeba, since an eccentric karyosome marks the invasive species","E":"A harmless commensal that does not explain the dysentery, so keep looking"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Bloody stool plus an amoeba is not enough; the organism's own features decide.","B":"Acanthamoeba (large karyosome, acanthopodia) is free-living and causes eye/CNS/skin disease, not colonic colonisation.","C":"Wrong species: E. histolytica has a CENTRIC karyosome with REGULAR chromatin — the nuclear pattern, not the RBC content, excludes it here.","D":"Inverted — the centric karyosome is the E. histolytica feature (p.52).","E":"Correct. Eccentric karyosome + irregular peripheral chromatin + no RBC = Entamoeba coli (p.46, p.52), non-pathogenic. The lecture teaches it precisely because it must be differentiated from E. histolytica (p.45) — the real cause (E. histolytica, Balantidium or Trichuris) still has to be found."},"img":"slides_i/L5_45.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An iron-haematoxylin stained stool smear shows an amoebic trophozoite with ONE nucleus that has an ECCENTRIC karyosome and IRREGULAR peripheral chromatin, and no ingested red cells. What is it, and what follows clinically?","o":{"E":"Entamoeba coli — a non-pathogenic commensal, so this finding does not explain the patient's symptoms","D":"Entamoeba histolytica — the eccentric karyosome is its defining nuclear feature","A":"Entamoeba dispar — the absence of red cells proves the species","B":"Entamoeba histolytica, and the absence of red cells simply means the infection is early","C":"Acanthamoeba spp. — the large karyosome and food vacuoles fit"},"r":{"E":"Correct. ECCENTRIC karyosome + IRREGULAR peripheral chromatin = E. coli, which is non-pathogenic — so you must keep looking for the real cause.","D":"E. histolytica has a CENTRIC karyosome with REGULAR peripheral chromatin. This is the exact inversion the table is drawn to prevent.","A":"E. dispar is morphologically IDENTICAL to E. histolytica — it has a centric karyosome too. Absent red cells alone cannot name it.","B":"Species wrong. And note that an E. histolytica trophozoite WITHOUT red cells cannot be distinguished from E. dispar at all.","C":"Acanthamoeba is a free-living amoeba of cornea and brain, not a stool commensal, and has acanthopodia."},"t":"histolytica vs coli — trophozoite","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L5_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>histolytica vs coli — trophozoite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Asymptomatic E. histolytica carrier — control","s":"A pre-employment stool test on a well 30-year-old restaurant cook shows spherical cysts about 12 µm across with four nuclei and cigar-shaped chromatoid bodies with rounded ends; PCR confirms the pathogenic species. He has no symptoms. What does the lecture's control section imply?","lec":"L5","sl":8,"c":"<b>Asymptomatic E. histolytica carrier — control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Treat him and any pigs at home, because the infection is zoonotic","B":"Treat him if a liver abscess develops, because carriers rarely transmit","C":"Treat him, because carriers passing these cysts spread the infection","D":"Leave him untreated, because cysts are not the stage that causes disease","E":"Leave him untreated, because a cyst with four nuclei is still immature"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Pig-directed control is the Balantidium coli slide (p.32).","B":"Carriers are the main reservoir; waiting for invasive disease leaves the source untouched.","C":"Correct. 12 µm, 4 nuclei, rounded cigar chromatoid bodies = mature E. histolytica cyst — the infective stage. 90% of infections are asymptomatic carriers (p.8, p.15) and they pass the cysts; the control slide says 'Treat patients and carriers' (p.19).","D":"Cysts do not cause symptoms, but they are what transmits — which is why carriers are treated.","E":"Four nuclei is the MATURE count for E. histolytica (p.11); fewer than 4 is immature."},"img":"slides_i/L5_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A cyst in an iodine preparation measures about 25 µm, contains EIGHT nuclei, and its chromatoid bars are splintery with sharp ends. Which statement is correct?","o":{"C":"It is a mature Entamoeba coli cyst — E. coli matures at 8 nuclei with splintery chromatoid bars, whereas E. histolytica matures at 4 with rounded or cigar-shaped bars","A":"It is a mature Entamoeba histolytica cyst — 8 nuclei is the mature count for both species","B":"It is an immature Entamoeba histolytica cyst that has over-divided","E":"It is a Balantidium coli cyst — the splintery bars are its cilia seen in section","D":"It is a Giardia cyst — 8 nuclei is the mature Giardia count"},"r":{"C":"Correct on both the nuclear count and the chromatoid bar shape — the two features the comparison slide puts side by side.","A":"E. histolytica never exceeds 4 nuclei; 4 IS its mature, infective count.","B":"There is no such thing as over-division past the mature count; the nuclear number is the species criterion.","E":"Balantidium cysts are 40–65 µm with a single kidney-shaped macronucleus and a thick wall.","D":"Giardia cysts have 2–4 nuclei with axonemes and median bodies, and are oval, not spherical."},"t":"Mature cyst — how many nuclei","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L5_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mature cyst — how many nuclei</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Acanthamoeba — trophozoite morphology","s":"A 19-year-old who sleeps in her soft contact lenses presents with severe eye pain, photophobia and a ring-shaped corneal infiltrate. Before the laboratory report arrives, which description of the trophozoite in the corneal scraping would confirm the amoeba you suspect?","lec":"L5","sl":36,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — trophozoite morphology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A kidney-shaped macronucleus, a cytostome and a surface covered with cilia","B":"One nucleus with a centric karyosome, ingested red cells and finger-like pseudopodia","C":"One nucleus with an eccentric karyosome, irregular chromatin and no ingested red cells","D":"One nucleus with a large karyosome, many food vacuoles and spiky surface projections","E":"Two anterior nuclei, a large adhesive disc, axonemes and four pairs of flagella"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Balantidium coli trophozoite (p.26), large-intestinal ciliate.","B":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoite (p.9–10) — a colonic pathogen, not an eye one.","C":"Entamoeba coli trophozoite (p.46), non-pathogenic, intestinal.","D":"Correct. Lens-associated keratitis = Acanthamoeba. Trophozoite: irregular, 10–40 µm, one nucleus with a BIG karyosome, numerous food vacuoles, acanthopodia (spiny projections) (p.36).","E":"Giardia intestinalis trophozoite (p.57), small-intestinal flagellate."},"img":"slides_i/L5_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about Acanthamoeba is correct?","o":{"D":"The TROPHOZOITE is the infective stage, entering through a soft contact lens, the nasal cavity or a skin lesion; both trophozoite and cyst are diagnostic","E":"The mature cyst is the infective stage and is swallowed in contaminated water, as in Entamoeba","A":"The cyst is infective and the trophozoite is the only diagnostic stage","C":"Only the trophozoite exists; Acanthamoeba has no cyst stage","B":"The infective stage is the fork-tailed cercaria released from freshwater snails"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and this is the exception worth flagging: for almost every other protozoan here the CYST is infective, so Acanthamoeba inverts the pattern you have been drilling.","E":"This is the pattern-matched wrong answer. Acanthamoeba is not acquired by swallowing, and the trophozoite is the invading stage.","A":"Inverted. Both stages are diagnostic (they are both found in corneal scrapings and biopsies); only the trophozoite infects.","C":"Acanthamoeba has a distinctive double-walled cyst — wrinkled ectocyst outside, polyhedral endocyst inside.","B":"Cercariae are schistosome stages from the trematode lecture."},"t":"Acanthamoeba — the stage that infects","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L5_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — the stage that infects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Giardia — morphology to clinical picture","s":"A fresh saline mount shows a pear-shaped, dorsoventrally flattened organism with two anterior nuclei that look like a pair of eyes, a large ventral disc and four pairs of flagella. Which clinical picture does this organism typically produce?","lec":"L5","sl":56,"c":"<b>Giardia — morphology to clinical picture</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Right upper quadrant pain, fever and an enlarged tender liver","B":"Mucous bloody stool with tenesmus and lower abdominal pain","C":"Painful keratitis with photophobia in a contact-lens wearer","D":"Greenish-yellow vaginal discharge with a strawberry cervix","E":"Chronic greasy, foul-smelling watery stool with malabsorption"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"RUQ pain + hepatomegaly = amoebic liver abscess (E. histolytica, p.15).","B":"Dysentery = E. histolytica, Balantidium or Trichuris — never Giardia.","C":"Keratitis in a lens wearer is Acanthamoeba (p.40).","D":"Trichomonas vaginalis is also a pear-shaped flagellate, but it has an undulating membrane and one nucleus, and is urogenital.","E":"Correct. The lecturer's 'bald man in glasses with a moustache' — 2 nuclei (eyes), adhesive disc (glasses), flagella (moustache) = Giardia intestinalis (p.57). Giardiasis: watery diarrhoea without blood or mucus, chronic steatorrhoea (fatty stool), foul-smelling, malabsorption, malnutrition (p.56, p.62)."},"img":"slides_i/L5_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A wet mount from a child with a month of fatty, foul-smelling, non-bloody diarrhoea shows a pear-shaped organism tumbling with a 'falling leaf' motion. Which set of features confirms it, and what is the treatment?","o":{"E":"Two anterior nuclei, a large ventral adhesive disc, two median bodies, axonemes and four pairs of flagella — Giardia intestinalis, treated with metronidazole, paromomycin or tetracycline","D":"A single nucleus with a centric karyosome and ingested red cells — Entamoeba histolytica, treated with metronidazole","A":"A kidney-shaped macronucleus with surface cilia — Balantidium coli, treated with metronidazole or tinidazole","B":"Two anterior nuclei and four pairs of flagella — Giardia intestinalis, treated with albendazole alone","C":"An undulating membrane with a single nucleus — Trichomonas vaginalis, treated with metronidazole"},"r":{"E":"Correct on every element. Note the symmetry of the organism — everything comes in pairs — and that the diarrhoea is watery/fatty WITHOUT blood or mucus.","D":"Ingested red cells and bloody mucous dysentery is the amoebic picture; this child has no blood in the stool.","A":"Balantidium is a large ciliate causing dysentery with flask-shaped ulcers, not steatorrhoea.","B":"Morphology right, drug wrong — albendazole is not on the treatment slide for giardiasis.","C":"Trichomonas is a urogenital flagellate, not a cause of steatorrhoea, and has only a trophozoite stage."},"t":"Giardia — trophozoite features","sl":57,"img":"slides_i/L5_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Giardia — trophozoite features</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Amoebic liver abscess — treatment sequence","s":"A 40-year-old woman with fever, right upper quadrant pain and hepatomegaly has a liver abscess whose aspirate looks like anchovy paste, with trophozoites found at the edge of the lesion. Which management plan follows the lecture?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"<b>Amoebic liver abscess — treatment sequence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Metronidazole, then paromomycin, with aspiration","B":"Tinidazole, then pyrimethamine, with aspiration","C":"Paromomycin, then metronidazole, with aspiration","D":"Albendazole, then praziquantel, with aspiration","E":"Metronidazole, then albendazole, with aspiration"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Anchovy pus = amoebic liver abscess (E. histolytica). p.18: metronidazole or tinidazole FOLLOWED BY a luminal agent (paromomycin) for invasive amoebiasis, plus aspirate removal for extraintestinal disease. The tissue drug treats the invasive trophozoites; the luminal agent clears the bowel so the patient does not relapse or keep shedding cysts.","B":"Tinidazole is an acceptable first drug, but pyrimethamine is a Toxoplasma/malaria drug (L7).","C":"Right drugs, wrong order — the slide puts the luminal agent after the tissue drug.","D":"Anthelminthics — the approach for helminth liver lesions (e.g. hydatid disease, liver flukes), not an amoeba.","E":"The luminal follow-up is paromomycin, not albendazole."},"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A man has right upper quadrant pain, fever and hepatomegaly, and aspiration yields anchovy-paste material. Which pair of statements is correct?","o":{"A":"Trophozoites are found at the EDGE of the lesion rather than in the centre of the pus, and treatment is metronidazole or tinidazole FOLLOWED by a luminal agent such as paromomycin, with aspiration","C":"Trophozoites are abundant in the centre of the anchovy pus, and metronidazole alone is sufficient","B":"Cysts are found in the abscess, and treatment is paromomycin alone","D":"The abscess contains E. dispar, and no treatment is needed because that species is non-invasive","E":"Treatment is albendazole plus aspiration, as for hydatid disease"},"r":{"A":"Correct on both. The centre of the abscess is necrotic debris; live trophozoites sit at the advancing margin — which is why an aspirate can be falsely negative.","C":"This is the intuitive but wrong half — and metronidazole alone leaves luminal cysts behind, so the patient stays infectious and can relapse.","B":"E. histolytica does not encyst in tissue; cysts form only in the bowel lumen.","D":"E. dispar is by definition non-invasive, so it cannot produce a liver abscess.","E":"Albendazole and aspiration is the ECHINOCOCCUS approach; this is amoebic, not hydatid."},"t":"Amoebic liver abscess — where the trophozoites are","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L5_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Amoebic liver abscess — where the trophozoites are</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"GAE — diagnostic work-up","s":"A 55-year-old on long-term chemotherapy has had two months of headache, personality change and seizures. Imaging shows several granulomatous lesions in the brain. He has never worn contact lenses. Which set of investigations matches the lecture's approach to the most likely amoebic cause?","lec":"L5","sl":40,"c":"<b>GAE — diagnostic work-up</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Brain biopsy, lumbar puncture for CSF and brain imaging","B":"Serum ELISA titre and aspiration of the lesion","C":"Corneal scraping, in vitro culture and PCR of the scraping","D":"Thick and thin blood films stained with Giemsa","E":"Stool FECT with saline and iodine wet mounts"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Chronic CNS disease in an immunocompromised host = granulomatous amoebic encephalitis (Acanthamoeba). p.42: biopsy of brain lesions (cysts & trophozoites), lumbar puncture for CSF examination, CT or MRI of the brain; culture and PCR also listed.","B":"ELISA titre + aspiration is the amoebic liver abscess (E. histolytica) approach.","C":"Corneal scraping is the AK specimen — this patient has no eye disease.","D":"Blood films are for malaria (L7).","E":"Stool examination is for the intestinal protozoa; Acanthamoeba is not a gut organism."},"img":"slides_i/L5_40.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which pairing of Acanthamoeba syndrome, route and patient matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Acanthamoeba keratitis enters through the EYE and typically affects otherwise healthy soft contact lens wearers; granulomatous amoebic encephalitis enters through the nasal cavity or skin and affects immunocompromised patients, with a mortality approaching 100%","D":"Acanthamoeba keratitis affects immunocompromised patients and granulomatous amoebic encephalitis affects contact lens wearers","E":"Both syndromes follow swimming in freshwater and produce a fulminant meningitis within days","C":"Granulomatous amoebic encephalitis is diagnosed by corneal scraping; keratitis is diagnosed by lumbar puncture","B":"Both are diagnosed by stool examination, since Acanthamoeba is an intestinal amoeba"},"r":{"A":"Correct on syndrome, route, host and outcome — four facts the slide gives in one place.","D":"The two host groups are swapped. Immunosuppression is the GAE risk factor.","E":"Fulminant amoebic meningoencephalitis after freshwater swimming is the NAEGLERIA picture. GAE is described as CHRONIC.","C":"The specimens are swapped: corneal scraping for AK, lumbar puncture / brain biopsy / CT-MRI for GAE.","B":"Acanthamoeba is a FREE-LIVING amoeba of soil, fresh water and air-conditioning units, not a gut parasite."},"t":"Acanthamoeba — two diseases, two hosts","sl":40,"img":"slides_i/L5_40.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — two diseases, two hosts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Balantidium vs Giardia — reproduction","s":"Two stool organisms are compared. X is a very large oval cell coated with cilia and carrying a kidney-shaped nucleus; Y is a pear-shaped cell with two anterior nuclei, an adhesive disc and four pairs of flagella. Which statement about how they reproduce is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":26,"c":"<b>Balantidium vs Giardia — reproduction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Y divides by transverse binary fission in humans and also undergoes conjugation","B":"X divides by longitudinal binary fission in humans and also undergoes conjugation","C":"Y divides by longitudinal binary fission and also undergoes conjugation in the colon","D":"X divides by transverse binary fission in humans and also undergoes conjugation","E":"X and Y both divide by longitudinal binary fission and have no sexual process"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Y = Giardia, which divides longitudinally and has no conjugation listed.","B":"Longitudinal fission is Giardia's (p.61), not the ciliate's.","C":"Half right — Giardia's fission is longitudinal, but conjugation is Balantidium's.","D":"Correct. X = Balantidium coli: asexual transverse binary fission (in human) and sexual conjugation (p.26, p.29).","E":"Balantidium divides transversely and conjugates."},"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about Balantidium coli is correct?","o":{"D":"It is the largest protozoan infecting humans; it reproduces asexually in the human gut by TRANSVERSE binary fission and sexually by conjugation; heavy infection produces flask-shaped ulcers that can perforate","E":"It reproduces in humans by LONGITUDINAL binary fission, like Giardia","C":"It is the smallest protozoan infecting humans and cannot cause ulceration","A":"Conjugation occurs in the human colon and is the source of the cysts passed in stool","B":"It has only a trophozoite stage, so transmission is direct person-to-person"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Note both the plane of fission (transverse — it is a ciliate) and that flask-shaped ulcers are NOT unique to Entamoeba.","E":"LONGITUDINAL binary fission is Giardia's. The direction of the split is a ciliate-vs-flagellate discriminator.","C":"It is explicitly the LARGEST protozoan in humans, and ulceration with perforation is listed.","A":"Conjugation is listed as its sexual process, but the slide attributes transverse binary fission to what happens in the human host; cysts arise by encystation, not conjugation.","B":"It has two stages, trophozoite AND cyst, and the mature cyst is the infective stage."},"t":"Balantidium — reproduction and ulcer","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L5_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Balantidium — reproduction and ulcer</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Giardia — life-cycle sequence","s":"A child swallows water contaminated with oval cysts that have four nuclei, axonemes running along the middle and median bodies. Which sequence traces this organism correctly from ingestion to the stool?","lec":"L5","sl":59,"c":"<b>Giardia — life-cycle sequence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Excysts in the duodenum → invades the colonic wall → encysts in the large intestine","B":"Excysts in the duodenum → lives in the small intestine → encysts in the large intestine","C":"Excysts in the stomach → lives in the large intestine → encysts in the large intestine","D":"Excysts in the duodenum → lives in the small intestine → encysts in the small intestine","E":"Excysts in the jejunum → reaches the liver in portal blood → encysts in the large intestine"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Giardia does not invade; that is why there is no blood (p.62).","B":"Correct. Mature Giardia cyst (4 nuclei, axonemes, median bodies). Excystation in the small intestine (duodenum, jejunum); habitat small intestine; encystation in the LARGE intestine (p.61, p.66).","C":"Excystation and habitat are small-intestinal; the large bowel is the E. histolytica / Balantidium habitat.","D":"Half right — encystation is listed separately as large intestine (p.66).","E":"Portal spread to the liver is the route of E. histolytica trophozoites."},"img":"slides_i/L5_59.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the site of each step in the Giardia life cycle is correct?","o":{"B":"Excystation and the trophozoite's habitat are in the SMALL intestine (duodenum and jejunum), but encystation occurs in the LARGE intestine","D":"Excystation, habitat and encystation all occur in the small intestine","E":"Excystation occurs in the stomach and the trophozoites live in the colon","A":"The trophozoite lives in the large intestine, which is why the diarrhoea contains blood and mucus","C":"Encystation occurs in the gallbladder, which is why the stool is fatty"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the split location is exactly the detail a careful stem will target. The adhesive disc anchors trophozoites to duodenal and jejunal mucosa; cysts form later, downstream.","D":"Half right — habitat yes, encystation no. The learning-guidance slide lists encystation in the large intestine on its own line.","E":"Excystation is in the small intestine, not the stomach.","A":"Giardia lives in the SMALL bowel and causes watery/fatty diarrhoea WITHOUT blood or mucus. Bloody mucous stool points to amoebiasis or balantidiasis.","C":"Giardia does not enter the gallbladder; the steatorrhoea comes from malabsorption at the small-bowel mucosa."},"t":"Giardia — where it lives, where it encysts","sl":61,"img":"slides_i/L5_61.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Giardia — where it lives, where it encysts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Saline vs iodine — what iodine costs you","s":"A fresh stool sample from a 5-year-old with a month of steatorrhoea reaches the laboratory. The technician, short of time, mixes the whole specimen with iodine before examining it. Which diagnostic observation has she made impossible?","lec":"L5","sl":58,"c":"<b>Saline vs iodine — what iodine costs you</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Counting the four nuclei of the mature cysts","B":"Measuring the cysts with the ocular micrometer","C":"Falling-leaf motility of the trophozoites","D":"Seeing the axonemes along the middle of the cysts","E":"Seeing the median bodies of the cysts"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Iodine makes nuclei easier to see (p.12, p.59).","B":"Size is unaffected by staining — measure every protozoan (×100 oil, 1 division = 1 µm).","C":"Correct. Child + steatorrhoea = Giardia. Falling-leaf motility is seen in a fresh simple (saline) wet smear (p.58, p.63); iodine kills the organism, so movement is lost. Saline shows it alive; iodine shows its nuclei.","D":"Axonemes are visible in the iodine-stained cyst (p.59).","E":"Median bodies are labelled on the iodine cyst image (p.59)."},"img":"slides_i/L5_58.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A cyst is examined first in normal saline and then in iodine. Which statement about what each preparation reveals is correct?","o":{"C":"Normal saline shows cigar-shaped or rounded chromatoid bodies; iodine stains the glycogen mass and makes the nuclei easier to count","D":"Iodine shows the chromatoid bodies; normal saline shows the glycogen mass","A":"Both preparations show identical features, so only one is needed","E":"Normal saline is used only for motility of trophozoites and reveals nothing in cysts","B":"Iodine dissolves the cyst wall, which is how the nuclei are released and counted"},"r":{"C":"Correct — this is precisely why the slide shows the same cyst twice, side by side.","D":"Straight swap of the two preparations.","A":"If they showed the same thing the laboratory would not do both; the slide's whole point is the contrast.","E":"Normal saline does show motility, but the chromatoid bodies are demonstrated in it too.","B":"Iodine stains; it does not dissolve the wall. Counting nuclei through an intact wall is exactly what it enables."},"t":"NSS vs iodine","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L5_12.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>NSS vs iodine</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Number of stages predicts transmission","s":"Two flagellates are compared. One causes greenish-yellow vaginal discharge and passes between sexual partners; the other causes chronic fatty diarrhoea in children and spreads through drinking water. Which difference in their life cycles best explains why their transmission differs?","lec":"L5","sl":6,"c":"<b>Number of stages predicts transmission</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The first forms a resistant cyst; the second exists as a trophozoite alone","B":"The first exists as a trophozoite alone; the second also forms a resistant cyst","C":"The first has promastigote and amastigote stages; the second has a cyst","D":"Both form resistant cysts; the first sheds its cysts in genital secretions","E":"Both exist as trophozoites alone; the second survives water using its disc"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Swapped.","B":"Correct. Trichomonas vaginalis has 1 stage (trophozoite); Giardia intestinalis has 2 (trophozoite and cyst) (p.6). No cyst → no survival outside the host → direct (sexual) contact; a cyst → faecal–oral / water-borne spread.","C":"Promastigote/amastigote are the two stages of Leishmania (p.6), a blood/tissue flagellate.","D":"Trichomonas has no cyst stage.","E":"Giardia has a cyst, and the mature 4-nucleus cyst is its infective stage."},"img":"slides_i/L5_6.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the number and kind of life-cycle stages is correct?","o":{"B":"Trichomonas vaginalis has only a trophozoite; Entamoeba histolytica and Giardia intestinalis have a trophozoite and a cyst; Leishmania has a promastigote and an amastigote","C":"All four have a trophozoite and a cyst stage","A":"Trichomonas vaginalis has a trophozoite and a cyst, which is how it survives outside the host","E":"Leishmania has a trophozoite and a cyst; promastigote and amastigote are its vector stages only","D":"Giardia has only a trophozoite, which is why transmission requires direct sexual contact"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The slide sorts the four organisms by stage count on purpose, because the number of stages predicts how each one is transmitted.","C":"Trichomonas has NO cyst — which is why it needs direct contact and cannot survive a faecal–oral journey — and Leishmania's two stages are not trophozoite and cyst.","A":"A cyst is exactly what Trichomonas lacks; the absence is the point.","E":"Promastigote and amastigote ARE Leishmania's two stages; the amastigote is the intracellular form in the mammalian host, not a vector-only stage.","D":"Giardia has both a trophozoite and a cyst, and the mature 4-nucleus cyst is its infective stage."},"t":"How many stages","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L5_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>How many stages</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Balantidium — the control measure unique to it","s":"In a village outbreak of chronic diarrhoea with dysentery, stool samples show huge oval cells coated with cilia, each with a kidney-shaped macronucleus, and thick-walled cysts 40–65 µm across. Besides treating patients, which control measure does the lecture list for this organism but not for the other intestinal protozoa it covers?","lec":"L5","sl":25,"c":"<b>Balantidium — the control measure unique to it</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Washing vegetables before eating","B":"Sanitary disposal of human faeces","C":"Sanitary disposal of pig faeces","D":"Boiling tap water before drinking","E":"Treating asymptomatic human carriers"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Shared by E. histolytica, Balantidium and Giardia.","B":"Sanitary disposal is shared with E. histolytica and Giardia.","C":"Correct. Balantidium coli — pigs are the main host and transmission is zoonotic (p.25, p.28), so the control slide adds 'manage sanitary disposal pig feces' and 'treat infected humans and pigs' (p.32).","D":"'Boil tap water before drinking' appears on the Giardia slide (p.65), not Balantidium's.","E":"'Treat patients and carriers' is on the E. histolytica and Giardia slides."},"img":"slides_i/L5_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which control measure is specific to balantidiasis rather than to the other intestinal protozoa in this lecture?","o":{"C":"Sanitary management of PIG faeces, and treating infected pigs as well as infected people","B":"Never using human faeces as fertiliser","E":"Boiling drinking water","D":"Washing vegetables thoroughly before eating","A":"Cleaning contact lenses with proper solution rather than tap water"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Pigs are the main host and the infection is explicitly zoonotic, so the control slide adds pig-directed measures that the Entamoeba and Giardia slides do not carry.","B":"This appears on the Entamoeba, Balantidium AND Giardia control slides — it does not discriminate.","E":"Shared with Giardia and Entamoeba.","D":"Shared with Giardia and Entamoeba.","A":"That is the ACANTHAMOEBA keratitis advice — a different organism entirely."},"t":"Balantidium — the pig link","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L5_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Balantidium — the pig link</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"E. histolytica — sexual transmission","s":"A 26-year-old man who has sex with men develops tenesmus and mucous bloody diarrhoea. His trichrome smear shows trophozoites with a central karyosome and ingested red cells. He drinks only bottled water, eats only home-cooked food, and no one else at home is ill. Which mode of transmission from the lecture best explains his infection?","lec":"L5","sl":13,"c":"<b>E. histolytica — sexual transmission</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Water-borne transmission through bathing water","B":"Sexual transmission through anal–oral contact","C":"Soil transmission through broken skin","D":"Vector-borne transmission by house flies","E":"Zoonotic transmission from contact with pigs"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Water-borne spread requires swallowing mature cysts in drinking water, which the history excludes.","B":"Correct. Centric karyosome + ingested RBCs = E. histolytica. Its transmission list: faecal–oral (food-borne, water-borne), person to person, and sexual (anal–oral contact) (p.13, p.20). With food and water excluded, the sexual route fits.","C":"Soil transmission through skin is an Acanthamoeba (and hookworm/Strongyloides) idea.","D":"Not a route on the E. histolytica slide.","E":"Zoonotic transmission is listed for Balantidium (and Giardia), not E. histolytica."},"img":"slides_i/L5_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which list of transmission routes for Entamoeba histolytica matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Faecal–oral via food-borne and water-borne spread, person-to-person, and sexual transmission by anal–oral contact","E":"Water-borne only, since the cyst is destroyed by drying","A":"Vector-borne by houseflies, plus water-borne","D":"Skin penetration by trophozoites, plus faecal–oral","C":"Air-borne inhalation of cysts, plus faecal–oral"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the sexual route is the one most often forgotten even though it is printed on both the transmission and the learning-guidance slide.","E":"Food-borne, person-to-person and sexual routes are all listed as well.","A":"Houseflies are mechanical vectors discussed in entomology, not a route listed here.","D":"Skin penetration is not a route for any Entamoeba; ingestion of the MATURE CYST is the mode of infection.","C":"Air-borne egg inhalation is ENTEROBIUS, from L1."},"t":"Amoebiasis — routes of transmission","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L5_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Amoebiasis — routes of transmission</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Acanthamoeba — host and habitat","s":"A 38-year-old liver-transplant recipient develops a chronic granulomatous encephalitis. Brain biopsy shows trophozoites with spine-like projections and double-walled cysts. He never swims and wears no contact lenses, but spends his days servicing building cooling systems. Which statement about the organism is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":35,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — host and habitat</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Humans are accidental hosts; it normally lives in the small intestine of rodents","B":"Humans are accidental hosts; it normally lives in soil, fresh water and air-conditioners","C":"Pigs are the main hosts; it normally lives in their large intestine and passes to humans","D":"Humans are definitive hosts; it normally lives in the large intestine and is shed as cysts","E":"Snails are intermediate hosts; it normally lives in fresh water and penetrates skin"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Small-intestinal habitat (with rodents among the hosts) is Giardia (p.61).","B":"Correct. Acanthamoeba is free-living: soil, warm fresh water (lakes, ponds, rivers, hot springs), taps, swimming pools and air-conditioning units (p.35, p.44); humans and animals are accidental hosts (p.39, p.44).","C":"Pigs as main host = Balantidium coli (p.25).","D":"Large intestine and cyst shedding describe the Entamoeba species.","E":"Snail intermediate hosts and skin-penetrating stages belong to the trematodes (L4)."},"img":"slides_i/L5_35.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which habitat list for Acanthamoeba matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Soil and warm fresh water — lakes, ponds, rivers, hot springs — plus taps, swimming pools and air-conditioning units","D":"Only the human large intestine, from which it is shed in cysts","E":"Only sea water and estuaries","C":"Only the cornea of contact lens wearers","A":"Only the soil of endemic tropical countries, with no aquatic reservoir"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the air-conditioning unit is the item people miss — it explains cases with no swimming history at all.","D":"Acanthamoeba is FREE-LIVING; it does not colonise the gut.","E":"The listed waters are fresh, not marine.","C":"The cornea is a site of DISEASE, not the environmental habitat.","A":"The distribution is worldwide and explicitly includes several aquatic reservoirs."},"t":"Acanthamoeba — where it lives","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L5_35.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Acanthamoeba — where it lives</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L5|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Giardia — epidemiology","s":"A day-care centre reports several toddlers with weeks of foul-smelling, greasy diarrhoea without blood. Their stools show oval cysts with a well-defined wall, up to four nuclei, axonemes running along the middle and median bodies. Which statement about the epidemiology of this infection is correct?","lec":"L5","sl":55,"c":"<b>Giardia — epidemiology</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is found worldwide, and its prevalence is higher in children than in adults","B":"It occurs mainly where pigs are kept, and its prevalence in humans is under 1%","C":"It infects about 50 million people, and about 90% of them are asymptomatic carriers","D":"It is confined to the tropics, and its prevalence is higher in adults than in children","E":"It is found worldwide, but it spreads mainly by sexual contact between adults"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Giardia intestinalis: worldwide, ~280 million new cases per year, prevalence 15–20% in children (p.55); 'more prevalent in children' (p.66).","B":"Pigs and <1% human prevalence are the Balantidium coli figures (p.25).","C":"50 million infected and 90% asymptomatic are the E. histolytica figures (p.8).","D":"Distribution is worldwide and children carry the higher burden.","E":"Sexual (anal–oral) spread is listed, but it is not the main route and does not explain a day-care outbreak."},"img":"slides_i/L5_55.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which epidemiological statement about giardiasis matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Distribution is worldwide, with roughly 280 million new cases a year and prevalence of 15–20% in children, in whom it is commoner than in adults","B":"It is confined to the tropics and affects mainly adults","C":"Prevalence in children is under 1%, the same figure given for Balantidium coli","E":"About 50 million people are infected worldwide, of whom 90% are asymptomatic carriers","D":"It occurs only where pigs are kept"},"r":{"A":"Correct on distribution, incidence and the age skew.","B":"Distribution is worldwide, and children carry the higher burden.","C":"Under 1% is BALANTIDIUM's human prevalence figure, imported here.","E":"50 million infected with 90% asymptomatic is the ENTAMOEBA HISTOLYTICA slide.","D":"The pig association belongs to Balantidium coli."},"t":"Giardia — the numbers","sl":55,"img":"slides_i/L5_55.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Giardia — the numbers</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233913444},{"id":"I|L6|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Cyclospora — the stage in fresh stool","s":"A 52-year-old kidney-transplant recipient on immunosuppressive drugs has had watery diarrhoea without blood for six weeks since a trip abroad. A modified acid-fast stool smear shows round oocysts about 10 µm across; some stain deep red, others pale pink or not at all. Which description fits the stage the laboratory is looking at?","lec":"L6","sl":16,"c":"<b>Cyclospora — the stage in fresh stool</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Spore with a coiled polar filament, infective","B":"Unsporulated oocyst, already infective","C":"Unsporulated oocyst, not yet infective","D":"Sporulated oocyst with two sporocysts, not yet infective","E":"Sporulated oocyst with four free sporozoites, infective"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A spore with a coiled polar filament is Microsporidia — 1–2 µm, Gram-chromotrope, not acid-fast.","B":"Right stage, wrong infectivity: 'already infective' is the Cryptosporidium property borrowed. Cyclospora's unsporulated oocyst needs 7–15 days before it can infect.","C":"Correct. Round + 8–12 µm (here ~10 µm) + acid-fast, with the patchy dark / pale / unstained staining the lecturer described, is Cyclospora cayetanensis. It is passed unsporulated (diagnostic stage, p.18–19) and needs 7–15 days outside the host to sporulate (p.16), so what is on the slide cannot yet infect anyone.","D":"Two sporocysts (each with two sporozoites) is Cyclospora's sporulated, infective oocyst — but that forms in the environment, not in fresh stool. And a sporulated oocyst is by definition infective, so 'not yet infective' contradicts itself.","E":"Four free sporozoites with no sporocyst is the sporulated oocyst of Cryptosporidium (4–6 µm, p.10) — the only coccidian shed already sporulated."},"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which stain-and-size combination identifies which organism?","o":{"C":"mAFB 4–6 µm sporulated = Cryptosporidium; mAFB 8–12 µm unsporulated = Cyclospora; mAFB 25 × 15 µm ellipsoid = Cystoisospora; gram-chromotrope 1–2 µm = Microsporidia","E":"mAFB 1–2 µm = Cryptosporidium; gram-chromotrope 4–6 µm = Microsporidia","A":"All four are modified acid-fast and are separated by size alone","D":"All four are gram-chromotrope positive and are separated by sporulation state","B":"mAFB 10 µm sporulated = Cyclospora; mAFB 4–6 µm unsporulated = Cryptosporidium"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Learn it as a four-row grid: stain, size, sporulation state. Only the stain and the size change between the past-paper stems — everything else is identical.","E":"Both are reversed, and Microsporidia is never acid-fast.","A":"Microsporidia is the exception: gram-chromotrope only.","D":"Only Microsporidia uses gram-chromotrope.","B":"Both the sporulation states are reversed. Crypto is SPORULATED; Cyclospora is UNSPORULATED."},"t":"The grid","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The grid</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Cryptosporidium — infective the moment it is passed","s":"A 41-year-old man with AIDS on a general medical ward has profuse watery diarrhoea without blood. A modified acid-fast stool smear shows many round red bodies about 5 µm across. The infection-control nurse asks from what point his freshly passed stool is a risk to other patients. What is the correct answer?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium — infective the moment it is passed</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Immediately, since the stage he passes is already infective","B":"After 7–15 days, once the stage he passes has sporulated outside","C":"At no point, since the stage he passes infects cattle but not people","D":"After a second sporocyst forms in the stage he passes, outside the body","E":"After several weeks in moist soil, once the stage he passes embryonates"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Round, red, 4–6 µm on modified acid-fast = Cryptosporidium. Its oocyst is passed already sporulated — p.10: 'sporulated oocysts (infective form) are passed in feces' — so the infective and diagnostic stages are the same (p.12) and person-to-person spread is listed (p.11). That is why it causes ward, daycare and water outbreaks.","B":"7–15 days to sporulate is Cyclospora cayetanensis (8–12 µm, p.16).","C":"C. parvum is zoonotic (the cow in the p.12 life cycle), but humans are infected and the p.11 transmission list includes person-to-person.","D":"Maturing from one sporocyst to two is Cystoisospora belli (unsporulated = 1 sporocyst, sporulated = 2; p.22).","E":"Embryonation in moist soil is what soil-transmitted helminth eggs do (L1), not a protozoan oocyst."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An HIV patient has chronic watery diarrhoea. Modified acid-fast staining shows numerous red bodies 4–6 µm across. What is the diagnostic stage?","o":{"E":"Unsporulated oocyst","A":"Sporulated oocyst","D":"Cyst","C":"Spore","B":"Trophozoite"},"r":{"E":"The trap, and it is right for BOTH of the other coccidia — so the wrong answer is correct two-thirds of the time and the examiner knows it. Say it as a pair: 'Crypto sporulated, the other two unsporulated.'","A":"Correct. Cryptosporidium's oocyst is already SPORULATED when it is passed, which is also why it can autoinfect and why the infection becomes so severe in AIDS.","D":"'Cyst' belongs to the amoebae and Giardia (L5).","C":"'Spore' is Microsporidia, which is not acid-fast.","B":"Trophozoites are intracellular here and are not the diagnostic stage in stool."},"t":"Cryptosporidium","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Microsporidia — mode of infection","s":"In 2024 several residents of a Bangkok condominium that shares one water tank develop red, irritated eyes. One resident, who has AIDS, also has chronic watery diarrhoea; her stool shows oval bodies 1–2 µm across on a special stain, some crossed by a pale stripe. How did she most likely acquire the intestinal infection?","lec":"L6","sl":31,"c":"<b>Microsporidia — mode of infection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of trophozoites in contaminated water or food","B":"Ingestion of sporulated oocysts in contaminated water or food","C":"Ingestion of mature cysts in contaminated water or food","D":"Ingestion of spores in contaminated water or food","E":"Ingestion of unsporulated oocysts in contaminated water or food"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Trophozoites are not an infective stage by ingestion for any organism in this lecture.","B":"Ingestion of a sporulated oocyst is the mode of infection for the three coccidia (Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora) — the right formula attached to the wrong organism.","C":"'Mature cysts' is the Entamoeba histolytica / Giardia answer from L5. The lab rule: these organisms have an oocyst or a spore, never a cyst.","D":"Correct. 1–2 µm oval bodies with a belt-like stripe (the coiled polar filament) on a special stain (Gram-chromotrope) = Microsporidia — the organism the lecturer linked to the 2024 Bangkok condominium water-tank outbreak with conjunctivitis. Its only stage is the spore, which is both infective and diagnostic (p.32–33).","E":"Unsporulated oocysts are what Cyclospora and Cystoisospora pass in stool; they are not infective, so they are never the mode of infection."},"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which three words, taken together, identify Microsporidia and exclude everything else in the lecture before you read the options?","o":{"B":"Barrel-shaped · iodine · 50 µm","E":"Ellipsoidal · modified acid-fast · 25 × 15 µm","C":"Round · modified acid-fast · 10 µm","A":"Round · modified acid-fast · 4–6 µm","D":"Belt-like · gram-chromotrope · 1–2 µm"},"r":{"B":"That is a Trichuris egg from L1.","E":"That is Cystoisospora belli.","C":"That is Cyclospora cayetanensis.","A":"That is Cryptosporidium.","D":"Correct, and this three-word giveaway has been reused for at least five years. The 'belt' is the coiled polar filament inside the spore. Note that the gold-standard confirmatory method is ELECTRON MICROSCOPY, which makes sense at 1–2 µm."},"t":"Microsporidia","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Microsporidia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Cyclospora — why household contacts stay well","s":"Twenty guests develop prolonged watery diarrhoea about a week after a wedding lunch; each guest's stool shows round acid-fast oocysts 8–12 µm across. None of their household members who skipped the lunch fall ill, although they share toilets and kitchens with the guests. Which feature of the organism best explains this?","lec":"L6","sl":16,"c":"<b>Cyclospora — why household contacts stay well</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Oocysts passed in stool are destroyed by chlorine in household tap water","B":"Oocysts passed in stool need 7–15 days outside a host to infect","C":"Oocysts passed in stool infect only people who are immunocompromised","D":"Oocysts passed in stool need a snail host before they can infect people","E":"Oocysts passed in stool infect animals but not other people"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Chlorine resistance is a Cryptosporidium property (p.10); nothing on the slides says chlorine destroys Cyclospora, and tap water would not explain why toilet-sharing contacts escape.","B":"Correct. Round, acid-fast, 8–12 µm = Cyclospora cayetanensis. It is shed unsporulated and needs 7–15 days to sporulate (p.16), so fresh stool is not infectious — its transmission slide lists only food-borne (starred) and water-borne routes, with no person-to-person (p.17). A shared food vehicle explains the cluster; the lag explains the absence of secondary cases.","C":"Healthy people are infected too (asymptomatic or self-limited watery diarrhoea, p.7, p.19) — the guests here are an ordinary wedding party.","D":"A snail intermediate host belongs to the trematodes (L4); the coccidia need no second host.","E":"Zoonotic transmission is listed for Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia, not Cyclospora — and here humans are plainly the ones infected."},"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Cyclospora and Cryptosporidium share a stain, a clinical picture, a habitat and a specimen. What are the only two things that separate them?","o":{"E":"Nothing separates them; they are reported together","C":"Stain (acid-fast vs gram-chromotrope) and habitat","A":"Host immune status and specimen type","D":"Shape (round vs ellipsoid) and the number of sporocysts","B":"Size (4–6 vs 8–12 µm) and sporulation state (sporulated vs unsporulated)"},"r":{"E":"They are separately reportable organisms with different sizes.","C":"Both are modified acid-fast, and both live in the small bowel.","A":"Both affect the immunocompromised most severely and both are found in stool.","D":"Both are round; the sporocyst count is not the taught discriminator.","B":"Correct, and the exam uses both. A year-59 item asked how you know it is Cyclospora on mAFB and keyed '10 micron, round, UNSPORULATED' — both halves of that answer matter. If a stem tells you the oocyst is unsporulated, Cryptosporidium is excluded outright."},"t":"Cyclospora vs Cryptosporidium","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Cyclospora vs Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Cystoisospora — what the infective oocyst holds","s":"A 36-year-old woman with HIV has had watery diarrhoea and weight loss for four months. Her stool contains ellipsoidal acid-fast oocysts about 28 × 14 µm, each holding a single rounded mass. What did the stage that infected her contain?","lec":"L6","sl":22,"c":"<b>Cystoisospora — what the infective oocyst holds</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Two sporocysts, each holding two sporozoites","B":"Two sporocysts, each holding four sporozoites","C":"A coiled polar filament, with no sporozoites","D":"One sporocyst holding four sporozoites","E":"Four sporozoites lying free, with no sporocyst"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Two sporocysts × two sporozoites is Cyclospora cayetanensis's sporulated oocyst (p.16).","B":"Correct. Ellipsoidal, 20–33 × 10–19 µm, one inner mass = the unsporulated (diagnostic) oocyst of Cystoisospora belli — the lecturer's 'boat with one seat'. The infective stage is the sporulated oocyst with 2 sporocysts, each holding 4 sporozoites (8 in all) — p.22–23.","C":"A coiled polar filament belongs to the Microsporidia spore (p.31).","D":"One sporocyst is the unsporulated oocyst she is passing (the diagnostic stage) — it holds no sporozoites yet and is not what infected her.","E":"Four free sporozoites with no sporocyst is Cryptosporidium (p.10)."},"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Stool from a patient with months of watery diarrhoea and weight loss shows an ellipsoidal structure with a 'neck-like appearance', 25 × 15 µm, modified acid-fast. What is it?","o":{"A":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","D":"Cryptosporidium spp.","B":"Cystoisospora belli","E":"Microsporidia","C":"Blastocystis sp."},"r":{"A":"Cyclospora is round and 8–12 µm.","D":"Cryptosporidium is round and 4–6 µm.","B":"Correct. 'Neck-like appearance' is the papers' own phrase for the tapered end of the ellipsoid — it does not appear on the slide, which is why it is worth planting now. A neat size anchor from the year-59 key: it is about the size of an Opisthorchis viverrini egg (25 × 12 µm).","E":"Microsporidia is 1–2 µm and gram-chromotrope.","C":"Blastocystis is a different organism with a large central vacuole."},"t":"Cystoisospora","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Cystoisospora</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"The zoonotic, person-to-person pair","s":"A veterinary student spends a week nursing calves with diarrhoea and then develops watery diarrhoea that settles on its own. Her stool shows round red bodies 4–6 µm across on modified acid-fast staining. Which organism has the same two extra transmission routes — animal-to-human and person-to-person — listed for her pathogen?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>The zoonotic, person-to-person pair</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","C":"Trichomonas vaginalis","D":"Cystoisospora belli","E":"Microsporidia"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"E. histolytica spreads person-to-person (including oral–anal sex) but has no animal reservoir — it is the past papers' 'not zoonotic' answer.","B":"Cyclospora lists food-borne (raspberries) and water-borne only (p.17) — its oocyst must mature 7–15 days outside first.","C":"T. vaginalis spreads person-to-person sexually but is not zoonotic.","D":"Cystoisospora lists food-borne and water-borne only (p.23).","E":"Correct. Calves + round red 4–6 µm acid-fast bodies = Cryptosporidium (C. parvum, reservoir cattle). Its transmission slide lists food-borne, water-borne, person-to-person and zoonotic (p.11); the only other organism in the lecture with the same four-item list is Microsporidia (p.32). Both shed a stage that is infective at once."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which of these protozoa is NOT zoonotic?","o":{"C":"Balantidium coli","D":"Cryptosporidium spp.","E":"Entamoeba histolytica","A":"Toxoplasma gondii","B":"Microsporidia"},"r":{"C":"Reservoirs include pigs, monkeys, dogs and rats.","D":"Reservoirs include cattle, buffalo, goats and sheep.","E":"Correct, and this item has been set twice with different option sets (years 59 and 60) and the same key. E. histolytica spreads faecal–orally through food, water, person-to-person and sexual (oral–anal) contact — but it is the HUMAN-ONLY one.","A":"The cat is the definitive host.","B":"Microsporidia is zoonotic. ⭐ Note the asymmetry inside L6: Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia are zoonotic; Cyclospora and Cystoisospora are not."},"t":"Zoonosis","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Zoonosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Water supply — what actually stops it","s":"A city's water supply, chlorinated at the standard dose, is traced as the source of an outbreak of watery diarrhoea affecting hundreds of thousands of people; patients with AIDS become chronically ill, and their stool shows round acid-fast oocysts 4–6 µm across. Which change at the treatment plant would most likely have prevented it?","lec":"L6","sl":8,"c":"<b>Water supply — what actually stops it</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Filtering the water before distribution","B":"Doubling the chlorine dose before distribution","C":"Freezing stored water before distribution","D":"Adding salt to the water before distribution","E":"Adjusting the pH of the water before distribution"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. 4–6 µm round acid-fast oocysts in a municipal outbreak = Cryptosporidium (Milwaukee 1993: >400,000 affected, 100 deaths, p.8). Its oocyst is resistant to chlorine, drying, freezing and salt water (p.10), so the prevention slide says cleaned / filtered / sterilised water (p.14) — physical removal, not more chlorine.","B":"The oocyst is chlorine-resistant (p.10); a supply chlorinated to standard is exactly what failed.","C":"The oocyst is listed as resistant to freezing (p.10).","D":"The oocyst is listed as resistant to salt water (p.10).","E":"'Add chlorine and adjust the pH' is the past-paper key for the free-living amoeba in a swimming pool (L5) — same-sounding question, different organism, opposite answer."},"img":"slides_i/L6_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why is chlorination the wrong answer for Cryptosporidium, and what is the right one?","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are chlorine-RESISTANT; prevention is filtration or sterilisation of drinking water","E":"Chlorine is correct for Cryptosporidium but not for Giardia","B":"Chlorination works but only at pH below 6","D":"Boiling is ineffective because the oocyst is heat-stable","C":"No preventive measure exists"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and the Milwaukee 1993 outbreak is the demonstration. ⚠ Contrast the free-living amoeba item from L5, where a child swims in a pool and the key IS 'add chlorine and adjust the pH'. Same-sounding question, opposite answers — read which organism the stem gives you.","E":"The opposite of the truth for Cryptosporidium.","B":"pH manipulation is the swimming-pool answer, not the Cryptosporidium one.","D":"Boiling is effective; the slide's wording is cleaned, filtered or sterilised water.","C":"Filtration is an effective and examinable measure."},"t":"Prevention","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L6_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Cryptosporidium — how the burden keeps rising","s":"A man with advanced AIDS keeps passing large numbers of round, red, 4–6 µm oocysts on modified acid-fast staining for months, although he has drunk only boiled water since his diagnosis. Which feature of this parasite best explains how his intestinal burden is maintained without re-exposure?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium — how the burden keeps rising</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its oocysts are already sporulated when they form in the gut","B":"Its oocysts leave the gut unsporulated and mature on the perianal skin","C":"Its spores inject their contents into neighbouring enterocytes","D":"Its trophozoites encyst and then re-excyst within the colon","E":"Its oocysts carry two sporocysts that hatch in the large bowel"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Cryptosporidium's oocyst is sporulated (infective) when it forms (p.10, p.12), so it can excyst and re-infect without leaving the gut — autoinfection. The lecturer named autoinfection for it, and the BM30 key lists it with Taenia solium, Hymenolepis nana and Strongyloides stercoralis. This is also why it becomes relentless in AIDS.","B":"Maturation on the perianal skin is Enterobius-type behaviour (L1); Cryptosporidium's oocyst is sporulated when passed, not after.","C":"Injecting contents through a polar filament is the Microsporidia spore mechanism.","D":"Encystation is the amoeba/flagellate vocabulary of L5; the coccidia form oocysts from a zygote, not cysts from trophozoites.","E":"Two sporocysts belong to Cyclospora and Cystoisospora; Cryptosporidium has no sporocysts (4 free sporozoites), and its habitat is the small intestine."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Cryptosporidium belongs to the autoinfection club even though the BM33 slides do not say so. What makes it possible?","o":{"D":"It survives in the bile duct and re-seeds the bowel","C":"Its trophozoite is acid-resistant and can re-invade","E":"It produces an egg that embryonates in the bowel lumen","B":"It migrates from the perianal skin back into the rectum","A":"Its oocyst is already sporulated when it forms, so it can excyst without ever leaving the gut"},"r":{"D":"Biliary cryptosporidiosis occurs in AIDS but is not the autoinfection mechanism.","C":"Trophozoites are intracellular and are not the re-infecting form.","E":"Sporozoa do not lay eggs.","B":"That route belongs to Enterobius.","A":"Correct. The BM30 answer key lists it explicitly alongside Taenia solium, Hymenolepis nana and Strongyloides stercoralis — and it follows logically from the sporulated oocyst. It is also why cryptosporidiosis becomes so severe in AIDS."},"t":"Autoinfection","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Autoinfection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-crypto","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"An HIV patient presents with chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool stained with modified acid-fast shows numerous red vacuoles 4–6 µm in diameter. What is this diagnostic stage?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q24 — mAFB, 4–6 µm, 'what is the diagnostic stage?'</span>","o":{"A":"Spore","E":"Unsporulated oocyst","D":"Cyst","C":"Sporulated oocyst","B":"Trophozoite"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A spore is Microsporidia — and Microsporidia is never acid-fast.","E":"Correct for Cyclospora and Cystoisospora, which is precisely why it is the distractor here.","D":"A cyst is the L5 vocabulary (Entamoeba, Giardia, Balantidium).","C":"Correct, and set in near-identical words for three years running.","B":"Not a stool diagnostic stage for this organism."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-crypto","BM29 Q24"]},{"id":"I|L6|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-micro","t":"Microsporidia","s":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive man has chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool examination reveals oval organisms with a belt-like appearance, about 2 µm, using gram-chromotrope staining. What is the most likely causative agent?","lec":"L6","sl":31,"c":"<b>Microsporidia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q40 · year 57 — belt-like, gram-chromotrope, 1–2 µm</span>","o":{"B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","A":"Cryptosporidium spp.","D":"Microsporidia","C":"Cystoisospora belli","E":"Blastocystis sp."},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Cyclospora is 8–12 µm and modified acid-fast.","A":"Cryptosporidium is 4–6 µm and modified ACID-FAST.","D":"Correct. Five appearances across four cohorts with the same three words. The 'belt' is the coiled polar filament. One BM30 version says DYSENTERY rather than watery diarrhoea — ignore that; the stain decides.","C":"Cystoisospora is 25 × 15 µm and modified acid-fast.","E":"Blastocystis is much larger with a prominent central vacuole. ⭐ Every distractor here would be acid-fast, so naming gram-chromotrope excludes them all before you read the list."},"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-micro","BM29 Q40","LS54 Q-micro (57)","BM28 Q46"]},{"id":"I|L6|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-aids","t":"Mode of infection","s":"A 50-year-old man with AIDS has a 3-month history of diarrhoea and 10 kg weight loss. What is the most likely mode of infection?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — AIDS, 3-month diarrhoea, 10 kg weight loss, 'mode of infection?'</span>","o":{"E":"Ingestion of unwashed vegetables","A":"Ingestion of mature cysts","B":"Ingestion of trophozoites","D":"Ingestion of uncooked pork","C":"Ingestion of water contaminated with sporulated oocysts"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Unwashed vegetables points at the soil-transmitted nematodes. ⭐ Every distractor is the right answer to a DIFFERENT lecture.","A":"'Mature cyst' is the answer for Entamoeba histolytica and Giardia (L5).","B":"Trophozoites are never infective by ingestion.","D":"Uncooked pork points at Taenia or Trichinella.","C":"Correct. The organism is never named — the lecturer's own written feedback explains the reasoning: AIDS plus chronic diarrhoea and weight loss means the opportunistic sporozoa group, so the mode of infection is ingestion of a sporulated oocyst."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-cysto","t":"Cystoisospora","s":"An HIV patient has chronic watery diarrhoea for three months. Stool examination shows an ellipsoidal structure with a neck-like appearance. What is the most likely mode of infection of this agent?","lec":"L6","sl":22,"c":"<b>Cystoisospora</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 Q35 — ellipsoidal with neck-like appearance</span>","o":{"B":"Ingestion of a sporulated oocyst","D":"Ingestion of spores","C":"Ingestion of cysts","A":"Ingestion of mature cysts","E":"Ingestion of trophozoites"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The organism is Cystoisospora belli, and every coccidian in this lecture is acquired by swallowing a SPORULATED oocyst — note that this is the INFECTIVE form even for the two organisms whose oocysts are passed UNSPORULATED.","D":"Spores are Microsporidia.","C":"Cysts belong to the amoebae and Giardia.","A":"Mature cysts are the E. histolytica and Giardia answer.","E":"Trophozoites are never infective by ingestion."},"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q35"]},{"id":"I|L6|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q41","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"An outbreak of diarrhoea among HIV patients is reported in Town A. Investigation of the town's water supply finds a 5 µm round organism. What is the most likely pathogen?","lec":"L6","sl":8,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q41 — outbreak in HIV patients, 5 µm round organism in the water supply</span>","o":{"E":"Cryptosporidium spp.","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis","D":"Cystoisospora belli","A":"Blastocystis sp.","C":"Microsporidia"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. A 5 µm round organism in a WATER SUPPLY causing an outbreak in immunocompromised people is Cryptosporidium — and the water route matters because the oocyst is chlorine-resistant, so filtration is what fails or succeeds.","B":"Cyclospora is 8–12 µm and is classically food-borne (raspberries and other fresh produce).","D":"Cystoisospora is 25 × 15 µm and ellipsoidal.","A":"Blastocystis is larger and is not the outbreak organism here.","C":"Microsporidia is 1–2 µm."},"img":"slides_i/L6_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L6|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q8 (59)","t":"Cyclospora","s":"On a modified acid-fast stain, how do you know an oocyst is Cyclospora cayetanensis?","lec":"L6","sl":16,"c":"<b>Cyclospora</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Q8 cohort 59 — 'how do you know it is Cyclospora on mAFB?'</span>","o":{"D":"About 5 µm, round, and sporulated","E":"About 10 µm, round, with a large central vacuole","A":"About 10 µm, round, and UNSPORULATED","C":"About 25 µm, ellipsoidal, with a neck-like appearance","B":"About 1–2 µm with a belt-like appearance"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"5 µm and sporulated is Cryptosporidium.","E":"A large central vacuole describes Blastocystis; this was the actual distractor offered.","A":"Correct, and BOTH halves of the answer matter — the size and the sporulation state. Cyclospora's oocyst is passed unsporulated, which excludes Cryptosporidium outright.","C":"That is Cystoisospora belli.","B":"That is Microsporidia, and it would not be acid-fast at all."},"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q-zoo (60)","t":"Zoonosis","s":"Which of the following does NOT have zoonotic transmission?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"<b>Zoonosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 — 'which is NOT a zoonosis?', cohorts 59 and 60</span>","o":{"D":"Entamoeba histolytica","A":"Microsporidia","E":"Blastocystis sp.","C":"Toxoplasma gondii","B":"Cryptosporidium spp."},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the same key was given both times the question was set. The answer key's note: E. histolytica spreads faecal–orally via food, water, person-to-person and sexual contact, but has no animal reservoir.","A":"Microsporidia is zoonotic.","E":"Blastocystis is zoonotic.","C":"The cat is Toxoplasma's definitive host.","B":"Cattle, buffalo, goats and sheep are reservoirs for C. parvum."},"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 Q-zoo (59)"]},{"id":"I|L6|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q27","t":"When routine stool exam is negative","s":"An AIDS patient has chronic watery diarrhoea. A routine stool examination is reported as NEGATIVE. Which further test is most likely to make the parasitological diagnosis?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"<b>When routine stool exam is negative</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q27 — AIDS patient with chronic watery diarrhoea whose routine stool examination is NEGATIVE; what should be requested?</span>","o":{"C":"Stool examination with modified acid-fast staining","B":"Stool examination with Giemsa stain","E":"Eosinophil count","A":"Duodenal aspiration for adult worms","D":"Serology for anti-parasite antibody"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and this is the whole reason the coccidia are taught as a block. Their oocysts are 4–25 µm, colourless and easily missed on a routine saline/iodine wet mount — you have to ASK for modified acid-fast staining. A 'negative' routine stool in an AIDS patient with chronic diarrhoea never excludes Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora or Cystoisospora.","B":"Giemsa is for blood parasites — malaria, Leishmania, Trypanosoma.","E":"Eosinophilia points to helminths; the intestinal sporozoa typically do NOT raise eosinophils.","A":"Duodenal aspiration is a Strongyloides/Giardia manoeuvre, and these organisms are not adult worms.","D":"Serology is not the routine route for any of these four. Remember the one exception in this lecture: Microsporidia needs gram-chromotrope, not acid-fast, with electron microscopy as the gold standard."},"img":"slides_i/L6_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L6|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"written Q4 (57)","t":"Chronic diarrhoea in HIV — the four","s":"Which set names the organisms that cause CHRONIC WATERY DIARRHOEA in an HIV/AIDS patient?","lec":"L6","sl":6,"c":"<b>Chronic diarrhoea in HIV — the four</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 57 & 60 (written) — name the four organisms causing chronic watery diarrhoea in an HIV patient</span>","o":{"C":"Cryptosporidium spp., Entamoeba histolytica, Balantidium coli, Trichuris trichiura","E":"Cryptosporidium spp., Cyclospora cayetanensis, Cystoisospora belli, Microsporidia","D":"Giardia intestinalis, Cyclospora cayetanensis, Plasmodium spp., Microsporidia","B":"Cystoisospora belli, Toxoplasma gondii, Leishmania spp., Blastocystis sp.","A":"Cryptosporidium spp., Cystoisospora belli, Schistosoma mekongi, Trichomonas vaginalis"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"Entamoeba, Balantidium and Trichuris cause DYSENTERY — bloody and mucoid, not watery.","E":"Correct, and this exact four-item list has been an examiner's written question in at least two cohorts. All four are opportunistic, all four give ACUTE SELF-LIMITED watery diarrhoea in the healthy and CHRONIC watery diarrhoea in the immunocompromised.","D":"Plasmodium is a blood parasite and causes no diarrhoea.","B":"Toxoplasma gives brain lesions in AIDS; Leishmania gives visceral disease.","A":"Schistosoma and Trichomonas are in neither the right organ nor the right lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L6_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["LS54 written Q4 (60)"]},{"id":"I|L6|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q17 (58)","t":"Megaloblastic anaemia","s":"Which parasitic infection is LEAST likely to cause megaloblastic anaemia?","lec":"L6","sl":25,"c":"<b>Megaloblastic anaemia</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 58 Q17 — which parasitic infection is LEAST likely to cause megaloblastic anaemia?</span>","o":{"D":"Isosporiasis (Cystoisospora belli)","B":"Amoebiasis","A":"Cyclosporiasis","C":"Cryptosporidiosis","E":"Microsporidiasis"},"a":"B","r":{"D":"Cystoisospora blunts duodenal and jejunal villi.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. Entamoeba histolytica invades the colon and causes blood loss — an IRON-DEFICIENCY, microcytic picture. The other four damage the SMALL-BOWEL villi, causing malabsorption of vitamin B12 and folate, which is macrocytic.","A":"Cyclospora does the same in the small bowel.","C":"Cryptosporidium likewise causes villous atrophy.","E":"Microsporidia infects enterocytes directly. Do not confuse this with the OTHER classic cause of megaloblastic anaemia in the block — Diphyllobothrium latum (L3), which competes for B12 in the ileum."},"img":"slides_i/L6_25.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L6|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Blood in the stool — the wrong lecture","s":"A 44-year-old man with HIV and a low CD4 count has had bloody mucoid diarrhoea with tenesmus and lower abdominal pain for ten days. His intern suspects one of the opportunistic sporozoa. What is the stool examination most likely to show?","lec":"L6","sl":7,"c":"<b>Blood in the stool — the wrong lecture</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Motile trophozoites containing ingested red cells","B":"Oval spores crossed by a belt-like stripe","C":"Round acid-fast oocysts that are already sporulated","D":"Round acid-fast oocysts that are still unsporulated","E":"Ellipsoidal acid-fast oocysts holding one sporocyst"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Dysentery (blood + mucus) with tenesmus means invasion of the large bowel — Entamoeba histolytica, whose diagnostic finding is the trophozoite with ingested RBCs (L5). The L6 organisms live in the small intestine and give chronic watery diarrhoea without blood (p.7) — a low CD4 count does not change that. BM30/31/32 set this item with L6 stages as bait. (Only when a stem also gives an acid-fast stain and a size — as one recalled BM30 stem did — does the stain/size override the stool description.)","B":"Belt-striped spores = Microsporidia: watery diarrhoea (plus eye/systemic disease), no dysentery.","C":"Sporulated round acid-fast oocysts = Cryptosporidium: watery diarrhoea without blood.","D":"Unsporulated round acid-fast oocysts = Cyclospora: watery diarrhoea without blood.","E":"Ellipsoidal oocyst with one sporocyst = Cystoisospora belli: watery diarrhoea without blood."},"img":"slides_i/L6_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Two children in the same ward have watery diarrhoea. One has Cryptosporidium, the other Cyclospora. Why is only one of them an immediate infection-control problem for the ward?","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium sheds SPORULATED (already infective) oocysts, so person-to-person spread is possible at once; Cyclospora sheds UNSPORULATED oocysts that need 7–15 days outside the host to become infective","C":"Cyclospora sheds sporulated oocysts and Cryptosporidium unsporulated ones, so Cyclospora is the ward problem","B":"Both shed sporulated oocysts; the difference is only that Cryptosporidium oocysts are smaller","D":"Neither is transmissible person-to-person; both require a food or water vehicle","E":"Cryptosporidium is the problem because it is the only one of the two that stains with modified acid-fast"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and this single fact explains everything downstream — why Cryptosporidium causes daycare and hospital outbreaks and why Cyclospora shows up instead as food-borne clusters.","C":"Straight inversion of the two life cycles.","B":"Size differs too (4–6 µm vs 8–12 µm), but the sporulation state is the difference that matters clinically.","D":"Cryptosporidium's transmission slide explicitly lists person-to-person and zoonotic routes.","E":"Both stain with modified acid-fast — that is exactly why they are confused and why size and sporulation are the discriminators."},"t":"Sporulated or not — the key split","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L6_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Sporulated or not — the key split</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Size ladder from lab descriptions","s":"An HIV clinic's laboratory log lists four stool findings: (1) round acid-fast oocysts, unsporulated, staining patchily; (2) ellipsoidal oocysts visible even without staining; (3) oval spores with a belt-like stripe on Gram-chromotrope; (4) round acid-fast oocysts passed already sporulated. Which order runs from the smallest organism to the largest?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"<b>Size ladder from lab descriptions</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"3 → 1 → 4 → 2","B":"4 → 1 → 3 → 2","C":"4 → 3 → 1 → 2","D":"3 → 4 → 1 → 2","E":"3 → 4 → 2 → 1"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Swaps Cyclospora and Cryptosporidium — the sporulated round one is the smaller (4–6 vs 8–12 µm), and size is the only separator the lab talk allows.","B":"Places both Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora below Microsporidia.","C":"Puts Cryptosporidium below Microsporidia — but the spore (1–2 µm) is the smallest thing in the lecture.","D":"Correct. Identify first, then size: (3) Microsporidia 1–2 µm → (4) Cryptosporidium 4–6 µm → (1) Cyclospora 8–12 µm → (2) Cystoisospora belli 20–33 × 10–19 µm. The lecturer's one-breath version: 'pen-dot, four-to-six, eight-to-twelve, OV-egg'.","E":"Puts Cystoisospora below Cyclospora — the ellipsoidal one is the largest, which is why it shows on a simple smear."},"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which description of the mature oocyst is correct?","o":{"D":"Cryptosporidium: 4 sporozoites with NO sporocyst · Cyclospora: 2 sporocysts each holding 2 sporozoites · Cystoisospora: 2 sporocysts each holding 4 sporozoites","B":"Cryptosporidium: 2 sporocysts each holding 2 sporozoites · Cyclospora: 4 free sporozoites · Cystoisospora: 1 sporocyst holding 4 sporozoites","A":"All three contain 2 sporocysts with 4 sporozoites each","E":"Cryptosporidium: 8 sporozoites · Cyclospora: 8 sporozoites · Cystoisospora: 4 sporozoites","C":"Only Cystoisospora has sporozoites; the other two contain spores"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the arithmetic is worth holding: 4, then 2×2 = 4, then 2×4 = 8 — the biggest oocyst carries the most.","B":"Every assignment is shuffled. Cryptosporidium is the one with no sporocyst at all.","A":"That is Cystoisospora's arrangement applied to all three.","E":"Invented numbers; none of these is 8 except Cystoisospora's total.","C":"Spores are MICROSPORIDIA's stage — a different organism with a different stain."},"t":"Counting sporocysts and sporozoites","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Counting sporocysts and sporozoites</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Acid-fast negative — the next stain","s":"A 33-year-old woman with AIDS has chronic watery diarrhoea and, for the past two weeks, a red, irritated eye. Two stool smears stained by modified acid-fast show no organisms. Which investigation is most likely to reveal the cause?","lec":"L6","sl":31,"c":"<b>Acid-fast negative — the next stain</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Giemsa-stained thick and thin blood films","B":"Gram-chromotrope staining of the stool","C":"Simple smear of the stool for large oocysts","D":"Iodine wet mount of the stool for cysts","E":"A third modified acid-fast stool smear"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Giemsa blood films are for blood parasites (malaria, L7) — no use for an intestinal organism.","B":"Correct. Chronic watery diarrhoea in AIDS + conjunctivitis + negative acid-fast = Microsporidia, the one organism of the four that is not acid-fast and the only one that leaves the gut (eye, systemic disease — p.34). Its stain is Gram-chromotrope (p.31, p.34, p.36).","C":"A simple smear shows Cystoisospora belli because it is large — but Cystoisospora is acid-fast and would already have appeared, and it does not cause eye disease.","D":"Iodine wet mounts are for amoebic and Giardia cysts (L5); a 1–2 µm spore is not a cyst and will not be seen this way.","E":"Modified acid-fast detects the three coccidia; however often it is repeated, it will not show Microsporidia."},"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient with AIDS has chronic watery diarrhoea. Stool modified acid-fast staining is negative. Which next step and rationale are correct?","o":{"B":"Request a Gram-chromotrope stain for Microsporidia — its 1–2 µm spores with a belt-like polar filament do not stain by modified acid-fast","A":"Repeat the modified acid-fast stain, because it detects all four intestinal sporozoa and must have been technically poor","C":"Request an iodine wet mount for amoebic cysts, because modified acid-fast excludes all protozoa","E":"Request a trichrome stain, which is the method for Microsporidia","D":"Stop testing — a negative modified acid-fast excludes an opportunistic parasite in this patient"},"r":{"B":"Correct on stain, organism, size and morphology. The deck's final slide sets the two stains side by side for exactly this decision.","A":"Modified acid-fast covers Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora and Cystoisospora — it does NOT cover Microsporidia.","C":"Iodine wet mounts are used for amoebic and Giardia cysts (L5); they will not show a 1 µm spore.","E":"Trichrome and iron haematoxylin are the L5 amoeba/flagellate stains. The slide names Gram-chromotrope for Microsporidia.","D":"Microsporidia is precisely the organism a negative acid-fast fails to exclude, and it can also cause conjunctivitis and multiorgan disease."},"t":"Which stain","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L6_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Which stain</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Simple smear — which one still shows","s":"A district hospital laboratory has run out of both modified acid-fast and Gram-chromotrope reagents. Stool from a man with AIDS and chronic watery diarrhoea can be examined only as an unstained simple smear. Which of these findings could it still reliably detect?","lec":"L6","sl":22,"c":"<b>Simple smear — which one still shows</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Round oocysts about 10 µm holding two sporocysts","B":"Oval spores about 1–2 µm crossed by a belt-like stripe","C":"Ellipsoidal oocysts about 25 × 15 µm with one sporocyst","D":"Round oocysts about 5 µm that are already sporulated","E":"Round oocysts about 10 µm that are still unsporulated"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A Cyclospora oocyst with two sporocysts is the sporulated stage, which forms in the environment and is not what is found in fresh stool — and it would still need the stain.","B":"Microsporidia (1–2 µm) needs Gram-chromotrope — 'a pen-dot under the microscope' without it.","C":"Correct. Cystoisospora belli's unsporulated oocyst (1 sporocyst) is large — about the size of an Opisthorchis viverrini egg — so its lab-investigation slide lists a simple smear as well as modified acid-fast (p.22, p.25). The other oocysts are small and transparent and need the special stain.","D":"Cryptosporidium (4–6 µm) needs modified acid-fast to be seen (p.13).","E":"Cyclospora (8–12 µm) needs modified acid-fast (p.19); the lab talk stressed that these small oocysts are hard to see unstained."},"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"An acid-fast stained stool contains ellipsoidal, oval structures roughly 25 × 15 µm. What are they?","o":{"C":"Cystoisospora belli oocysts — the largest and the only ellipsoidal one of the intestinal sporozoa","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis oocysts — 8–12 µm and spherical","A":"Cryptosporidium oocysts — 4–6 µm and spherical","D":"Microsporidia spores — 1–2 µm with a belt-like stripe","E":"Entamoeba histolytica cysts — 10–15 µm and spherical"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Shape settles it as fast as size does: Cystoisospora is ELLIPSOIDAL while Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora are spherical or round.","B":"Cyclospora is roughly half this size and round.","A":"Cryptosporidium is about a fifth of this size.","D":"Microsporidia spores are an order of magnitude smaller and need a different stain.","E":"Entamoeba cysts are not acid-fast, and the shape and size do not fit."},"t":"Size ladder","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L6_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Size ladder</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Who is truly high-risk","s":"During a waterborne outbreak of an organism producing round acid-fast oocysts 4–6 µm across, five adults drink from the same contaminated supply. Which of them is most likely to develop chronic, severe watery diarrhoea with weight loss and dehydration?","lec":"L6","sl":6,"c":"<b>Who is truly high-risk</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A man with AIDS whose CD4 count is very low","B":"A healthy man with no underlying illness","C":"A man with well-controlled essential hypertension","D":"A healthy pregnant woman who empties a cat's litter tray","E":"A man with HIV on treatment whose CD4 count is normal"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The chronic severe picture belongs to the immunocompromised (p.7). The lecturer specified HIV with a low CD4 count / AIDS — an HIV patient on treatment with a good CD4 count is not the high-risk group.","B":"Healthy people are asymptomatic or have acute self-limited watery diarrhoea (p.7).","C":"Controlled hypertension is not immunosuppression; the high-risk list is HIV/AIDS, immunosuppressive drugs, travellers and malnutrition (p.6), plus lymphoma added verbally.","D":"Pregnancy with cat exposure is the Toxoplasma framing (p.5, L7), not a risk factor for these organisms.","E":"The lecturer's own caveat: HIV that is treated with a normal CD4 count does not count as immunocompromised for this purpose; expect at most a self-limited illness."},"img":"slides_i/L6_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A swimming pool is linked to an outbreak of watery diarrhoea despite documented adequate chlorination. Which organism and which prevention measure fit?","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium — its oocyst resists chlorine, drying, freezing and salt water, so prevention relies on FILTRATION or boiling rather than chlorination","C":"Cyclospora — its oocyst is the chlorine-resistant one, and the answer is to raise the chlorine dose","B":"Entamoeba histolytica — its mature cyst survives chlorination, and the answer is metronidazole for swimmers","E":"Microsporidia — its spore is chlorine-resistant, and prevention is hand hygiene alone","D":"Any of these — all protozoan cysts and oocysts are equally chlorine-resistant"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the mechanistic reason the Milwaukee 1993 water-supply outbreak reached more than 400,000 people through a treated municipal system.","C":"Chlorine resistance is stated only on the Cryptosporidium slide, and raising the dose is the wrong lesson from it.","B":"Entamoeba is not the pool organism the deck highlights, and the measure offered treats cases rather than preventing exposure.","E":"Microsporidia's slide makes no chlorine-resistance claim; hand hygiene alone would not stop a waterborne source.","D":"The deck singles out Cryptosporidium for this property — generalising it is the trap."},"t":"Chlorine resistance","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L6_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Chlorine resistance</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Where the sexual cycle happens","s":"A man with AIDS passes numerous round, red, 4–6 µm oocysts on modified acid-fast staining. Where does the sexual phase of this parasite's life cycle take place?","lec":"L6","sl":9,"c":"<b>Where the sexual cycle happens</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In the intestine of a cat, with humans as intermediate hosts","B":"In cattle alone, with humans shedding no oocysts at all","C":"In the human small intestine, where gametes fuse into a zygote","D":"In soil or water, after the oocyst has left the human host","E":"In a mosquito, with humans carrying only the asexual phase of the cycle"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cat definitive host with humans as intermediate hosts is Toxoplasma gondii (L7).","B":"C. parvum does infect cattle, but humans also complete the cycle and shed oocysts — that is what the stain is showing.","C":"Correct. Cryptosporidium completes both cycles in the one host: asexual (sporozoite → trophozoite) and sexual (macrogamete + microgamete → zygote) in the small intestine, and the zygote becomes the thick-walled oocyst passed in faeces (p.9, p.12). That is why the patient sheds oocysts at all.","D":"What happens outside the host is sporulation (for Cyclospora and Cystoisospora) — an asexual step, and Cryptosporidium is already sporulated when passed.","E":"Sexual phase in the mosquito, asexual in humans is Plasmodium (L7)."},"img":"slides_i/L6_9.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about Cyclospora cayetanensis epidemiology matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Transmission is mainly food-borne, with outbreaks classically linked to raspberries, and the 7–15 day sporulation delay means the food is contaminated well before it is eaten","D":"Transmission is mainly person-to-person in daycare centres, because the shed oocyst is already infective","E":"Transmission is zoonotic from cattle, as for Cryptosporidium","B":"Transmission is by ingesting spores in undercooked pork","A":"Transmission occurs only in immunocompromised people; healthy people cannot be infected"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the two facts explain each other — an oocyst that needs one to two weeks to ripen cannot spread directly, so it arrives on produce.","D":"Person-to-person spread requires an already-infective oocyst, which is CRYPTOSPORIDIUM's situation, not Cyclospora's.","E":"Zoonotic transmission is listed for Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia, not for Cyclospora.","B":"Spores and pork belong to other organisms entirely.","A":"Healthy people are infected too — the deck says they are asymptomatic or have self-limited watery diarrhoea."},"t":"Cyclospora — the food vehicle","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Cyclospora — the food vehicle</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Cyclospora — the food the slide flags","s":"A traveller returns home with two weeks of watery diarrhoea. A modified acid-fast stool smear shows round oocysts 8–12 µm across, passed unsporulated. Which food history best fits the transmission pattern the lecture flags for this organism?","lec":"L6","sl":17,"c":"<b>Cyclospora — the food the slide flags</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Eating undercooked pork sausage","B":"Drinking unpasteurised goat's milk","C":"Eating raw water chestnuts","D":"Eating imported fresh raspberries","E":"Eating raw freshwater fish salad"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Undercooked pork is Taenia solium / Trichinella (helminth lectures).","B":"Unpasteurised milk is a Toxoplasma tachyzoite route (L7), not a Cyclospora vehicle.","C":"Raw water plants such as water chestnut carry Fasciolopsis buski metacercariae (L4).","D":"Correct. 8–12 µm, round, unsporulated = Cyclospora cayetanensis. Its transmission slide stars food-borne with the footnote 'outbreak associated with raspberries' (p.17); the lecturer added that the berries' fine hairs trap oocysts and resist washing, and the prevention slide adds fruits to the washing advice (p.20).","E":"Raw freshwater fish is Opisthorchis viverrini and other fish-borne trematodes (L4)."},"img":"slides_i/L6_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about Microsporidia is correct?","o":{"D":"The SPORE is both the infective and the diagnostic stage; beyond diarrhoea it can cause conjunctivitis and systemic multiorgan disease, and the spore's belt-like stripe is the polar filament","A":"The oocyst is both infective and diagnostic, and disease is confined to the small intestine","C":"The spore is infective but the diagnostic stage is the trophozoite seen in biopsies","E":"It causes only keratitis and never diarrhoea","B":"It is not reported in Thailand"},"r":{"D":"Correct on all three. Microsporidia is the organism in this lecture that leaves the gut, which is what makes it worth a separate stem.","A":"Microsporidia produces SPORES, not oocysts, and the deck lists eye and systemic disease explicitly.","C":"The life-cycle slide names the spore as both stages; no trophozoite is listed as diagnostic.","E":"Diarrhoea is its main presentation; conjunctivitis is an additional site.","B":"The deck carries a slide headed 'Reported in Thailand'."},"t":"Microsporidia beyond the gut","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L6_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Microsporidia beyond the gut</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Microsporidia — beyond the gut","s":"A kidney-transplant recipient has chronic diarrhoea. A Gram-chromotrope stain of her stool shows tiny oval spores, some crossed by a pale band. Which statement about this organism is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":32,"c":"<b>Microsporidia — beyond the gut</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It can also infect the eye, and its spores may be sought outside the stool","B":"Its oocyst is passed unsporulated, and it matures outside in 7–15 days","C":"It has no animal reservoir, and it spreads by food and water alone","D":"It is the largest of the group, and it is visible on an unstained simple smear","E":"It stains red with modified acid-fast, and it measures about 4–6 µm"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Gram-chromotrope + tiny oval spores + belt (polar filament) = Microsporidia. Its symptom slide adds eye (conjunctivitis) and systemic multiorgan disease, and the diagnostic stage is 'spore in feces / other specimens' (p.34) — the only one of the four that leaves the gut.","B":"Unsporulated oocyst maturing in 7–15 days is Cyclospora; Microsporidia forms a spore, not an oocyst.","C":"Microsporidia's transmission slide lists zoonotic and person-to-person routes as well as food and water (p.32).","D":"Largest and visible on a simple smear is Cystoisospora belli; Microsporidia is the smallest (1–2 µm).","E":"Red on modified acid-fast at 4–6 µm is Cryptosporidium; Microsporidia is not acid-fast."},"img":"slides_i/L6_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which two of the intestinal sporozoa have person-to-person and zoonotic transmission listed on their transmission slides?","o":{"A":"Cryptosporidium spp. and Microsporidia","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis and Cystoisospora belli","E":"Cryptosporidium spp. and Cyclospora cayetanensis","C":"Cystoisospora belli and Microsporidia","D":"All four have identical transmission routes"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and it is consistent with their biology — both shed a stage that is infective the moment it leaves the body.","B":"Both of these list only food-borne and water-borne routes, because their oocysts must mature outside first.","E":"Cryptosporidium is right, Cyclospora is not.","C":"Microsporidia is right, Cystoisospora is not.","D":"The four transmission slides differ, and the difference is the examinable point."},"t":"Who spreads person-to-person","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Who spreads person-to-person</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Same organism, two hosts","s":"Two friends drink untreated stream water on a trekking holiday. One is healthy; the other takes tacrolimus after a kidney transplant. Both later pass round acid-fast oocysts about 10 µm across, shed unsporulated. Which pair of outcomes is most likely?","lec":"L6","sl":7,"c":"<b>Same organism, two hosts</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Healthy — brief self-limited watery diarrhoea; transplant — conjunctivitis and multiorgan disease","B":"Healthy — brief self-limited watery diarrhoea; transplant — chronic severe watery diarrhoea","C":"Healthy — chronic severe watery diarrhoea; transplant — brief self-limited watery diarrhoea","D":"Healthy — bloody diarrhoea with tenesmus; transplant — chronic severe watery diarrhoea","E":"Healthy — asymptomatic carriage; transplant — bloody mucoid diarrhoea with tenesmus"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Conjunctivitis and multiorgan disease are the extra features of Microsporidia (p.34), not of an acid-fast oocyst.","B":"Correct. The organism is Cyclospora cayetanensis (round, 8–12 µm, unsporulated; water-borne as well as food-borne). All four organisms share one clinical slide: healthy — asymptomatic or acute self-limited watery diarrhoea; immunocompromised (immunosuppressive drugs count) — chronic severe watery diarrhoea without blood, weight loss, dehydration (p.7, p.19).","C":"Reversed: the immunosuppressed host gets the chronic disease.","D":"No organism in this lecture causes bloody diarrhoea or tenesmus in anyone — that is Entamoeba histolytica / Balantidium territory.","E":"Asymptomatic carriage in the healthy friend is possible, but the transplant patient's diarrhoea is watery and without blood."},"img":"slides_i/L6_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about the clinical behaviour of the intestinal sporozoa is correct?","o":{"B":"In immunocompetent people the illness is asymptomatic or an acute self-limited watery diarrhoea; in HIV/AIDS it becomes chronic severe watery diarrhoea WITHOUT blood, with weight loss and dehydration","E":"In immunocompetent people the diarrhoea is bloody; in HIV/AIDS it is watery","A":"The diarrhoea contains blood and mucus in both groups, which distinguishes it from giardiasis","C":"Only people with HIV/AIDS can be infected at all","D":"Immunosuppressive drugs, travel and malnutrition are not risk factors; only HIV is"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and 'without blood' is the phrase to hold — it is what separates this group from amoebic colitis and balantidiasis.","E":"Neither group has bloody diarrhoea from these organisms.","A":"Blood and mucus points to Entamoeba histolytica or Balantidium coli, not to the sporozoa.","C":"Healthy people are infected; their infection is simply self-limited.","D":"The high-risk slide lists HIV/AIDS, immunosuppressive drugs, travellers AND malnutrition."},"t":"Same symptom, two hosts","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L6_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Same symptom, two hosts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Stage seen in stool = stage that infects","s":"Four patients with HIV and chronic watery diarrhoea have these stool results — P: round acid-fast oocysts 4–6 µm; Q: round acid-fast oocysts 8–12 µm; R: ellipsoidal acid-fast oocysts 20–33 µm long; S: oval Gram-chromotrope spores 1–2 µm. In which two patients is the stage seen in stool the same stage that infected them?","lec":"L6","sl":12,"c":"<b>Stage seen in stool = stage that infects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"P and S","B":"P and Q","C":"Q and R","D":"R and S","E":"Q and S"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. P = Cryptosporidium (sporulated oocyst is both infective and diagnostic, p.12) and S = Microsporidia (spore is both, p.33). Q = Cyclospora and R = Cystoisospora are passed unsporulated (diagnostic) but infect as sporulated oocysts (p.18, p.24).","B":"Q (Cyclospora) is diagnosed as the unsporulated oocyst but infects as the sporulated one.","C":"These are exactly the two whose diagnostic and infective stages differ.","D":"R (Cystoisospora) differs: unsporulated with 1 sporocyst in stool, sporulated with 2 sporocysts when infective.","E":"Q (Cyclospora) differs — it needs 7–15 days outside to become infective."},"img":"slides_i/L6_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"The lecture sorts the sporozoa by the body system they occupy. Which sorting is correct?","o":{"A":"Gastrointestinal: Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora and Microsporidia · Haematopoietic and lymphatic: Toxoplasma gondii and Plasmodium spp., with Toxoplasma carrying the pregnancy-and-cat association","D":"Gastrointestinal: Toxoplasma and Plasmodium · Haematopoietic: Cryptosporidium and Cyclospora","B":"All the sporozoa are gastrointestinal; Plasmodium is a flagellate","C":"Cystoisospora belli is haematopoietic because it is an opportunistic parasite in AIDS","E":"Microsporidia is classified with the amoebae because it moves by pseudopodia"},"r":{"A":"Correct, including the two 'others' — Blastocystis and Microsporidia sit outside the classical Sporozoa but are grouped with the gut organisms here.","D":"The two groups are swapped.","B":"Plasmodium is a sporozoan (Apicomplexa), and Toxoplasma and Plasmodium are placed in blood and lymphatic tissue.","C":"Being an AIDS-associated opportunist does not change its site; Cystoisospora is intestinal.","E":"Sporozoa lack organelles of motility altogether — that is the basis of the classification."},"t":"Sporozoa by body system","sl":4,"img":"slides_i/L6_4.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Sporozoa by body system</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Round vs ellipsoidal unsporulated oocysts","s":"Two patients with HIV pass unsporulated acid-fast oocysts: in one they are round and about 10 µm across, in the other ellipsoidal and about 28 × 14 µm. Which statement about the two organisms is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":16,"c":"<b>Round vs ellipsoidal unsporulated oocysts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The ellipsoidal one is the one linked to raspberry outbreaks","B":"The round one is the one visible on an unstained simple smear","C":"Both are passed in a form that can infect a new host at once","D":"Both have person-to-person and zoonotic routes on their slides","E":"Both must mature outside a host before they can infect anyone"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Reversed: raspberries are Cyclospora's flagged vehicle (p.17).","B":"Reversed: the large ellipsoidal Cystoisospora is the one seen on a simple smear (p.25).","C":"Infective on passage is the Cryptosporidium (and Microsporidia) property, not these two.","D":"Both list food-borne and water-borne only (p.17, p.23); the four-route list belongs to Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia.","E":"Correct. Round 10 µm = Cyclospora; ellipsoidal 28 × 14 µm = Cystoisospora belli. Both are diagnosed as unsporulated oocysts and infect as sporulated oocysts (p.18, p.24), so both must sporulate in the environment (Cyclospora: 7–15 days, p.16) — which is why neither lists person-to-person spread."},"img":"slides_i/L6_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about detecting Cystoisospora belli is correct?","o":{"E":"Its oocyst is large enough to be seen on a simple smear, and modified acid-fast staining confirms it; the stage found in stool is the UNSPORULATED oocyst with one sporocyst","A":"It can only be seen after modified acid-fast staining, and the stage in stool is the sporulated oocyst","D":"It requires Gram-chromotrope staining, like Microsporidia","B":"It is detected by antigen ELISA only; microscopy is insensitive","C":"Its diagnostic stage is the free sporozoite in stool"},"r":{"E":"Correct on both counts. Its size (20–33 µm) is what makes the simple smear feasible — the smaller coccidia are not reliably seen that way.","A":"The deck lists simple smear explicitly under lab investigation, and the stool stage is UNsporulated.","D":"Gram-chromotrope is for Microsporidia.","B":"No antigen ELISA is listed for this organism in the lecture.","C":"Sporozoites are inside sporocysts, not free in stool."},"t":"Cystoisospora — two stains","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L6_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Cystoisospora — two stains</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Sporozoa by body system","s":"A pregnant woman who cleans her cat's litter tray acquires a sporozoan infection. In the lecture's classification of the sporozoa by body system, where is this organism placed, and alongside which genus?","lec":"L6","sl":5,"c":"<b>Sporozoa by body system</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Gastrointestinal tract, alongside Cryptosporidium","B":"Gastrointestinal tract, alongside Cystoisospora","C":"Gastrointestinal tract, alongside Microsporidia","D":"Haematopoietic and lymphatic systems, alongside Cyclospora","E":"Haematopoietic and lymphatic systems, alongside Plasmodium"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Cryptosporidium is in the gastrointestinal group, but Toxoplasma is not — even though its oocyst is swallowed, the lecture classifies it by where it lives.","B":"Cystoisospora belli is gastrointestinal; Toxoplasma is not placed with it.","C":"Microsporidia sits with the gastrointestinal group (as 'others'); Toxoplasma is not there.","D":"Right system, wrong partner: Cyclospora is one of the gastrointestinal opportunists.","E":"Correct. Pregnancy + cat = Toxoplasma gondii — the slide's own annotation 'pregnant woman associated with cat'. Slide 5 places Toxoplasma and Plasmodium under haematopoietic and lymphatic systems, and the four opportunists in AIDS under the gastrointestinal tract."},"img":"slides_i/L6_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which prevention statement matches the lecture for this group of organisms?","o":{"E":"Drink cleaned, filtered or sterilised water; wash vegetables — and for Cyclospora, fruit — thoroughly; keep good hand hygiene; control rests on treating carriers and defecating in a toilet","C":"Wear shoes and gloves when handling soil","D":"Freeze food below −10 °C for 24–48 hours","A":"Avoid raw freshwater crab and crayfish","B":"Boil bathing water for one minute to kill cercariae"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and note the FRUIT wording appears on the Cyclospora slide specifically, matching the raspberry outbreaks.","C":"Shoes and gloves block soil-transmitted nematode larvae (L1).","D":"Freezing is the Diphyllobothrium measure (L3) — and Cryptosporidium oocysts explicitly resist freezing.","A":"Raw crab is the Paragonimus exposure (L4).","B":"Boiling bathing water is schistosomiasis prevention (L4)."},"t":"Prevention across the group","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L6_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Prevention across the group</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L6|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Habitat and the stool it produces","s":"A man receiving chemotherapy for lymphoma has had diarrhoea and weight loss for months. His stool contains ellipsoidal acid-fast oocysts with a tapered, neck-like end, each holding a single sporocyst. Where does this parasite live, and what kind of stool does it produce?","lec":"L6","sl":7,"c":"<b>Habitat and the stool it produces</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Large intestine — watery stool without blood","B":"Small intestine — watery stool without blood","C":"Bile ducts — pale stool with obstructive jaundice","D":"Large intestine — bloody mucoid stool with tenesmus","E":"Small intestine — bloody mucoid stool"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right stool, wrong site: none of the L6 organisms lives in the large intestine.","B":"Correct. Ellipsoidal, 'neck-like', one sporocyst = the unsporulated oocyst of Cystoisospora belli. Habitat: small intestine (p.23–24), like all four organisms; in the immunocompromised (the lecturer added lymphoma to the list) it gives chronic watery diarrhoea without blood (p.7, p.25).","C":"Bile ducts are the habitat of Opisthorchis viverrini (L4) — whose egg is about the same size as this oocyst, which is the lecturer's size anchor, not a habitat link.","D":"Large bowel + dysentery + tenesmus is Entamoeba histolytica / Balantidium coli (L5) or Trichuris (L1).","E":"Right site, wrong stool: the L6 organisms are non-invasive and cause no blood."},"img":"slides_i/L6_7.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about Cryptosporidium's habitat and stages is correct?","o":{"B":"It lives in the small intestine, and the SAME stage — the sporulated oocyst containing four sporozoites — is both the infective and the diagnostic stage","C":"It lives in the large intestine, which is why the diarrhoea is dysenteric","D":"It lives in the small intestine, but the diagnostic stage is the unsporulated oocyst","E":"It lives in the bile duct, like Opisthorchis","A":"It lives inside red blood cells, like Plasmodium"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the single-stage answer is what makes it directly transmissible — there is no maturation step between shedding and infecting.","C":"All four intestinal sporozoa inhabit the SMALL intestine, and the diarrhoea is watery, not dysenteric.","D":"Unsporulated shedding belongs to Cyclospora and Cystoisospora.","E":"Biliary habitat is Opisthorchis viverrini (L4).","A":"Intraerythrocytic life is Plasmodium, covered in L7."},"t":"Cryptosporidium — where it lives","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L6_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Cryptosporidium — where it lives</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233917182},{"id":"I|L7|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Definitive hosts of both sporozoa","s":"A seronegative pregnant woman seroconverts a few weeks after she starts emptying her new kitten's litter tray. In the same month her husband, a rubber tapper on the Thai–Cambodian border, develops fever with chills, and his thin film shows banana-shaped gametocytes. For the parasite infecting each of them, which is the definitive host?","lec":"L7","sl":7,"c":"<b>Definitive hosts of both sporozoa</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cat for the wife's parasite; female Anopheles mosquito for the husband's","B":"Cat for the wife's parasite; female Aedes mosquito for the husband's","C":"Cat for the wife's parasite; human for the husband's","D":"Human for the wife's parasite; human for the husband's","E":"Human for the wife's parasite; female Anopheles mosquito for the husband's"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Step 1: litter tray + seroconversion = Toxoplasma gondii; banana-shaped gametocyte = Plasmodium falciparum. Step 2: the definitive host is where SEXUAL reproduction happens — the cat's intestinal epithelium for Toxoplasma (slide 15–16) and the female Anopheles for Plasmodium (slide 31). Humans are the intermediate host of both.","B":"Aedes is the wrong mosquito (dengue/Zika). Ittisak's tell: malaria's vector and definitive host is the FEMALE ANOPHELES.","C":"Right for Toxoplasma, wrong for Plasmodium: the human liver and red-cell cycles are schizogony (asexual), so the human is the intermediate host.","D":"The 'where the disease is' misconception. Illness has nothing to do with host status; humans carry only asexual stages of both parasites.","E":"Right for Plasmodium, wrong for Toxoplasma: humans never host the sexual cycle of Toxoplasma and never shed oocysts — only cats do."},"img":"slides_i/L7_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about host roles is correct for BOTH Toxoplasma and Plasmodium?","o":{"E":"The human is the definitive host of both, because that is where disease occurs","A":"The definitive host is where sexual reproduction occurs — the cat for Toxoplasma and the female Anopheles for Plasmodium — so the human is the intermediate host in both","D":"The human is the definitive host of Plasmodium and the intermediate host of Toxoplasma","B":"The mosquito is the intermediate host of Plasmodium because the parasite only passes through","C":"Host status is arbitrary and is not examinable"},"r":{"E":"Disease has nothing to do with host classification.","A":"Correct, and one rule covers both: definitive host = where SEXUAL reproduction happens. It is counter-intuitive, which is exactly why it is examined. And say FEMALE Anopheles — only females take blood meals, so 'Anopheles mosquito' without 'female' is an incomplete answer.","D":"The human is the intermediate host of both.","B":"The mosquito is the DEFINITIVE host of Plasmodium.","C":"It has been set repeatedly."},"t":"Host status","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L7_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Host status</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Toxoplasma — infective vs diagnostic stage","s":"A 30-year-old woman who gardens without gloves develops fever, myalgia and tender cervical lymph nodes. Stray cats defecate in her vegetable beds and she eats the salad greens raw. Which pairing gives the stage that infected her, then the stage a laboratory could find in her own specimens?","lec":"L7","sl":8,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma — infective vs diagnostic stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sporulated oocyst — oocyst in her stool","B":"Sporulated oocyst — tachyzoite or bradyzoite","C":"Sporulated oocyst — sporozoite in her blood film","D":"Tachyzoite — tachyzoite or bradyzoite","E":"Unsporulated oocyst — oocyst in her stool"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right infective stage, wrong diagnostic stage: oocysts are produced only in the cat's intestine, so a human stool examination for Toxoplasma oocysts is always negative.","B":"Correct. Cat faeces + raw vegetables + lymphadenopathy = acquired toxoplasmosis via ingestion of food contaminated with cat faeces containing (sporulated) oocysts. But humans are intermediate hosts and never make oocysts, so the diagnostic stages in humans are only tachyzoite and bradyzoite (slide 12 and 16).","C":"Sporozoites are released from the oocyst in the gut and become tachyzoites; they are not a blood-film diagnostic stage. Hunting for parasites in a blood film is the malaria method.","D":"Tachyzoites infect through unpasteurised milk, transfusion or the placenta — not through soil on vegetables. The diagnostic half is right.","E":"The oocyst leaves the cat unsporulated and needs 3–5 days in the soil to sporulate before it can infect; and in any case humans never shed oocysts in stool."},"img":"slides_i/L7_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which stages of Toxoplasma gondii are INFECTIVE for humans, and which are DIAGNOSTIC in humans?","o":{"B":"Infective: all three (oocyst, tachyzoite, bradyzoite). Diagnostic: tachyzoite and bradyzoite only","C":"Infective: oocyst only. Diagnostic: all three","E":"Infective and diagnostic are the same three stages","D":"Infective: tachyzoite only. Diagnostic: oocyst only","A":"Infective: bradyzoite only. Diagnostic: tachyzoite only"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Only the CAT produces oocysts, because only the cat hosts the sexual cycle — so an oocyst never appears in a human specimen, even though you can be infected by swallowing one. Say it as two lines: 'Infective in humans — all three. Diagnostic in humans — tachyzoite and bradyzoite.'","C":"You can also be infected by tachyzoites (unpasteurised milk, transfusion, transplacental) and bradyzoites (undercooked meat, transplant).","E":"They are different lists, and a question asking 'which stage would you find in this patient?' has a different answer from 'which stage can infect a human?'.","D":"Reversed.","A":"Both halves are wrong."},"t":"Toxoplasma stages","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L7_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Toxoplasma stages</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Congenital toxoplasmosis — who is at risk","s":"An antenatal clinic reviews five women at 18 weeks' gestation. Whose fetus is at greatest risk of the infection that causes retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus and intracranial calcification?","lec":"L7","sl":17,"c":"<b>Congenital toxoplasmosis — who is at risk</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A woman whose husband had toxoplasmic lymphadenopathy two years ago","B":"A woman with Toxoplasma IgG since before pregnancy who works on a pig farm","C":"A woman with Toxoplasma IgG since before pregnancy who keeps three indoor cats","D":"A woman seronegative before pregnancy who adopted a kitten and eats rare steak","E":"A woman seronegative before pregnancy who eats only well-cooked food and has no cat"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Toxoplasma is not sexually transmitted; the six routes are cat faeces, milk, placenta, transfusion, raw meat and transplantation.","B":"Pre-existing IgG means past infection and immunity, so the pig-farm exposure does not create the congenital risk of a primary infection.","C":"Already immune before pregnancy, so exposure to her cats does not carry the congenital risk the lecture describes.","D":"Correct. Step 1: the triad = congenital toxoplasmosis. Step 2: slide 17's high-risk group is the pregnant woman who has NEVER been exposed — she has no immunity, so a primary infection during pregnancy can cross the placenta as tachyzoites. This woman is both non-immune and newly exposed on two routes (cat faeces → oocyst; rare meat → bradyzoite). Lecture tell: infection in the first trimester tends to end in abortion; later infection gives the triad.","E":"Non-immune, but she has removed both main exposures (cooked food, no cat) — the prevention list on slide 21 — so her risk is lower than A's."},"img":"slides_i/L7_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A newborn of a mother exposed to cat faeces during pregnancy has which triad?","o":{"A":"Retinochoroiditis, MICROcephaly, intracranial calcification","D":"Retinochoroiditis, HYDROcephalus, intracranial calcification","E":"Myelitis, microcephaly, retinochoroiditis","C":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, myelitis","B":"Anencephaly, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification"},"r":{"A":"The most dangerous distractor, because it is closest to the truth. Microcephaly belongs to ZIKA, a virus asked about in the virology half of the SAME exam.","D":"Correct — R-H-IC, and the key every year. Toxoplasma gives a BIG head (fluid); Zika gives a SMALL head.","E":"Same microcephaly trap, plus myelitis substituted for the calcification.","C":"Swaps out the intracranial calcification.","B":"Anencephaly is a neural-tube defect, not an infection at all."},"t":"Congenital triad","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L7_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Congenital triad</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"The single stage that separates all four species","s":"At a practical station you may examine only one parasite stage, together with the red cell it sits in, and must name the species from the four that light microscopy can separate. Which stage would let you tell all four apart on its own?","lec":"L7","sl":51,"c":"<b>The single stage that separates all four species</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mature schizont","B":"Early trophozoite","C":"Merozoite","D":"Early schizont","E":"Gametocyte"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct — Ittisak said it directly: the mature schizont is the only single stage that identifies all four. It gives both steps of the key at once: RBC size (normal = 'FM', enlarged = 'OV') and merozoite count (>12 = P. falciparum or P. vivax; <12 = P. malariae or P. ovale).","B":"The ring separates P. falciparum (⅕ of the RBC, double chromatin, multiple infection) from the rest (⅓), but it cannot split vivax, ovale and malariae from each other.","C":"A free merozoite has no host red cell and no count; it is not a species-identification stage.","D":"An early schizont has only two nuclei — there is no final merozoite count yet, so step 2 of the key cannot be done.","E":"Only the banana-shaped P. falciparum gametocyte is distinctive; vivax, ovale and malariae gametocytes are all round."},"img":"slides_i/L7_51.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which finding on a blood film identifies Plasmodium falciparum?","o":{"C":"Amoeboid trophozoite","E":"Band form","B":"Comet shape","D":"Fimbriated red cell","A":"Ring form with double chromatin dots"},"r":{"C":"An amoeboid trophozoite in an enlarged red cell with pink Schüffner's dots is P. VIVAX.","E":"The band form is P. MALARIAE.","B":"Comet shape is P. OVALE.","D":"A fimbriated (ragged-edged) red cell is P. OVALE.","A":"Correct, and set three years running. Every distractor is another species' signature, which is why the set is worth learning together."},"t":"Species identification","sl":51,"img":"slides_i/L7_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Species identification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Endemic area — which test","s":"A 40-year-old woodcutter who has lived all his life in a malaria-endemic district of Tak has had fever, chills and sweating for three days. The district hospital must confirm whether he has malaria at present. Which investigation should be chosen?","lec":"L7","sl":43,"c":"<b>Endemic area — which test</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A thin blood film alone, which detects low counts better than a thick film","B":"Liver ultrasound, which detects the hepatic stage before the blood stage","C":"Thick and thin blood films, which show whether parasites are in his blood","D":"Antibody serology, which gives a quicker answer than reading a blood film","E":"Antibody serology, which stays positive for longer than a blood film does"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The thick film is the high-sensitivity film (more blood per field); the thin film is for species identification. Using a thin film alone lowers sensitivity.","B":"The liver stage is microscopic and asymptomatic; no imaging test detects it. Diagnosis is made on the blood stage.","C":"Correct. Microscopy of thick and thin films is labelled the gold standard on slide 44, and it shows current parasitaemia. The lecturer quoted this old item: almost everyone chose serology because it is fast, and that is wrong.","D":"The lecturer's trap. Speed does not help: people who live in an endemic area already carry antibodies from past infections, so a positive antibody test does not prove current malaria.","E":"Staying positive for a long time is exactly the problem — it reflects past exposure, not the present illness."},"img":"slides_i/L7_43.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient has fever and chills every 48 hours and malaria is suspected. Which method is best for SPECIES identification, and why?","o":{"E":"Malaria rapid diagnostic test — it names the species directly","A":"Thick blood smear — it concentrates about 20 times more blood into one field","C":"Thin blood smear — the red cells stay intact, so red-cell size, Schüffner's dots and shape are visible","B":"ELISA for antibody — it distinguishes species by antigen","D":"Modified acid-fast staining"},"r":{"E":"RDTs detect antigen and are a screening tool, not the species-identification method taught here.","A":"Not wrong as a test — it is simply the answer to a DIFFERENT question. Thick films lyse the red cells, so you lose the very features that name the species. Learn it as a pair: THICK = is there malaria? THIN = which malaria?","C":"Correct, and the lecturer's own written feedback says so: you can see the parasite's characteristics, the various dots and the appearance of the infected red cells, which is what tells the species apart.","B":"Antibody detection cannot distinguish current species-level infection.","D":"Modified acid-fast is L6's stain for the intestinal sporozoa — reading 'acid-fast' in a malaria question should feel wrong immediately."},"t":"Blood films","sl":44,"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Blood films</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Where the parasite hides between attacks","s":"A soldier developed fever and chills after a posting in a Kanchanaburi forest; his film showed enlarged infected red cells with pink stippling, and a blood-stage drug cured him. Seven months later, having stayed in Bangkok with no further bites, he again has fever every 48 hours. Where were the parasites during the symptom-free months?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Where the parasite hides between attacks</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"As sequestered schizonts in brain capillaries","B":"As dormant forms inside his hepatocytes","C":"As sporozoites persisting in the skin at the bite","D":"As gametocytes circulating at low density","E":"As merozoites held within the spleen"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Sequestration of schizont-containing red cells in deep vessels is a P. falciparum phenomenon, and falciparum does not enlarge the red cell or form hypnozoites.","B":"Correct. Enlarged RBC + Schüffner's (pink) dots + 48 h = P. vivax or P. ovale — the only two species that form hypnozoites, sporozoites that do not undergo schizogony and stay dormant in the liver (slide 36). A blood-stage drug does not reach them, so they later reactivate: a relapse.","C":"Injected sporozoites travel to the liver; they do not persist in the skin.","D":"Gametocytes do not cause clinical attacks and do not persist for months after the blood stage is cleared; they are the stage the mosquito takes up.","E":"Merozoites invade red cells within minutes of release; there is no splenic reservoir in the lecture's life cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What is the difference between relapse and recrudescence in malaria?","o":{"A":"The terms are interchangeable","D":"Relapse comes from the blood; recrudescence comes from the liver","C":"Both come from hypnozoites, and any species can relapse","E":"Relapse occurs within 24 hours; recrudescence occurs after a year","B":"Relapse comes from dormant LIVER hypnozoites (P. vivax and P. ovale only); recrudescence comes from persisting BLOOD-stage parasites, usually P. falciparum after incomplete treatment"},"r":{"A":"They are distinguished as a written question in the archive.","D":"Reversed.","C":"Only vivax and ovale form hypnozoites.","E":"Neither is defined by a time interval.","B":"Correct. One line: relapse comes from the liver, recrudescence comes from the blood. Therefore relapse means you failed to give PRIMAQUINE; recrudescence means you gave too little of the blood-stage drug."},"t":"Relapse vs recrudescence","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Relapse vs recrudescence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Mode of infection — writing it right","s":"A 22-year-old ranger develops fever with chills and sweating ten days after a night patrol in a Chanthaburi forest, and his film shows ring forms. A written question asks for the mode of infection of his parasite. Which answer earns the marks?","lec":"L7","sl":32,"c":"<b>Mode of infection — writing it right</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Injection of sporozoites into the skin by an infected female Anopheles","B":"Ingestion of water contaminated with sporozoites from mosquito larvae","C":"Injection of merozoites into the skin by an infected female Anopheles","D":"Vector-borne transmission through the bite of a female Anopheles","E":"Injection of sporozoites into the skin by an infected female Aedes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Forest + fever + chills = malaria. The mode of infection is written as action + infective stage + how it arrives: injection of sporozoites (the infective stage) by the infected female Anopheles (slide 32, 38).","B":"The 'water-borne' error the lecturer tells about (the student who learned malaria from the drama บุพเพสันนิวาส). Malaria is vector-borne.","C":"Wrong stage: merozoites are made in the liver and red cells. The mosquito injects sporozoites.","D":"True, but it is the mode of TRANSMISSION (vector-borne), which is a separate line on the same slide. Answering transmission when infection is asked loses the marks.","E":"Wrong mosquito — Aedes carries dengue. The lecturer repeats that the malaria vector is the female Anopheles."},"img":"slides_i/L7_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Four parasites enter through the skin. Which verb belongs to which?","o":{"D":"Plasmodium is deposited; filarial larvae are injected; Schistosoma is deposited","B":"Plasmodium penetrates intact skin; Schistosoma is injected; Leishmania is deposited","E":"All four are injected by an arthropod vector","C":"All four penetrate intact skin","A":"Plasmodium sporozoites are INJECTED; filarial L3 larvae are DEPOSITED and penetrate the bite wound; Schistosoma cercariae PENETRATE INTACT SKIN; Leishmania promastigotes are INJECTED by the sand fly"},"r":{"D":"All three assignments are wrong.","B":"Every verb is misassigned, and Schistosoma has no vector at all.","E":"Schistosoma has no arthropod vector.","C":"Only Schistosoma does this.","A":"Correct, and the papers swap them deliberately — BM31's Leishmania question offered 'injection of sporozoite during mosquito's blood meals' as a wrong option, i.e. the malaria answer planted in a non-malaria stem."},"t":"Three verbs","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L7_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Three verbs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Dark urine in severe malaria","s":"A rubber tapper from Trat becomes confused and drowsy. His film shows numerous small rings, many cells carrying two parasites, and banana-shaped forms. He then passes urine as dark as cola. Which process best explains the colour of his urine?","lec":"L7","sl":39,"c":"<b>Dark urine in severe malaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Massive intravascular haemolysis releasing haemoglobin into the urine","B":"Muscle breakdown during rigors releasing myoglobin into the urine","C":"Acute hepatic failure releasing conjugated bilirubin into the urine","D":"Sequestered parasites in the kidney shedding pigment into the urine","E":"Immune-complex damage to glomeruli leaking red cells into the urine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The film is P. falciparum (small rings, multiple infection, banana gametocytes) and the confusion suggests severe malaria. Dark urine = haemoglobinuria / blackwater fever from massive intravascular haemolysis — listed on slide 42 as a severe complication.","B":"Rhabdomyolysis is not a complication the lecture lists, and rigors in the cold stage do not cause it.","C":"Jaundice in malaria is haemolytic, and acute hepatic failure is not on the lecture's severe-malaria list (a past paper keys it as the EXCEPT option).","D":"Malarial pigment (haemozoin) stays inside the parasite and phagocytes; it does not colour the urine.","E":"Immune-complex nephropathy belongs to chronic P. malariae infection, not to acute falciparum; and blood in the urine is not what blackwater means."},"img":"slides_i/L7_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which is the most serious complication of P. malariae infection?","o":{"A":"Irreversible nephrotic syndrome (quartan malarial nephropathy)","E":"Cerebral malaria","B":"Severe anaemia","D":"Lactic acidosis","C":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome"},"r":{"A":"Correct. A nice discriminating fact: the mild, chronic species has the one renal complication.","E":"Cerebral malaria belongs to P. FALCIPARUM and is its commonest severe manifestation.","B":"Severe anaemia is most characteristic of falciparum.","D":"Lactic acidosis accompanies severe falciparum malaria.","C":"Also a falciparum complication."},"t":"Complications","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Complications</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Silent liver phase and the paroxysm","s":"A volunteer bitten by an infected mosquito on day 0 feels well until day 11, then has attacks of shaking chills, high fever and drenching sweat every 48 hours. Which pairing explains first the symptom-free interval, then what sets off each attack?","lec":"L7","sl":36,"c":"<b>Silent liver phase and the paroxysm</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hypnozoites inside hepatocytes; release of gametocytes into the blood","B":"Multiplication in red cells; synchronous rupture of hepatic schizonts","C":"Schizogony inside hepatocytes; synchronous rupture of infected red cells","D":"Sporogony inside hepatocytes; synchronous rupture of infected red cells","E":"Schizogony inside hepatocytes; synchronous rupture of hepatic schizonts"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Hypnozoites explain relapse months later in P. vivax/ovale, not the first incubation; gametocytes cause no symptoms.","B":"Reversed: the silent phase is in the liver and the attacks come from the red cells.","C":"Correct. The liver stage — each sporozoite undergoing schizogony to 10,000–30,000 merozoites — is the asymptomatic phase (slide 36). Attacks start once the erythrocytic cycle is established: each cold–hot–sweating paroxysm follows rupture of infected red cells at the end of the 48 h cycle (the lecturer: RBC rupture 'tricks the brain' into feeling cold).","D":"Sporogony is the sexual-then-sporozoite-forming cycle in the mosquito, not in the human liver.","E":"The liver ruptures only once to seed the blood; hepatic schizonts do not burst every 48 h. The periodicity belongs to the erythrocytic cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L7_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What are the three mechanisms of anaemia in malaria?","o":{"B":"Rupture of infected red cells only","C":"Rupture of infected red cells; destruction and clearance of NON-infected red cells; dyserythropoiesis mediated by TNF-alpha","D":"Iron loss from gut bleeding, haemolysis and folate deficiency","E":"Splenic sequestration, renal failure and blood loss","A":"Autoimmune haemolysis alone"},"r":{"B":"That accounts for only part of the red-cell loss.","C":"Correct. The part people leave out is 'AND NON-INFECTED' — it is why the anaemia is out of proportion to the parasitaemia.","D":"Gut bleeding is hookworm; folate deficiency is not the mechanism here.","E":"Splenomegaly contributes to clearance, but renal failure and blood loss are not the taught mechanisms.","A":"Immune-mediated destruction of uninfected cells is one of three, not the whole story."},"t":"Anaemia","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Anaemia</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Toxoplasma in the immunocompetent — confirming it","s":"A healthy 26-year-old veterinary nurse who looks after the clinic's cats has had low-grade fever, muscle pain, headache and painless swollen neck nodes for three weeks. She has not travelled anywhere, and her blood count is normal. Which is the primary method for confirming the most likely infection?","lec":"L7","sl":18,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma in the immunocompetent — confirming it</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Giemsa-stained thick and thin films of blood","B":"Stool concentration looking for shed oocysts","C":"Rapid antigen test on a finger-prick blood","D":"Modified acid-fast stain of a stool smear","E":"Serology for Toxoplasma-specific antibodies"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Blood films are the malaria method. Malaria has no lymphadenopathy, and she has not been to a forest or endemic area.","B":"Humans never shed Toxoplasma oocysts; only the cat, the definitive host, does.","C":"The rapid antigen test on slide 44 is the malaria RDT.","D":"Modified acid-fast staining is L6's method for the intestinal coccidia (Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Cystoisospora).","E":"Correct. Cat contact + lymphadenopathy, myalgia, headache and fever = symptomatic acquired toxoplasmosis in an immunocompetent person (slide 18). Slide 20 names serologic tests for specific antibody (IgG, IgM, IgA, IgE) as the primary method."},"img":"slides_i/L7_18.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which feature is NOT common in Plasmodium falciparum infection, and why does it matter?","o":{"D":"Fever with chills","E":"A general rash — its absence separates malaria from dengue and from lymphatic filariasis","B":"Anaemia","A":"Splenomegaly","C":"Headache"},"r":{"D":"The malarial paroxysm — cold stage, hot stage, sweating stage — is the defining symptom.","E":"Correct, and set in two cohorts. Malaria characteristically has neither rash nor lymphadenopathy. That is what separates it from dengue (rash, myalgia) and from filariasis (lymphadenopathy) in a 'fever in a returning traveller' stem — and mechanistically it fits, because sequestration is least in the skin.","B":"Anaemia is expected.","A":"Splenomegaly is common.","C":"Headache is common."},"t":"Clinical","sl":41,"img":"slides_i/L7_41.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Clinical</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q39","t":"Congenital toxoplasmosis","s":"A 35-year-old pregnant woman was exposed to cat faeces. Which triad affects the newborn?","lec":"L7","sl":19,"c":"<b>Congenital toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q39 · BM30 SUM1 Q41 · BM29 — pregnancy, cat faeces, which triad</span>","o":{"D":"Retinochoroiditis, microcephaly, intracranial calcification","E":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification","C":"Myelitis, microcephaly, retinochoroiditis","B":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, myelitis","A":"Anencephaly, hydrocephalus, intracranial calcification"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"The closest and therefore most dangerous distractor: MICROcephaly is ZIKA, which the same exam asks about in its virology half.","E":"Correct — R-H-IC, keyed identically in three cohorts, and dressed up again on this year's Road-to-NL slide as a veterinarian with first-trimester lymphadenopathy whose newborn has hydrocephalus and intracranial calcification.","C":"Same microcephaly trap.","B":"Drops the calcification.","A":"Anencephaly is a neural-tube defect, not an infection."},"img":"slides_i/L7_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q41","BM29 Q-triad"]},{"id":"I|L7|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-pf","t":"P. falciparum film","s":"A child with fever is suspected of Plasmodium falciparum infection. What finding on the blood film would confirm the species?","lec":"L7","sl":59,"c":"<b>P. falciparum film</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 · BM29 SUM1 Q18 — what do you find in P. falciparum?</span>","o":{"E":"Amoeboid form","A":"Band form","B":"Comet shape","C":"Fimbriated red cell","D":"Ring form with double chromatin"},"a":"D","r":{"E":"Amoeboid trophozoite is P. vivax.","A":"Band form is P. malariae.","B":"Comet shape is P. ovale.","C":"Fimbriated red cell is P. ovale.","D":"Correct. Multiple infection of one red cell and the double-chromatin ring are falciparum's signatures, along with the banana-shaped gametocyte."},"img":"slides_i/L7_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q-pf","BM29 Q18"]},{"id":"I|L7|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q42","t":"P. vivax","s":"A patient has fever with chills and sweating every 48 hours. The blood film shows red cells larger than normal, a pseudopodia-like parasite, and pink dots. Which species?","lec":"L7","sl":61,"c":"<b>P. vivax</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q42 — 48-hour fever, enlarged RBC, pseudopodia, pink dots</span>","o":{"A":"Plasmodium vivax","D":"Plasmodium ovale","B":"Plasmodium knowlesi","C":"Plasmodium malariae","E":"Plasmodium falciparum"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Four independent pointers all agree: 48-hour periodicity, ENLARGED red cell, AMOEBOID (pseudopodia-like) trophozoite, and PINK Schüffner's dots.","D":"P. ovale also fits '48 hours' and also enlarges the red cell — what separates it is the FIMBRIATED, comet-shaped red cell rather than an amoeboid parasite.","B":"P. knowlesi has a 24-hour cycle.","C":"P. malariae has a 72-hour cycle and a band form in a normal-sized red cell.","E":"P. falciparum gives an irregular fever and does not enlarge the red cell."},"img":"slides_i/L7_61.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q11","t":"Falciparum stages in blood","s":"A man with paroxysmal fever and myalgia, who is not immunocompromised, is diagnosed with UNCOMPLICATED Plasmodium falciparum malaria. Which stages would you expect on his blood film?","lec":"L7","sl":59,"c":"<b>Falciparum stages in blood</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 · BM30 SUM1 Q13 · BM29 SUM1 Q13 — which stages are found in the blood? ⚠ The BM30 and BM29 answer keys DISAGREE on this item; the BM33 slides settle it.</span>","o":{"C":"Trophozoite, schizont and gametocyte","E":"Trophozoite and gametocyte only","A":"Cyst, tachyzoite and bradyzoite","B":"Hypnozoite, schizont and cyst","D":"Ookinete, trophozoite and cyst"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"⚠ This is the answer BM29's student-written key gives, and it is wrong; BM30's key highlights 'trophozoite and gametocyte only', which agrees with the slides. If schizonts DO appear in a peripheral film, that is a marker of SEVERE malaria — so the word 'uncomplicated' in the stem is doing the work. Where two circulating keys disagree, follow the BM33 deck.","E":"Correct, and the lecture says so twice. Slide 59 prints beside the mature schizont: 'This stage is NOT found in uncomplicated malaria patients', because maturing parasites CYTOADHERE to deep capillary endothelium and disappear from the circulation. Slide 63 then lists the diagnostic stages of P. falciparum as early trophozoite (ring forms, double chromatin, multiple infection, Accolé, Maurer's clefts) and the banana-shaped gametocyte — no schizont.","A":"That is Toxoplasma gondii's life cycle, taught in the same lecture.","B":"Hypnozoites are in the LIVER, and P. falciparum makes none at all — only vivax and ovale do.","D":"The ookinete is a MOSQUITO stage, formed in the midgut after fertilisation."},"img":"slides_i/L7_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q13","BM29 Q13"]},{"id":"I|L7|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-film","t":"Diagnosis","s":"A case of malaria is suspected in a patient with fever and chills every two days. What is the best method for SPECIES identification?","lec":"L7","sl":44,"c":"<b>Diagnosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 formative — fever and chills every 2 days, best method for species identification</span>","o":{"C":"Thin blood smear","D":"Thick blood smear","B":"Malaria rapid diagnostic test","E":"ELISA for antibody detection","A":"Modified acid-fast staining"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and the lecturer's written feedback explains it: the thin film preserves the infected red cell, so you can see its size, the dots and the parasite's shape — which is what names the species.","D":"The designed distractor. A thick film concentrates about 20 times more blood and is better for DETECTION, but it lyses the red cells and destroys the species features.","B":"An RDT screens; it is not the taught species method.","E":"Antibody cannot establish the current species.","A":"Modified acid-fast belongs to L6's intestinal sporozoa — it should feel wrong in a malaria question."},"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q12 (59)","t":"Toxoplasma in HIV","s":"An HIV-positive patient has a space-occupying lesion in the brain. Which organism is most likely?","lec":"L7","sl":17,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma in HIV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Q12 (59) — HIV patient with a brain lesion</span>","o":{"B":"Entamoeba histolytica","C":"Toxoplasma gondii","E":"Naegleria fowleri","D":"Balamuthia mandrillaris","A":"Acanthamoeba spp."},"a":"C","r":{"B":"A companion item asks which organism does NOT cause a brain lesion, and the key is E. histolytica — it makes a LIVER abscess instead.","C":"Correct. Toxoplasmic encephalitis is the classic reactivation disease of the immunocompromised, and BM32's paper carries the treatment follow-up (pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine, with leucovorin for the marrow toxicity).","E":"Naegleria causes primary amoebic meningoencephalitis, an acute fulminant meningitis rather than a mass lesion.","D":"Balamuthia does cause granulomatous encephalitis, but it is far rarer and is not the expected answer in HIV.","A":"Acanthamoeba causes GAE in the immunocompromised, again far less commonly than Toxoplasma."},"img":"slides_i/L7_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"written (57)","t":"Toxoplasma routes","s":"Which source-and-route set is correct for the three infective stages of Toxoplasma gondii?","lec":"L7","sl":13,"c":"<b>Toxoplasma routes</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 — written question, cohorts 57 and 60: source and route for all three stages</span>","o":{"C":"Only the bradyzoite is infective for humans","A":"Sporulated oocyst → undercooked meat; tachyzoite → cat faeces; bradyzoite → milk","D":"All three stages are acquired only from cat faeces","E":"Sporulated oocyst → transplacental; tachyzoite → cat faeces; bradyzoite → water","B":"Sporulated oocyst → food or water contaminated with cat faeces; tachyzoite → unpasteurised milk, blood transfusion, transplacental; bradyzoite → undercooked meat or organ, organ transplantation"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"All three stages are infective for humans — that is the point of the written question.","A":"Every source is misassigned.","D":"Meat and milk are both established routes.","E":"All three are misassigned.","B":"Correct, and it is the marking key's own answer. A neat way to hold it: the three lines are grouped by PHYSICAL STATE — faeces, fluid, solid tissue."},"img":"slides_i/L7_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 written (60)"]},{"id":"I|L7|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q25 (58)","t":"Complications","s":"Which is the most serious complication of Plasmodium malariae infection?","lec":"L7","sl":42,"c":"<b>Complications</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Q25 (58) — most serious complication of P. malariae</span>","o":{"A":"Lactic acidosis","B":"Cerebral malaria","D":"Severe anaemia","C":"Irreversible nephrotic syndrome","E":"Renal failure from blackwater fever"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Lactic acidosis accompanies severe falciparum.","B":"Cerebral malaria belongs to P. falciparum.","D":"Severe anaemia is a falciparum complication.","C":"Correct — quartan malarial nephropathy, an immune-complex glomerulopathy that does not reverse with antimalarial treatment.","E":"Blackwater fever is massive intravascular haemolysis in falciparum infection."},"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L7|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q4 (59)","t":"Plasmodium ovale","s":"A 30-year-old man has relapsing fever every 48 hours. The blood film shows FIMBRIATED, comet-shaped red cells with Schüffner's dots. Which species is this?","lec":"L7","sl":62,"c":"<b>Plasmodium ovale</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 59 Q4 — 48-hour fever with FIMBRIATED red cells</span>","o":{"C":"Plasmodium malariae","B":"Plasmodium vivax","A":"Plasmodium falciparum","D":"Plasmodium ovale","E":"Plasmodium knowlesi"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"P. malariae runs on a 72-hour cycle with a BAND-form trophozoite and rosette schizonts.","B":"P. vivax also gives a 48-hour cycle, enlarged cells and Schüffner's dots — which is exactly why the examiner adds 'fimbriated'. Vivax shows an AMOEBOID trophozoite and MORE than 12 merozoites per schizont.","A":"P. falciparum gives Maurer's clefts, ring forms with double chromatin dots, and banana-shaped gametocytes; the red cell is not enlarged.","D":"Correct. Fimbriated (ragged-edged) or comet-shaped red cells are unique to P. ovale. Its mature schizont holds FEWER than 12 merozoites (6–12).","E":"P. knowlesi has a 24-hour cycle and mimics malariae."},"img":"slides_i/L7_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L7|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q22 (57)","t":"What falciparum does NOT do","s":"Which finding is NOT expected in malaria?","lec":"L7","sl":41,"c":"<b>What falciparum does NOT do</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 57 & 58 — 'what is NOT common in P. falciparum infection?'</span>","o":{"C":"Jaundice","D":"Fever with chills and rigors","B":"Generalised rash","E":"Anaemia","A":"Headache, nausea and muscle pain"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Jaundice follows haemolysis and is listed on the same slide.","D":"The malaria paroxysm — cold stage, hot stage, sweating stage — is the classic presentation.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer, and the lecture prints it as a line of its own: 'No rash and No lymphadenopathy.' This is how malaria is separated from dengue and from the viral exanthems, which are its main clinical rivals in a febrile Thai patient.","E":"Anaemia is expected, from lysis of both infected AND uninfected red cells.","A":"These are the non-specific early symptoms the lecture warns are indistinguishable from flu."},"img":"slides_i/L7_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 Q22 (58)","LS54 Q8 (57)"]},{"id":"I|L7|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q1 (57)","t":"Severe malaria","s":"All of the following are recognised complications of SEVERE malaria EXCEPT:","lec":"L7","sl":42,"c":"<b>Severe malaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q1 — complications of severe malaria, EXCEPT</span>","o":{"A":"Acute renal failure","C":"Cerebral malaria","B":"Acute hepatic failure","E":"Pulmonary oedema","D":"Black water fever with haemoglobinuria"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Acute renal failure is on the list.","C":"Cerebral malaria is listed first and is the MOST COMMON severe complication.","B":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. Jaundice occurs in severe malaria, but it is HAEMOLYTIC — the liver itself does not fail. Acute hepatic failure is not on the WHO severe-malaria list the lecture reproduces.","E":"Pulmonary oedema is on the list.","D":"Black water fever — massive intravascular haemolysis with haemoglobinuria — is on the list. All of these are effectively P. falciparum, the major species for severe disease."},"img":"slides_i/L7_42.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"written Q3 (57)","t":"Hypnozoite and relapse","s":"A true RELAPSE of malaria — as opposed to recrudescence — is caused by which of the following?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Hypnozoite and relapse</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 written Q3–4 — define relapse and distinguish it from recrudescence</span>","o":{"C":"Persistence of small numbers of asexual parasites in the blood after incomplete treatment","E":"Reactivation of dormant hypnozoites in the liver, in P. vivax and P. ovale only","B":"Re-infection from a second mosquito bite","D":"Release of gametocytes from the bone marrow","A":"Reactivation of dormant hypnozoites, in all five species"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"That defines RECRUDESCENCE, which is the falciparum pattern — no hypnozoite, so any recurrence must come from surviving blood-stage parasites.","E":"Correct. Only P. VIVAX and P. OVALE make hypnozoites — sporozoites that sit dormant in the hepatocyte instead of undergoing schizogony. That is why these two need PRIMAQUINE added to chloroquine: chloroquine clears the blood stage, primaquine kills the liver stage.","B":"Re-infection is a new infection, not a relapse.","D":"Gametocytes are the transmissible stage and do not cause clinical recurrence.","A":"The species restriction is the entire point of the question; falciparum, malariae and knowlesi do not form hypnozoites."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["LS54 written Q4 (57)"]},{"id":"I|L7|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q49","t":"Stages in the human host","s":"Which stages of Plasmodium are found in the HUMAN host?","lec":"L7","sl":34,"c":"<b>Stages in the human host</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q49 — which stages of Plasmodium are found in the human host?</span>","o":{"C":"Tachyzoite and bradyzoite only","A":"Sporozoite, ookinete, oocyst, gametocyte","B":"Trophozoite, cyst and mature cyst only","D":"Sporozoite, hepatic schizont (± hypnozoite), merozoite, trophozoite, erythrocytic schizont, gametocyte","E":"Merozoite, ookinete and sporozoite only"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"Tachyzoite and bradyzoite belong to Toxoplasma gondii, taught in the same lecture — a deliberate near-miss.","A":"Ookinete and oocyst are MOSQUITO stages — the sporogonic cycle in the gut wall.","B":"Trophozoite and cyst are the intestinal protozoa (L5).","D":"Correct. The human is the INTERMEDIATE host and carries everything from the injected sporozoite through to the gametocyte. The mosquito is the DEFINITIVE host because that is where sexual reproduction happens.","E":"Mixes human and mosquito stages and omits most of them."},"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L7|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Cause, vector and route of malaria","s":"In a Thai period drama a character catches a fever with chills recurring every other day after drinking from a water jar in a forest hut. Explaining the plot to a friend, which statement about this disease would the lecturer mark correct?","lec":"L7","sl":26,"c":"<b>Cause, vector and route of malaria</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is caused by the female Anopheles and is vector-borne through its bite","B":"It is caused by a protozoan and is vector-borne through the female Aedes","C":"It is caused by a virus and is vector-borne through the female Anopheles","D":"It is caused by a protozoan and is water-borne through the stored jar water","E":"It is caused by a protozoan and is vector-borne through the female Anopheles"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The every-year trap: the mosquito is the vector, not the cause. 'Malaria is caused by…' → Plasmodium.","B":"Aedes is the dengue vector; malaria needs the female Anopheles.","C":"Plasmodium is a protozoan (Apicomplexa), not a virus.","D":"The drama error Ittisak quotes (บุพเพสันนิวาส): a student answered water-borne and the examiners commented on it. The jar water is a distraction.","E":"Correct. Fever with chills every other day after a forest stay = malaria, caused by Plasmodium (a protozoan) and transmitted by the female Anopheles (slide 26), i.e. vector-borne (slide 32)."},"img":"slides_i/L7_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A stem asks you to name the definitive host of Plasmodium. Which answer is correct, and on what principle?","o":{"E":"The female Anopheles mosquito — sexual reproduction (gamogony and sporogony) occurs in it, and the definitive host is defined by sexual reproduction","D":"Humans — the definitive host is the one that becomes ill","C":"Humans — the definitive host is the one in which the parasite spends most of its life","A":"The female Anopheles mosquito — because it is the vector, and vectors are always definitive hosts","B":"Both, because Plasmodium reproduces sexually in the human liver and in the mosquito gut"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and it is the counter-intuitive one: the mosquito is the definitive host and we are merely the intermediate host where schizogony happens.","D":"Disease severity has nothing to do with host classification — a tempting but wrong principle.","C":"Duration is also irrelevant to the definition.","A":"Right answer, wrong reason. Being a vector does not make an arthropod a definitive host — in filariasis (L2) the mosquito is the INTERMEDIATE host.","B":"No sexual reproduction occurs in humans. The liver stage is schizogony, which is asexual."},"t":"Which host is which","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L7_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Which host is which</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Normal-size cell with a rosette schizont","s":"In the ideal area of a thin film, an infected red cell the same size as its neighbours holds a mature schizont of about 8 merozoites arranged like petals around central pigment. Which pair of further findings fits this species?","lec":"L7","sl":37,"c":"<b>Normal-size cell with a rosette schizont</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A banana-shaped gametocyte, and paroxysms every 24–48 hours","B":"A comet-shaped infected cell, and paroxysms every 48 hours","C":"An amoeboid growing trophozoite, and paroxysms every 72 hours","D":"A band-form growing trophozoite, and paroxysms every 72 hours","E":"A band-form growing trophozoite, and paroxysms every 48 hours"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Banana gametocyte and a 24–48 h cycle are P. falciparum, whose mature schizont has >12 (about 24) merozoites.","B":"The comet-shaped, fimbriated cell is P. ovale — which also has <12 merozoites, but in an enlarged cell.","C":"Right cycle, wrong shape: the amoeboid trophozoite belongs to P. vivax, in an enlarged cell.","D":"Correct. Normal RBC ('FM') + fewer than 12 merozoites in a rosette = P. malariae (slide 52, 60). Its growing trophozoite is the band form, and its erythrocytic cycle is 72 h (slide 37).","E":"Right shape, wrong cycle: 48 h is P. vivax and P. ovale."},"img":"slides_i/L7_37.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A thin film shows ENLARGED infected red cells and a mature schizont containing about 8 merozoites. Which species is it?","o":{"D":"Plasmodium ovale — enlarged RBC narrows it to vivax or ovale, and fewer than 12 merozoites settles it as ovale","C":"Plasmodium vivax — enlarged RBC means vivax, and merozoite number does not discriminate","B":"Plasmodium malariae — 6–12 merozoites is the malariae count","A":"Plasmodium falciparum — schizonts with few merozoites are typical of falciparum","E":"Plasmodium knowlesi — it is the only species with enlarged cells and few merozoites"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the algorithm is worth running in this order every time: RBC size first, then merozoite count.","C":"Merozoite count is exactly what separates vivax (>12) from ovale (<12) once the cell is enlarged.","B":"P. malariae also has 6–12 merozoites, but it infects NORMAL-sized cells — step 1 has already excluded it.","A":"P. falciparum infects normal-sized cells, has >12 merozoites, and its schizonts are usually absent from peripheral blood altogether.","E":"The deck states P. knowlesi cannot be distinguished by microscopy, so it is never the answer to a morphology stem."},"t":"Species ID — the two-step algorithm","sl":51,"img":"slides_i/L7_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Species ID — the two-step algorithm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Describe the cell, not the parasite","s":"A spot-test field shows an enlarged infected red cell with pink stippling that contains a mature schizont of about 8 merozoites. The answer sheet then asks you to describe how the growing-trophozoite stage of this species appears. Which description scores?","lec":"L7","sl":55,"c":"<b>Describe the cell, not the parasite</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The parasite forms a band across the infected red cell","B":"The infected red cell is fimbriated and comet-shaped","C":"The parasite is amoeboid with pseudopodia-like extensions","D":"The parasite is comet-shaped inside a normal red cell","E":"The infected red cell is normal in size with Maurer's clefts"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The band form is P. malariae, in a normal-sized cell.","B":"Correct. Enlarged cell + Schüffner's dots ('OV') + fewer than 12 merozoites = P. ovale (slide 58, 62). Its growing-trophozoite stage is recognised by the infected RBC: fimbriated, comet-shaped (slide 55). The lecturer marks 'comet-shaped parasite' as zero — the comet describes the red cell.","C":"The amoeboid form is P. vivax — the other enlarged-cell species, but its mature schizont has more than 12 merozoites.","D":"The zero-mark wording: comet/fimbriated is a feature of the red cell, and the ovale-infected cell is enlarged, not normal.","E":"Normal size with Maurer's clefts is P. falciparum."},"img":"slides_i/L7_55.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of growing-trophozoite appearance with species is correct?","o":{"B":"Band form across the red cell = P. malariae; amoeboid with pseudopodia-like extensions = P. vivax; fimbriated, comet-shaped red cell = P. ovale","D":"Band form = P. vivax; amoeboid = P. malariae; comet-shaped cell = P. falciparum","A":"Amoeboid = P. ovale; band = P. falciparum; comet-shaped cell = P. vivax","E":"All four species show an amoeboid growing trophozoite; only the gametocyte discriminates","C":"Band form = P. falciparum, which is why its schizonts are sequestered"},"r":{"B":"Correct on all three, and each fits its cell: vivax stretches inside an enlarged cell, malariae stretches across a normal one, ovale distorts the cell into a comet.","D":"Band and amoeboid are swapped, and the comet cell is ovale, not falciparum.","A":"Every assignment is wrong.","E":"The growing trophozoite is one of the most useful discriminators in the deck; the gametocyte helps only for falciparum.","C":"P. falciparum's growing trophozoite is described simply as 'only bigger'; sequestration explains the missing schizont, not a band form."},"t":"Growing trophozoite shapes","sl":55,"img":"slides_i/L7_55.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Growing trophozoite shapes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Transplant-acquired toxoplasmosis","s":"Six months after a heart transplant, a man on immunosuppressants develops encephalitis. A Giemsa smear of his CSF shows crescent-shaped organisms about 6 × 3 µm with a central nucleus. Stored donor serum carries IgG against this organism; his own pre-transplant serum was negative. In what form did the parasite most likely reach him?","lec":"L7","sl":10,"c":"<b>Transplant-acquired toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"As sporozoites released from oocysts in the donor heart","B":"As sporulated oocysts lodged in the donor heart tissue","C":"As tachyzoites dividing rapidly in the donor heart muscle","D":"As tachyzoites in the residual blood within the donor heart","E":"As bradyzoites inside tissue cysts in the donor heart muscle"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Sporozoites come out of an ingested oocyst in the gut; they are not a tissue stage in an organ donor.","B":"Oocysts form only in the cat's intestine; they never occur in human or animal tissue.","C":"Rapidly dividing tachyzoites mean active infection; a latent, symptom-free donor carries slow-multiplying bradyzoites.","D":"Tachyzoites are the transfusion route. A donor with only IgG and no illness has no circulating tachyzoites.","E":"Correct, and the two-step trap is deliberate. The CSF shows TACHYZOITES (crescent, 5–12 × 2–4 µm, body fluids) — but a seropositive, never-ill donor carries latent tissue cysts, and slide 13/16 lists 'receiving an infected organ transplant' as the BRADYZOITE route. The bradyzoites then convert to tachyzoites in the immunosuppressed recipient."},"img":"slides_i/L7_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of transmission route with the Toxoplasma stage responsible is correct?","o":{"E":"Unpasteurised milk, blood transfusion and mother-to-child transmission all deliver the TACHYZOITE; raw meat and organ transplantation deliver the BRADYZOITE; contaminated food or water from cat faeces delivers the OOCYST","C":"All three routes deliver the oocyst, since it is the only environmentally resistant stage","D":"Raw meat delivers the tachyzoite; transplacental transmission delivers the bradyzoite","A":"Blood transfusion delivers the oocyst; raw meat delivers the tachyzoite","B":"Only the oocyst is infective; tachyzoites and bradyzoites are diagnostic stages only"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The route tells you the stage, and the stage tells you the prevention — which is why the deck draws the same information twice.","C":"The oocyst comes ONLY from cat faeces. Milk, blood and the placenta carry tachyzoites; meat and transplanted organs carry bradyzoites.","D":"Exactly inverted: raw meat carries the tissue-cyst BRADYZOITE, and congenital transmission carries the TACHYZOITE.","A":"Both halves inverted — transfusion carries tachyzoites and raw meat carries bradyzoites.","B":"The deck states plainly that ALL parasite stages are infective stages; that is what makes Toxoplasma so hard to avoid."},"t":"Toxoplasma — route carries stage","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L7_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Toxoplasma — route carries stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"What the mosquito must take up","s":"Entomologists let laboratory-reared female Anopheles feed on two untreated malaria patients. Two weeks later the mosquitoes that fed on patient X carry sporozoites in their salivary glands, while those that fed on patient Y carry none. Which stage was most likely missing from patient Y's blood?","lec":"L7","sl":5,"c":"<b>What the mosquito must take up</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Merozoite","B":"Gametocyte","C":"Growing trophozoite","D":"Mature schizont","E":"Ring form"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Merozoites invade human red cells; they cannot fertilise.","B":"Correct. The mosquito is the definitive host because the SEXUAL cycle happens in it, and that cycle starts only from ingested gametocytes (macro = female, micro = male; slide 5, 49) — then zygote, oocyst and finally sporozoites in the sporogonic cycle (slide 33).","C":"Growing trophozoites are asexual blood stages, digested by the mosquito.","D":"Schizonts are asexual and release merozoites into human red cells, not into the mosquito cycle.","E":"Ring forms (early trophozoites) are asexual; a mosquito that ingests only rings cannot start sporogony."},"img":"slides_i/L7_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the malaria life cycle is correct?","o":{"B":"Only P. vivax and P. ovale form hypnozoites, which stay dormant in hepatocytes; the erythrocytic cycle takes 48 h in vivax and ovale, 72 h in P. malariae, and 24–48 h in P. falciparum","D":"All five species form hypnozoites, which is why relapse can follow any malaria","E":"Hypnozoites are dormant forms inside red cells, and the erythrocytic cycle is 48 h in every species","C":"P. malariae forms hypnozoites and has the shortest erythrocytic cycle","A":"P. falciparum forms hypnozoites, which is why it causes cerebral malaria"},"r":{"B":"Correct on both the species restriction and all three cycle lengths — and the hypnozoite is why vivax and ovale need radical cure.","D":"Only vivax and ovale form hypnozoites; falciparum and malariae do not relapse from the liver.","E":"Hypnozoites are in HEPATOCYTES, not red cells, and the cycle lengths differ between species.","C":"P. malariae has the LONGEST cycle at 72 h and forms no hypnozoite.","A":"P. falciparum forms no hypnozoite; cerebral malaria comes from sequestration of infected red cells, not from liver forms."},"t":"Hypnozoite and periodicity","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L7_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Hypnozoite and periodicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Predict the rest of the film","s":"In the ideal area of a thin film from a febrile traveller, the infected red cells are the same size as their neighbours, the rings are so small that about five would span a cell's diameter, and several cells hold two rings. Which additional finding would you expect on this film?","lec":"L7","sl":51,"c":"<b>Predict the rest of the film</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fimbriated infected cells with a comet-like tail","B":"Rosette schizonts of six to twelve merozoites","C":"Band-form trophozoites lying across the red cell","D":"Pink Schüffner's dots throughout the infected cells","E":"Banana-shaped gametocytes spanning the red cell"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Fimbriated, comet-shaped cells are P. ovale, which enlarges the cell.","B":"The 6–12-merozoite rosette is P. malariae; the P. falciparum mature schizont has >12 (about 24) and is not found in uncomplicated cases.","C":"The band form is P. malariae — also a normal-size ('FM') species, which is why it is tempting, but its ring is ⅓ of the cell and multiple infection is a falciparum sign.","D":"Schüffner's dots belong to P. vivax and P. ovale, both in enlarged cells. P. falciparum shows Maurer's clefts instead.","E":"Correct. Normal RBC + ring ⅕ of the cell + double/multiple infection = P. falciparum (slide 51, 54). Its other diagnostic stage in peripheral blood is the banana-shaped gametocyte (slide 56, 63)."},"img":"slides_i/L7_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A traveller from the Thai–Myanmar border has a film showing numerous small rings occupying about a fifth of each red cell, several cells with two parasites, some rings hugging the cell margin, and a banana-shaped gametocyte. No schizonts are seen. Which statement is correct?","o":{"E":"This is P. falciparum, and the ABSENCE of schizonts is expected because its schizont-containing cells sequester in deep capillaries rather than circulating","A":"This is P. falciparum, and the absence of schizonts means the infection is too early to treat","B":"This is P. vivax, because multiple infection of one cell is its hallmark","C":"This is P. malariae, because its rings are the smallest of the four species","D":"The banana-shaped gametocyte excludes falciparum, since banana forms are seen only in P. ovale"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Every listed feature is falciparum, and the deck notes explicitly that the schizont stage is not found in uncomplicated malaria because those red cells adhere in deep vessels.","A":"Species right, inference wrong — and dangerously so. Falciparum is the species you treat urgently, not the one you wait on.","B":"Multiple infection of a single cell is a FALCIPARUM feature; vivax enlarges the cell and shows Schuffner’s dots.","C":"P. malariae infects normal-sized cells but is recognised by its BAND form and rosette schizonts with 6–12 merozoites.","D":"The banana (crescent) gametocyte is the classic falciparum finding; P. ovale gametocytes are round."},"t":"Falciparum film features","sl":59,"img":"slides_i/L7_59.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Falciparum film features</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Ideal area — reading step 1 honestly","s":"A student reads a thin film near its feathered tail, where the red cells lie spread flat and none shows a central pale area. The infected cells look larger than their neighbours elsewhere, so she reports an enlarged-cell species. What should her supervisor tell her?","lec":"L7","sl":45,"c":"<b>Ideal area — reading step 1 honestly</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Re-read on the thick film, as it preserves red-cell size better than the thin film","B":"Re-read where most cells keep central pallor, as flattened cells look falsely enlarged","C":"Re-read at the thick end, as cells overlapping there make their size easier to compare","D":"Keep the report, as an enlarged infected cell is diagnostic wherever it is found","E":"Keep the report, as flattened cells shrink, so real enlargement is if anything understated"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The thick film lyses the red cells, so their size cannot be judged at all; it is for sensitivity, not identification.","B":"Correct. Slide 45 marks the 'ideal area' between the too-thin and too-thick zones; the lecturer defines it as ≥80% of RBCs still showing central pallor (biconcave). In the too-thin zone cells flatten and look enlarged — which fakes step 1 (RBC size) of the species key.","C":"The thick end is labelled 'smear too thick' — overlapping cells make size comparison impossible.","D":"RBC size is only interpretable in the ideal area; outside it, 'enlargement' can be an artefact.","E":"The opposite of what happens: flattened cells look bigger, not smaller."},"img":"slides_i/L7_45.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about stippling in infected red cells is correct?","o":{"B":"Schüffner's dots appear in P. vivax and P. ovale, both of which enlarge the red cell; Maurer's clefts appear in P. falciparum, which does not","C":"Schüffner's dots appear in P. falciparum and Maurer's clefts in P. vivax","A":"Schüffner's dots appear in all four species and therefore cannot discriminate","D":"Maurer's clefts are the malarial pigment seen at high power","E":"Stippling appears only in P. malariae, which is why its band form is so distinct"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the pairing is easy to hold: the two species that swell the cell are the two that show Schuffner’s dots.","C":"Straight swap of the two.","A":"If stippling were universal it would be useless — its restriction to vivax and ovale is precisely its diagnostic value.","D":"Malarial pigment (haemozoin) is yellowish-brown granular material inside the parasite; Maurer’s clefts are red-cell membrane structures.","E":"P. malariae shows no Schuffner’s dots; its discriminator is the band-form trophozoite in a normal-sized cell."},"t":"Dots and clefts","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L7_47.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Dots and clefts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Oocyst sporulation time and litter hygiene","s":"A seronegative woman at 12 weeks' gestation lives with an indoor cat that also hunts mice in the garden. Her husband empties the litter tray once a week. Which advice best targets the risk that comes from the tray, for the right reason?","lec":"L7","sl":9,"c":"<b>Oocyst sporulation time and litter hygiene</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Empty the tray daily, as shed oocysts need 3–5 days before they can infect","B":"Keep weekly emptying, as oocysts become infective only inside a rodent","C":"Empty the tray fortnightly, as shed oocysts need 7–15 days before they can infect","D":"Rehome the cat, as its faeces carry tachyzoites that infect on contact","E":"Disinfect the tray with chlorine, as the oocysts are infective when passed"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The oocyst leaves the cat unsporulated and takes 3–5 days to sporulate (slide 9), so a tray emptied every day never holds an infective oocyst — the practical form of 'manage cat faeces disposal' (slide 22).","B":"Oocysts sporulate in the environment and infect humans directly; the mouse is an intermediate host for the cat, not a required step.","C":"7–15 days is Cyclospora's sporulation time (L6). A fortnightly tray would be full of infective oocysts.","D":"Cats shed oocysts, not tachyzoites; tachyzoites are the milk/transfusion/placental stage.","E":"Freshly passed oocysts are unsporulated and not yet infective; being infective when passed describes Cryptosporidium (L6), which is also chlorine-resistant."},"img":"slides_i/L7_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A pregnant woman asks whether she must give away her indoor cat. Which reply is consistent with the lecture?","o":{"A":"The oocyst is shed UNsporulated and takes 3–5 days to become infective, so daily litter-box emptying (ideally by someone else), cooking meat thoroughly and washing vegetables matter more than rehoming the cat","D":"The oocyst is infective the moment it is passed, so any contact with the litter box transmits infection immediately","C":"Cats shed tachyzoites in their faeces, so the litter box is the main danger and the cat must go","B":"Toxoplasma is transmitted only by raw meat, so the cat is irrelevant","E":"The oocyst needs 7–15 days to sporulate, the same as Cyclospora"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and the 3–5 day window is the practical point: a litter box emptied every day never contains an infective oocyst.","D":"The oocyst leaves the cat UNsporulated, which is exactly why daily cleaning works.","C":"Cats shed OOCYSTS, not tachyzoites, and rehoming is not what the control slide recommends.","B":"Raw meat is one route among several — the deck lists six modes of infection.","E":"7–15 days is CYCLOSPORA’s sporulation time from L6; Toxoplasma takes 3–5 days."},"t":"Toxoplasma oocyst timing","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L7_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Toxoplasma oocyst timing</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Mode of infection — stage and vehicle","s":"Meat inspectors examining pork from a village market find, within the muscle, thick-walled spherical structures over 200 µm across, each packed with hundreds of slender single-nucleus organisms. A family eats this pork grilled rare. Which answer states their mode of infection?","lec":"L7","sl":11,"c":"<b>Mode of infection — stage and vehicle</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing tachyzoites","B":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing bradyzoites","C":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing cysticerci","D":"Ingestion of undercooked pork contaminated with oocysts","E":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing sporozoites"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Tachyzoites are the free, rapidly multiplying stage in body fluids (milk, blood, CSF), not the contents of a tissue cyst.","B":"Correct. A thick-walled cyst >200 µm holding 50–3,000 single-nucleus organisms is the Toxoplasma tissue cyst of bradyzoites (slide 11). The mode of infection follows the lecturer's formula — action + vehicle + stage: ingestion of raw meat with the bradyzoite stage (slide 13, 16).","C":"A cysticercus (Taenia solium) is a single larva with one scolex in a fluid-filled bladder, not a cyst packed with hundreds of organisms.","D":"Oocysts come only from cat faeces; they are the food/water route, not a meat stage.","E":"Sporozoites are the contents of a sporulated oocyst (2 sporocysts × 4 sporozoites), not of a tissue cyst."},"img":"slides_i/L7_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the two Toxoplasma stages found in humans?","o":{"B":"The tachyzoite is a crescent-shaped rapidly multiplying form, 5–12 × 2–4 µm, found in nucleated cells and body fluids such as blood, milk and CSF; the bradyzoite is the slowly multiplying form inside a tissue cyst over 200 µm across holding 50–3,000 organisms","D":"The bradyzoite is the rapidly multiplying form in body fluids and the tachyzoite is the dormant tissue form","E":"Both are found only in cat intestinal epithelium","A":"The tissue cyst contains 4 sporozoites within 2 sporocysts","C":"Neither is infective; only the oocyst can transmit toxoplasmosis"},"r":{"B":"Correct on shape, size, speed and location for both stages — the names encode the speed: tachy- fast, brady- slow.","D":"Inverted. Tachy means fast; it is the tachyzoite that multiplies rapidly in body fluids.","E":"Cat intestinal epithelium is where the SEXUAL cycle and oocyst production occur; tachyzoites and bradyzoites occupy tissues throughout the intermediate host.","A":"Two sporocysts with four sporozoites each describes the sporulated OOCYST, not the tissue cyst, which holds 50–3,000 bradyzoites.","C":"All three stages are infective — stated explicitly on the stages slide."},"t":"Tachyzoite vs bradyzoite","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L7_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Tachyzoite vs bradyzoite</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Personal prevention of malaria","s":"A seasonal worker will spend a month harvesting durian in a Chanthaburi orchard at the forest edge, where pickers who come back with fever, chills and sweating are common. Which is a correct personal prevention measure against this disease?","lec":"L7","sl":66,"c":"<b>Personal prevention of malaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sleeping under an insecticide-treated net and using repellent","B":"Wearing closed shoes to stop larvae penetrating the feet","C":"Avoiding undercooked meat and unpasteurised milk in the camp","D":"Boiling or filtering all water collected from forest streams","E":"Spraying an insecticide such as DDT directly onto exposed skin"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Forest + fever + chills + sweating = malaria. Slide 66's prevention is avoiding Anopheles bites: insecticide-treated bed nets, protective clothing and insect repellents.","B":"Skin penetration by larvae is the hookworm/Strongyloides route, not malaria.","C":"That is Toxoplasma prevention (bradyzoites in meat, tachyzoites in milk).","D":"Malaria is vector-borne, not water-borne.","E":"The answer the lecturer marks wrong: insecticide is sprayed for vector control, never onto the skin — the personal measure is a repellent."},"img":"slides_i/L7_66.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A febrile traveller returning from Ranong has chills, headache, myalgia, nausea and jaundice, but no rash and no enlarged lymph nodes. Which statement is most useful?","o":{"A":"Absence of rash and lymphadenopathy is consistent with malaria and helps separate it from dengue and from toxoplasmosis, and the classical paroxysm runs cold stage → hot stage → sweating stage","E":"Absence of rash argues against malaria, which typically produces a maculopapular eruption","D":"Lymphadenopathy is the cardinal sign of malaria and its absence excludes the diagnosis","C":"The paroxysm runs hot stage → cold stage → sweating stage","B":"Jaundice excludes malaria, since haemolysis does not occur"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The deck prints ‘No rash and No lymphadenopathy’ on the symptom slide precisely because that is how malaria is separated from its febrile mimics.","E":"Malaria produces no rash — that is the point of the slide.","D":"Lymphadenopathy is a TOXOPLASMOSIS sign, and its absence supports rather than excludes malaria.","C":"The paroxysm begins with the COLD stage (rigors), then hot, then sweating.","B":"Jaundice and anaemia are both listed under uncomplicated malaria; haemolysis is central to the disease."},"t":"No rash, no lymphadenopathy","sl":40,"img":"slides_i/L7_40.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>No rash, no lymphadenopathy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Name the stage — not its role","s":"A practical station shows an elongated, curved parasite with rounded ends and dense cytoplasm with no vacuole, stretching a red cell no larger than its neighbours; the patient had fever after a Tak forest trip. The question reads 'Name the stage shown.' Which answer scores?","lec":"L7","sl":48,"c":"<b>Name the stage — not its role</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ookinete","B":"Mature schizont","C":"Diagnostic stage","D":"Gametocyte","E":"Mature trophozoite"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The ookinete is also elongated, but it is a mosquito-midgut stage after fertilisation and is never seen in human blood.","B":"A mature schizont is a rounded cluster of merozoites (about 24 in P. falciparum), not an elongated body.","C":"Zero marks by the lecturer's own rule: 'diagnostic stage' is its role, not its name. Name the stage.","D":"Correct. Elongated/banana shape in a normal-size RBC = the P. falciparum gametocyte (slide 56, 59). The lecturer's tip: a trophozoite keeps a trace of its ring/vacuole however much it grows; a gametocyte never had one and its cytoplasm is solid.","E":"A trophozoite, however mature, keeps its vacuole/ring trace and is not banana-shaped."},"img":"slides_i/L7_48.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about severe malaria matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"Severe and complicated malaria is chiefly P. falciparum, cerebral malaria is its commonest severe manifestation, and haemoglobinuria from massive intravascular haemolysis is called blackwater fever","E":"Severe malaria is chiefly P. vivax because of its hypnozoites, and blackwater fever is renal in origin","C":"P. malariae causes the most severe disease because of its 72-hour cycle","A":"Cerebral malaria is rare; acute renal failure is the commonest severe manifestation","B":"Blackwater fever refers to black stools from gastrointestinal bleeding in DIC"},"r":{"D":"Correct on species, on the commonest complication, and on the meaning of blackwater fever — dark urine from free haemoglobin, not from the gut.","E":"Hypnozoites cause RELAPSE, not severity. Severe disease is falciparum.","C":"P. malariae causes the mildest, most chronic disease of the four.","A":"Cerebral malaria is explicitly named as the commonest severe manifestation.","B":"Blackwater fever is haemoglobinURIA — the darkness is in the urine."},"t":"Severe malaria","sl":39,"img":"slides_i/L7_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Severe malaria</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Congenital toxoplasmosis — what to look for","s":"A newborn has bilateral retinal scars and dilated cerebral ventricles on ultrasound, and the mother recalls swollen neck glands in early pregnancy. Which microscopic finding would directly demonstrate the responsible parasite?","lec":"L7","sl":10,"c":"<b>Congenital toxoplasmosis — what to look for</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sporulated oocysts in a modified acid-fast smear of the infant's stool","B":"Thick-walled oocysts in a wet mount of the mother's stool","C":"Ring forms with double chromatin in the infant's thin blood film","D":"Bradyzoite tissue cysts in the mother's peripheral blood film","E":"Crescent-shaped tachyzoites in a Giemsa smear of placental tissue"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Humans do not produce Toxoplasma oocysts, and modified acid-fast is the L6 intestinal coccidia stain.","B":"The mother is an intermediate host like any human and sheds no oocysts; only cats do.","C":"Double-chromatin rings are P. falciparum in malaria, a different blood parasite.","D":"Bradyzoite cysts are tissue stages in organs (muscle, brain…), not in peripheral blood.","E":"Correct. Retinochoroiditis + hydrocephalus + maternal lymphadenopathy = congenital toxoplasmosis (transplacental tachyzoites). Slide 20 lists Giemsa detection of tachyzoites in lymph node, blood, CSF, bone marrow and placenta."},"img":"slides_i/L7_10.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about diagnosing toxoplasmosis matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"Serological antibody testing (IgG, IgM, IgA, IgE) is the primary method, with Giemsa-stained smears of lymph node, blood, CSF, bone marrow or placenta for tachyzoites, plus PCR and CT/MRI for brain lesions","B":"Stool examination for oocysts is the primary method in humans","C":"Modified acid-fast staining of stool is the method of choice","D":"Diagnosis is made only by tissue biopsy showing bradyzoite cysts; serology is unhelpful","A":"Thick and thin blood films are the primary method, as for malaria"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and note the breadth of acceptable specimens for tachyzoite detection — placenta is the one people forget in a congenital case.","B":"Humans do not shed Toxoplasma oocysts; only cats do.","C":"Modified acid-fast is for the intestinal coccidia of L6.","D":"Serology is named as the PRIMARY method, so calling it unhelpful inverts the slide.","A":"Blood films are the malaria method; Toxoplasma is not an intraerythrocytic parasite."},"t":"Toxoplasma diagnosis","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L7_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Toxoplasma diagnosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Order of stages in the human","s":"A man's infection begins with the bite of an infected mosquito and ends when a second mosquito becomes infected by feeding on him. Which sequence of stages inside the man is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":33,"c":"<b>Order of stages in the human</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sporozoite → hepatic schizont → merozoite → ring form → ookinete → gametocyte","B":"Sporozoite → hepatic schizont → merozoite → gametocyte → ring form → erythrocytic schizont","C":"Merozoite → hepatic schizont → sporozoite → ring form → erythrocytic schizont → gametocyte","D":"Sporozoite → hepatic schizont → merozoite → ring form → erythrocytic schizont → gametocyte","E":"Sporozoite → ring form → hepatic schizont → merozoite → erythrocytic schizont → gametocyte"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The ookinete is a mosquito-midgut stage after fertilisation; it never forms in the human.","B":"Gametocytes develop in red cells from merozoites of the erythrocytic cycle; they are the end of the human chain, not the start of it.","C":"Sporozoites are the injected infective stage, so they come first, not after the liver schizont.","D":"Correct. Injected sporozoite → schizogony in the hepatocyte → merozoites released into blood → ring form (early trophozoite) → erythrocytic schizont → some merozoites develop into gametocytes, which the next mosquito ingests (slide 34–37).","E":"The ring form is a red-cell stage; it cannot come before the liver cycle (exo-erythrocytic comes first)."},"img":"slides_i/L7_33.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which set of names for the three phases of the Plasmodium life cycle is correct?","o":{"D":"Exo-erythrocytic cycle in the human liver, erythrocytic cycle in human red cells, and sporogonic cycle in the female Anopheles","E":"Sporogonic cycle in the human liver, erythrocytic cycle in red cells, and exo-erythrocytic cycle in the mosquito","A":"Schizogony in the mosquito, gamogony in the liver, and sporogony in red cells","B":"Exo-erythrocytic cycle in red cells and erythrocytic cycle in the liver","C":"There are only two cycles, one in each host"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Two of the three happen in us, which is why the human is the intermediate host twice over and the mosquito only once.","E":"Sporogony happens in the MOSQUITO; ‘exo-erythrocytic’ means outside the red cell, i.e. the liver.","A":"Schizogony is asexual replication in humans; gamogony and sporogony occur in the mosquito.","B":"The two are swapped — exo-erythrocytic is by definition the non-red-cell (liver) phase.","C":"The deck states there are THREE cycles."},"t":"Three cycles","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L7_33.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Three cycles</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"When the thin film looks negative","s":"A man back from a forest trip in Trat four days ago has fever with chills, but an intern finds no parasites after scanning his thin film. The attending suspects a very low parasite count. Which step best addresses this, and why?","lec":"L7","sl":44,"c":"<b>When the thin film looks negative</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Examine a thick film, which keeps red-cell size intact for naming the species","B":"Examine more thin film, which keeps red cells intact so sparse parasites stand out","C":"Examine the thin film's feathered tail, where the cells lie farthest apart","D":"Examine a thick film, which lyses red cells so more blood is seen per field","E":"Send antibody serology, which turns positive before parasites reach the blood"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Right film, wrong reason: the thick film destroys red-cell outlines, so it cannot be used for species identification.","B":"The thin film is spread one cell thick, so it contains less blood per field — it is the less sensitive film.","C":"The feathered tail is the 'too thin' zone — fewer cells and flattened artefacts, not higher sensitivity.","D":"Correct. Slide 45: thin film = species identification; thick film = high sensitivity, because the dehaemoglobinised layer packs much more blood into each field.","E":"Antibodies follow infection; they do not precede parasitaemia, and serology is not the detection method taught for a febrile patient."},"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about malaria laboratory methods matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"The thin film is used for species identification while the thick film concentrates more blood and so detects low parasitaemia; antigen-detecting rapid tests and PCR are also listed","A":"The thick film is used for species identification and the thin film for sensitivity","C":"Only PCR can identify the species; microscopy merely confirms that malaria is present","E":"Rapid diagnostic tests detect antibody, so they are positive only after two weeks","B":"Serology replaces microscopy in endemic areas"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The thin film preserves red-cell morphology, which is what the species algorithm depends on; the thick film lyses the cells to pack more blood into the field.","A":"Swapped. A thick film destroys the red-cell outline, so RBC size — step 1 of the algorithm — cannot be judged on it.","C":"Microscopy is what identifies the species in routine practice; PCR is listed as an additional molecular method.","E":"The rapid test listed detects ANTIGEN, so it is positive during active parasitaemia.","B":"No such replacement is described; microscopy remains central."},"t":"Blood films and RDT","sl":44,"img":"slides_i/L7_44.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Blood films and RDT</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L7|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"The fifth species","s":"A 45-year-old man who camps in the forests of Ranong, where macaques are common, has fever with chills. His thin film shows band-like trophozoites and small rings in normal-sized red cells, and two microscopists disagree about the species. Which species should be suspected, and how is it confirmed?","lec":"L7","sl":25,"c":"<b>The fifth species</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"P. knowlesi, confirmed by counting merozoites on a thin film","B":"P. knowlesi, confirmed by PCR for parasite DNA","C":"P. ovale, confirmed by fimbriated red cells on a thin film","D":"P. falciparum, confirmed by banana gametocytes on a thin film","E":"P. malariae, confirmed by rosette schizonts on a thin film"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right species, wrong method: no microscopic feature, including the merozoite count, separates knowlesi reliably.","B":"Correct. P. knowlesi (a monkey malaria) is the fifth human species and 'cannot be distinguished by light microscopy' (slide 27) — it mimics P. malariae/falciparum — so molecular detection (PCR, slide 44) is needed. Ranong had the most Thai knowlesi cases (slide 25).","C":"P. ovale enlarges the red cell; these cells are normal-sized.","D":"Small rings in normal cells can suggest falciparum, but a banana gametocyte is not described and would not resolve a knowlesi mimic.","E":"Band forms in normal cells look like P. malariae — which is exactly how knowlesi is misread; the forest/macaque history and the disagreement point away from it."},"img":"slides_i/L7_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about Plasmodium knowlesi in Thailand matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"It cannot be distinguished from the other species by light microscopy, and Thai case numbers have risen sharply, with Ranong, Songkhla and Trat reporting the most","D":"It is readily identified on a thin film by its banana-shaped gametocyte","C":"It has disappeared from Thailand since the malaria elimination programme began","E":"It is the species that forms hypnozoites and therefore relapses","A":"It infects only macaques and has never been reported in humans in Thailand"},"r":{"B":"Correct on both: the deck marks it ไม่สามารถแยกได้ด้วยกล้องจุลทรรศน์ and gives the provincial breakdown, concentrated along the borders.","D":"Banana-shaped gametocytes belong to P. falciparum; knowlesi closely resembles P. malariae on film.","C":"Cases have RISEN, from 2 to 105, and provinces previously declared malaria-free have seen renewed transmission.","E":"Hypnozoites are formed by P. vivax and P. ovale.","A":"Human cases are exactly what the slide is reporting."},"t":"P. knowlesi","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L7_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>P. knowlesi</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233919896},{"id":"I|L8|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Answer format — chigger mite","s":"A 50-year-old rice farmer from Nan has had fever, chills, headache, myalgia and enlarged lymph nodes for a week. On his groin there is a black crusted lesion that looks like a cigarette burn. The written paper asks for the common name of the arthropod responsible and its medical importance. Which entry scores the mark?","lec":"L8","sl":59,"c":"<b>Answer format — chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Chigger mite — Orientia tsutsugamushi","B":"Soft tick — vector of scrub typhus","C":"Chigger mite — causes scrub typhus","D":"Hard tick — vector of Orientia tsutsugamushi","E":"Chigger mite — vector of scrub typhus"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Right arthropod, but a bare organism name with no 'vector of' scores zero — slide 65 marks the bare answer with a red cross.","B":"Soft ticks are listed only for tick-borne relapsing fever and tick bite paralysis.","C":"Wrong verb. The mite carries Orientia; it does not cause the disease. The lecturer used the same point for 'Anopheles causes malaria'.","D":"Correct phrasing but the wrong arthropod. Hard ticks carry Lyme disease, RMSF, relapsing fever, tick-borne meningoencephalitis, SFTSV and babesiosis — not scrub typhus.","E":"Correct. Rice field + fever/myalgia/lymphadenopathy + an eschar 'like a cigarette burn' = scrub typhus, carried by the chigger mite (larval trombiculid mite). The answer must open with 'Vector of…'; 'vector of Orientia tsutsugamushi' would also score (slide 65)."},"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A photograph of a chigger mite is shown and you are asked 'What is the medical importance?'. Which written answer scores the mark?","o":{"D":"Scrub typhus","E":"Vector of scrub typhus","A":"Orientia tsutsugamushi","B":"It causes scrub typhus","C":"Eschar resembling a cigarette burn"},"r":{"D":"Slide 65 marks a bare 'scrub typhus' WRONG in red — this is the single commonest way the mark is lost.","E":"Correct. Every answer must open with one of three phrases — 'Direct injury…', 'Vector of…' or 'Intermediate host of…'. 'Vector of Orientia tsutsugamushi' would also have scored.","A":"Naming the organism alone fails for the same reason. The lecturer will accept the organism, but only after 'vector of'.","B":"Wrong verb. The mite CARRIES the organism; it does not cause the disease. He used the same point about Anopheles and malaria.","C":"A clinical sign, not a medical importance."},"t":"Answer format","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L8_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Answer format</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Chigger mite — the leg-count exception","s":"A 0.2 mm arthropod is picked off the skin of a soldier after an exercise in scrubland. It has no antennae and no wings, a plate on its dorsal surface, and three pairs of legs. Which statement explains the leg count?","lec":"L8","sl":12,"c":"<b>Chigger mite — the leg-count exception</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is a hard tick nymph; tick nymphs have three pairs of legs","B":"It is a larva; its nymph and adult have four pairs","C":"It is an adult mite; mites shed one pair after they attach","D":"It is a follicle mite; its fourth pair is bud-like and hidden","E":"It is an adult insect; every insect has three pairs of legs"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Tick nymphs and adults have 4 pairs of legs; only the tick larva has 3. Ticks are also 2–10 mm, ten times this size.","B":"Correct. No antennae + no wings = Arachnida, whose adults have 4 pairs of legs and larvae 3 pairs (slide 12). A 0.1–0.3 mm six-legged arachnid with a dorsal scutum is the chigger — the larval trombiculid mite, the only parasitic stage; nymph and adult are free-living with 4 pairs (slide 59).","C":"Invented. Mites do not shed legs; the adult trombiculid is free-living and has 4 pairs.","D":"Demodex has 4 pairs of bud-like legs, a cylindrical body and lives inside hair follicles — not a scutum-bearing skin-surface larva.","E":"Insects do have 3 pairs of legs, but they also have antennae (1 pair) and usually wings — this specimen has neither."},"img":"slides_i/L8_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An arachnid specimen measures 0.2 mm, has a dorsal scutum, and has THREE pairs of legs. What is it, and why does the leg count fit?","o":{"D":"Demodex — its legs are bud-like and easily miscounted","A":"A hard tick — adult hard ticks have 3 pairs of legs","C":"A soft tick — it has no scutum, which is why only 3 pairs are visible","B":"A chigger mite larva — the larva is the only parasitic stage and it has 3 pairs of legs","E":"Sarcoptes scabiei — burrowing mites lose a pair of legs"},"r":{"D":"Demodex has 4 pairs of stubby legs and a cylindrical body, and lives inside a hair follicle.","A":"Adult and nymphal ticks have 4 pairs, and a tick is 2–10 mm — ten to fifty times this size.","C":"Soft ticks do lack a scutum, but this specimen has one, and they still have 4 pairs of legs.","B":"Correct, and this is the lecture's one deliberate exception to 'arachnid = 8 legs'. The adult and nymph are free-living with 4 pairs; only the 6-legged larva, the 'chigger', feeds on a host.","E":"Sarcoptes has 4 pairs of legs; nothing loses a pair."},"t":"Chigger mite","sl":59,"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Soft tick → its listed importance","s":"A tick removed from a patient is leathery, has no dorsal shield, and its mouthparts cannot be seen when it is viewed from above. Which medical importance is listed in the lecture for this kind of tick?","lec":"L8","sl":56,"c":"<b>Soft tick → its listed importance</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of tick-borne relapsing fever","B":"Vector of tick-borne meningoencephalitis","C":"Vector of Lyme disease","D":"Vector of babesiosis","E":"Vector of Rocky Mountain spotted fever"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. No scutum + capitulum not visible from above = soft tick (Argasidae). Its slide lists only tick-borne relapsing fever and tick bite paralysis (toxin).","B":"Hard tick list (viral), with SFTSV.","C":"Hard tick (Ixodes scapularis) — hard ticks have the scutum and a capitulum visible from above.","D":"Hard tick list — the protozoan, malaria-like disease.","E":"Hard tick list (bacterial)."},"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"What is the single feature that separates a hard tick from a soft tick, and what does it explain about how the two look when engorged?","o":{"C":"The hard tick has festoons; the soft tick has a capitulum","B":"The soft tick has a scutum; the hard tick's cuticle is flexible","E":"The hard tick has four pairs of legs; the soft tick has three","A":"The hard tick has a scutum, which locks its body shape; the soft tick has none and swells like a balloon","D":"The hard tick feeds on blood; the soft tick does not feed"},"r":{"C":"Festoons are a hard-tick feature, but the capitulum is present in both — it is simply not VISIBLE from above in a soft tick.","B":"Exactly reversed. Ixodidae (hard) have the scutum; Argasidae (soft) do not.","E":"Both have four pairs as adults; only the LARVA of either has three.","A":"Correct. The scutum is a rigid dorsal plate. With it the outline is fixed; without it the body expands as it fills with blood or eggs — the lecturer's own image.","D":"Both are blood-feeding ectoparasites."},"t":"Ticks","sl":56,"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Ticks</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Anopheles recognition → answer format","s":"In the practical, a female mosquito has palpi as long as its proboscis and wing margins with alternating white and black scales. Which written medical importance scores the mark?","lec":"L8","sl":1005,"c":"<b>Anopheles recognition → answer format</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Causes malaria","B":"Plasmodium spp.","C":"Vector of Japanese B encephalitis","D":"Vector of dengue virus","E":"Vector of malaria"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Wrong verb — 'Does Anopheles cause malaria? No.' The lecturer marks this wrong.","B":"A bare organism name with no 'vector of' scores zero.","C":"Culex — short palpi and plain brown scales.","D":"Aedes — short palpi and snow-white thoracic scales.","E":"Correct. Long palpi = anopheline; spotted wing = Anopheles (slide 9) → vector of malaria (and of W. bancrofti) (slide 10). 'Vector of Plasmodium spp.' would also score."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1005.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Under the microscope a mosquito has palpi as long as its proboscis and wing margins with alternating white and black scales. What is its medical importance?","o":{"C":"Vector of Japanese B encephalitis","A":"Vector of dengue virus, chikungunya and Zika virus","E":"Vector of malaria, and vector of lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti)","B":"Vector of lymphatic filariasis (Brugia malayi)","D":"Mechanical transmitter of enteric pathogens"},"r":{"C":"Japanese B encephalitis is Culex, which is plain brown with short palpi.","A":"That is Aedes, identified by snow-white scales on the thorax and short palpi.","E":"Correct. Palpi equal to the proboscis makes it an ANOPHELINE, and the spotted wing confirms Anopheles — the malaria vector, which also carries W. bancrofti.","B":"B. malayi is carried by Mansonia only — the exception that every matching question is built on.","D":"Mosquitoes are biological, not mechanical, transmitters."},"t":"Mosquito ID","sl":1005,"img":"slides_i/L8_1005.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Aedes recognition → filarial species","s":"A mosquito has short palpi, a trilobed scutellum, a pointed abdominal tip and snow-white scales on the thorax. Which of these is a medical importance listed for it in the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":1017,"c":"<b>Aedes recognition → filarial species</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of Japanese B encephalitis virus","B":"Vector of Leishmania spp.","C":"Vector of Plasmodium spp.","D":"Vector of Wuchereria bancrofti","E":"Vector of Brugia malayi"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Culex.","B":"The sand fly (Phlebotomus), not a mosquito.","C":"Anopheles.","D":"Correct. Snow-white thoracic scales + pointed tip = Aedes. Its slide lists dengue, chikungunya, Zika AND lymphatic filariasis (W. bancrofti) — the fourth one is the one students forget.","E":"Mansonia's species (L2 also lists Anopheles) — not Aedes."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1017.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which feature should you look at FIRST to tell Aedes from Culex?","o":{"B":"Resting at 45 degrees to the surface","C":"A trilobed scutellum","A":"Short palpi relative to the proboscis","D":"Snow-white scales on the thorax","E":"Pilose antennae"},"r":{"B":"An Anopheles feature — and useless in the lab anyway, since a mounted specimen is not resting on anything.","C":"Shared by all three culicines (Culex, Aedes, Mansonia), so it cannot separate them.","A":"Also shared by all three culicines; it only separates them from Anopheles.","D":"Correct. The lecturer said to go straight to the thorax: Aedes carries scales 'like snow settled on it'. Culex is uniformly brown with no distinguishing marking at all — it is identified by the ABSENCE of a feature.","E":"Pilose antennae mark a FEMALE, not a genus."},"t":"Mosquito ID","sl":1013,"img":"slides_i/L8_1013.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Linear lesion → rove beetle recognition","s":"A 20-year-old woman wakes with several linear erythematous streaks with papules and a burning feeling on her neck. The night before she wiped a small insect off her neck while reading under a fluorescent lamp. Which description fits that insect?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Linear lesion → rove beetle recognition</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"2.5 cm, cone-shaped head, wings overlapping in an X shape","B":"4–7 mm, black head and tail tip, orange thorax, blue elytra","C":"1.5–2.5 mm, light brown, hairy body and wings held in a V","D":"0.3–0.4 mm, cylindrical body with four pairs of bud-like legs","E":"2–6 mm, wingless, flattened side to side, jumping hind legs"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Kissing bug — vector of T. cruzi, no dermatitis toxin.","B":"Correct. Linear lesion + attracted to fluorescent light = Paederus dermatitis → rove beetle: 4–7 mm, black head and abdominal apex, orange thorax and abdominal base, iridescent blue elytra (slide 34; NL item slide 68).","C":"Sand fly — vector of leishmaniasis.","D":"Demodex — folliculitis and acne inside hair follicles.","E":"Flea — flea-allergy dermatitis, not a linear burn."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A student wakes with burning, itchy red patches on the front of the elbow AND on the facing surface of the forearm, having crushed an insect there the night before. What is the diagnosis, and why do the two patches match?","o":{"B":"Paederus dermatitis — paederin transferred to the opposite skin surface when the joint was flexed","E":"Classical scabiasis — the mite burrowed along the flexure","C":"Pediculosis — the louse fed at two adjacent points","A":"Bedbug bites — bugs feed in rows along exposed skin","D":"Norwegian scabiasis — crusted lesions appear symmetrically"},"r":{"B":"Correct. This is the 'kissing lesion'. Paederin digests tissue, so wherever the toxin lands it burns, and a flexed joint presses the two skin surfaces together — giving mirror-image lesions. The phrase 'after crushing the arthropod' appears in every past-paper version.","E":"Scabies gives BURROWS, classically between the fingers, with night-time itch and no burning and no crushed insect.","C":"Lice cause itching without a mirror-image burn.","A":"Bedbug lesions are separate papules in a line, not matched patches, and there is no crushing history.","D":"Crusted scabies occurs in the immunocompromised with a huge mite burden — a different clinical picture entirely."},"t":"Rove beetle","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Rove beetle</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Blister beetle — prevention","s":"A farmer working among his tomato plants develops large fluid-filled blisters on the forearms where he brushed off some beetles, and later a painful red eye with keratitis. Which preventive advice does the lecture list for this beetle?","lec":"L8","sl":38,"c":"<b>Blister beetle — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Apply insect repellent to exposed skin before work","B":"Control rodents around the fields and the barn","C":"Change white fluorescent bulbs to yellow ones","D":"Remove pigweed and cut grass before it flowers","E":"Close doors and windows tightly after dark"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Repellent is the chigger and flea measure.","B":"Rodent control is for the oriental rat flea.","C":"Rove beetle measure — it is drawn to fluorescent light.","D":"Correct. Large blisters + ocular keratitis = cantharidin → blister beetle (new in the 2567 criteria). Its prevention slide: avoid crushing, wash with soap, keep toxin out of the eyes, remove weeds like pigweed and cut grass before flowering, wear long sleeves, trousers and gloves.","E":"Rove beetle measure."},"img":"slides_i/L8_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which beetle toxin can threaten vision, and what does it do to skin?","o":{"C":"Paederin — it causes large blisters, and in the eye conjunctivitis only","D":"Cantharidin — it causes large blisters, and in the eye keratitis and corneal ulceration","E":"Cantharidin — it digests tissue, producing kissing and linear lesions","A":"Paederin — it causes corneal ulceration and renal failure","B":"Both toxins are identical; only the beetle's name differs"},"r":{"C":"Paederin belongs to the ROVE beetle and gives pustules and kissing/linear lesions, with no ocular hazard listed.","D":"Correct. Cantharidin is the blister beetle's toxin. Skin: vesicles and large blisters. Eye: conjunctivitis, keratitis, corneal abrasion and ulceration, which may threaten vision. Ingested, it can cause renal failure, shock and death.","E":"Tissue digestion and the kissing lesion are paederin's, not cantharidin's.","A":"The toxin name is wrong for both effects.","B":"They are different molecules with clearly different clinical pictures — which is exactly what the exam tests."},"t":"Beetles","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L8_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Beetles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Mechanical vs biological transmission","s":"In which arthropod–pathogen pairing does the pathogen neither multiply nor develop inside the arthropod?","lec":"L8","sl":16,"c":"<b>Mechanical vs biological transmission</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Wingless jumping insect of rats — Yersinia pestis","B":"Hairy 2 mm pool-feeding fly — Leishmania spp.","C":"Six-legged larval mite of scrub — Orientia tsutsugamushi","D":"Sponging-mouthed non-biting fly — Shigella spp.","E":"Day-biting mosquito with white thoracic scales — dengue virus"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Flea → plague: listed as biological transmission (slide 18).","B":"Sand fly → leishmaniasis: biological.","C":"Chigger mite → scrub typhus: biological.","D":"Correct. The house fly (sponging mouthparts) is a mechanical transmitter: it picks organisms up from faeces and drops them on food — dysentery (E. histolytica), poliomyelitis and Shigella gastroenteritis (slide 17).","E":"Aedes → dengue virus: biological."},"img":"slides_i/L8_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What defines a MECHANICAL transmitter?","o":{"B":"The arthropod must take a blood meal to transmit","E":"The pathogen multiplies inside the arthropod but does not change stage","C":"The pathogen develops but does not multiply inside the arthropod","D":"The pathogen neither multiplies nor develops inside the arthropod","A":"The arthropod is always an insect rather than an arachnid"},"r":{"B":"The house fly and cockroach, the two commonest mechanical vectors, do not bite at all.","E":"Multiplication inside the vector makes it BIOLOGICAL.","C":"Development inside the vector also makes it biological.","D":"Correct, and it is printed on slide 16 as the key characteristic. The organism is simply carried on the body or feet — picked up from faeces and dropped on food, drink or tissue.","A":"Irrelevant to the definition — and both classes contain biological vectors."},"t":"Vector types","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L8_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Vector types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Cat flea → its vector role","s":"Fleas combed from a household pet are identified as Ctenocephalides felis. Which vector role does the lecture list for this species?","lec":"L8","sl":50,"c":"<b>Cat flea → its vector role</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of cat scratch disease","B":"Vector of tick-borne relapsing fever","C":"Vector of plague","D":"Vector of murine typhus","E":"Vector of Lyme disease"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Ctenocephalides felis (cat flea) → cat scratch disease (slide 51). Fleas as a group are also intermediate hosts of Dipylidium caninum and Hymenolepis, and cause flea-allergy dermatitis.","B":"Ticks (hard and soft).","C":"Xenopsylla cheopis, the oriental rat flea.","D":"Also Xenopsylla cheopis.","E":"Hard tick."},"img":"slides_i/L8_50.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which single arthropod in this lecture can correctly be answered under ALL THREE headings of medical importance?","o":{"A":"The flea","B":"The chigger mite","D":"The rove beetle","C":"The tsetse fly","E":"Demodex"},"r":{"A":"Correct — the lecturer called it the answer that 'covers the universe'. VECTOR: plague and murine typhus (Xenopsylla cheopis), cat-scratch disease (Ctenocephalides felis). INTERMEDIATE HOST: Dipylidium caninum, Hymenolepis nana, H. diminuta. DIRECT INJURY: flea-allergy dermatitis.","B":"Vector only.","D":"Direct injury only — it carries nothing and hosts nothing.","C":"Vector only.","E":"Direct injury only (folliculitis, inflammatory acne)."},"t":"Flea","sl":51,"img":"slides_i/L8_51.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Flea</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Sleeping sickness → tsetse recognition","s":"A safari guide returning from East Africa has fever followed weeks later by daytime somnolence, and trypomastigotes are found in his blood. Which feature would identify the insect that transmitted it?","lec":"L8","sl":31,"c":"<b>Sleeping sickness → tsetse recognition</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Wing margins spotted white and black","B":"A hairy body and wings held in a V","C":"Wings overlapping in an X over the abdomen","D":"Snow-white scales on the thorax","E":"A forward-projecting piercing proboscis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Anopheles — malaria.","B":"Sand fly — leishmaniasis.","C":"Kissing bug — Trypanosoma cruzi, American trypanosomiasis.","D":"Aedes — dengue, chikungunya, Zika, W. bancrofti.","E":"Correct. African sleeping sickness → Trypanosoma brucei → tsetse fly (Glossina), whose one feature is the proboscis projecting forward off the head."},"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Match the three blood-feeding Diptera to their one identifying feature.","o":{"B":"Tsetse = hairy body; kissing bug = forward-projecting proboscis; sand fly = wings forming an 'X'","D":"Tsetse = wings forming an 'X'; kissing bug = hairy body; sand fly = forward-projecting proboscis","E":"Tsetse = forward-projecting proboscis; kissing bug = wings forming an 'X'; sand fly = hairy body and wings","A":"All three are identified by a piercing-sucking proboscis and cannot be separated on morphology","C":"Tsetse = cone-shaped head; kissing bug = spotted wings; sand fly = short palpi"},"r":{"B":"Same three features, shuffled again.","D":"All three features are present in the lecture but assigned to the wrong insects.","E":"Correct. One feature each is all the lecturer asked for: the tsetse's proboscis projects straight forward off the head, the kissing bug's wings overlap into a distinct letter X, and the sand fly is 'a mosquito with hair on it' — hairy body AND wings.","A":"All three do have piercing mouthparts, but each has a printed distinguishing feature — which is exactly what the exam asks for.","C":"A cone-shaped head belongs to the kissing bug, spotted wings to Anopheles, and short palpi to the culicine mosquitoes."},"t":"Blood-sucking flies","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Blood-sucking flies</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Kissing bug → answer format","s":"An insect collected from a rural house in Brazil is about 2.5 cm long, has a cone-shaped head, and its wings overlap on the abdomen to form a distinct letter X. What is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":32,"c":"<b>Kissing bug → answer format</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of sleeping sickness","B":"Vector of Trypanosoma cruzi","C":"Vector of Trypanosoma brucei","D":"Vector of Leishmania spp.","E":"Chagas disease"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Sleeping sickness is African trypanosomiasis — tsetse fly.","B":"Correct. Cone head + 'X' wings = kissing bug (Triatoma) → vector of T. cruzi / American trypanosomiasis (Chagas disease).","C":"T. brucei is the tsetse fly's parasite (Africa).","D":"Leishmania is carried by the sand fly.","E":"Right disease but a bare disease name scores zero."},"img":"slides_i/L8_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of parasite, vector and continent is correct?","o":{"C":"Trypanosoma brucei — sand fly — visceral leishmaniasis","A":"Trypanosoma brucei — kissing bug — American trypanosomiasis","E":"Trypanosoma cruzi — tsetse fly — African sleeping sickness","D":"Trypanosoma cruzi — sand fly — Chagas disease","B":"Trypanosoma brucei — tsetse fly — African sleeping sickness"},"r":{"C":"Two errors: wrong vector and wrong disease.","A":"The vector and the continent have both been swapped. The kissing bug carries T. cruzi in the Americas.","E":"The parasite and vector are mismatched.","D":"The sand fly carries Leishmania, not a trypanosome.","B":"Correct. T. brucei travels with the tsetse fly (Glossina) and causes AFRICAN trypanosomiasis, i.e. sleeping sickness."},"t":"Trypanosomes","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Trypanosomes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Brugia malayi → where to look for larvae","s":"A night blood film from a villager in southern Thailand shows Brugia malayi. Where should the larval survey for the genus that the entomology lecture lists as its vector be concentrated?","lec":"L8","sl":1022,"c":"<b>Brugia malayi → where to look for larvae</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ponds and swamps covered with floating aquatic plants","B":"Coconut shells and bamboo stumps holding rainwater","C":"Drains and ditches of dirty, low-oxygen water","D":"Water with cattle dung in and around the cow shed","E":"Water jars and cement tanks used to store household water"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. B. malayi → Mansonia (commonest in the south) → swamps and ponds covered with aquatic plants, or forest water with roots in it; its larvae attach to plant roots for air.","B":"Aedes — natural containers.","C":"Culex.","D":"Culex — water with animal droppings.","E":"Aedes — domestic water-storage containers."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1022.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which breeding site matches which mosquito?","o":{"A":"Anopheles = clean natural slow-moving water; Culex = dirty low-oxygen water; Aedes = clean water in household containers; Mansonia = swamps with aquatic plants","D":"Anopheles = household containers; Culex = forest streams; Aedes = dirty drains; Mansonia = rice fields","E":"Anopheles = dirty water; Culex = household containers; Aedes = forest streams; Mansonia = brackish water","B":"All four breed in any standing water and cannot be separated by habitat","C":"Anopheles = swamps with aquatic plants; Culex = rock pools; Aedes = animal footprints; Mansonia = water jars"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and this four-line summary is printed verbatim as an answer key in the หลักสูตร 54 bank. Water quality is the fastest way to hold the grid: wild-clean, dirty, household-clean, plant.","D":"Every pairing is shuffled.","E":"Anopheles and Culex are swapped, as are Aedes and Mansonia.","B":"Habitat is exactly what these questions test.","C":"Rock pools and animal footprints are Anopheles sites; water jars are Aedes sites."},"t":"Mosquito breeding","sl":1011,"img":"slides_i/L8_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Mosquito breeding</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"W. bancrofti microfilaria → which genera","s":"A night blood film shows a sheathed, gently curved microfilaria with a short head space and separate body nuclei that stop short of the tail tip. According to the mosquito lecture, which genera are listed as vectors of this species?","lec":"L8","sl":1010,"c":"<b>W. bancrofti microfilaria → which genera</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Culex and Mansonia","B":"Aedes and Mansonia","C":"Mansonia alone","D":"Anopheles, Culex and Aedes","E":"Anopheles, Culex, Aedes and Mansonia"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Culex yes, Mansonia no.","B":"Aedes yes, Mansonia no.","C":"Mansonia is the Brugia malayi vector.","D":"Correct. No nuclei in the tail tip + separated nuclei + gentle curves = Wuchereria bancrofti (L2). In L8 it is listed under Anopheles, Culex and Aedes; Mansonia carries Brugia malayi — the one split every matching question is built on.","E":"Mansonia's only listed pathogen is B. malayi."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1010.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Three of the four mosquito genera taught carry the same filarial species and one carries a different one. Which is the odd one out?","o":{"B":"Anopheles, which carries Brugia malayi while the other three carry W. bancrofti","D":"Mansonia, which carries Brugia malayi while the other three carry Wuchereria bancrofti","A":"Aedes, which carries Brugia timori while the other three carry W. bancrofti","C":"Culex, which carries no filaria at all","E":"All four carry W. bancrofti; none carries B. malayi"},"r":{"B":"Anopheles carries W. bancrofti, like Culex and Aedes.","D":"Correct, and this single split is the highest-value line in the whole mosquito table — a vector-matching question can only be built on it. Mansonia is also commonest in southern Thailand.","A":"Aedes carries W. bancrofti; B. timori is not taught here.","C":"Culex is listed as a W. bancrofti vector.","E":"Mansonia's species is B. malayi, printed on its own slide."},"t":"Filariasis","sl":1010,"img":"slides_i/L8_1010.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Filariasis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Myiasis — type and cause","s":"A bedridden elderly patient has a foul nasal discharge, and fly larvae are seen crawling from the nostril. Which classification and cause match the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":25,"c":"<b>Myiasis — type and cause</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cutaneous myiasis caused by larvae of non-blood-sucking flies","B":"Cavitary myiasis caused by adult flies feeding on mucosa","C":"Cavitary myiasis caused by larvae of non-blood-sucking flies","D":"Wound myiasis caused by the larvae of blood-sucking flies","E":"Creeping myiasis caused by the larvae of blood-sucking flies"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cutaneous myiasis is furuncular or creeping/migratory in skin, not in the nose.","B":"Adults have sponging mouthparts and take liquid food; the larva is what invades.","C":"Correct. Nasal = cavitary myiasis (with oral, ophthalmic, intestinal, ear, urogenital, rarely brain). Myiasis is caused by the maggot of the non-blood-sucking flies (house, blow, flesh flies).","D":"Wound myiasis is in an open wound, and myiasis is listed under the non-blood-sucking flies.","E":"Creeping myiasis is a subtype of cutaneous myiasis; wrong fly group too."},"img":"slides_i/L8_25.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which stage of the fly causes myiasis, and which classification is NOT used on the slide?","o":{"C":"The larva (maggot); haematogenous myiasis is not a listed type","A":"The adult female; cavitary myiasis is not a listed type","E":"The larva; wound myiasis is not a listed type","B":"The pupa; cutaneous myiasis is not a listed type","D":"The egg; furuncular myiasis is not a listed type"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The three listed types are CUTANEOUS (furuncular or creeping/migratory), WOUND and CAVITARY (nasal, oral, ophthalmic, intestinal, ears, urogenital, rarely brain). Maggots invade from the surface — there is no bloodborne form.","A":"The adult only lays the eggs; cavitary myiasis is listed.","E":"Wound myiasis is one of the three listed types.","B":"The pupa is non-feeding; cutaneous myiasis is listed.","D":"Eggs are washed off by cleaning; furuncular is a listed subtype of cutaneous."},"t":"Myiasis","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L8_26.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Myiasis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Cyclops — answer format","s":"In the practical, a student is shown Cyclops, a microscopic freshwater crustacean, and asked for its medical importance. Which answer is complete and correct?","lec":"L8","sl":19,"c":"<b>Cyclops — answer format</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"First intermediate host of Gnathostoma spinigerum","B":"Vector of Gnathostoma spinigerum","C":"First intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","D":"Second intermediate host of Gnathostoma spinigerum","E":"Gnathostomiasis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and slide 19 spells out 'first'.","B":"Wrong role: a helminth develops in an intermediate host; 'vector' is for transmitted pathogens.","C":"Paragonimus's first intermediate host is a snail; the crab is its second.","D":"Wrong ordinal — Cyclops is the first intermediate host.","E":"A bare disease name scores zero — it must start 'Intermediate host of…'."},"img":"slides_i/L8_19.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which pairing of arthropod and helminth is correct?","o":{"D":"Freshwater crab = intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana","E":"Cyclops = second intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","A":"Cyclops = first intermediate host of Gnathostoma spinigerum","B":"Flea = second intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","C":"Chigger mite = intermediate host of Dipylidium caninum"},"r":{"D":"Hymenolepis uses the FLEA.","E":"Paragonimus uses the freshwater/mountain CRAB as its second intermediate host; the first is a small freshwater snail.","A":"Correct, and printed on slide 19 with 'first' spelt out.","B":"The flea hosts Hymenolepis and Dipylidium, not Paragonimus.","C":"The chigger mite is a vector, not an intermediate host — it hosts nothing."},"t":"Intermediate hosts","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L8_19.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Intermediate hosts</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Egg morphology → adult recognition","s":"Mosquito eggs collected from a flower vase in a house are oval, rugby-ball shaped and have no floats. Which feature should be looked for on the adults that laid them?","lec":"L8","sl":1025,"c":"<b>Egg morphology → adult recognition</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Spotted white-and-black wing margins","B":"Snow-white scales on the thorax","C":"Plain brown body with a blunt abdominal tip","D":"Palpi as long as the proboscis","E":"Wide wings with mixed dark and pale scales"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Anopheles — its eggs are boat-shaped with floats.","B":"Correct. Rugby/oval eggs without floats = Aedes, which breeds in household containers such as vases. The lecturer said go straight to the thorax for the snow-white scales.","C":"Culex — cup-shaped corolla (raft) in dirty water.","D":"Anopheles.","E":"Mansonia — sunflower-shaped egg cluster under aquatic plants."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1025.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A lab image shows mosquito eggs laid singly, each boat-shaped with a float on either side. Which genus?","o":{"E":"Mansonia","D":"Culex","C":"Aedes","B":"Anopheles","A":"Toxorhynchites"},"r":{"E":"Mansonia lays a sunflower-shaped cluster with pointed tips that pierce aquatic plant roots.","D":"Culex lays a raft, described on the slide as a cup-shaped corolla, like a ring of flower petals.","C":"Aedes also lays singly, but the eggs are rugby-ball / oval shaped with a patterned shell and NO floats. They also survive drought for up to a year.","B":"Correct. Boat-shaped with lateral floats, laid singly — the floats are the discriminator.","A":"Toxorhynchites is the large non-biting mosquito mentioned only in passing."},"t":"Mosquito eggs","sl":1025,"img":"slides_i/L8_1025.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Mosquito eggs</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Demodex — recognition and habitat","s":"A 22-year-old has inflammatory acne that is not settling on anti-inflammatory treatment alone. A skin surface sample shows 0.3–0.4 mm cylindrical arthropods with four pairs of stubby legs. Where does this organism live?","lec":"L8","sl":62,"c":"<b>Demodex — recognition and habitat</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"On the shafts of scalp hair, with cemented eggs","B":"In the seams of clothing, visiting skin to feed","C":"In burrows in the skin between the fingers","D":"Within the hair follicles of the skin","E":"On grass and scrub, attaching to skin to feed"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The head louse.","B":"The body louse.","C":"Sarcoptes scabiei (scabies).","D":"Correct. Cylindrical body, 0.3–0.4 mm, 4 pairs of bud legs = Demodex (follicle mite), which lives within hair follicles — medical importance includes folliculitis and inflammatory acne. Treating with anti-inflammatories alone leaves the mite in place.","E":"The chigger larva."},"img":"slides_i/L8_62.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which description matches Demodex spp., and what is its medical importance?","o":{"E":"Leathery body without a scutum; causes tick paralysis","B":"Laterally flattened with jumping hind legs; causes flea-allergy dermatitis","D":"Cylindrical body with four pairs of bud-like legs, living in hair follicles; causes folliculitis and inflammatory acne","C":"Three pairs of legs with a dorsal scutum; vector of scrub typhus","A":"Cone-shaped head with an X on the wings; vector of Chagas disease"},"r":{"E":"That is the soft tick.","B":"That is the flea.","D":"Correct. It is the only arthropod in the lecture whose habitat is inside a human hair follicle, and a Thai report attributes roughly 30% of inflammatory acne to it. Treating that acne with anti-inflammatories alone leaves the mite in place.","C":"That is the chigger mite larva.","A":"That is the kissing bug."},"t":"Demodex","sl":62,"img":"slides_i/L8_62.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Demodex</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Rove beetle — first aid","s":"A nurse on night duty feels a small insect with an orange thorax between black ends crawl onto her neck under the ward's fluorescent lights. What should she do?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Rove beetle — first aid</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Flick it off with paper, then apply a warm compress to the skin","B":"Blow it off, wash the skin with soap, then use a cold compress","C":"Slap it at once, wash the skin with soap, then a cold compress","D":"Blow it off, then spray insecticide onto the exposed skin","E":"Brush it off with the palm, wash with soap, then a cold compress"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Removal is fine, but the slide's after-care is washing with soap and a cold, not warm, compress.","B":"Correct. Do not hit or crush it — blow or wipe it away; wash the area with soap immediately and use cold compression later (slide 36).","C":"Crushing releases paederin — that is what causes the dermatitis.","D":"Insecticide on your own skin is marked wrong wherever it appears.","E":"The lecturer's specific warning: the palm resists paederin, but you then scratch elsewhere and spread it — there are case reports."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A rove beetle is crawling on your forearm. What should you do?","o":{"B":"Gently blow it away, or flick it off with a piece of paper","A":"Slap it quickly so it cannot move further","C":"Brush it off firmly with the palm, whose skin is thick enough to resist the toxin","D":"Rinse it off with hot water","E":"Spray insecticide onto the skin"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Crushing releases paederin instantly; blowing or brushing with paper does not.","A":"Slapping is exactly what produces the dermatitis.","C":"The specific warning he gave: the palm resists, but you then scratch elsewhere and spread the toxin over the body. There are case reports.","D":"Wash with soap and water and then apply a cold compress — but only after the beetle is off, and heat is not part of the advice.","E":"Spraying insecticide on your own skin is marked wrong wherever it appears — for chigger prevention the answer is REPELLENT."},"t":"Prevention","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L8_36.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q41","t":"Chigger mite","s":"A man has a skin lesion described as an eschar resembling a cigarette burn. What bit him?","lec":"L8","sl":60,"c":"<b>Chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Lecture Q41 · BM30 SUM1 Q42 — 'eschar cigarette-like'</span>","o":{"B":"Head louse","A":"Bedbug","E":"Soft tick","D":"Scabies mite","C":"Chigger mite"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Head lice cause scalp itching and nits on hair shafts, with no eschar.","A":"Bedbug bites are separate itchy papules, often in a row along exposed skin — there is no necrotic crust.","E":"A soft tick bite can cause relapsing fever and tick paralysis but does not produce this eschar.","D":"Scabies produces burrows and intense night itch, classically between the fingers.","C":"Correct. The eschar is the signature of the chigger-mite bite and of scrub typhus, and the lecturer told the class in so many words that NL asks about this lesion."},"img":"slides_i/L8_60.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q42"]},{"id":"I|L8|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q41+","t":"Chigger mite","s":"A 45-year-old male farmer presents with a week of fever, headache and myalgia. He works in rice fields in a rural area. Examination shows a black crusted wound. What is the most likely pathogen?","lec":"L8","sl":59,"c":"<b>Chigger mite</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Slide 69 (NL2023) · BM32 SUM2 Q41+ · BM31 SUM2 Q34 · BM29</span>","o":{"E":"Leptospira interrogans","A":"Orientia tsutsugamushi","C":"Clostridium perfringens","B":"Sporothrix schenckii","D":"Rickettsia rickettsii"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Leptospirosis is the deliberate near-miss: a febrile rice-field worker with myalgia fits perfectly — until you look at the eschar, which leptospirosis does not produce.","A":"Correct. The eschar is what makes this scrub typhus rather than anything else on the list, and the rice field is where chigger mites live.","C":"Gas gangrene follows a contaminated penetrating wound and is acutely toxic, not a week of fever.","B":"Sporotrichosis follows inoculation from vegetation and gives nodules that ascend along a lymphatic channel.","D":"Rocky Mountain spotted fever is a HARD TICK disease of the Americas, not a Thai mite disease."},"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q34","BM30 slide 69 / NL2023","BM29 Q4"]},{"id":"I|L8|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q34","t":"Rove beetle","s":"A patient has a kissing lesion with burning and itching at the flexure of the right arm after crushing an arthropod. What is the most likely diagnosis?","lec":"L8","sl":35,"c":"<b>Rove beetle</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Lecture Q34 · BM30 SUM1 Q36 · BM29 · BM30 formative</span>","o":{"D":"Local necrotizing ulcer","B":"Classical scabiasis","C":"Pediculosis","A":"Paederus dermatitis","E":"Norwegian scabiasis"},"a":"A","r":{"D":"A necrotizing ulcer is a deeper, progressive lesion, not a symmetrical flexural burn.","B":"Scabies gives burrows, typically in finger webs, with night-time itch and no burning, and no crushing history.","C":"Lice cause itch without a mirror-image burn.","A":"Correct. Three words give it away: KISSING lesion, BURNING, and 'after crushing'. Paederin digests tissue and transfers to the facing skin surface when the joint bends.","E":"Crusted scabies occurs in the immunocompromised with an enormous mite burden — a different picture."},"img":"slides_i/L8_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q36","BM30 Paederus item","BM29 Q2"]},{"id":"I|L8|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q38","t":"Hard tick","s":"A patient was bitten by a small, round, black arthropod that has a scutum and a capitulum visible at the anterior end. What is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":56,"c":"<b>Hard tick</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q38 · BM29 — 'scutum and visible capitulum'</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of leishmaniasis","B":"Vector of filariasis","E":"Vector of hepatitis B","C":"Vector of scrub typhus","D":"Vector of Lyme disease"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Leishmaniasis is carried by the sand fly.","B":"Filariasis is mosquito-borne.","E":"Hepatitis B is bloodborne and has no arthropod vector.","C":"The planted near-miss: scrub typhus is the CHIGGER MITE, which also has a scutum but has 3 pairs of legs and is ten to fifty times smaller.","D":"Correct. The answer key spells out the reasoning: four pairs of legs, a scutum on the dorsal body and a VISIBLE capitulum identify a HARD tick — vector of Lyme disease and Rocky Mountain spotted fever."},"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q-tick"]},{"id":"I|L8|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q32 (57)","t":"Vector matching","s":"Which of the following vectors does NOT match its organism or disease?","lec":"L8","sl":18,"c":"<b>Vector matching</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q32 · cohorts 57 and 58</span>","o":{"A":"Culex spp. — Japanese B encephalitis","D":"Anopheles spp. — Plasmodium spp.","C":"Sand fly — plague","B":"Mansonia spp. — Brugia malayi","E":"Tsetse fly — trypanosomiasis"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Correct pairing.","D":"Correct pairing, printed on the biological-vector slide.","C":"Correct — this is the wrong pairing. The sand fly carries LEISHMANIASIS; plague is carried by the FLEA (Xenopsylla cheopis, the oriental rat flea).","B":"Correct, and the one species split worth memorising — only Mansonia carries B. malayi.","E":"Correct: Glossina carries Trypanosoma brucei."},"img":"slides_i/L8_18.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["LS54 Q32 (58)"]},{"id":"I|L8|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q9","t":"Sand fly","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with a painless lesion on his left arm after being bitten by a small, hairy, mosquito-like insect. Skin biopsy shows oval-shaped organisms with a kinetoplast within macrophages. What is the diagnosis?","lec":"L8","sl":33,"c":"<b>Sand fly</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q9 — painless lesion, kinetoplast in macrophages</span>","o":{"C":"Cyclosporiasis","D":"Malaria","A":"Toxoplasmosis","B":"Leishmaniasis","E":"Lymphatic filariasis"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Cyclospora is a faecal-oral intestinal coccidian.","D":"Malaria is Anopheles-borne and the parasites sit inside red cells, not macrophages.","A":"Toxoplasma is acquired from cat faeces or undercooked meat, not from a bite, and has no kinetoplast.","B":"Correct. 'Small, hairy, mosquito-like' is the slide's description of the SAND FLY word for word, and amastigotes with a kinetoplast inside macrophages are Leishmania.","E":"Filariasis gives lymphatic obstruction, and the microfilariae are in blood, not in macrophages."},"img":"slides_i/L8_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q6","t":"Pubic louse","s":"A 25-year-old woman has itching of her eyelids and pubic area. A small arthropod is isolated from her eyelash and pubic hair. Which is the most effective prevention for this patient?","lec":"L8","sl":20,"c":"<b>Pubic louse</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q6 · BM29 เฉลย · BM31 SUM1 — eyelash and pubic hair</span>","o":{"B":"Treating the patient and her family","C":"Using a condom","E":"Avoiding head-to-head contact","A":"Avoiding sexual activity with the infested person","D":"Avoiding prolonged antibiotic use"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Sensible for treating an existing infestation, but the question asks about prevention of transmission.","C":"The trap, and the BM29 key says so explicitly: the louse lives on the HAIR AROUND the genitals, not on the organ, so a condom does not prevent transmission.","E":"That is the HEAD louse measure — the wrong louse for this site.","A":"Correct. Pthirus pubis spreads by close body contact during sex, so the effective measure is avoiding contact with the infested partner.","D":"Antibiotics have no role in a louse infestation."},"img":"slides_i/L8_20.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q6","BM29 Q6"]},{"id":"I|L8|25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q5 (59)","t":"Aedes breeding","s":"Where does the mosquito that transmits dengue lay its eggs?","lec":"L8","sl":1019,"c":"<b>Aedes breeding</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q5 · cohort 59</span>","o":{"C":"Clean water stored in household containers","E":"Dirty drainage water","A":"Water troughs used by household cattle","D":"Standing water in the forest","B":"Under the leaves of aquatic plants"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Aedes is domestic: water jars, uncovered tanks, plant-pot saucers, cupboard-leg dishes, vases, tyres, tins and coconut shells. The answer key prints a four-line summary of all four genera.","E":"That is Culex, which prefers dirty, low-oxygen water.","A":"Also Culex territory — water contaminated with animal dung.","D":"That is Anopheles: rock pools, animal footprints and slow-moving forest streams.","B":"That is Mansonia, whose larvae attach to the roots of aquatic plants."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1019.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (59)","t":"Head louse","s":"A mother finds nits on her daughter's hair. What is the organism?","lec":"L8","sl":20,"c":"<b>Head louse</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q6 · cohort 59</span>","o":{"D":"Body louse","C":"Pubic louse","E":"Head louse","A":"Scabies mite","B":"Demodex"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"The body louse lives in CLOTHING SEAMS, not on hair — and it is the only louse that is a disease vector.","C":"The pubic louse is found on pubic hair, eyelashes, eyebrows and axillae.","E":"Correct. Nits are louse eggs cemented to hair shafts, and the scalp is the head louse's site. SITE is the discriminator in every louse question.","A":"Scabies produces burrows in skin folds, not eggs on hair.","B":"Demodex lives inside hair follicles and is invisible to the naked eye."},"img":"slides_i/L8_20.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|27","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q26","t":"Scabies","s":"A child has intense itching and burrow tracks between the fingers; siblings and classmates are affected too. Skin biopsy shows a mite with four pairs of legs. What is it?","lec":"L8","sl":20,"c":"<b>Scabies</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q26 · BM28 Q47 · หลักสูตร 54 Q11 (59)</span>","o":{"B":"Pediculus humanus capitis","D":"Pthirus pubis","C":"Sarcoptes scabiei","E":"Pediculus humanus corporis","A":"Demodex spp."},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Head lice live on the scalp.","D":"The pubic louse is found on pubic hair and eyelashes and makes no burrows.","C":"Correct. Burrows in skin folds, night-time itch and spread among close contacts is classical scabies.","E":"Body lice live in clothing seams.","A":"Demodex is a follicle mite that causes folliculitis and acne, not burrows between the fingers."},"img":"slides_i/L8_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["BM28 Q47","LS54 Q11 (59)"]},{"id":"I|L8|28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q27","t":"Hymenoptera","s":"Which of the following is the most severe allergic reaction to a Hymenoptera sting?","lec":"L8","sl":44,"c":"<b>Hymenoptera</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Lecture Q27</span>","o":{"A":"Anaphylaxis","C":"Swelling","B":"Rash","D":"Headache","E":"Itch"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the single medical importance the lecturer said to remember for bees, wasps and ants. Treatment is epinephrine plus antihistamine and systemic steroid.","C":"Part of the LOCAL reaction, along with redness, itching and pain.","B":"A local or cutaneous manifestation, not the most severe.","D":"Not a listed sting reaction at all.","E":"Part of the local reaction."},"img":"slides_i/L8_44.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"slide 67 / NL","t":"Bee sting","s":"A farmer presents with a red papule, swelling and intense pain on the right arm after being stung by an arthropod. A stinger was found in the middle of the swollen area. What is the most likely causative arthropod?","lec":"L8","sl":67,"c":"<b>Bee sting</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Slide 67 · NL worked question with the key shown</span>","o":{"E":"Centipede","D":"Wasp","C":"Spider","A":"Hornet","B":"Bee"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Centipedes bite with modified forelegs and leave no sting.","D":"A wasp's sting is smooth, stays attached and can be used repeatedly — it leaves nothing behind.","C":"Spiders bite with fangs; they have no sting.","A":"Hornets, like wasps, retain their sting.","B":"Correct, and the word that decides it is STINGER. The honeybee's sting is barbed; it tears away with part of the abdomen and stays in the skin — after which the bee dies. That is the reasoning the lecturer gave when the class objected."},"img":"slides_i/L8_67.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q13 (59)","t":"Rove beetle","s":"Which toxin does the rove beetle secrete?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Rove beetle</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q13 · cohort 59</span>","o":{"A":"Apitoxin","D":"Cantharidin","B":"Melittin","E":"Paederin","C":"Tetrodotoxin"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Apitoxin is bee venom.","D":"Cantharidin is the BLISTER beetle's toxin — the classic swap, and the reason both beetles are taught together.","B":"Melittin is a component of bee venom.","E":"Correct. Paederin is carried in the beetle's abdomen and is released when the insect is crushed.","C":"Tetrodotoxin belongs to pufferfish and some other animals, not to any arthropod here."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (60)","t":"Cockroach","s":"Which of the following best illustrates the role of a mechanical transmitter?","lec":"L8","sl":17,"c":"<b>Cockroach</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q6 · cohort 60</span>","o":{"A":"A sand fly in which Leishmania develops into promastigotes","E":"An Anopheles mosquito in which Plasmodium completes sexual reproduction","C":"A chigger mite in which Orientia multiplies before being inoculated","B":"A flea in which Yersinia pestis multiplies and blocks the proventriculus","D":"A cockroach walking through faeces and then across food, carrying organisms on its legs"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Stage development inside the fly is biological transmission.","E":"Development and multiplication inside the vector makes this biological.","C":"Multiplication inside the mite is biological transmission.","B":"Multiplication inside the flea is biological transmission.","D":"Correct. Nothing about the organism changes inside the cockroach — it is simply moved from one surface to another. Note that the cockroach can ALSO act biologically for some protozoa, which is why the house fly is the safest 'purely mechanical' example."},"img":"slides_i/L8_17.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L8|32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q8","t":"Leishmania — mode of infection","s":"A 40-year-old man has a painless ulcer on his left arm with a raised border and a central crater. Skin biopsy shows numerous ovoid organisms with a KINETOPLAST inside macrophages. What is the mode of infection?","lec":"L8","sl":33,"c":"<b>Leishmania — mode of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q8 — painless ulcer with a raised border and central crater; kinetoplast-bearing organisms inside macrophages. Asks the MODE, where BM30 Q9 asked the diagnosis.</span>","o":{"E":"Ingestion of a sporulated oocyst","D":"Injection of sporozoites during a mosquito's blood meal","C":"Injection of third-stage larvae through the bite wound during a mosquito's blood meal","A":"Ingestion of a mature cyst","B":"Injection of promastigotes through the skin when an infected female sand fly takes a blood meal"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Sporulated oocysts are swallowed — Cryptosporidium, Toxoplasma.","D":"Sporozoites injected by a mosquito are malaria.","C":"Third-stage larvae injected by a mosquito are lymphatic filariasis.","A":"Mature cysts are swallowed — Entamoeba and Giardia.","B":"Correct. The amastigote (ovoid, with a kinetoplast, inside macrophages) is the stage you SEE; the PROMASTIGOTE is the stage that is INJECTED by the female Phlebotomus sand fly. The exam repeatedly makes you flip between the diagnostic stage and the infective stage of the same organism."},"img":"slides_i/L8_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L8|33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q7 (60)","t":"Sand fly","s":"You visit a country where a small mosquito-like insect is common: it is very HAIRY on body and wings, and holds its wings up in a V at rest. Which disease is this country likely to carry?","lec":"L8","sl":33,"c":"<b>Sand fly</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 60 Q7 — a mosquito-like insect, very hairy, wings held up at an angle at rest; what disease risk does the country carry?</span>","o":{"A":"Scrub typhus","B":"Malaria","D":"Dengue","E":"Leishmaniasis","C":"Lymphatic filariasis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Scrub typhus is transmitted by the chigger MITE, an arachnid larva, not a fly.","B":"Malaria's vector is Anopheles, which rests with its body at 45° to the surface but is not described as hairy.","D":"Dengue's vector is Aedes, recognised by black-and-white banded legs.","E":"Correct. Hairy body and wings, 1.5–2.5 mm, V-shaped wings held erect at rest = sand fly, Phlebotomus spp., the vector of leishmaniasis. Only the female takes blood, and it is a POOL feeder with short mouthparts.","C":"Filariasis is carried by Culex, Mansonia, Aedes and Anopheles."},"img":"slides_i/L8_33.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q-flea","t":"Flea","s":"A flea is shown. What is its medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":51,"c":"<b>Flea</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative · parasitology — flea shown; state the medical importance. The examiner's feedback listed all three roles.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of scrub typhus","D":"Vector of Lyme disease","B":"Causative agent of Paederus dermatitis","C":"Intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana and H. diminuta","E":"Vector of lymphatic filariasis"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Scrub typhus is the chigger mite.","D":"Lyme disease is the HARD TICK.","B":"Paederus dermatitis is the rove beetle (แมลงก้นกระดก).","C":"Correct, and the lecture gives the flea THREE roles: intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana, H. diminuta and Dipylidium caninum; VECTOR of plague and murine typhus (Xenopsylla cheopis) and cat-scratch disease (Ctenocephalides felis); and a cause of flea-allergy dermatitis. Any of the three earns the mark — but phrase it as a role, not a bare disease name.","E":"Filariasis is mosquitoes."},"img":"slides_i/L8_51.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q4 (57)","t":"Chigger — prevention","s":"Soldiers training in scrub grassland are developing scrub typhus. What is the most appropriate prevention?","lec":"L8","sl":60,"c":"<b>Chigger — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q4 — a soldier catches scrub typhus; how is it prevented?</span>","o":{"B":"Eliminating standing water around the camp","D":"Sleeping under an insecticide-treated bed net","E":"Taking weekly doxycycline for life","C":"Wearing boots and long trousers treated with repellent, and clearing scrub vegetation","A":"Avoiding sexual contact with infected persons"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Removing standing water targets mosquito breeding, not mites.","D":"Bed nets work for night-biting MOSQUITOES. Chiggers are picked up from vegetation by day.","E":"Doxycycline is the TREATMENT, and chemoprophylaxis is not the lecture's prevention answer.","C":"Correct. The chigger is the six-legged LARVAL mite; it waits on grass tips and attaches to skin at points where clothing is tight. Barrier clothing plus repellent plus habitat clearance is the answer, which is why the option mentions boots.","A":"Scrub typhus is not sexually transmitted. Recall the lesion: an eschar like a cigarette burn."},"img":"slides_i/L8_60.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (57)","t":"Aedes — the three arboviruses","s":"Which mosquito transmits Chikungunya virus?","lec":"L8","sl":1018,"c":"<b>Aedes — the three arboviruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohort 57 Q6 — the Chikungunya vector</span>","o":{"E":"Aedes spp.","C":"Culex spp.","D":"Anopheles spp.","A":"Mansonia spp.","B":"Phlebotomus spp."},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Learn Aedes as a set of FOUR: dengue, chikungunya, Zika and lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti). It breeds in clean stored household water and lays boat-shaped eggs with lateral floats.","C":"Culex carries Japanese B encephalitis and filariasis, and breeds in dirty water.","D":"Anopheles carries malaria and breeds in slow-moving clean stream water.","A":"Mansonia carries Brugia malayi and lays eggs in a sunflower-shaped cluster under water plants.","B":"Phlebotomus is the sand fly — leishmaniasis, not an arbovirus."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1018.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L8|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Class from features → intermediate host","s":"A man ate a raw, pickled arthropod from a mountain stream. It has two pairs of antennae, five pairs of legs and a body in two parts. Weeks later he has a chronic cough with blood-stained sputum. Which answer gives the arthropod's class and medical importance?","lec":"L8","sl":12,"c":"<b>Class from features → intermediate host</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Arachnida — second intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","B":"Crustacea — first intermediate host of Paragonimus spp.","C":"Insecta — intermediate host of Hymenolepis nana","D":"Crustacea — vector of Paragonimus spp.","E":"Crustacea — second intermediate host of Paragonimus spp."},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Arachnids have no antennae and four pairs of legs.","B":"The first intermediate host of Paragonimus is a snail.","C":"Insects have one pair of antennae and three pairs of legs; the flea is the Hymenolepis host.","D":"The crab is an intermediate host, not a vector.","E":"Correct. Two pairs of antennae + five pairs of legs = Crustacea (slide 12) — here the mountain crab, second intermediate host of Paragonimus (lung fluke → haemoptysis) (slide 19)."},"img":"slides_i/L8_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An arthropod removed from a patient has NO antennae, NO wings, FOUR pairs of legs as an adult, and a body in two parts (cephalothorax and abdomen). Which class is it, and what follows?","o":{"E":"Arachnida — which is why its larva has only 3 pairs of legs, as in the chigger mite","C":"Insecta — 4 pairs of legs is the adult insect count","A":"Crustacea — 2 pairs of antennae would have been lost during removal","D":"Chilopoda — one pair of legs per body segment","B":"Diplopoda — two pairs of legs per body segment"},"r":{"E":"Correct on both. The adult 4 / larva 3 leg-pair rule is exactly what makes the chigger LARVA the parasitic stage you must recognise.","C":"Insects have ONE pair of antennae, THREE pairs of legs, two pairs of wings and a three-part body.","A":"Crustacea have TWO pairs of antennae and five pairs of legs — and antennae are not something that simply falls off in the description.","D":"Chilopoda (centipedes) have one pair of antennae and many segments each bearing one pair of legs.","B":"Diplopoda (millipedes) have one pair of antennae and two pairs of legs per segment."},"t":"Class by antennae, legs, wings","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L8_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Class by antennae, legs, wings</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Fire ant sting — the expected lesion","s":"A gardener kneels on a soil mound and is stung many times by swarming small insects of the family Formicidae. What lesion does the lecture describe for this exposure over the next day?","lec":"L8","sl":41,"c":"<b>Fire ant sting — the expected lesion</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Large fluid-filled blisters after the insect is crushed","B":"An expanding bullseye rash around the bite site","C":"A black crusted eschar resembling a cigarette burn","D":"Red swellings topped by blisters that become sterile pustules","E":"Paired mirror-image burns on facing skin at a flexure"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Blister beetle — cantharidin.","B":"Bullseye rash — Lyme disease, hard tick.","C":"Eschar — chigger mite bite, scrub typhus.","D":"Correct. Formicidae = ants → fire ant: swollen red spots that develop a blister on top, sterile pustule within 24 h; anaphylaxis is rare (slide 47).","E":"Kissing lesion — rove beetle (paederin)."},"img":"slides_i/L8_41.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about mechanical transmission is correct?","o":{"D":"The pathogen neither multiplies nor develops inside the arthropod; the lecture's examples are amoebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica), poliomyelitis and Shigella gastroenteritis carried by houseflies","B":"The pathogen multiplies inside the arthropod but does not change form; the example is plague in the oriental rat flea","C":"Mechanical transmission requires the arthropod to bite, which is why only blood-sucking flies qualify","E":"Mechanical transmission is the route for malaria, since Plasmodium is only carried and not changed by the mosquito","A":"Mechanical vectors are also intermediate hosts, because the pathogen depends on them"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The defining feature is that nothing happens to the pathogen inside the fly — it is carried from faeces to food and dropped there.","B":"Plague in the oriental rat flea is BIOLOGICAL transmission; Yersinia multiplies in the flea.","C":"Mechanical vectors are typically NON-biting flies. Biting is not required — contaminated feet and body parts are enough.","E":"Plasmodium undergoes its entire sexual cycle in the mosquito, which is the definition of biological transmission.","A":"An intermediate host is one the parasite REQUIRES for development. A mechanical vector is dispensable — that is the whole distinction."},"t":"Mechanical vector — the named diseases","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L8_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mechanical vector — the named diseases</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|39","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"40","t":"Paederin vs cantharidin","s":"Patient 1 crushed a small orange-and-black beetle on his neck under a fluorescent lamp and next morning has a burning linear streak with pustules. Patient 2, a child, swallowed a beetle picked from sesbania leaves and now has vomiting, haematemesis and haematuria. Which toxins are responsible?","lec":"L8","sl":34,"c":"<b>Paederin vs cantharidin</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cantharidin in both, by different routes","B":"Cantharidin in the first, paederin in the second","C":"Paederin in the first, cantharidin in the second","D":"Paederin in the first, hymenopteran venom in the second","E":"Paederin in both, at different doses"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cantharidin does act by skin, eye and ingestion routes, but patient 1's beetle is the rove beetle.","B":"The two toxins swapped.","C":"Correct. Rove beetle (Paederus) → paederin → linear/kissing lesions and pustules. Blister beetle (Meloidae, found on sesbania, pigweed, legumes, tomato) → cantharidin → systemic toxicity on ingestion: haematemesis, haematuria, renal failure, shock, death.","D":"Hymenopteran venom comes from a sting (bees, wasps, ants), not from a swallowed beetle.","E":"Two different toxins are named for the two beetles."},"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A farmer brushed an insect off his arm and later developed blistering. Which statement correctly separates the two beetles in this lecture?","o":{"C":"The rove beetle (Paederus spp.) releases PAEDERIN and typically gives linear and 'kissing' lesions; the blister beetle (Meloidae) releases CANTHARIDIN, which can also cause keratitis and corneal ulceration if it reaches the eye and systemic toxicity with renal failure if swallowed","A":"The rove beetle releases cantharidin and the blister beetle releases paederin","E":"Both release paederin; only the dose differs","B":"Neither beetle bites or stings, so neither can cause dermatitis","D":"The rove beetle's toxin causes systemic renal failure and the blister beetle's is confined to the skin"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The two toxins have different names and different reach — cantharidin is the one with eye and systemic routes listed.","A":"The toxin names are swapped. Paederus → paederin; Meloidae → cantharidin.","E":"Two distinct toxins are named in the deck, and their effect profiles differ.","B":"Neither beetle bites or stings — but both release toxin on contact, which is exactly why the advice is to blow or brush them off rather than crush them.","D":"Inverted: it is CANTHARIDIN that causes haematemesis, haematuria, renal failure and shock when ingested."},"t":"Paederin vs cantharidin","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L8_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Paederin vs cantharidin</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|40","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"41","t":"Scrub typhus → vector stage and habitat","s":"A patient in northern Thailand has fever, chills and lymphadenopathy, and scrub typhus is confirmed. The district team wants to find the arthropod responsible. Which description of the transmitting stage and where it waits for a host matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":59,"c":"<b>Scrub typhus → vector stage and habitat</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Adult trombiculid mites living in mattresses and bedding","B":"Oriental rat fleas living on rodents in grain stores","C":"Larval trombiculid mites on grass, scrub and haystacks","D":"Female Anopheles breeding in clear forest streams","E":"Hard ticks attached to dogs and cattle around the house"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The adult (and nymph) trombiculid mite is free-living and does not feed on humans.","B":"Xenopsylla cheopis carries plague and murine typhus — a rickettsial fever, but not scrub typhus.","C":"Correct. Disease → chigger mite → only the larva feeds on hosts. Slide 59 lists its habitat: shrubs, scrub, rice fields, haystacks, forests, plantations and gardens — which is why the past-paper patients are farmers and soldiers.","D":"Anopheles is the vector of malaria and W. bancrofti.","E":"Hard ticks carry Lyme disease, RMSF and others — not Orientia tsutsugamushi."},"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A rice farmer has fever, headache, myalgia, enlarged lymph nodes and an eschar. Which statement about the responsible arthropod is correct, and how must the medical importance be written?","o":{"C":"Only the LARVAL stage of the trombiculid mite is parasitic (3 pairs of legs, dorsal scutum, 0.1–0.3 mm); the answer must be written as 'Vector of scrub typhus / vector of Orientia tsutsugamushi', not as the bare disease name","D":"The adult mite is the parasitic stage, and writing 'scrub typhus' alone is a complete answer","B":"Both nymph and adult feed on humans, and the larva is free-living","A":"The mite is an insect, so the larva has 4 pairs of legs and the adult 3","E":"The eschar is produced by the toxin of the mite, not by an infectious agent, so no pathogen need be named"},"r":{"C":"Correct on both. The deck devotes a whole slide to marking ‘ตอบลอย ๆ scrub typhus ✗’ — a bare disease name scores nothing.","D":"Adults and nymphs are free-living; and the answer-format slide explicitly rejects the bare disease name.","B":"Exactly inverted — the larva is the only parasitic stage.","A":"Mites are ARACHNIDS, not insects: adults have 4 pairs of legs and the larva 3.","E":"The eschar marks the bite site where Orientia tsutsugamushi was inoculated; naming the pathogen is precisely what the answer format requires."},"t":"Chigger mite — which stage bites","sl":59,"img":"slides_i/L8_59.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Chigger mite — which stage bites</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|41","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"42","t":"Babesiosis → hard tick recognition","s":"A dog owner who has never been to a malaria area develops a malaria-like febrile illness two weeks after pulling a tick off his leg; the blood film shows intra-erythrocytic protozoa. Which feature identifies the tick family that the lecture lists as the vector of this infection?","lec":"L8","sl":56,"c":"<b>Babesiosis → hard tick recognition</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A dorsal scutum, with the capitulum visible from above","B":"A wingless body flattened side to side, with jumping legs","C":"No scutum, with the capitulum hidden beneath the body","D":"A cylindrical body with four pairs of bud-like legs","E":"Three pairs of legs, a scutum and a length of 0.1–0.3 mm"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Tick-borne, malaria-like protozoan = babesiosis, listed only under the hard tick (Ixodidae), recognised by its scutum and a capitulum visible from above.","B":"The flea — plague, murine typhus, cat scratch disease.","C":"The soft tick — listed only for tick-borne relapsing fever and tick bite paralysis.","D":"Demodex — folliculitis and inflammatory acne, no vector role.","E":"The chigger larva — vector of scrub typhus, not a protozoan disease."},"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about ticks matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Hard ticks (Ixodidae) have a scutum and transmit Lyme disease, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, tick-borne relapsing fever, tick-borne meningoencephalitis, SFTS virus and babesiosis; soft ticks (Argasidae) lack a scutum and are listed for tick-borne relapsing fever — and BOTH can cause tick bite paralysis by toxin","B":"Soft ticks have the scutum and carry all the listed diseases; hard ticks carry none","E":"Only hard ticks can cause tick bite paralysis, because only they attach for long periods","D":"Babesiosis is a viral disease of hard ticks","C":"Ticks are insects and their larvae have 4 pairs of legs"},"r":{"A":"Correct, including the detail that BOTH families are credited with tick bite paralysis, which is toxin-mediated rather than infectious.","B":"The scutum is the HARD tick’s defining plate — that is what ‘hard’ refers to.","E":"The deck lists tick bite paralysis under both hard and soft ticks.","D":"Babesia is a PROTOZOAN, listed under ‘vector of protozoan diseases’ and described as malaria-like.","C":"Ticks are arachnids; the larva has 3 pairs of legs and the nymph and adult 4."},"t":"Hard vs soft tick","sl":56,"img":"slides_i/L8_56.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Hard vs soft tick</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|42","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"43","t":"Stinger origin → who stings","s":"An entomologist remarks that among bees, wasps and ants, only females can sting. Which explanation matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":41,"c":"<b>Stinger origin → who stings</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Males have a stinger but lack the venom sac that supplies it","B":"The stinger is a modified hind leg used by females in defence","C":"The stinger is a modified mouthpart that males have lost","D":"The stinger is a modified ovipositor joined to a venom sac","E":"Only females make venom, which they need for egg production"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Males have no stinger at all, because they have no ovipositor.","B":"Modified hind legs are the flea's jumping adaptation.","C":"The stinger is not a mouthpart.","D":"Correct. Slide 42: the venom sac combines with the stinger, which is developed from the ovipositor — an egg-laying organ, so only females have one.","E":"Invented — venom production is not tied to egg production in the lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L8_41.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about Hymenoptera stings matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"The stinger is a modified OVIPOSITOR combined with a venom sac, so only females sting; a 'large local reaction' means swelling over 10 cm that enlarges for 24–48 hours and is dangerous on the head, neck, tongue or throat","E":"The stinger is a modified mouthpart, so both sexes sting; a large local reaction is any swelling over 2 cm","C":"Any swelling after a sting is by definition a systemic reaction requiring adrenaline","B":"Large local reactions resolve within 6 hours; anything longer is anaphylaxis","A":"Only wasps sting; bees bite and inject venom through the mandibles"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the ovipositor origin is the reason only females sting — a point worth carrying because it explains the anatomy rather than just naming it.","E":"The ovipositor is an egg-laying structure, not a mouthpart, and 2 cm is not the threshold.","C":"Local and systemic reactions are explicitly separated, and only the systemic one calls for adrenaline.","B":"A large local reaction ENLARGES over 24–48 hours — duration alone does not make it anaphylaxis.","A":"Both bees and wasps sting; the slide covers them together."},"t":"Sting apparatus and the reaction","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L8_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Sting apparatus and the reaction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|43","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"44","t":"Large local reaction — classify and treat","s":"A man stung on the forearm by a wasp has redness and swelling that is 12 cm across and still enlarging 36 hours later. He has no facial swelling, wheeze or hypotension. How should this be classified and treated?","lec":"L8","sl":43,"c":"<b>Large local reaction — classify and treat</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Systemic toxic reaction — epinephrine and renal monitoring","B":"Local reaction — reassurance, as it settles within hours","C":"Large local reaction — epinephrine, antihistamine, steroid","D":"Large local reaction — cold compress, antihistamine, steroid","E":"Anaphylaxis — epinephrine, antihistamine, systemic steroid"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Systemic toxic reaction (renal failure, rhabdomyolysis, DIC) needs systemic findings.","B":"A simple local reaction does not exceed 10 cm or keep growing for 24–48 h.","C":"Right category, wrong treatment — epinephrine is the anaphylaxis regimen.","D":"Correct. Growing over 24–48 h and >10 cm = large local reaction (slide 43). Local reactions are treated with cold compression, antihistamine and steroid; epinephrine is reserved for anaphylaxis (slide 48).","E":"No systemic features — this is not anaphylaxis."},"img":"slides_i/L8_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which management statement matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"Remove the sting, use cold compression with an antihistamine and a steroid for a local reaction, and give epinephrine with an antihistamine and systemic steroid for anaphylaxis; a fire ant sting characteristically forms a STERILE pustule within 24 hours","A":"A fire ant pustule is infected and requires antibiotics within 24 hours","B":"Epinephrine is the first step for every sting, regardless of reaction type","C":"The sting should be left in place, since removing it squeezes more venom in","E":"Cold compression is used for anaphylaxis and epinephrine for local reactions"},"r":{"D":"Correct across all three limbs, and the word STERILE matters — the fire ant pustule is a venom effect, not an infection.","A":"Calling it infected leads to unnecessary antibiotics; the deck specifies a sterile pustule.","B":"Epinephrine is reserved for the systemic reaction; local reactions get cold compression, antihistamine and steroid.","C":"The first listed step is to REMOVE the sting.","E":"Inverted — the two treatments are matched to the wrong severities."},"t":"Sting management","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L8_47.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Sting management</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|44","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"45","t":"Plague → flea → control","s":"A rat die-off in a port warehouse is followed by a cluster of plague cases among the workers. Which control measure from the lecture targets the vector responsible?","lec":"L8","sl":51,"c":"<b>Plague → flea → control</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Remove pond weeds, the breeding site of Mansonia","B":"Remove standing water, the breeding site of Aedes","C":"Clear scrub, the habitat of larval trombiculid mites","D":"Bathe and treat pet cats, the hosts of the cat flea","E":"Control rodents, hosts of the oriental rat flea"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Mansonia control — Brugian filariasis.","B":"Aedes control — dengue, not plague.","C":"Chigger mite habitat — scrub typhus.","D":"Treating and bathing pets reduces cat/dog fleas — C. felis carries cat scratch disease, not plague.","E":"Correct. Plague → Xenopsylla cheopis (oriental rat flea) → control rodents (slide 52). Personal protection would be repellent on skin/clothing and covered clothing."},"img":"slides_i/L8_51.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about fleas matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"Xenopsylla cheopis, the oriental rat flea, transmits plague and murine typhus; Ctenocephalides felis, the cat flea, transmits cat scratch disease; and fleas also serve as INTERMEDIATE HOSTS for Dipylidium caninum, Hymenolepis nana and H. diminuta","C":"Ctenocephalides felis transmits plague and Xenopsylla cheopis transmits cat scratch disease","B":"Fleas are vectors only; no helminth uses them as an intermediate host","A":"Fleas have two pairs of wings, which is how they move between hosts","D":"Pulex irritans is the oriental rat flea and the vector of plague"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and note the flea appears in TWO of the three categories of medical importance — vector and intermediate host — plus flea-allergy dermatitis.","C":"The two species are swapped.","B":"Fleas are named intermediate hosts for three cestodes on the same slide.","A":"Fleas have NO wings; they have modified hind legs for jumping.","D":"Pulex irritans is the HUMAN flea; Xenopsylla is the oriental rat flea."},"t":"Flea — two species, two roles","sl":49,"img":"slides_i/L8_49.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Flea — two species, two roles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|45","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"46","t":"Myiasis prevention","s":"A diabetic inpatient has a chronic heel ulcer with purulent, foul-smelling discharge, and flies are often seen around the bed. Which measure from the lecture most directly prevents the complication the team fears?","lec":"L8","sl":28,"c":"<b>Myiasis prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Replace the ward's white fluorescent lights with yellow","B":"Leave the ulcer uncovered so that its surface can dry out","C":"Apply insect repellent to the skin around the ulcer","D":"Change the dressing daily so odour and discharge stay low","E":"Spray insecticide directly onto the ulcer every morning"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Yellow bulbs are the rove-beetle measure.","B":"Uncovered wounds are exactly what flies lay eggs on — the slide says cover them.","C":"Repellent is listed for chigger and flea bites, not myiasis.","D":"Correct. The fear is wound myiasis. Slide 28: clean, disinfect and cover wounds with sterile dressings, changed daily to prevent the odour and discharge that attract flies.","E":"Insecticide is a fly-population control measure, not something applied to a wound."},"img":"slides_i/L8_28.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which classification of myiasis matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"Cutaneous myiasis (furuncular or creeping/migratory), wound myiasis in open wounds, and cavitary myiasis of the nose, mouth, eye, intestine, ears, urogenital tract and rarely the brain — all caused by fly LARVAE (maggots)","C":"Myiasis is caused by adult flies feeding on tissue, and is classified as biting or non-biting","B":"Myiasis has only two forms, cutaneous and intestinal, and is caused by blood-sucking flies","A":"Creeping myiasis is caused by hookworm larvae in the skin","E":"Cavitary myiasis refers to maggots in a body cavity of a corpse only and has no clinical relevance"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the mechanism is what to hold: it is the LARVA, not the adult fly, that invades tissue.","C":"Adult flies have sponging mouthparts and absorb liquid food; they do not invade tissue.","B":"The deck gives three forms, and the responsible flies are the non-blood-sucking house, blow and flesh flies.","A":"Creeping eruption from larvae in skin is animal HOOKWORM (L1); creeping myiasis is a migratory maggot.","E":"Cavitary myiasis is listed with named living-patient sites including nose, mouth, eye, ears, intestine, urogenital tract and rarely brain."},"t":"Myiasis — the three types","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L8_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Myiasis — the three types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|46","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"47","t":"Scutellum → subfamily → partner feature","s":"Viewed from above, a mosquito's scutellum is a single smooth curve rather than three lobes. Which other feature should the same specimen show?","lec":"L8","sl":1005,"c":"<b>Scutellum → subfamily → partner feature</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Snow-white scales on the thorax","B":"A pointed tip to the abdomen","C":"A body resting parallel to the surface","D":"Wide wings with mixed dark and pale scales","E":"Palpi about as long as the proboscis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Aedes — a culicine with a trilobed scutellum.","B":"Aedes (Culex has a blunt tip) — trilobed scutellum.","C":"Culicine posture ('body in two axes'); Anopheles rests at 45° in one axis.","D":"Mansonia — a culicine with a trilobed scutellum.","E":"Correct. Round scutellum = Anophelinae (Anopheles), which also has palpi as long as the proboscis and rests at 45°."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1005.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A mosquito has a ROUND scutellum, palpi as long as the proboscis, spotted wings, and rests at about 45° to the surface. Which genus, and what does it transmit?","o":{"E":"Anopheles — vector of malaria and of lymphatic filariasis due to Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Culex — vector of Japanese encephalitis and Wuchereria bancrofti","B":"Aedes — vector of dengue, chikungunya and Zika","A":"Mansonia — vector of Brugia malayi","C":"Toxorhynchites — a non-biting mosquito that transmits nothing"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Round scutellum and long palpi both point to Anophelinae, and the 45° resting posture and spotted wings confirm it.","D":"Culex has a TRILOBED scutellum and SHORT palpi, and rests parallel to the surface.","B":"Aedes also has a trilobed scutellum and short palpi, plus snow-white scales and a pointed abdominal tip.","A":"Mansonia has a trilobed scutellum and broad, mixed dark-and-pale wing scales.","C":"Toxorhynchites is listed as a third subfamily but has none of these features and is not a medical vector."},"t":"Scutellum plus palpi","sl":1006,"img":"slides_i/L8_1006.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Scutellum plus palpi</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|47","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"48","t":"Antennae → sex → epidemiological meaning","s":"During a dengue outbreak survey, a mosquito from an indoor trap has bushy, feathery antennae. What does this tell the investigator?","lec":"L8","sl":1004,"c":"<b>Antennae → sex → epidemiological meaning</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is a male that bites only at dusk","B":"It is a female that has not yet laid eggs","C":"It is a female and has already fed on blood","D":"It is an anopheline, whatever its sex","E":"It is a male and does not take blood meals"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Males feed on nectar and do not bite at all.","B":"Female antennae are pilose, whatever her reproductive state.","C":"Females have pilose (sparsely haired) antennae.","D":"Antennal type marks sex, not genus — every genus has both.","E":"Correct. Plumose (feathery) antennae = male; pilose = female. Only the female sucks blood; the male feeds on plant nectar, so this specimen is not transmitting anything."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1004.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A mosquito caught indoors has bushy, feathery (plumose) antennae. What follows?","o":{"E":"It is a MALE, and males feed on plant nectar rather than blood, so it cannot be the one transmitting disease","C":"It is a FEMALE, and plumose antennae help it locate hosts, so it is the disease-transmitting sex","B":"It is a male, but males also take blood meals, so it can still transmit disease","D":"Antennal type varies with genus, not sex, and plumose antennae identify Anopheles","A":"It is a female of the genus Toxorhynchites, the only genus with plumose antennae"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Plumose = male, pilose = female, and only the female takes a blood meal — so the antenna tells you at a glance whether the specimen matters epidemiologically.","C":"Pilose (sparsely haired) antennae mark the female.","B":"Males feed on nectar; the deck states this explicitly.","D":"Antennal type marks SEX, not genus — every genus has both.","A":"Plumose antennae occur in the males of all mosquito genera."},"t":"Antennae tell you the sex","sl":1004,"img":"slides_i/L8_1004.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Antennae tell you the sex</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|48","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"49","t":"Dirty-water larvae → adult features","s":"Larvae collected from a roadside ditch of dirty, low-oxygen water contaminated with animal dung are reared to adults. Which set of features will the adults most likely show?","lec":"L8","sl":1013,"c":"<b>Dirty-water larvae → adult features</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Short palpi, trilobed scutellum, plain brown scales, blunt tip","B":"Short palpi, trilobed scutellum, wide mixed dark-and-pale wings","C":"Short palpi, trilobed scutellum, snow-white scales, pointed tip","D":"Long palpi, round scutellum, spotted wings, resting at 45°","E":"Long palpi, trilobed scutellum, plain brown scales, pointed tip"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Dirty, low-oxygen water with animal dung = Culex (slide 15). Culex is identified by having no special marking — plain brown, small wing scales, blunt abdominal end.","B":"Mansonia — ponds and swamps covered with aquatic plants.","C":"Aedes — breeds in clean water in household containers.","D":"Anopheles — clean natural water (seepage, streams, rock pools, footprints).","E":"No genus in the lecture combines long palpi with a trilobed scutellum."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1013.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which breeding-site pairing matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Anopheles: clean natural water — seepage, flowing streams, rock pools, animal footprints, rice fields and brackish water · Culex: dirty, low-oxygen water such as drains and water with animal droppings · Aedes: clean water in containers in and around houses · Mansonia: ponds and swamps covered with aquatic plants","C":"Anopheles: water jars inside houses · Aedes: flowing forest streams · Culex: plant-covered swamps · Mansonia: rice fields","E":"All four breed in the same clean container water; only their biting times differ","B":"Aedes breeds in dirty drain water, which is why dengue follows poor sanitation","D":"Mansonia breeds in brackish coastal water, which is why Brugian filariasis is coastal"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and each site explains its disease: Aedes’s household containers put dengue in towns, while Anopheles’s forest and border streams keep malaria rural.","C":"Every pairing is swapped.","E":"The four slides give four different habitats; biting time differs too (Aedes by day, Anopheles and Culex at night).","B":"Aedes breeds in CLEAN stored water — jars, vases, saucers — which is why dengue control is about containers, not drains.","D":"Mansonia’s sites are weed-covered ponds and swamps; brackish water is listed under Anopheles."},"t":"Breeding site → genus","sl":1011,"img":"slides_i/L8_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Breeding site → genus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|49","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"50","t":"Malaria → main control measure","s":"Several forest-border villagers who sleep near the rubber plantation develop fever, and ring forms are seen in their blood films. Which measure does the lecture list as the main control measure for the vector genus?","lec":"L8","sl":1012,"c":"<b>Malaria → main control measure</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Temephos granules in stored household water","B":"Removing aquatic weeds from pond surfaces","C":"Residual insecticide spraying","D":"Thermal fogging around each case's house","E":"Draining ditches and filling unused pools"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Aedes larval control.","B":"Mansonia control.","C":"Correct. Ring forms → malaria → Anopheles; slide 12 names residual insecticide spraying as the main measure (focal spraying and destroying breeding sites are supplementary).","D":"Aedes adult control.","E":"Culex control (drainage, filling unused pools)."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1012.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which control measure is matched correctly to its mosquito, and why?","o":{"C":"Removing the aquatic weeds from a pond controls MANSONIA, because its larvae and pupae attach to the roots of water plants for air","A":"Removing aquatic weeds controls AEDES, because its larvae feed on the plants","D":"Adding temephos (abate) to natural forest streams is the main measure against Mansonia","E":"Releasing larvivorous fish is listed only for Anopheles, since its larvae live in open water","B":"Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis (Bti) is a chemical larvicide used against adult Aedes"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the mechanism is the point — Mansonia immatures obtain air from plant roots, so removing the plants removes the habitat.","A":"Aedes larvae live in stored water in containers, not among pond weeds.","D":"Temephos (abate) is listed under AEDES larval control, applied to stored water.","E":"Larvivorous fish appear under both Culex and Aedes control in the deck.","B":"Bti is a BACTERIAL larvicide used against LARVAE, not a chemical against adults."},"t":"Larval control per genus","sl":1020,"img":"slides_i/L8_1020.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Larval control per genus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|50","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"51","t":"Tsetse, kissing bug, sand fly — discriminator","s":"Three blood-feeding insects are taught side by side: the tsetse fly, the kissing bug and the sand fly. Which statement matches the lecture?","lec":"L8","sl":31,"c":"<b>Tsetse, kissing bug, sand fly — discriminator</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The tsetse fly rests with its wings held up in a V shape","B":"The sand fly carries Leishmania on its body surface","C":"The kissing bug has a proboscis projecting forward off its head","D":"The sand fly has short mouthparts and feeds from a pool of blood","E":"The tsetse fly carries the trypanosome of Chagas disease"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The V-shaped wing is the sand fly's; the tsetse holds its wings crossed over the abdomen at rest.","B":"The sand fly is a biological vector — Leishmania develops inside it (slide 18).","C":"The forward-projecting proboscis is the tsetse fly's; the kissing bug is recognised by its cone-shaped head and 'X' wings.","D":"Correct. Sand fly (Phlebotomus): 1.5–2.5 mm, light brown, hairy body and wings, short mouthparts, pool feeder, only the female takes blood.","E":"Chagas disease (T. cruzi) is the kissing bug's; the tsetse carries T. brucei."},"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which morphological pairing is correct?","o":{"D":"Tsetse fly (Glossina): 6–15 mm, large head and eyes, a forward-projecting piercing proboscis, wings crossed over the abdomen at rest — vector of Trypanosoma brucei · Kissing bug (Triatoma): about 2.5 cm, cone-shaped head, wings overlapping to form a distinct 'X' — vector of Trypanosoma cruzi","B":"The tsetse fly has the cone-shaped head and the 'X' wings; the kissing bug has the forward-projecting proboscis","C":"Both transmit Trypanosoma cruzi; only their geography differs","E":"The kissing bug transmits sleeping sickness and the tsetse fly transmits Chagas disease","A":"Neither is a biological vector; both merely carry trypanosomes on their mouthparts"},"r":{"D":"Correct on size, morphology and pathogen for both. The ‘X’ over the back is the fastest way to call the kissing bug.","B":"The two sets of features are swapped.","C":"Tsetse carries T. brucei (African sleeping sickness); the kissing bug carries T. cruzi (American Chagas disease).","E":"The diseases are swapped along with the vectors.","A":"Both are biological vectors — the trypanosomes develop inside them."},"t":"Tsetse vs kissing bug","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L8_31.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Tsetse vs kissing bug</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L8|51","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"52","t":"Mansonia recognition → answer format","s":"A mosquito in the practical has short palpi, a trilobed scutellum and unusually wide wings covered with large mixed dark and pale scales. Which written medical importance scores the mark?","lec":"L8","sl":1021,"c":"<b>Mansonia recognition → answer format</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vector of Japanese B encephalitis","B":"Vector of Brugia malayi","C":"Vector of dengue virus","D":"Brugian filariasis","E":"Vector of Wuchereria bancrofti"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Culex.","B":"Correct. Wide wing scales with mixed dark and pale scales = Mansonia → vector of lymphatic filariasis (Brugia malayi).","C":"Aedes.","D":"Right disease, but a bare disease name scores zero — it must start 'Vector of…'.","E":"The filaria of Anopheles, Culex and Aedes."},"img":"slides_i/L8_1021.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which mosquito–pathogen pairing is NOT supported by the lecture?","o":{"B":"Mansonia spp. as a vector of dengue virus","D":"Aedes spp. as a vector of Zika virus","C":"Culex spp. as a vector of Japanese encephalitis virus","A":"Anopheles spp. as a vector of Plasmodium spp.","E":"Mansonia spp. as a vector of Brugia malayi"},"r":{"B":"Correct — this is the unsupported pairing. Mansonia’s slide lists only Brugia malayi; dengue belongs to Aedes.","D":"Supported: Aedes is listed for dengue, chikungunya, Zika and Wuchereria bancrofti.","C":"Supported: Culex is listed for Japanese B encephalitis and Wuchereria bancrofti.","A":"Supported: Anopheles is listed for malaria and Wuchereria bancrofti.","E":"Supported: this is Mansonia’s only listed pathogen."},"t":"The mosquito vector grid","sl":1010,"img":"slides_i/L8_1010.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>The mosquito vector grid</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233923340},{"id":"I|L9|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Uncomplicated falciparum — valid partner pairings","s":"A 30-year-old rubber tapper from a forested border district has had fever for three days. He is alert, eating normally and has no organ dysfunction. His thin film shows many small ring forms and a few banana-shaped gametocytes. Which regimen is appropriate for him?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Uncomplicated falciparum — valid partner pairings</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Artesunate by continuous intravenous infusion","B":"Dihydroartemisinin with piperaquine by mouth","C":"Chloroquine alone by mouth","D":"Dihydroartemisinin with mefloquine by mouth","E":"Chloroquine with primaquine by mouth"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Wrong route twice over: IV artesunate is for severe/complicated malaria, and even then it is a bolus over 1–2 min — slide 12 says 'Do not continuous infusion'.","B":"Correct. Banana-shaped gametocytes = P. falciparum; alert, eating and no organ dysfunction = uncomplicated. Slide 11 lists three oral options: artesunate + mefloquine, artesunate/lumefantrine, and dihydroartemisinin/piperaquine (DHA being the active artesunate derivative).","C":"Chloroquine alone is for the other species (P. malariae, P. knowlesi), not falciparum.","D":"The fabricated pairing the lecturer warned about (it was the BM32 distractor). Mefloquine partners artesunate; dihydroartemisinin partners piperaquine.","E":"Chloroquine + primaquine is the P. vivax / P. ovale regimen (primaquine clears hypnozoites, which falciparum does not form)."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 28-year-old returns from a forested border district with fever. A film shows P. falciparum. He is alert, has no organ dysfunction, and is keeping oral fluids down. Which regimen fits?","o":{"E":"Chloroquine alone","C":"Artesunate by intravenous bolus over 1–2 minutes","B":"Artesunate + mefloquine, given orally","D":"Chloroquine + primaquine","A":"Dihydroartemisinin + mefloquine"},"r":{"E":"Chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species (P. malariae, P. knowlesi).","C":"IV artesunate is reserved for SEVERE malaria. Nothing in the stem meets a severity criterion.","B":"Correct. No organ dysfunction and able to swallow means UNCOMPLICATED, and for P. falciparum the slide's first-line oral option is artesunate + mefloquine.","D":"Chloroquine + primaquine is the vivax/ovale regimen — primaquine is added to clear hypnozoites, which falciparum does not form.","A":"A fabricated pairing, and the most common distractor. Dihydroartemisinin partners PIPERAQUINE; mefloquine partners artesunate."},"t":"Malaria regimens","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Malaria regimens</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Severe criteria — vomiting overrides species","s":"A 25-year-old soldier returns from the Thai–Myanmar border with fever every 48 hours. His film shows enlarged red cells with Schüffner's dots and amoeboid trophozoites. He has no organ dysfunction, but he has vomited every tablet given since admission. What is the appropriate treatment now?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Severe criteria — vomiting overrides species</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Artesunate by intravenous bolus over 1–2 minutes","B":"Artesunate by continuous intravenous infusion","C":"Artesunate plus mefloquine by mouth","D":"Chloroquine plus primaquine by mouth","E":"Chloroquine alone by mouth, adding primaquine later"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Enlarged cells with Schüffner's dots = P. vivax, which would normally get chloroquine + primaquine — but slide 10 lists 'nausea, vomiting' and 'cannot take medication' as criteria for severe/complicated malaria in their own right. Severe → artesunate by bolus injection over 1–2 min.","B":"Right drug, wrong administration: slide 12 prints 'Do not continuous infusion'.","C":"The uncomplicated falciparum regimen — wrong species and still oral.","D":"The textbook vivax regimen, and the reflex trap: tablets that keep coming back up cannot work, which is exactly why vomiting reclassifies the case.","E":"Chloroquine alone is the P. malariae / P. knowlesi regimen, and it is still oral."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which single finding is enough on its own to classify malaria as severe and move the patient to intravenous artesunate?","o":{"C":"A temperature of 39.5 °C","A":"Persistent vomiting that prevents the patient taking tablets","B":"A palpable spleen","E":"A positive rapid diagnostic test","D":"Headache and myalgia"},"r":{"C":"Fever is universal in malaria and is not a severity criterion.","A":"Correct. 'Nausea, vomiting' and 'cannot take medication' are listed as severity criteria in their own right — because an oral drug that comes straight back up will fail, whatever the parasite is doing.","B":"Splenomegaly is an expected finding, not a complication.","E":"A positive test establishes the diagnosis, not the severity.","D":"Both are ordinary malaria symptoms."},"t":"Severity criteria","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Severity criteria</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Praziquantel — identified by mechanism, then PK","s":"In a laboratory study, tapeworm larvae exposed to drug X go into marked, sustained contraction within minutes, then develop vacuoles in the tegument and detach from the culture surface. Which pharmacokinetic statement from the lecture applies to drug X?","lec":"L9","sl":9,"c":"<b>Praziquantel — identified by mechanism, then PK</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It is absorbed better with a high-fat meal","B":"It has a half-life of 20–60 days","C":"It is a prodrug converted to an active metabolite","D":"It crosses the blood–brain barrier only minimally","E":"It is widely distributed and enters the brain"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"High-fat meal absorption belongs to albendazole and chloroquine.","B":"20–60 days is chloroquine's half-life.","C":"Albendazole and artesunate are the prodrugs; praziquantel is metabolised by the liver to inactive products.","D":"Minimal BBB crossing is ivermectin (which causes flaccid, not spastic, paralysis).","E":"Correct. Marked contraction (spastic paralysis) + vacuolisation + dislodgement = praziquantel (increased calcium permeability). Slide 9 PK: rapid absorption, hepatic metabolism to inactive products, widely distributed and enters the brain, urinary excretion."},"img":"slides_i/L9_9.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Ivermectin and praziquantel both paralyse the parasite, but by opposite routes. Which pairing is correct?","o":{"A":"Both raise calcium permeability; ivermectin also causes vacuolization","B":"Ivermectin raises calcium permeability causing spastic paralysis; praziquantel opens chloride channels causing flaccid paralysis","C":"Both open chloride channels; only the target tissue differs","D":"Ivermectin blocks microtubules; praziquantel blocks fumarate reductase","E":"Ivermectin opens chloride channels causing flaccid paralysis; praziquantel raises calcium permeability causing spastic paralysis"},"r":{"A":"Vacuolization is praziquantel's second mechanism, but ivermectin does not use calcium.","B":"Exactly reversed — and this reversal is the commonest way the mark is lost.","C":"Only ivermectin uses chloride.","D":"Microtubules and fumarate reductase are both ALBENDAZOLE's targets.","E":"Correct. Chloride entering a cell makes it LESS excitable, so the pharyngeal muscle goes limp; calcium is the universal contraction signal, so praziquantel produces sustained contraction."},"t":"Mechanisms","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Mechanisms</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Artesunate — delayed haemolysis follow-up","s":"A 35-year-old man is discharged after three days of intravenous treatment for severe falciparum malaria; his film is now clear. At a follow-up visit about three weeks later, which problem attributable to his treatment should you be looking for?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Artesunate — delayed haemolysis follow-up</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Blurred vision from retinal toxicity","B":"Haemolysis with a falling urine output","C":"Relapse from dormant liver-stage parasites","D":"Pancytopenia with megaloblastic anaemia","E":"Flank pain from crystals in the urine"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Blurred vision is chloroquine's, and only with long-term use.","B":"Correct. Severe malaria → IV artesunate, and slide 12 lists post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis about 3 weeks after treatment → acute kidney failure. The lecturer: remember this one; the rest of artesunate matters less.","C":"Hypnozoite relapse belongs to P. vivax and P. ovale; falciparum has no dormant liver stage (and a relapse would show parasites).","D":"Reversible pancytopenia with megaloblastic anaemia is pyrimethamine's adverse effect (rescued by leucovorin).","E":"Crystalluria is sulfadiazine's adverse effect (toxoplasmosis regimen)."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Three weeks after being successfully treated with intravenous artesunate for severe malaria, a patient returns with anaemia, jaundice and a falling urine output. Films show no parasites. What is the most likely cause?","o":{"D":"Sulfadiazine crystalluria","E":"Relapse from dormant liver hypnozoites","C":"Blackwater fever from the original infection","A":"Chloroquine-induced retinopathy","B":"Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis"},"r":{"D":"Sulfadiazine was not given; crystalluria also causes stones and haematuria rather than haemolysis.","E":"Hypnozoites belong to P. vivax and P. ovale, not to the falciparum infection artesunate was given for — and a relapse would show parasites.","C":"Blackwater fever is intravascular haemolysis during acute falciparum infection, not three weeks after clearance.","A":"Chloroquine was not given, and retinopathy needs long-term use and affects vision, not the kidney.","B":"Correct. The slide dates it at 'about 3 weeks after treatment' and names the consequence: the haemolytic load causes ACUTE KIDNEY FAILURE. The lecturer singled this out as the one artesunate fact to keep."},"t":"Adverse effects","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Adverse effects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Praziquantel — CYP3A4 inducers","s":"A 32-year-old pig farmer with multiple viable brain cysts is started on praziquantel because the first-choice drug is unavailable. He also receives dexamethasone and carbamazepine. A month later the cysts have not changed. Which explanation fits the lecture best?","lec":"L9","sl":8,"c":"<b>Praziquantel — CYP3A4 inducers</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Carbamazepine competes with praziquantel for excretion in the urine","B":"Dexamethasone blocks the calcium influx through which praziquantel acts","C":"Praziquantel penetrates poorly into brain tissue behind the blood–brain barrier","D":"Two of his co-medications speed up the hepatic metabolism of praziquantel","E":"Praziquantel is a prodrug whose activation the co-medications prevent"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Competition for renal excretion is not the interaction described.","B":"No such pharmacodynamic antagonism is taught; the interaction is metabolic.","C":"The opposite — praziquantel is 'widely distributed and enters the brain'.","D":"Correct. Slide 9's red box: dexamethasone, phenytoin, rifampin and carbamazepine are CYP3A4 inducers → ↑ metabolism of praziquantel. The awkward part: three of the four are drugs you deliberately co-prescribe in neurocysticercosis.","E":"Praziquantel is not a prodrug; the liver converts it to inactive products, which inducers accelerate."},"img":"slides_i/L9_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient with neurocysticercosis is treated with praziquantel and, as expected, also receives dexamethasone and phenytoin. Three weeks later the cysts are unchanged. What most likely happened?","o":{"D":"Dexamethasone and phenytoin induce CYP3A4 and lowered the praziquantel concentration","E":"Praziquantel does not cross into the central nervous system","C":"The corticosteroid suppressed the immune killing the drug depends on","A":"Phenytoin displaced praziquantel from plasma albumin","B":"Praziquantel needs a high-fat meal that was withheld"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and clinically awkward: the two co-drugs you are obliged to give — a steroid for the inflammation and an antiepileptic for the seizures — are both on the slide's CYP3A4 inducer list, along with rifampin and carbamazepine.","E":"The opposite is true. Praziquantel is 'widely distributed and enters the brain', which is precisely why it is usable here.","C":"The drug kills by raising intracellular calcium; it does not depend on the host immune response.","A":"Displacement would raise free drug, not lower the effect, and is not the mechanism described.","B":"Fatty-meal absorption is albendazole's and chloroquine's footnote, not praziquantel's."},"t":"Drug interactions","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L9_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Drug interactions</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Ivermectin — meningitis contraindication","s":"A 45-year-old man with strongyloidiasis also has acute bacterial meningitis. The drug of first choice for his worm infection is listed as one that should not be used in meningitis. Which property of that drug explains this?","lec":"L9","sl":4,"c":"<b>Ivermectin — meningitis contraindication</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It relies on an intact blood–brain barrier to stay away from human GABA-A receptors","B":"It is converted by the liver into a metabolite that is toxic to neurons","C":"It raises calcium permeability in human neurons and lowers the seizure threshold","D":"It inhibits mitosis in the meningeal cells that repair the inflamed membrane","E":"It suppresses the immune response the meninges need to clear bacteria"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Strongyloidiasis → ivermectin (> albendazole). Slide 5: minimal BBB crossing and 100× affinity for the parasite GABA-A receptor are its safety margin; an inflamed, leaky barrier lets the drug reach human GABA-A receptors — hence 'should not be used during … meningitis'.","B":"No neurotoxic metabolite is described; ivermectin is excreted in the faeces.","C":"Calcium permeability is praziquantel's mechanism (on the parasite).","D":"Mitosis inhibition is albendazole's mechanism (fetal harm, leukopenia, alopecia).","E":"Ivermectin is not immunosuppressive; that describes corticosteroids."},"img":"slides_i/L9_4.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which single pharmacokinetic property of ivermectin explains BOTH its very low toxicity and its uselessness against a helminth in the central nervous system?","o":{"E":"Excretion in the faeces","A":"Minimal blood–brain barrier crossing","C":"Its 100-fold selectivity for the parasite GABA receptor","D":"Good absorption from the gut","B":"Pregnancy category C"},"r":{"E":"The excretion route does not determine CNS penetration.","A":"Correct — one line, two consequences. Staying out of the brain spares human GABA receptors (few adverse effects) and also means the drug never reaches a CNS parasite.","C":"Real, and it contributes to safety, but it says nothing about why CNS parasites are not reached.","D":"Absorption determines how much gets into the blood, not whether it enters the brain.","B":"A pregnancy category is a safety classification, not a mechanism."},"t":"Ivermectin","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Ivermectin</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Ivermectin — Mazzotti-like reaction","s":"A patient with microfilariae in his blood is given a single dose of ivermectin. Within hours he develops fever, headache, vertigo, drowsiness and a fall in blood pressure. Which interpretation and response match the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Ivermectin — Mazzotti-like reaction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Direct drug toxicity at human GABA-A receptors, managed by halving the dose","B":"A meningitis-like reaction to dying cysts, managed with an antiepileptic","C":"Folate depletion in the host marrow, managed with folinic acid","D":"An IgE-mediated allergy to the drug, managed with an antihistamine","E":"An immune reaction to the dying microfilariae, managed with a corticosteroid"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Wrong logic: a lower dose does not stop an immune reaction to dying parasites, and the barrier keeps ivermectin away from human GABA-A receptors.","B":"The meningitis-like reaction (seizure) is praziquantel's, in neurocysticercosis (corticosteroid + antiepileptic).","C":"Folate depletion and folinic acid rescue belong to pyrimethamine.","D":"Not framed as a drug allergy; the slide's answer is a corticosteroid.","E":"Correct. Slide 5: Mazzotti-like reaction (immune response to microfilaria) — fever, headache, dizziness, vertigo, sedation, hypotension — with the green arrow to corticosteroids. It is the host's response to parasite antigen, not drug toxicity."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient with strongyloidiasis develops fever, headache, dizziness and hypotension within hours of the first ivermectin dose. What would have prevented this?","o":{"C":"An antihistamine","B":"A lower ivermectin dose","E":"Corticosteroid premedication","D":"Leucovorin","A":"Giving the drug with a high-fat meal"},"r":{"C":"Antihistamine is the wrong axis — this is not a type I hypersensitivity to the drug.","B":"Wrong logic. Reducing the dose does not stop an immune reaction to parasite antigen; it only leaves more worms alive.","E":"Correct. This is the Mazzotti-like reaction: the HOST'S IMMUNE RESPONSE to dying microfilariae, not drug toxicity — so it is suppressed with a corticosteroid, which is exactly what the slide lists.","D":"Leucovorin rescues pyrimethamine's marrow toxicity and has no role here.","A":"Food affects absorption, not the immune reaction."},"t":"Ivermectin","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Ivermectin</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Albendazole — the antimitotic adverse effects","s":"A 28-year-old woman has been on a prolonged course of the first-choice drug for her neurocysticercosis. She now has patchy hair loss and a white cell count of 2.8 × 10⁹/L, and she hopes to become pregnant next year. The mechanism behind her two new problems also underlies which other listed warning?","lec":"L9","sl":6,"c":"<b>Albendazole — the antimitotic adverse effects</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Reversible megaloblastic anaemia","B":"Hepatotoxicity","C":"Fetal resorption","D":"Impaired fetal development","E":"Kernicterus in the newborn"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Reversible (megaloblastic) pancytopenia is pyrimethamine's antifolate effect, rescued by leucovorin.","B":"Hepatotoxicity is on the albendazole slide too, but as a separate bullet — it is not a consequence of blocking mitosis.","C":"Fetal resorption is artesunate's warning.","D":"Correct. Albendazole inhibits microtubules — the mitotic spindle — so fast-dividing tissues suffer: hair follicles (alopecia), marrow (reversible leukopenia) and the fetus. Slide 6: 'Albendazole inhibits mitosis and impair fetal development.'","E":"Kernicterus in late pregnancy is sulfadiazine's warning."},"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient on prolonged albendazole develops hair loss and a falling white cell count. Which single property of the drug accounts for both?","o":{"D":"It inhibits cytoplasmic microtubules, and therefore the mitotic spindle","E":"It is hepatotoxic","A":"It antagonises folate","C":"It generates free radicals in host membranes","B":"It is eliminated by renal excretion"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Microtubules build the spindle, so the tissues that divide fastest take the damage: hair follicles (alopecia), bone marrow (leukopenia) and the fetus (impaired development). One mechanism, three effects.","E":"Hepatotoxicity is a separate listed effect and explains neither finding.","A":"Folate antagonism is pyrimethamine's and sulfadiazine's mechanism.","C":"Free-radical damage is the artesunate/chloroquine mechanism, and it acts on the parasite.","B":"The excretion route causes neither effect."},"t":"Albendazole","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Albendazole</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Pyrimethamine pancytopenia — folinic acid rescue","s":"A 38-year-old man with HIV and cerebral toxoplasmosis was started on pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine alone. Three weeks later his haemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL with macrocytes, neutrophils 0.6 × 10⁹/L and platelets 60 × 10⁹/L. Which step does the lecture give for this?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Pyrimethamine pancytopenia — folinic acid rescue</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Alkalinise the urine with fluids","B":"Add folinic acid","C":"Change sulfadiazine to co-trimoxazole","D":"Stop pyrimethamine permanently","E":"Add dexamethasone for oedema"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Hydration and urine alkalinisation prevent sulfadiazine crystalluria.","B":"Correct. Megaloblastic anaemia + leucopenia + thrombocytopenia = pyrimethamine's reversible pancytopenia (DHFR inhibition → ↓ tetrahydrofolate). Slide 15: + leucovorin (folinic acid supplement). Watch the name swap — NL may write folinic acid instead of leucovorin. The patient can use ready-made folinic acid; the parasite cannot, so its block stays.","C":"Co-trimoxazole is itself an antifolate pair and does not address pyrimethamine's effect.","D":"The pancytopenia is 'reversible' and managed with leucovorin rather than by abandoning the drug.","E":"Dexamethasone treats symptoms from cerebral oedema (the BM32 question), not the drug's marrow toxicity."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Leucovorin is added to pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine. Why does it rescue the patient without also rescuing Toxoplasma?","o":{"E":"Leucovorin is given at a dose too low to affect the parasite","D":"Leucovorin is actively excluded from the parasite by an efflux pump","C":"Leucovorin only reaches the bone marrow and not the brain","A":"Leucovorin inactivates sulfadiazine but not pyrimethamine","B":"Humans can absorb ready-made folinic acid, whereas the parasite must synthesise folate itself"},"r":{"E":"A subtherapeutic dose would not prevent the pancytopenia either.","D":"No efflux mechanism is described; the difference is uptake capability, not export.","C":"Distribution is not the explanation, and cerebral toxoplasmosis is treated with this regimen anyway.","A":"Leucovorin does not inactivate either drug.","B":"Correct. Supplying the finished product bypasses the blocked human pathway; the parasite has no uptake route for it and stays blocked. Note also that leucovorin and folinic acid are the SAME substance — the lecturer warned that NL alternates between the two words."},"t":"Toxoplasmosis","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Pregnancy warnings — which drug, which warning","s":"A 26-year-old woman at 30 weeks' gestation asks about several antiparasitic drugs. Which drug is paired with the pregnancy warning the lecture actually gives for it?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Pregnancy warnings — which drug, which warning</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ivermectin — impaired fetal development","B":"Albendazole — pregnancy category C","C":"Pyrimethamine — kernicterus late in pregnancy","D":"Artesunate — fetal resorption","E":"Chloroquine — fetal resorption"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Impaired fetal development (via mitosis inhibition) is albendazole's.","B":"Category C is ivermectin's label.","C":"Kernicterus in late pregnancy is sulfadiazine's; pyrimethamine is safe in pregnancy and breastfeeding.","D":"Correct. Slide 12 lists fetal resorption under artesunate's adverse effects.","E":"Chloroquine is one of only two drugs marked safe in pregnancy (with pyrimethamine)."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A pregnant woman in her third trimester needs treatment. Which statement about the drugs in this lecture is correct?","o":{"D":"Chloroquine and pyrimethamine are stated to be safe; sulfadiazine risks kernicterus late in pregnancy","B":"Artesunate and chloroquine are both safe; pyrimethamine is contraindicated","E":"Albendazole is safe because it is a prodrug activated only in the worm","A":"Ivermectin is category A and may be used without restriction","C":"All antiparasitic drugs are contraindicated in pregnancy"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Those are the only two drugs the slides mark as safe, and sulfadiazine displaces bilirubin from albumin, which risks bilirubin encephalopathy in the newborn.","B":"Artesunate causes FETAL RESORPTION; pyrimethamine is the safe one.","E":"Albendazole inhibits mitosis and impairs fetal development — it is not selectively activated in the worm.","A":"Ivermectin is category C, and should not be used during breastfeeding.","C":"Too absolute — chloroquine and pyrimethamine are explicitly safe."},"t":"Pregnancy & G6PD","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L9_13.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Pregnancy & G6PD</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Artesunate and chloroquine — one mechanism","s":"A 24-year-old man with falciparum malaria becomes unrousable and has repeated seizures. He is given the drug the lecture lists for this situation. Which statement about that drug's mechanism is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Artesunate and chloroquine — one mechanism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It inhibits cytoplasmic microtubules, as albendazole also does","B":"It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, as chloroquine also does","C":"It raises calcium permeability of the parasite, as praziquantel also does","D":"It blocks heme polymerisation to hemozoin, as pyrimethamine also does","E":"It blocks heme polymerisation to hemozoin, as chloroquine also does"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Microtubule inhibition is albendazole's mechanism against helminths.","B":"DHFR inhibition is pyrimethamine; neither artesunate nor chloroquine is an antifolate.","C":"Calcium permeability → spastic paralysis is praziquantel's mechanism against flukes and tapeworms.","D":"Right mechanism, wrong partner: pyrimethamine is an antifolate (DHFR inhibitor).","E":"Correct. Altered consciousness and seizures = severe malaria → IV artesunate. Slides 12 and 13 print the identical mechanism for artesunate and chloroquine: inhibits heme polymerization and hemozoin → toxic free heme → free radical → destabilizes membrane."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pair of drugs in this lecture share an identical mechanism of action?","o":{"A":"Pyrimethamine and chloroquine","B":"Ivermectin and praziquantel","D":"Albendazole and praziquantel","E":"Artesunate and chloroquine","C":"Sulfadiazine and ivermectin"},"r":{"A":"Pyrimethamine is an antifolate; chloroquine works on heme.","B":"Opposite ions — chloride versus calcium.","D":"Albendazole starves the worm of ATP; praziquantel floods it with calcium.","E":"Correct. Slides 12 and 13 print the same sentence for both: inhibits heme polymerization and hemozoin → toxic free heme → free radical → destabilizes membrane.","C":"Two unrelated mechanisms, in two different parasites."},"t":"Mechanisms","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Mechanisms</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Artesunate — why a partner drug","s":"An intern proposes treating uncomplicated falciparum malaria with oral artesunate on its own. Which pharmacokinetic fact is the lecture's reason for always pairing it with mefloquine, lumefantrine or piperaquine?","lec":"L9","sl":12,"c":"<b>Artesunate — why a partner drug</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its active metabolite has a half-life of about 45 minutes","B":"It is poorly absorbed unless taken with a high-fat meal","C":"It is rapidly cleared by CYP3A4 induction from co-medications","D":"It is excreted unchanged in the faeces within hours","E":"It is 50–60% bound to plasma proteins"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 12: artesunate (prodrug) t½ 20–45 min → dihydroartemisinin (active) t½ 45 min → '+ Drug partner (Long acting)'. A fast short-acting killer plus a long-acting partner is the whole design of combination therapy.","B":"The high-fat meal note belongs to albendazole and chloroquine.","C":"The CYP3A4-inducer interaction is on the praziquantel slide, not the artesunate slide.","D":"Faecal excretion is ivermectin's pharmacokinetic line.","E":"50–60% protein binding is chloroquine's — whose t½ of 20–60 days is why it can be used alone."},"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why is artesunate always combined with a partner drug rather than used alone?","o":{"E":"It cannot be given by mouth","C":"It is a prodrug and needs a second drug to activate it","D":"It is inactive against P. falciparum on its own","B":"The partner drug prevents delayed haemolysis","A":"Its half-life is only about 45 minutes, so alone it would be cleared before the infection is"},"r":{"E":"Oral artesunate is exactly what uncomplicated malaria receives.","C":"Artesunate is converted to dihydroartemisinin by the body, not by the partner drug.","D":"It is highly active against falciparum — that is why it is first-line.","B":"Nothing prevents the delayed haemolysis; it is a recognised late adverse effect.","A":"Correct. The slide prints t½ 20–45 min for the prodrug and 45 min for dihydroartemisinin, then the instruction '+ Drug partner (Long acting)'. This is the whole design principle of artemisinin-based combination therapy: a fast killer plus a slow mop-up."},"t":"Artesunate","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Artesunate</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Sulfadiazine crystalluria — prevention","s":"A 40-year-old man on treatment for cerebral toxoplasmosis develops flank pain and haematuria; urine microscopy shows drug crystals and the urine pH is 5.5. Which measure does the lecture give to prevent this?","lec":"L9","sl":16,"c":"<b>Sulfadiazine crystalluria — prevention</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vigorous hydration with urine acidification to below pH 5.0","B":"Vigorous hydration with urine alkalinisation to above pH 7.15","C":"Fluid restriction with urine acidification to below pH 5.0","D":"Adding folinic acid and taking every dose with a full meal","E":"Restricting fluid intake with urine alkalinisation to above pH 7.15"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Hydration is right but acid urine promotes precipitation.","B":"Correct. Crystalluria is sulfadiazine's; slide 16: vigorous hydration and urine alkalinization (pH > 7.15). The drug is poorly soluble in acidic, concentrated urine.","C":"Both halves reversed — concentrated, acidic urine is exactly what makes crystals form.","D":"Folinic acid and taking doses with a meal are pyrimethamine's two manoeuvres (marrow and GI).","E":"Alkalinisation is right but restricting fluid concentrates the urine."},"img":"slides_i/L9_16.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"How is sulfadiazine crystalluria prevented?","o":{"E":"Fluid restriction to reduce urine volume","A":"Acidification of the urine","B":"Vigorous hydration and alkalinisation of the urine to pH above 7.15","D":"Adding leucovorin","C":"Giving the drug with a high-fat meal"},"r":{"E":"Concentrating the urine makes precipitation worse.","A":"The exact opposite, and the planted error — acid urine makes crystals MORE likely.","B":"Correct, and printed on the slide. Sulfonamides are poorly soluble in acidic, concentrated urine, so you dilute it and raise the pH.","D":"Leucovorin addresses pyrimethamine's marrow toxicity, not solubility.","C":"Food affects absorption of albendazole and chloroquine, not sulfadiazine solubility."},"t":"Sulfadiazine","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L9_16.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Sulfadiazine</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Vivax relapse — primaquine and G6PD","s":"A 30-year-old man had malaria treated with chloroquine alone four months ago and recovered. He now has fever again, and the film shows enlarged red cells with Schüffner's dots. Before you give the drug that should have been added the first time, which test does the lecture tell you to check?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Vivax relapse — primaquine and G6PD</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Serum creatinine and eGFR","B":"Urine pH","C":"G6PD enzyme activity","D":"Liver function tests","E":"Retinal examination"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Kidney monitoring relates to artesunate's delayed haemolysis → acute kidney failure, not to starting primaquine.","B":"Urine pH (alkalinise above 7.15) is the sulfadiazine crystalluria manoeuvre.","C":"Correct. Enlarged cells + Schüffner's dots + relapse months later = P. vivax from hypnozoites. Chloroquine clears the blood only; primaquine should have been added. Slide 11 flags it in red: 'Precaution in G-6-PD' — primaquine causes haemolysis in G6PD deficiency.","D":"Hepatotoxicity is listed for albendazole (and hepatitis, uncommonly, for praziquantel), not as the primaquine precaution.","E":"Eye checks relate to chloroquine's blurred vision with long-term use, not to primaquine."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why is primaquine added to chloroquine for P. vivax and P. ovale but not for P. malariae?","o":{"A":"Only vivax and ovale are chloroquine-resistant","C":"Only vivax and ovale form dormant liver hypnozoites, and only primaquine clears them","D":"Only vivax and ovale cause severe anaemia","B":"P. malariae is treated with artesunate instead","E":"Primaquine is contraindicated in P. malariae infection"},"r":{"A":"Resistance is a separate issue, and resistance would upgrade the case to complicated malaria.","C":"Correct. Chloroquine cures the blood stage and the illness; without primaquine the sleeping liver forms wake weeks later and the patient relapses.","D":"Severe anaemia is more characteristic of falciparum.","B":"P. malariae is treated with chloroquine.","E":"There is no such contraindication; it is simply unnecessary."},"t":"Hypnozoites","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Hypnozoites</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Strongyloidiasis hyperinfection — drug of choice","s":"A 60-year-old rice farmer has taken prednisolone for a year for an autoimmune disease. He now has cough, wheeze and progressive breathlessness, a winding urticarial rash across the trunk, and larvae in his sputum. Which drug does the lecture give first?","lec":"L9","sl":3,"c":"<b>Strongyloidiasis hyperinfection — drug of choice</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Albendazole","C":"Ivermectin","D":"Chloroquine","E":"Pyrimethamine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Praziquantel is for cestodes and trematodes (second choice for neurocysticercosis), not nematodes.","B":"Albendazole works for strongyloidiasis but is second to ivermectin — the lecture's intended near-miss. (Albendazole is the winner for neurocysticercosis instead.)","C":"Correct. Steroid-treated host + pneumonia/ARDS + serpiginous urticarial rash + larvae = Strongyloides hyperinfection. Slide 4 prints ivermectin first and larger; the lecturer said it is the more effective of the two.","D":"Chloroquine is an antimalarial.","E":"Pyrimethamine is the antifolate for toxoplasmosis."},"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient has a serpiginous urticarial rash, intermittent wheeze and eosinophilia. Strongyloidiasis is confirmed. Which drug is first choice, and why is the alternative second?","o":{"A":"Albendazole, because ivermectin cannot be given orally","C":"Ivermectin, because it is more effective than albendazole for this infection","B":"Praziquantel, because the parasite is a nematode","E":"Albendazole, because ivermectin does not reach the gut","D":"Ivermectin, because albendazole is contraindicated in eosinophilia"},"r":{"A":"Ivermectin is well absorbed from the gut and is an oral drug.","C":"Correct. The slide prints ivermectin first and larger, and the lecturer said plainly that ivermectin is the more effective of the two here.","B":"Praziquantel is for cestodes and trematodes, not nematodes.","E":"Ivermectin is excreted in the faeces and acts in the gut perfectly well.","D":"No such contraindication exists."},"t":"Strongyloidiasis","sl":3,"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Strongyloidiasis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Neurocysticercosis — drug of choice","s":"A 30-year-old man who raises pigs and eats undercooked pork has his first generalised seizure. He is afebrile, HIV-negative, and has had headaches for two months. MRI shows several small cysts, each with an eccentric dot. Which drug does the lecture rank first for him?","lec":"L9","sl":7,"c":"<b>Neurocysticercosis — drug of choice</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Albendazole","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Artesunate","E":"Pyrimethamine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Seizure without fever + cysts with a scolex (the eccentric dot) = neurocysticercosis (Taenia solium). Slide 8 prints 'Albendazole > Praziquantel'.","B":"Ivermectin barely crosses the BBB and is first choice for strongyloidiasis, not a CNS cestode.","C":"Praziquantel works and enters the brain, but it is ranked second — and its level is lowered by the steroid and antiepileptics he will need.","D":"Artesunate is an antimalarial.","E":"Pyrimethamine treats cerebral toxoplasmosis — the ring-enhancing lesions of advanced HIV, not cysts with a scolex."},"img":"slides_i/L9_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A previously well 30-year-old has a first seizure. He is afebrile. Imaging shows multiple cystic brain lesions. Which drug is first choice?","o":{"C":"Praziquantel","A":"Albendazole","E":"Ivermectin","D":"Artesunate","B":"Pyrimethamine"},"r":{"C":"Praziquantel works and does enter the brain, but the slide marks it second.","A":"Correct. Seizures WITHOUT fever plus cystic brain lesions is neurocysticercosis, and the slide prints 'Albendazole > Praziquantel'.","E":"Ivermectin has minimal blood–brain barrier crossing and is useless for a CNS parasite.","D":"Artesunate is an antimalarial.","B":"Pyrimethamine treats toxoplasmosis — the other HIV-associated brain lesion, which is the intended near-miss."},"t":"Cysticercosis","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L9_7.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Cysticercosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Artesunate — how to give it","s":"A 22-year-old woman with falciparum malaria becomes jaundiced and her urine output falls sharply. The ward nurse asks how the drug of choice for her should be given. Which instruction is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Artesunate — how to give it</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Deep intramuscular injection once weekly","B":"Continuous intravenous infusion over 12 hours","C":"Oral loading dose through a nasogastric tube","D":"Intravenous bolus injection over 1–2 minutes","E":"Slow intravenous infusion over 4 hours"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"No depot or weekly preparation is taught; a long dosing interval suits chloroquine's 20–60-day half-life, not artesunate's 45 minutes.","B":"Slide 12 says 'Do not continuous infusion' — the short half-life is solved by a partner drug, not by an infusion.","C":"Severe malaria is exactly the situation where oral therapy is unreliable; the IV route is chosen.","D":"Correct. Jaundice and oliguria are organ-dysfunction indicators → severe malaria → artesunate, which slide 10 gives as a 'bolus injection, 1–2 min'.","E":"Still an infusion rather than a 1–2-minute bolus."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"How is artesunate administered in severe malaria?","o":{"C":"Continuous intravenous infusion over 24 hours","D":"Intravenous bolus over 1–2 minutes, not as a continuous infusion","B":"Intramuscular depot injection weekly","E":"Oral loading dose followed by continuous infusion","A":"Rectal suppository only"},"r":{"C":"Explicitly excluded on the slide.","D":"Correct, and both halves are printed on the slides — 'bolus injection, 1-2 min' and, in red, 'Do not continuous infusion'.","B":"No depot preparation is described.","E":"Severe malaria means oral therapy is unreliable, which is why the IV route was chosen.","A":"Not the route taught here."},"t":"Route","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Route</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Sequential folate blockade — order of the steps","s":"Toxoplasma must make its own folate. The standard regimen for cerebral toxoplasmosis attacks this pathway at two consecutive steps. Which description of the two blocks, in the order the pathway runs, is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":15,"c":"<b>Sequential folate blockade — order of the steps</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sulfadiazine at thymidylate synthase, then pyrimethamine at dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Pyrimethamine at dihydropteroate synthase, then sulfadiazine at dihydrofolate reductase","C":"Sulfadiazine at dihydropteroate synthase, then pyrimethamine at dihydrofolate reductase","D":"Sulfadiazine at dihydrofolate reductase, then pyrimethamine at thymidylate synthase","E":"Pyrimethamine at dihydrofolate reductase, then sulfadiazine at dihydropteroate synthase"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Thymidylate synthase is not in the lecture's pathway; sulfadiazine acts at DHPS.","B":"Both enzymes swapped between the drugs.","C":"Correct. Slide 16's diagram: PABA → dihydropteroate synthase (DHPS, blocked by sulfonamides competing with PABA) → dihydrofolic acid → dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR, blocked by pyrimethamine/trimethoprim) → tetrahydrofolic acid → purines → DNA. Two steps of one pathway = sequential blockade.","D":"Sulfadiazine does not act at DHFR, and thymidylate synthase is not a target taught here.","E":"The drug–enzyme pairs are right but the order is reversed — DHPS comes first."},"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine are given together. Which enzymes do they block, and what is the design called?","o":{"A":"Both block hemozoin formation — additive toxicity","E":"Pyrimethamine blocks DHPS and sulfadiazine blocks DHFR — sequential blockade","D":"Both block DHFR at different sites — competitive synergy","B":"Pyrimethamine blocks thymidylate synthase and sulfadiazine blocks DHFR","C":"Pyrimethamine blocks DHFR and sulfadiazine blocks DHPS — sequential blockade of one pathway"},"r":{"A":"Hemozoin is the antimalarial mechanism.","E":"The enzymes are the right pair but assigned to the wrong drugs.","D":"They block different enzymes, not the same one.","B":"Thymidylate synthase is not the target described in this lecture.","C":"Correct. Sulfadiazine competes with PABA at dihydropteroate synthase, the earlier step; pyrimethamine blocks dihydrofolate reductase, the later one. Hitting one road twice is far harder to escape — the same design as co-trimoxazole."},"t":"Antifolates","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Antifolates</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 7","t":"Malaria regimens","s":"A 40-year-old man was diagnosed with uncomplicated malaria from Plasmodium falciparum. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Malaria regimens</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 · Infectious summative 1 · Lect 7 Antiparasitic drugs</span>","o":{"A":"Chloroquine","B":"Artesunate","C":"Primaquine","E":"Artesunate + Mefloquine","D":"Dihydroartemisinin + Mefloquine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species.","B":"Artesunate alone has a 45-minute half-life and always needs a long-acting partner.","C":"Primaquine is added to chloroquine for vivax and ovale, to clear hypnozoites; it is never used alone for falciparum.","E":"Correct. The slide's first-listed option for uncomplicated falciparum malaria.","D":"The classic fabricated pairing. Dihydroartemisinin partners PIPERAQUINE; mefloquine partners artesunate. Reading the two names quickly is exactly how this distractor works."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q30 (57)","t":"G6PD","s":"Which drug most commonly causes haemolysis in a patient with G6PD deficiency?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>G6PD</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q30 · cohorts 57 and 58</span>","o":{"B":"Artesunate","A":"Chloroquine","C":"Tetracycline","D":"Primaquine","E":"Mefloquine"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Artesunate causes DELAYED haemolysis at about three weeks in anyone, which is a different phenomenon from G6PD-triggered haemolysis.","A":"Chloroquine is the drug marked SAFE IN PREGNANCY and carries no G6PD warning here.","C":"Not an antimalarial in this lecture at all — included to test whether you are reading the list.","D":"Correct, and set in two cohorts with the same five options. The slide carries the warning 'Precaution in G-6-PD' next to primaquine.","E":"Mefloquine is the partner drug; no G6PD warning is given."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 Q30 (58)"]},{"id":"I|L9|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 7","t":"Toxoplasmosis","s":"A 50-year-old man with HIV presents with limb weakness and seizures. Blood tests are positive for Toxoplasma. He is already receiving pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine. Which drug will most help reduce his symptoms?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Toxoplasmosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 · Infectious summative 1 · Lect 7 Antiparasitic drugs</span>","o":{"B":"Co-trimoxazole","D":"Leucovorin","C":"Carbamazepine","A":"Dexamethasone","E":"Mannitol"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"An alternative anti-Toxoplasma regimen — he is already on effective therapy, and swapping it does not relieve today's symptoms.","D":"The reflex answer whenever pyrimethamine appears, and the reason this question works. Leucovorin treats the DRUG'S marrow toxicity, and the stem mentions no cytopenia.","C":"An antiepileptic addresses the seizure but does nothing for the limb weakness.","A":"Correct. Read what is asked: his SYMPTOMS. Both the limb weakness and the seizures come from oedema around the cerebral lesion, and a corticosteroid reduces that — covering both at once.","E":"Osmotic therapy is for acute raised intracranial pressure, not the listed treatment for this presentation."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L9|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"first-line set","t":"Malaria regimens","s":"Which statement about first-line malaria therapy matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Malaria regimens</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 · first-line therapy items</span>","o":{"E":"Malariae and knowlesi require primaquine to prevent relapse","A":"All species are treated with chloroquine plus primaquine","D":"Complicated malaria is treated with oral artesunate plus mefloquine","C":"Vivax and ovale are treated with artesunate plus lumefantrine","B":"Complicated falciparum malaria is treated with intravenous artesunate; vivax and ovale with chloroquine plus primaquine; malariae and knowlesi with chloroquine alone"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Neither species forms hypnozoites, so there is nothing for primaquine to clear.","A":"Primaquine is only for the two hypnozoite-forming species, and falciparum needs an artemisinin combination.","D":"Complicated malaria means the oral route is unreliable — it is treated intravenously.","C":"Artesunate/lumefantrine is a falciparum option.","B":"Correct. This is the answer key's own three-line summary, and it matches slides 10–11 exactly."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q16 (58)","t":"Opportunistic infection","s":"Which of the following is NOT typically defined as an AIDS-associated opportunistic infection?","lec":"L9","sl":3,"c":"<b>Opportunistic infection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 327-2 Q16 · cohort 58</span>","o":{"C":"Intestinal microsporidiosis","A":"Cryptosporidiosis","B":"Toxoplasmic encephalitis","E":"Disseminated strongyloidiasis","D":"Cyclosporiasis"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"A recognised opportunistic intestinal infection in advanced HIV.","A":"A recognised AIDS-defining diarrhoeal infection.","B":"The classic AIDS-associated brain lesion — and the one this lecture treats with pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine.","E":"Correct. The answer key's note is the reason: disseminated strongyloidiasis is characteristically precipitated by CORTICOSTEROID therapy rather than by HIV, which is why it sits outside the AIDS-defining list.","D":"Also seen in advanced HIV."},"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q20f (59)","t":"Co-trimoxazole","s":"Co-trimoxazole is listed as an alternative regimen for toxoplasmosis. What is it, and why does it work against a protozoan?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Co-trimoxazole</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 · Ax_Infectious 321-2 Q20f · cohorts 59 and 60</span>","o":{"B":"A macrolide combination with intrinsic antiprotozoal activity","A":"Trimethoprim plus sulfadiazine — it blocks heme polymerization","C":"Trimethoprim plus sulfamethoxazole — they block DHFR and DHPS, the same sequential folate blockade as pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine","D":"A sulfonamide alone, which blocks DHFR","E":"A folate supplement that starves the parasite by feedback inhibition"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Neither component is a macrolide.","A":"Heme polymerization is the antimalarial mechanism, and the second drug is sulfamethoxazole.","C":"Correct. The lecturer pointed out that the same two-enzyme attack is why an antibacterial combination is effective against Toxoplasma, and said it would be revisited in the antibacterial topic.","D":"Co-trimoxazole is two drugs, and the sulfonamide half blocks DHPS, not DHFR.","E":"It is not a supplement; that describes leucovorin, which does the opposite."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 Q20f (60)"]},{"id":"I|L9|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q16 (59)","t":"Vivax / ovale radical cure","s":"A traveller returns with P. vivax malaria. Which regimen does the lecture give, and why is the second drug needed?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Vivax / ovale radical cure</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank, cohorts 59 Q16 & 57 written Q7 — the per-species malaria regimens</span>","o":{"C":"Chloroquine alone — vivax has no dormant liver stage","A":"Chloroquine plus primaquine — chloroquine clears the blood stage, primaquine kills the liver hypnozoite and prevents relapse","D":"Artesunate plus mefloquine — vivax is treated exactly like falciparum","B":"Primaquine alone — it covers both the blood and the liver stage","E":"Intravenous artesunate — all vivax infections are classed as complicated"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Chloroquine has no activity against hypnozoites, so chloroquine alone gives relapse weeks to months later.","A":"Correct, and it is the same for P. OVALE. These are the only two species with hypnozoites, so they are the only two needing a radical cure. Check G6PD first — primaquine causes haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, which the lecture flags as a precaution.","D":"Artesunate + mefloquine is the uncomplicated FALCIPARUM regimen. P. malariae and P. knowlesi take chloroquine alone.","B":"Primaquine is a weak blood schizonticide; it is a partner drug, never a solo cure.","E":"IV artesunate is reserved for SEVERE/complicated malaria — organ dysfunction, pregnancy, or inability to take oral drugs."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["LS54 written Q7 (57)"]},{"id":"I|L9|25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Lect 7","t":"Praziquantel","s":"What is the mechanism by which praziquantel kills flukes and tapeworms?","lec":"L9","sl":9,"c":"<b>Praziquantel</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Lecture-flagged — the lecture devotes a slide to praziquantel's mechanism and to the CYP3A4 interaction that matters when it is combined with dexamethasone in neurocysticercosis</span>","o":{"E":"It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase in the parasite","B":"It blocks microtubule polymerisation and glucose uptake in the worm","D":"It opens glutamate-gated chloride channels, causing flaccid paralysis","C":"It increases calcium permeability of the tegument, causing spastic paralysis and vacuolisation","A":"It generates free radicals after activation by haem iron"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"DHFR inhibition is pyrimethamine (with sulfadiazine, DHPS) for toxoplasmosis.","B":"Microtubules and glucose uptake are ALBENDAZOLE and mebendazole.","D":"Glutamate-gated chloride channels and FLACCID paralysis are IVERMECTIN — the exact opposite paralysis, which is the planted contrast.","C":"Correct. Calcium floods in, the worm contracts hard (SPASTIC paralysis), the tegument vacuolates and the parasite is dislodged. Praziquantel crosses into the brain, which is why it is used in neurocysticercosis.","A":"Haem-activated free radicals are the ARTEMISININS. Watch the interaction the slide flags: dexamethasone, phenytoin, rifampicin and carbamazepine induce CYP3A4 and LOWER praziquantel levels."},"img":"slides_i/L9_9.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L9|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Ivermectin — synergy with GABA-A agonists","s":"A 70-year-old woman who takes diazepam every night for insomnia is given ivermectin for strongyloidiasis. That night she becomes difficult to rouse and her breathing slows. Which statement from the lecture explains this?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Ivermectin — synergy with GABA-A agonists</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ivermectin acts on GABA-A receptors and synergises with GABA-A agonists","B":"Ivermectin blocks folate synthesis in the brainstem respiratory centre","C":"Ivermectin raises calcium permeability in neurons and so deepens sedation","D":"Diazepam displaces ivermectin from albumin and raises its free fraction","E":"Diazepam induces CYP3A4 and raises the level of a toxic ivermectin metabolite"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 5 lists 'synergizing effect with GABA-A agonist' as an adverse effect; diazepam is a GABA-A agonist, and the lecturer walked through the chain to sedation, respiratory depression and coma.","B":"Folate synthesis inhibition belongs to pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine.","C":"Calcium permeability is praziquantel's mechanism.","D":"Protein-binding displacement is not described for ivermectin.","E":"Diazepam is not on the CYP3A4-inducer list (dexamethasone, phenytoin, rifampin, carbamazepine), and inducers lower, not raise, the parent drug — that interaction is praziquantel's."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient with severe falciparum malaria is prescribed intravenous artesunate. Which statement about administration and pharmacology is correct?","o":{"A":"It is given as a BOLUS over 1–2 minutes and must NOT be given by continuous infusion; it is a prodrug converted to dihydroartemisinin, whose half-life is only about 45 minutes, which is why a long-acting partner drug is added","E":"It is given by continuous infusion over 24 hours to maintain steady levels, because its half-life is short","C":"It is the active drug itself, and dihydroartemisinin is an inactive metabolite excreted in bile","D":"It is given orally in severe malaria, since absorption is excellent","B":"No partner drug is needed, because artesunate's long half-life covers the whole erythrocytic cycle"},"r":{"A":"Correct on route, timing, prodrug status and the reason for combination therapy — four separate facts the slides set out together.","E":"The deck explicitly says 'Do not continuous infusion'. The short half-life is precisely why a PARTNER drug, not an infusion, solves the problem.","C":"Inverted: artesunate is the prodrug and dihydroartemisinin is the ACTIVE metabolite.","D":"Severe malaria is defined partly by being unable to take oral medication, which is why the intravenous route is chosen.","B":"Artesunate's half-life is short, not long — hence mefloquine, lumefantrine or piperaquine as the partner."},"t":"Artesunate — how to give it","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Artesunate — how to give it</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Pregnancy is a severe-malaria criterion","s":"A 27-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant has confirmed falciparum malaria. She is alert and eating, and her blood tests show no organ dysfunction. How does the lecture classify and treat her?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Pregnancy is a severe-malaria criterion</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Complicated malaria — oral chloroquine plus primaquine","B":"Uncomplicated malaria — oral dihydroartemisinin/piperaquine","C":"Complicated malaria — intravenous artesunate","D":"Uncomplicated malaria — oral artesunate plus mefloquine","E":"Uncomplicated malaria — oral chloroquine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Right category, wrong drug: chloroquine + primaquine is for vivax/ovale, and complicated malaria is treated intravenously.","B":"Also an uncomplicated-falciparum option — ignores the pregnancy criterion.","C":"Correct by the lecture. Slide 10 lists pregnancy as a criterion of severe/complicated malaria on its own — no organ dysfunction is required — and the treatment given is artesunate by IV bolus.","D":"This is the regimen she would get if she were not pregnant.","E":"The trap for anyone who remembers 'chloroquine is safe in pregnancy': chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species, and pregnancy has already made this complicated."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Three weeks after successful artesunate treatment, a patient returns with new anaemia and rising creatinine. Blood films show no parasites. What is the most likely explanation?","o":{"C":"Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis — a recognised effect appearing about three weeks after treatment that can progress to acute kidney failure","E":"Relapse from hypnozoites, which requires primaquine","D":"Recrudescence from artesunate resistance, requiring a repeat course","A":"Chloroquine-induced retinopathy with secondary renal injury","B":"Sulfadiazine crystalluria"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the negative film is the key — the haemolysis is a drug effect on previously parasitised red cells, not ongoing infection.","E":"Relapse would show parasites on the film, and hypnozoites belong to P. vivax and P. ovale.","D":"Recrudescence also means parasites return to the blood; the film here is negative.","A":"Chloroquine's long-term ocular effect is blurred vision, and it is not implicated in this timeline.","B":"Crystalluria is a SULFADIAZINE effect from the toxoplasmosis regimen, not part of malaria treatment."},"t":"Delayed haemolysis","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L9_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Delayed haemolysis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Drug resistance = complicated malaria","s":"A 33-year-old man with uncomplicated falciparum malaria has taken artesunate plus mefloquine correctly for three days. He looks well and is eating, but his parasite count has not fallen, and drug resistance is suspected. What does the lecture advise next?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>Drug resistance = complicated malaria</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Switch to oral dihydroartemisinin plus mefloquine","B":"Switch to oral chloroquine plus primaquine","C":"Repeat the same oral combination for a further three days","D":"Treat as complicated malaria with intravenous artesunate","E":"Add primaquine to the current oral combination"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A fabricated pairing (DHA partners piperaquine) and still oral.","B":"The vivax/ovale regimen — wrong species and oral.","C":"A longer course of a failing regimen is not what the slide gives.","D":"Correct. Slide 11's bottom line: 'Drug resistance = Complicated malaria ► Artesunate (iv.)' — regardless of how well the patient looks.","E":"Primaquine clears vivax/ovale hypnozoites; it does nothing for resistant falciparum blood stages."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient with cerebral toxoplasmosis is started on pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine plus leucovorin. Which explanation of this three-drug regimen is correct?","o":{"D":"Sulfadiazine blocks DHPS and pyrimethamine blocks DHFR, two sequential steps of the same folate pathway; leucovorin is preformed folinic acid that rescues human marrow, which the parasite cannot use","C":"Pyrimethamine blocks DHPS and sulfadiazine blocks DHFR; leucovorin increases pyrimethamine absorption","E":"Leucovorin is given to prevent sulfadiazine crystalluria","B":"All three drugs act on the same enzyme, so the combination simply increases potency","A":"Leucovorin is an antiparasitic in its own right and is what actually clears the tissue cysts"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the rescue logic is the examinable part — supplying downstream folinic acid protects the host without protecting the parasite.","C":"The two enzymes are swapped. Sulfonamides are the DHPS agents; pyrimethamine is the DHFR inhibitor.","E":"Crystalluria is managed by vigorous hydration and urine alkalinisation to pH above 7.15, not by leucovorin.","B":"Two different enzymes in sequence is exactly the point — that is what makes the blockade sequential rather than merely additive.","A":"Leucovorin has no antiparasitic action; it is purely a host-protective supplement."},"t":"The antifolate pair","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The antifolate pair</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Praziquantel — meningitis-like reaction","s":"Two days after starting praziquantel for neurocysticercosis, a 35-year-old woman develops severe headache, neck stiffness and a seizure. Which interpretation and management match the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":9,"c":"<b>Praziquantel — meningitis-like reaction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mazzotti-like reaction — an antihistamine and a lower dose","B":"Direct neurotoxicity of the drug — halve the dose and continue","C":"Bacterial meningitis seeded from a ruptured cyst — antibiotics alone","D":"Meningitis-like reaction — corticosteroid plus an antiepileptic","E":"Drug-induced hepatitis — stop praziquantel and check liver tests"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The Mazzotti-like reaction is ivermectin's (to microfilariae), and the slide's answer there is a corticosteroid, not an antihistamine.","B":"Dose reduction does not address an inflammatory reaction to parasite death.","C":"The lecture frames this as a meningitis-like drug reaction, not bacterial infection.","D":"Correct. Slide 9 lists 'Meningitis-like reaction (seizure)' as a praziquantel adverse effect, managed with corticosteroids and antiepileptic drugs (dexamethasone 0.1 mg/kg/day IV). The inflammation is a response to the dying cysts.","E":"Hepatitis is an uncommon listed effect but would not produce neck stiffness and a seizure."},"img":"slides_i/L9_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient with neurocysticercosis is on dexamethasone and phenytoin. Which statement about praziquantel in this setting is correct?","o":{"D":"Both drugs are CYP3A4 INDUCERS and increase praziquantel's metabolism, lowering its concentration — which is one reason albendazole is preferred over praziquantel for neurocysticercosis","C":"Both are CYP3A4 inhibitors and raise praziquantel levels, risking toxicity","A":"Neither interacts with praziquantel; the interaction listed is with albendazole","E":"Dexamethasone should be stopped, because corticosteroids have no role in neurocysticercosis","B":"Phenytoin should be stopped, because antiepileptics are contraindicated with praziquantel"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the clinical irony is worth holding: the very drugs you must co-prescribe for the inflammation and the seizures undercut praziquantel.","C":"Induction, not inhibition — the slide names them under CYP3A4 inducers and shows metabolism increasing.","A":"The interaction box sits on the praziquantel slide specifically.","E":"Corticosteroids are explicitly part of the regimen, with dexamethasone 0.1 mg/kg/day intravenously.","B":"Antiepileptics are also explicitly part of the regimen — the patient is seizing."},"t":"Praziquantel and its CYP3A4 problem","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L9_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Praziquantel and its CYP3A4 problem</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Mechanisms — identify the drug by its role first","s":"Each option identifies a drug by its role in the lecture and then states a mechanism. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L9","sl":5,"c":"<b>Mechanisms — identify the drug by its role first</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The first-choice drug for neurocysticercosis opens GABA-gated chloride channels in the worm","B":"The first-choice drug for strongyloidiasis raises calcium permeability, causing spastic paralysis","C":"The partner of pyrimethamine for toxoplasmosis competes with PABA at dihydropteroate synthase","D":"The second-choice drug for neurocysticercosis inhibits heme polymerisation to hemozoin","E":"The drug used alone for P. malariae inhibits dihydrofolate reductase in the parasite"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Neurocysticercosis → albendazole, which inhibits microtubules, glucose uptake and fumarate reductase; chloride channels are ivermectin.","B":"Strongyloidiasis → ivermectin, which opens GABA-gated chloride channels; calcium → spastic paralysis is praziquantel.","C":"Correct. Pyrimethamine's partner = sulfadiazine, a sulfonamide competing with PABA at DHPS (slide 16).","D":"Second choice for neurocysticercosis → praziquantel, which raises calcium permeability; heme polymerisation is artesunate/chloroquine.","E":"P. malariae → chloroquine, which inhibits heme polymerisation; DHFR is pyrimethamine."},"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement explains ivermectin's safety margin and its contraindications?","o":{"C":"It opens GABA-gated chloride channels for which it has about 100 times more affinity than the human receptor, and it barely crosses the blood–brain barrier — which is why it is avoided in meningitis, where the barrier is disrupted, and why it synergises with GABA-A agonists","E":"It is safe because it is not absorbed from the gut at all and acts only in the lumen","B":"It is safe because humans have no GABA receptors; the contraindication in meningitis is unrelated to the BBB","D":"It crosses the blood–brain barrier freely, which is why it is effective in cerebral parasitic disease","A":"It is pregnancy category A and is the preferred agent in breastfeeding women"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the meningitis contraindication only makes sense once you link it to the barrier — an inflamed meninges lets the drug reach human GABA receptors.","E":"The deck states it is WELL ABSORBED from the gut; it is then excreted in the faeces.","B":"Humans certainly have GABA receptors — the 100-fold selectivity, not their absence, is the safety margin.","D":"Minimal BBB crossing is stated explicitly, and it is a safety feature, not an efficacy one.","A":"It is pregnancy category C and should NOT be used during breastfeeding."},"t":"Ivermectin — why it is safe in us","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Ivermectin — why it is safe in us</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Albendazole — high-fat meal","s":"A 29-year-old woman with neurocysticercosis is given the lecture's first-choice drug and told to take it first thing in the morning on an empty stomach with water. Her cysts respond poorly. Which advice, based on the drug's pharmacokinetics, would most likely have helped?","lec":"L9","sl":6,"c":"<b>Albendazole — high-fat meal</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Take each dose on an empty stomach at night","B":"Take each dose with an acidic fruit juice","C":"Take each dose with a low-fat, high-fibre meal","D":"Take each dose with a high-fat meal","E":"Take each dose with extra water to dilute the urine"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Fasting is the problem, whatever the time of day.","B":"No acid effect is described for albendazole.","C":"It is the fat that carries a poorly water-soluble drug across the gut wall.","D":"Correct. Albendazole has low water solubility and low oral bioavailability; slide 6: absorption is improved if taken with a high-fat meal (chloroquine carries the same footnote).","E":"Vigorous hydration is the sulfadiazine crystalluria manoeuvre and does nothing for absorption."},"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about albendazole matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"It is a prodrug with low water solubility and poor oral bioavailability whose absorption improves when taken with a HIGH-FAT meal; it is eliminated renally, and prolonged use can cause reversible leukopenia and alopecia as well as hepatotoxicity","A":"It should be taken on an empty stomach, since food reduces its absorption","B":"It is eliminated entirely by biliary excretion, so it is contraindicated in renal failure","E":"Its mechanism is to increase calcium permeability, causing spastic paralysis of the worm","C":"It is safe in pregnancy, because it acts only on parasite microtubules"},"r":{"D":"Correct on formulation, the fat-meal point, the route of elimination and all three adverse effects.","A":"The opposite — a HIGH-FAT meal is what rescues its poor bioavailability, which matters when you need systemic levels for tissue cysts.","B":"The deck states RENAL excretion.","E":"Calcium influx with spastic paralysis is PRAZIQUANTEL. Albendazole immobilises the worm by disrupting microtubules and starving it of ATP.","C":"It inhibits mitosis and impairs fetal development — the opposite of safe."},"t":"Albendazole — absorption and harms","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Albendazole — absorption and harms</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Albendazole — how it kills","s":"A woman from a pig-rearing village presents with seizures without fever, and imaging shows multiple brain cysts. She receives the lecture's drug of first choice. How does that drug kill the parasite?","lec":"L9","sl":6,"c":"<b>Albendazole — how it kills</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It blocks heme polymerisation, so toxic free heme destroys its membranes","B":"It opens GABA-gated chloride channels, paralysing the pharyngeal muscles","C":"It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, so the parasite cannot make purines","D":"It impairs glucose uptake and inhibits fumarate reductase, cutting ATP production","E":"It raises calcium permeability of the tegument, causing marked contraction and vacuolisation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Artesunate and chloroquine share this mechanism.","B":"Ivermectin's mechanism.","C":"Pyrimethamine's mechanism.","D":"Correct. Neurocysticercosis → albendazole. Slide 6: inhibits cytoplasmic microtubules and impairs glucose uptake → immobilisation; impairs glucose uptake and inhibits fumarate reductase → impaired ATP generation → death.","E":"Praziquantel's mechanism — the second-choice drug for this disease, which is the intended near-miss."},"img":"slides_i/L9_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of drug with mechanism is correct?","o":{"D":"Ivermectin opens GABA-gated CHLORIDE channels causing paralysis of pharyngeal muscles · Praziquantel increases CALCIUM permeability causing spastic paralysis and vacuolisation · Albendazole inhibits cytoplasmic microtubules and impairs glucose uptake and fumarate reductase","B":"Ivermectin increases calcium permeability · Praziquantel opens chloride channels · Albendazole inhibits heme polymerisation","A":"All three act by inhibiting heme polymerisation and generating toxic free heme","C":"Albendazole increases calcium permeability and praziquantel blocks glucose uptake","E":"Ivermectin inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, which is why leucovorin is co-administered"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The two ion mechanisms are worth contrasting directly: chloride in → the worm goes limp; calcium in → the worm clamps down and is dislodged.","B":"The two ions are swapped, and heme polymerisation is the ANTIMALARIAL mechanism.","A":"Heme polymerisation inhibition belongs to artesunate and chloroquine, not to the anthelmintics.","C":"Both halves are swapped between praziquantel and albendazole.","E":"DHFR inhibition is PYRIMETHAMINE, which is why leucovorin accompanies it — not ivermectin."},"t":"Two mechanisms, two paralyses","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Two mechanisms, two paralyses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Severe criteria — organ dysfunction on the labs","s":"A 40-year-old man with falciparum malaria is alert and taking tablets. Which one additional finding would, by itself, reclassify his malaria and move him to intravenous artesunate?","lec":"L9","sl":10,"c":"<b>Severe criteria — organ dysfunction on the labs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Headache with generalised myalgia","B":"Blood glucose of 2.1 mmol/L","C":"Temperature of 40.1 °C","D":"Rigors recurring every 48 hours","E":"A spleen palpable 3 cm below the costal margin"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Ordinary malaria symptoms, not a criterion.","B":"Correct. Hypoglycaemia is on slide 10's list of organ-dysfunction indicators (with anaemia, thrombocytopenia, jaundice, seizures, oliguria, altered consciousness); dysfunction in at least one system is enough.","C":"High fever is part of ordinary malaria and is not a severity criterion.","D":"Periodic rigors describe the paroxysm pattern, not organ dysfunction.","E":"Splenomegaly is an expected finding, not listed as a complication."},"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about treating UNCOMPLICATED malaria matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"P. falciparum is treated with an artemisinin combination such as artesunate + mefloquine, dihydroartemisinin/piperaquine or artesunate/lumefantrine; the other species get chloroquine, with primaquine added for P. vivax and P. ovale under G6PD precaution; and drug resistance is managed as complicated malaria with intravenous artesunate","D":"All species are treated with chloroquine alone; artemisinins are reserved for severe disease","A":"Primaquine is added for P. falciparum to eradicate its hypnozoites","E":"Mefloquine is given alone as monotherapy for falciparum malaria","C":"Resistant uncomplicated malaria is managed with a longer oral course of the same combination"},"r":{"B":"Correct across species, partner drugs, radical cure and the resistance rule.","D":"Chloroquine is for the non-falciparum species; falciparum needs an artemisinin combination.","A":"P. falciparum forms no hypnozoites. Primaquine is added for P. vivax and P. ovale.","E":"Mefloquine appears only as the PARTNER to artesunate, never alone.","C":"The deck states that drug resistance is treated as COMPLICATED malaria with intravenous artesunate."},"t":"Uncomplicated malaria by species","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Uncomplicated malaria by species</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"P. malariae — chloroquine alone","s":"A 50-year-old man has fever spikes every 72 hours. His film shows band-form trophozoites and rosette schizonts. He is well, eating, and has no organ dysfunction. Which treatment does the lecture give?","lec":"L9","sl":11,"c":"<b>P. malariae — chloroquine alone</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Chloroquine plus primaquine","B":"Chloroquine alone","C":"Artesunate plus mefloquine","D":"Dihydroartemisinin plus mefloquine","E":"Intravenous artesunate by bolus"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Primaquine is for the two hypnozoite-forming species (vivax, ovale); P. malariae has none.","B":"Correct. 72-hour (quartan) fever, band forms and rosette schizonts = P. malariae, uncomplicated. Slide 11: other species → chloroquine; primaquine is added only for vivax and ovale.","C":"The uncomplicated falciparum regimen.","D":"The fabricated pairing (DHA partners piperaquine).","E":"Reserved for severe/complicated malaria or resistance — nothing here qualifies."},"img":"slides_i/L9_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which patient with a positive blood film should be treated as SEVERE malaria according to the lecture?","o":{"B":"A pregnant woman with falciparum parasitaemia and no organ dysfunction","C":"A man with a parasitaemia of 0.1% who is afebrile and eating normally","E":"A child whose film shows P. malariae band forms and who has mild headache","D":"A traveller with P. vivax and a normal full blood count who tolerates tablets","A":"A man whose film is negative but whose rapid test is positive, with no symptoms"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Pregnancy is listed on the severe-malaria slide in its own right, alongside organ dysfunction and inability to take oral medication — no complication is required.","C":"Parasites without organ dysfunction, pregnancy or vomiting is uncomplicated malaria.","E":"P. malariae with a mild headache and no organ dysfunction is uncomplicated.","D":"A normal blood count and tolerated tablets fails every severity criterion.","A":"The slide requires malaria parasites DETECTED IN BLOOD as the starting point, and there is no organ dysfunction here."},"t":"What makes malaria severe","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L9_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>What makes malaria severe</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Toxoplasmosis regimen in breastfeeding","s":"A 29-year-old woman who is exclusively breastfeeding her 2-month-old is diagnosed with toxoplasmic encephalitis. Which statement about the standard regimen matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":15,"c":"<b>Toxoplasmosis regimen in breastfeeding</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pyrimethamine is unsafe in breastfeeding because of pancytopenia in the infant","B":"Neither drug is a concern once the infant is older than one month","C":"Both drugs are safe in breastfeeding, provided folinic acid is added to the regimen","D":"Sulfadiazine is safe in breastfeeding, but pyrimethamine makes the regimen unsuitable","E":"Pyrimethamine is safe in breastfeeding, but its partner makes the regimen unsuitable"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Pyrimethamine's pancytopenia is a patient effect; the slide calls it safe in breastfeeding.","B":"No age threshold is given; sulfadiazine is simply not for breastfeeding.","C":"Folinic acid protects the patient's marrow; it does nothing about sulfadiazine in breast milk.","D":"The two drugs' safety profiles are swapped.","E":"Correct. Slide 15: pyrimethamine is safe in pregnancy and breast feeding; slide 16: sulfadiazine should not be used during breast feeding. The lecturer's point: the regimen is unsuitable even though one of its drugs is fine."},"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about these drugs in pregnancy and lactation matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"Chloroquine is stated to be safe in pregnancy and pyrimethamine safe in pregnancy and breastfeeding, whereas sulfadiazine risks kernicterus in late pregnancy and should not be used while breastfeeding, ivermectin is category C and not for breastfeeding, and albendazole impairs fetal development","D":"All five drugs are contraindicated in pregnancy without exception","A":"Sulfadiazine is the safest of the group in late pregnancy because it is an old, well-studied drug","C":"Albendazole is safe in pregnancy and chloroquine is contraindicated","B":"Ivermectin is category A and pyrimethamine is contraindicated in breastfeeding"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and the contrast within the toxoplasmosis regimen itself is the sharp edge: pyrimethamine is safe in pregnancy while its partner sulfadiazine is not, late on.","D":"Chloroquine and pyrimethamine are both stated to be safe in pregnancy.","A":"Sulfadiazine carries the kernicterus warning in late pregnancy — the opposite of safest.","C":"Both halves inverted.","B":"Ivermectin is category C, and pyrimethamine is stated safe in breastfeeding."},"t":"Pregnancy and breastfeeding grid","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L9_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Pregnancy and breastfeeding grid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"G6PD — the second drug","s":"A 34-year-old man with HIV is started on the standard regimen for toxoplasmic encephalitis. He mentions he was once told he has G6PD deficiency. Which drug in his regimen carries a warning of haemolytic anaemia for him?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>G6PD — the second drug</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Folinic acid","B":"Sulfadiazine","C":"Primaquine","D":"Pyrimethamine","E":"Dexamethasone"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Folinic acid (leucovorin) is the rescue supplement, with no G6PD warning.","B":"Correct. Slide 16: 'Hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency' under sulfadiazine — the lecture's second G6PD drug after primaquine.","C":"Primaquine is the classic G6PD drug, but it is the vivax/ovale hypnozoite drug and not part of a toxoplasmosis regimen.","D":"Pyrimethamine causes megaloblastic anaemia within a reversible pancytopenia (folate lack), not haemolysis.","E":"Dexamethasone may be added for cerebral oedema; it carries no G6PD warning in this lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which precaution belongs specifically to sulfadiazine?","o":{"B":"Vigorous hydration with urine alkalinisation to a pH above 7.15, to prevent crystalluria — plus caution in G6PD deficiency because of haemolytic anaemia","C":"Taking the dose with a high-fat meal to improve absorption","E":"Giving it as a bolus over 1–2 minutes and never by infusion","A":"Co-administering leucovorin to prevent its own bone marrow suppression","D":"Avoiding it in meningitis because of blood–brain barrier disruption"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Both manoeuvres sit on the sulfadiazine slide, and the pH figure — above 7.15 — is the kind of number a stem will quote back at you.","C":"The high-fat meal point belongs to albendazole and chloroquine.","E":"Bolus over 1–2 minutes is intravenous ARTESUNATE.","A":"Leucovorin is paired with PYRIMETHAMINE’s marrow suppression, not sulfadiazine’s.","D":"The meningitis caution belongs to IVERMECTIN."},"t":"Sulfadiazine — the two manoeuvres","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L9_16.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Sulfadiazine — the two manoeuvres</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Pyrimethamine — GI intolerance","s":"A woman on pyrimethamine, sulfadiazine and folinic acid has cramping abdominal pain and vomits after each morning dose, which she takes before breakfast. Her blood count, urine and liver tests are normal. Which manoeuvre listed in the lecture addresses her problem?","lec":"L9","sl":15,"c":"<b>Pyrimethamine — GI intolerance</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Adding dexamethasone to each dose","B":"Taking pyrimethamine with a meal","C":"Alkalinising the urine to above pH 7.15","D":"Switching sulfadiazine to co-trimoxazole","E":"Doubling the dose of folinic acid"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Corticosteroids are for cerebral oedema or the praziquantel/ivermectin reactions, not GI intolerance.","B":"Correct. Slide 15: pyrimethamine GI intolerance — abdominal pain, vomiting → '+ Take with meal'. Normal counts exclude the marrow problem that folinic acid addresses.","C":"Urine alkalinisation prevents sulfadiazine crystalluria; her urine is normal.","D":"Co-trimoxazole is the alternative regimen, not the listed answer to GI intolerance.","E":"Folinic acid rescues the marrow, and her blood count is normal."},"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about chloroquine matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Its half-life is 20–60 days, it is 50–60% protein bound, absorption improves with a high-fat meal, it is excreted in urine, and long-term use can blur vision","D":"Its half-life is 20–45 minutes, matching artesunate's","C":"It is excreted unchanged in the faeces and is unaffected by food","E":"Its characteristic long-term adverse effect is alopecia with reversible leukopenia","A":"It acts by inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase"},"r":{"B":"Correct on all five points. The very long half-life is what allows weekly dosing and also why toxicity accumulates with chronic use.","D":"20–45 minutes is ARTESUNATE’s half-life. Chloroquine’s is measured in DAYS — a thousand-fold difference and an easy trap when the numbers look similar on the page.","C":"It is excreted in the URINE, and a high-fat meal improves absorption.","E":"Alopecia with reversible leukopenia is ALBENDAZOLE’s prolonged-use profile.","A":"Chloroquine inhibits heme polymerisation; DHFR inhibition is pyrimethamine."},"t":"Chloroquine pharmacokinetics","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L9_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Chloroquine pharmacokinetics</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Cerebral toxoplasmosis — regimen","s":"A 36-year-old man with advanced HIV has fever, headache, a new hemiparesis and bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy. MRI shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions, and serology is positive for a protozoan acquired from cat faeces. Which regimen does the lecture give?","lec":"L9","sl":14,"c":"<b>Cerebral toxoplasmosis — regimen</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine plus folinic acid","B":"Albendazole plus dexamethasone and an antiepileptic","C":"Pyrimethamine plus primaquine plus folinic acid","D":"Praziquantel plus dexamethasone","E":"Ivermectin plus a corticosteroid"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. HIV + ring-enhancing lesions + cat-borne protozoan = cerebral toxoplasmosis. Slide 14: pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + leucovorin (folinic acid) — NL alternates the two names. Co-trimoxazole is the alternative.","B":"The neurocysticercosis approach — cysts with a scolex in an afebrile patient, not ring-enhancing lesions in advanced HIV.","C":"Near-miss: primaquine is the vivax/ovale hypnozoite drug; pyrimethamine's partner is sulfadiazine.","D":"Praziquantel is a cestode/trematode drug (second choice for neurocysticercosis).","E":"Ivermectin is for strongyloidiasis, and barely enters the brain."},"img":"slides_i/L9_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about strongyloidiasis from the pharmacology lecture is correct?","o":{"A":"It may be asymptomatic, or cause malabsorption, pneumonia and ARDS, and a serpiginous urticarial or linear rash; ivermectin and albendazole are the treatments, and corticosteroids are flagged as a hazard","B":"It presents with bloody dysentery and rectal prolapse, and is treated with praziquantel","E":"Corticosteroids are part of the treatment, as they are in neurocysticercosis","D":"It is treated with chloroquine plus primaquine","C":"The rash is a fixed maculopapular eruption that does not migrate"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and the corticosteroid flag matters — it is the same hyperinfection hazard L1 warns about, arriving here from the pharmacology side.","B":"Bloody dysentery with rectal prolapse is TRICHURIS (L1), and praziquantel is for flukes and tapeworms.","E":"Corticosteroids belong to NEUROCYSTICERCOSIS treatment; in strongyloidiasis they are the danger.","D":"Chloroquine and primaquine are antimalarials.","C":"The rash is described as serpiginous and linear — it migrates, which is what larva currens means."},"t":"Strongyloidiasis — the clinical picture","sl":3,"img":"slides_i/L9_3.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Strongyloidiasis — the clinical picture</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L9|39","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"40","t":"Toxoplasmosis regimen in late pregnancy","s":"A woman at 36 weeks' gestation has acute toxoplasmosis. The neonatologist is worried about severe neonatal jaundice with brain injury if the standard regimen is continued to delivery. Which statement matches the lecture?","lec":"L9","sl":15,"c":"<b>Toxoplasmosis regimen in late pregnancy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pyrimethamine risks kernicterus at this stage, whereas sulfadiazine is stated safe","B":"Sulfadiazine risks kernicterus at this stage, whereas pyrimethamine is stated safe","C":"Both drugs are stated safe, because folinic acid protects the fetus","D":"Pyrimethamine causes fetal resorption, so sulfadiazine is used alone","E":"Sulfadiazine impairs fetal development by inhibiting mitosis in the fetus"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The two drugs are swapped.","B":"Correct. Kernicterus = bilirubin encephalopathy; slide 16 lists it for sulfadiazine in late pregnancy, and slide 15 says pyrimethamine is safe in pregnancy (2 clinical studies). Sulfonamides displace bilirubin from albumin.","C":"Folinic acid rescues the mother's marrow; it does not remove sulfadiazine's kernicterus warning.","D":"Fetal resorption is artesunate's warning.","E":"Mitosis inhibition with impaired fetal development is albendazole's."},"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient on pyrimethamine for toxoplasmosis has abdominal pain and vomiting, and the full blood count shows a falling haemoglobin, white cell and platelet count. What do the lecture's two listed manoeuvres address?","o":{"B":"Take the drug WITH a meal for the GI intolerance, and give leucovorin (folinic acid) for the reversible pancytopenia","A":"Take the drug on an empty stomach for the GI intolerance, and stop pyrimethamine permanently for the cytopenia","D":"Give leucovorin for the GI intolerance and alkalinise the urine for the cytopenia","E":"Give a corticosteroid for both effects","C":"Neither effect is described for pyrimethamine; both belong to sulfadiazine"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Each adverse effect on the slide is printed next to its own countermeasure — meal for the gut, leucovorin for the marrow.","A":"The slide says to take it WITH a meal, and the pancytopenia is reversible and managed with leucovorin rather than by abandoning the drug.","D":"The two countermeasures are swapped, and urine alkalinisation belongs to sulfadiazine.","E":"Corticosteroids play no part in managing either effect.","C":"Both are listed explicitly under pyrimethamine; sulfadiazine has its own separate profile."},"t":"Pyrimethamine — tolerability","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L9_15.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Pyrimethamine — tolerability</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233927138},{"id":"I|L10|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Naked virus — stability and the way out","s":"Alcohol hand rub failed to stop an outbreak of vomiting and watery diarrhoea in a nursery, and the agent passed readily from hand to hand and on shared toys. Which further property does this agent have?","lec":"L10","sl":11,"c":"<b>Naked virus — stability and the way out</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It spreads chiefly in large droplets and transfused blood","B":"It leaves the infected cell by lysing it","C":"It leaves the infected cell by budding from the membrane","D":"It takes its outer coat from the host cell membrane","E":"It loses infectivity rapidly once a surface has dried"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Slide 11 lists large droplets, secretions, transplants and transfusion as the spread pattern of enveloped viruses such as influenza and the coronaviruses — the opposite column.","B":"Alcohol gel failing, plus fomite and hand-to-hand spread, means the agent has no lipid envelope to dissolve — it is a naked virus. Slide 33 gives the matching exit route: naked viruses are released by host cell lysis, enveloped ones by budding.","C":"Budding is the enveloped route (slide 33). It is non-cytolytic and is exactly what this agent does not do.","D":"An envelope is host membrane acquired on the way out. If it had one, 70% ethanol would have dissolved it and the hand rub would have worked.","E":"Drying is one of the four things (acid, detergent, drying, heat) that destroy enveloped viruses. Naked viruses survive drying, which is why fomites and toys transmit them."},"img":"slides_i/L10_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does a NAKED virus survive the gastrointestinal tract while an enveloped virus usually does not?","o":{"C":"Naked viruses carry enzymes that neutralise stomach acid","B":"The protein capsid resists acid, bile detergents and digestive enzymes, whereas a lipid envelope is dissolved by them","D":"Naked viruses replicate faster than the gut can clear them","A":"Enveloped viruses cannot bind receptors on gut epithelium","E":"Naked viruses have a double-stranded genome, which is chemically more stable"},"r":{"C":"No virus carries acid-neutralising enzymes.","B":"Correct, and this one fact drives the whole transmission table. The envelope is a LIPID bilayer taken from the host membrane, so acid, bile salts, detergents and drying destroy it; a protein capsid does not care. That is why faecal–oral gastroenteritis viruses are naked and respiratory/blood-borne viruses can afford an envelope.","D":"Replication speed is irrelevant — the particle is destroyed before it reaches a cell.","A":"Receptor binding is not the limiting step; particle survival is.","E":"Strandedness confers no environmental stability. The practical corollary: enveloped viruses are killed by 70% alcohol gel, naked ones much less so."},"t":"Naked vs enveloped","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L10_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Naked vs enveloped</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Poxvirus — size, site, packaged enzyme","s":"Electron microscopy of a skin lesion shows a brick-shaped double-stranded DNA virus, the largest of the human viruses, replicating in factories within the cytoplasm. Which consequence follows from where it replicates?","lec":"L10","sl":28,"c":"<b>Poxvirus — size, site, packaged enzyme</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its genome integrates before transcription can begin","B":"Its transcripts are spliced by the host spliceosome","C":"The particle must carry a reverse transcriptase","D":"Its genome is copied by the host nuclear DNA polymerase","E":"The particle must carry its own DNA polymerase"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Integration is the retroviral step (and the reason HIV breaks the RNA cytoplasm rule). Poxvirus does not integrate.","B":"Splicing is a nuclear process; a cytoplasmic virus cannot rely on it. This is closer to the reason influenza must visit the nucleus.","C":"Reverse transcriptase belongs to Baltimore classes VI (Retroviridae) and VII (Hepadnaviridae), not to poxvirus.","D":"Host DNA polymerase is in the nucleus, which is precisely the compartment this virus cannot use — that is why it is the exception to the nuclear rule.","E":"Brick-shaped, largest, dsDNA identifies poxvirus. The lecturer's one-sentence chain: it is too large to enter the nucleus, so it replicates in the cytoplasm, so it must bring the DNA polymerase it cannot borrow from the nucleus. Slide 28 and the summary slide 55 print the exception itself."},"img":"slides_i/L10_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about the SITE of viral genome replication is correct?","o":{"E":"DNA viruses replicate in the nucleus EXCEPT poxvirus; RNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm EXCEPT influenza and the retroviruses","C":"All DNA viruses replicate in the nucleus and all RNA viruses in the cytoplasm, with no exceptions","B":"All viruses replicate in the nucleus because they need host polymerases","A":"DNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm; RNA viruses replicate in the nucleus","D":"The site depends on whether the virus is enveloped"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The summary slide states the rule and its exceptions in exactly this form. Poxvirus is big enough to carry its own DNA polymerase, so it stays in the cytoplasm; influenza steals host nuclear capped primers; retroviruses must integrate, which requires the nucleus.","C":"It is the EXCEPTIONS the examiner asks about — a rule with no exceptions is the giveaway that the option is wrong.","B":"Most RNA viruses never enter the nucleus; they bring their own RdRp.","A":"Exactly reversed.","D":"The envelope has nothing to do with replication site."},"t":"Where each genome replicates","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L10_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Where each genome replicates</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Polarity — which purified genome is infectious","s":"Purified genome from virus X is transfected into cells with no viral protein present and yields infectious progeny. Purified genome from virus Y, handled the same way, yields none. Which pair of genome descriptions fits X and Y?","lec":"L10","sl":14,"c":"<b>Polarity — which purified genome is infectious</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"X positive-sense single-stranded RNA; Y negative-sense single-stranded RNA","B":"X single-stranded DNA; Y positive-sense single-stranded RNA","C":"X negative-sense single-stranded RNA; Y positive-sense single-stranded RNA","D":"X double-stranded RNA; Y positive-sense single-stranded RNA","E":"X negative-sense single-stranded RNA; Y double-stranded DNA"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"A (+)ssRNA genome is itself mRNA — a ribosome reads it the moment it is in the cytoplasm, so the naked genome alone is infectious. A (−)ssRNA genome is the complement of mRNA and is unreadable until the RdRp carried inside the virion copies it, so purified (−) genome does nothing on its own.","B":"The second half is false — (+)ssRNA genomes are infectious alone. Parvoviridae ssDNA is also the wrong contrast to draw; the lecture's contrast is (+) versus (−) sense RNA.","C":"Reversed. This is the commonest way the polarity question is failed.","D":"Double-stranded RNA also needs the virion-packaged RdRp (one per segment in Reoviridae), so it is not infectious alone; and the second half of the pair is false, since (+)ssRNA is infectious.","E":"The first half is wrong for the same reason as B, and dsDNA is not the non-infectious case the lecture contrasts."},"img":"slides_i/L10_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A virus has a (+) single-stranded RNA genome. What follows directly from that?","o":{"D":"The genome must be reverse transcribed before any protein is made","C":"The virion must carry its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase in the particle","A":"The genome acts as mRNA and can be translated immediately, so naked genome alone is infectious","B":"The genome must enter the nucleus to be transcribed by host RNA polymerase II","E":"The genome is translated only after it is converted to double-stranded DNA"},"r":{"D":"Reverse transcription is Baltimore VI (retroviruses) and VII (HBV) only.","C":"That is (−)ssRNA. A negative-sense genome cannot be read, so the virion MUST package RdRp — influenza, rabies, measles.","A":"Correct. (+)ssRNA reads like mRNA, so the ribosome translates it the moment it is uncoated — which also means the purified genome on its own is infectious. Picornaviridae and Coronaviridae work this way.","B":"Nuclear transcription by host Pol II is the DNA-virus route.","E":"Again retroviruses only."},"t":"Polarity of the genome","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L10_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Polarity of the genome</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Sorting a virus by genome type (HHAPPPPy)","s":"A child is seen with common warts on the fingers. Which other illness in this list is caused by a virus whose genome is the same type of nucleic acid as the wart virus?","lec":"L10","sl":16,"c":"<b>Sorting a virus by genome type (HHAPPPPy)</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pharyngoconjunctival fever","B":"Hand, foot and mouth disease","C":"Chikungunya fever","D":"Rabies","E":"Rotavirus gastroenteritis"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Warts are caused by human papillomavirus, one of the seven DNA families in HHAPPPPy (Herpes, Hepadna, Adeno, Pox, Papilloma, Polyoma, Parvo). Pharyngoconjunctival fever is adenovirus — also in the mnemonic, so also a DNA virus.","B":"Hand, foot and mouth disease is enterovirus, a picornavirus — (+)ssRNA.","C":"Chikungunya virus is a togavirus — an RNA family.","D":"Rabies virus is a rhabdovirus — (−)ssRNA.","E":"Rotavirus is the Reoviridae member, and the one RNA exception in that it is double-stranded RNA. Still RNA, not DNA."},"img":"slides_i/L10_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which set contains ONLY DNA viruses?","o":{"D":"Hepadnaviridae, Coronaviridae, Rhabdoviridae, Polyomaviridae","E":"Herpesviridae, Orthomyxoviridae, Adenoviridae, Retroviridae","C":"Poxviridae, Reoviridae, Picornaviridae, Parvoviridae","B":"Adenoviridae, Flaviviridae, Togaviridae, Papillomaviridae","A":"Herpesviridae, Hepadnaviridae, Adenoviridae, Poxviridae, Papillomaviridae, Polyomaviridae, Parvoviridae"},"r":{"D":"Corona and Rhabdo are RNA. Learn the rule that goes with the list: all RNA viruses are ssRNA EXCEPT Reoviridae, which is dsRNA.","E":"Orthomyxo (influenza) and Retro are RNA.","C":"Reo and Picorna are RNA.","B":"Flavi and Toga are RNA — these are the arboviruses of L14.","A":"Correct — and the lecture gives you the mnemonic on the slide: HHAPPPPy (Herpes, Hepadna, Adeno, Pox, Papilloma, Polyoma, Parvo). Everything NOT on that list is an RNA virus."},"t":"The DNA virus list","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L10_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The DNA virus list</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Permissiveness — why infection can be abortive","s":"A cell line carries the receptor for a virus and takes the particle up, yet no progeny virus is produced and the cell survives. Which explanation matches the lecture?","lec":"L10","sl":35,"c":"<b>Permissiveness — why infection can be abortive</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The nucleic acid type of the virus decides which tissue it enters","B":"The particle entered by phagocytosis instead of by fusion","C":"The cell lacks the proteases and transcription factors the cycle needs","D":"Release by budding spares the cell, so no progeny are formed","E":"Neutralising antibody had already blocked the attachment protein"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"This is the trap keyed against in BM30 Q46 — tropism is set by receptors and cellular enzymes, never by whether the genome is DNA or RNA.","B":"Fusion, endocytosis and phagocytosis are alternative penetration routes (slide 24); the lecturer said to know the three names, not to attach them to outcomes. The route does not decide whether the cycle completes.","C":"Slide 35 lists two determinants of infectivity at cell level: host receptors and cellular proteins — proteases, transcription factors, other enzymes. The receptor only opens the door. Without the internal proteins the genome cannot be expressed, so the cycle starts and stops: abortive infection in a non-permissive cell (slide 34).","D":"Budding does spare the cell, but budding means progeny are being released. Here none are made at all, which is a different outcome.","E":"The stem states the particle was taken up, so attachment was not blocked. Antibody against the attachment protein prevents step 1 altogether."},"img":"slides_i/L10_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What determines the TROPISM of a virus — the tissue it can infect?","o":{"C":"Matching between the viral attachment protein and a receptor on the host cell, plus host proteases and transcription factors that permit the cycle to finish","D":"The nucleic acid type of the virus — DNA viruses infect epithelium, RNA viruses infect nerve","E":"Whether the virus is enveloped or naked","B":"The size of the virion relative to the capillary pore size of the organ","A":"The route by which the patient acquired the virus"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Tropism is decided first at ATTACHMENT — no receptor, no entry — and then by whether the cell supplies the proteases, transcription factors and other machinery the virus needs. Both halves are on the slide, and past papers have tested both.","D":"Genome type says nothing about which tissue is infected. This exact wrong option has been offered in a real paper.","E":"Naked and enveloped viruses are found in every tissue.","B":"Particle size does not select tissue.","A":"Route determines where infection STARTS, not which cells are permissive."},"t":"Tropism","sl":23,"img":"slides_i/L10_23.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Tropism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Cold-adapted nasal vaccine — which mutation","s":"A live intranasal influenza vaccine strain multiplies well in the nasopharynx at about 25 °C but fails to multiply in the lower airway at 37 °C, so it immunises without causing pneumonia. Which change in the vaccine virus does this describe?","lec":"L10","sl":40,"c":"<b>Cold-adapted nasal vaccine — which mutation</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Phenotypic mixing","B":"Attenuating mutation","C":"Drug-resistant mutation","D":"Conditional lethal mutation","E":"Antigenic variation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Phenotypic mixing is an interaction between two viruses in one cell, not a mutation in one virus, and it lasts a single generation.","B":"An attenuating mutation makes a variant milder under all conditions — the Omicron example. It is the distractor students pick here, because a vaccine strain is obviously weakened; the discriminator is that this weakness appears only at one temperature.","C":"A drug-resistant mutation modifies the target of an antiviral, the HIV example on slide 40. No drug is involved here.","D":"A temperature-sensitive mutant grows under permissive conditions (the cool nose) and produces no infectious virus under restrictive ones (the warm lung). That conditional behaviour is the definition on slide 40, and the cold-adapted nasal flu vaccine is the lecturer's own example of it, built on the 25 °C nose versus 37 °C lung gradient.","E":"Antigenic variation, or drift, alters a surface protein so pre-existing antibody no longer inhibits the virus. Nothing in the stem concerns antibody escape."},"img":"slides_i/L10_40.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Match the change to its name: a surface protein alters gradually so that existing antibody no longer inhibits the virus.","o":{"A":"Complementation","E":"Reassortment (antigenic shift)","B":"Attenuating mutation","C":"Conditional lethal mutation","D":"Antigenic variation (antigenic drift)"},"r":{"A":"Complementation is one virus supplying a gene product for another — not a mutation at all.","E":"SHIFT is the sudden swap of whole genome SEGMENTS between two viruses infecting one cell — that is reassortment, and it causes pandemics.","B":"An attenuating mutation makes the virus cause LESS serious disease.","C":"A conditional lethal mutant fails to grow only under a restrictive condition, e.g. temperature.","D":"Correct. DRIFT is gradual point mutation of a surface protein — immune evasion, and the reason the influenza vaccine is reformulated yearly. RNA viruses drift faster than DNA viruses because RdRp has no proofreading."},"t":"Mutation vocabulary","sl":39,"img":"slides_i/L10_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Mutation vocabulary</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Interaction that changes the coat only","s":"Progeny from a cell infected with two viruses are neutralised by antiserum raised against virus B, yet the generation after them is neutralised by antiserum against virus A instead. Which interaction produced the first generation?","lec":"L10","sl":43,"c":"<b>Interaction that changes the coat only</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Complementation","B":"Phenotypic mixing","C":"Antigenic variation","D":"Recombination","E":"Reassortment"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Complementation is a helper virus supplying a missing gene product so a defective virus can grow. It rescues replication rather than reassigning the coat.","B":"Antiserum sees the coat. Progeny neutralised as B but breeding true as A means an A genome was packaged inside capsid proteins specified by B — a protein-level event that changes no gene, so it reverts in one generation.","C":"Antigenic variation is accumulated mutation of a surface protein within one virus lineage; it needs no second virus and does not reverse.","D":"Recombination is intramolecular genetic exchange between two viruses, or virus and host. Again genetic and therefore heritable.","E":"Reassortment swaps whole genome segments between two segmented viruses. The change is genetic, so it is inherited and would not revert."},"img":"slides_i/L10_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Two different viruses infect the same cell. Progeny particles are produced that carry the GENOME of virus A inside the CAPSID of virus B, but their offspring revert to normal virus A. What is this?","o":{"B":"Phenotypic mixing","A":"Recombination","D":"Reassortment","E":"Complementation","C":"Antigenic drift"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the give-away is that the change is NOT heritable — the genome itself was never altered, only the coat it happened to be packaged in. One generation later it is gone.","A":"Recombination is genuine intramolecular exchange of nucleic acid, so it IS heritable.","D":"Reassortment swaps whole segments and is also heritable — and it needs a SEGMENTED genome.","E":"Complementation is a helper virus supplying a missing gene product to a defective virus; again nothing is inherited, but the defect is functional, not structural.","C":"Drift is point mutation of surface protein."},"t":"Interactions between viruses","sl":41,"img":"slides_i/L10_41.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Interactions between viruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Subviral agents — telling the three apart","s":"A transmissible spongiform encephalopathy follows exposure to surgical instruments that had been through routine disinfection, and no nucleic acid can be recovered from the diseased brain. Which description fits the agent?","lec":"L10","sl":36,"c":"<b>Subviral agents — telling the three apart</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A particle with nucleic acid and protein that needs a helper","B":"An enveloped RNA virus that integrates into neuronal DNA","C":"An infectious protein carrying no genes at all","D":"A circular RNA molecule with no protein coat","E":"A double-stranded DNA virus that replicates in the cytoplasm"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"That is a defective virus, which has nucleic acid and protein but lacks a function and needs a helper virus, as hepatitis D needs hepatitis B.","B":"Integration into host DNA is the retroviral strategy; it also requires a genome, which the stem excludes.","C":"Slide 36 defines a prion as an infectious protein molecule without any genes, causing the slow disease transmissible spongiform encephalopathy. No genome explains both findings in the stem: nothing for a nucleic acid assay to detect, and nothing that ordinary disinfection targets. Prion is also named in the licensing criteria printed on slide 4.","D":"That is a viroid — a single molecule of circular RNA with no coat and no envelope. It does have a genome, so nucleic acid would be recoverable.","E":"Cytoplasmic dsDNA replication describes poxvirus, which has a large genome and is not linked to spongiform disease."},"img":"slides_i/L10_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about atypical and subviral agents is correct?","o":{"D":"Bacteriophages are a subviral agent because they lack a genome","A":"A viroid is a small protein with no nucleic acid","B":"A prion is a naked circular RNA molecule","E":"A defective virus replicates normally but cannot assemble a capsid","C":"A defective virus has nucleic acid and protein but needs a HELPER virus to replicate — hepatitis D virus needs hepatitis B"},"r":{"D":"Bacteriophages are complete viruses that infect bacteria, and they are the most abundant members of the human virome.","A":"A viroid is naked circular RNA with NO protein; the protein-only agent is the prion.","B":"Reversed — prion = protein, viroid = RNA.","E":"The defect is in replication itself, not assembly alone.","C":"Correct, and HDV is the example worth remembering clinically: it cannot make its own envelope, so it borrows HBsAg — which is why HBV vaccination also prevents hepatitis D."},"t":"Subviral agents","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L10_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Subviral agents</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Eclipse, window and incubation","s":"Six hours after a synchronised infection, cultured cells are broken open and their contents titrated: no infectious virus can be recovered although viral protein is being made. The same patient's antigen and antibody tests would stay negative for some weeks. Which term names the six-hour finding, and which names the serological interval?","lec":"L10","sl":22,"c":"<b>Eclipse, window and incubation</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Incubation period; window period","B":"Latent infection; incubation period","C":"Window period; eclipse phase","D":"Eclipse phase; window period","E":"Eclipse phase; incubation period"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The incubation period runs from exposure to the onset of symptoms and is measured on the patient, so it fits neither half.","B":"Latent infection is persistence of the genome with no progeny made at all, which is not what a six-hour synchronised culture is showing.","C":"Reversed. The window period is defined by test negativity in a person, not by titration of cell contents.","D":"The eclipse phase is measured on the virus's assembly line: after uncoating, the parts exist but nothing complete can be recovered from the cell. The window period is measured on the laboratory: standard tests cannot yet detect antigen or antibody.","E":"The first half is right, but the serological interval is the window period; incubation is about symptoms."},"img":"slides_i/L10_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"What is the ECLIPSE phase of a viral growth cycle?","o":{"C":"The time taken for the virus to travel from the portal of entry to its target organ","A":"The interval between infection and the first detectable antibody","E":"The period during which the patient is infectious but has no symptoms","D":"The interval after entry during which no infectious virion can be recovered from the cell, because the genome has been uncoated and progeny are not yet assembled","B":"The phase in which the viral genome is integrated and silent"},"r":{"C":"That is dissemination.","A":"That is the WINDOW PERIOD, which is a serology concept and the reason early HIV antibody tests miss acute infection. The lecture puts these two side by side precisely because students conflate them.","E":"That describes asymptomatic shedding.","D":"Correct — 'eclipse' because if you lyse the cell during it you find NOTHING infectious; the genome exists but is naked and unassembled.","B":"That is latency."},"t":"Eclipse and window","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L10_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Eclipse and window</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"What dominates the healthy human virome","s":"Metagenomic sequencing is performed on faeces from a healthy adult and the viral reads are classified. Which group is expected to account for most of them?","lec":"L10","sl":47,"c":"<b>What dominates the healthy human virome</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Enteric picornaviruses from the environment","B":"Herpesviruses reactivating from latency","C":"Plant viruses swallowed with food","D":"Endogenous viral elements of human DNA","E":"Bacteriophages of the order Caudovirales"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Picornaviridae appear in the gastrointestinal panel of slide 49, and they expand in some disease states, but they never outweigh the phages.","B":"Herpesviruses are genuine members of the eukaryotic fraction and are listed at several body sites, but that whole fraction is the small minority.","C":"Plant viruses are included in the eukaryotic category on slide 47 and are diet-derived, not dominant.","D":"Endogenous viral elements are counted as part of the virome on slide 47, yet they are one of its four components rather than the dominant one.","E":"Slide 47 divides the virome into prokaryotic viruses, eukaryotic viruses, endogenous viral elements and viruses from the environment, and puts more than 90% of the community in the bacteriophage group. Slide 49 then annotates Caudovirales in the gastrointestinal panel as the most abundant virus."},"img":"slides_i/L10_47.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the human VIROME is correct?","o":{"E":"It is the whole collection of viruses in and on the body, and bacteriophages of the order Caudovirales are its most abundant members","D":"It consists only of eukaryotic viruses actively causing disease","C":"It is identical at every body site and stable from birth to old age","A":"It has no interaction with the immune system","B":"It is composed mainly of DNA viruses that have integrated into human chromosomes"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Most of the virome is bacteriophage, not human virus, and Caudovirales dominate — which is exactly the fact a recent past paper turned into a clinical stem about IBD.","D":"The virome includes phages, plant viruses from food and quiescent human viruses.","C":"It differs sharply by body site and changes with age.","A":"It helps equilibrate innate and adaptive responses and activates antiviral IFNs and IL-22.","B":"Endogenous retroviral elements exist but are not the bulk of the virome."},"t":"Virome","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L10_47.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Virome</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q42","t":"Enteric virus characteristics","s":"An outbreak of fever, nausea and watery diarrhoea occurs in a school. Stool examination shows a (+)ssRNA virus. Which property does this virus have?","lec":"L10","sl":11,"c":"<b>Enteric virus characteristics</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q43 · BM32 SUM1 Q42 · BM30 Formative virology Q2 · BM28 Q18 — the gastroenteritis-outbreak stem, asked in four papers</span>","o":{"C":"It is readily inactivated by any detergent","E":"It is rapidly destroyed under ordinary environmental conditions","B":"It uses the host cell's DNA polymerase for genome replication","D":"It carries its own DNA polymerase","A":"It is NAKED, so it resists gastric acid and digestive enzymes, and it replicates in the cytoplasm"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Detergents dissolve LIPID envelopes. A naked virus has no envelope to dissolve, which is why alcohol gel is comparatively poor against it.","E":"The opposite: naked viruses are environmentally stable, which is why they spread so efficiently in schools, cruise ships and nursing homes.","B":"An RNA virus cannot use a DNA polymerase at all — the BM30 answer key writes this out explicitly as the reason.","D":"Same error: no DNA polymerase is involved.","A":"Correct, and it is two deductions in one. GI symptoms ⇒ the particle survived the gut ⇒ NAKED. And an RNA genome ⇒ replication in the CYTOPLASM using RdRp, not the nucleus."},"img":"slides_i/L10_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q43","BM30 virology Q2","BM28 Q18"]},{"id":"I|L10|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q45","t":"Reassortment and pandemics","s":"A novel ENVELOPED virus with a NEGATIVE-sense SEGMENTED RNA genome appears, and within months is declared a pandemic. Which genetic change is responsible?","lec":"L10","sl":42,"c":"<b>Reassortment and pandemics</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q45 · BM29 SUM1 Q44 · BM28 Q5 — 'which genetic change leads to pandemics?', asked in three cohorts with the same five options</span>","o":{"C":"Complementation","D":"Recombination","B":"Reassortment","E":"Antigenic variation","A":"Conditional lethal mutation"},"a":"B","r":{"C":"Complementation is a helper virus supplying a gene product — nothing heritable happens.","D":"Recombination is intramolecular exchange and does not require segments; it drives gradual change, not a pandemic.","B":"Correct. The word SEGMENTED is the whole question: only viruses with segmented genomes — influenza and the reoviruses — can reassort, swapping entire segments when two strains infect one cell. That is antigenic SHIFT, and shift is what makes a pandemic because no one has pre-existing immunity.","E":"Antigenic variation is DRIFT: gradual, causes seasonal epidemics, not pandemics. This is the planted near-miss, because drift and shift are both 'antigenic change'.","A":"Conditional lethal mutants are laboratory tools."},"img":"slides_i/L10_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q44","BM28 Q5"]},{"id":"I|L10|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q41","t":"Viral attachment protein","s":"Which part of an enveloped virus is both the part that mediates ENTRY into the cell and the rational target for a vaccine?","lec":"L10","sl":23,"c":"<b>Viral attachment protein</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q44 (answer recorded as Viral Attachment Protein) · BM32 SUM1 Q41</span>","o":{"D":"The spike — the viral attachment protein on the envelope","A":"The genome","E":"The capsid","C":"The matrix protein beneath the envelope","B":"The viral polymerase carried in the particle"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The viral attachment protein (VAP), seen as the spike, binds the host receptor — so it determines tropism AND it is the surface the antibody must block. Neutralising antibody is by definition antibody against the VAP.","A":"The genome is inside and never seen by antibody.","E":"In an ENVELOPED virus the capsid is internal, so antibody against it does not neutralise. In a naked virus the capsid IS the outer surface and does carry the attachment site — that distinction is what the examiner is checking.","C":"Matrix protein is internal and structural.","B":"The packaged polymerase is internal."},"img":"slides_i/L10_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q44"]},{"id":"I|L10|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q44","t":"Negative-sense RNA virus","s":"A nasopharyngeal swab yields an ENVELOPED virus with a (−)ssRNA genome. Which property does it have?","lec":"L10","sl":29,"c":"<b>Negative-sense RNA virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q44 — nasopharyngeal swab grows an enveloped (−)ssRNA virus; which property follows?</span>","o":{"B":"Its genome integrates into the host chromosome","C":"Its genome can be translated directly into viral protein","D":"Reverse transcriptase inhibitors block its replication","E":"Genome replication occurs in both the nucleus and the cytoplasm","A":"The virion contains its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Integration is a retrovirus property.","C":"Direct translation is the (+)ssRNA property — the exact opposite.","D":"Reverse transcriptase belongs to retroviruses (Baltimore VI) and HBV (VII).","E":"Influenza is the odd (−)ssRNA virus that uses the nucleus, but 'both nucleus and cytoplasm' is not the general property being asked for.","A":"Correct, and it is forced by the polarity. A (−) strand cannot be read by a ribosome, and the cell has no enzyme that copies RNA into RNA, so the virus MUST bring RdRp in the particle. Its naked genome is therefore NOT infectious."},"img":"slides_i/L10_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L10|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q89","t":"Retrovirus — integration","s":"A 27-year-old man has flu-like symptoms. Testing shows a viral genome INTEGRATED into the chromosomes of his lymphocytes. Which description fits this virus?","lec":"L10","sl":30,"c":"<b>Retrovirus — integration</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q89 — flu-like illness; viral genome found INTEGRATED into the chromosome of lymphocytes</span>","o":{"A":"A DNA virus whose genome encodes RdRp","C":"An RNA virus whose genome replicates in the cytoplasm without a DNA intermediate","D":"A DNA virus whose genome replicates in the host nucleus","B":"An RNA virus that carries RdRp","E":"An RNA virus that carries reverse transcriptase"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"No DNA virus encodes RdRp.","C":"No DNA intermediate means no integration — that is the ordinary (+)ssRNA strategy.","D":"A DNA virus in the nucleus is plausible-sounding, but the stem's point is the RNA-to-DNA step; and the classic integrating lymphotropic viruses here are retroviruses.","B":"RdRp copies RNA to RNA and can never produce the DNA needed to integrate.","E":"Correct. Integration into a host chromosome requires a DNA copy, and an RNA virus can only make one with REVERSE TRANSCRIPTASE. RNA genome + lymphocytes + integration = a retrovirus (HIV or HTLV-1)."},"img":"slides_i/L10_30.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q43","t":"Uncoating blocked","s":"Drug X has antiviral activity because it competes with the CAPSID for binding to the viral genome. What is the consequence for the replication cycle?","lec":"L10","sl":25,"c":"<b>Uncoating blocked</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q43 — a drug competes with the capsid for the viral genome; what accumulates?</span>","o":{"D":"Accumulation of viral non-structural protein outside the cell","C":"Increased cellular attachment","A":"Increased viral release","E":"Accumulation of viral genome inside the cell, because progeny cannot be packaged","B":"Increased production of viral capsid protein"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Non-structural proteins act inside the cell; they are not exported.","C":"Attachment happens before this block and is unaffected.","A":"Release falls to near zero, not rises.","E":"Correct. Capsid binding to genome IS the assembly step. Block it and everything upstream still works — entry, transcription, genome replication — so copies pile up INTRACELLULARLY with no way to leave.","B":"Capsid synthesis is unchanged — the drug stops the capsid from doing its job, not from being made."},"img":"slides_i/L10_25.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q90","t":"Attenuating mutation","s":"The Omicron variant of SARS-CoV-2 was reported to cause milder disease than earlier variants. Which mechanism reduced its virulence?","lec":"L10","sl":40,"c":"<b>Attenuating mutation</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q90 — why the Omicron variant caused milder disease</span>","o":{"D":"Attenuating mutation","B":"Phenotypic mixing","C":"Antigenic variation","E":"Conditional lethal mutation","A":"Drug-resistance mutation"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and the definition is on the slide almost word for word: an attenuating mutation produces variants that cause LESS SERIOUS disease. The same principle underlies live attenuated vaccines.","B":"Phenotypic mixing changes the coat for a single generation and is not heritable, so it cannot define a variant.","C":"Antigenic variation is immune ESCAPE — Omicron did that too, but escape explains transmissibility, not reduced severity. This is the planted near-miss.","E":"Conditional lethal mutants only fail under restrictive laboratory conditions.","A":"Drug resistance says nothing about virulence."},"img":"slides_i/L10_40.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q48","t":"Virome and IBD","s":"A 43-year-old man is diagnosed with inflammatory bowel disease. What change in the gut VIROME would be expected?","lec":"L10","sl":53,"c":"<b>Virome and IBD</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q48 — a 43-year-old diagnosed with IBD; what happens to the gut virome?</span>","o":{"A":"Expansion of Microviridae with a fall in Caudovirales","C":"Expansion of Caudovirales with a fall in Microviridae, and reduced overall diversity","B":"Complete loss of all bacteriophages","E":"A rise in eukaryotic plant viruses only","D":"No change — the virome is unrelated to bowel disease"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Reversed. Microviridae expansion is not the IBD signature.","C":"Correct. The lecture's dysbiosis slide states it directly: expansion of Caudovirales drives TLR-9-dependent activation of CD4+ T cells and prolonged inflammation in IBD, alongside a fall in diversity.","B":"Phages are expanded, not lost — this is a shift in composition, not an elimination.","E":"Plant viruses come from diet and are not the driver here.","D":"The whole reason virome is in this lecture is that its composition tracks with disease — ulcerative colitis, IBD and C. difficile infection are the examples given."},"img":"slides_i/L10_53.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q1","t":"What defines a virus","s":"Which description most likely identifies a VIRUS?","lec":"L10","sl":6,"c":"<b>What defines a virus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q1 (examiner-written) — which pathogen is most likely to be the virus?</span>","o":{"E":"Contains ribosomes, endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi complex","B":"Can be observed under a light microscope","C":"Can be cultured on blood agar","D":"Has both DNA and RNA as its genome","A":"Depends entirely on a host cell for replication"},"a":"A","r":{"E":"Organelles are the mark of a cell, which is exactly what a virus is not.","B":"At 20–300 nm viruses are below the resolution of light microscopy; you need electron microscopy (Mimivirus is the curiosity exception).","C":"Viruses cannot grow on cell-free media — they need cell culture, embryonated eggs or animals.","D":"A virus contains RNA or DNA, never both.","A":"Correct — obligate intracellular parasitism is THE defining property. A virus has no ribosomes and no energy-generating machinery, so it must borrow the cell's."},"img":"slides_i/L10_6.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L10|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q4","t":"Family–genome matching","s":"Which pairing of virus family and characteristic is CORRECT?","lec":"L10","sl":16,"c":"<b>Family–genome matching</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 virology Q4 — which correlation between virus family and characteristic is correct?</span>","o":{"A":"Poxviridae — replication in the nucleus","E":"Parvoviridae — ssRNA genome","B":"Papillomaviridae — segmented genome","C":"Reoviridae — dsRNA genome","D":"Retroviridae — replication entirely in the cytoplasm"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Poxvirus is the famous DNA-virus exception that replicates in the CYTOPLASM, because it carries its own polymerase.","E":"Parvoviridae is the ssDNA family — it is on the HHAPPPPy DNA list.","B":"Papillomavirus has a circular dsDNA genome in one piece; segmented genomes belong to influenza and the reoviruses.","C":"Correct, and it is the single exception worth memorising: every RNA virus is ssRNA EXCEPT Reoviridae, which is dsRNA and segmented (rotavirus lives here).","D":"Retroviruses must reach the nucleus to integrate."},"img":"slides_i/L10_16.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L10|20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q46","t":"Virus interaction — naming it","s":"Two influenza viruses infect one cell and produce a hybrid strain carrying gene segments from both parents. What is this interaction called?","lec":"L10","sl":43,"c":"<b>Virus interaction — naming it</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q46 — name the interaction occurring between the two viruses in Q45</span>","o":{"E":"Antigenic shift caused by point mutation","D":"Phenotypic mixing","C":"Complementation","A":"Attenuation","B":"Reassortment"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Antigenic shift is real, but it is caused by reassortment, NOT by point mutation — point mutation is drift. The option is self-contradictory, which is how you spot it.","D":"Phenotypic mixing swaps the COAT only, and the change disappears in the next generation.","C":"Complementation is a helper supplying a missing product to a defective virus; no genetic material changes hands.","A":"Attenuation is loss of virulence.","B":"Correct. Segments from both parents packaged into one heritable hybrid is reassortment — the mechanism of antigenic shift."},"img":"slides_i/L10_43.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L10|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Inactivation conditions and storage","s":"A laboratory must keep one aliquot of a virus isolate usable for a study running over the next two years, and must decontaminate the used glassware in a dry-heat oven. Which pair of conditions matches the lecture?","lec":"L10","sl":12,"c":"<b>Inactivation conditions and storage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Storage at −80 °C; oven at 160–180 °C for 2 hours","B":"Storage at 4 °C; oven at 100 °C for 10 minutes","C":"Storage at −80 °C; oven at 121 °C for 15 minutes","D":"Storage at 4 °C; oven at 160–180 °C for 2 hours","E":"Storage at −20 °C; oven at 121 °C for 30 minutes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 12 gives storage at 4 °C for 2–3 days only and at −80 °C or −196 °C for years, so a two-year study needs the deep-freeze figure. The same slide gives incineration or dry heat at 160–180 °C for 2 hours — the slowest method, because dry air transfers heat poorly.","B":"100 °C for 10 minutes is boiling, and 4 °C is short-term storage — both halves are wrong for this scenario.","C":"121 °C for 15 minutes is the autoclave, which is moist heat under 15 lb pressure, not a dry-heat oven.","D":"The oven figure is right but 4 °C holds a specimen for 2–3 days, not two years.","E":"−20 °C is not one of the storage temperatures given in the lecture, and the time quoted for 121 °C is 15 minutes, not 30."},"img":"slides_i/L10_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A specimen containing virus must be inactivated, and a second aliquot stored for a study running over the next two years. Which pair of statements matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Autoclaving at 121 °C for 15 minutes inactivates it; long-term storage is at −80 °C or −196 °C","C":"Autoclaving at 121 °C for 2 hours inactivates it; long-term storage is at 4 °C","D":"Boiling at 100 °C for 15 minutes inactivates it; long-term storage is at −20 °C","B":"Dry heat at 160–180 °C for 15 minutes inactivates it; long-term storage is at 4 °C for up to a year","E":"70% ethanol inactivates every virus reliably; storage temperature is irrelevant once the sample is fixed"},"r":{"A":"Correct on both. The numbers to hold are boil 100 °C for 10 min, autoclave 121 °C for 15 min, dry heat 160–180 °C for 2 hours; 4 °C buys 2–3 days, −80/−196 °C buys years.","C":"Two hours is the DRY-HEAT (incineration) time, not the autoclave time, and 4 °C holds a sample for only 2–3 days.","D":"Boiling is 10 minutes, not 15 — 15 minutes belongs to the autoclave. −20 °C is not a storage option on the slide.","B":"Dry heat needs 2 HOURS at that temperature, and 4 °C is a days-long option.","E":"70% ethanol is listed, but naked viruses are explicitly resistant to detergents and drying — 'every virus reliably' is exactly the overreach being tested."},"t":"Inactivation and storage numbers","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L10_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Inactivation and storage numbers</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Hepadnavirus — dsDNA genome with reverse transcriptase","s":"A man with chronic hepatitis B responds to a reverse-transcriptase inhibitor, although the virion he carries holds a partially double-stranded DNA genome. Which statement accounts for the drug working?","lec":"L10","sl":20,"c":"<b>Hepadnavirus — dsDNA genome with reverse transcriptase</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its DNA is copied from an RNA intermediate by the viral polymerase","B":"Its genome is single-stranded RNA copied into DNA soon after entry","C":"Its RNA is copied by a virion-packaged RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","D":"Its DNA is replicated only by the host DNA-dependent DNA polymerase","E":"Its DNA is integrated by an integrase packaged inside the virion"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Hepadnaviridae is Baltimore class VII: a dsDNA virus that replicates through an RNA intermediate, with HBV Pol acting as the reverse transcriptase. That step is what a reverse-transcriptase inhibitor blocks, and it is why tenofovir treats HIV and chronic hepatitis B at the same time.","B":"That is class VI, Retroviridae — the virion holds RNA and reverse transcribes it. The stem states the opposite starting material, which is the whole point of the item.","C":"RNA-dependent RNA polymerase is the class III and class V enzyme (Reoviridae, and the negative-sense RNA families); it is not inhibited by a reverse-transcriptase inhibitor.","D":"If only host DNA polymerase were involved there would be no reverse-transcriptase step for the drug to hit.","E":"Integrase belongs to the retroviral cycle. Class VII is defined by reverse transcription, not by integration."},"img":"slides_i/L10_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A virus has a double-stranded DNA genome and carries a polymerase with reverse-transcriptase activity. Which Baltimore class is it, and which family?","o":{"A":"Class VII — Hepadnaviridae (hepatitis B virus)","B":"Class VI — Retroviridae (HIV, HTLV)","C":"Class I — Adenoviridae and Herpesviridae","E":"Class II — Parvoviridae","D":"Class III — Reoviridae"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Class VII is the odd one: a DNA virus that nevertheless goes through an RNA intermediate, which is why HBV Pol has reverse-transcriptase activity.","B":"Class VI is (+) ssRNA WITH reverse transcriptase — the genome is RNA, not DNA. This is the pair the question is built to separate.","C":"Class I is plain dsDNA with no reverse transcriptase.","E":"Class II is ssDNA — Parvoviridae, the one exception to 'all DNA viruses are dsDNA'.","D":"Class III is dsRNA — Reoviridae, the one exception to 'all RNA viruses are ssRNA'."},"t":"Baltimore class from a description","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L10_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Baltimore class from a description</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Drift versus shift, from the epidemiology","s":"Each winter a virus causes epidemics that partly evade the previous season's antibody; roughly once a decade a strain appears against which the population has no immunity at all and which spreads worldwide. Which pair of mechanisms produces these two patterns, in that order?","lec":"L10","sl":39,"c":"<b>Drift versus shift, from the epidemiology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Reassortment, then antigenic drift","B":"Recombination, then antigenic drift","C":"Phenotypic mixing, then reassortment","D":"Antigenic drift, then complementation","E":"Antigenic drift, then reassortment"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The order is inverted: segment swapping is the rare, pandemic event, and point mutation is the annual one.","B":"Recombination is intramolecular genetic exchange and does not need a segmented genome, but the lecture ties the seasonal escape pattern to drift rather than to recombination.","C":"Phenotypic mixing changes only the coat of one generation of particles and cannot drive yearly epidemics.","D":"Complementation is a helper virus rescuing a defective one; it is not a mechanism of population level immune escape.","E":"Antigenic variation, or drift, is accumulated mutation of a surface protein, so pre-existing antibody inhibits the virus only partially — enough immunity survives to keep it an epidemic. Reassortment, or antigenic shift, swaps whole segments and replaces a surface protein in one step, leaving no pre-existing immunity at all, which is the pandemic pattern."},"img":"slides_i/L10_39.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement correctly separates antigenic drift from antigenic shift?","o":{"E":"Drift is antigenic variation from accumulated mutation in a surface protein, so pre-existing antibody inhibits it only partially; shift is REASSORTMENT, which requires a SEGMENTED genome and produced the 2009 H1N1 swine flu pandemic","A":"Drift requires a segmented genome and shift does not","B":"Shift is recombination — intramolecular genetic exchange — and can occur in any virus","C":"Drift occurs only in DNA viruses because they mutate faster","D":"Both terms describe the same process; 'shift' is simply the older name"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and the segmented-genome requirement is the discriminator — it is why influenza and the reoviruses can shift and most viruses cannot.","A":"Inverted. Drift needs only point mutation, which any virus can do; SHIFT is the one that needs segments to swap.","B":"Recombination and reassortment are listed as SEPARATE interactions. Recombination is intramolecular exchange; reassortment is whole-segment swapping, and only reassortment is called antigenic shift.","C":"RNA viruses mutate FASTER than DNA viruses, because RdRp has no error-checking.","D":"They are different mechanisms with different requirements and different epidemiological consequences."},"t":"Drift vs shift","sl":39,"img":"slides_i/L10_39.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Drift vs shift</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Outcomes for the infected cell","s":"A culture keeps releasing infectious virus into the medium for several weeks while the monolayer stays intact and the cells go on dividing. Which outcome does this illustrate?","lec":"L10","sl":34,"c":"<b>Outcomes for the infected cell</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Transformation of the cell by an integrated genome","B":"Productive infection without cell death","C":"Productive infection with cell death","D":"Latent infection with later reactivation","E":"Abortive infection in a non-permissive cell"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Transformation is not one of the four outcomes on this slide, and the stem describes ongoing virus release rather than altered growth behaviour.","B":"Slide 34 sets out four outcomes by asking two independent questions: is progeny virus made, and does the cell survive. Virus is being released and the cells are alive, which is the productive-without-cell-death box — the enveloped, budding pattern of continuous shedding.","C":"Productive with cell death is the lytic box; the monolayer would break down rather than stay intact.","D":"Latent infection means the genome persists with no progeny produced until reactivation. Here virus is being released continuously.","E":"Abortive infection means the cycle starts but no virus is produced, typically because the cell lacks the proteases or transcription factors needed."},"img":"slides_i/L10_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which list of the possible outcomes for a virus-infected cell matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Productive infection WITH cell death, productive infection WITHOUT cell death, latent infection, and abortive infection","C":"Productive infection, latent infection, and transformation — three outcomes only","E":"Lytic and lysogenic infection only, as in bacteriophages","D":"Acute, chronic and persistent infection","A":"Cytopathic and non-cytopathic infection only"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The slide splits PRODUCTIVE infection in two by whether the cell survives, which is why some viruses shed continuously without killing the cell.","C":"Transformation is not on this slide, and it collapses the productive category into one.","E":"Lytic/lysogenic is bacteriophage vocabulary; the human-cell scheme here has four outcomes.","D":"Acute, chronic and persistent describe the course of infection in the HOST, not the fate of a single cell.","A":"This is a two-way split of one of the four categories, not the full list — it drops latent and abortive infection entirely."},"t":"Four outcomes for the infected cell","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L10_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Four outcomes for the infected cell</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Why some viral polymerases are selective drug targets","s":"An antiviral programme wants an enzyme activity that human cells do not possess at all, so that inhibiting it cannot damage the host. Which two polymerase activities meet that requirement on the central-dogma slide?","lec":"L10","sl":26,"c":"<b>Why some viral polymerases are selective drug targets</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Reverse transcriptase and DNA-dependent DNA polymerase","B":"DNA-dependent RNA polymerase and reverse transcriptase","C":"DNA-dependent DNA polymerase and RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","D":"RNA-dependent RNA polymerase and reverse transcriptase","E":"DNA-dependent RNA polymerase and DNA-dependent DNA polymerase"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Reverse transcriptase qualifies, but DNA-dependent DNA polymerase is a human enzyme.","B":"DNA-dependent RNA polymerase is human transcription; inhibiting it would poison the host cell.","C":"DNA-dependent DNA polymerase is human replication, so only one half of this pair qualifies.","D":"Slide 26 marks two arrows as absent from human cells: RNA-dependent RNA polymerase and RNA-dependent DNA polymerase, which is reverse transcriptase. Because we have no counterpart, a drug against either is selective — and that is why a virus needing them has to package them in the particle.","E":"Both of these are the human arrows on the slide, so neither is a selective target."},"img":"slides_i/L10_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why does the lecture single out RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRp) on the central-dogma slide?","o":{"D":"Because it has no human counterpart — humans have DNA-dependent DNA and DNA-dependent RNA polymerases, so an enzyme that copies RNA from RNA is virus-specific and therefore an attractive selective drug target","B":"Because it is the most abundant enzyme in the human cell","A":"Because it is the enzyme that integrates viral DNA into the host genome","E":"Because only DNA viruses carry it","C":"Because it proofreads viral RNA, which is why RNA viruses mutate slowly"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The slide labels the human polymerases 'Found in human' and RdRp 'Not found in human' — the whole point is selective toxicity.","B":"It is absent from human cells altogether.","A":"Integration is the job of INTEGRASE; reverse transcriptase makes the DNA copy.","E":"RdRp is carried by RNA viruses, not DNA viruses.","C":"The opposite: RdRp LACKS error-checking, which is why RNA viruses mutate faster than DNA viruses."},"t":"Why RdRp is a drug target","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L10_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Why RdRp is a drug target</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Where an envelope comes from","s":"A respiratory virus is shed continuously for weeks from cells that remain alive, and the shed particles lose infectivity quickly on dry surfaces or after contact with detergent. From where does the outermost layer of these particles come?","lec":"L10","sl":10,"c":"<b>Where an envelope comes from</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The membrane of the host cell it left","B":"A lipid layer built by viral enzymes","C":"Matrix protein laid down after release","D":"Capsomers assembled within the cytoplasm","E":"Glycoprotein spikes with no lipid present"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Shedding without killing the cell is budding, and lability to detergent and drying means a lipid coat. Slide 10 defines the envelope as a lipid bilayer derived from the host cell membrane, which the virus wraps around itself on the way out (slide 33).","B":"Viruses do not synthesise their own lipid bilayer; that is exactly why the envelope carries host membrane lipid.","C":"Matrix protein lies beneath the envelope and is laid down before release, not after.","D":"Capsomers are the protein subunits that build the capsid, which sits inside the envelope and is not dissolved by detergent.","E":"Spikes are the attachment proteins set into the envelope. Without lipid there would be nothing for detergent to dissolve, and the particle would be stable, not labile."},"img":"slides_i/L10_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A virus is released from infected cells without killing them, and the released particles are readily destroyed by detergent and by stomach acid. Which statement fits?","o":{"A":"It is an ENVELOPED virus leaving by budding, which is non-cytolytic — and its lipid envelope is exactly what makes it labile to detergent, acid and drying","E":"It is a NAKED virus leaving by budding; naked viruses are the detergent-sensitive group","D":"It is an enveloped virus leaving by host cell lysis, which is why the cells survive","C":"It is a naked virus leaving by lysis, and its stability in the gut is why it spreads faecal–orally","B":"Release mechanism and envelope status are unrelated"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and the two halves are causally linked — the envelope is stolen host membrane, acquired during budding, and a lipid membrane is what detergent and acid destroy.","E":"Naked viruses are the STABLE group; they resist detergent, acid and drying.","D":"Lysis kills the cell by definition, so this is self-contradictory.","C":"Naked viruses do lyse the cell and are gut-stable, but the stem says the cells are NOT killed and the particles ARE labile.","B":"They are tightly related — budding is how the envelope is acquired."},"t":"Assembly and release","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L10_33.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Assembly and release</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"The two RNA viruses that need the nucleus","s":"Two RNA viruses break the general rule for their genome type by carrying out nuclear steps: one has a segmented negative-sense genome, the other carries reverse transcriptase. What sends each of them into the nucleus?","lec":"L10","sl":29,"c":"<b>The two RNA viruses that need the nucleus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"One integrates its DNA copy, the other needs a host DNA polymerase there","B":"One steals host messenger caps, the other integrates its DNA copy","C":"Both are too large to complete their assembly in the cytoplasm","D":"One needs host ribosomes, the other needs nuclear proteases","E":"Both need the nuclear spliceosome to process their own transcripts"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The order is inverted and the second half is wrong: neither of these RNA viruses replicates its genome with a host DNA polymerase.","B":"The segmented negative-sense virus is influenza, which must reach the nucleus to cap-snatch — to steal 5′ caps from host messenger RNA for its own transcripts. The virus with reverse transcriptase is HIV, which must reach the nucleus to integrate its DNA copy as a provirus, which is also the reason HIV is never cleared.","C":"Size is the reason poxvirus stays out of the nucleus — the opposite exception, and it belongs to the DNA rule.","D":"Ribosomes are cytoplasmic and no virus brings or seeks its own; proteases are not the nuclear requirement described in the lecture.","E":"Splicing is not the reason the lecture gives for either exception."},"img":"slides_i/L10_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the human virome matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"More than 90% of the viral community is PROKARYOTIC virus — bacteriophage — with Caudovirales the most abundant, alongside eukaryotic viruses, endogenous viral elements and viruses from the environment","A":"More than 90% is eukaryotic virus infecting human cells, with herpesviruses the most abundant","E":"The virome contains only latent human viruses; bacteriophages are part of the microbiome, not the virome","C":"The virome is the collection of all microbes in and on the body, including bacteria and fungi","D":"Endogenous viral elements are excluded, because they are host DNA rather than virus"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The counter-intuitive part is that most of 'your' virome does not infect you at all — it infects your bacteria.","A":"The proportions are inverted; eukaryotic viruses are the minority.","E":"Bacteriophages are explicitly the largest component of the VIROME on the slide.","C":"That definition is the MICROBIOME; the virome is the viral subset.","D":"Endogenous viral elements are listed as one of the four components."},"t":"The virome — what is actually in it","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L10_47.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>The virome — what is actually in it</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Virome dysbiosis — the IBD mechanism","s":"In the pathway the lecture gives for inflammatory bowel disease, expansion of a phage order is followed by activation of CD4+ T cells. Through which receptor does that activation run, and which mediator does it then sustain?","lec":"L10","sl":53,"c":"<b>Virome dysbiosis — the IBD mechanism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Toll-like receptor 4, sustaining IL-22","B":"Toll-like receptor 4, sustaining interferon-γ","C":"Toll-like receptor 3, sustaining interferon-α","D":"Toll-like receptor 9, sustaining IL-22","E":"Toll-like receptor 9, sustaining interferon-γ"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Both halves are drawn from the wrong parts of the lecture.","B":"TLR-4 is the lipopolysaccharide sensor and is not the receptor named in this pathway.","C":"Neither TLR-3 nor interferon-α is named in the IBD pathway.","D":"IL-22 does appear in the lecture, but on slide 51 as one of the virome's protective, anti-inflammatory functions — the friend side, not the disease mechanism.","E":"Slide 53 prints the chain: expansion of Caudovirales leads to TLR-9-dependent activation of CD4+ T cells, then prolonged interferon-γ, then chronic inflammation. The four steps are the examinable unit."},"img":"slides_i/L10_53.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"By what mechanism does gut virome dysbiosis contribute to inflammatory bowel disease, according to the lecture?","o":{"E":"Expansion of Caudovirales activates CD4+ T cells in a TLR-9-dependent way, producing prolonged interferon-γ and chronic inflammation","D":"Loss of Caudovirales removes the interferon signal, and the resulting immunodeficiency permits bacterial invasion","B":"Bacteriophages lyse the epithelium directly, producing ulceration","C":"Expansion of eukaryotic viruses activates TLR-4 and produces IL-22","A":"Phage therapy causes IBD, which is why it is not used for Clostridioides difficile infection"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and the chain is worth memorising in order: Caudovirales expansion → TLR-9 → CD4+ T cells → prolonged IFN-γ → chronic inflammation.","D":"It is EXPANSION, not loss, and the result is too much interferon rather than too little.","B":"The mechanism described is immunological, not direct epithelial lysis — phages lyse bacteria, not human cells.","C":"The receptor is TLR-9 and the cytokine is IFN-γ; IL-22 appears among the virome's BENEFICIAL functions.","A":"Phage therapy and faecal virome transplant are presented as TREATMENTS for C. difficile infection."},"t":"How the virome drives IBD","sl":52,"img":"slides_i/L10_52.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>How the virome drives IBD</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Defective virus rescued by a helper","s":"A man with chronic hepatitis B acquires a second agent that has its own RNA genome and antigen but can only produce particles because the hepatitis B virus supplies a gene product it lacks. Which term names what the hepatitis B virus is doing for it?","lec":"L10","sl":36,"c":"<b>Defective virus rescued by a helper</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Phenotypic mixing","B":"Complementation","C":"Reassortment","D":"Antigenic variation","E":"Recombination"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Phenotypic mixing is the random packaging of one virus's genome inside capsid proteins specified by another, with no missing function being supplied and no lasting effect.","B":"Slide 36 defines a defective virus as one with nucleic acid and protein that cannot replicate without a helper virus providing a missing function, and slide 43 names that rescue complementation — with the hepatitis B and hepatitis D pair drawn as the illustration.","C":"Reassortment needs two viruses with segmented genomes shuffling whole segments, which neither of these agents has.","D":"Antigenic variation is mutation of a surface protein within one virus and involves no second virus at all.","E":"Recombination is intramolecular genetic exchange, so the genome itself would be altered; here nothing is exchanged genetically."},"img":"slides_i/L10_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A defective virus is grown with a helper virus. In one experiment the helper supplies a missing protein so the defective virus replicates; in another, the defective genome ends up packaged inside capsid proteins specified by the helper. What are these two phenomena?","o":{"B":"The first is COMPLEMENTATION, the second is PHENOTYPIC MIXING — and neither changes the defective virus's own genome","C":"The first is phenotypic mixing, the second is complementation","E":"Both are recombination, differing only in whether the exchange is genetic or protein-level","D":"The first is reassortment, the second is recombination","A":"The first is complementation, the second is reassortment"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the closing clause is the examinable subtlety: in both cases the defective genome is unchanged, so the progeny revert to being defective.","C":"The two are swapped. Complementation supplies a PROTEIN; phenotypic mixing mis-packages a genome.","E":"Recombination is intramolecular genetic exchange, a third and separate phenomenon.","D":"Reassortment requires segmented genomes and swaps whole segments — neither description here.","A":"Only the first half is right."},"t":"Complementation vs phenotypic mixing","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L10_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Complementation vs phenotypic mixing</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Which replication step a drug blocks","s":"A compound binds a pocket in the capsid and prevents it from coming apart after the particle has crossed the plasma membrane. Which step of the cycle is blocked, and what is the immediate result inside the cell?","lec":"L10","sl":25,"c":"<b>Which replication step a drug blocks</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Assembly, so progeny capsids remain empty","B":"Penetration, so the particle stays outside the cell","C":"Uncoating, so the genome is never released","D":"Attachment, so the receptor is never engaged","E":"Release, so progeny stay trapped within the cell"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Assembly is the packaging of new genomes with new proteins, several steps later; that is what a drug competing with the capsid for the genome would hit.","B":"Penetration is crossing the plasma membrane by fusion, endocytosis or phagocytosis. The stem states that this has already happened.","C":"Slide 25 defines uncoating as disassembly of the capsid with release of the viral ribonucleoprotein or genome, and it follows penetration. A compound that stops the capsid coming apart after entry therefore blocks step 3, and the genome never reaches the machinery that would copy it.","D":"Attachment is the binding of the attachment protein to its receptor, step 1, and it also has already happened.","E":"Release is the exit of finished virions by budding or lysis, the last step of the cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L10_25.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the entry steps matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Penetration — crossing the plasma membrane — occurs by fusion, endocytosis or phagocytosis; uncoating is the separate, later step in which the capsid is disassembled and the viral ribonucleoprotein or genome is released","E":"Penetration and uncoating are the same step under two names","B":"Penetration occurs only by fusion, which is why only enveloped viruses can infect cells","D":"Uncoating precedes penetration, since the genome must be free before it can cross the membrane","A":"Uncoating is the binding of the virus attachment protein to its receptor"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The six-step scheme keeps attachment, penetration and uncoating as three distinct steps, and the deck defines uncoating separately on its own slide.","E":"They are steps 2 and 3 of six, with different definitions.","B":"Endocytosis and phagocytosis are listed alongside fusion, and naked viruses enter by those routes.","D":"The order is penetration then uncoating — the particle crosses the membrane before the capsid comes apart.","A":"That is step 1, ATTACHMENT."},"t":"Penetration and uncoating","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L10_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Penetration and uncoating</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Early versus late gene expression","s":"Inside a single infected cell, a viral enzyme needed to copy the genome appears well before the proteins that build the particle. Which description of viral gene expression does this match?","lec":"L10","sl":21,"c":"<b>Early versus late gene expression</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Late transcripts encode the genome polymerase","B":"Early transcripts encode non-structural proteins","C":"Early transcripts encode the structural proteins","D":"Host enzymes make the early viral proteins","E":"All viral proteins come from one polyprotein"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The polymerase that copies the genome is a non-structural, early product — it has to be present before the genome can be copied.","B":"Slide 21 splits step 4 in two: early messenger RNA and protein synthesis makes non-structural proteins for replicating the genome, and late synthesis makes the structural proteins that build the particle. Genome first, packaging second — and the early non-structural products are why the dengue NS1 antigen test works before antibody appears.","C":"Reversed. Structural proteins are the late products.","D":"Viral proteins are translated from viral messenger RNA on host ribosomes; the host does not supply the proteins themselves.","E":"Polyprotein processing is one strategy used by some families, not a rule for every virus, and the lecture's scheme is the early or late split."},"img":"slides_i/L10_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the order of viral gene expression matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Early mRNA and protein synthesis makes NON-STRUCTURAL proteins needed to replicate the genome; late synthesis makes the STRUCTURAL proteins that build the particle","C":"Early synthesis makes structural proteins so the capsid is ready, and late synthesis makes the enzymes","E":"All viral proteins are made simultaneously from a single polyprotein in every virus family","D":"Early proteins are made by the host and late proteins by the virus","A":"Non-structural proteins are made only after release, in the next host cell"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the logic is worth holding: you need the replication machinery before you need the packaging, so the enzymes come first.","C":"Inverted — building capsids before there are genomes to put in them would be wasted effort.","E":"Polyprotein processing is one strategy (picornaviruses), not a universal rule, and the slide explicitly separates early from late.","D":"Both classes are viral proteins; the host supplies the ribosomes.","A":"Non-structural proteins are the EARLY products, made inside the cell that is currently infected."},"t":"Early vs late gene expression","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L10_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Early vs late gene expression</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Defining a virus by what it lacks","s":"A report describes an agent that will not grow on any cell-free medium, is unaffected by broad-spectrum antibiotics, and cannot be resolved by light microscopy. Which additional finding would be consistent with its being a virus?","lec":"L10","sl":6,"c":"<b>Defining a virus by what it lacks</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A cell wall containing peptidoglycan","B":"A mitochondrion supplying the particle's energy","C":"A single kind of nucleic acid in the particle","D":"Ribosomes visible within the particle","E":"Both DNA and RNA inside one particle"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Peptidoglycan is bacterial, and it is the target of several of the antibiotics the stem says had no effect.","B":"Mitochondria are cellular organelles; a virus has none and cannot generate its own energy.","C":"Slide 6 defines a virus largely by absence: 20–300 nm, only RNA or DNA but never both, an obligate intracellular parasite, no organelles such as mitochondria or ribosomes, self-assembling, and resistant to antibiotics. Carrying one type of nucleic acid is the positive statement of that rule.","D":"No virus carries ribosomes — it borrows the host's, which is also why antibiotics acting on bacterial ribosomes do nothing to it.","E":"An agent with both nucleic acids is a cell, not a virus. This was a keyed distractor in the BM30 medical virology formative."},"img":"slides_i/L10_6.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the general properties of viruses matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"They are mostly 20–300 nm, contain either RNA or DNA but never both, are obligate intracellular parasites, lack organelles such as mitochondria and ribosomes, self-assemble, and are resistant to antibiotics","B":"They contain both RNA and DNA, which is how they replicate independently of the host","A":"They possess ribosomes but not mitochondria, which is why they need host energy","D":"They are 20–300 µm, which is why they can be seen under the light microscope","E":"They are sensitive to antibiotics acting on protein synthesis, since they use host ribosomes"},"r":{"C":"Correct on all six properties, including the size range and the Mimivirus caveat at 370–600 nm.","B":"Containing only one nucleic acid type, never both, is one of the defining properties.","A":"They lack ribosomes too — which is precisely why they must borrow the host's.","D":"Nanometres, not micrometres; that is why an electron microscope is needed.","E":"Resistance to antibiotics is listed as a general property; antibacterial agents have no viral target."},"t":"General properties","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L10_6.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>General properties</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Capsid, capsomers and the nucleocapsid","s":"A virion is described as a helical arrangement of capsomers around its genome, with a lipid layer bearing spikes outside that. Which pairing of components is correctly named?","lec":"L10","sl":13,"c":"<b>Capsid, capsomers and the nucleocapsid</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The lipid layer is assembled out of capsomers","B":"Genome plus lipid layer together are the nucleocapsid","C":"Capsid plus lipid layer together are the nucleocapsid","D":"Capsomers are the enzymes packaged beside the genome","E":"Genome plus capsid together are the nucleocapsid"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The lipid bilayer comes from the host cell membrane, not from viral protein subunits.","B":"The capsid is the component that must be included; a genome plus envelope has no agreed name in the lecture.","C":"This is the commonest misreading — adding the envelope in. The envelope is not part of the nucleocapsid.","D":"Capsomers are the protein subunits that build the capsid (slide 13). The packaged enzymes are separate items on slide 8, such as RdRp, reverse transcriptase and integrase.","E":"Slide 8 states it directly: nucleic acid plus capsid equals nucleocapsid. The envelope and any packaged enzymes sit outside that definition and are optional components."},"img":"slides_i/L10_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the capsid matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"The capsid is built from protein subunits called capsomers and takes one of three forms — icosahedral, helical or complex; capsid plus nucleic acid together are the NUCLEOCAPSID","B":"The capsid is a lipid bilayer taken from the host cell membrane","C":"Capsid plus envelope together are called the nucleocapsid","D":"Only two capsid forms exist, icosahedral and helical; 'complex' refers to enveloped viruses","A":"Capsomers are the enzymes carried inside the particle, such as RdRp and integrase"},"r":{"E":"Correct on the subunit name, the three architectures and the definition of nucleocapsid.","B":"The lipid bilayer is the ENVELOPE; the capsid is protein.","C":"Nucleocapsid = nucleic acid + capsid. The envelope sits outside it.","D":"The slide lists three forms, and 'complex' is a genuine capsid architecture — poxvirus and bacteriophage are the usual examples.","A":"Capsomers are the structural protein subunits; RdRp and integrase are separate packaged enzymes."},"t":"Capsid architecture","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L10_8.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Capsid architecture</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L10|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Viral vectors — vaccine versus gene therapy","s":"A product is made by engineering a replication-defective adenovirus so that the cells it enters manufacture the surface protein of an unrelated respiratory virus. Which description of it matches the lecture?","lec":"L10","sl":44,"c":"<b>Viral vectors — vaccine versus gene therapy</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Gene therapy correcting an inherited defect in the recipient","B":"A viral vector vaccine carrying another virus's gene","C":"A live attenuated vaccine of the respiratory virus","D":"An inactivated whole-particle preparation of the vector","E":"A conditional lethal mutant restricted to the upper airway"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Gene therapy is the other use on slide 44: delivering a new functional gene to a patient with a genetic disease. Nothing of the recipient's own genome is being corrected.","B":"Slide 44 gives two uses of viral vectors. A viral vector vaccine is a genetically engineered virus carrying the genes of another virus, so that the recipient's own cells make that other virus's protein and are immunised against it — the adenovirus-vectored COVID-19 vaccine the lecturer used as her example.","C":"A live attenuated vaccine is the pathogen itself, weakened by an attenuating mutation. Here the respiratory virus is not present at all, only one of its genes.","D":"An inactivated preparation contains killed whole particles and cannot enter cells and direct protein synthesis, which is what the stem describes.","E":"A conditional lethal mutant is defined by replicating only under permissive conditions, such as the cold-adapted nasal influenza vaccine; the defect here is in replication itself, not in a condition."},"img":"slides_i/L10_44.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement distinguishes the two uses of viral vectors in the lecture?","o":{"B":"Gene therapy uses a virus to deliver a new functional gene to a patient with a genetic disease; a viral vector vaccine is an engineered virus carrying the GENES OF ANOTHER VIRUS so the immune system is primed against that other virus","A":"Gene therapy uses live attenuated virus; viral vector vaccines use inactivated virus","C":"Both deliver the patient's own corrected gene; only the route of administration differs","D":"Viral vector vaccines deliver antibody rather than genes","E":"Gene therapy is a form of conditional lethal mutation"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Both exploit the same property — a virus is an efficient delivery system — but the cargo and the purpose differ.","A":"Neither is defined by attenuation or inactivation; both are genetically engineered delivery vehicles.","C":"Only gene therapy delivers a therapeutic gene to the patient; a vector vaccine delivers another virus's antigen gene.","D":"They deliver GENES; the antibody, if any, is made by the recipient.","E":"Conditional lethal mutation is a category of viral MUTATION, unrelated to vector technology."},"t":"Viral vectors","sl":44,"img":"slides_i/L10_44.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Viral vectors</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233348571},{"id":"I|L11|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Pathogenicity (dose) vs virulence (outcome)","s":"In a volunteer challenge study, virus J causes illness only when about a million particles are given, and nearly everyone who falls ill recovers within days. Virus K causes illness in almost every volunteer after a few particles, and untreated cases are almost always fatal. Using the lecture's definitions, how are J and K classified?","lec":"L11","sl":6,"c":"<b>Pathogenicity (dose) vs virulence (outcome)</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"J and K differ in pathogenicity alone, because the case outcome reflects the host, not the agent","B":"J has low pathogenicity and low virulence; K has high pathogenicity and high virulence","C":"J has low pathogenicity and high virulence; K has high pathogenicity and low virulence","D":"J and K differ in virulence alone, because the dose needed reflects the host, not the agent","E":"J has high pathogenicity and low virulence; K has low pathogenicity and high virulence"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Severity of outcome in a uniform volunteer group is the agent's virulence, so J and K differ on virulence too.","B":"Correct. The lecturer's reframing: pathogenicity is about DOSE (can it cause disease at all — a virus that needs a huge inoculum is low), virulence is about OUTCOME (how badly it damages the host). J behaves like hepatitis A in the slide-7 table (low/low); K behaves like rabies (high/high).","C":"Pathogenicity for J is right (low), but its outcome is mild, so virulence is low, not high; K's fatal outcome is high virulence, not low.","D":"The dose needed to cause disease is the agent's pathogenicity (slide 6: disease depends on pathogenicity and dose), so J and K differ on pathogenicity too.","E":"Both halves are inverted. Needing a million particles is low pathogenicity; a few particles sufficing is high pathogenicity."},"img":"slides_i/L11_6.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Rabies virus is described as HIGH pathogenicity and HIGH virulence; hepatitis A virus as LOW and LOW; measles as HIGH pathogenicity but LOW virulence. What do the two words mean?","o":{"C":"Pathogenicity refers to the agent, virulence refers to the host's immune status","E":"Pathogenicity is severity; virulence is the ability to cause disease at all","B":"Pathogenicity is the ability to cause disease at all; virulence is the SEVERITY of the disease once caused","A":"They are synonyms, and the slide uses both only for emphasis","D":"Pathogenicity is measured in the laboratory; virulence is measured in the field"},"r":{"C":"Both terms describe the AGENT. Host factors are handled separately on the host slide.","E":"Exactly reversed, and this is the option that catches people.","B":"Correct, and the table is built to force the distinction. Measles infects almost everyone it reaches (high pathogenicity) but usually does not kill them (low virulence). Rabies is high on both — it infects and it kills.","A":"They are deliberately contrasted, not synonymous.","D":"Neither is defined by where it is measured."},"t":"Pathogenicity vs virulence","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L11_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Pathogenicity vs virulence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Incidence vs prevalence in a changing population","s":"A province introduces a drug that lets people with a chronic, incurable viral infection live many years longer. The drug does not cure the infection, and the number of people newly diagnosed each year stays the same. Over the next decade, what happens to the two measures defined in the lecture?","lec":"L11","sl":11,"c":"<b>Incidence vs prevalence in a changing population</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Both rise, as each new case is added to both counts","B":"Incidence stays about the same; prevalence rises","C":"Incidence falls; prevalence rises","D":"Both fall, as treated patients stop counting as cases","E":"Incidence rises; prevalence stays about the same"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Incidence counts only new cases in a period; keeping people alive longer adds nothing to it.","B":"Correct. Incidence is the rate of NEW cases in a period — unchanged, because new diagnoses per year are unchanged. Prevalence is the total number of EXISTING cases at a point in time — it rises because people stay in the pool for longer instead of dying.","C":"The stem fixes new diagnoses per year, so incidence cannot fall.","D":"A treated but uncured patient is still an existing case, so prevalence does not fall.","E":"Reversed. Longer survival changes how long people remain cases (prevalence), not how many new cases arrive (incidence)."},"img":"slides_i/L11_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pair of definitions is correct?","o":{"B":"Incidence = rate of NEW cases in a period; prevalence = TOTAL existing cases at a point","A":"Incidence = total existing cases; prevalence = rate of new cases","E":"Incidence = cases in an epidemic; prevalence = cases in an endemic state","C":"Incidence = deaths from the disease; prevalence = cases of the disease","D":"Incidence = cases per hospital; prevalence = cases per population"},"r":{"B":"Correct. New cases flow IN — incidence. Prevalence is the standing pool, and it grows when either incidence rises or people survive longer with the disease.","A":"Reversed — the single most common error in this topic.","E":"Endemic, epidemic and pandemic describe the LEVEL of occurrence, a separate axis entirely.","C":"Deaths are mortality rate; illness is morbidity rate.","D":"Neither term is defined by the denominator being a hospital."},"t":"Incidence vs prevalence","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L11_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Incidence vs prevalence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"The five-step spine — placing an event","s":"After reaching the liver through the bloodstream, a virus shuts down host DNA, RNA and protein synthesis in hepatocytes while it produces progeny, and the hepatocytes die. In the lecturer's five-step spine of viral pathogenesis, which step is this, and which step comes immediately after it?","lec":"L11","sl":18,"c":"<b>The five-step spine — placing an event</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cell injury, followed by virus shedding","B":"Recovery, followed by virus shedding","C":"Viral spread, followed by cell injury","D":"Cell injury, followed by recovery","E":"Viral entry and replication, followed by viral spread"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Shedding is the fifth step; recovery sits between cell injury and shedding.","B":"Recovery is followed by shedding, but death of infected hepatocytes is cell injury, not recovery.","C":"Travelling to the liver through the blood was the spread step; killing the hepatocyte is the next one.","D":"Correct. The spine (slides 18, 27, 31, 39, 43, 62) runs entry and replication → spread → cell injury → recovery → shedding. Host-synthesis shutdown with cell death is the 'cell death' type of cellular pathogenesis (slide 33), so it sits at cell injury, and recovery comes next.","E":"Entry and replication happen at the portal of entry, before the virus reached the liver."},"img":"slides_i/L11_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"What are the five steps of viral pathogenesis, in the order the lecture repeats them?","o":{"B":"Entry → latency → reactivation → transformation → shedding","C":"Exposure → adhesion → colonisation → invasion → infection","E":"Attachment → penetration → uncoating → replication → release","A":"Incubation → prodrome → onset → acute phase → convalescence","D":"Viral entry and replication → viral spread → cell injury → recovery → virus shedding"},"r":{"B":"Latency and transformation are possible OUTCOMES, not steps every infection passes through.","C":"That is the steps of BACTERIAL pathogenesis; it has appeared as a distractor in a real paper.","E":"Those are the steps of viral REPLICATION inside one cell (L10) — a different scale entirely.","A":"Those are the clinical STAGES OF ILLNESS, which the lecture also covers but on a separate slide.","D":"Correct. The lecturer puts this five-box diagram back on screen four separate times, greying out the boxes already covered — that repetition is the clearest signal in the deck about what is examinable."},"t":"The five steps","sl":18,"img":"slides_i/L11_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The five steps</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Transformation — what it is","s":"Cells taken from a virus-induced lesion keep dividing in culture long after the original infection. Viral DNA sequences are found inserted into the host chromosomes, and every daughter cell carries them. Which statement describes this type of cellular pathogenesis as the lecture defines it?","lec":"L11","sl":34,"c":"<b>Transformation — what it is</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A dormant state in which the genome persists without replication inside a cell with low MHC class I","B":"An irreversible genetic change in which integrated viral DNA alters host growth activators and suppressors","C":"A change in membrane structure that makes infected and uninfected cells fuse into one multinucleated cell","D":"A reversible change in growth rate that disappears once the viral genome has been cleared from the cell","E":"A shutdown of host DNA, RNA and protein synthesis while the virus replicates and releases its progeny"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"That is latency (slide 59). A latent genome does not drive the cell to divide.","B":"Correct — slide 34 word for word: transformation is an irreversible genetic process caused by integration of viral DNA into host DNA, altering growth activator and suppressor gene products and producing a tumour (examples on the slide: HIV, HPV).","C":"That is cell fusion / syncytium formation (slide 35 — herpesvirus, paramyxovirus).","D":"The slide stresses IRREVERSIBLE; integrated DNA is inherited by daughter cells, so it does not disappear.","E":"That is cell death (slide 33 — adenovirus, poliovirus)."},"img":"slides_i/L11_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"The lecture divides cell injury into three types of viral pathogenesis at the cellular level. Which set is it?","o":{"D":"Attachment · penetration · uncoating","A":"Cell death · apoptosis · necrosis","C":"Lysis · budding · latency","E":"Inflammation · fibrosis · scarring","B":"Cell death · transformation · cell fusion (syncytia formation)"},"r":{"D":"These are replication steps from L10.","A":"Apoptosis and necrosis are sub-types of cell death, so this list collapses to one item.","C":"Budding is a release mechanism, not an injury.","E":"These are tissue-level consequences, not the cellular classification the lecture uses.","B":"Correct. Three outcomes, three different diseases: death gives lytic illness, transformation gives cancer (L13), and fusion gives the multinucleated giant cell you see on a Tzanck smear or in measles."},"t":"The three cellular outcomes","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L11_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The three cellular outcomes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Cowdry type A row of the inclusion table","s":"A Tzanck smear from recurrent vesicles on a patient's lip shows multinucleated giant cells whose nuclei contain eosinophilic inclusions. In the lecture's inclusion-body table, what is this inclusion called, and which other virus is listed in the same row?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Cowdry type A row of the inclusion table</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Intracytoplasmic acidophilic, with poxvirus","B":"Owl's eye, with the SSPE measles virus","C":"Cowdry type A, with adenovirus","D":"Owl's eye, with adenovirus","E":"Cowdry type A, with the SSPE measles virus"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The stem places the inclusion in the nucleus. Intracytoplasmic acidophilic inclusions are the poxvirus row.","B":"Wrong name for the inclusion; SSPE measles belongs to Cowdry A.","C":"Adenovirus is in the owl's-eye row with CMV, not the Cowdry A row.","D":"Owl's eye is intranuclear BASOPHILIC in an enlarged cell (CMV, adenovirus) — the stem says eosinophilic, in a giant cell from a vesicle.","E":"Correct. Recurrent lip vesicles with giant cells on a Tzanck smear = HSV. The slide-38 table (an image in the PDF) lists 'Cowdry type A (intranuclear) — HSV, subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (measles) virus'. L12 adds that these inclusions are eosinophilic."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Where would you expect to find inclusion bodies, and why?","o":{"B":"Intranuclear for RNA viruses and intracytoplasmic for DNA viruses","A":"Always intranuclear, whatever the genome","C":"Always intracytoplasmic, whatever the genome","E":"Intranuclear for DNA viruses and intracytoplasmic for RNA viruses, because inclusions mark the site of transcription and genome replication","D":"Only in enveloped viruses, at the site of budding"},"r":{"B":"Exactly inverted.","A":"CMV's owl's eye is intranuclear, but rabies' Negri body is not.","C":"The reverse error.","E":"Correct, and it is a derivable rule rather than a memorised list: an inclusion body marks where transcription and genome replication happen, and that is the nucleus for DNA viruses and the cytoplasm for RNA viruses.","D":"Inclusions have nothing to do with the envelope. Remember the exception that proves the rule: poxvirus is a DNA virus that replicates — and forms its inclusions — in the cytoplasm."},"t":"Inclusion bodies","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Inclusion bodies</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Localized vs systemic; haematogenous spread","s":"An unvaccinated child inhales a virus that first multiplies in the airway epithelium and the regional lymph nodes. About a week later, virus is recovered from her blood and a rash spreads over her whole body. How does the lecture classify this pattern of dissemination?","lec":"L11","sl":26,"c":"<b>Localized vs systemic; haematogenous spread</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Abortive infection, since the airway cells were non-permissive","B":"Localized infection, spreading cell to cell from the airway to the skin","C":"Localized infection, replicating only in cells at the site of entry","D":"Systemic infection, spread through the bloodstream after local replication","E":"Systemic infection, spread along peripheral nerves after local replication"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"An abortive infection produces no progeny, so nothing could have reached the blood.","B":"Skin all over the body is not contiguous with the airway; the lecture's localized infection does not leave the entry site.","C":"A localized infection stays at the site of entry; this one reached blood and skin.","D":"Correct. Slide 26: a systemic infection replicates locally within the initial organ before spreading. Virus in the blood = haematogenous spread (slide 30), and once in the blood the virus reaches almost every tissue — here the skin.","E":"Neurotropic spread travels via nerves to neural tissue; virus in the blood and a whole-body rash point to the bloodstream."},"img":"slides_i/L11_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A virus enters through the respiratory tract but causes disease in the brain. Which term describes this, and what is the usual route?","o":{"E":"Abortive infection, because the virus fails to complete its cycle at the entry site","B":"Localised infection, spreading by direct cell-to-cell contact only","C":"Latent infection, because the virus must integrate before moving","D":"Systemic infection, most often by haematogenous spread — viraemia carrying virus from the portal of entry to a distant target organ","A":"Transformation, because the virus alters the growth of neurons"},"r":{"E":"An abortive infection produces no progeny, so nothing spreads anywhere.","B":"A localised infection replicates only at the entry site — the common cold, for example.","C":"Latency requires no movement at all.","D":"Correct. A SYSTEMIC infection is one that spreads beyond the site of entry, and the commonest vehicle is the blood — primary viraemia seeds an organ, replication there produces a larger secondary viraemia, and the target organ is reached.","A":"Transformation is an oncogenic outcome, not a mode of spread. The other route worth knowing is NEURAL spread along axons — how rabies and HSV reach the CNS."},"t":"Viral spread","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L11_30.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Viral spread</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Evading NK cells after losing MHC I","s":"A virus strongly down-regulates host MHC class I on infected cells, which lets it escape cytotoxic T cells. Surprisingly, the infected cells are not killed by NK cells either. Which strategy on the lecture's NK-evasion slide best explains the second observation?","lec":"L11","sl":53,"c":"<b>Evading NK cells after losing MHC I</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mutating surface epitopes so that neutralising antibody no longer binds","B":"Increasing expression of ligands for the NK activating receptor on the cell","C":"Expressing a viral MHC class I homologue that engages the NK inhibitory receptor","D":"Up-regulating host complement regulators on the infected cell surface","E":"Secreting a mimic of an NK-stimulating cytokine that over-activates NK cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"That is antigenic variation — evasion of antibody (slide 56), not of NK cells.","B":"The slide lists DOWN-regulating or antagonising activating ligands; increasing them would invite NK killing.","C":"Correct. Losing MHC I should trigger NK killing ('missing self'). Slide 53's first mechanism, 'mimetic', is a viral MHC class I homologue that engages the MHC I inhibitory receptor on the NK cell and switches it off (a related route, 'substitution', keeps HLA-C/HLA-E).","D":"Mimicry or up-regulation of complement regulators evades complement (slide 54), not NK cells.","E":"The slide lists INHIBITION of NK-stimulating cytokines and chemokines, not their mimicry."},"img":"slides_i/L11_53.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which of the following is an immune-evasion strategy the lecture attributes to viruses?","o":{"D":"Producing interferons to protect the infected cell","C":"Producing VIROKINES — viral proteins that mimic host cytokines or their receptors and jam normal immune signalling","B":"Increasing MHC class I expression so infected cells are recognised faster","E":"Destroying plasma cells directly in the bone marrow","A":"Inducing apoptosis of the infected cell to release progeny sooner"},"r":{"D":"Interferon is the HOST's antiviral defence; viruses work to BLOCK its synthesis and signalling, not to produce it.","C":"Correct. Virokines and viral cytokine-receptor mimics are on the evasion slide, and this exact idea reappeared as a poxvirus clinical stem in the most recent paper.","B":"Viruses DOWN-regulate MHC I to hide from cytotoxic T cells — which then exposes them to NK cells, so they must evade those too.","E":"Direct destruction of plasma cells is not a listed mechanism, and it has been used as a wrong option in a real paper.","A":"Inducing apoptosis of the host cell is a host defence that limits virus production; viruses more often DELAY it."},"t":"Immune evasion menu","sl":52,"img":"slides_i/L11_52.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Immune evasion menu</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"GI route — M cells","s":"In a nursery outbreak, a naked virus swallowed with contaminated food crosses the intestinal lining and quickly reaches the bloodstream. According to the lecture, which cell does the virus infect to achieve this?","lec":"L11","sl":22,"c":"<b>GI route — M cells</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Keratinised squamous cells","B":"M cells","C":"Mucus-secreting goblet cells","D":"Lamina propria macrophages","E":"Ciliated epithelial cells"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Keratinised squamous epithelium is skin, a natural barrier that needs a break (slide 20).","B":"Correct. Slide 22: in the GI tract, viruses can infect M cells and easily reach the bloodstream. M cells sample the gut lumen for the immune system, which makes them a built-in door.","C":"Mucus is a barrier, not the portal named for GI entry.","D":"Macrophages are listed as a respiratory barrier; the lecture names M cells as the GI portal.","E":"Ciliated cells are one of the respiratory tract's barriers (slide 21), not the GI portal."},"img":"slides_i/L11_22.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which is the MOST COMMON route of viral entry into the human body?","o":{"C":"Gastrointestinal tract","B":"Respiratory tract","A":"Skin","E":"Urogenital tract","D":"Conjunctiva"},"r":{"C":"The GI tract is second and is the route for the naked, acid-resistant viruses.","B":"Correct, and the slide says it in as many words: the respiratory tract is the most common route of viral entry, by aerosolised droplets from coughing and sneezing or by contact with saliva.","A":"Skin is a natural barrier and needs a break, a bite or an injection.","E":"The urogenital route matters for HSV, HPV and HBV but is far from the commonest overall.","D":"Conjunctival entry is explicitly described as a RARE route."},"t":"Routes of entry","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L11_21.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Routes of entry</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Immunopathology — cytokine excess","s":"A previously healthy adult with severe viral pneumonia deteriorates on day 8 even though serial respiratory swabs show a falling viral load. Serum IL-6 and TNF are markedly raised, and imaging shows diffuse inflammatory lung damage. Which mechanism best explains the deterioration?","lec":"L11","sl":61,"c":"<b>Immunopathology — cytokine excess</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Immunopathology from an excess of proinflammatory cytokines","B":"Reactivation of a latent virus in lung tissue after immune suppression","C":"Direct cytopathic lysis of alveolar cells at peak viral replication","D":"Viroceptors sequestering host cytokines away from their receptors","E":"Antibody-dependent enhancement of viral entry into macrophages"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 61: an overt immune response sometimes harms the host — first example, an excess of proinflammatory cytokines triggering an excessive response (a cytokine storm). The clue is worsening while the virus is falling.","B":"Nothing suggests a latent virus or immune suppression; the cytokines point to an over-active response, not a failed one.","C":"Direct lysis would track viral load; here the load is falling while the patient worsens.","D":"Viroceptors would reduce cytokine signalling; this patient has too much of it.","E":"ADE (dengue on slide 61) needs pre-existing non-neutralising antibody from an earlier infection and increases viral load — neither fits."},"img":"slides_i/L11_61.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What does the lecture mean by IMMUNOPATHOLOGY in viral infection?","o":{"A":"Damage caused by the host's own immune response rather than by the virus itself, e.g. a cytokine storm","E":"Failure of the immune system to respond at all","D":"The virus attacking immune cells directly","B":"Autoantibodies present before the infection began","C":"A vaccine failing to generate protective antibody"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Sometimes the response does more harm than the virus: an excess of pro-inflammatory cytokines — a cytokine storm — is the example the lecture gives, and it is what kills in severe influenza and COVID-19.","E":"Failure to respond is immunodeficiency, the opposite problem.","D":"A virus attacking immune cells is direct cytopathology, as in HIV.","B":"Pre-existing autoantibody is autoimmunity unconnected to this infection.","C":"Vaccine failure is an immunisation issue, not immunopathology."},"t":"Immunopathology","sl":61,"img":"slides_i/L11_61.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Immunopathology</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-phase","t":"When to sample for virus","s":"A 27-year-old man presents with a systemic viral infection. Which period is BEST for testing for the PRESENCE OF THE VIRUS?","lec":"L11","sl":16,"c":"<b>When to sample for virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q47 · BM31 SUM1 · BM29 SUM1 · BM28 Q8 — the single most repeated virology item in the archive, asked in four cohorts with identical options</span>","o":{"A":"Acute phase","B":"Incubation period","E":"Prodrome","D":"Onset","C":"Recovery (convalescent) phase"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. On the lecture's curve, viral titre PEAKS in the acute phase, which is when antigen and nucleic-acid detection are most sensitive. Note what the question asks — the VIRUS, not the antibody.","B":"Titre is still climbing and may be below the limit of detection.","E":"Prodrome is early and the load is still low.","D":"Onset is the very start of the acute illness; the peak has not been reached.","C":"By convalescence the virus is being cleared. Convalescence is when you look for ANTIBODY — and a four-fold rise between acute and convalescent serum is the classic serological proof. Getting the two halves of that curve the wrong way round is the whole trap."},"img":"slides_i/L11_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q47","BM29 Q-phase","BM28 Q8"]},{"id":"I|L11|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q46","t":"What is true of viral pathogenesis","s":"Which statement about viral pathogenesis is CORRECT?","lec":"L11","sl":17,"c":"<b>What is true of viral pathogenesis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q46 — 'which statement about viral pathogenesis is CORRECT?' The BM30 key writes out why each distractor is false.</span>","o":{"D":"Specific antibody first appears during the convalescent phase","B":"Mother-to-child transmission is a form of horizontal transmission","A":"The GI tract is the most common route of viral entry","C":"Tissue tropism is determined by the nucleic acid type of the virus","E":"How severely a given virus affects one person depends partly on that person's own genetic make-up"},"a":"E","r":{"D":"Specific antibody begins to rise in the ACUTE phase and peaks in convalescence. (In a previously vaccinated person it can be detected very early indeed.)","B":"Mother to child is VERTICAL transmission. Horizontal is between individuals in a population.","A":"The RESPIRATORY tract is the most common route; the slide says so explicitly.","C":"Tropism is set by receptors, proteases and transcription factors — not by whether the genome is DNA or RNA.","E":"Correct. The host-factor slide lists immunity, genetics, general health and exposure level — outcome is an interplay of viral AND host factors, not a property of the virus alone."},"img":"slides_i/L11_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q48","t":"Cytopathic effect — owl's eye","s":"A 48-year-old is admitted to ICU with fever and abdominal pain from CMV infection. Which cytopathic effect would be found in the tissue?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Cytopathic effect — owl's eye</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q48 — ICU patient with CMV infection; what CPE is seen in tissue?</span>","o":{"D":"Negri bodies","C":"Owl's eye appearance — a large intranuclear inclusion with a clear halo","E":"Molluscum bodies","A":"Syncytia formation","B":"Koilocytes"},"a":"C","r":{"D":"Negri bodies are the intracytoplasmic inclusion of RABIES — an RNA virus, exactly as the rule predicts.","C":"Correct, and it follows from the rule: CMV is a DNA (herpes) virus, so its inclusion is INTRANUCLEAR, and the classic description is the owl's eye.","E":"Molluscum bodies belong to molluscum contagiosum (a poxvirus), and note that poxvirus is the DNA virus that replicates in the CYTOPLASM, so its inclusion is cytoplasmic too.","A":"Syncytia are seen with RSV, measles, HSV and VZV.","B":"Koilocytes are the HPV change on a Pap smear (L13)."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q3","t":"HSV — outcome of infection","s":"Which is an outcome of herpes simplex virus infection of host cells?","lec":"L11","sl":35,"c":"<b>HSV — outcome of infection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q3 (examiner-written, official answer CELL FUSION)</span>","o":{"C":"Cell lysis","E":"Cell division","B":"Transformation","A":"Cell detachment","D":"Cell fusion"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"HSV does lyse cells, but the option the examiner wanted is the one unique to it. When two options are both partly true, take the more SPECIFIC one.","E":"Viruses do not drive normal cell division; a virus that drives proliferation is doing TRANSFORMATION.","B":"HSV is not an oncogenic herpesvirus; EBV and HHV-8 are (L13).","A":"Detachment is a non-specific CPE seen with many viruses, not the HSV signature.","D":"Correct, and it is the examiner's own answer key. HSV alters the infected cell membrane so infected and uninfected cells fuse into a MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELL — which is precisely what you see on a Tzanck smear, and why that smear is diagnostic."},"img":"slides_i/L11_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L11|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q51","t":"Tropism in a clinical stem","s":"Virus X replicates in neural cells but not in liver cells. What primarily determines this pattern?","lec":"L11","sl":28,"c":"<b>Tropism in a clinical stem</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q51 — virus X replicates in neural cells but not liver cells; what determines this?</span>","o":{"A":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","B":"Presence of co-infecting bacteria","C":"Viral mutation rate","D":"The host's cytokine profile","E":"Tropism — the match between the viral attachment protein and a receptor present on the host cell"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"ADE is a dengue mechanism (L14) that worsens disease; it does not set tropism.","B":"Bacterial co-infection does not create viral receptors.","C":"Mutation rate affects evolution over time, not the tissue pattern of a single infection.","D":"Cytokines shape the RESPONSE to infection, not which cells are permissive.","E":"Correct. No receptor, no entry. Receptor distribution is the first and strongest determinant of which tissue a virus can infect, which is why poliovirus hits motor neurons and HIV hits CD4+ cells."},"img":"slides_i/L11_28.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q49","t":"Epidemiological measures","s":"Which epidemiological measure best describes the number of NEW cases of influenza in a population during a specified time period?","lec":"L11","sl":11,"c":"<b>Epidemiological measures</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q49 — which measure describes the number of NEW influenza cases in a period?</span>","o":{"E":"Incidence","A":"Prevalence","B":"Outbreak","C":"Morbidity rate","D":"Endemic"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct — new cases over a defined period is the definition of incidence.","A":"Prevalence counts ALL existing cases, old and new, at a point in time.","B":"An outbreak is a sudden rise in cases in a limited area; it is a description of occurrence, not a measure.","C":"Morbidity rate is the general rate of illness in a population and is not specific to new cases.","D":"Endemic describes a disease being continuously present at a baseline level."},"img":"slides_i/L11_11.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q50","t":"Transmission routes","s":"Which of the following viruses is commonly transmitted by the RESPIRATORY route?","lec":"L11","sl":46,"c":"<b>Transmission routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q50 — which virus is commonly transmitted by the respiratory route?</span>","o":{"A":"Influenza virus","D":"Hepatitis B virus","C":"Dengue virus","E":"HTLV-1","B":"Hepatitis C virus"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Influenza spreads by aerosolised droplets from coughing and sneezing — the respiratory tract is both its portal of entry and its portal of exit.","D":"HBV is blood-borne, sexual and vertical — the slide lists it under transmucosal/urogenital and blood.","C":"Dengue is vector-borne by Aedes (L14).","E":"HTLV-1 is transmitted by breast milk, sexual contact and blood.","B":"HCV is predominantly blood-borne."},"img":"slides_i/L11_46.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q52","t":"Rabies inclusion","s":"What structure is characteristically found in a rabies-infected neuron?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Rabies inclusion</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q52 — what structure is found in a rabies-infected cell?</span>","o":{"E":"Molluscum body","A":"Owl's eye intranuclear inclusion","B":"Koilocyte","C":"Multinucleated giant cell","D":"Negri body — an intracytoplasmic inclusion"},"a":"D","r":{"E":"Molluscum bodies are molluscum contagiosum.","A":"Owl's eye is CMV — a DNA virus, hence intranuclear.","B":"Koilocytes are HPV.","C":"Giant cells are HSV, VZV, measles and RSV.","D":"Correct, and the location is not arbitrary: rabies is an RNA virus (Rhabdoviridae), RNA viruses replicate in the CYTOPLASM, so the inclusion is cytoplasmic. Negri bodies are found in hippocampal and Purkinje neurons."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q54","t":"Virokines","s":"A patient with poxvirus infection is found to have reduced complement activity because the virus secretes a protein that MIMICS a host complement regulator. What is the role of this viral product?","lec":"L11","sl":52,"c":"<b>Virokines</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q54 — a poxvirus secretes a protein mimicking a host complement receptor, reducing complement activity; what is this product's role?</span>","o":{"D":"It serves as a viral adhesion molecule for attachment","E":"It functions as the viral polymerase","C":"Immune evasion — it acts as a decoy for a host immune mediator and blocks complement-mediated destruction","A":"It is a structural capsid protein","B":"It is a viral protease needed for maturation"},"a":"C","r":{"D":"An adhesion molecule would INCREASE attachment; this protein is secreted to neutralise a host defence.","E":"A secreted decoy has no polymerase function, and polymerase acts inside the cell.","C":"Correct. This is molecular mimicry used for immune evasion: by copying a host complement regulator, the virus prevents complement from opsonising and lysing infected cells. The lecture groups it with virokines and viroceptors.","A":"It is secreted, not packaged into the particle.","B":"Proteases act on viral polyprotein inside the cell and do not touch complement."},"img":"slides_i/L11_52.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L11|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q53","t":"HSV recurrence","s":"A 40-year-old woman has recurrent cold sores. HSV is confirmed. Which mechanism explains the recurrences?","lec":"L11","sl":42,"c":"<b>HSV recurrence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q53 & Q71 — recurrent cold sores; what mechanism is responsible?</span>","o":{"A":"Repeated re-infection from new external exposures","E":"Continuous low-grade replication in the skin that never fully clears","C":"Latency — the genome persists in sensory ganglion neurons without active replication, then reactivates","B":"Integration of the viral genome into the host chromosome, transforming the cell","D":"Antigenic drift producing a new serotype each episode"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Re-infection cannot explain why the lesion returns to exactly the same site.","E":"If it replicated continuously the lesion would never heal between episodes.","C":"Correct. HSV establishes latency in sensory ganglia — the trigeminal ganglion for HSV-1. The genome sits there making almost no protein, so the cell presents little antigen and cytotoxic T cells cannot find it; stress, fever or UV light reactivate it and the virus travels back down the axon to the same dermatome.","B":"HSV does not integrate, and it does not transform cells.","D":"Drift would give a genuinely new antigenic strain; recurrence here is the SAME virus waking up. The same latency principle explains shingles from VZV."},"img":"slides_i/L11_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM32 Q71"]},{"id":"I|L11|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q7","t":"Determinants of severity","s":"Which of the following is NOT a factor that makes a viral infection more severe?","lec":"L11","sl":17,"c":"<b>Determinants of severity</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 virology Q7 — which is NOT a factor making a viral infection severe? (recorded answer: antiviral drug)</span>","o":{"B":"The host's age and immune status","D":"The infecting dose of virus","A":"Administration of an effective antiviral drug","E":"The route of entry","C":"The virulence of the strain"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Age and immune status are listed host factors; the very young, the old and the immunocompromised do worse.","D":"A larger infecting dose gives more severe disease.","A":"Correct as the NEGATIVE answer. An effective antiviral makes the infection LESS severe — it belongs on the other side of the ledger from every other option.","E":"Route matters — the same virus delivered intravenously can be far worse than one acquired across a mucosa.","C":"Strain virulence is a viral factor and directly affects severity."},"img":"slides_i/L11_17.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L11|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Stage-of-illness table — the incubation row","s":"A household contact of a confirmed case is tested on day 3 after exposure while he feels completely well. His throat-swab PCR and his serum virus-specific antibody are both negative, but he develops the illness four days later. Which statement fits the lecture's stage-of-illness table?","lec":"L11","sl":15,"c":"<b>Stage-of-illness table — the incubation row</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"He was not yet infected on day 3, since virus is detectable at every stage of a true infection","B":"He was in the onset phase, when virus is rarely detectable and antibody is usually present","C":"He was in the incubation period, when virus is frequently detectable and antibody occasionally present","D":"He was in the prodrome, when virus is frequently detectable and antibody is absent","E":"He was in the incubation period, when virus is rarely detectable and antibody is absent"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The table itself says virus is rarely detectable in incubation, so a negative test on day 3 is expected in a true infection.","B":"Onset needs symptoms; and at onset virus is frequently detectable and antibody only occasionally present.","C":"Right stage, wrong row: incubation is 'rarely' for virus and 'no' for antibody.","D":"He had no symptoms, so this is not the prodrome; and the table gives the prodrome 'occasionally', not 'frequently'.","E":"Correct. Symptom-free after exposure, then ill = incubation (below the symptom threshold on slide 15). Slide 16: incubation — virus detectable RARELY, specific antibody NO. So a negative PCR and serology do not exclude infection."},"img":"slides_i/L11_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"The lecture tabulates three viruses against pathogenicity and virulence. Which row is correct, and what does it illustrate?","o":{"E":"Measles virus — HIGH pathogenicity but LOW virulence: almost everyone exposed becomes ill, yet the illness is usually not severe","C":"Measles virus — low pathogenicity but high virulence: few people are infected, but those who are die","B":"Rabies virus — high pathogenicity but low virulence","A":"Hepatitis A virus — low pathogenicity but high virulence","D":"All three viruses have identical pathogenicity and virulence, since the two terms mean the same thing"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and the measles row is the whole point of the table: the two properties are independent. Pathogenicity is the ABSOLUTE ability to cause disease; virulence is how badly it damages the host.","C":"Inverted. Measles is famously highly infectious and pathogenic but not usually lethal in a healthy host.","B":"Rabies is HIGH on both — that is why it is placed at the bottom of the table.","A":"HAV is LOW on both.","D":"If they meant the same thing the table would have one column. The deck gives them separate definitions."},"t":"The pathogenicity/virulence table","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L11_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The pathogenicity/virulence table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Negri body vs owl's eye — location and reason","s":"Patient X died of encephalitis several weeks after a bite from a stray dog. Patient Y, a kidney-transplant recipient, died of pneumonitis with markedly enlarged cells in the lung. Which statement correctly contrasts the inclusion bodies expected in their tissues?","lec":"L11","sl":38,"c":"<b>Negri body vs owl's eye — location and reason</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"X: intranuclear inclusions in neurons; Y: intranuclear inclusions in enlarged cells","B":"X: multinucleated giant neurons; Y: cytoplasmic inclusions in enlarged cells","C":"X: intranuclear inclusions in neurons; Y: cytoplasmic inclusions in enlarged cells","D":"X: cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons; Y: intranuclear inclusions in enlarged cells","E":"X: cytoplasmic inclusions in macrophages; Y: intranuclear inclusions in enlarged cells"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"X is wrong: rabies is an RNA virus, so its inclusion is cytoplasmic (quiz slide 66's other trap, 'nucleus of neurons').","B":"Multinucleated giant cells are syncytia (slide 37), not the rabies finding; and CMV's inclusion is intranuclear.","C":"Both locations inverted — the rule is DNA → nucleus, RNA → cytoplasm.","D":"Correct — the two boxed rows of slide 38. X = rabies: Negri bodies, intracytoplasmic, in neurons (rabies is an RNA virus, and RNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm). Y = CMV: intranuclear basophilic owl's-eye inclusion in an enlarged cell (a DNA virus, replicating in the nucleus).","E":"Quiz slide 66's trap: the Negri body is in the cytoplasm of NEURONS, not macrophages."},"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Brain tissue from a patient who died after a dog bite shows eosinophilic inclusion bodies in the CYTOPLASM of neurons. Which statement explains this?","o":{"D":"Rabies is an RNA virus, and inclusion bodies mark the site of viral transcription and genome replication — cytoplasmic for RNA viruses, intranuclear for DNA viruses","C":"Rabies is a DNA virus, so its inclusions are intranuclear; a cytoplasmic location indicates a different diagnosis","B":"Inclusion bodies are aggregates of host debris and carry no information about the virus's genome type","A":"Cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons indicate herpes simplex encephalitis","E":"The inclusions are multinucleated syncytia seen end-on"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the rule generalises: find the inclusion in the nucleus and think DNA virus; find it in the cytoplasm and think RNA virus. Rabies’ cytoplasmic neuronal inclusions are Negri bodies.","C":"Rabies is an RNA virus (Rhabdoviridae), so cytoplasmic inclusions are exactly what is expected.","B":"The slide defines them as the SITES of viral transcription and genome replication — they are highly informative.","A":"HSV is a DNA virus and gives INTRANUCLEAR inclusions (Cowdry A).","E":"Syncytia are multinucleated cells, a separate cytopathic effect listed on its own slide."},"t":"Inclusion bodies — where and whose","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L11_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Inclusion bodies — where and whose</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Virokine vs viroceptor — classify two proteins","s":"Virus P secretes a soluble protein that binds host TNF in the tissue fluid, so TNF cannot reach its receptors on cells. Virus Q secretes a protein that resembles host IL-10 and binds the IL-10 receptor on immune cells, damping their signalling. How does the lecture classify these two viral proteins?","lec":"L11","sl":52,"c":"<b>Virokine vs viroceptor — classify two proteins</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"P makes a viroceptor; Q makes a virokine","B":"Both make viroceptors, as both act on cytokine pathways","C":"Both make virokines, as both are secreted","D":"P mimics a complement regulator; Q makes a viroceptor","E":"P makes a virokine; Q makes a viroceptor"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 52: a viroCEPTOR is a soluble viral protein that mimics a host cytokine RECEPTOR and binds and sequesters the cytokine (P). A viroKINE mimics the host CYTOKINE itself and binds host cytokine receptors to disturb normal signalling (Q). The suffix says what is copied.","B":"Q copies a cytokine and binds the host's receptor, which makes it a virokine.","C":"Being secreted does not decide it; P copies a receptor, so it is a viroceptor.","D":"P binds a cytokine, not a complement component; complement-regulator mimicry is slide 54 (and the BM32 poxvirus item).","E":"The two definitions swapped — the commonest error on this pair."},"img":"slides_i/L11_52.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A virus encodes a soluble protein that resembles the host's cytokine receptor, binds the cytokine in solution, and prevents it reaching cells. What is this called, and how does it differ from the related strategy?","o":{"A":"A VIROCEPTOR — it mimics a host RECEPTOR and sequesters the cytokine; a virokine instead mimics the CYTOKINE itself and binds host receptors to misdirect signalling","E":"A VIROKINE — it mimics a host receptor; a viroceptor mimics the cytokine","C":"An interferon receptor decoy, which is a form of antigenic variation","D":"A viroceptor — it mimics a host cytokine and binds host cytokine receptors","B":"Complement mimicry, since both involve soluble regulatory proteins"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The suffix tells you what is being copied: viroCEPTOR copies a receptor, viroKINE copies a cytokine.","E":"The two definitions are swapped.","C":"IFN receptor decoys are a related idea but sit on the interferon slide; antigenic variation is a mutation strategy, not a decoy protein.","D":"That description is a VIROKINE.","B":"Complement mimicry is a separate evasion route listed on the complement slide."},"t":"Virokines vs viroceptors","sl":51,"img":"slides_i/L11_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Virokines vs viroceptors</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Immunopathology — two mechanisms in one patient","s":"A man has had a chronic viral hepatitis with persistently positive surface antigen for 20 years. His liver enzymes flare repeatedly although the virus does little direct damage to the cells it infects, and he now develops a vasculitic rash with circulating antigen–antibody complexes. Which pair of mechanisms explains his liver injury and his vasculitis?","lec":"L11","sl":61,"c":"<b>Immunopathology — two mechanisms in one patient</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Direct viral lysis of hepatocytes; antibody-dependent enhancement in vessels","B":"CTL killing of infected hepatocytes; immune complexes deposited in vessels","C":"Transformation of hepatocytes; direct viral infection of the vessel wall","D":"Antibody-dependent enhancement in the liver; excess proinflammatory cytokines","E":"Immune complexes deposited in the liver; CTL attack on infected endothelium"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The stem says the virus does little direct damage; ADE is the dengue example and does not cause vasculitis on this slide.","B":"Correct. Slide 61 names hepatitis B twice: the CTL response (with HCV) damages infected hepatocytes, and immune-complex formation causes vasculitis. Both are immunopathology — the host response, not the virus, does the harm.","C":"Transformation causes tumours, not enzyme flares, and the vasculitis is immune-complex mediated.","D":"ADE is the dengue example; cytokine excess does not explain circulating immune complexes.","E":"The two mechanisms are attached to the wrong organs."},"img":"slides_i/L11_61.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of immunopathological mechanism with its example matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Antibody-dependent enhancement — dengue virus; cytotoxic T lymphocyte-mediated damage — hepatitis B and C; immune-complex vasculitis — hepatitis B","E":"Antibody-dependent enhancement — hepatitis B; CTL-mediated damage — dengue; immune-complex vasculitis — rabies","C":"All four examples describe direct viral cytopathic damage rather than host immune damage","D":"Immune-complex vasculitis — dengue; ADE — hepatitis C; CTL damage — measles","A":"Excess proinflammatory cytokines is the only listed mechanism; the others are hypothetical"},"r":{"B":"Correct on all three. Note hepatitis B appears TWICE, by two different mechanisms — CTL killing of infected hepatocytes and immune-complex vasculitis.","E":"Every pairing is wrong; ADE is the dengue mechanism and underlies severe secondary dengue.","C":"The whole point of immunopathology is that the HOST response causes the damage.","D":"All three pairings are scrambled.","A":"Four mechanisms are listed with named examples."},"t":"Immunopathology — four named examples","sl":61,"img":"slides_i/L11_61.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Immunopathology — four named examples</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"What determines shedding","s":"Two children have the same diarrhoeal illness. Child 1 passes a trace of an enveloped virus for two days, and the virus dies within hours on surfaces. Child 2 passes large quantities of a naked virus for three weeks, and it stays infectious for weeks on toys. The lecture attributes child 2's greater risk to others to which set of factors?","lec":"L11","sl":44,"c":"<b>What determines shedding</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The receptors, transcription regulators and proteases of cells","B":"The pathogenicity, virulence and infecting dose of the agent","C":"The amount, duration and environmental survival of shed virus","D":"The age, general health and immune status of the patient","E":"The incubation, prodromal and convalescent periods of illness"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"These are the determinants of tissue tropism (slide 29).","B":"These are agent properties from the epidemiology section (slides 6–7); they describe disease in the host, not how much virus leaves it.","C":"Correct. Slide 44: shedding (release of infectious virions from the host) depends on amount, duration and survival time in the environment. Child 2 wins on all three.","D":"These are the 'status of the person' factors in pathogenesis (slide 17).","E":"These are the stages of disease development (slide 15)."},"img":"slides_i/L11_44.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of shedding route with virus matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Faeces — reovirus, hepatitis A and poliovirus; aerosols and pharyngeal secretions — rhinovirus and influenza; saliva — rabies; semen — HIV","D":"Faeces — rhinovirus and influenza; aerosols — hepatitis A and poliovirus","E":"Skin lesions — poliovirus and reovirus; saliva — papillomavirus","A":"Urine is not a route of viral shedding in any infection","B":"Breast milk carries only bacterial pathogens, never viruses"},"r":{"C":"Correct across all four routes. The exit route predicts the transmission route, which is why this slide answers prevention questions too.","D":"The two routes are swapped — HAV and poliovirus are the classic faecal–oral enteroviruses.","E":"Poliovirus and reovirus are shed in faeces; papillomavirus is shed from SKIN.","A":"Viruria is explicitly listed, occurring in several systemic viral infections.","B":"Breast milk is named as a route for HBV, HIV, HCV and CMV."},"t":"Shedding route → virus","sl":44,"img":"slides_i/L11_44.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Shedding route → virus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Neurotropic spread","s":"A virus reaches the brain by travelling inside the axons of peripheral nerves, where circulating antibody cannot reach it. Which statement about this form of spread matches the lecture?","lec":"L11","sl":30,"c":"<b>Neurotropic spread</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It usually begins with direct inoculation into a neuron","B":"It is the form of spread that defines a localized infection","C":"It is usually preceded by a primary viraemia","D":"It is the form of spread started by arthropod vectors","E":"It gives the virus access to almost every tissue"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The slide says direct inoculation to neurons as the primary site is RARE.","B":"A localized infection stays at the site of entry; reaching the brain is dissemination.","C":"Correct. Slide 30: neurotropic spread is usually preceded by primary viraemia; direct inoculation to neurons as the primary site of infection is rare. Named examples: rabies virus and the alpha-herpesviruses.","D":"Arthropod vectors, transfusion and IV drug use put virus directly into the BLOOD (haematogenous spread).","E":"That is the property of haematogenous spread — once in the blood, access to almost every tissue."},"img":"slides_i/L11_30.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about how viruses spread within the host matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Neurotropic spread is usually preceded by a primary viraemia; direct inoculation into neurons as the primary site is rare. Rabies virus and the alphaherpesviruses are the named neurotropic examples","B":"Neurotropic spread always begins with direct inoculation into a nerve, which is why rabies follows a bite","A":"Haematogenous spread is restricted to the organ of entry, which is what makes an infection localised","E":"Arthropod vectors, transfusion and intravenous drug use all deliver virus into the nervous system directly","D":"A localised infection is one that replicates in the initial organ before spreading elsewhere"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the ‘usually preceded by primary viraemia’ clause is the detail most people miss — even neurotropic viruses normally reach the nerve through the blood first.","B":"Direct inoculation to neurons as the PRIMARY site is described as rare.","A":"Once in the blood the virus has access to almost every tissue — that is the definition of systemic spread.","E":"Those three deliver virus into the BLOOD, which is haematogenous, not neurotropic.","D":"That describes a SYSTEMIC infection; a localised one replicates only at the site of entry."},"t":"Two kinds of spread","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L11_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Two kinds of spread</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Susceptible but non-permissive","s":"In cell culture, a virus binds its receptor and enters cell line S exactly as it does in cell line T, but only T yields new virions. By the lecture's tropism slides, how is cell line S best described?","lec":"L11","sl":28,"c":"<b>Susceptible but non-permissive</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Inaccessible — a physical barrier shields it from contact with the virus","B":"Transformed — it lacks the growth suppressors that limit cell division","C":"Latently infected — it lacks the MHC class I needed to display antigen","D":"Non-susceptible — it lacks the receptor needed for viral attachment","E":"Non-permissive — it lacks cellular proteins that regulate viral transcription"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The virus reached and entered S, so S was accessible (slide 17: accessible, susceptible and permissive are separate requirements).","B":"Transformation is a consequence of integrating viral DNA, not a cause of failed replication.","C":"Latency is a pattern of infection (slides 41, 59), not a reason a culture yields no progeny from a fresh infection; low MHC I is a host-evasion feature.","D":"S binds and enters normally, so it has the receptor.","E":"Correct. Slide 28's non-permissive cell includes the path 'binding → entry → no replication' (non-productive or abortive infection). Slide 29 explains why: tropism needs cellular proteins that regulate viral transcription and cell proteases/enzymes, not just a receptor."},"img":"slides_i/L11_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A virus binds its receptor on a cell but no progeny virus is produced. Which statement best explains this, per the lecture?","o":{"E":"The cell is susceptible but NOT PERMISSIVE — tropism needs more than a receptor: cellular proteins that regulate viral transcription, plus proteases and other enzymes, must also be present","A":"The cell lacks the receptor, which is the sole determinant of tropism","B":"The virus is pantropic, and pantropic viruses cannot complete replication in any single tissue","D":"The cell is permissive but not accessible, which is the same thing as non-susceptible","C":"This cannot happen — receptor binding is both necessary and sufficient for productive infection"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Susceptible means it can get in; permissive means it can complete the cycle. The determinants slide lists three requirements, only one of which is the receptor.","A":"If the receptor were the sole determinant, the non-permissive cell would not exist as a category.","B":"Pantropic means it replicates in MANY organs — the opposite of the stem.","D":"Accessible, susceptible and permissive are three different requirements, not synonyms.","C":"The permissive/non-permissive distinction exists precisely because it is not sufficient."},"t":"Determinants of tissue tropism","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L11_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Determinants of tissue tropism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Latency — where it hides and how it returns","s":"A 75-year-old man who had a generalised itchy vesicular rash as a child now develops painful vesicles confined to a single band on one side of his trunk. Where did the virus reside in the intervening decades, and how did it reach the skin this time?","lec":"L11","sl":42,"c":"<b>Latency — where it hides and how it returns</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In a dorsal root ganglion neuron, then through a secondary viraemia","B":"In a dorsal root ganglion neuron, then down the peripheral nerve","C":"In skin keratinocytes, replicating slowly and continuously for decades","D":"In circulating lymphocytes, then through a secondary viraemia","E":"In spinal cord motor neurons, then up the peripheral nerve"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right site, wrong route: a dermatomal band reflects one nerve, not blood-borne spread.","B":"Correct. Childhood varicella → zoster. Slide 42: after primary infection the virus transits UP the peripheral nerve to latency in a dorsal root ganglion neuron; age and X-irradiation (acting via depressed CMI) reactivate it, and it transits DOWN the peripheral nerve to the dermatome.","C":"Latent virus does not replicate (slide 59); continuous low-level replication would be a chronic or slow pattern.","D":"The latency site on slide 42 is the ganglion neuron, not lymphocytes.","E":"Latency is in the sensory (dorsal root) ganglion; 'up the nerve' is the direction of the primary infection, not of recurrence."},"img":"slides_i/L11_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why is latency in a neuron such an effective immune-evasion strategy for herpes simplex virus?","o":{"B":"The virus neither replicates actively nor makes much viral protein, so there is little antigen to present — and neurons express MHC class I at low levels, so even what is made is poorly displayed to cytotoxic T cells","E":"Neurons express MHC class I at unusually HIGH levels, which paralyses cytotoxic T cells by overstimulation","C":"Latent virus replicates rapidly but inside a membrane vesicle that antibodies cannot reach","A":"Neurons lack receptors for the virus, so the infection is abortive rather than latent","D":"Latency occurs only in dendritic cells, whose maturation the virus blocks"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the two halves compound: almost no antigen made, in a cell type that barely displays antigen anyway.","E":"Neurons are named specifically as LOW MHC class I expressers.","C":"Latency is defined by NOT actively replicating.","A":"Neurons are the site of HSV latency, so they clearly bear the receptor; abortive infection is a different outcome.","D":"Dendritic cell impairment is a separate evasion strategy on its own slide; HSV latency is neuronal."},"t":"Latency and MHC I","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L11_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Latency and MHC I</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Endemic vs epidemic vs pandemic","s":"A mosquito-borne viral fever is reported in one province every year at a fairly steady level. One rainy season the number of cases there rises far above what is normally expected, although cases stay confined to that region. How does the lecture classify the usual situation, and then that season?","lec":"L11","sl":12,"c":"<b>Endemic vs epidemic vs pandemic</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Endemic, then epidemic","B":"Sporadic, then endemic","C":"Endemic, then pandemic","D":"Epidemic, then pandemic","E":"Epidemic, then endemic"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 12: endemic = regularly and continuously present in a population; epidemic = a significant increase above what is expected in a specific area or group.","B":"A steady yearly presence is endemic, and the rise above expected is the epidemic, not the endemic state.","C":"A pandemic is an epidemic spread across many countries or continents; this one stayed in one province.","D":"The baseline is steady and expected, so it is not an epidemic; and a rise confined to one region is not a pandemic.","E":"The order is reversed."},"img":"slides_i/L11_12.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which set of definitions matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"R₀ is the average number of new infections generated by one infected person; morbidity is the diseased state or disability resulting from a condition; an epidemic is a rise in cases above what is expected in an area, and a pandemic is an epidemic spread across many countries or continents","E":"R₀ is the total number of existing cases at a point in time; morbidity is the proportion who die","B":"Endemic means a disease has been eradicated from a population","C":"Prevalence is the rate of NEW cases in a period and incidence the total existing cases","D":"A pandemic is any disease present continuously in a population"},"r":{"A":"Correct on all four terms.","E":"R₀ is a transmission measure, not a count of existing cases, and morbidity is the diseased state — MORTALITY is the proportion who die.","B":"Endemic means regularly and continuously PRESENT, not eradicated.","C":"Precisely inverted: incidence is new cases over a period, prevalence is existing cases at a point.","D":"That is the definition of ENDEMIC."},"t":"Measures of disease","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L11_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Measures of disease</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Patterns of viral infection — slow infection","s":"A 12-year-old develops progressive intellectual decline and myoclonic jerks. As an infant he had an acute febrile rash illness and recovered fully. The same virus is now found in his brain; it persisted and built up over the years with no illness in between, and disease appeared only at the end. Which pattern on the lecture's 'patterns of viral infection' slide does this represent?","lec":"L11","sl":41,"c":"<b>Patterns of viral infection — slow infection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Chronic infection","B":"Acute infection","C":"Slow infection","D":"Latent infection","E":"Abortive infection"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A chronic infection is persistently infectious from the start (HBV, HCV, JC virus, papillomaviruses on slide 41), not a slow build-up after full recovery.","B":"An acute infection is cleared after one episode.","C":"Correct. Slide 41: a slow infection is an acute episode followed by a long, gradually increasing phase and late disease — its first listed example is measles (SSPE), which this stem describes. SSPE measles is also the second virus in the Cowdry A row of slide 38.","D":"Latent infection gives repeated episodes of the same disease with a non-infectious gap between them (HSV-1, VZV); it does not build up.","E":"An abortive infection produces no progeny, so the virus could not build up in the brain."},"img":"slides_i/L11_41.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of cellular pathogenesis type with its named examples matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Cell death — adenovirus and poliovirus · Transformation — HIV and HPV · Cell fusion with syncytium formation — herpesvirus and paramyxovirus","D":"Cell death — herpesvirus and paramyxovirus · Cell fusion — adenovirus and poliovirus","B":"Transformation — adenovirus and poliovirus · Cell death — HIV and HPV","E":"All three types are produced by every virus; the examples are interchangeable","A":"Transformation is a reversible change in growth rate caused by cytoplasmic viral protein"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Each type has its own mechanism: shutdown of host synthesis, integration altering growth regulators, and membrane change causing fusion.","D":"The two are swapped.","B":"Also swapped.","E":"The examples are chosen because they are characteristic; they are not interchangeable.","A":"Transformation is defined as an IRREVERSIBLE genetic process caused by integration of viral DNA into host DNA."},"t":"Cellular pathogenesis — three types","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L11_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Cellular pathogenesis — three types</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Vertical transmission — perinatal","s":"A woman with active genital vesicles at term gives birth vaginally. She had no illness during pregnancy and the baby is normal at birth, but the baby acquired the virus during passage through the birth canal and develops vesicles and encephalitis in the second week of life. On the lecture's transmission slide, how is this transmission classified?","lec":"L11","sl":46,"c":"<b>Vertical transmission — perinatal</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vertical, germ line","B":"Horizontal, skin contact","C":"Horizontal, sexual","D":"Vertical, perinatal","E":"Vertical, transplacental"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Germ-line transmission is the retrovirus route (integrated provirus inherited), not acquisition in the birth canal.","B":"Mother to child is vertical, not horizontal — BM30 Q46 planted exactly this error.","C":"The virus is sexually transmitted between adults, but passing it from mother to baby at delivery is vertical.","D":"Correct. Slide 46: mother to child is VERTICAL, with three sub-types — transplacental (rubella, CMV), perinatal (HSV, HIV) and germ line (retroviruses). Infection during delivery is perinatal.","E":"Transplacental infection happens in utero, and the baby would usually be affected at birth."},"img":"slides_i/L11_46.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about routes of viral entry matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"The respiratory tract is the commonest route, with mucus, ciliated cells, macrophages and IgG/IgA as its barriers; in the gastrointestinal tract viruses infect M CELLS and thereby reach the bloodstream","B":"The gastrointestinal tract is the commonest route, and its viruses enter through ciliated cells","E":"The conjunctiva is a common route of entry and the barrier there is gastric acid","A":"Blood is not a route of entry because it is an internal compartment","C":"Intact skin is a route of entry for most viruses, which is why gloves are worn"},"r":{"D":"Correct on both. The M-cell detail explains how an enteric virus crosses an epithelium built to keep things out.","B":"The respiratory tract is named as the commonest route; ciliated cells are a respiratory BARRIER, not a GI portal.","E":"The conjunctiva is described as a RARE route, and gastric acid is a GI barrier.","A":"Blood is listed as a route of entry via transfusion and organ transplant.","C":"Skin is a natural BARRIER; a break in it is what allows access."},"t":"Route of entry — the GI detail","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L11_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Route of entry — the GI detail</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Which arm is being evaded","s":"A virus reduces the amount of MHC class I on the surface of infected cells and also prevents those cells from undergoing apoptosis. On the lecture's immune-evasion slides, both strategies are listed under evasion of which arm of immunity?","lec":"L11","sl":57,"c":"<b>Which arm is being evaded</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Natural killer cells","B":"Cell-mediated immunity","C":"The interferon response","D":"Humoral immunity","E":"The complement system"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Loss of MHC I actually exposes cells to NK killing; NK evasion is a separate slide (53).","B":"Correct. Slide 57: viral evasion of cell-mediated immunity = inhibition of antigen presentation to helper T cells and to CTLs (MHC I down-regulation, quiz slide 65) and inhibition of apoptosis.","C":"Interferon evasion = inhibiting IFN synthesis or signalling and IFN receptor decoys (slide 51).","D":"Humoral evasion is antigenic variation — many serotypes, high epitope mutation, drift and shift (slide 56).","E":"Complement evasion = avoiding complement binding to antibody–antigen complexes and mimicking complement regulators (slide 54)."},"img":"slides_i/L11_57.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which list of mechanisms by which viruses evade CELL-MEDIATED immunity matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Inhibiting antigen presentation to helper T cells, inhibiting antigen presentation to cytotoxic T lymphocytes, and inhibiting apoptosis — with dendritic cells additionally compromised in recruitment, antigen uptake, migration, maturation and T-cell activation","E":"Producing interferons, enhancing antigen presentation and stimulating antibody production","A":"Antigenic drift and shift, which is how viruses escape T cells","D":"Inducing apoptosis in every infected cell so that no antigen survives to be presented","C":"Blocking complement binding to antigen–antibody complexes"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Note that inhibiting apoptosis is an evasion strategy — keeping the infected cell alive denies the host its main way of aborting the infection.","E":"These are all things that HELP the immune response; the quiz slide uses them as distractors.","A":"Antigenic drift and shift are antibody-evasion strategies, listed under humoral immunity.","D":"The opposite — viruses INHIBIT apoptosis.","C":"That is a complement-evasion mechanism, part of innate rather than cell-mediated immunity."},"t":"Evading cell-mediated immunity","sl":57,"img":"slides_i/L11_57.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Evading cell-mediated immunity</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Iceberg concept — most infections are subclinical","s":"After a community outbreak, a serosurvey shows that most residents with virus-specific antibody recall no illness at all, while a few were hospitalised and one died. Which statement matches how the lecture interprets this?","lec":"L11","sl":14,"c":"<b>Iceberg concept — most infections are subclinical</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Most infections are severe, but symptoms are forgotten before sampling","B":"Most infections are latent, since the virus waits in neurons without symptoms","C":"Most infections are subclinical, as killing the host is not in the virus's interest","D":"Most infections are abortive, since antibody can form without viral replication","E":"Most infections are fatal, and survivors are the ones sampled by serosurveys"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The iceberg says the hidden bulk is truly asymptomatic, not forgotten severe illness.","B":"Latency is a pattern for particular viruses (HSV, VZV), not the lecture's explanation for silent infection in general.","C":"Correct. Slide 14: the majority of viral infections are subclinical; it is not in the interest of the virus to severely harm or kill the host. Slide 40's iceberg shows the same thing — clinical disease is the visible tip, asymptomatic infection the bulk below the waterline.","D":"Abortive infection is a cell-level outcome in a non-permissive cell; it does not explain population-wide silent seroconversion.","E":"The survey itself shows most infected people had no illness, let alone death."},"img":"slides_i/L11_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about viral pathogenesis matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"Most viral infections are subclinical, because severely harming or killing the host is not in the virus's interest; whether disease results depends on the person's age, health and immune status, the viral dose, and the genetics of both host and virus","A":"Most viral infections produce disease; subclinical infection is rare and epidemiologically unimportant","B":"Viral dose is irrelevant — a single virion produces the same outcome as a large inoculum","E":"Host genetics play no part; the outcome is determined entirely by the virus","D":"Killing the host rapidly is the optimal strategy for viral spread"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the evolutionary logic is stated on the slide — a dead host transmits nothing.","A":"The deck states the majority are subclinical.","B":"Viral dose is explicitly listed: more virus increases the risk of disease.","E":"Genetics of both host AND virus are listed under virus–host interaction.","D":"The slide says the opposite in as many words."},"t":"Most infections are silent","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L11_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Most infections are silent</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L11|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Agent, host or environment","s":"In the lecture's worked example of a sexually and blood-transmitted virus that attacks the immune system and predisposes to opportunistic infections, which of the following is classified as an environmental factor?","lec":"L11","sl":10,"c":"<b>Agent, host or environment</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Humans being the virus's primary host","B":"The virus's attack on the host immune system","C":"The immune status of the exposed person","D":"Transmission via blood and sexual contact","E":"Stigma that makes people avoid being tested"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Listed under HOST on slide 10.","B":"Listed under AGENT on slide 10.","C":"A host factor — slide 8: immunity, genetics, health and exposure level affect outcome.","D":"Listed under AGENT on slide 10.","E":"Correct. Slide 10 (AIDS): environment = more STDs → more spread, not reporting cases, poverty → less access to care, discrimination → people avoid testing or treatment. Slide 9: environmental factors include biological, social, cultural and physical aspects."},"img":"slides_i/L11_10.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"In the lecture's HIV worked example, which factors are classed as ENVIRONMENT rather than agent or host?","o":{"E":"A higher background rate of other sexually transmitted diseases, under-reporting of cases, poverty limiting access to healthcare, and discrimination deterring testing and treatment","A":"The virus's attack on the immune system and its transmission by blood, sex and mother-to-child routes","D":"That humans are the primary host of HIV","B":"The CD4 receptor and the CXCR4/CCR5 co-receptors","C":"Only physical factors such as temperature count as environment; social factors do not"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The environment slide states that environmental factors include biological, SOCIAL, CULTURAL and physical aspects — which is why poverty and discrimination belong here.","A":"Those are properties of the AGENT.","D":"That is the HOST.","B":"Receptor and co-receptors are agent/host molecular detail, not environment.","C":"The deck explicitly includes social and cultural factors."},"t":"Agent–host–environment for HIV","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L11_6.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Agent–host–environment for HIV</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233356421},{"id":"I|L12|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Roseola — the herpesvirus among the rashes","s":"Five children are seen in the same clinic morning. Which child's illness is caused by a member of the same virus family as the agent of recurrent cold sores?","lec":"L12","sl":25,"c":"<b>Roseola — the herpesvirus among the rashes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A 5-year-old with mild fever, tender nodes behind the ears and a pink rash that began on the face","B":"A 7-year-old whose bright red cheeks were followed a day later by a lace-like rash on the limbs","C":"A 4-year-old with high fever, cough and red eyes whose red rash began at the hairline and spread downward","D":"A 10-month-old whose pink rash began on the trunk and neck once three days of high fever had settled","E":"A 3-year-old with fever, sore throat and small vesicles and ulcers confined to the back of the throat"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Low fever + posterior auricular nodes + pink facial rash = rubella — Matonaviridae, enveloped (+)ssRNA.","B":"Slapped cheek then a lacy rash = erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) — parvovirus B19, Parvoviridae, non-enveloped ssDNA.","C":"High fever + CCCP + rash starting on the face and spreading downward (cephalocaudal) = measles — a Paramyxoviridae, enveloped (−)ssRNA virus.","D":"Correct. Two steps: rash appearing as the fever subsides, starting on the trunk (densest at the neck, as the lecturer said) in an infant = roseola infantum = HHV-6/HHV-7. Cold sores are HSV-1. Both are Herpesviridae (enveloped dsDNA).","E":"Vesicles/ulcers confined to the oropharynx = herpangina. Despite the name it is NOT a herpesvirus disease: Coxsackie A (or EV71), Picornaviridae, (+)ssRNA — the lecture's naming trap."},"img":"slides_i/L12_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Match each maculopapular exanthem to the ONE feature that identifies it.","o":{"C":"Measles = rash as the fever falls; Roseola = Koplik spots; Rubella = slapped cheek","E":"Measles = slapped cheek; Rubella = Koplik spots; Roseola = lacy rash on the limbs","D":"Measles = Koplik spots; Rubella = posterior auricular/occipital lymphadenopathy; Erythema infectiosum = slapped cheek then lacy rash; Roseola = rash appears AS THE FEVER FALLS","B":"All four begin on the trunk and spread outward, and cannot be told apart clinically","A":"Rubella = Koplik spots; Measles = posterior auricular lymphadenopathy"},"r":{"C":"Rash appearing as the fever breaks is roseola (HHV-6/7), which is the exact trap in one BM30 item.","E":"Slapped cheek is parvovirus B19, not measles. Koplik is measles, not rubella.","D":"Correct. Four diseases, four one-line discriminators — and every past-paper exanthem item in this block is answered by one of them. Learn this row and you have the topic.","B":"They are distinguished clinically, and the exam expects you to do it.","A":"Both halves reversed."},"t":"The discriminator table","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L12_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The discriminator table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Measles — CCCP and vitamin A","s":"An unvaccinated 3-year-old has had 3 days of high fever, cough, runny nose, red watery eyes and discomfort in bright light. Tiny white spots on a red base are seen on the buccal mucosa; the skin is still clear. In class the lecturer explained the eye signs by what this virus does to the child's nutrition. Which measure follows from that explanation?","lec":"L12","sl":10,"c":"<b>Measles — CCCP and vitamin A</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Vitamin A supplementation","B":"Oral aciclovir for 5 days","C":"Corticosteroid eye drops","D":"Oral tecovirimat for 14 days","E":"Oral iron supplementation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. CCCP (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, photophobia) + Koplik spots in the prodrome = measles. The lecturer's explanation: measles consumes the child's vitamin A, and the deficiency damages conjunctiva and cornea — hence the prominent eye signs and why vitamin A is given. There is no specific antiviral; care is otherwise supportive.","B":"Aciclovir is for HSV and VZV (adults with chickenpox, zoster). Slide 13: no specific antiviral for measles.","C":"Steroids are immunosuppressive. In measles that is the thing to avoid (BM28 Q27) — the immunocompromised get giant-cell pneumonia or encephalitis without a rash.","D":"Tecovirimat (ST-246) is the lecture's drug for mpox, a poxvirus — not for a paramyxovirus.","E":"Iron is the BM30 parvovirus B19 trap ('aplastic crisis from iron deficiency') — wrong there too, and irrelevant to measles."},"img":"slides_i/L12_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 6-year-old has high fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and photophobia, with small white spots on the buccal mucosa. What is the diagnosis and what is the buccal finding called?","o":{"E":"Scarlet fever — strawberry tongue","C":"Rubella — Forchheimer spots, which appear with the rash","A":"Measles — Koplik spots, which appear in the prodrome BEFORE the skin rash","D":"Hand, foot and mouth disease — herpangina","B":"Roseola — oral vesicles"},"r":{"E":"Scarlet fever is bacterial (Streptococcus) — it is the 'second disease' in the classic numbering and is not a viral exanthem.","C":"Forchheimer spots exist in rubella but are non-specific; rubella's discriminator is tender posterior auricular and occipital nodes.","A":"Correct. Remember measles as the THREE Cs plus a fourth: Cough, Coryza, Conjunctivitis — and Koplik spots. Koplik is an ENANTHEM (mucosal) and is the earliest specific sign, appearing 1–2 days before the rash; photophobia comes with the conjunctivitis.","D":"HFMD gives vesicles on hands, feet and mouth, not Koplik spots and not the three Cs.","B":"Roseola gives no enanthem worth the name. Measles is an ENVELOPED (−)ssRNA paramyxovirus, and its late complication is SSPE."},"t":"Measles","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L12_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Measles</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Chickenpox vs smallpox — one ripe, one clear","s":"In a preparedness drill, a febrile adult with a widespread blistering rash must be classified as chickenpox or smallpox before isolation is arranged. Which examination finding favours chickenpox over smallpox?","lec":"L12","sl":36,"c":"<b>Chickenpox vs smallpox — one ripe, one clear</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Every lesion reaching the pustular stage on the same day","B":"Lesions that pass through a vesicular stage before crusting","C":"A prodrome of fever and malaise before the rash appears","D":"Papules, clear vesicles and crusts side by side on the trunk","E":"Lesions densest on the face and extremities, sparse on the trunk"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"All lesions at the same stage (synchronous) is smallpox.","B":"Both pass through a vesicular stage (varicella: papule → vesicle → crust → scar; smallpox: macule → papule → vesicle → pustule → crust) — it does not discriminate.","C":"Both have a febrile prodrome (varicella: fever and malaise; smallpox: fever and malaise) — it does not discriminate.","D":"Correct. Chickenpox is asynchronous — successive crops, so lesions at several stages coexist (อีสุกอีใส, 'one ripe, one clear') — and it begins on and is heaviest on the trunk. Asked as 'several stages at once' in BM29, BM30, BM31 and NL slide 50.","E":"Centrifugal distribution (face and extremities > trunk) is smallpox — the lecturer's centrifuge image."},"img":"slides_i/L12_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"How does the rash of chickenpox differ from that of smallpox?","o":{"E":"Both are identical and can only be separated by PCR","C":"Chickenpox lesions are all at the same stage; smallpox lesions are at several stages","D":"Chickenpox lesions are at SEVERAL DIFFERENT STAGES at once and are densest on the trunk; smallpox lesions are all at the SAME stage and are densest on the face and limbs","B":"Chickenpox is centrifugal, smallpox centripetal","A":"Chickenpox spares the mucosa; smallpox spares the trunk entirely"},"r":{"E":"They are distinguished clinically; PCR confirms.","C":"Exactly reversed.","D":"Correct, and these two features are the whole comparison: STAGE and DISTRIBUTION. Chickenpox comes in crops, so macules, papules, vesicles and crusts coexist — the 'several stages' phrase that past papers reuse verbatim. Smallpox evolves in lockstep.","B":"The direction words are the wrong way round and are not how the lecture frames it.","A":"Chickenpox does involve mucosa, and smallpox does not spare the trunk — it is simply less dense there."},"t":"Varicella vs smallpox","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L12_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Varicella vs smallpox</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Parvovirus B19 — where it replicates","s":"An 8-year-old with β-thalassaemia major becomes suddenly pale and breathless; his haemoglobin has fallen sharply and the reticulocyte count is almost zero. Ten days earlier his sister was sent home from school with 'fifth disease'. Where does the responsible virus replicate to produce this crisis?","lec":"L12","sl":20,"c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 — where it replicates</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow","B":"Haematopoietic stem cells of every marrow lineage","C":"Mature red cells circulating in the peripheral blood","D":"Memory B cells in the lymph nodes and bone marrow","E":"Splenic macrophages that remove damaged red cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Fifth disease = erythema infectiosum = parvovirus B19. It enters via P antigen and replicates in erythroid precursors, stopping red-cell production. A normal host only dips slightly; a patient with haemolytic anaemia (short red-cell survival) collapses into an APLASTIC CRISIS. The near-zero reticulocyte count shows the defect is production.","B":"The lecture's defect is confined to the erythroid line (slide 20: 'replicate in erythroid precursor cells'), not a pancytopenia from stem-cell infection.","C":"Direct destruction of circulating red cells would raise the reticulocyte count, not abolish it.","D":"Memory B cells are the latency site of EBV (HHV-4).","E":"Splenic sequestration/hypersplenism is not the lecture's mechanism; B19 acts in the marrow."},"img":"slides_i/L12_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does parvovirus B19 cause an APLASTIC CRISIS in a patient with sickle cell disease but only a rash in a healthy child?","o":{"D":"B19 destroys mature red cells directly by haemolysis","A":"B19 infects erythroid progenitor cells and halts red-cell production; a healthy marrow tolerates the pause, but a patient whose red cells already have a short lifespan cannot","C":"B19 causes iron deficiency by blocking absorption","E":"B19 suppresses the whole marrow, causing pancytopenia in everyone","B":"B19 induces autoantibodies against red-cell antigens"},"r":{"D":"It kills the PRECURSORS, not the circulating cells.","A":"Correct. B19 uses the P antigen (globoside) to enter erythroid progenitors in the marrow and shuts down erythropoiesis for about a week. Normal red cells live 120 days, so nothing is noticed; sickle cells live ~20 days, so the haemoglobin falls off a cliff.","C":"A BM30 distractor claimed aplastic crisis arises from iron deficiency. It does not — the block is on production, not on substrate.","E":"The defect is confined to the red-cell line.","B":"No autoantibody is involved. Two other B19 facts worth holding: it is a NAKED ssDNA virus, and once the rash is present the patient is no longer infectious."},"t":"Parvovirus B19","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L12_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Parvovirus B19</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"HSV — only PCR can type it","s":"A 22-year-old woman has painful genital vesicles coalescing into ulcers, with tender inguinal nodes, five days after oral–genital contact with a partner who gets cold sores. A scraping shows multinucleated giant cells with eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions. The clinic wants to know whether this is HSV-1 or HSV-2. Which test answers that?","lec":"L12","sl":33,"c":"<b>HSV — only PCR can type it</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Anti-VCA IgM and IgG on a serum sample","B":"Haemagglutination inhibition on paired sera","C":"A repeat Tzanck smear from a fresh vesicle","D":"Cowdry A inclusion staining of the scraping","E":"PCR for viral DNA on a lesion swab"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Anti-VCA IgM/IgG are the EBV-specific serology for infectious mononucleosis.","B":"HI detects antibody against a haemagglutinating virus — the rubella serology item, irrelevant to typing HSV.","C":"Tzanck giant cells say 'herpes group' (HSV-1, HSV-2 or VZV) and nothing more — repeating it adds nothing.","D":"Cowdry A (eosinophilic intranuclear) inclusions are found with both HSV types and VZV — they cannot type the virus.","E":"Correct. Slide 33: 'Viral DNA by PCR: differentiation HSV-1/HSV-2'. The lecturer said it outright — Tzanck cannot type HSV-1 vs HSV-2, Cowdry A cannot either, only PCR can. The site does not type it either: slide 32 notes genital herpes from HSV-1 via oral–genital contact."},"img":"slides_i/L12_33.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the herpes vesicular exanthems is correct?","o":{"B":"Acyclovir eradicates latent virus and cures all three","C":"All three are latent in B lymphocytes","D":"HSV-1 causes genital lesions and HSV-2 causes cold sores, exclusively","A":"Only VZV establishes latency; HSV infections are fully cleared","E":"HSV-1 is latent in the trigeminal ganglion and recurs as herpes labialis; HSV-2 is latent in sacral ganglia and recurs as genital herpes; VZV is latent in dorsal root ganglia and recurs as zoster in a dermatome"},"r":{"B":"Acyclovir suppresses replication; it cannot touch the latent genome, which is why 'acyclovir can cure this disease' is a wrong option in a real paper.","C":"B-cell latency is EBV (and HHV-8). This option has been offered in a real paper about a genital HSV case.","D":"Either type can infect either site; the stated pattern is only the usual one.","A":"All three herpesviruses establish lifelong latency.","E":"Correct, and the pattern is one idea repeated: herpesviruses hide in NEURONS and come back in the territory of the nerve they hid in. That is why zoster is limited to a dermatome."},"t":"HSV-1 vs HSV-2 vs VZV","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L12_30.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HSV-1 vs HSV-2 vs VZV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Zoster — what the Tzanck smear shows","s":"A 72-year-old woman has had burning pain around the left side of her chest for 3 days; clustered vesicles have now appeared in a band that stops at the midline. Besides multinucleated giant cells, what would a Tzanck smear from a fresh vesicle show?","lec":"L12","sl":37,"c":"<b>Zoster — what the Tzanck smear shows</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cytoplasmic vacuolisation without inclusions","B":"Eosinophilic intranuclear inclusion bodies","C":"Basophilic intranuclear owl's-eye inclusions","D":"Eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies","E":"Perinuclear cytoplasmic acidophilic inclusions"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Vacuolisation is a distractor from the NL item; herpesviruses give fusion (giant cells) plus nuclear inclusions.","B":"Correct. Painful dermatomal vesicles in an older adult = herpes zoster (VZV reactivating from a dorsal root ganglion). VZV is a DNA virus replicating in the nucleus → Cowdry A = eosinophilic INTRAnuclear inclusions, added to the BM33 slides the same year the lecturer printed NL slide 51 asking exactly this.","C":"Owl's-eye basophilic intranuclear inclusions are CMV (and adenovirus) — L11's inclusion table.","D":"Intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions are Negri bodies (rabies) or poxvirus inclusions — the planted near-miss on NL slide 51 ('intracytoplasmic inclusion body').","E":"Perinuclear cytoplasmic acidophilic inclusions are reovirus on L11's table."},"img":"slides_i/L12_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A Tzanck smear from the base of a vesicle shows MULTINUCLEATED GIANT CELLS. Which viruses does this indicate?","o":{"C":"Coxsackie A virus and enterovirus 71","A":"Parvovirus B19 only","D":"Measles virus only","B":"HSV-1, HSV-2 or VZV — it confirms a herpes-group infection but does not separate them","E":"Variola or monkeypox virus"},"r":{"C":"HFMD vesicles do not show herpetic giant cells.","A":"B19 causes no vesicles at all.","D":"Measles does produce multinucleated giant cells — Warthin–Finkeldey cells — but in respiratory tissue, not in a skin vesicle.","B":"Correct, and the limitation matters as much as the finding: the smear says 'herpes group', and you need PCR or culture to say which one. The giant cell arises because these viruses fuse infected with uninfected cells (the cell-fusion CPE of L11).","E":"Poxvirus lesions show intracytoplasmic Guarnieri/molluscum-type inclusions instead."},"t":"Tzanck smear","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L12_33.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Tzanck smear</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Congenital rubella — proving fetal infection","s":"At 11 weeks of pregnancy a woman had low-grade fever, a pink rash that began on her face, tender nodes behind her ears and petechiae on the soft palate. Her obstetrician wants direct evidence of fetal infection. Which laboratory approach does the lecture list for this purpose?","lec":"L12","sl":18,"c":"<b>Congenital rubella — proving fetal infection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"In situ hybridisation for viral RNA in the amniotic membrane","B":"PCR for viral DNA in a sample of the amniotic fluid","C":"A single maternal rubella IgG level taken at booking","D":"Tzanck smear of cells spun down from the amniotic fluid","E":"Heterophile antibody testing of the maternal serum"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Face-first pink rash + posterior auricular nodes + Forchheimer spots = rubella, in the first trimester when CRS is most serious. Slide 18 lists for CRS: viral culture from amniotic specimen, anti-rubella IgM, and in situ hybridisation of amniotic membrane to detect rubella RNA.","B":"Rubella is an RNA virus — there is no viral DNA to amplify. 'Viral DNA by PCR from amniotic fluid' is the parvovirus B19 line on slide 23 (and 'RT-PCR – viral DNA' is the recurring planted error: RT = RNA).","C":"One maternal IgG shows past infection or immunity; it says nothing about whether the fetus is infected.","D":"Tzanck smears detect herpesvirus giant cells in skin scrapings, not fetal rubella.","E":"Heterophile antibody is the EBV (infectious mononucleosis) test."},"img":"slides_i/L12_18.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A woman contracts rubella in the first trimester. What is the classic triad in the newborn?","o":{"A":"Sensorineural deafness, cataract and congenital heart disease (patent ductus arteriosus)","C":"Retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus and intracranial calcification","E":"Microcephaly, limb contractures and ocular abnormality","B":"Hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice and purpura alone","D":"Cleft lip, spina bifida and clubfoot"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and DEAFNESS is the single commonest defect — which is exactly what one recent paper asked. Risk is highest in the first trimester and falls thereafter.","C":"That triad is congenital TOXOPLASMOSIS (L7) — the exam deliberately runs the congenital infections together, so keep them separate.","E":"Microcephaly with limb contractures is congenital ZIKA (L14).","B":"The blueberry-muffin picture occurs in CRS but is not the classic triad.","D":"These are structural defects unrelated to rubella. In the mother herself, the commonest complication is polyarthritis, especially in women."},"t":"Congenital rubella","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Congenital rubella</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"EBV — CD21 behind the amoxicillin rash","s":"A 17-year-old with fever, exudative sore throat and tender cervical nodes is given amoxicillin for presumed tonsillitis. Two days later a diffuse maculopapular rash appears, and the spleen tip is palpable. Through which receptor does the causative virus enter the cells in which it becomes latent?","lec":"L12","sl":39,"c":"<b>EBV — CD21 behind the amoxicillin rash</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"CD150 on activated lymphocytes","B":"Nectin-4 on epithelial cells","C":"CD4 on helper T lymphocytes","D":"P antigen on erythroid progenitors","E":"CD21 on B lymphocytes"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"CD150 is the wild-type measles receptor on lymphocytes.","B":"Nectin-4 is the other wild-type measles receptor, on epithelium.","C":"CD4 on helper T cells is the HIV receptor (L15).","D":"P antigen (globoside) is the parvovirus B19 receptor.","E":"Correct. Pharyngitis + lymphadenopathy + splenomegaly in a teenager, and a diffuse rash after amoxicillin (slide 41: avoid ampicillin and amoxicillin) = EBV infectious mononucleosis. EBV enters B cells via CD21 and stays latent in memory B cells."},"img":"slides_i/L12_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 22-year-old has two weeks of fever, sore throat, cervical lymphadenopathy and a mildly enlarged spleen. What would the blood tests show?","o":{"C":"A low lymphocyte percentage with neutrophilia","E":"Atypical lymphocytosis with a POSITIVE heterophile antibody test","A":"Raised haematocrit","D":"Positive HBsAg","B":"Marked eosinophilia"},"r":{"C":"Lymphocytes RISE, they do not fall — this exact inversion has been offered as an option.","E":"Correct. EBV infectious mononucleosis gives atypical (reactive) lymphocytes and a positive heterophile (Monospot/Paul-Bunnell) test. EBV is latent in B LYMPHOCYTES, entering via CD21.","A":"Haematocrit is unaffected.","D":"HBsAg belongs to hepatitis B, and that is a different clinical picture.","B":"Eosinophilia points to parasites, not EBV. One practical point: advise against contact sport because of the risk of splenic rupture."},"t":"Infectious mononucleosis","sl":40,"img":"slides_i/L12_40.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Infectious mononucleosis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q60","t":"Chickenpox — several stages","s":"A 7-year-old has a rash on the trunk in which macules, papules, vesicles and crusts are ALL PRESENT AT THE SAME TIME. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L12","sl":36,"c":"<b>Chickenpox — several stages</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q60 · BM31 SUM1 Q60 · BM29 SUM1 Q62 — 'lesions at several stages at once', asked in three cohorts running</span>","o":{"D":"Varicella-zoster virus","E":"HSV-1","B":"HHV-6","A":"Parvovirus B19","C":"Coxsackie A virus"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Chickenpox arrives in successive crops, so lesions of different ages coexist — and it is densest on the TRUNK. Those two features together are the standard answer to this stem.","E":"HSV gives grouped vesicles at one site, all of the same age.","B":"HHV-6 causes roseola, which is a maculopapular rash with no vesicles.","A":"B19 gives slapped cheek and a lacy rash — again no vesicles.","C":"Coxsackie A gives HFMD, with vesicles confined to hands, feet and mouth. Smallpox is the real near-miss: same lesion type, but all at ONE stage and densest on face and extremities."},"img":"slides_i/L12_36.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q60","BM29 Q62"]},{"id":"I|L12|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-roseola","t":"Roseola — rash after the fever","s":"A 7-month-old infant had three days of high fever. As the fever SUBSIDED, a maculopapular rash appeared. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L12","sl":25,"c":"<b>Roseola — rash after the fever</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q63 · BM31 SUM1 — a 7-month-old whose rash appears 3 days AFTER the fever subsides. ⚠ The student-made BM30 key records 'Rubella'; that is an error.</span>","o":{"C":"HHV-6 or HHV-7","B":"Rubella virus","D":"Measles virus","A":"Parvovirus B19","E":"HPV"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum, the 'sixth disease') is defined by its timing: several days of high fever in a well-looking infant, then the fever BREAKS and only then does the rash appear. The lecture prints the note 'As fever subsides, exanthem appears' on the HHV-6/7 slide.","B":"Rubella's rash comes WITH the fever, and its marker is tender posterior auricular and occipital nodes. ⚠ Be careful here: the circulating BM30 answer key marks Rubella for this stem, and it is wrong — BM31's version of the same question lists HHV 6,7 as an option precisely because that is the answer. Trust the timing, not the key.","D":"Measles brings cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and Koplik spots, and the rash appears while the child is still very febrile.","A":"B19 gives the slapped cheek then a lacy rash, typically in school-age children.","E":"HPV causes warts, not a febrile exanthem."},"img":"slides_i/L12_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q63"]},{"id":"I|L12|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q61","t":"Parvovirus B19 — aplastic crisis","s":"Which statement about parvovirus B19 is CORRECT?","lec":"L12","sl":20,"c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 — aplastic crisis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q61 · BM28 Q42 — which statement about parvovirus B19 is correct / 'aplastic anaemia' keyword</span>","o":{"B":"Once the rash has appeared the patient is no longer infectious","D":"Aplastic crisis occurs because the virus causes iron deficiency","E":"The virus attaches to the A antigen of the red cell","C":"It is an enveloped RNA virus","A":"It causes congenital cataract and deafness"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and it is the clinically useful fact: B19 is transmitted by respiratory droplets during the febrile PRODROME, and by the time the slapped cheek appears the rash is immune-mediated and the child can go back to school.","D":"The BM30 key records this as the intended answer, but the reasoning on the slide is different and better: the virus kills ERYTHROID PROGENITORS in the marrow, so production stops. Iron is not the problem.","E":"The receptor is the P antigen (globoside), not A antigen — a one-letter trap.","C":"B19 is a NAKED ssDNA parvovirus, which is why it survives well in the environment.","A":"Cataract and deafness are congenital RUBELLA. B19 in pregnancy causes hydrops fetalis."},"img":"slides_i/L12_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q42"]},{"id":"I|L12|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q7","t":"Measles — Koplik spots","s":"A 6-year-old boy has fever, cough, conjunctivitis and photophobia, with Koplik's spots on the oral mucosa. What is the causative virus?","lec":"L12","sl":10,"c":"<b>Measles — Koplik spots</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q7 (examiner-written) · BM28 Q27 — cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, photophobia and Koplik spots</span>","o":{"A":"HHV-6","D":"Rubella virus","C":"Measles virus","B":"Coxsackie A virus","E":"Mumps virus"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"HHV-6 gives roseola: high fever in an infant, rash only after the fever falls.","D":"Rubella is milder, without the three Cs, and is marked by posterior auricular lymphadenopathy.","C":"Correct — Koplik spots are pathognomonic for measles, and the three Cs plus photophobia complete the picture.","B":"Coxsackie A gives HFMD and herpangina — vesicles, not Koplik spots.","E":"Mumps gives parotitis rather than an exanthem. There is no specific antiviral for measles; care is supportive, and prevention is the vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L12_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q27"]},{"id":"I|L12|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q57","t":"Monkeypox","s":"A man has fever, enlarged lymph nodes and vesicles containing yellow fluid. His partner, who is from West Africa, has the same rash. What is the causative virus?","lec":"L12","sl":48,"c":"<b>Monkeypox</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q57 · BM31 SUM1 Q62 · BM30 SUM1 Q62 — febrile illness, enlarged lymph nodes, vesicles with yellow fluid, a West African partner with the same rash</span>","o":{"E":"Molluscum contagiosum virus","D":"Varicella-zoster virus","A":"HSV-1","B":"Rubella virus","C":"Monkeypox virus (Mpox)"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Molluscum gives painless umbilicated papules with no fever.","D":"Chickenpox lesions are at several stages at once, are superficial, and prominent lymphadenopathy is not typical.","A":"HSV gives grouped vesicles at a single site without this systemic picture.","B":"Rubella's rash is maculopapular, not vesicular.","C":"Correct, and LYMPHADENOPATHY is the deciding feature: it is what separates mpox from smallpox, which does not cause it. Add the West African link (the Clade II lineage) and the deep, well-circumscribed pustules. Treatment is tecovirimat; diagnosis is PCR on a lesion swab."},"img":"slides_i/L12_48.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q62","BM30 Q62"]},{"id":"I|L12|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-match","t":"Herpes labialis","s":"A 25-year-old woman has a blister on her lip that has recurred several times. Which disease-and-virus pairing is correct?","lec":"L12","sl":31,"c":"<b>Herpes labialis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q60 · BM31 SUM1 — a 25-year-old with a recurrent blister on the lip; match the disease to its virus</span>","o":{"E":"Herpes labialis — HSV-1","C":"Herpes zoster — Coxsackie A virus","B":"Smallpox — Coxsackie A virus","D":"Hand, foot and mouth disease — Enterovirus 71 only","A":"Chickenpox — HSV-2"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. A cold sore that keeps returning to the same spot is HSV-1 reactivating from the trigeminal ganglion.","C":"Herpes zoster is VARICELLA-ZOSTER virus.","B":"Smallpox is VARIOLA virus.","D":"HFMD is caused by Coxsackie A viruses AND enterovirus 71 — 'only' makes the option false.","A":"Chickenpox is VZV, not HSV-2."},"img":"slides_i/L12_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q60"]},{"id":"I|L12|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-tzanck","t":"Genital herpes","s":"A 35-year-old man has painful vesicular lesions on the penis for 1–2 days. A Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells. Which statement about this disease is CORRECT?","lec":"L12","sl":32,"c":"<b>Genital herpes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q21 · BM31 SUM1 — painful vesicular penile lesions, Tzanck smear showing multinucleated giant cells; which statement is correct?</span>","o":{"A":"The causative virus is latent in B lymphocytes","D":"Aciclovir shortens and suppresses episodes but cannot cure the infection","E":"The causative virus is transmitted by the oral–faecal route","B":"The varicella vaccine prevents this infection","C":"The causative virus commonly causes infertility in men"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"B-cell latency is EBV. Herpes simplex hides in NEURONS.","D":"Correct. Genital herpes is usually HSV-2, latent in the SACRAL ganglia. Aciclovir inhibits viral DNA polymerase during replication; the latent genome in the neuron is untouched, so recurrences continue and the infection is lifelong.","E":"HSV-2 is sexually transmitted, not faecal–oral.","B":"The varicella vaccine covers VZV only; there is no licensed HSV vaccine.","C":"HSV does not cause male infertility — mumps orchitis is the classic viral cause of that."},"img":"slides_i/L12_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM28 Q21"]},{"id":"I|L12|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q61","t":"Infectious mononucleosis","s":"A 22-year-old man has had fever for two weeks, with an erythematous rash, cervical lymphadenopathy and a mildly enlarged spleen. What would the laboratory show?","lec":"L12","sl":41,"c":"<b>Infectious mononucleosis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q61 — 2 weeks of fever, erythematous rash, cervical lymphadenopathy, mildly enlarged spleen; what will the laboratory show?</span>","o":{"C":"Marked eosinophilia","B":"HBsAg positive","D":"Raised haematocrit","E":"Reduced lymphocyte percentage","A":"Heterophile antibody positive"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"Eosinophilia suggests parasites. A classic clinical pearl: giving ampicillin to a patient with IM produces a florid maculopapular rash.","B":"Hepatitis B gives a different picture and no pharyngitis.","D":"Haematocrit is not raised.","E":"Lymphocytes RISE — this inversion is the planted trap.","A":"Correct. Fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly in a young adult is EBV infectious mononucleosis, and the heterophile (Monospot) test is the bedside confirmation, backed by atypical lymphocytes on the film."},"img":"slides_i/L12_41.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q26","t":"Erythema infectiosum","s":"A 6-year-old girl has fever and a LACY, reticular rash. Her mother says the rash started on her cheeks yesterday and spread to the trunk and limbs this morning. Which virus is responsible?","lec":"L12","sl":22,"c":"<b>Erythema infectiosum</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q26 — 6-year-old with fever and a LACY rash that began on the cheeks yesterday and spread to trunk and limbs today</span>","o":{"E":"Varicella-zoster virus","C":"Measles virus","D":"Rubella virus","A":"HHV-6","B":"Parvovirus B19"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"VZV is vesicular.","C":"Measles also starts on the face, but the rash is confluent maculopapular and comes with the three Cs and Koplik spots.","D":"Rubella's rash is pinky maculopapular and is accompanied by tender occipital nodes.","A":"Roseola's rash appears only after the fever falls and is not lacy.","B":"Correct. Slapped cheek first, then a LACY (reticular) rash on trunk and limbs one to two days later — that sequence is erythema infectiosum, the fifth disease, caused by parvovirus B19."},"img":"slides_i/L12_22.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q58","t":"Congenital rubella","s":"A newborn has congenital rubella. What is the most likely complication?","lec":"L12","sl":17,"c":"<b>Congenital rubella</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q58 — the most likely complication in a newborn with congenital rubella</span>","o":{"B":"Cholestatic jaundice","C":"Thrombocytopenia","A":"Intracranial calcification","D":"Limb contractures","E":"Sensorineural deafness"},"a":"E","r":{"B":"Cholestasis is not a feature.","C":"Thrombocytopenia does occur (the blueberry-muffin rash) but is much less common than deafness, which makes it the well-designed near-miss.","A":"Intracranial calcification with hydrocephalus and retinochoroiditis is congenital TOXOPLASMOSIS.","D":"Limb contractures with microcephaly suggest congenital Zika.","E":"Correct — deafness is the commonest single defect of congenital rubella syndrome, alongside cataract and patent ductus arteriosus in the classic triad."},"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L12|18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q25","t":"Who is at risk from rubella","s":"A 23-year-old man is diagnosed with rubella. Which contact is at GREATEST risk of serious consequences?","lec":"L12","sl":17,"c":"<b>Who is at risk from rubella</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q25 — a 23-year-old man has rubella; who around him is at greatest risk?</span>","o":{"A":"Someone who has completed the full rubella vaccine course","E":"A man of uncertain immune status","C":"Someone who has previously had the disease","B":"A woman in the first trimester of pregnancy","D":"Someone whose rubella-specific IgG is positive"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"A fully vaccinated person is protected.","E":"An unsure adult man might catch a mild illness — unpleasant, not serious.","C":"Past infection confers durable immunity.","B":"Correct. Rubella is mild in the patient himself; the entire public-health concern is the FETUS. First-trimester infection carries the highest risk of congenital rubella syndrome.","D":"Positive specific IgG means existing immunity — which is exactly why it is offered here as a plausible-sounding wrong answer."},"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L12|19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q79","t":"RT-PCR in an exanthem","s":"A 5-year-old has fever, swollen lymph nodes and a rash that began on the face and spread to the body. A throat swab is tested by RT-PCR. What does RT-PCR contribute to the diagnosis?","lec":"L12","sl":18,"c":"<b>RT-PCR in an exanthem</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q79 — a 5-year-old with fever, rash spreading from face to body and swollen nodes; a throat swab is tested by RT-PCR. What does RT-PCR contribute?</span>","o":{"E":"It visualises virus particles under the microscope","B":"It measures the child's immune response to the virus","C":"It identifies the viral RNA genome directly, confirming which virus is present","A":"It detects antibody produced against the virus","D":"It cultures the virus so that its cytopathic effect can be read"},"a":"C","r":{"E":"Visualising particles is electron microscopy.","B":"Measuring the immune response is serology — IgM and IgG.","C":"Correct. RT = reverse transcription, so the assay first turns viral RNA into cDNA and then amplifies it — it is a direct test for the GENOME of an RNA virus. In this stem (measles or rubella) it names the pathogen during the acute phase, when the virus is still being shed.","A":"Antibody detection is ELISA, HI or immunochromatography, not PCR.","D":"Culture and CPE reading is a separate, much slower method. Match the method to the target: genome → PCR, antigen → ELISA/immunochromatography, antibody → serology, particle → EM, cell change → light microscopy."},"img":"slides_i/L12_18.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L12|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"SSPE — naming the measles virus","s":"A 9-year-old boy shows slowly progressive decline in school performance, personality change and myoclonic jerks. At 14 months, before vaccination, he had high fever, cough, runny nose and red eyes, then a rash that spread from the face downward. Which describes the virus responsible for both illnesses?","lec":"L12","sl":9,"c":"<b>SSPE — naming the measles virus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Enveloped negative-sense ssRNA virus of the Paramyxoviridae","B":"Non-enveloped positive-sense ssRNA virus of the Picornaviridae","C":"Non-enveloped single-stranded DNA virus of the Parvoviridae","D":"Enveloped double-stranded DNA virus of the Herpesviridae","E":"Enveloped positive-sense ssRNA virus of the Matonaviridae"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Infant measles (CCCP, cephalocaudal rash) followed years later by progressive neurological decline = subacute sclerosing panencephalitis, the late complication charted 2–10 years out on slide 9. Measles = Paramyxoviridae, enveloped (−)ssRNA.","B":"Picornaviridae is Coxsackie A / EV71 — EV71 gives acute encephalitis/paralysis with HFMD, not a delayed panencephalitis.","C":"Parvoviridae (B19) causes erythema infectiosum and aplastic crisis, not CNS disease in the lecture.","D":"Herpesviridae fits HSV-1 encephalitis or the roseola viruses, not a late sequel of a CCCP illness.","E":"Rubella (Matonaviridae, (+)ssRNA) — the lecturer stressed the opposite polarity to measles. Its listed CNS complication is encephalitis, not SSPE."},"img":"slides_i/L12_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"The lecture numbers the six classic childhood exanthems. Which statement about that list is correct?","o":{"A":"The second and fourth diseases are BACTERIAL — scarlet fever from Streptococcus pyogenes and Duke's disease from Staphylococcus aureus — while the first, third, fifth and sixth are viral","C":"All six are viral, which is why the lecture groups them together","B":"The fifth disease is roseola infantum and the sixth is erythema infectiosum","D":"The first disease is rubella and the third is measles","E":"The sixth disease is caused by HHV-3, the same virus as chickenpox"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and it is the reason the numbered list is worth learning as a list — two of the six are not viruses at all, which makes them ideal distractors in a viral-exanthem stem.","C":"Scarlet fever and Duke's disease are bacterial; the deck names the organisms explicitly.","B":"Inverted. FIFTH = erythema infectiosum (slapped cheek, B19); SIXTH = roseola infantum (HHV-6/7).","D":"Inverted. FIRST = measles (rubeola); THIRD = rubella.","E":"Roseola is HHV-6 and HHV-7. HHV-3 is varicella-zoster."},"t":"The six classic exanthems","sl":4,"img":"slides_i/L12_4.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The six classic exanthems</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Measles — the protein that makes syncytia","s":"Respiratory secretions from an unvaccinated child with high fever, conjunctivitis, photophobia and white buccal spots show large syncytial giant cells. Which viral protein directly produces this cytopathic effect?","lec":"L12","sl":8,"c":"<b>Measles — the protein that makes syncytia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The neuraminidase (N) glycoprotein","B":"The haemagglutinin (H) glycoprotein","C":"The large polymerase (L) protein","D":"The fusion (F) glycoprotein","E":"The matrix (M) protein"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Measles has only two envelope glycoproteins, H and F (slide 8) — no neuraminidase. N belongs to influenza.","B":"H mediates adsorption to the cell surface — the most common swap on this slide.","C":"L is the polymerase for transcription and replication.","D":"Correct. CCCP + Koplik = measles. Slide 8: fusion protein (F) → cell entry → syncytial formation. 'H hooks on, F fuses.'","E":"M protein is for viral assembly."},"img":"slides_i/L12_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Measles virus forms multinucleated giant cells in infected tissue. Which protein is responsible, and what else does the lecture attribute to the measles envelope?","o":{"D":"The FUSION (F) protein mediates cell entry and syncytium formation; the other envelope glycoprotein, haemagglutinin (H), mediates adsorption to the cell surface","C":"The haemagglutinin (H) protein mediates fusion and syncytium formation; F mediates adsorption","B":"The matrix (M) protein mediates fusion, since it lies just beneath the envelope","E":"The polymerase (L) protein mediates syncytium formation during transcription","A":"Syncytia in measles are formed by the host cell, not by any viral protein"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The names are the mnemonic: H for Hooking on, F for Fusing. F is why measles produces syncytial giant cells in skin and respiratory specimens.","C":"The two functions are swapped — the single most common error on this slide.","B":"M protein is for viral ASSEMBLY, not fusion.","E":"L is the polymerase for transcription and replication.","A":"The deck attributes syncytium formation directly to the F protein disrupting the cytoskeleton."},"t":"Measles glycoproteins","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L12_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Measles glycoproteins</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Parvovirus B19 — why soap fails","s":"A primary school has an outbreak of bright red cheeks followed by lace-like rashes on the limbs. Staff note that it kept spreading despite routine soap handwashing and alcohol gel. The lecturer tied this persistence to one structural feature of the virus. Which is it?","lec":"L12","sl":19,"c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 — why soap fails</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It has no lipid envelope around its capsid","B":"It has a brick-shaped, double-membrane virion","C":"Its genome is positive-sense single-stranded RNA","D":"It carries its own viral DNA polymerase","E":"Its genome is double-stranded DNA"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slapped cheek → lacy rash = parvovirus B19, a small NON-ENVELOPED ssDNA virus. With no lipid envelope for soap or alcohol to dissolve, it survives hand hygiene ('ทนสบู่') — L10's stability rule.","B":"Brick-shaped with a double membrane describes Poxviridae (slide 45).","C":"(+)ssRNA is rubella (enveloped) or the picornaviruses (non-enveloped) — not B19.","D":"Carrying its own DNA polymerase is the poxvirus trait (cytoplasmic replication), not B19.","E":"B19 is single-stranded DNA (probably the only ssDNA human pathogen). dsDNA is the herpes and pox viruses."},"img":"slides_i/L12_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A child with hereditary spherocytosis develops fever and a sudden fall in haemoglobin with a very low reticulocyte count. Which statement explains the mechanism?","o":{"A":"Parvovirus B19 uses P antigen (globoside) as its receptor and replicates in erythroid progenitor cells in the marrow, arresting red cell production — which in someone whose red cells already have a short lifespan produces an APLASTIC CRISIS","D":"Parvovirus B19 uses the CD21 receptor on B cells and destroys them, causing marrow failure","C":"The virus lyses mature circulating erythrocytes directly, which is why the reticulocyte count is high","B":"This is roseola; HHV-6 suppresses the marrow through P antigen","E":"Parvovirus B19 is an enveloped dsDNA virus whose envelope binds erythrocyte membranes non-specifically"},"r":{"A":"Correct. In a normal host, briefly stopping erythropoiesis only dents the haemoglobin; in chronic haemolysis, where cells are being destroyed constantly, the same arrest is catastrophic.","D":"CD21 is the EBV receptor on B cells.","C":"The reticulocyte count FALLS — the defect is in production, not a peripheral haemolysis, which is exactly what the low reticulocyte count tells you.","B":"HHV-6 causes roseola and does not use P antigen.","E":"B19 is a small NON-enveloped ssDNA virus, and its binding is specific to P antigen."},"t":"Parvovirus B19 receptor","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L12_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 receptor</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Rubella serology — the HI endpoint","s":"A teacher who is 9 weeks pregnant was exposed to a colleague with low-grade fever, a pink rash that began on the face and tender nodes behind the ears. Her serum is tested by haemagglutination inhibition. Which well appearance and interpretation make a positive result?","lec":"L12","sl":15,"c":"<b>Rubella serology — the HI endpoint</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"No lattice forms, because her antibody has blocked the viral haemagglutinin","B":"A lattice forms, because her antibody has blocked the viral haemagglutinin","C":"No lattice forms, because her serum contains no rubella antibody","D":"The red cells lyse, because her antibody has fixed complement on them","E":"A lattice forms, because rubella particles in her serum bind to the red cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The colleague has rubella. In HI, serum is mixed with virus first; antibody neutralises the haemagglutinin, so the red cells cannot be cross-linked → NO lattice (a button) = positive. BM28 Q14 asked exactly this endpoint; HA = virus, HI = antibody (BM30 Q51 · BM31 · BM32 Q80).","B":"If the haemagglutinin were blocked, no lattice could form — the appearance and interpretation contradict each other.","C":"With no antibody, the virus is free to agglutinate the red cells and a lattice forms — no lattice means antibody present.","D":"Haemolysis belongs to complement-fixation read-outs, and HI does not rely on haemolysis at all.","E":"Detecting virus particles by lattice formation is the plain haemagglutination (HA) assay, not HI."},"img":"slides_i/L12_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which set of definitions and groupings matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"A vesicle is a fluid-filled elevated lesion under 0.5 cm, and the vesicular group is herpes labialis, shingles, chickenpox and hand-foot-and-mouth disease; the pustular group is smallpox and monkeypox","D":"A vesicle is a fluid-filled lesion under 1 cm, and measles and rubella belong to the vesicular group","C":"A macule is a solid elevated lesion and a papule is flat and pigmented","E":"Smallpox and monkeypox are maculopapular diseases; chickenpox is pustular","B":"Roseola infantum and erythema infectiosum are vesicular diseases"},"r":{"A":"Correct, including the size cut-off that differs — the vesicle is the only one defined at 0.5 cm rather than 1 cm.","D":"The size is 0.5 cm for a vesicle, and measles and rubella are MACULOPAPULAR.","C":"Inverted: macule is flat and pigmented, papule is solid and elevated.","E":"Inverted: the poxviruses are the PUSTULAR group and chickenpox is vesicular.","B":"Both are maculopapular."},"t":"Rash morphology definitions","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L12_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Rash morphology definitions</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Measles — the contagious window","s":"A 4-year-old who was at nursery until yesterday now has high fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and white spots on the buccal mucosa, but no skin rash yet. For contact tracing and exclusion, over which period is this child considered infectious?","lec":"L12","sl":8,"c":"<b>Measles — the contagious window</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"From 24–48 hours before rash onset until 4–5 days after scarring begins","B":"During the first week of illness and for a week after symptoms decline","C":"From 1–2 days before symptoms began until 4 days after the rash appears","D":"From 5 days before the rash appears until 6 days after it appears","E":"From the start of the prodrome until the rash appears, but not after"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"24–48 h before rash onset to 4–5 days after scar onset is varicella (slide 34).","B":"Contagious in the first week of illness and a week after symptoms decline is Coxsackie A / EV71 (slide 42).","C":"Correct. CCCP + Koplik before the rash = measles prodrome. Slide 8: contagious from 1–2 days before symptom onset to 4 days after the rash appears — so nursery contacts from before he was even ill are exposed.","D":"5 days before to 6 days after the rash is rubella's highest-contagiousness window (slide 15).","E":"Losing infectiousness once the rash is out is the parvovirus B19 pattern (rash after antibody)."},"img":"slides_i/L12_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about measles transmissibility and course matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"R₀ is 12–18, incubation is 8–12 days, and the patient is contagious from 1–2 days before symptoms begin until 4 days after the rash appears","D":"The patient becomes contagious only once the rash has appeared, which is why isolation begins then","B":"R₀ is 1–2, comparable with seasonal influenza","E":"Incubation is 17–18 days and contagiousness ends when the fever breaks","A":"Measles is contagious for the entire 6 days the rash lasts and for 2 weeks afterwards"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and the pre-symptomatic window is the public-health point: cases are infectious before anyone knows they have measles.","D":"If that were true, measles would be far easier to contain. Contagiousness begins 1–2 days BEFORE symptom onset.","B":"R₀ of 12–18 makes measles one of the most contagious human viruses; 1–2 is far too low.","E":"17–18 days is the PARVOVIRUS B19 rash timing. Measles incubation is 8–12 days.","A":"Contagiousness ends 4 days after the rash appears, not 2 weeks later."},"t":"Measles contagious window","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L12_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Measles contagious window</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Rubella — immune-complex polyarthritis","s":"A 24-year-old woman had 3 days of low-grade fever with a pink facial rash spreading to the trunk, tender swellings behind both ears and petechiae on the soft palate. A week later she has painful swelling of the small joints of both hands. What is the mechanism of the joint disease?","lec":"L12","sl":17,"c":"<b>Rubella — immune-complex polyarthritis</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cytotoxic T cells attacking infected synovium","B":"Deposition of immune complexes in the joints","C":"Reactivation of latent virus in the dorsal root ganglia","D":"Staphylococcus aureus superinfection of the joints","E":"Viral lysis of synovial cells bearing P antigen"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Cytotoxic T cells attacking infected cells is the lecture's mechanism for the smallpox rash, not rubella arthritis.","B":"Correct. Posterior auricular nodes + Forchheimer spots (petechiae on soft palate/uvula) + face-first pink rash = rubella. Slide 17: polyarthritis caused by immune complex, most common in women — an immunopathology example.","C":"Dorsal root ganglion latency and reactivation is VZV (zoster); rubella has no latent phase.","D":"S. aureus is the fourth disease (Duke's disease) organism — a bacterial distractor, not the rubella mechanism.","E":"P antigen on synovium is the parvovirus B19 receptor story (B19 arthralgia follows its rash)."},"img":"slides_i/L12_17.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"A young woman has a pink maculopapular rash starting on the face, tender nodes behind the ears, petechiae on the soft palate, and joint pains. Which statement is correct?","o":{"B":"This is rubella — the palatal petechiae are Forchheimer spots, seen in about 20%, and the polyarthritis is immune-complex mediated and commonest in women","E":"This is measles — the palatal lesions are Koplik spots and the arthritis is from direct viral invasion of synovium","D":"This is rubella, but the palatal lesions are Koplik spots, which are pathognomonic for it","A":"This is erythema infectiosum; Forchheimer spots and posterior auricular nodes are its hallmark","C":"This is roseola; the rash appears as the fever subsides and arthritis is typical"},"r":{"B":"Correct on the sign, its frequency, the mechanism of the arthritis and the sex skew — four separate details from two slides.","E":"Koplik spots are on the BUCCAL mucosa and belong to measles, whose rash is erythematous rather than pink and which is not typically arthritic.","D":"Forchheimer spots are rubella's; Koplik spots are measles'. Mixing the eponyms is the intended error.","A":"B19 gives slapped cheek then a lacy rash; arthralgia does occur but Forchheimer spots do not.","C":"Roseola affects infants 6 months to 4 years and starts on the TRUNK."},"t":"Rubella — mouth and node signs","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L12_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Rubella — mouth and node signs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"HSV-1 — the journey behind each recurrence","s":"A 30-year-old nurse notices tingling and then a cluster of blisters at the same spot on her upper lip every time she has a fever or heavy sun exposure. Which journey does the virus make to produce each new episode?","lec":"L12","sl":30,"c":"<b>HSV-1 — the journey behind each recurrence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Haematogenous, from latently infected memory B cells to the lip","B":"Retrograde from the lip along a facial sensory nerve to the trigeminal ganglion","C":"Viraemic, after fresh replication in the oropharyngeal epithelium","D":"Anterograde from a dorsal root ganglion along a sensory nerve to the lip","E":"Anterograde from the trigeminal ganglion along a sensory nerve to the lip"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Memory B-cell latency is EBV; HSV latency is in neurons of the sensory ganglia.","B":"Retrograde lip → ganglion is the journey that ESTABLISHES latency after the primary infection, not a recurrence.","C":"Oropharyngeal replication with viraemia is the ACUTE (primary) phase — gingivostomatitis — not reactivation.","D":"Dorsal root ganglia are VZV's latency site; lip sensation runs through the trigeminal ganglion.","E":"Correct. Recurrent lip vesicles at one spot = herpes labialis (HSV-1 reactivation). Latency: retrograde to the ganglion; reactivation: ANTEROGRADE along facial sensory nerves to the epithelium (slide 30) — by kinesin (BM23 Q48). One nerve, so the same spot every time."},"img":"slides_i/L12_30.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about herpesvirus latency and reactivation matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"HSV travels RETROGRADE to sensory ganglia to establish latency and ANTEROGRADE along sensory nerves on reactivation; VZV is latent specifically in dorsal root ganglia, and its reactivation gives painful vesicles along a dermatome","D":"HSV travels anterograde to the ganglion and retrograde to the skin","C":"VZV is latent in the trigeminal ganglion only, which is why shingles affects the face","B":"Both viruses are latent in B lymphocytes, as EBV is","A":"Latency requires continuous viral replication in the ganglion, which is why acyclovir prevents it"},"r":{"E":"Correct on direction of travel for both phases and on the specific ganglion for VZV — which is why zoster is confined to a dermatome.","D":"The directions are swapped. Retrograde means toward the cell body, i.e. into the ganglion.","C":"VZV is latent in DORSAL ROOT ganglia; zoster most often affects the trunk, though cranial nerve involvement occurs.","B":"B-cell latency is EBV. HSV and VZV are latent in neurons.","A":"Latency is defined by the ABSENCE of active replication, which is also why antivirals cannot eradicate it."},"t":"Where latency sits","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L12_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Where latency sits</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"EBV — what the atypical lymphocytes are","s":"A 19-year-old university student has fever, pharyngitis, generalised lymphadenopathy and a palpable spleen. The blood count shows lymphocytosis, and the film is dominated by large lymphocytes with abundant, indented cytoplasm. What are these cells?","lec":"L12","sl":39,"c":"<b>EBV — what the atypical lymphocytes are</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Plasma cells secreting heterophile antibody","B":"CD8+ T cells reacting to virus-infected B cells","C":"Monocytes carrying the latent viral genome","D":"Virus-infected B cells transformed by the virus","E":"Virus-infected CD4+ T cells undergoing lysis"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Heterophile antibody comes from EBV-infected B cells (slide 39), but the Downey cell is a reactive T cell.","B":"Correct. Teenager with pharyngitis, nodes and splenomegaly = EBV infectious mononucleosis. Slide 39: the host's cytotoxic CD8+ T cells respond to infected B cells → enlarged atypical lymphocytes (Downey cells). The film shows the immune response, not the infected cells.","C":"Monocytes as a latency site is CMV (HHV-5) on slide 28.","D":"Infected B cells drive heterophile antibody production, but they are not the atypical cells on the film.","E":"CD4+ T-cell infection and lysis is HIV (L15); EBV's target is the B cell via CD21."},"img":"slides_i/L12_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A 17-year-old has fever, pharyngitis and lymphadenopathy, and the blood film shows large atypical lymphocytes. Which statement is correct?","o":{"B":"EBV infects B cells through CD21; the atypical Downey cells are the patient's own cytotoxic CD8+ T cells responding to those infected B cells — and ampicillin or amoxicillin should be avoided because they provoke a diffuse rash","A":"The atypical cells are the EBV-infected B cells themselves","C":"EBV infects T cells directly through CD21, which is why the T cells look abnormal","D":"Ampicillin is the treatment of choice, since the pharyngitis is usually streptococcal","E":"A negative heterophile antibody test excludes EBV, so no further testing is needed"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the mechanism explains the blood film: you are looking at the immune RESPONSE, not the infected cells.","A":"Infected B cells are not what dominates the film; the reactive CD8+ T cells are.","C":"EBV infects B cells via CD21; the T cells are reacting, not infected.","D":"Ampicillin and amoxicillin are specifically to be AVOIDED because they provoke a diffuse rash.","E":"The deck lists heterophile antibody alongside EBV-specific VCA IgM/IgG, so a negative heterophile result does not end the workup."},"t":"EBV — why the lymphocytes are atypical","sl":39,"img":"slides_i/L12_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>EBV — why the lymphocytes are atypical</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"VZV — where it hides, what it does later","s":"A 6-year-old has fever and an itchy rash concentrated on the trunk; on any one day there are papules, clear vesicles on a red base and crusts side by side. After recovery, where does the virus persist, and what can it cause decades later?","lec":"L12","sl":34,"c":"<b>VZV — where it hides, what it does later</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Erythroid progenitors — an aplastic crisis in later life","B":"Sacral ganglia — recurrent vesicles on the genitalia","C":"Brain tissue — a sclerosing panencephalitis years later","D":"Dorsal root ganglia — painful vesicles confined to one dermatome","E":"Memory B cells — a lymphoma if T-cell control is lost"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Erythroid progenitors are parvovirus B19's target in acute infection — not a latency site.","B":"Sacral-ganglion latency with recurrent genital vesicles is HSV-2.","C":"SSPE years after infection is the measles late complication.","D":"Correct. Asynchronous, trunk-first papulovesicular rash = chickenpox (VZV/HHV-3). Latent phase: retrograde to the DORSAL ROOT ganglia; reactivation in adults = herpes zoster, painful vesicles along one dermatome of head or trunk.","E":"Memory B-cell latency with lymphoma when T-cell control fails is EBV (slide 39 note)."},"img":"slides_i/L12_34.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement separates the smallpox rash from the chickenpox rash, as the lecture describes them?","o":{"D":"Smallpox is centrifugal — worse on face and extremities than on the trunk — and evolves through macule, papule, vesicle, pustule and crust over 2–3 weeks; chickenpox begins on the trunk and spreads to neck and extremities","A":"Smallpox begins on the trunk and chickenpox on the face and extremities","B":"Both rashes are centripetal and cannot be told apart clinically","C":"Smallpox lesions are maculopapular only and never form pustules","E":"Chickenpox lesions all appear simultaneously and evolve together, which is the classic discriminator"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Centrifugal distribution plus lesions all at the same stage is the smallpox picture; the deck’s own NL example stem describes chickenpox as ‘multiple papule and vesicle in varying stage’.","A":"The distributions are swapped.","B":"They are distinguishable on distribution and on whether lesions are synchronous.","C":"The smallpox rash explicitly progresses macule → papule → vesicle → pustule → crust.","E":"Inverted — CHICKENPOX lesions are in varying stages simultaneously; SMALLPOX lesions are synchronous."},"t":"Smallpox vs chickenpox distribution","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L12_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Smallpox vs chickenpox distribution</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"VZV — who gets aciclovir","s":"A healthy 6-year-old develops fever and a papulovesicular rash that starts on the trunk and comes in crops. Sixteen days later his 38-year-old father, who never had the illness, develops the same rash. Which management plan matches the lecture?","lec":"L12","sl":38,"c":"<b>VZV — who gets aciclovir</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Supportive care for both; no antiviral acts on this virus","B":"Aciclovir for the boy; supportive care for the father","C":"Aciclovir for both, started within 24 hours of the rash","D":"No antiviral for the boy; aciclovir for the father","E":"Tecovirimat for the father; supportive care for the boy"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Aciclovir is listed for adult chickenpox and for zoster; 'supportive only' is the measles/rubella/B19/EBV line.","B":"The ages are reversed.","C":"The slide specifically withholds antiviral from the immunocompetent child.","D":"Correct. Crops of trunk-first papulovesicles = chickenpox; the father's illness about two weeks later fits exposure during the boy's contagious window (from 24–48 h before his rash) plus the incubation on slide 35. Slide 38: no antiviral for an immunocompetent child; aciclovir for adults (who get pneumonitis — NL slide 50).","E":"Tecovirimat is the mpox drug."},"img":"slides_i/L12_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about varicella-zoster management matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"An immunocompetent child with chickenpox needs no antiviral; adults with chickenpox and all patients with herpes zoster get acyclovir; the varicella vaccine is for children aged 1–12 and the zoster vaccine for adults over 50","A":"Every case of chickenpox requires acyclovir regardless of age","B":"Herpes zoster requires no antiviral because it is a reactivation rather than a primary infection","E":"The zoster vaccine is given to children aged 1–12 and the varicella vaccine to adults over 50","C":"Both vaccines are inactivated, which is why they are safe in immunocompromised patients"},"r":{"D":"Correct on all four points, and the age cut-offs are the detail worth holding.","A":"The deck states no antiviral is needed for an immunocompetent child.","B":"Acyclovir is listed for herpes zoster without qualification.","E":"The two vaccines and their age groups are swapped.","C":"The varicella vaccine is LIVE ATTENUATED, and the zoster vaccine is live attenuated or recombinant — so the blanket safety claim is wrong."},"t":"VZV — treat or not","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L12_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>VZV — treat or not</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"HFMD — EV71 and the nervous system","s":"During a nursery outbreak, several 2- to 4-year-olds develop fever, vesicles on the palms and soles and ulcers on the tongue. One child then becomes drowsy with a weak left leg. Which agent most likely caused that child's illness, and how did it spread through the nursery?","lec":"L12","sl":43,"c":"<b>HFMD — EV71 and the nervous system</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HSV-1 — saliva, kissing and close contact with lesions","B":"Enterovirus 71 — droplets and the faecal–oral route","C":"Varicella-zoster virus — airborne spread and lesion fluid","D":"Enterovirus 71 — airborne spread and fluid from skin lesions","E":"Coxsackie A virus — droplets and the faecal–oral route"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"HSV-1 spreads by saliva/kissing and causes gingivostomatitis, not a hand-and-foot outbreak.","B":"Correct. Vesicles on hands and feet + mouth ulcers in a child under 10 = HFMD. Slide 43: Coxsackie A causes HFMD and herpangina; ENTEROVIRUS 71 causes HFMD and herpangina WITH encephalitis/paralysis. Slide 42: spread by droplets from coughs/sneezes and the faecal–oral route.","C":"VZV gives a trunk-first asynchronous rash, not vesicles restricted to palms, soles and mouth.","D":"Right virus, wrong route — airborne spread plus skin-lesion fluid is varicella's transmission line.","E":"Right route, wrong virus — Coxsackie A is not the one the lecture links to encephalitis and paralysis."},"img":"slides_i/L12_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Two children have hand-foot-and-mouth disease. One develops encephalitis with limb weakness. Which statement is correct?","o":{"B":"Both are picornaviruses spread by droplets and the faecal–oral route, but ENTEROVIRUS 71 is the one the lecture associates with encephalitis and paralysis, whereas Coxsackie A causes HFMD and herpangina without them","E":"Coxsackie A is the neurovirulent one; enterovirus 71 causes only mouth ulcers","A":"Neurological disease in HFMD indicates a herpesvirus co-infection","C":"Both viruses are transmitted only by the faecal–oral route, so droplet precautions are unnecessary","D":"Herpangina is caused by HSV-1, as the name indicates"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Both cause the same two syndromes; EV71 is the one that adds neurological disease, which is why outbreaks of it are taken more seriously.","E":"Inverted.","A":"No co-infection is invoked — EV71 itself is neurovirulent.","C":"The deck lists BOTH droplet spread from coughs and sneezes AND the faecal–oral route.","D":"Herpangina is an enteroviral disease despite the name; HSV-1 causes herpes gingivostomatitis."},"t":"HFMD — Coxsackie A vs EV71","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/L12_42.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HFMD — Coxsackie A vs EV71</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Mpox — confirmation and treatment","s":"A 30-year-old man develops fever, headache, myalgia and tender enlarged lymph nodes after close skin contact with several new partners. Two days later papules appear on his face, palms and genitals; all of them become deep pustules together and then scab. Which pairing of confirmatory test and specific treatment matches the lecture?","lec":"L12","sl":49,"c":"<b>Mpox — confirmation and treatment</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Tzanck smear of a lesion base — tecovirimat","B":"PCR for viral DNA from a lesion — aciclovir","C":"Paired serum IgM and IgG — supportive care","D":"PCR for viral DNA from a lesion — tecovirimat","E":"Tzanck smear of a lesion base — oral aciclovir"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Right drug, wrong test: the Tzanck smear is the herpesvirus test.","B":"Right test, wrong drug: aciclovir has no place in mpox in this lecture.","C":"Paired serology with supportive care is the measles/rubella pattern; mpox has both a lesion PCR and a specific drug.","D":"Correct. Febrile prodrome with LYMPHADENOPATHY, then synchronous papules → vesicles → pustules → scabs, spread by close contact = monkeypox (lymphadenopathy is what separates it from smallpox). Slide 49: viral DNA by PCR from the lesion; treatment tecovirimat (ST-246).","E":"Tzanck + aciclovir is the HSV/VZV pairing — BM31 Q62 offered aciclovir for the mpox vignette as a trap."},"img":"slides_i/L12_49.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about monkeypox matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"It has two clades — Clade I (Central African / Congo Basin) and Clade II (West African) — is zoonotic from rodents and small mammals, is diagnosed by PCR on a lesion specimen, and is treated with tecovirimat (ST-246)","B":"It has a single stable serotype and no animal reservoir, which is why it was eradicated","C":"It is treated with acyclovir, like the other DNA viruses in this lecture","A":"Clade I is the West African clade and Clade II the Congo Basin clade","E":"There is no vaccine option of any kind for outbreak control"},"r":{"D":"Correct on clades, reservoir, diagnosis and treatment.","B":"Single stable serotype and no animal reservoir are two of the reasons SMALLPOX could be eradicated — monkeypox is zoonotic, which is precisely why it cannot be.","C":"Aciclovir has no activity here; the listed drug is tecovirimat.","A":"The clade names are swapped.","E":"The smallpox vaccine is listed as an option for outbreak control."},"t":"Monkeypox clades and drug","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L12_47.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Monkeypox clades and drug</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Why mpox cannot be eradicated like smallpox","s":"An outbreak of fever, lymphadenopathy and pustular rash is traced to pet prairie dogs that had been housed with rodents imported from Africa. Even after every human case is isolated, new cases keep appearing among animal handlers. Which of the five factors that made smallpox eradicable does this virus fail to meet?","lec":"L12","sl":46,"c":"<b>Why mpox cannot be eradicated like smallpox</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The disease can be recognised clinically","B":"The virus has a single, stable serotype","C":"Humans are the only host, with no animal reservoir","D":"The antibody response after exposure is prompt","E":"There is no carrier state or subclinical infection"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Factor 4 — clinical recognisability — is met: the outbreak was recognised by its rash and lymphadenopathy.","B":"Factor 1 — antigenic stability — would not explain new cases appearing after all human cases were isolated; the stem points to a non-human source.","C":"Correct. Pox-type pustular rash + lymphadenopathy + rodent source = monkeypox, a ZOONOSIS maintained in rodents and small mammals (slide 47). New cases arising from animals despite isolating every human case is exactly the failure of factor 2 — smallpox had no animal reservoir.","D":"Factor 3 — a prompt antibody response — is why the smallpox vaccine still works for mpox outbreak control (slide 49).","E":"Factor 5 concerns hidden human spread; the stem has every human case isolated, and new cases still come from animals."},"img":"slides_i/L12_46.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"The lecture lists five factors that made smallpox eradication possible. Which is one of them?","o":{"C":"There is no carrier state and no subclinical infection, so every infected person is visible to surveillance","E":"The virus has many serotypes, so vaccination covered a broad range","A":"There is a large animal reservoir that could be vaccinated alongside humans","B":"Immunity after vaccination is lifelong, so a single campaign sufficed","D":"The disease is usually subclinical, so most cases resolved without spreading"},"r":{"C":"Correct — this is the fifth of the five factors, and it is what made ring vaccination work.","E":"Factor 1 is a SINGLE, STABLE serotype.","A":"Factor 2 is that there is NO animal reservoir and humans are the only host.","B":"The slide says immunity after DISEASE is lifelong but immunity after VACCINATION lasts about 10 years.","D":"The opposite — factor 5 is that there is no subclinical infection, so no case was invisible."},"t":"Why smallpox could be eradicated","sl":46,"img":"slides_i/L12_46.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Why smallpox could be eradicated</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Measles giant-cell pneumonia without rash","s":"An unvaccinated 5-year-old receiving chemotherapy for leukaemia develops fever, cough and worsening hypoxia but never a rash. Two weeks earlier his brother had high fever, cough, red eyes and a rash that started on the face. A transbronchial biopsy shows multinucleated giant cells with eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions. Which virus is responsible?","lec":"L12","sl":11,"c":"<b>Measles giant-cell pneumonia without rash</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Parvovirus B19","B":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Herpes simplex virus type 1","D":"Measles virus","E":"Varicella-zoster virus"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"B19 in the immunocompromised gives persistent infection with anaemia, not giant-cell pneumonia.","B":"CMV gives owl's-eye BASOPHILIC intranuclear inclusions (L11), and there is no CMV contact here.","C":"HSV-1 also gives Cowdry A inclusions, but the brother's illness and the rash-free pneumonia in an immunocompromised child point to measles.","D":"Correct. Brother's CCCP-type illness with a face-first rash = measles exposure. Slide 11: in the immunocompromised, measles gives pneumonia or encephalitis WITHOUT rash; slide 12 shows exactly this biopsy — giant-cell measles pneumonia with intranuclear eosinophilic inclusions. Hence BM28 Q27's answer: avoid immunosuppressive drugs in measles.","E":"VZV does give giant cells with Cowdry A inclusions and pneumonitis, but its pneumonitis follows a trunk-first vesicular rash; the exposure here is a face-first CCCP illness."},"img":"slides_i/L12_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about measles complications and diagnosis matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"An immunocompromised patient may develop measles pneumonia or encephalitis WITHOUT any rash; subacute sclerosing panencephalitis is a listed late complication; and anti-measles IgM becomes detectable around day 4 after rash onset","A":"The rash is always present, which is why a rash-free illness excludes measles","B":"SSPE occurs within days of the acute illness and is the commonest complication","E":"Anti-measles IgM is detectable before the rash appears, so a negative result on the day of the rash excludes measles","C":"Diagnosis rests on finding intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies in neurons"},"r":{"D":"Correct on all three. The rash-free presentation in an immunocompromised host is the trap — the illness that is defined by its rash may present without one.","A":"The deck explicitly describes pneumonia and encephalitis WITHOUT rash in immunocompromised patients.","B":"SSPE is a SUBACUTE, late complication, and it is rare rather than commonest.","E":"IgM is listed as detectable on day FOUR after rash onset, so testing too early can be falsely negative.","C":"Measles cytopathology is SYNCYTIAL giant cells with intraNUCLEAR eosinophilic inclusions; cytoplasmic neuronal inclusions are rabies."},"t":"Measles in the immunocompromised","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L12_11.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Measles in the immunocompromised</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L12|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Parvovirus B19 — serology on the day of the rash","s":"A 7-year-old's cheeks turned bright red yesterday, about a week after a runny nose and mild fever; today a lace-like rash has appeared on her arms and trunk. Serum is drawn at this visit. Which result fits the course of this infection as the lecture charts it?","lec":"L12","sl":21,"c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 — serology on the day of the rash</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Specific IgM negative until 4 days after the rash","B":"Specific IgM already strongly positive","C":"No antibody yet; the rash marks peak viraemia","D":"Specific IgG positive, IgM not yet detectable","E":"Viraemia at its peak, reticulocytes rising"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"IgM from day 4 after rash onset is the measles and rubella rule (slides 11, 18) — the trap of carrying one virus's timing to another.","B":"Correct. Slapped cheek → lacy rash = B19. Slide 21: viraemia ~1 week after inoculation with IgM appearing then; IgG at weeks 2–3; rash at 17–18 days. Slide 23: specific IgM is highly positive by the time the rash is present — and the child is no longer infectious.","C":"The rash comes after antibody, not at peak viraemia — that is why B19 is immune-mediated at the rash stage.","D":"IgM precedes IgG in B19 as everywhere; by the rash both are rising, with IgM strongly positive.","E":"Viraemia is around week 1, before the rash, and reticulocytes FALL during it."},"img":"slides_i/L12_21.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which sequence for parvovirus B19 infection matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Viraemia at about 1 week with IgM appearing then and reticulocytes falling; IgG by the second to third week; rash at 17–18 days, followed by arthralgia","E":"Rash at day 4, viraemia at week 3, IgG before IgM","D":"Reticulocytes rise during viraemia, which is how the marrow compensates","A":"The rash appears during the viraemic phase, which is when the patient is most contagious","C":"IgM appears only after the rash has faded, so serology is useless acutely"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and the sequence explains the clinical paradox: by the time the rash appears the patient has antibody and is no longer very infectious.","E":"Every element is wrong; IgM precedes IgG.","D":"Reticulocytes FALL during viraemia, because the virus is destroying erythroid precursors.","A":"The rash comes at 17–18 days, well after the ~1-week viraemia — which is why contagiousness has passed by then.","C":"IgM is described as highly positive by the time the rash is present."},"t":"Parvovirus B19 timeline","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L12_21.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Parvovirus B19 timeline</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233438639},{"id":"I|L13|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Summary table — EBV row","s":"A 38-year-old man has nasal obstruction, recurrent nosebleeds and a painless neck mass. Biopsy shows undifferentiated carcinoma arising in the nasopharynx, and viral latency proteins are detected in the tumour cells. Which oncoprotein and mechanism does the lecture's summary table give for the causative virus?","lec":"L13","sl":21,"c":"<b>Summary table — EBV row</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"LMP-1 — c-Myc dysregulation and inhibition of apoptosis","B":"HBx — transactivation of cellular oncogenes","C":"E6 — inactivation of p53 in squamous cells","D":"LANA — inactivation of p53 and pRb","E":"Tax — immortalisation of infected CD4+ T cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is an EBV cancer (slide 21). The summary table (slide 35) lists EBNA-1, EBNA-2, LMP-1, LMP-2 and BamHI, acting through c-Myc dysregulation, proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis and indirect immunosuppression.","B":"HBx is the HBV oncoprotein (hepatocellular carcinoma), not EBV.","C":"E6 is HPV's; HPV causes cervical, vaginal and anal cancer, not nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","D":"LANA belongs to HHV-8 (Kaposi's sarcoma), another herpesvirus — the classic herpesvirus mix-up.","E":"Tax is HTLV-1's oncoprotein (adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma)."},"img":"slides_i/L13_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which row of the oncogenic-virus table is CORRECT?","o":{"D":"HHV-8 — ssRNA — Kaposi's sarcoma — EBNA-1 — direct integration","C":"EBV — ssRNA — Burkitt lymphoma — LANA — inactivation of p53","B":"HBV — dsRNA — hepatocellular carcinoma — E6 — chronic inflammation only","E":"HTLV-1 — dsDNA — adult T-cell leukaemia — HBx — transactivation","A":"HPV — dsDNA — cervical cancer — E6 and E7 — inactivation of p53 and pRb"},"r":{"D":"HHV-8 is a dsDNA herpesvirus; EBNA-1 is EBV's.","C":"EBV is a dsDNA herpesvirus, its oncoproteins are EBNA-1, EBNA-2, LMP-1 and LMP-2, and Burkitt lymphoma turns on c-MYC deregulation. LANA belongs to HHV-8.","B":"HBV is partially circular dsDNA and its oncoprotein is HBx.","E":"HTLV-1 is an enveloped (+)ssRNA retrovirus and its oncoprotein is Tax.","A":"Correct, and this row is the most-asked fact in the lecture. Learn the whole table as five rows: HPV/E6+E7, EBV/EBNA+LMP, HHV-8/vIRF+vIL-6, HBV/HBx, HTLV-1/Tax."},"t":"The summary table","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L13_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The summary table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Direct vs indirect oncogenesis","s":"In tumour group 1, every cancer cell carries the viral genome and expresses a viral protein that inactivates p53 and pRb. In group 2, the liver tumours arose after decades of hepatitis with persistent immune-cell infiltration caused by an RNA virus that forms no DNA intermediate. How does the lecture classify the main route in each group?","lec":"L13","sl":8,"c":"<b>Direct vs indirect oncogenesis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Group 1 indirect; group 2 direct through chronic inflammation","B":"Both direct, because both viruses persist in the tissue","C":"Group 1 direct; group 2 indirect through immunosuppression","D":"Group 1 indirect through immunosuppression; group 2 direct","E":"Group 1 direct; group 2 indirect through chronic inflammation"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Reversed: an oncoprotein inside the tumour cell is direct; chronic inflammation is indirect.","B":"Persistence alone does not make a mechanism direct; group 2's tumours come from inflammatory damage.","C":"Immunosuppression is the other indirect mechanism, with HIV as the prototype (e.g. HHV-8 lymphoma) — the stem describes inflammation, not immune collapse.","D":"Group 1 is the textbook direct mechanism (oncoprotein → p53/pRb).","E":"A viral oncoprotein acting inside the transformed cell is the DIRECT mechanism (slide 8). Tissue damage from a chronic inflammatory microenvironment is INDIRECT (slide 11) — the lecture's main route for HCV (slides 31, 35)."},"img":"slides_i/L13_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"What distinguishes a DIRECT from an INDIRECT mechanism of viral oncogenesis?","o":{"E":"Direct: the virus expresses an oncoprotein that drives the cell cycle in the transformed cell itself. Indirect: the virus causes chronic inflammation or immunosuppression, and the cancer arises as a consequence","C":"Direct means DNA viruses and indirect means RNA viruses","A":"Direct means the virus integrates; indirect means it does not","B":"Direct means the cancer appears within weeks; indirect means it takes years","D":"Direct applies to solid tumours and indirect to leukaemias"},"r":{"E":"Correct. HPV, EBV and HTLV-1 work mainly DIRECTLY, through oncoproteins expressed in the cell that becomes malignant. HBV and HCV work largely INDIRECTLY, through decades of chronic inflammation, regeneration and cirrhosis.","C":"Both DNA and RNA viruses appear on both sides — HTLV-1 is an RNA virus acting directly.","A":"HCV does not integrate at all, yet causes cancer; and integration is not what the direct/indirect split means.","B":"Every viral cancer takes years to decades. Speed is not the distinction.","D":"HPV gives a solid tumour directly; HTLV-1 gives a leukaemia directly."},"t":"Direct vs indirect","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L13_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Direct vs indirect</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"E6/E7 — why both matter","s":"A 41-year-old woman's cervical biopsy shows a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion with koilocytic atypia, and HPV 16 DNA is detected. Which pair of cellular consequences of the two viral early proteins lets the infected cells keep dividing despite DNA damage?","lec":"L13","sl":17,"c":"<b>E6/E7 — why both matter</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Loss of p53-driven arrest and apoptosis; loss of the pRb G1/S block","B":"Promoter hypermethylation of p53; deletion of both pRb alleles","C":"Activation of p53 by E7; activation of pRb by E6","D":"Gain of p53-driven arrest and apoptosis; loss of the pRb G1/S block","E":"Point mutation of c-Myc; amplification of a growth-factor receptor"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"E6 inactivates p53 and E7 inactivates pRb (slide 17): p53 normally arrests damaged cells or sends them into apoptosis, and pRb holds the G1/S checkpoint. Losing both = uncontrolled proliferation (slide 35: 'inhibition of cell-cycle arrest and apoptotic function').","B":"Hypermethylation and allele deletion are also distractors from her quiz slides — HPV acts through viral proteins.","C":"The viral proteins inactivate, not activate, the suppressors — and E6 targets p53, E7 targets pRb.","D":"Gaining p53 function would stop the cells, not transform them.","E":"Host proto-oncogene mutation is a non-viral route; the lecturer's quiz (slide 36) uses it as a distractor."},"img":"slides_i/L13_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does inactivating BOTH p53 and pRb transform a cell?","o":{"C":"Both are growth factors, and their loss starves the cell into malignancy","D":"p53 and pRb are proto-oncogenes, so inactivating them switches on proliferation directly","E":"p53 repairs DNA and pRb replicates it, so cells cannot divide without them","A":"pRb normally holds the cell at the G1/S checkpoint and p53 normally triggers arrest or apoptosis when DNA is damaged — losing both removes the brake AND the emergency stop","B":"Both are viral proteins normally supplied by the host"},"r":{"C":"Neither is a growth factor.","D":"They are TUMOUR SUPPRESSOR genes, the opposite category. Proto-oncogenes (such as c-MYC) cause cancer when switched ON, not off.","E":"Neither protein performs DNA replication itself.","A":"Correct. pRb is the gatekeeper of G1/S — phosphorylate or inactivate it and the cell enters S phase unchecked. p53 is the guardian of the genome — it halts the cycle for repair or triggers apoptosis. Remove the brake and you get uncontrolled division; remove the emergency stop and the damaged cell survives to accumulate more mutations.","B":"Both are host proteins, which is precisely why the virus must supply proteins to disable them."},"t":"p53 and pRb","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L13_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>p53 and pRb</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"HPV types — low vs high risk","s":"Two women attend the same clinic. Woman P has soft, painless, cauliflower-like perianal lesions. Woman Q has a cervical mass that bleeds on contact; biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. Lesion tissue from both is typed by PCR. Which pair of results fits the lecture's risk table?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>HPV types — low vs high risk</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"P: HPV 16; Q: HPV 11","B":"P: HPV 31; Q: HPV 44","C":"P: HPV 11; Q: HPV 42","D":"P: HPV 18; Q: HPV 6","E":"P: HPV 6; Q: HPV 18"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Reversed: 16 is high-risk (cancer) and 11 is low-risk (warts).","B":"31 is high-risk (would not be the wart type) and 44 is low-risk (not the cancer type).","C":"42 is a low-risk type, so it cannot account for Q's carcinoma.","D":"Reversed: 18 is high-risk and 6 is low-risk.","E":"Genital warts come from LOW-risk types (6, 11, 40, 42 …); cervical cancer from HIGH-risk types (16, 18, 31, 33, 45 …) — slide 19."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A healthy woman is found to be infected with HPV type 11. What is the expected outcome?","o":{"E":"Genital warts (condylomata acuminata); types 6 and 11 are LOW-risk and do not cause cervical cancer","A":"High risk of cervical cancer within five years","B":"Lifelong latency in B lymphocytes","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma","D":"Adult T-cell leukaemia"},"r":{"E":"Correct. HPV types are split by transforming potential: 6 and 11 are low-risk and produce anogenital warts; 16 and 18 are high-risk and produce cervical cancer.","A":"High cervical-cancer risk belongs to 16 and 18, not 11.","B":"HPV infects squamous epithelium; B-cell latency is EBV.","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8.","D":"Adult T-cell leukaemia is HTLV-1. Most HPV infections in an immunocompetent host clear within about two years."},"t":"HPV risk stratification","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HPV risk stratification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"HPV — laboratory work-up","s":"A 45-year-old woman's annual Pap smear shows squamous cells with perinuclear halos and enlarged, irregular nuclei. According to the lecture's laboratory slide, which test is used to confirm the viral cause, and when is a biopsy indicated?","lec":"L13","sl":20,"c":"<b>HPV — laboratory work-up</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Viral culture of cervical cells; biopsy once it grows","B":"HPV DNA by PCR; biopsy if either test is abnormal","C":"Serum anti-HPV IgM; biopsy once the titre rises","D":"HPV DNA by PCR; biopsy only when a mass is visible","E":"Tzanck smear of the cervix; biopsy if giant cells appear"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"HPV is not diagnosed by culture in the lecture.","B":"Slide 20: Pap smear, PCR for HPV DNA, and biopsy 'if abnormal findings are detected from Pap smears or HPV DNA tests'. The cells described are koilocytes.","C":"Serology is not on the lecture's HPV investigation list.","D":"Biopsy is triggered by an abnormal Pap or HPV DNA result, not only by a visible mass.","E":"The Tzanck smear belongs to HSV/VZV (L12), not HPV."},"img":"slides_i/L13_20.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A Pap smear shows squamous cells with perinuclear halos and irregular nuclei (koilocytes). What does this indicate and what is the next step?","o":{"B":"HPV cytopathic change; follow with HPV DNA testing by PCR, and colposcopy with biopsy if abnormalities persist","E":"Herpes simplex infection; start aciclovir","D":"Adenocarcinoma; proceed straight to hysterectomy","C":"Candida infection; treat with topical antifungal","A":"A normal finding requiring no action"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The koilocyte — a squamous cell with a perinuclear halo and an irregular, raisin-like nucleus — is the cytological footprint of HPV. The lecture's investigation list is exactly this: Pap smear, then HPV DNA by PCR, then biopsy if findings are abnormal.","E":"Herpes gives multinucleated giant cells on a Tzanck smear, not halos.","D":"Koilocytosis is a low-grade squamous change (LSIL), not adenocarcinoma, and hysterectomy is never the next step after a smear.","C":"Candida is identified by budding yeasts and pseudohyphae.","A":"Koilocytosis is abnormal and requires follow-up."},"t":"Koilocytes and the Pap smear","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L13_20.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Koilocytes and the Pap smear</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"HBV serology — vaccine vs past infection","s":"Before a surgical rotation, student S has HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive and anti-HBc negative. Student T, who grew up in a high-prevalence area, has HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive and anti-HBc IgG positive. How are the two results interpreted?","lec":"L13","sl":27,"c":"<b>HBV serology — vaccine vs past infection</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"S chronic carrier; T immune after past infection","B":"S immune after past infection; T immune by vaccination","C":"S immune by vaccination; T immune after past infection","D":"S immune by vaccination; T chronic carrier","E":"S in the window period; T with acute infection"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A carrier is HBsAg positive; S is HBsAg negative.","B":"Reversed — anti-HBc is the marker of natural infection.","C":"Vaccine contains only HBsAg, so a vaccinee makes anti-HBs but never anti-HBc. Anti-HBc appears only after real infection; with HBsAg gone and anti-HBs present, T has recovered (slide 27).","D":"T is HBsAg negative and anti-HBs positive — not a carrier.","E":"The window period has anti-HBc IgM with no HBsAg or anti-HBs; acute infection is HBsAg positive."},"img":"slides_i/L13_27.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which serological pattern indicates immunity from VACCINATION rather than from past infection?","o":{"D":"HBsAg positive, anti-HBs negative, anti-HBc positive","A":"HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc positive","C":"HBsAg negative, anti-HBs POSITIVE, anti-HBc NEGATIVE","E":"HBsAg positive, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc negative","B":"HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, anti-HBc positive"},"r":{"D":"HBsAg positive with anti-HBc positive is current infection.","A":"Anti-HBs positive WITH anti-HBc positive is immunity from RESOLVED NATURAL INFECTION.","C":"Correct, and anti-HBc is the discriminator. The vaccine contains only surface antigen, so a vaccinated person makes anti-HBs and NEVER anti-HBc. Anti-HBc positive means the person met the whole virus.","E":"HBsAg and anti-HBs both positive is an unusual pattern and not a vaccination profile.","B":"Isolated anti-HBc is the window period, a resolved infection with waned anti-HBs, or occult infection. This panel is worth learning cold — recent papers build hepatitis stems on it."},"t":"HBV serology","sl":27,"img":"slides_i/L13_27.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HBV serology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"HTLV-1 — the virus behind the flower cells","s":"A 48-year-old woman has generalised lymphadenopathy and lymphocytosis; her blood film shows abnormal T cells with multilobed 'flower' nuclei. Her mother, who breastfed her, had the same disease. Which description fits the causative virus?","lec":"L13","sl":32,"c":"<b>HTLV-1 — the virus behind the flower cells</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Enveloped positive-sense ssRNA virus with no DNA intermediate","B":"Enveloped positive-sense ssRNA virus with reverse transcriptase","C":"Enveloped linear dsDNA virus that stays latent in B cells","D":"Enveloped partially double-stranded DNA virus with a polymerase","E":"Non-enveloped circular dsDNA virus that infects squamous cells"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"That is HCV — ss(+)RNA but no DNA intermediate and no oncogene (slide 29).","B":"Flower cells = adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma; breast-feeding is a listed route. HTLV-1 is a retrovirus: enveloped ss(+)RNA with reverse transcriptase, targeting CD4+ T cells (slide 32).","C":"That is EBV — B-cell latency, Burkitt and nasopharyngeal cancer.","D":"That is HBV (Hepadnaviridae) — liver cancer.","E":"That is HPV — squamous epithelium, cervical cancer."},"img":"slides_i/L13_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about HTLV-1 is correct?","o":{"A":"It transforms B lymphocytes through the EBNA-1 protein","E":"It is a dsDNA herpesvirus causing Burkitt lymphoma","C":"It causes Kaposi's sarcoma in patients with AIDS","D":"It is prevented by the hepatitis B vaccine","B":"It is an enveloped (+)ssRNA retrovirus transmitted by breast milk, sex and blood, and it causes adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma via the Tax protein"},"r":{"A":"EBNA-1 and B-cell transformation are EBV; HTLV-1 targets CD4+ T cells.","E":"Burkitt lymphoma is EBV, a dsDNA herpesvirus.","C":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8.","D":"The hepatitis B vaccine prevents HBV — and, because HDV needs HBsAg, hepatitis D as well. It does nothing for HTLV-1.","B":"Correct. HTLV-1 sits in Retroviridae with HIV: enveloped, (+)ssRNA, reverse transcriptase. Unlike most retroviruses, which carry only gag, pol and env, it has extra regulatory genes — Tax being the transforming one."},"t":"HTLV-1","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L13_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HTLV-1</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"EBV — linking its cancers","s":"An 8-year-old boy has a rapidly enlarging jaw mass. Biopsy shows a B-cell lymphoma with a c-Myc translocation, and the tumour cells carry the genome of the herpesvirus that causes 'kissing disease'. Which other cancer does the lecture attribute to the same virus?","lec":"L13","sl":21,"c":"<b>EBV — linking its cancers</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","B":"Anal squamous carcinoma","C":"Adult T-cell leukaemia","D":"Kaposi's sarcoma","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Burkitt's lymphoma is EBV (HHV-4), whose cancers are Burkitt's, nasopharyngeal, Hodgkin's and non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (slides 13, 21).","B":"Anal cancer is a high-risk HPV cancer.","C":"Adult T-cell leukaemia is HTLV-1.","D":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8 — a different herpesvirus.","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma belongs to HBV and HCV."},"img":"slides_i/L13_21.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which set of malignancies is associated with EBV?","o":{"E":"Kaposi's sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma","C":"Cervical cancer and anogenital warts","A":"Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma and post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease","B":"Hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Adult T-cell leukaemia and tropical spastic paraparesis"},"r":{"E":"Kaposi's sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma are HHV-8.","C":"Cervical cancer and warts are HPV.","A":"Correct. EBV's range is wide because it immortalises B cells and infects nasopharyngeal epithelium: endemic Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, and PTLD in the immunosuppressed.","B":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is HBV and HCV; cholangiocarcinoma is the liver FLUKE Opisthorchis (L4), not a virus at all.","D":"Both of those are HTLV-1."},"t":"EBV-associated cancers","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L13_21.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>EBV-associated cancers</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-pap","t":"HPV — E6 and E7","s":"A Pap smear shows abnormal squamous epithelial cells. Which molecular event underlies this?","lec":"L13","sl":17,"c":"<b>HPV — E6 and E7</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q53 · BM31 SUM1 · BM29 SUM1 · BM28 Q38 — abnormal squamous cells on a Pap smear; which mechanism? Asked in four cohorts, and the ORDER of the two targets is always the trap.</span>","o":{"C":"LANA inhibits p53 and pRb","B":"E6 inhibits pRb and E7 inhibits p53, respectively","D":"E6 inhibits p53 and E7 inhibits pRb, respectively","E":"EBNA-1 drives c-myc deregulation","A":"HBx transactivates host growth genes"},"a":"D","r":{"C":"LANA is an HHV-8 protein.","B":"The swapped version, and it is offered as an option in every cohort that has asked this. Reading past the first three words is what loses the mark here.","D":"Correct. Keep the order by counting: E6 with p53 — the SMALLER number goes with the SMALLER number if you read 6 before 7 and p53 before pRb in the paper's own phrasing. E6 targets p53 for degradation; E7 binds and inactivates pRb. Together they abolish both the G1/S brake and the apoptotic safety net.","E":"EBNA-1 is EBV; c-myc deregulation is the Burkitt mechanism.","A":"HBx is hepatitis B."},"img":"slides_i/L13_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q53","BM29 Q-pap","BM28 Q38"]},{"id":"I|L13|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q67","t":"Kaposi's sarcoma","s":"A 50-year-old man with AIDS has multiple cutaneous vascular lesions and oral nodules. Biopsy confirms Kaposi's sarcoma. Which virus is responsible?","lec":"L13","sl":23,"c":"<b>Kaposi's sarcoma</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q67 · BM31 SUM1 · BM30 SUM1 Q55 · BM29 SUM1 Q57 · BM30 Formative virology Q6 — five appearances, identical options</span>","o":{"B":"HBV","A":"HHV-8","E":"HCV","C":"HPV","D":"HTLV-1"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"HBV causes hepatocellular carcinoma.","A":"Correct. HHV-8 is also called Kaposi's sarcoma-associated herpesvirus (KSHV) — the name gives the answer away, which is presumably why the item has survived five cohorts. It also causes primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease, and it works through lytic proteins such as vIRFs, vIL-6, vCCLs and vGPCR, plus latent LANA.","E":"HCV also causes hepatocellular carcinoma.","C":"HPV causes cervical and other anogenital cancers.","D":"HTLV-1 causes adult T-cell leukaemia."},"img":"slides_i/L13_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q-kaposi","BM30 Q55","BM29 Q57","BM30 virology Q6"]},{"id":"I|L13|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q56","t":"HCC — reading the serology","s":"A 45-year-old man has weight loss, jaundice and hepatomegaly. HBsAg NEGATIVE, anti-HBs POSITIVE, anti-HBc NEGATIVE, AFP high, MRI shows hepatocellular carcinoma. Which viral oncoprotein is implicated?","lec":"L13","sl":27,"c":"<b>HCC — reading the serology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q58 · BM31 SUM1 Q56 — HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HBc NEGATIVE, high AFP, HCC on MRI. BM31 asks for the oncoprotein instead of the virus.</span>","o":{"A":"EBNA-1","B":"NS3","C":"HBc","E":"LANA","D":"HBx"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"EBNA-1 is EBV.","B":"NS3 is a hepatitis C protein.","C":"HBc is the core structural antigen, not an oncoprotein.","E":"LANA is HHV-8.","D":"Correct as the answer the archive records, and HBx is the HBV oncoprotein — it transactivates host growth-control genes and the integrated HBV DNA disrupts the genome. ⚠ Read the serology carefully, though: anti-HBc NEGATIVE with anti-HBs positive is the VACCINATED pattern, not past infection, so this stem as recalled is internally inconsistent. Learn the oncoprotein; treat the serology numbers as the thing to check in the real paper."},"img":"slides_i/L13_27.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM29 Q58"]},{"id":"I|L13|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q54","t":"HCC — the HCV version","s":"A patient with hepatocellular carcinoma has anti-HAV NEGATIVE, anti-HBs POSITIVE and anti-HCV POSITIVE. Which infection is responsible for the tumour?","lec":"L13","sl":29,"c":"<b>HCC — the HCV version</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q54 — anti-HAV negative, anti-HBs positive, anti-HCV POSITIVE, with hepatocellular carcinoma</span>","o":{"C":"Co-infection of hepatitis B and C","A":"Hepatitis A","D":"Hepatitis B","B":"Co-infection of hepatitis A and C","E":"Hepatitis C"},"a":"E","r":{"C":"There is no evidence of active HBV. HCV is an enveloped (+)ssRNA flavivirus whose oncogenesis is largely INDIRECT, through chronic inflammation and cirrhosis.","A":"Hepatitis A causes an acute self-limited illness and never chronic infection or cancer; anti-HAV negative rules it out anyway.","D":"Anti-HBs positive without HBsAg is protection, not disease.","B":"Both halves fail.","E":"Correct, and it is decided by which antibody means what. Anti-HCV POSITIVE indicates HCV infection. Anti-HBs positive on its own means IMMUNITY to hepatitis B (vaccination or resolved infection), not current infection — you would need HBsAg for that. So the only active oncogenic infection here is HCV."},"img":"slides_i/L13_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L13|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q59","t":"EBV — Burkitt mechanism","s":"By which mechanism does Epstein-Barr virus drive Burkitt lymphoma?","lec":"L13","sl":22,"c":"<b>EBV — Burkitt mechanism</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q59 · BM28 Q41 — the mechanism of Epstein-Barr virus oncogenesis / the Burkitt question</span>","o":{"A":"Chronic inflammation alone","B":"Inactivation of p53","E":"Inactivation of pRb","D":"Hepatitis B X transactivation","C":"c-MYC deregulation following a chromosomal translocation in an EBV-immortalised B cell"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Chronic inflammation is the INDIRECT mechanism used by HBV and HCV; EBV's route here is a defined genetic lesion.","B":"p53 inactivation is HPV E6 (and a lytic HHV-8 effect).","E":"pRb inactivation is HPV E7.","D":"X transactivation is HBV.","C":"Correct. EBV immortalises B lymphocytes through EBNA and LMP proteins; on that background a t(8;14) translocation moves c-MYC next to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain enhancer, so a master proliferation gene is driven permanently on."},"img":"slides_i/L13_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q41"]},{"id":"I|L13|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-hpv11","t":"Low-risk HPV","s":"A healthy woman acquires HPV type 11. What is the most likely consequence?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>Low-risk HPV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 Q66 · BM31 SUM1 — a healthy woman acquires HPV type 11; what happens?</span>","o":{"B":"Warts (condylomata acuminata), since types 6 and 11 are low-risk","D":"High risk of cervical cancer","C":"The virus will persist for life in every case","A":"Increased risk of developing AIDS","E":"Kaposi's sarcoma"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The lecture splits HPV by transforming potential: 6 and 11 are LOW-risk and give anogenital warts; 16 and 18 are HIGH-risk and give cervical cancer. Two numbers each — that is the whole classification.","D":"Cervical cancer risk belongs to 16 and 18.","C":"Most HPV infections in an immunocompetent host clear within about two years, which is why 'will clear within 2 years' also appears as an option in these papers.","A":"HPV has no causal link to AIDS.","E":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM29 Q66"]},{"id":"I|L13|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q72","t":"Genital warts","s":"A 32-year-old woman has multiple PAINLESS, CAULIFLOWER-LIKE lesions around the genital area. What is the causative agent?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>Genital warts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q72 — a 32-year-old woman with multiple painless cauliflower-like lesions around the genital area</span>","o":{"B":"HSV-2","C":"HPV subtype 16","E":"HSV-1","D":"HPV subtype 6","A":"Molluscum contagiosum virus"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Same reasoning; HSV-2 genital herpes is painful and recurrent.","C":"HPV 16 is high-risk and causes cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and carcinoma, which do not present as exophytic warts.","E":"HSV lesions are PAINFUL vesicles and ulcers — the word 'painless' is placed in the stem to exclude them.","D":"Correct. Cauliflower-shaped and PAINLESS is condyloma acuminatum, caused by the low-risk types 6 and 11.","A":"Molluscum gives small umbilicated papules, not cauliflower masses."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q37","t":"HTLV-1","s":"A patient is diagnosed with adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma. Which virus is implicated?","lec":"L13","sl":32,"c":"<b>HTLV-1</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q37 — a patient with adult T-cell leukaemia; which virus?</span>","o":{"C":"HTLV-1","E":"EBV","A":"HHV-8","B":"HPV 18","D":"HBV"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. HTLV-1 is a retrovirus that infects CD4+ T cells; besides ATLL it causes HTLV-1-associated myelopathy (tropical spastic paraparesis). Its oncoprotein is Tax, and note that most retroviruses carry only gag, pol and env — HTLV-1's extra regulatory genes are what make it oncogenic.","E":"EBV transforms B cells, not T cells.","A":"HHV-8 causes Kaposi's sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma.","B":"HPV 18 causes cervical cancer.","D":"HBV causes hepatocellular carcinoma."},"img":"slides_i/L13_32.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L13|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q40","t":"HBV — the X gene","s":"Which hepatitis B gene product acts by TRANSACTIVATION of host growth-control genes?","lec":"L13","sl":28,"c":"<b>HBV — the X gene</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q40 — the HBV gene responsible for transactivation</span>","o":{"C":"HBx","E":"HBsAg","D":"HBcAg","A":"HBV polymerase","B":"HBeAg"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. The X protein transactivates cellular promoters and interferes with DNA repair and apoptosis; together with integration of viral DNA and the chronic inflammation of long-standing hepatitis, it makes chronic carriers the high-risk group for HCC.","E":"HBsAg is the surface antigen — the marker of active infection and the component of the vaccine.","D":"HBcAg is the core protein; anti-HBc marks past or present natural infection.","A":"The polymerase is a reverse transcriptase (HBV is Baltimore VII) but is not the oncoprotein.","B":"HBeAg is a marker of high replication and infectivity."},"img":"slides_i/L13_28.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L13|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q39","t":"HPV and condylomata","s":"Which HPV types cause condylomata acuminata?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>HPV and condylomata</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 Q39 — which HPV strains cause condylomata acuminatum?</span>","o":{"A":"1 and 2","E":"16 and 18","D":"31 and 33","B":"6 and 11","C":"52 and 58"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"1 and 2 cause common skin warts, not anogenital condylomata.","E":"16 and 18 are the high-risk pair behind roughly 70% of cervical cancers.","D":"31 and 33 are additional high-risk types.","B":"Correct — the low-risk pair. Hold the four numbers as two pairs: 6/11 warts, 16/18 cancer.","C":"52 and 58 are further high-risk types covered by the nonavalent vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L13|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"HBV genes and the antigens they encode","s":"A 30-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B has HBsAg and anti-HBc in his serum. Using the lecture's list of the four HBV genes, which genes encode the two viral antigens behind these results?","lec":"L13","sl":25,"c":"<b>HBV genes and the antigens they encode</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HBsAg from the S gene; HBcAg from the P gene","B":"HBsAg from the C gene; HBcAg from the S gene","C":"HBsAg from the P gene; HBcAg from the X gene","D":"HBsAg from the X gene; HBcAg from the C gene","E":"HBsAg from the S gene; HBcAg from the C gene"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"P encodes the DNA polymerase, not the core antigen.","B":"The letters match the antigens: S → surface, C → core.","C":"P = polymerase and X = HBx; neither encodes these antigens.","D":"X encodes HBx (transactivator), not the surface antigen.","E":"Slide 25: C = core protein (HBcAg), P = DNA polymerase, S = surface antigen (HBsAg), X = possible transcriptional transactivator. Anti-HBc is antibody to the C-gene product."},"img":"slides_i/L13_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about the hepatitis B genome and its role in cancer matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"The genome has four genes — C (core antigen), P (DNA polymerase), S (surface antigen) and X — and the X protein, HBx, transactivates cellular oncogenes as one of HBV's DIRECT oncogenic mechanisms","B":"The genome has four genes and the S protein is the transactivator responsible for oncogenesis","D":"HBx acts only indirectly, by provoking chronic inflammation; HBV has no direct mechanism","C":"The C gene encodes the DNA polymerase and the P gene the core antigen","A":"HBV has no integration step, which is why it cannot act directly on the host genome"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and HBV is worth noting as the virus with BOTH mechanisms — direct (integration, tumour suppressor inactivation, HBx transactivation) and indirect (chronic inflammation).","B":"S encodes the SURFACE antigen, HBsAg — the one you measure to diagnose infection.","D":"Chronic inflammation is listed as HBV's indirect mechanism, but three direct mechanisms are listed alongside it.","C":"The two are swapped: C = core, P = polymerase.","A":"Chromosomal integration with cis activation is the first listed direct mechanism."},"t":"HBV's four genes","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L13_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HBV's four genes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Why HCV leaves no viral DNA in the tumour","s":"Two men develop hepatocellular carcinoma. Tumour DNA from the first, a chronic HBV carrier, contains integrated viral sequences; no viral sequences are found in the second man's tumour DNA, although he has had chronic HCV for 30 years. Which feature of HCV accounts for the difference?","lec":"L13","sl":29,"c":"<b>Why HCV leaves no viral DNA in the tumour</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It has no oncogene and makes no DNA intermediate","B":"Its reverse transcriptase is blocked by interferon","C":"Its dsDNA genome is excised during tumour growth","D":"It persists as an episome that is lost on division","E":"It integrates only into non-dividing hepatocytes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 29: 'HCV is an RNA virus that has no oncogene and forms no DNA intermediate during replication' — so nothing can integrate. Its carcinogenesis is mainly indirect (chronic inflammation, slide 31).","B":"HCV has no reverse transcriptase.","C":"HCV has a ss(+)RNA genome, not dsDNA.","D":"Episomes are a DNA-virus form of persistence (slide 8), not HCV's.","E":"HCV never integrates at all."},"img":"slides_i/L13_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Both HBV and HCV cause hepatocellular carcinoma. What does the lecture give as the essential difference in how they do it?","o":{"A":"HCV has no oncogene and forms no DNA intermediate, so its summary row lists no viral oncoprotein and attributes the cancer to CHRONIC INFLAMMATION; HBV additionally acts directly through integration and HBx","B":"HCV integrates into the host genome and HBV does not","E":"HCV encodes an oncoprotein called HBx, the same one as HBV","D":"HCV causes cancer only in patients co-infected with HIV","C":"HBV acts only through chronic inflammation and HCV only through direct integration"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and it follows from its biology: a (+) ssRNA flavivirus that never makes DNA cannot integrate, so the route to cancer must be indirect.","B":"The opposite — HCV cannot integrate, precisely because it makes no DNA intermediate.","E":"HBx belongs to HBV. HCV's direct contribution, if any, is tentatively linked to its core and non-structural proteins and the deck says the mechanism remains unclear.","D":"No such requirement is stated; chronic HCV infection occurs in 50–85% of those infected.","C":"Both halves are inverted."},"t":"Why HCV is different","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L13_29.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Why HCV is different</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"HHV-8 — latency oncoprotein","s":"A man with advanced HIV has bluish-purple skin nodules; biopsy shows spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces with extravasated red cells in the dermis. During latent infection, which viral protein takes control of tumour suppression, and how?","lec":"L13","sl":23,"c":"<b>HHV-8 — latency oncoprotein</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"LANA, by inactivating p53 and pRb","B":"vIL-6, by inactivating p53 and pRb","C":"vGPCR, by inactivating p53 alone","D":"Tax, by activating T-cell growth","E":"LMP-1, by deregulating c-Myc"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Kaposi's sarcoma = HHV-8. Slide 24: in LATENT infection, LANA inactivates p53 and pRb.","B":"vIL-6 is a LYTIC-phase protein that promotes angiogenic and inflammatory factors (indirect) — it does not target p53/pRb.","C":"vGPCR is also a lytic protein, acting indirectly through secreted factors.","D":"Tax is HTLV-1.","E":"LMP-1 and c-Myc belong to EBV."},"img":"slides_i/L13_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A man with advanced HIV develops bluish-purple skin lesions that on biopsy show spindle-cell vascular proliferation with slit-like spaces and extravasated red cells. Which statement about the mechanism is correct?","o":{"A":"HHV-8 (KSHV) causes Kaposi's sarcoma; in latency its oncoprotein LANA inactivates p53 and pRb, while lytic proteins such as vIL-6 and vGPCR drive angiogenic and inflammatory factors — and HIV contributes indirectly by removing CD8 T-cell control","E":"HHV-8's oncoprotein is E6, which inactivates p53","B":"Kaposi's sarcoma is caused by EBV, whose LMP-1 protein drives the vascular proliferation","D":"HIV itself transforms the endothelial cells directly through its Tax protein","C":"LANA acts only in the lytic phase; the latent phase is not oncogenic"},"r":{"A":"Correct. HHV-8 is the clearest case of direct and indirect mechanisms acting together — LANA disables the brakes while HIV removes the immune surveillance.","E":"E6 is HPV’s. HHV-8’s latent oncoprotein is LANA, though both converge on p53.","B":"LMP-1 is an EBV protein; Kaposi’s sarcoma is HHV-8/KSHV.","D":"Tax is HTLV-1’s protein, and HIV’s contribution here is indirect — immunosuppression, not transformation.","C":"LANA is explicitly the LATENT-phase oncoprotein; the lytic proteins are the vIRFs, vIL-6, vCCLs and vGPCR."},"t":"LANA","sl":23,"img":"slides_i/L13_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>LANA</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"HPV vaccines — what each adds","s":"Parents choosing an HPV vaccine for their 12-year-old are told the quadrivalent vaccine covers more than the bivalent one. Against which condition does the quadrivalent vaccine add protection, and through which types?","lec":"L13","sl":20,"c":"<b>HPV vaccines — what each adds</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cervical cancer, through types 6 and 11","B":"Anal cancer, through types 45 and 52","C":"Genital warts, through types 16 and 18","D":"Genital warts, through types 6 and 11","E":"Cervical cancer, through types 31 and 33"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"6 and 11 are low-risk types that cause warts, not cancer.","B":"45 and 52 are also nonavalent-only types.","C":"16 and 18 are already in the bivalent vaccine, and they are cancer types, not wart types.","D":"Bivalent = 16, 18; quadrivalent = 6, 11, 16, 18 (slide 20). Types 6 and 11 are low-risk wart types (slide 19).","E":"31 and 33 are added only by the nonavalent vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L13_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about HPV vaccines matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"The bivalent vaccine covers types 16 and 18; the quadrivalent adds the LOW-risk types 6 and 11 that cause genital warts; the nonavalent adds the further high-risk types 31, 33, 45, 52 and 58","E":"The bivalent vaccine covers types 6 and 11, the quadrivalent adds 16 and 18","A":"The nonavalent vaccine covers nine low-risk types only, since high-risk types cannot be vaccinated against","C":"All three vaccines cover the same types and differ only in adjuvant","B":"Types 6 and 11 are high-risk types included because they cause cervical cancer"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the logic of the quadrivalent vaccine is worth noting — it adds coverage for a benign disease (genital warts), not for more cancer.","E":"Inverted — the bivalent covers the two commonest CANCER types, 16 and 18.","A":"The nonavalent covers two low-risk plus seven high-risk types.","C":"They differ in which HPV types they cover, which is the entire point of the valency naming.","B":"6 and 11 are LOW-risk types causing genital warts and benign cervical change."},"t":"HPV vaccine valency","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HPV vaccine valency</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Proto-oncogene vs tumour suppressor","s":"In tumour X, a single mutated allele of a gene encoding the transcription factor c-Myc drives proliferation. In tumour Y, both alleles of the gene encoding pRb have been lost. How does the lecture classify the two genes and the kind of change?","lec":"L13","sl":9,"c":"<b>Proto-oncogene vs tumour suppressor</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"X a proto-oncogene with loss of function; Y a tumour suppressor with gain of function","B":"X a proto-oncogene with gain of function; Y a tumour suppressor with loss of function","C":"X and Y both tumour suppressors, differing only in how many alleles changed","D":"X and Y both proto-oncogenes, differing only in how many alleles changed","E":"X a tumour suppressor with gain of function; Y a proto-oncogene with loss of function"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Directions reversed — oncogenes gain, suppressors lose.","B":"Slide 9: proto-oncogenes code for growth factors, receptors, signal transducers and transcription factors (c-Myc); tumour suppressors code for p53 and Rb. Slide 10: cancer = proto-oncogene GAIN of function or tumour-suppressor LOSS of function.","C":"c-Myc is a proto-oncogene product.","D":"pRb is a tumour suppressor.","E":"Reversed classification."},"img":"slides_i/L13_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the two categories of cell-cycle regulatory gene matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"Proto-oncogenes cause cancer through GAIN of function and encode growth factors, growth factor receptors, signal transducers and transcription factors such as c-Myc; tumour suppressor genes cause cancer through LOSS of function and include p53 and Rb","C":"Proto-oncogenes cause cancer through loss of function and tumour suppressors through gain of function","E":"p53 and Rb are proto-oncogenes whose overactivity drives proliferation","A":"c-Myc is a tumour suppressor gene inactivated by EBV","D":"Both categories act by gain of function; the difference is only which tissue they affect"},"r":{"B":"Correct, and it frames every oncogenic mechanism in the lecture: viral proteins either mimic a gain-of-function accelerator or disable a loss-of-function brake.","C":"Inverted — an accelerator causes trouble when stuck on, a brake when it fails.","E":"p53 and Rb are the two named TUMOUR SUPPRESSORS; their LOSS is what matters.","A":"c-Myc is listed as a transcription factor encoded by a PROTO-ONCOGENE.","D":"The two categories are defined by opposite directions of change."},"t":"Gain vs loss of function","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L13_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Gain vs loss of function</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Episome or integration","s":"In one virus-associated tumour, every cancer cell carries the viral genome as a circular molecule separate from the chromosomes. In another, viral DNA is found inserted into the host genome. How does the lecture interpret these two findings?","lec":"L13","sl":8,"c":"<b>Episome or integration</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Evidence of the indirect mechanism through inflammation","B":"Two forms of genome persistence in the direct mechanism","C":"The indirect mechanism in the first; direct in the second","D":"Abortive infection, as neither makes progeny virus","E":"The direct mechanism in the first; indirect in the second"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"A viral genome maintained inside the tumour cell is the direct route; inflammation needs no viral genome in the tumour cell.","B":"Slide 8: after infection, tumour viruses are persistently maintained as genetic elements — either as episomes or integrated into host DNA. Both belong to the direct mechanism.","C":"The episome is not an indirect route either.","D":"Persistence without progeny is exactly what transformation needs; the lecture does not call it abortive.","E":"Both forms are direct — integration is not an indirect route."},"img":"slides_i/L13_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about the direct mechanism of viral oncogenesis matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"The viral genome is persistently maintained in the cell either as an EPISOME or by INTEGRATING into host genomic DNA, and most virus-induced cancers develop only after a long period of persistent infection or latency","C":"The viral genome must always integrate; episomal persistence cannot cause cancer","D":"Direct oncogenesis occurs within days of infection, which is why latency is irrelevant","E":"The virus must remain lytic, since only actively replicating virus can transform a cell","A":"Only RNA viruses can persist as episomes"},"r":{"B":"Correct on both halves. The episome route matters because it explains how a virus can transform a cell without ever integrating.","C":"Episomal maintenance is one of the two routes drawn on the slide.","D":"The deck stresses that most virus-induced cancers follow a LONG period of persistent infection or latency.","E":"Transformation depends on persistent, largely LATENT infection — a lytic cycle kills the cell.","A":"The episome panel refers to DNA tumour viruses; the claim is backwards."},"t":"Episome or integrated","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L13_7.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Episome or integrated</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Immunosuppression — who was keeping the virus in check","s":"A man with HIV whose CD4 count has fallen steadily develops an HHV-8-associated lymphoma. He had carried HHV-8 for years without any disease before his HIV progressed. According to the lecture, which cells had been controlling the HHV-8 infection?","lec":"L13","sl":11,"c":"<b>Immunosuppression — who was keeping the virus in check</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Tissue macrophages","B":"Memory B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ helper T cells","D":"Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells","E":"Regulatory T cells"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Monocytes are listed as HHV-8 target cells, not as its controllers.","B":"B cells are an HHV-8 target (slide 23), not its controller.","C":"The falling CD4 count is how HIV destroys immunity, but the slide names CD8 T cells as the controllers.","D":"Slide 11(B): in immunocompetent people HHV-8 infection is efficiently controlled by cytotoxic CD8 T cells; as HIV progresses and immune responses collapse, HHV-8 lymphoma risk rises.","E":"Not named on the slide."},"img":"slides_i/L13_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"The lecture gives two indirect mechanisms of viral oncogenesis. Which description of the immunosuppression mechanism is correct?","o":{"D":"HIV is the prototype agent: in immunocompetent people HHV-8 infection is efficiently controlled by cytotoxic CD8 T cells, and as HIV progresses and that response collapses the risk of HHV-8-associated tumours rises","C":"HIV directly integrates beside a proto-oncogene in endothelial cells, which is a direct mechanism","B":"Immunosuppression works by increasing chronic inflammation in the infected tissue","A":"HHV-8 is controlled by antibody, and HIV causes cancer by depleting B cells","E":"Immunosuppression is a theoretical mechanism with no named example in the lecture"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and it explains why Kaposi’s sarcoma became an AIDS-defining illness — the tumour virus was always there, the control was not.","C":"HIV’s role here is explicitly INDIRECT; it does not transform the endothelial cell.","B":"Chronic inflammation is the OTHER indirect mechanism, described separately on the same slide.","A":"The slide names cytotoxic CD8 T cells as the controlling arm.","E":"HIV and HHV-8 are the named worked example."},"t":"Immunosuppression as an indirect route","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L13_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Immunosuppression as an indirect route</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"HTLV-1 — Tax and HBZ","s":"In T cells infected with the retrovirus that causes adult T-cell leukaemia, one extra viral protein enhances proliferation and interferes with DNA repair, while a second acts in late-stage tumour formation. Which pair is it?","lec":"L13","sl":33,"c":"<b>HTLV-1 — Tax and HBZ</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HBx early; Tax late","B":"Tax early; HBZ late","C":"Tax early; LANA late","D":"HBZ early; Tax late","E":"LMP-1 early; HBZ late"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"HBx is HBV's transactivator — similar name, different virus.","B":"Slide 33: Tax — enhanced proliferative potential of T lymphocytes and interference with cell regulation and DNA repair; HBZ (HTLV-1 basic leucine zipper factor) — late-stage tumorigenesis.","C":"LANA is HHV-8's latency protein.","D":"Reversed: HBZ is the late-stage factor.","E":"LMP-1 is EBV's."},"img":"slides_i/L13_33.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about HTLV-1 matches the lecture?","o":{"B":"It targets CD4+ T cells and causes both adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma and HAM/TSP; its oncoprotein Tax enhances T-cell proliferation and disrupts cell regulation and DNA repair, while HBZ acts in late-stage tumorigenesis","A":"It targets CD8+ T cells, and its only oncoprotein is LANA","E":"Tax acts in late-stage tumorigenesis and HBZ drives the initial proliferation","D":"HTLV-1 carries only gag, pol and env, like every other retrovirus","C":"It is transmitted only by blood transfusion, so breastfeeding carries no risk"},"r":{"B":"Correct on target cell, both diseases and the division of labour between the two oncoproteins.","A":"It targets CD4+ T cells, and LANA is HHV-8’s protein.","E":"The roles are swapped — Tax drives early proliferation, HBZ acts late.","D":"The deck notes that some retroviruses carry EXTRA transactivating regulatory genes, which is exactly how HTLV-1 gets Tax and HBZ.","C":"Mother-to-child transmission through breastfeeding is listed explicitly."},"t":"HTLV-1 — Tax and HBZ","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L13_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HTLV-1 — Tax and HBZ</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"EBV — oncoproteins and mechanism","s":"A patient with AIDS develops a B-cell lymphoma whose cells express EBNA-1, EBNA-2, LMP-1 and LMP-2. According to the lecture's summary, which combination of mechanisms explains this tumour?","lec":"L13","sl":22,"c":"<b>EBV — oncoproteins and mechanism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"c-Myc dysregulation, blocked apoptosis and immunosuppression","B":"Transactivation of oncogenes by HBx, plus chronic inflammation","C":"Chronic inflammation alone, with no viral oncoprotein","D":"Tax-driven proliferation and interference with DNA repair","E":"p53 and pRb inactivation by LANA, plus immunosuppression"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 35 EBV row: EBNA-1, EBNA-2, LMP-1, LMP-2, BamHI → c-Myc dysregulation, cell proliferation, inhibition of apoptosis, indirect action of immunosuppression (slide 22: AIDS-associated lymphoma).","B":"HBx and chronic inflammation are HBV's.","C":"'Chronic inflammation alone, no oncoprotein' is the HCV row.","D":"Tax belongs to HTLV-1.","E":"LANA is HHV-8's latency protein — the other herpesvirus with an immunosuppression route."},"img":"slides_i/L13_22.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about EBV as an oncogenic virus matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Its oncoproteins are EBNA-1, EBNA-2, LMP-1, LMP-2 and BamHI; the mechanism includes c-Myc dysregulation, driven proliferation and inhibition of apoptosis, with immunosuppression as an additional indirect route","C":"Its oncoprotein is E7 and its mechanism is inactivation of pRb","B":"It causes only Burkitt's lymphoma; no other malignancy is associated","E":"It is latent in T lymphocytes and nasopharyngeal endothelium","D":"Its genome is ssRNA, which is why it has no DNA-based mechanism"},"r":{"A":"Correct. EBV is the virus with the longest oncoprotein list in the table, and c-Myc dysregulation is the mechanism behind Burkitt’s lymphoma.","C":"E7 and pRb are HPV’s.","B":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and Hodgkin’s/non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma are listed alongside Burkitt’s.","E":"EBV is latent in B LYMPHOCYTES and nasopharyngeal EPITHELIAL cells.","D":"EBV is a dsDNA herpesvirus."},"t":"EBV's oncoproteins","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L13_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>EBV's oncoproteins</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"The RNA oncoviruses","s":"Among the lecture's six oncogenic viruses, two have single-stranded RNA genomes. The one that has NO reverse transcriptase and forms no DNA intermediate is linked to which cancer?","lec":"L13","sl":13,"c":"<b>The RNA oncoviruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Burkitt's lymphoma","B":"Kaposi's sarcoma","C":"Adult T-cell leukaemia","D":"Anal squamous carcinoma","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Burkitt's lymphoma is EBV, a dsDNA virus.","B":"Kaposi's sarcoma is HHV-8, a dsDNA virus.","C":"ATLL is caused by HTLV-1, the ssRNA virus that DOES have reverse transcriptase.","D":"Anal cancer is HPV, a dsDNA virus.","E":"The ssRNA oncoviruses are HCV and HTLV-1 (slide 13). HTLV-1 carries reverse transcriptase; HCV does not (slide 29) — and HCV causes hepatocellular carcinoma."},"img":"slides_i/L13_13.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which grouping of the six oncogenic viruses by genome matches the lecture's table?","o":{"E":"dsDNA — HPV, EBV, HHV-8 and HBV; ssRNA — HCV and HTLV-1","D":"dsDNA — HCV and HTLV-1; ssRNA — HPV, EBV, HHV-8 and HBV","B":"All six are dsDNA viruses, which is why they can integrate","A":"dsDNA — HPV and HBV only; the other four are ssRNA","C":"HBV is listed as ssRNA because it uses reverse transcriptase"},"r":{"E":"Correct — four DNA viruses and two RNA viruses, and the two RNA ones are the two with no classical direct mechanism.","D":"Entirely inverted.","B":"HCV and HTLV-1 are RNA viruses.","A":"EBV and HHV-8 are dsDNA herpesviruses.","C":"Despite its reverse transcriptase, the table lists HBV’s genome as dsDNA — it is a Baltimore class VII dsDNA virus."},"t":"Genome type across the table","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L13_13.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Genome type across the table</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Treatment — HBV vs HCV","s":"A 50-year-old man with chronic hepatitis is anti-HCV positive with detectable HCV RNA, and hepatocellular carcinoma screening is planned. Which group of drugs does the lecture list as direct-acting antivirals for his infection?","lec":"L13","sl":31,"c":"<b>Treatment — HBV vs HCV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Aciclovir, ganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir","B":"Lamivudine, adefovir, entecavir, tenofovir","C":"Antiretroviral agents with interferon-alpha","D":"Sofosbuvir, daclatasvir, ledipasvir, velpatasvir","E":"Oseltamivir, zanamivir, amantadine, rimantadine"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Anti-herpesvirus drugs — not used for HCV.","B":"Those are the HBV drugs (slide 28).","C":"ART plus interferon-alpha is the HTLV-1 treatment (slide 34).","D":"Slide 31: HCV treatment = PR (PegIFNα + ribavirin) or DAAs sofosbuvir, daclatasvir, ledipasvir, velpatasvir.","E":"Anti-influenza drugs."},"img":"slides_i/L13_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of hepatitis virus with its listed treatment matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"HBV — lamivudine, adefovir, entecavir, tenofovir or pegylated interferon alfa; HCV — pegylated interferon alfa with ribavirin, or direct-acting antivirals such as sofosbuvir, daclatasvir, ledipasvir and velpatasvir","C":"HBV — sofosbuvir and ledipasvir; HCV — entecavir and tenofovir","A":"Both are treated identically with aciclovir","E":"Neither has any treatment; only vaccination is available","B":"HCV has a vaccine and HBV does not"},"r":{"D":"Correct for both. The HCV direct-acting antivirals are the ‘-buvir/-asvir’ group and are curative, unlike the HBV suppressive agents.","C":"The two drug lists are swapped.","A":"Aciclovir treats herpesviruses and has no role in viral hepatitis.","E":"Both have listed treatments.","B":"Inverted — the deck lists a hepatitis B VACCINE for prevention; there is no HCV vaccine."},"t":"HBV and HCV treatment","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L13_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HBV and HCV treatment</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"What makes a virus oncogenic","s":"A research group proposes that a newly found virus causes a human skin tumour. Which pair of observations would best support the claim, according to the lecture's properties of an oncogenic virus?","lec":"L13","sl":5,"c":"<b>What makes a virus oncogenic</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Infected cells lyse quickly; viral markers are in surrounding stroma","B":"Tumours arise years after infection; no viral markers remain in them","C":"Tumours arise only after RNA-virus infection; markers are in serum","D":"Tumours arise days after infection; virus is cleared from tumour cells","E":"Tumours arise years after infection; viral markers are in tumour cells"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Rapid lysis kills the cell — the opposite of transformation.","B":"Viral markers are expected in the tumour cells.","C":"Oncogenic viruses can be either DNA or RNA viruses.","D":"Latency is long, and markers stay in the tumour cells.","E":"Slide 5: long latency between infection and tumorigenesis, and viral markers present in tumour cells."},"img":"slides_i/L13_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about the defining properties of an oncogenic virus matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"There is a long latency between viral infection and tumorigenesis, and viral markers are present in the tumour cells; oncogenic viruses may be either DNA or RNA viruses","A":"Tumours appear within weeks of infection, and the virus is cleared before the tumour develops","D":"Only DNA viruses can be oncogenic, because only they can integrate","B":"Viral markers are absent from tumour cells, which is why the association is only epidemiological","C":"Oncogenic viruses kill the cells they infect, and the tumour arises from neighbouring uninfected cells"},"r":{"E":"Correct on all three. Viral markers in the tumour cells are what makes the causal claim more than epidemiological.","A":"The defining feature is a LONG latency, and the virus persists.","D":"HCV and HTLV-1 are RNA viruses on the same table.","B":"Presence of viral markers IN tumour cells is one of the listed properties.","C":"Transformation requires the cell to survive; a killed cell cannot become a tumour."},"t":"What makes a virus oncogenic","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L13_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>What makes a virus oncogenic</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"The four leading cancer viruses","s":"Of the four viruses the lecture names as the leading causes of virus-associated human cancer, two cause the same cancer but have different genome types. Which pair is it?","lec":"L13","sl":4,"c":"<b>The four leading cancer viruses</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HBV and EBV","B":"EBV and HHV-8","C":"HBV and HCV","D":"HPV and HBV","E":"HCV and HTLV-1"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"HBV and EBV cause different cancers.","B":"HHV-8 is not one of the leading four; both are dsDNA herpesviruses.","C":"Leading four = HPV, HBV, HCV, EBV (slide 4). HBV (dsDNA) and HCV (ssRNA) both cause hepatocellular carcinoma (slide 13).","D":"HPV and HBV cause different cancers, and both are dsDNA.","E":"HTLV-1 is not one of the leading four, and both are ssRNA."},"img":"slides_i/L13_4.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which four viruses does the lecture name as the LEADING causative viruses associated with human cancers?","o":{"C":"HPV, HBV, HCV and EBV","D":"HPV, HHV-8, HTLV-1 and HIV","B":"HBV, HCV, HIV and CMV","E":"EBV, HHV-8, HSV-1 and HSV-2","A":"HTLV-1, HIV, measles virus and rubella virus"},"r":{"C":"Correct — the four the deck highlights on its causes-of-cancer slide, which together account for the great majority of virus-attributable cancer worldwide.","D":"HHV-8 and HTLV-1 are in the full table but are not among the four leading ones, and HIV acts indirectly.","B":"HIV and CMV are not on the leading list.","E":"HSV-1 and HSV-2 are not oncogenic viruses in this lecture.","A":"Measles and rubella are not oncogenic."},"t":"The four leading cancer viruses","sl":4,"img":"slides_i/L13_4.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>The four leading cancer viruses</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L13|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"HPV risk — what decides it","s":"HPV 16 and HPV 11 both infect the squamous epithelium of the genital tract, yet only HPV 16 is classed as high-risk. On what does the lecture base this risk classification?","lec":"L13","sl":19,"c":"<b>HPV risk — what decides it</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The antigenicity of the late (L) capsid proteins","B":"The receptor the virus uses to enter cells","C":"Whether the genome is circular or linear","D":"The transforming potential of the early (E) proteins","E":"Whether the virion carries a lipid envelope"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The L genes build the capsid (slide 15); they do not decide oncogenic risk.","B":"Both infect the same squamous cells; entry is not the basis of the classification.","C":"All HPV types have circular dsDNA.","D":"Slide 19: 'Risk assessment based on transformative potential of a strain's E proteins' — E6/E7 are the oncoproteins (slide 17).","E":"All HPV types are non-enveloped (slide 14)."},"img":"slides_i/L13_19.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about human papillomavirus structure matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"It is a NON-enveloped virus with a circular dsDNA genome and an icosahedral nucleocapsid that infects human squamous epithelial cells, and its genome is divided into early (E) and late (L) genes","E":"It is an enveloped virus with linear dsDNA that infects columnar epithelium","A":"Its genome contains only late (L) genes, which is why E6 and E7 are host proteins","B":"It is a retrovirus that integrates using reverse transcriptase","C":"It infects endothelial cells, which is why it causes vascular tumours"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the early/late split matters because E6 and E7 — the transforming proteins — are EARLY genes.","E":"HPV is non-enveloped with a CIRCULAR genome and infects SQUAMOUS epithelium.","A":"E6 and E7 are viral early proteins; that is the basis of the whole mechanism.","B":"HPV is a dsDNA papillomavirus, not a retrovirus.","C":"Endothelial tropism and vascular tumours describe HHV-8."},"t":"HPV structure and target cell","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L13_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>HPV structure and target cell</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233838319},{"id":"I|L14|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Zika — reading the comparison table","s":"A 26-year-old woman returns from a two-week trip and develops low-grade fever, an itchy maculopapular rash, marked bilateral conjunctivitis and swelling of the hands. Her platelet and white-cell counts are normal and she has no bleeding. Which statement about the most likely virus is correct according to the lecture?","lec":"L14","sl":62,"c":"<b>Zika — reading the comparison table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its principal vector is Culex tritaeniorhynchus","B":"It circulates as four distinct serotypes","C":"Most infected people (72–97%) develop symptoms","D":"It belongs to Togaviridae, genus Alphavirus","E":"About one in four infected people develop symptoms"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Culex tritaeniorhynchus is the vector of Japanese encephalitis (slide 46). Zika is carried by Aedes aegypti.","B":"Four serotypes (DENV-1 to -4) is dengue. Zika has two lineages — African and Asian (slide 36).","C":"72–97% symptomatic is CHIKUNGUNYA (slide 57) — the outlier that makes almost everyone ill. Chikungunya also scores +++ for leukopenia/thrombocytopenia, which this patient does not have.","D":"Togaviridae, genus Alphavirus is chikungunya. Zika is Flaviviridae, genus Flavivirus (slide 36).","E":"Correct. Conjunctivitis (+++), oedema (++), rash (+++) and NORMAL platelets/white cells (Zika is '−' for leukopenia/thrombocytopenia on the slide-62 table) point to Zika. Slide 39: only 1 in 4 infected people develop symptoms."},"img":"slides_i/L14_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Dengue, chikungunya and Zika all present as fever with rash after a mosquito bite. Which set of discriminators is correct?","o":{"E":"Dengue — thrombocytopenia, leukopenia and haemorrhage; Chikungunya — severe, persistent POLYARTHRITIS; Zika — usually mild or asymptomatic, with conjunctivitis, and microcephaly if in pregnancy","D":"Dengue — severe polyarthritis; Chikungunya — thrombocytopenia and haemorrhage; Zika — encephalitis","A":"All three are clinically identical and can only be separated by RT-PCR","B":"Dengue — jaundice; Chikungunya — microcephaly; Zika — shock","C":"Dengue — encephalitis; Chikungunya — hepatitis; Zika — haemorrhage"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and this is the row the final summary slide is built on. One word each: dengue BLEEDS, chikungunya ACHES, Zika is QUIET until someone is pregnant.","D":"The first two are swapped — and this swap is exactly the option offered in a recent paper.","A":"Serology and PCR confirm, but the exam expects clinical separation.","B":"Jaundice is YELLOW FEVER; microcephaly is Zika; shock is dengue.","C":"Encephalitis belongs to Japanese encephalitis and West Nile, not dengue."},"t":"The comparison table","sl":62,"img":"slides_i/L14_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The comparison table</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Antibody-dependent enhancement — the cell and the receptor","s":"A 10-year-old Thai boy had laboratory-confirmed dengue two years ago. He is now infected with a different serotype and progresses to shock. According to the mechanism the lecture marks as the most important in severe dengue, how does the new virus gain increased entry into host cells?","lec":"L14","sl":24,"c":"<b>Antibody-dependent enhancement — the cell and the receptor</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Bound to maternal IgM, it enters neurons across the blood–brain barrier","B":"Coated with neutralising antibody, it enters hepatocytes via complement receptors","C":"Held by cross-reactive memory T cells, it enters dendritic cells via MHC class I","D":"Bound to complement, it enters endothelial cells and lyses them directly","E":"Coated with non-neutralising antibody, it enters monocytes via Fc receptors"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Nerve cells are the target of JE and West Nile. IgM is not the enhancing antibody, and the stem's risk comes from his OWN earlier infection.","B":"Neutralising antibody protects (the upper path on slide 25). Hepatocytes are yellow fever's target, not dengue's.","C":"Cross-reactive memory T cells are ORIGINAL ANTIGENIC SIN (slide 26) — a T-cell response problem, not a route of viral entry.","D":"Complement and cytokines activating endothelium is the IMMUNE COMPLEX mechanism (slide 27), which increases vascular permeability; it is not a route of viral entry.","E":"Correct — ADE (marked ** on slide 24). Slide 25: cross-reactive NON-neutralising anti-DENV antibody forms complexes with a HETEROLOGOUS serotype, and the complexes are taken up into monocytes via the Fc receptor, increasing virus production. Dengue's target cells are monocytes, macrophages and dendritic cells (slide 12)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why is a SECOND dengue infection with a different serotype more dangerous than the first?","o":{"B":"The second serotype is intrinsically more virulent than the first","E":"Cross-reactive, non-neutralising antibody from the first infection binds the new serotype and carries it into Fc-receptor-bearing cells — antibody-dependent enhancement","A":"The first infection destroys the bone marrow, so the patient cannot respond","C":"Antibody from the first infection neutralises the patient's own platelets","D":"The second infection is always caused by a mosquito carrying a larger viral dose"},"r":{"B":"All four serotypes can cause severe disease; the lecture notes DEN-2 as highest risk, but that is a modifier, not the mechanism.","E":"Correct, and ADE is the mechanism the lecturer marks with a double asterisk. Antibody raised against serotype 1 cross-reacts with serotype 2 but does not neutralise it; the antibody-virus complex is then taken up through Fc receptors on monocytes and macrophages, so MORE cells are infected, not fewer. The result is the cytokine surge and plasma leakage of DHF/DSS.","A":"The marrow is not destroyed — the thrombocytopenia is consumptive and immune-mediated.","C":"Anti-platelet autoantibody plays a minor part but is not ADE.","D":"Dose is not the explanation. Two related ideas travel with ADE: ORIGINAL ANTIGENIC SIN, where cross-reactive memory T cells respond suboptimally to the new serotype, and immune-complex activation of endothelium."},"t":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L14_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Antibody-dependent enhancement</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"The critical phase at defervescence","s":"A 12-year-old girl has had high fever for four days. This morning her temperature is 37.2 °C and her mother says she looks better, but she has abdominal pain and cold hands, her haematocrit has risen since yesterday and her platelet count has fallen. Which phase of illness is she in, and what is the main danger now?","lec":"L14","sl":22,"c":"<b>The critical phase at defervescence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Critical phase — plasma leakage leading to shock","B":"Febrile phase — high fever leading to dehydration","C":"Recovery phase — reabsorbed fluid leading to overload","D":"Toxic phase — hepatocyte necrosis leading to jaundice","E":"Recovery phase — fever resolving with little further risk"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. On the WHO course-of-illness graph (slide 22) the critical phase starts as the temperature FALLS (around days 3–6): haematocrit rises, platelets fall, and the risks are shock, bleeding and organ impairment. Rising Hct = haemoconcentration from plasma leakage (slide 21).","B":"Dehydration is the risk of the FEBRILE phase (days 1–3), when temperature is ~40 °C and viraemia peaks.","C":"Fluid reabsorption and overload belong to the RECOVERY phase (days 7–10), which comes after the critical phase.","D":"A 'toxic phase' with hepatocyte damage and jaundice is YELLOW FEVER's second phase (slide 17), not dengue.","E":"Defervescence feels like improvement but is exactly when the dangerous phase begins; recovery is marked by bradycardia, returning appetite and a haematocrit returning to normal (slide 23)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"In dengue, WHEN does the patient most often deteriorate into shock?","o":{"B":"On the first day of fever, at the peak of viraemia","A":"At DEFERVESCENCE — as the fever falls, around days 3–7, when plasma leakage begins","E":"Only after the rash has completely resolved","C":"During the convalescent phase, once appetite returns","D":"Two to three weeks after the acute illness"},"r":{"B":"Viraemia peaks early, but shock does not — the damage is immune-mediated and lags behind.","A":"Correct, and it is the most counter-intuitive fact in the lecture: the patient looks better as the fever settles, and that is exactly when the critical phase of plasma leakage begins. Watch for a rising haematocrit with a falling platelet count.","E":"Shock precedes, not follows, resolution of the rash.","C":"Return of appetite, stabilising vital signs and bradycardia mark RECOVERY, which is the phase after the danger has passed.","D":"Far too late. Practically: never discharge a dengue patient just because the fever has broken."},"t":"The critical phase","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L14_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The critical phase</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Yellow fever — target cell explains the bleeding","s":"A 30-year-old man returns from rural West Africa. After three days of fever, chills, back pain and vomiting he seemed to improve, but a day later the fever returned with jaundice, bleeding gums and dark vomit. According to the lecture, which cell does this virus primarily target, and how does that explain his bleeding?","lec":"L14","sl":12,"c":"<b>Yellow fever — target cell explains the bleeding</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Joint fibroblasts — inflammation spreads into nearby vessels","B":"Hepatocytes — liver damage reduces clotting-factor synthesis","C":"Nerve cells — brainstem infection disrupts vascular tone","D":"Monocytes — antibody-coated virus is taken up via Fc receptors","E":"Keratinocytes — skin infection produces a petechial rash"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Joints, muscle and skin (fibroblasts) are chikungunya's targets — they give arthralgia; chikungunya scores '−' for haemorrhage on slide 62.","B":"Correct. Biphasic illness → toxic phase with jaundice, haemorrhage and 'black vomit' (slide 17) = yellow fever, whose target is the HEPATOCYTE (slide 12). The pathogenesis figure: hepatocyte apoptosis → jaundice AND reduced clotting factors, then DIC and platelet dysfunction → haemorrhage.","C":"Nerve cells are the target of Japanese encephalitis and West Nile, which cause encephalitis, not haemorrhage.","D":"Fc-receptor uptake into monocytes is dengue's ADE (slide 25). Dengue bleeds from vascular leak and thrombocytopenia, not from liver failure.","E":"Keratinocytes (with dendritic cells) are Zika's entry cells at the bite site (slide 36); they explain Zika's rash, not bleeding."},"img":"slides_i/L14_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Match the arbovirus to the target the lecture assigns it.","o":{"E":"All arboviruses target vascular endothelium only","A":"Yellow fever → neurons; Dengue → hepatocyte; West Nile → endothelium","B":"Yellow fever → hepatocyte (jaundice); Dengue → endothelium and blood elements (haemorrhage); Japanese encephalitis and West Nile → neurons (encephalitis)","C":"Chikungunya → hepatocyte; Zika → endothelium","D":"Japanese encephalitis → hepatocyte; Yellow fever → neurons"},"r":{"E":"Only dengue is primarily endothelial.","A":"Yellow fever is named for the jaundice it causes; it is hepatotropic.","B":"Correct — the disease-syndrome slide maps each virus to its target tissue, and the clinical picture falls straight out of it: liver → jaundice, endothelium and platelets → bleeding and shock, neurons → encephalitis.","C":"Chikungunya targets joints and Zika is neurotropic for the fetal brain.","D":"Both reversed."},"t":"Which arbovirus attacks which organ","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L14_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Which arbovirus attacks which organ</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Reading NS1 / IgM / IgG — primary vs secondary","s":"A 16-year-old has had fever for two days. Rapid tests show dengue NS1 antigen positive, anti-dengue IgM negative and anti-dengue IgG strongly positive. How should this result be interpreted?","lec":"L14","sl":30,"c":"<b>Reading NS1 / IgM / IgG — primary vs secondary</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Early primary infection, with a lower risk of severe disease","B":"Early secondary infection, with a higher risk of severe disease","C":"Late primary infection, after the viraemia has cleared","D":"Late secondary infection, after the viraemia has cleared","E":"Past dengue infection, with the current fever from another cause"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Early PRIMARY infection has NS1 +++ but IgG '−' — no IgG exists yet in the first days of a first infection.","B":"Correct. Slide 30: early SECONDARY infection = IgG ++, IgM −/+, NS1 ++, PCR +++. NS1 proves current viraemic infection; IgG this early can only be left over from a previous infection. Slide 28: severity increases with secondary infection.","C":"Late primary: IgM ++, IgG −/+, NS1 '−'. A positive NS1 rules out the late phase.","D":"Late secondary: IgG ++, IgM +, NS1 '−'. NS1 has already disappeared by then.","E":"IgG alone would fit past infection, but a positive NS1 antigen means dengue virus is replicating now."},"img":"slides_i/L14_30.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient is on day 2 of a suspected dengue illness. Which test is most likely to be positive?","o":{"E":"IgG, which appears on day 1 of a primary infection","B":"NS1 antigen or RT-PCR — both are positive early, in the first few days of fever","C":"IgM, which is reliably positive from the first day of fever","D":"A four-fold rise in paired sera, which can be demonstrated on day 2","A":"Tourniquet test, which is a specific virological test"},"r":{"E":"In a PRIMARY infection IgG appears late; early IgG suggests a SECONDARY infection, which is itself a useful clue.","B":"Correct. The lecture's table is a timeline: NS1 antigen and PCR are the EARLY tests (first ~5 days, while the patient is viraemic), IgM appears around day 5 and IgG later. Day 2 is squarely in the antigen/PCR window.","C":"IgM is usually not detectable until around day 5.","D":"A four-fold rise needs paired acute AND convalescent sera, so it is retrospective by definition.","A":"The tourniquet test detects capillary fragility (>10 petechiae per square inch after 5 minutes). It supports the clinical diagnosis but is not virological."},"t":"Dengue laboratory windows","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L14_30.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Dengue laboratory windows</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Yellow fever — the sylvatic cycle","s":"In a South American forest, howler monkeys are found dead and forest-canopy mosquitoes test positive for a flavivirus. A timber worker then develops fever that remits and returns with jaundice. There are no cases in nearby towns. According to the lecture, how is the virus maintained in this setting?","lec":"L14","sl":1005,"c":"<b>Yellow fever — the sylvatic cycle</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Pigs and Culex mosquitoes, in an amplifying cycle","B":"Wild birds and Culex mosquitoes, in an enzootic cycle","C":"Humans and Aedes aegypti, in an urban cycle","D":"Monkeys and forest mosquitoes, in a sylvatic cycle","E":"Rodents and hard ticks, in a transovarial cycle"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Pigs amplifying virus for Culex is Japanese encephalitis (slide 46).","B":"A bird–Culex enzootic cycle is West Nile virus (slide 52).","C":"The urban human–Aedes aegypti cycle is yellow fever's OTHER cycle; the stem places the case in the forest with no town cases.","D":"Correct. Jaundice after a remission = yellow fever. The teaching document (p.5) gives yellow fever two cycles: the SYLVATIC (jungle) cycle between monkeys and forest mosquitoes, and the URBAN cycle between humans and urban mosquitoes. A forest case with no town cases is the sylvatic cycle — which is why yellow fever is the answer to the lecturer's own 'animal reservoir' example question (slides 5 and 63).","E":"Ticks carry Colorado tick fever (Reoviridae) and tick-borne encephalitis; none of the lecture's six viruses is tick-borne."},"img":"slides_i/L14_1005.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Japanese encephalitis virus is maintained in nature by which cycle?","o":{"D":"An animal–arthropod–animal cycle, with pigs and wading birds as amplifying hosts and Culex as the vector; humans are DEAD-END hosts","B":"A man–arthropod–man cycle in which humans are the reservoir","C":"Direct person-to-person respiratory transmission","E":"A cycle requiring a freshwater snail as intermediate host","A":"Transmission by the Aedes mosquito from human to human only"},"r":{"D":"Correct. JE runs an ANIMAL–arthropod–animal cycle: pigs are the amplifying host, wading birds the maintenance reservoir, Culex the vector. Humans are incidental DEAD-END hosts because our viraemia is too low to re-infect a mosquito — which is why living near pig farms is the risk factor.","B":"A man–arthropod–man cycle is DENGUE, where humans are the reservoir.","C":"No arbovirus spreads by the respiratory route.","E":"Snails are intermediate hosts for trematodes, not viruses.","A":"JE's vector is Culex, not Aedes."},"t":"Transmission cycles","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L14_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Transmission cycles</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Zika in adults — mechanism of the neurological complication","s":"A 34-year-old man had fever, a maculopapular rash and red eyes after a trip to an outbreak area; his platelet count was normal. Ten days later he develops weakness of both legs that spreads upward, and his reflexes are lost. According to the lecture, what mechanism underlies this complication?","lec":"L14","sl":41,"c":"<b>Zika in adults — mechanism of the neurological complication</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The virus destroys neural progenitor cells","B":"Antibody-coated virus is carried into monocytes","C":"The immune system attacks the peripheral nerves","D":"Immune complexes and complement activate the endothelium","E":"Activated microglia release cytokines that kill neurons"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Destruction of neural progenitor cells is the mechanism of Zika MICROCEPHALY in the fetus (teaching document p.11) — the right virus, the wrong patient.","B":"Fc-receptor uptake of antibody-coated virus into monocytes is dengue ADE (slide 25).","C":"Correct. Rash + conjunctivitis + normal platelets = Zika; ascending weakness with lost reflexes in an ADULT = Guillain–Barré syndrome, defined on slide 41 as a condition in which the immune system attacks nerves, leading to muscle weakness and even paralysis.","D":"Cytokine and complement activation of endothelium is dengue's immune-complex mechanism (slide 27), which causes plasma leakage.","E":"Microglial activation releasing IL-6, TNF-α and IL-1β that kill neurons is Japanese encephalitis (slide 49)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_41.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which two complications of Zika virus infection does the lecture emphasise?","o":{"A":"Aplastic crisis and hydrops fetalis","B":"Haemorrhagic shock and hepatic failure","D":"Chronic destructive arthritis and permanent joint deformity","C":"Congenital microcephaly after infection in pregnancy, and Guillain-Barré syndrome in adults","E":"Cholangiocarcinoma and portal hypertension"},"r":{"A":"Aplastic crisis and hydrops fetalis are parvovirus B19 (L12).","B":"Haemorrhagic shock and hepatic failure belong to dengue and yellow fever.","D":"Chronic destructive arthritis is chikungunya.","C":"Correct. These are the two that made Zika notorious: congenital microcephaly in babies infected in utero, and Guillain-Barré syndrome — an immune attack on peripheral nerves causing weakness and sometimes paralysis — in infected adults.","E":"Cholangiocarcinoma is the liver fluke Opisthorchis (L4)."},"t":"Zika complications","sl":41,"img":"slides_i/L14_41.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Zika complications</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Chikungunya — the structural odd one out","s":"An outbreak of fever with disabling symmetrical pain in the fingers, wrists and ankles spreads through a Thai village with many uncovered water jars; in some patients the joint pain lasts for months. Which statement correctly contrasts the causative virus with the other five viruses in this lecture?","lec":"L14","sl":57,"c":"<b>Chikungunya — the structural odd one out</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It exists as four serotypes; the other five each have a single one","B":"It is carried by Culex mosquitoes; the other five are carried by Aedes","C":"It is an alphavirus in Togaviridae; the other five are flaviviruses","D":"It has a negative-sense RNA genome; the other five are positive-sense","E":"It is a naked virus; the other five have a lipid envelope"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Four serotypes is dengue (slide 20), so the 'other five' half is wrong too.","B":"Chikungunya is Aedes-borne (slide 57); it is JE and West Nile that use Culex.","C":"Correct. Severe polyarthralgia persisting for months + Aedes breeding in water jars = chikungunya — Togaviridae, genus Alphavirus (slide 57). Yellow fever, dengue, Zika, JE and West Nile are all Flaviviridae (slide 7).","D":"Both families have a linear (+)ssRNA genome (slides 14 and 15).","E":"Both families are enveloped (slides 14 and 15)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_57.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which grouping of arbovirus families is correct?","o":{"C":"Flaviviridae: yellow fever, dengue, Zika, Japanese encephalitis, West Nile. Togaviridae: chikungunya","A":"Flaviviridae: chikungunya only. Togaviridae: dengue and Zika","D":"All arboviruses belong to Flaviviridae","E":"Flaviviridae: dengue and chikungunya. Togaviridae: West Nile","B":"All arboviruses are Bunyaviridae"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Five Flaviviridae and one Togaviridae is the whole taxonomy you need for this lecture. Both families are enveloped with linear (+)ssRNA genomes; chikungunya sits in the genus Alphavirus within Togaviridae.","A":"Chikungunya is the Togaviridae member, not a flavivirus.","D":"Chikungunya is the exception that makes the question worth asking.","E":"Both halves wrong — West Nile is a flavivirus.","B":"Bunyaviruses include Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever, but they are not the whole group."},"t":"Which family","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L14_7.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Which family</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q63","t":"Chikungunya — arthritis","s":"Which feature BEST differentiates chikungunya virus infection from dengue?","lec":"L14","sl":57,"c":"<b>Chikungunya — arthritis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q63 · BM31 SUM1 · BM28 Q17 — 'the major clinical manifestation of Chikungunya' / 'what best differentiates chikungunya from dengue?'</span>","o":{"B":"Thrombocytopenia","D":"Rash","E":"Retro-orbital pain","A":"Severe polyarthritis","C":"Haemorrhage"},"a":"A","r":{"B":"Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia are dengue's laboratory signature; chikungunya usually spares the platelets.","D":"All three — dengue, chikungunya and Zika — produce a rash, so rash separates nothing.","E":"Retro-orbital pain is classically DENGUE.","A":"Correct. The name itself means 'that which bends up' — the joint pain is severe, symmetrical, often in the small joints, and can persist for months after the fever has gone. Chikungunya is the ONLY one of the three in which arthritis dominates.","C":"Haemorrhage is dengue. Same mosquito (Aedes), same region, often the same outbreak — the joints are what tell them apart."},"img":"slides_i/L14_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q-chik","BM28 Q17"]},{"id":"I|L14|9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q58","t":"Zika in pregnancy","s":"A pregnant woman is infected with Zika virus. What is the most likely serious consequence?","lec":"L14","sl":42,"c":"<b>Zika in pregnancy</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q58 — a pregnant woman infected with Zika; what is the most likely outcome?</span>","o":{"A":"Congenital cataract and deafness","B":"Guillain-Barré syndrome in the infant","D":"Rash in the mother","C":"Haemorrhagic fever in the mother","E":"Microcephaly in the infant"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Cataract and deafness are congenital RUBELLA (L12).","B":"Guillain-Barré is the neurological complication in INFECTED ADULTS, not in the infant — reading which person the option refers to is the trick here.","D":"Rash occurs but is trivial; only about one in four infected people has any symptoms at all.","C":"Haemorrhagic fever is dengue.","E":"Correct. Zika crosses the placenta and is neurotropic for the developing fetal brain, producing microcephaly and the wider congenital Zika syndrome. This is the whole reason Zika became an emergency in 2015–16."},"img":"slides_i/L14_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q57","t":"Japanese encephalitis — prevention","s":"Which is the recommended prevention of Japanese encephalitis?","lec":"L14","sl":51,"c":"<b>Japanese encephalitis — prevention</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q57 · the lecturer's own quiz slide 64 — the recommended prevention of JE</span>","o":{"C":"Administration of antiviral drugs","A":"Vaccination","E":"Use of antibiotics","B":"Indoor residual spraying of insecticide","D":"Use of antimalarial drugs"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"No antiviral is available for JE.","A":"Correct. There is NO specific treatment for JE, and fewer than 1% of those infected develop clinical disease — but when encephalitis does develop it is severe and often fatal. So the answer is prevention, and an effective vaccine exists (it is in the Thai EPI schedule). Mosquito avoidance and repellent are the secondary measures the lecturer's own quiz slide names.","E":"Antibiotics have no role against a virus.","B":"Culex breeds outdoors in rice paddies, so indoor residual spraying — a malaria tactic aimed at indoor-resting Anopheles — is poorly matched.","D":"Antimalarials do nothing against a virus."},"img":"slides_i/L14_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L14|11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q56","t":"Recognising dengue","s":"Which patient is most likely to have dengue?","lec":"L14","sl":21,"c":"<b>Recognising dengue</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q56 · Q59 — two stems in the same paper asking which patient has dengue</span>","o":{"B":"A patient with malaise, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting alone","E":"A patient with headache and neck stiffness","A":"A patient with high blood pressure and fever","C":"A man with a dry cough who has diabetes","D":"A man with high fever, chills, severe muscle and joint ache, retro-orbital pain and a rash over the whole body"},"a":"D","r":{"B":"Malaise, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are WARNING SIGNS in an established dengue case, but on their own they describe any number of illnesses. The examiner offered this option in the same paper to see whether you would pick warning signs over the diagnostic picture.","E":"Headache with neck stiffness points to meningitis or encephalitis — think Japanese encephalitis or West Nile among the arboviruses.","A":"Hypertension with fever is non-specific and is not a dengue feature.","C":"A dry cough suggests a respiratory virus; dengue is not primarily respiratory.","D":"Correct. High fever + severe myalgia and arthralgia + retro-orbital pain + generalised rash is the textbook dengue fever picture — hence the old name 'breakbone fever'."},"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q59"]},{"id":"I|L14|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q5","t":"West Nile virus","s":"What is the most severe clinical manifestation of West Nile virus infection?","lec":"L14","sl":54,"c":"<b>West Nile virus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Formative virology 2023 Q5 (examiner-written) — the most severe clinical manifestation of West Nile virus infection</span>","o":{"C":"Encephalitis","E":"Jaundice","B":"Arthritis","A":"Rash","D":"Malaise"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct, and it follows from the family: West Nile belongs to the Japanese encephalitis antigenic serocomplex, so it is neurotropic. About 80% of infections are asymptomatic, ~20% give West Nile fever, and a small minority progress to neuroinvasive disease — encephalitis, meningitis or acute flaccid paralysis.","E":"Jaundice is YELLOW FEVER, whose target is the hepatocyte.","B":"Arthritis is chikungunya.","A":"Rash occurs in West Nile fever but is not the severe manifestation.","D":"Malaise is part of the mild febrile illness."},"img":"slides_i/L14_54.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q64","t":"Zika transmission routes","s":"Which route of Zika virus transmission is LEAST likely?","lec":"L14","sl":37,"c":"<b>Zika transmission routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q64 — which route of Zika transmission is LEAST likely?</span>","o":{"B":"Sexual contact","E":"Mosquito bite","C":"Breast milk","A":"Vertical (mother to fetus)","D":"Blood transfusion"},"a":"C","r":{"B":"Sexual transmission is well documented, and the virus persists in SEMEN for up to about 6 months — far longer than in blood, saliva or urine.","E":"The mosquito bite is the primary route.","C":"Correct as the LEAST likely. Zika has been detected in breast milk, but breastfeeding transmission is not established and WHO continues to recommend breastfeeding — so it is the weakest of the five routes.","A":"Vertical transmission is the route behind congenital Zika syndrome.","D":"Blood-transfusion transmission has been documented, which is why donors from outbreak areas are deferred."},"img":"slides_i/L14_37.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q19","t":"Pig farms and mosquitoes","s":"A community lives beside pig farms where mosquitoes are abundant. Which viral infection should be anticipated?","lec":"L14","sl":47,"c":"<b>Pig farms and mosquitoes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 SUM1 virology Q19 — a pig farm with many mosquitoes; which virus should be expected?</span>","o":{"E":"Dengue virus","B":"Japanese encephalitis virus","A":"Chikungunya virus","C":"Zika virus","D":"Yellow fever virus"},"a":"B","r":{"E":"Dengue is urban and human-to-human via Aedes; pigs play no part.","B":"Correct. Pigs are the AMPLIFYING host for Japanese encephalitis: the virus multiplies to high titre in them without making them ill, so mosquitoes feeding nearby pick it up and can pass it to humans. Pig farm + mosquitoes + rice paddies (Culex breeding) is the JE setting.","A":"Chikungunya likewise cycles through humans and Aedes.","C":"Zika is Aedes-borne with a human cycle.","D":"Yellow fever's sylvatic cycle involves monkeys, not pigs, and it does not occur in Asia."},"img":"slides_i/L14_47.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"L14 quiz","t":"Animal reservoir","s":"Which of the following viruses causing human disease has an ANIMAL RESERVOIR?","lec":"L14","sl":8,"c":"<b>Animal reservoir</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's OWN quiz, slides 5 and 63 (asked twice in the deck) — which virus causing human disease has an animal reservoir?</span>","o":{"B":"Japanese encephalitis virus","A":"Cytomegalovirus","D":"Hepatitis C virus","E":"Human papillomavirus","C":"Varicella-zoster virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct, and the lecturer asked this twice in her own deck — at the start and again at the end — which is about as strong a signal as a lecture gives. JE is maintained in pigs and wading birds; humans are dead-end hosts.","A":"CMV is a human herpesvirus with no animal reservoir.","D":"Hepatitis C is a strictly human infection.","E":"HPV is human-specific — the name says so.","C":"VZV is human-only. Of these five, only the arbovirus is a zoonosis."},"img":"slides_i/L14_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L14|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q62","t":"Dengue laboratory picture","s":"Which laboratory pattern fits dengue fever?","lec":"L14","sl":21,"c":"<b>Dengue laboratory picture</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q62 — which laboratory values rise or fall in dengue</span>","o":{"C":"Haematocrit down with a raised platelet count","E":"Platelets up, white cells up, haematocrit down","D":"Eosinophils markedly raised with a normal platelet count","A":"Platelets DOWN, white cells DOWN, haematocrit UP, transaminases UP","B":"Isolated lymphocytosis with normal platelets and haematocrit"},"a":"A","r":{"C":"A falling haematocrit suggests frank bleeding or fluid overload rather than early leakage — and a rising haematocrit with falling platelets is the pairing that should prompt admission.","E":"Every direction is inverted.","D":"Eosinophilia points to parasites.","A":"Correct, and the four move together for one reason: plasma leaks out of the vessels. Platelets fall, white cells fall, the haematocrit RISES because the red cells are left behind in a shrinking plasma volume, and the transaminases rise from liver involvement.","B":"Dengue does not give an isolated lymphocytosis; that is closer to infectious mononucleosis."},"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"Supporting"},{"id":"I|L14|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"LS54","n":"Q6 (57)","t":"Aedes as vector","s":"Which statement about the mosquito vector of dengue is correct?","lec":"L14","sl":20,"c":"<b>Aedes as vector</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>หลักสูตร 54 Ax bank cohort 57 Q6 · cohort 59 Q5 — the Aedes questions, cross-referenced from the L8 entomology bank</span>","o":{"A":"Anopheles transmits dengue as well as malaria","B":"Aedes breeds in dirty drain water and bites only at night","C":"Culex is the dengue vector and breeds in clean household water","D":"Aedes aegypti bites by day, breeds in CLEAN stored water around the house, and also transmits chikungunya and Zika","E":"Mansonia is the dengue vector and breeds under aquatic plants"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Anopheles carries malaria and breeds in slow, clean stream water.","B":"Dirty water is Culex; Aedes is a day-biter.","C":"Culex breeds in dirty water and carries Japanese encephalitis and filariasis.","D":"Correct, and the breeding site is the control message: Aedes lays in CLEAN standing water in and around houses — water jars, vases, plant saucers, old tyres — so vector control is a household task. It bites by day and carries dengue, chikungunya, Zika and Wuchereria bancrofti.","E":"Mansonia carries Brugia malayi and attaches its eggs under aquatic plants."},"img":"slides_i/L14_20.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["LS54 Q5 (59)"]},{"id":"I|L14|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Factors influencing dengue severity","s":"All four dengue serotypes circulate in a Thai province. Using the lecture's factors that influence disease severity, which patient is at greatest risk of severe dengue?","lec":"L14","sl":28,"c":"<b>Factors influencing dengue severity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A well-nourished 9-year-old reinfected by the same serotype as before","B":"A 9-year-old with diabetes and a first-ever infection by DEN-4","C":"A malnourished 9-year-old with a first-ever infection by DEN-2","D":"A well-nourished 9-year-old with past DEN-1 infection, now infected with DEN-2","E":"A healthy 30-year-old with a first-ever infection by DEN-1"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Reinfection with the SAME serotype is protected: the teaching document (p.8) says infection gives lifelong immunity to that serotype; ADE requires a DIFFERENT (heterologous) serotype.","B":"Diabetes is not a listed factor (it is a distractor in the lecturer's own question), and this is a primary infection.","C":"Slide 28: severe dengue is LESS common in malnourished children — the lecturer's planted trap. It is also a primary infection.","D":"Correct — every factor on slide 28 points the same way: SECONDARY infection with a different serotype (ADE), DEN-2 (the highest-risk serotype), a child under 15, and good nutrition. The lecturer's own example question (slide 64) answers 'pre-existing antibody'.","E":"Adult, primary infection, and DEN-1 — the LOWEST-risk serotype (DEN-2 > DEN-3 > DEN-4 > DEN-1)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which host or viral factor does the lecture link to INCREASED severity of dengue?","o":{"D":"A secondary infection with a different serotype in someone who already has non-neutralising antibody from a previous dengue infection","C":"Malnutrition, which is why severe dengue clusters in undernourished children","A":"Infection with DEN-1, the serotype carrying the greatest risk","E":"Adult age between 20 and 40, the peak risk group","B":"Co-infection with HIV, the strongest single risk factor"},"r":{"D":"Correct — pre-existing cross-reactive antibody is the answer to the deck's own NL example question, and it is the basis of antibody-dependent enhancement.","C":"The counter-intuitive point on the slide: severe dengue is LESS common in malnourished children, presumably because the immunopathology needs a competent immune response.","A":"The ranking is DEN-2 greatest, then DEN-3, DEN-4, and DEN-1 LAST.","E":"Risk is increased in children under 15 and in the elderly, and is greatest in INFANTS.","B":"HIV is not on the list; the listed factors are viral strain and serotype, prior exposure, nutrition, age and host genetics."},"t":"What makes dengue severe","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L14_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>What makes dengue severe</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"DHF vs DSS — the defining criterion","s":"A 10-year-old with dengue on day 5 has a positive tourniquet test, a platelet count of 70,000/mm³, a haematocrit 22% above her baseline, cool extremities and a blood pressure of 100/84 mmHg. How is she classified, and on which criterion?","lec":"L14","sl":21,"c":"<b>DHF vs DSS — the defining criterion</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Dengue haemorrhagic fever, because the platelet count is above 50,000/mm³","B":"Dengue shock syndrome, because the pulse pressure is below 20 mmHg","C":"Dengue shock syndrome, because the haematocrit has risen by more than 20%","D":"Dengue haemorrhagic fever, because the systolic pressure is still 100 mmHg","E":"Dengue fever, because there is no overt mucosal or gut bleeding"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The DHF platelet threshold on slide 21 is ≤ 100,000/mm³, not 50,000 — and she has circulatory failure, which escalates DHF to DSS.","B":"Correct. 100 − 84 = 16 mmHg. Slide 21 defines DSS by circulatory failure with pulse pressure < 20 mmHg — a narrow pulse pressure, even with a 'normal-looking' systolic.","C":"Right label, wrong reason. The rising haematocrit (haemoconcentration) is a DHF criterion reflecting plasma leakage; what makes it DSS is circulatory failure with pulse pressure < 20 mmHg.","D":"A normal systolic does not exclude shock; the slide's criterion is pulse pressure, not systolic pressure.","E":"A positive tourniquet test is already a haemorrhagic manifestation, and platelets ≤ 100,000 with rising Hct are DHF criteria — she is well beyond simple DF."},"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A child with dengue has a platelet count of 80,000/mm³, a haematocrit that has risen 22% above baseline, and a blood pressure of 90/75 mmHg. How should this be classified, and on what criterion?","o":{"B":"Dengue SHOCK syndrome — the pulse pressure is 15 mmHg, below the 20 mmHg threshold that defines circulatory failure","C":"Dengue haemorrhagic fever — the platelet count below 100,000 is what defines shock","D":"Dengue fever — neither thrombocytopenia nor haemoconcentration is part of the classification","E":"Dengue shock syndrome — defined by the systolic pressure falling below 100 mmHg","A":"Dengue haemorrhagic fever — DSS requires overt bleeding, which is absent here"},"r":{"B":"Correct. 90 − 75 = 15 mmHg. A NARROW pulse pressure, not a low systolic, is what marks dengue shock — the blood pressure can look almost normal.","C":"Thrombocytopenia and haemoconcentration define DHF; it is the circulatory failure that escalates it to DSS.","D":"Platelets ≤100,000 and a rising haematocrit are both explicit DHF criteria.","E":"The systolic here is 90 and the criterion on the slide is pulse pressure below 20 mmHg.","A":"DSS is defined by circulatory failure, not by overt bleeding — which is exactly why a child can be in shock without visible haemorrhage."},"t":"DF / DHF / DSS criteria","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>DF / DHF / DSS criteria</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Original antigenic sin","s":"In a child with a second dengue infection, investigators find that the expanded T cells are memory clones raised against the first serotype. They bind the new serotype poorly and fail to clear it, yet release large amounts of cytokines. Which pathogenic mechanism from the lecture does this describe?","lec":"L14","sl":26,"c":"<b>Original antigenic sin</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Superantigen activation","B":"Antibody-dependent enhancement","C":"Original antigenic sin","D":"Immune complex mechanism","E":"Antigenic shift"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Superantigens cross-link MHC II and TCR non-specifically (staphylococcal/streptococcal toxins); they are not part of dengue pathogenesis in this lecture.","B":"ADE is the ANTIBODY arm: non-neutralising cross-reactive antibody carries virus into monocytes via Fc receptors (slide 25). The stem describes T cells, not antibody.","C":"Correct. Slide 26: serotype cross-reactive MEMORY T CELLS contribute to the pathogenesis of DHF/DSS, leading to an inappropriate T-cell response — the T-cell arm of dengue immunopathology.","D":"The immune complex mechanism is endothelial activation by cytokines and complement → increased vascular permeability (slide 27); it is the third listed mechanism, separate from the T-cell one.","E":"Antigenic shift is reassortment of segmented genomes (influenza). Flaviviruses have a linear, non-segmented genome."},"img":"slides_i/L14_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"The lecture lists three pathogenic mechanisms in dengue. Which statement correctly separates two of them?","o":{"C":"In ADE, cross-reactive NON-NEUTRALISING ANTIBODY forms complexes with a heterologous serotype and carries it into monocytes through the Fc receptor, increasing virus production; in original antigenic sin, serotype cross-reactive MEMORY T CELLS mount an inappropriate response","E":"In ADE, memory T cells respond inappropriately; in original antigenic sin, antibody carries virus into monocytes","A":"Both describe the same antibody-mediated process under two names","D":"ADE requires neutralising antibody against the same serotype","B":"Original antigenic sin is the formation of immune complexes that activate endothelium"},"r":{"C":"Correct. One is a B-cell/antibody phenomenon, the other a T-cell phenomenon — and the third listed mechanism, immune complexes activating endothelium, is separate again.","E":"The two are swapped.","A":"They are listed as distinct mechanisms with different effector arms.","D":"ADE depends on NON-neutralising, cross-reactive antibody against a DIFFERENT serotype; neutralising same-serotype antibody protects.","B":"That describes the immune-complex mechanism on p.27."},"t":"ADE vs original antigenic sin","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L14_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>ADE vs original antigenic sin</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"JE — why humans are dead-end hosts","s":"A 7-year-old boy from a rural family that raises pigs beside rice paddies develops fever and vomiting, then seizures and altered consciousness. A relative suggests isolating him in hospital to stop the virus spreading to his siblings. Why does the lecture predict isolation will not affect transmission?","lec":"L14","sl":46,"c":"<b>JE — why humans are dead-end hosts</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The virus reaches people mainly through undercooked pork","B":"Aedes mosquitoes in the house keep carrying it between people","C":"His siblings already carry protective maternal antibody to it","D":"The virus spreads mainly by droplets before symptoms appear","E":"His viraemia is too low for a feeding mosquito to acquire the virus"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Pigs amplify the virus for MOSQUITOES; people are not infected by eating pork.","B":"JE's vector is Culex (particularly C. tritaeniorhynchus), not Aedes, and the man–mosquito–man cycle belongs to dengue and urban yellow fever.","C":"Maternal antibody has no bearing on the question — the siblings remain susceptible to bites from mosquitoes infected on pigs.","D":"No arbovirus in the lecture spreads by droplets; JE passes to people only through the bite of an infected Culex mosquito.","E":"Correct. Pigs + rice paddies + encephalitis with seizures in a child = Japanese encephalitis. Slide 46: the virus is maintained among mosquitoes, birds and pigs, and humans are incidental or DEAD-END hosts; the teaching document (p.14–16) explains that human viraemia is too low for a biting mosquito to pick up the virus, so JE does not pass from person to person."},"img":"slides_i/L14_46.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about arbovirus transmission cycles matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"Japanese encephalitis virus is maintained in a mosquito–bird–pig cycle with humans as incidental DEAD-END hosts, so human cases do not sustain transmission; dengue and yellow fever by contrast can run a man–arthropod–man cycle","A":"Humans are the reservoir for Japanese encephalitis, which is why case isolation controls outbreaks","B":"West Nile virus is maintained in an enzootic cycle between mosquitoes and PIGS","D":"All arboviruses use an animal–arthropod–man cycle; none uses man–arthropod–man","C":"Being a dead-end host means the person cannot develop disease"},"r":{"E":"Correct, and the practical consequence is on the deck’s own NL question: JE is prevented by mosquito repellent, not by avoiding birds or pets.","A":"Humans are incidental dead-end hosts for JEV; the reservoir is the bird–pig–mosquito cycle.","B":"West Nile virus cycles between mosquitoes and BIRDS; pigs are JEV’s amplifying host.","D":"The deck gives both cycles, with dengue and yellow fever as man–arthropod–man examples.","C":"A dead-end host can be severely ill — it simply does not develop enough viraemia to reinfect a mosquito."},"t":"Dead-end host","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L14_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Dead-end host</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Zika — persistence in body fluids","s":"A 30-year-old man developed fever, rash, joint pain and red eyes during an Aedes-borne outbreak; his platelet count was normal and the illness settled within a week. His wife hopes to conceive. Six weeks after his recovery, in which body fluid does the lecture say the virus may still be detected?","lec":"L14","sl":38,"c":"<b>Zika — persistence in body fluids</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Eye secretions","B":"Blood","C":"Saliva","D":"Semen","E":"Urine"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Eye secretions clear in 5–7 days.","B":"Blood clears in 5–7 days.","C":"Saliva clears in 5–7 days.","D":"Correct. Rash + conjunctivitis + normal platelets = Zika. Slide 38: semen stays positive for up to 2 MONTHS — the reason sexual transmission (slide 37) remains a concern long after recovery, and why the teaching document advises condoms or abstinence with a diagnosed partner.","E":"Urine outlasts blood (up to 14 days) — which is why urine is an RT-PCR specimen — but not six weeks."},"img":"slides_i/L14_38.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A man recovered from Zika six weeks ago asks how long he should use condoms. Which statement matches the lecture's detection data?","o":{"D":"Zika is detectable in SEMEN for up to 2 months — far longer than in blood, saliva or eye secretions (5–7 days) or urine (up to 14 days) — which is why sexual transmission remains a concern well after recovery","B":"Zika clears from all body fluids within 7 days, so no precautions are needed after a week","C":"Urine is the longest-persisting compartment, at up to 2 months","E":"Zika is not sexually transmitted; it is exclusively mosquito-borne","A":"Semen and blood clear together at 5–7 days"},"r":{"D":"Correct, and the semen figure is the outlier that drives the sexual-transmission advice on the transmission slide.","B":"Only blood, saliva and eye secretions clear that fast.","C":"Urine is up to 14 days; semen is the 2-month compartment.","E":"The transmission slide lists sexual contact explicitly, alongside mosquito, blood, and pregnancy/childbirth.","A":"Semen persists roughly eight times longer than blood."},"t":"Zika — where the virus persists","sl":37,"img":"slides_i/L14_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Zika — where the virus persists</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Yellow fever — prevention","s":"A 25-year-old volunteer will work in rural West Africa, where a mosquito-borne disease is endemic that causes fever, then after a short remission a return of fever with jaundice, bleeding and 'black vomit'. Which prevention does the lecture give for this disease?","lec":"L14","sl":18,"c":"<b>Yellow fever — prevention</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A live attenuated vaccine based on the 17D strain","B":"A DNA-based vaccine still in development","C":"A two-dose tetravalent vaccine at 0 and 3 months","D":"No licensed vaccine, so prevention relies on repellent","E":"A recombinant vaccine expressing the E glycoprotein"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Biphasic illness with jaundice and black vomit = yellow fever. Slide 18: YFV 17D live attenuated vaccine strain, in use since 1937; the teaching document (p.6) adds that it can be given from 9 months of age and protects for at least 10 years.","B":"A DNA-based vaccine is one of the Zika candidates under development (slide 44).","C":"Two doses of 0.5 mL at 0 and 3 months is Takeda's tetravalent dengue vaccine, Qdenga (slide 35).","D":"'No licensed vaccine' applies to Zika, West Nile and chikungunya — not yellow fever.","E":"A recombinant virus expressing the E glycoprotein is another Zika development approach (slide 44)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_18.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about yellow fever matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"The illness is biphasic — fever, then a toxic phase with jaundice, haemorrhage and 'black vomit'; about 1 in 7 of those with initial symptoms progress to severe disease, and 30–60% of those die; prevention is the live attenuated 17D vaccine, in use since 1937","C":"The illness is uniphasic and 30–60% of ALL infected people die","D":"There is a specific antiviral treatment, which is why mortality has fallen","E":"The 17D vaccine is an inactivated vaccine introduced in 1987","B":"Yellow fever is transmitted by Culex mosquitoes and has no animal reservoir"},"r":{"A":"Correct on the biphasic course, both fractions, and the vaccine. Note the 30–60% applies to those with SEVERE disease, not to everyone infected.","C":"Most people have no symptoms at all; the high case fatality applies only to the severe subset.","D":"There is no specific treatment — stated explicitly.","E":"17D is a LIVE ATTENUATED vaccine and has been in use since 1937.","B":"Yellow fever is transmitted by AEDES and does have an animal reservoir — it is the answer to the deck’s own example question about animal reservoirs."},"t":"Yellow fever course and vaccine","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L14_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Yellow fever course and vaccine</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"JE vs West Nile — the clean discriminator","s":"Patient 1 is a Thai child from a pig-farming village with fever, seizures and coma. Patient 2 is a 70-year-old in the United States with encephalitis and acute flaccid paralysis during a summer when many dead crows were reported. Which statement correctly distinguishes the two causative viruses?","lec":"L14","sl":51,"c":"<b>JE vs West Nile — the clean discriminator</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Virus 1 spreads person to person, whereas virus 2 does not","B":"IgM in CSF confirms virus 1, whereas it cannot confirm virus 2","C":"Virus 1 is a togavirus, whereas virus 2 is a flavivirus","D":"Virus 1 is carried by Aedes, whereas virus 2 is carried by Culex","E":"Virus 1 has a licensed human vaccine, whereas virus 2 has none"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Neither spreads person to person by casual contact; humans are dead-end hosts for both (West Nile can pass by transfusion or transplant, slide 52).","B":"Virus-specific IgM in serum or CSF is listed for BOTH (slides 51 and 56).","C":"Both are Flaviviridae; West Nile is even in the Japanese encephalitis antigenic serocomplex (slide 52).","D":"Both are Culex-borne (slides 46 and 52).","E":"Correct. Pigs → Japanese encephalitis; birds + flaccid paralysis → West Nile. Slide 51: 'An effective vaccine is available' for JE; slide 56: West Nile has NO licensed vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L14_51.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of encephalitic flavivirus with its epidemiological figures matches the lecture?","o":{"E":"Japanese encephalitis — about 1 in 250 infections produces severe illness, case fatality reaches 30%, and 30–50% of encephalitis survivors have permanent neurological or psychiatric sequelae; West Nile — about 80% asymptomatic, ~20% West Nile fever, and neuroinvasive disease in under 1% (about 1 in 150)","D":"Japanese encephalitis — 80% asymptomatic with neuroinvasive disease in 1 in 150; West Nile — 1 in 250 severe with 30% mortality","B":"Both viruses cause clinical illness in the majority of those infected","A":"Neither virus causes long-term sequelae; recovery is always complete","C":"West Nile virus has a licensed human vaccine and Japanese encephalitis does not"},"r":{"E":"Correct on all five figures. The sequelae rate is the one people omit, and it is why JE vaccination matters even though most infections are silent.","D":"The two sets of figures are swapped.","B":"Both are overwhelmingly subclinical — that is the point of the fractions.","A":"JE leaves permanent neurological or psychiatric sequelae in 30–50% of encephalitis cases, and WNV can leave long-term neurological sequelae.","C":"Inverted — an effective JE vaccine is available; WNV has NO licensed vaccine."},"t":"JEV vs WNV numbers","sl":47,"img":"slides_i/L14_47.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>JEV vs WNV numbers</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Tourniquet test — performing and reading it","s":"A child with suspected dengue has a blood-pressure cuff inflated on the upper arm and held for 5 minutes. After release, once the circulation has returned to normal, 14 petechiae are counted within one square inch of forearm skin. How should the result be interpreted?","lec":"L14","sl":29,"c":"<b>Tourniquet test — performing and reading it</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Positive, indicating increased capillary fragility","B":"Positive, confirming dengue virus infection","C":"Negative, because petechiae are counted per cm²","D":"Invalid, because it is read with the cuff inflated","E":"Negative, because the cut-off is 20 petechiae"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 29: read after a full 5 minutes with the circulation returned to normal; more than 10 spots in 1 square inch = positive. It is a bedside sign of capillary fragility — the haemorrhagic tendency of DHF.","B":"A positive test supports the clinical picture but is not virological; confirmation needs NS1 antigen, RT-PCR or serology (slides 30–31).","C":"The unit on the slide is petechiae per square inch.","D":"The reading is taken after release, once the circulation has returned to normal — which is what was done here.","E":"The threshold is more than 10 per square inch, so 14 is positive."},"img":"slides_i/L14_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"How is the tourniquet test performed and read, according to the lecture?","o":{"A":"Read after a full 5 minutes once the circulation has returned to normal; more than 10 petechiae in one square inch counts as positive","D":"Read immediately on release; more than 3 petechiae per square inch is positive","C":"Read after 30 minutes; any petechiae at all are positive","E":"Read after 5 minutes; more than 100 petechiae per square centimetre is positive","B":"The test is obsolete and is not described in the lecture"},"r":{"A":"Correct on both the timing and the threshold — the deck gives a dedicated slide with the Thai instructions.","D":"The reading is taken after 5 minutes with circulation restored, and the cut-off is more than 10 per square inch.","C":"Five minutes, not thirty, and a few petechiae are not positive.","E":"The unit is petechiae per square INCH, and the number is 10.","B":"It has its own slide."},"t":"Tourniquet test","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L14_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Tourniquet test</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Chikungunya — course and outcome","s":"In an outbreak in a southern Thai village, most people infected developed abrupt fever with severe, symmetrical pain in the wrists, fingers and ankles, so that many walked bent over. The mosquitoes were breeding in household water containers. What course does the lecture describe for this infection?","lec":"L14","sl":58,"c":"<b>Chikungunya — course and outcome</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"As the fever falls on day 3–6, plasma leakage may then cause shock","B":"Death is common, and it occurs mainly in infants under one year","C":"Most infections are silent, and fewer than 1% reach severe disease","D":"Acute illness settles in 7–10 days, but joint pain may persist for months","E":"After brief improvement, fever returns with jaundice and renal failure"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Defervescence followed by plasma leakage and shock is the dengue critical phase (slide 22).","B":"Slide 58: mortality is RARE and occurs mostly in OLDER ADULTS.","C":"Chikungunya is the opposite — 72–97% of infected people develop symptoms (slide 57). 'Mostly silent, <1% severe' fits JE and West Nile.","D":"Correct. Mostly symptomatic + severe polyarthralgia + 'walking bent over' (the meaning of the name, teaching document p.18) + Aedes = chikungunya. Slide 58: acute symptoms resolve in 7–10 days, but some patients relapse and joint pain can persist for months or years.","E":"Remission followed by a toxic phase with jaundice and albuminuria/anuria is yellow fever (slide 17; teaching document p.6)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_58.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about chikungunya matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"It belongs to Togaviridae, genus Alphavirus, and is unusual among arboviruses in that 72–97% of infected people develop symptoms; acute illness resolves in 7–10 days but joint pain may persist for months or years, and mortality is rare and mostly in older adults","A":"It belongs to Flaviviridae, and only about 1 in 4 infected people develop symptoms","E":"Joint symptoms always resolve completely within 10 days","B":"Mortality is high and concentrated in infants","C":"It is transmitted by Culex mosquitoes and is maintained in a bird cycle"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The symptomatic fraction is the striking figure — where Zika shows symptoms in 1 in 4 and JEV in under 1%, chikungunya makes almost everyone ill.","A":"It is a TOGAVIRUS; 1 in 4 symptomatic is ZIKA’s figure.","E":"Persistent arthralgia for months or years is explicitly described.","B":"Mortality is rare and occurs mostly in OLDER ADULTS.","C":"It is transmitted by Aedes; the bird cycle belongs to West Nile virus."},"t":"Chikungunya — the odd statistics","sl":57,"img":"slides_i/L14_57.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Chikungunya — the odd statistics</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"The recovery phase — the opposite danger","s":"On day 8 of dengue, a 14-year-old's appetite has returned, his pulse is 58/min, urine output has increased, and a rash with small pale islands in a sea of red has appeared on his legs. He is still on the intravenous infusion started two days earlier. What is the main risk now?","lec":"L14","sl":23,"c":"<b>The recovery phase — the opposite danger</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fluid overload as leaked plasma is reabsorbed","B":"Relapse into a toxic phase with jaundice","C":"Shock from continuing plasma leakage","D":"Haemorrhage from a still-falling platelet count","E":"Dehydration from a persistent high fever"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Returning appetite, bradycardia, increased urine output and the convalescent rash ('isles of white in a sea of red') mark the RECOVERY phase (slide 23). On the course-of-illness graph (slide 22) the recovery-phase issue is reabsorption of leaked fluid → fluid overload, so continuing the critical-phase infusion is now the hazard.","B":"A toxic phase with jaundice after remission is yellow fever, not dengue.","C":"Continuing leakage and shock belong to the CRITICAL phase (around days 3–6); here the leak is reversing.","D":"In recovery the platelet count is rising and the haematocrit is returning to normal (slides 22–23).","E":"Dehydration from high fever is the FEBRILE-phase risk."},"img":"slides_i/L14_23.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which laboratory and clinical features of dengue does the lecture describe?","o":{"A":"Leukopenia with falling neutrophils and a relative lymphocytosis, atypical lymphocytes at 10–20%, and in recovery a convalescent rash described as 'isles of white in a sea of red' accompanied by bradycardia","C":"Leukocytosis with neutrophilia and a rash appearing only during the febrile phase","B":"Atypical lymphocytes above 50%, which distinguishes dengue from infectious mononucleosis","D":"Tachycardia and a falling haematocrit mark the recovery phase","E":"The convalescent rash is a vesicular eruption on the palms and soles"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Bradycardia in recovery is a useful reassuring sign, and the ‘isles of white in a sea of red’ description is the deck’s own phrase.","C":"Dengue causes LEUKOPENIA, and the convalescent rash appears during recovery.","B":"The figure given is 10–20%.","D":"Recovery is marked by BRADYCARDIA and a haematocrit RETURNING TO NORMAL from a raised level.","E":"It is a confluent erythematous rash with spared islands, not vesicular."},"t":"Dengue blood picture","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L14_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Dengue blood picture</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"The ChimeriVax platform","s":"The lecture's ChimeriVax platform keeps the backbone of a live attenuated flavivirus vaccine strain that has been in use since 1937, and swaps in one gene from West Nile virus, JE virus or each dengue serotype so that the neutralising response is aimed at the new virus. Which gene is swapped?","lec":"L14","sl":18,"c":"<b>The ChimeriVax platform</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Non-structural NS5","B":"Non-structural NS1","C":"Envelope (E)","D":"Capsid (C)","E":"Non-structural NS3"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"NS5 is the viral RNA polymerase; it stays with the 17D backbone.","B":"NS1 is the secreted non-structural antigen used for early dengue diagnosis (slide 30), not the swapped gene.","C":"Correct. The backbone is yellow fever 17D (since 1937). Slide 18: 'Replace E gene with E gene of choice' → West Nile, JEV and four-serotype DENV ChimeriVax candidates. The envelope protein is the surface target of neutralising antibody, so swapping it redirects immunity while keeping the proven attenuated backbone — the basis of Sanofi's tetravalent vaccine (slide 34).","D":"Capsid is an internal structural protein packaging the genome; antibody against it does not neutralise the virion.","E":"NS3 is a non-structural replication enzyme; it stays with the 17D backbone."},"img":"slides_i/L14_18.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about arbovirus classification matches the lecture?","o":{"C":"There are around 500 arboviruses, and most belong to four families — Flaviviridae, Togaviridae, Bunyaviridae (Rift Valley fever virus) and Reoviridae (Colorado tick fever virus); all are transmitted by blood-sucking arthropods, chiefly mosquitoes and ticks","E":"All arboviruses belong to Flaviviridae, which is why they share a (+) ssRNA genome","A":"Colorado tick fever virus is a togavirus and chikungunya a reovirus","D":"Arboviruses are defined by causing encephalitis, regardless of how they are transmitted","B":"Arboviruses are transmitted by any arthropod, including non-blood-feeding species such as houseflies"},"r":{"C":"Correct on the count, the four families, their example viruses and the defining mode of transmission.","E":"Chikungunya is a togavirus and the deck names four families.","A":"Swapped — Colorado tick fever is the reovirus and chikungunya the togavirus.","D":"Arbovirus is defined by TRANSMISSION, not by syndrome; encephalitis is only one of three syndromes.","B":"The definition specifies BLOOD-SUCKING arthropods — chiefly mosquitoes and ticks."},"t":"The four arbovirus families","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L14_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>The four arbovirus families</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Dengvaxia — who may receive it, and why","s":"Sanofi's ChimeriVax tetravalent dengue vaccine came under scrutiny after deaths in vaccinated children. Reasoning from the mechanism that makes a second dengue infection dangerous, to which group does the lecturer's teaching document say its use is restricted?","lec":"L14","sl":34,"c":"<b>Dengvaxia — who may receive it, and why</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"People who have never had dengue","B":"Travellers already given the 17D vaccine","C":"Infants younger than nine months","D":"People with a previous dengue infection","E":"Pregnant women living in outbreak areas"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The reverse — seronegative recipients are exactly the group at risk.","B":"Prior 17D yellow fever vaccination is not the criterion; the backbone is 17D, but eligibility depends on prior DENGUE infection.","C":"Infants already carry the greatest age-related risk of severe dengue (slide 28); they are not the group the restriction targets.","D":"Correct. Teaching document p.9–10: Dengvaxia's use is restricted to people with a history of prior dengue infection, because those never infected are at risk of more severe disease. In a seronegative person the vaccine acts like a 'first infection', priming them for ADE when a natural infection follows.","E":"Pregnancy is not the restriction the teaching document describes."},"img":"slides_i/L14_34.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement about dengue vaccines matches the lecture?","o":{"D":"Sanofi's Dengvaxia is a ChimeriVax tetravalent vaccine whose safety came into question after deaths were investigated in vaccinated children; Takeda's TDV (Qdenga) is given as 0.5 mL in a two-dose schedule at 0 and 3 months","E":"Qdenga is a single-dose vaccine and Dengvaxia requires three doses at 0, 1 and 6 months","C":"No dengue vaccine has ever reached human use","B":"Dengvaxia is an inactivated whole-virus vaccine unrelated to the yellow fever backbone","A":"Both vaccines are monovalent against DEN-2, the most dangerous serotype"},"r":{"D":"Correct on both products and on the Qdenga schedule.","E":"Qdenga is a two-dose schedule at 0 and 3 months.","C":"Both have been licensed and used.","B":"Dengvaxia is built on the ChimeriVax yellow fever 17D backbone with the E gene replaced.","A":"Both are TETRAVALENT, covering all four serotypes — which is the point, since monovalent protection would risk ADE."},"t":"The dengue vaccines","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L14_34.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>The dengue vaccines</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"JE — confirming current infection","s":"A 9-year-old Thai girl who received all her routine childhood vaccines develops fever, headache and vomiting, then seizures and confusion. Her family keeps pigs. Which laboratory result would best confirm a current infection with the suspected virus?","lec":"L14","sl":51,"c":"<b>JE — confirming current infection</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"JE-specific IgM in cerebrospinal fluid","B":"Raised hepatic aminotransferases in serum","C":"Atypical lymphocytes above 10% on a blood film","D":"Dengue NS1 antigen on a rapid strip","E":"JE-specific IgG in a single serum sample"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Pigs + seizures in a child = Japanese encephalitis. Slide 51 lists JE-specific IgM in serum or CSF. IgM indicates a CURRENT infection, and CSF shows the virus has reached the central nervous system.","B":"Raised aminotransferases are a feature of dengue fever (slide 21) and yellow fever hepatitis, not a confirmatory test for JE.","C":"Atypical lymphocytes of 10–20% are dengue's blood picture (slide 21).","D":"NS1 antigen is the early marker for DENGUE (slide 30), not JE.","E":"IgG in a single sample cannot separate current infection from past exposure or vaccination — and JE vaccine is part of the routine Thai childhood schedule (teaching document p.15)."},"img":"slides_i/L14_51.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which statement comparing the two main arbovirus families' particles matches the lecture?","o":{"A":"Both are enveloped with a linear (+) ssRNA genome; flaviviruses are spherical and about 50 nm across, togaviruses spherical and icosahedral at 65–70 nm","C":"Flaviviruses are naked and togaviruses enveloped","E":"Flaviviruses carry a (−) ssRNA genome and togaviruses a (+) ssRNA genome","B":"Togaviruses are the smaller of the two at about 50 nm","D":"Both have segmented genomes, which is why reassortment drives their epidemics"},"r":{"A":"Correct on envelope, genome and both sizes.","C":"Both families are enveloped.","E":"Both carry (+) ssRNA — which is why neither needs to package a polymerase.","B":"Flaviviruses are the smaller at ~50 nm; togaviruses are 65–70 nm.","D":"Both genomes are LINEAR and non-segmented, so reassortment does not occur — that is an influenza phenomenon."},"t":"Flavivirus vs togavirus particle","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L14_14.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Flavivirus vs togavirus particle</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L14|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Disease-mechanism table — syndrome profile","s":"Mosquito surveillance near rice paddies and pig pens collects Culex tritaeniorhynchus carrying a flavivirus. On the lecture's disease-mechanism table, which combination of syndromes does this virus produce?","lec":"L14","sl":13,"c":"<b>Disease-mechanism table — syndrome profile</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Flu-like illness with diarrhoea and dehydration","B":"Flu-like illness with hepatitis, haemorrhage and shock","C":"Flu-like illness, with arthralgia as its systemic feature","D":"Flu-like illness with encephalitis","E":"Flu-like illness, with microcephaly in the fetus"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Diarrhoea is not an arbovirus syndrome — it is the answer to the lecturer's own 'less common manifestation' example question (slide 5).","B":"Flu-like + hepatitis + haemorrhage + shock is the row shared by yellow fever and dengue.","C":"Flu-like illness with arthralgia (footnote a) is chikungunya.","D":"Correct. Culex tritaeniorhynchus + pigs = Japanese encephalitis virus (slide 46). On slide 13, JE (and West Nile) are marked for flu-like/systemic illness plus ENCEPHALITIS, matching its nerve-cell tropism (slide 12).","E":"Flu-like illness with fetal microcephaly (footnote b) is Zika."},"img":"slides_i/L14_13.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which clinical feature is LEAST characteristic of arbovirus infection, according to the lecture?","o":{"D":"Diarrhoea","A":"Malaise","B":"Rash","E":"Encephalitis","C":"Haemorrhagic fever"},"r":{"D":"Correct. The three recognised categories are febrile illness with rash, encephalitis and haemorrhagic fever — diarrhoea is the odd one out, and this is the deck’s own example question.","A":"Malaise is part of the febrile illness category.","B":"Rash accompanies the febrile illness category.","E":"Encephalitis is one of the three categories in its own right.","C":"Haemorrhagic fever is the third category."},"t":"The three clinical syndromes","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L14_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>The three clinical syndromes</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233359183},{"id":"I|L15|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"HIV enzymes and structure","s":"An experimental drug blocks the viral enzyme that inserts the DNA copy of the genome into host chromosomes, so treated cells accumulate unintegrated viral DNA. Which enzyme is blocked, and which description of the virus explains why it needs that enzyme?","lec":"L15","sl":16,"c":"<b>HIV enzymes and structure</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Integrase — a naked virus with circular dsDNA episomes","B":"Reverse transcriptase — a naked virus with dsDNA","C":"RNA polymerase — an enveloped virus with ss(−)RNA","D":"Integrase — an enveloped virus with diploid ss(+)RNA","E":"Protease — an enveloped retrovirus with diploid ss(+)RNA"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Circular dsDNA episomes describe HPV (slide 5), which is naked and has no integrase.","B":"RT makes the DNA copy but does not insert it, and HIV is enveloped with an RNA genome.","C":"HIV is a positive-sense RNA virus that replicates through a DNA intermediate.","D":"HIV: Retroviridae/Lentivirus, enveloped, linear diploid ss(+)RNA, with RT, integrase and protease (slide 16). RT makes the DNA copy; integrase inserts it.","E":"Protease cleaves the viral polyproteins during maturation — it has nothing to do with integration."},"img":"slides_i/L15_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which description of HIV is correct?","o":{"D":"An enveloped lentivirus carrying two copies of (+)ssRNA together with reverse transcriptase, integrase and protease","C":"A naked retrovirus with one copy of (−)ssRNA and its own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","B":"An enveloped DNA virus that replicates entirely in the cytoplasm","A":"An enveloped lentivirus whose genome can be translated directly, so it needs no reverse transcription","E":"A naked (+)ssRNA virus that integrates using the host's own integrase"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Diploid (+)RNA and three packaged enzymes are the retrovirus signature, and each enzyme is a drug target.","C":"HIV is ENVELOPED and (+)sense, and it carries RT, not an RdRp.","B":"It is an RNA virus, and integration happens in the nucleus.","A":"HIV's (+)RNA is not used as mRNA on entry. It is first reverse-transcribed to DNA and integrated.","E":"The integrase is viral and packaged in the virion; the host has none."},"t":"HIV structure","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L15_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HIV structure</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"p24 vs gp120/gp41","s":"A blood bank adds an antigen assay to its antibody screen so it can pick up early infection with any HIV strain, without needing to tell HIV-1 from HIV-2. Which viral protein should the antigen assay target?","lec":"L15","sl":17,"c":"<b>p24 vs gp120/gp41</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Reverse transcriptase, an envelope enzyme","B":"gp41, a type-specific transmembrane protein","C":"p24, a group-specific core antigen","D":"gp120, a type-specific envelope protein","E":"Integrase, a group-specific surface protein"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"RT is an internal enzyme, not an envelope protein, and not the screening antigen.","B":"gp41 is also a type-specific envelope glycoprotein.","C":"Slide 17: p24 is the group-specific antigen in the nucleocapsid core; antibodies to it do not neutralise but it is an important serological marker. 4th-generation screens detect p24 + antibody.","D":"gp120 is type-specific, so it is the wrong choice for a strain-agnostic screen.","E":"Integrase is an internal enzyme, not a surface protein."},"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A laboratory wants a marker that simply says 'HIV is present' without separating HIV-1 from HIV-2, and that appears early in plasma. Which antigen fits, and why?","o":{"C":"p24 — a group-specific capsid antigen shared across HIV, used as an early diagnostic marker","A":"gp120 — a type-specific envelope protein that separates HIV-1 from HIV-2","E":"gp41 — the fusion stalk, which appears before viral RNA","D":"Reverse transcriptase — measured as a routine plasma marker","B":"CD4 — the viral receptor, measured as antigen"},"r":{"C":"Correct. p24 is GROUP-specific (it says HIV but not which type) and is the antigen in 4th-generation Ag/Ab screening.","A":"gp120 is TYPE-specific, which is the opposite of what was asked.","E":"Viral RNA is detectable first (~day 10), and gp41 is not the early marker.","D":"RT is not a routine diagnostic marker.","B":"CD4 is a host molecule, and its count is a monitoring test, not a viral antigen."},"t":"gp120/gp41 vs p24","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>gp120/gp41 vs p24</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Why neutralising antibody fails","s":"A patient has high titres of neutralising antibody against the envelope protein that binds CD4, yet his viral load keeps rising over the year. Which property of the virus explains this?","lec":"L15","sl":17,"c":"<b>Why neutralising antibody fails</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its envelope protein is hidden inside the capsid core","B":"Its reverse transcriptase copies host antibody genes into the provirus","C":"Its reverse transcriptase lacks proofreading, so escape variants arise","D":"Its protease cleaves the antibody before it can bind","E":"Its integrase inserts the envelope gene into memory B cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"gp120 is on the envelope surface — that is why antibody can neutralise it at all.","B":"RT copies the viral genome only.","C":"Slide 17: the gp120 gene mutates rapidly because RT lacks an editing function → many antigenic variants ('quasispecies'); anti-gp120 is neutralising 'but does not help'.","D":"Viral protease cleaves viral polyproteins, not antibodies.","E":"HIV does not target B cells for integration, and this would not explain escape."},"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An HIV patient has high titres of neutralising anti-gp120 antibody, yet his viral load keeps rising. What best explains this?","o":{"C":"RT lacks proofreading, so the gp120 gene mutates rapidly into a swarm of escape variants (quasispecies)","B":"Anti-gp120 is non-neutralising, so it cannot bind the virus","E":"The antibody is IgM and cannot neutralise","D":"HIV has no envelope for the antibody to bind","A":"Neutralising antibody against p24 is required instead"},"r":{"C":"Correct. The slide's phrase is 'neutralising Ab but does not help'. New variants appear faster than the antibody response can follow.","B":"Anti-gp120 IS neutralising; it is ANTI-p24 that is not.","E":"Isotype is not the issue; escape by mutation is.","D":"HIV is enveloped, and gp120 sits on that envelope.","A":"Anti-p24 does not neutralise at all; it is only a marker."},"t":"Why anti-gp120 does not protect","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Why anti-gp120 does not protect</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Replication order and drug targets","s":"Three drugs are added to HIV-infected cell cultures: drug X blocks reverse transcription, drug Y blocks integration and drug Z blocks cleavage of viral polyproteins. In what order do the three blocked steps occur in the viral replication cycle?","lec":"L15","sl":18,"c":"<b>Replication order and drug targets</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"X, then Y, then Z","B":"Y, then Z, then X","C":"Y, then X, then Z","D":"Z, then X, then Y","E":"X, then Z, then Y"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Attachment/fusion → reverse transcription (RT) → integration (integrase) → transcription/translation → assembly → budding with protease-driven maturation (slide 18). Three enzymes = three drug targets.","B":"This reverses the whole cycle.","C":"Integration needs the DNA copy that reverse transcription makes first.","D":"Polyprotein cleavage is a late (maturation) step.","E":"Protease acts after the provirus has been transcribed and translated, i.e. after integration."},"img":"slides_i/L15_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which sequence of HIV replication steps is correct?","o":{"A":"gp120 binds CD4 and a co-receptor → fusion → reverse transcription → integration → transcription → budding → protease maturation","D":"Integration → reverse transcription → fusion → budding → maturation","B":"Fusion → integration → reverse transcription → maturation → budding","C":"gp41 binds CD4 → uncoating in the nucleus → translation of genomic RNA → budding","E":"Protease maturation → reverse transcription → integration → budding"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Each enzyme acts at its own step: RT in the cytoplasm, integrase in the nucleus, protease after budding.","D":"Viral DNA must exist (RT) before it can be integrated.","B":"Integration cannot precede reverse transcription.","C":"gp120, not gp41, binds CD4. gp41 mediates fusion.","E":"Protease acts last, on budded immature virions."},"t":"Replication order","sl":18,"img":"slides_i/L15_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Replication order</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Target cells → clinical picture","s":"A man with long-standing untreated HIV develops, over several months, forgetfulness, slowed movements and a change in personality. Which viral target cells explain this picture?","lec":"L15","sl":16,"c":"<b>Target cells → clinical picture</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Schwann cells lining the peripheral nerves","B":"CD8+ T cells in the cerebrospinal fluid","C":"B lymphocytes transformed within the meninges","D":"Motor neurons reached by retrograde axonal transport","E":"Macrophages and brain cells such as astrocytes"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Schwann cells are not on the HIV target list.","B":"CD8 cells are not HIV target cells.","C":"B-cell transformation is EBV's pattern.","D":"Retrograde axonal spread describes rabies/HSV, not HIV dementia.","E":"HIV-associated dementia (triad of cognitive, motor and behavioural dysfunction, slide 23). Targets include macrophages, brain endothelium and brain cells (astrocytes, oligodendroglia) — slide 16."},"img":"slides_i/L15_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A man with long-standing untreated HIV develops progressive forgetfulness, slowed movement and a change in personality over several months. Which property of HIV best explains it?","o":{"E":"HIV infects macrophages and brain cells (astrocytes, oligodendroglia), causing HIV-associated dementia","A":"HIV infects only CD4 T cells, so the dementia must be caused by another virus","D":"HIV integrates into neurons and makes them rupture within days","B":"Anti-p24 antibody attacks the brain","C":"HIV causes dementia only during the acute retroviral syndrome"},"r":{"E":"Correct. HAD (~7%) is the cognitive + motor + behavioural triad developing over weeks to months. Macrophages carry the virus into the CNS.","A":"The slide lists brain endothelium, astrocytes and oligodendroglia as targets.","D":"The time course is months, not days.","B":"Not a mechanism taught.","C":"HAD is a late complication of the immunodeficiency years."},"t":"Target cells → clinical picture","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L15_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Target cells → clinical picture</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Killing of uninfected CD4 cells","s":"In an untreated patient the CD4 count falls much further than the number of infected cells would predict. Which mechanism on the lecture's slide destroys CD4 cells that are NOT infected?","lec":"L15","sl":28,"c":"<b>Killing of uninfected CD4 cells</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Reverse transcriptase copies itself into their DNA","B":"Cytotoxic T cells kill them via viral peptides on MHC I","C":"Free gp120 binds their CD4, leading to complement lysis","D":"Anti-p24 antibody drives them into apoptosis","E":"Budding virions puncture their plasma membranes"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Uninfected cells contain no RT.","B":"CTL killing via MHC I needs viral peptides — so it targets infected cells.","C":"Slide 28: 'Complement-mediated lysis (killing uninfected T4 cells) — binding of free gp120 to CD4 antigen'. Syncytia also pull in bystanders.","D":"Anti-p24 is a non-neutralising marker antibody, not a killing mechanism in the lecture.","E":"Membrane puncture by budding kills the infected cell itself."},"img":"slides_i/L15_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does the CD4 count fall far more than the number of infected cells would predict?","o":{"C":"Uninfected CD4 cells are destroyed too: they fuse into syncytia with infected cells, and free gp120 bound to their CD4 marks them for complement lysis","A":"Nearly every CD4 cell in the body becomes productively infected during the first weeks, so all loss is direct viral killing","E":"HIV infects bone-marrow stem cells, so the marrow stops producing new lymphocytes of any kind","B":"Activated CD8 cells are converted into CD4 cells, so the balance of the two subsets shifts","D":"Anti-p24 antibody binds the surface of uninfected CD4 cells and lyses them directly"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Syncytium formation and complement lysis of gp120-coated cells take down BYSTANDERS. This was the point she dwelt on.","A":"Only a small fraction of CD4 cells is infected at any time.","E":"Marrow failure is not a mechanism listed on the slide.","B":"Not a biological process.","D":"Anti-p24 is a marker, not a lytic mechanism."},"t":"CD4 loss — bystanders","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L15_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>CD4 loss — bystanders</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Enhancing antibody","s":"Anti-gp120 antibody can help HIV enter macrophages by a second route that does not need CD4. Which receptor does this route use, and which other virus from this course relies on the same principle in its severe disease?","lec":"L15","sl":27,"c":"<b>Enhancing antibody</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Complement receptor — Epstein-Barr virus","B":"Fc receptor — measles virus","C":"CXCR4 — influenza virus","D":"Fc receptor — dengue virus","E":"CCR5 — dengue virus"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"EBV uses CD21 to enter B cells, which is not antibody enhancement.","B":"Measles severity is not explained by ADE in this course.","C":"CXCR4 is an HIV co-receptor; influenza uses sialic acid.","D":"Slide 27: virus opsonised by anti-gp120 binds macrophage Fc receptors = enhancing antibody — the lecturer compared it to dengue ADE on secondary infection (L14).","E":"CCR5 is a co-receptor for the normal CD4-dependent route, not the antibody route."},"img":"slides_i/L15_27.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"HIV can enter macrophages that it would otherwise reach less efficiently when the patient already has anti-gp120 antibody. The same principle appears in which other lecture's virus?","o":{"D":"Dengue — antibody-dependent enhancement through Fc receptors on monocytes and macrophages","C":"Influenza — antigenic shift","B":"HPV — E6/E7 inactivation of p53","A":"Measles — giant-cell pneumonia without rash","E":"Rabies — retrograde axonal transport"},"r":{"D":"Correct. She said outright 'this is like dengue'. Non-neutralising antibody plus an Fc receptor gives the virus a second way in.","C":"Shift is reassortment, a genetic event, not an entry route.","B":"E6/E7 is oncogenesis, not entry.","A":"That is a consequence of immunosuppression, not antibody-mediated entry.","E":"A spread route, unrelated to antibody."},"t":"Enhancing antibody","sl":27,"img":"slides_i/L15_27.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Enhancing antibody</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"The HIV reservoir","s":"A man whose viral load has been undetectable for 5 years on ART stops his tablets, and within weeks his plasma viral load rebounds. Where had the virus persisted?","lec":"L15","sl":26,"c":"<b>The HIV reservoir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Free virions circulating below the detection limit","B":"Integrated provirus in resting memory CD4 T cells","C":"Episomal DNA in sensory ganglion neurons","D":"Actively replicating virus in renal tubules","E":"Viral RNA stored inside circulating red cells"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Plasma virions have a short half-life; they cannot sustain 5 years without a cellular source.","B":"Integration makes the provirus permanent; long-lived resting memory CD4 cells express no viral protein, so neither ART nor immunity clears them — the reason HIV cannot be cured (slides 23–26).","C":"Episomal latency in ganglia is HSV/VZV.","D":"Active replication would have been detected and blocked by ART.","E":"Red cells have no nucleus and are not HIV targets."},"img":"slides_i/L15_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient on fully suppressive ART for 5 years stops treatment, and within weeks the viral load rebounds. Where did the virus come from?","o":{"B":"Integrated provirus in long-lived resting memory CD4 T cells, which make no viral protein while latent and so escape drugs and immunity","A":"Virus that survived in the plasma at undetectable levels despite complete RT inhibition","D":"Re-infection, since a rebound cannot come from inside the patient","E":"The virus had developed resistance, so it was replicating all along","C":"Virus hiding in neutrophils, which ART cannot reach"},"r":{"B":"Correct. ARVs block ACTIVE steps (RT, integration, maturation), and a silent integrated provirus has none to block. That is why HIV is incurable.","A":"Circulating virus is suppressed; the source is cellular.","D":"Rebound after stopping ART is the classic reservoir phenomenon.","E":"Resistance would have shown as virological failure ON therapy.","C":"Neutrophils lack CD4 and are not the reservoir."},"t":"The HIV reservoir","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L15_26.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The HIV reservoir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Natural history stage","s":"An untreated man diagnosed with HIV 8 years ago has been well until now, when he develops Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which stage of the natural history has he reached?","lec":"L15","sl":25,"c":"<b>Natural history stage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Acute retroviral syndrome, with high RNA","B":"Recovery and seroconversion","C":"The asymptomatic phase at the viral set point","D":"AIDS, with a CD4 count below 200/µL","E":"The eclipse phase before any marker appears"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Acute retroviral syndrome occurs 2–3 weeks after infection.","B":"Seroconversion follows the acute syndrome in weeks.","C":"The asymptomatic phase has no opportunistic disease.","D":"After an asymptomatic chronic phase averaging ~8 years, AIDS = CD4 < 200/µL with opportunistic infections (slides 15, 25).","E":"The eclipse phase is the first days after exposure."},"img":"slides_i/L15_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An untreated HIV-infected man remains well for years, then develops Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which statement about where he is in the natural history is correct?","o":{"D":"He has entered AIDS: the CD4 count has fallen below 200/mm³ after an asymptomatic phase that averages about 8 years","B":"He is in the acute retroviral syndrome, which lasts about 8 years","C":"PCP marks seroconversion, 2–4 weeks after infection","A":"He has a CD4 count above 500, where PCP typically occurs","E":"Death is expected within 2–3 weeks of PCP in every patient"},"r":{"D":"Correct. AIDS = CD4 <200 with rising viral load; the complication table puts PCP at <200. Death typically comes 10–11 years after infection untreated.","B":"The acute syndrome lasts 2–3 weeks.","C":"Seroconversion is early and has nothing to do with PCP.","A":">500 is the range of acute retroviral syndrome, candida vaginitis and PGL.","E":"Not a statement on the slide."},"t":"Natural history numbers","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L15_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Natural history numbers</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Acute retroviral syndrome","s":"Three weeks after a high-risk exposure, a 24-year-old has fever, sore throat, lymphadenopathy and a rash. An antibody-only HIV test is negative. What should be done next, and why?","lec":"L15","sl":25,"c":"<b>Acute retroviral syndrome</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Drug-resistance testing before any other assay","B":"Plasma HIV RNA, as antibody may not have formed yet","C":"Monospot test, as a negative HIV test excludes infection","D":"CD4 count, as a low value confirms early infection","E":"Repeat the antibody test now, as the first was faulty"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Resistance testing follows a confirmed diagnosis (slide 34).","B":"Acute retroviral syndrome at 2–3 weeks: HIV RNA > 10,000 copies/mL with negative or indeterminate serology (slide 25). RNA appears before antibody (slide 33).","C":"A negative antibody test in this window does not exclude HIV.","D":"A transient CD4 fall is non-specific and does not confirm HIV.","E":"The test is not faulty — he is in the window before seroconversion."},"img":"slides_i/L15_25.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Three weeks after unprotected sex, a 24-year-old develops fever, sore throat, lymphadenopathy and a rash. The HIV antibody test is negative. What is the best next step?","o":{"B":"Test plasma HIV RNA, because this may be acute retroviral syndrome before seroconversion","D":"Reassure him: a negative antibody test excludes HIV","E":"Repeat the antibody test in 10 years","A":"Measure the CD4 count, which is diagnostic","C":"Diagnose infectious mononucleosis without further HIV testing"},"r":{"B":"Correct. The primary-infection criterion on the slide is HIV RNA >10,000 copies/mL with negative or indeterminate serology.","D":"Antibody appears only after ~3 weeks; a negative test now does not exclude HIV.","E":"Waiting misses the diagnosis and the chance to stop transmission.","A":"CD4 falls transiently but diagnoses nothing.","C":"A mono-like picture after an exposure must include acute HIV."},"t":"Acute retroviral syndrome","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L15_25.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Acute retroviral syndrome</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Marker timeline","s":"A nurse's blood is tested 12 days after a needlestick from a patient with uncontrolled HIV. If she has been infected, which result pattern fits the lecture's marker timeline?","lec":"L15","sl":33,"c":"<b>Marker timeline</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HIV RNA negative; p24 positive; antibody positive","B":"HIV RNA positive; p24 positive; antibody positive","C":"HIV RNA negative; p24 negative; antibody positive","D":"HIV RNA negative; p24 positive; antibody negative","E":"HIV RNA positive; p24 negative; antibody negative"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"RNA is the first marker; it cannot be negative while later markers are positive.","B":"Antibody is not expected before about 3 weeks.","C":"Antibody cannot precede RNA and p24.","D":"p24 does not appear before RNA.","E":"'Know what comes first': eclipse → RNA (~day 10) → p24 antigen → antibody (≥ day 21). At day 12 only RNA is expected (slide 33)."},"img":"slides_i/L15_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"In what order do HIV markers become detectable after infection?","o":{"E":"HIV RNA → p24 antigen → HIV antibody","D":"HIV antibody → p24 antigen → HIV RNA","C":"p24 antigen → HIV antibody → HIV RNA","B":"CD4 fall → HIV antibody → HIV RNA","A":"All three appear on the same day, at the end of the eclipse period"},"r":{"E":"Correct. She said 'you must know what comes first and what comes after'. RNA is amplified by PCR, so it is first.","D":"Reversed. Antibody is last.","C":"p24 follows RNA, and antibody is last.","B":"The CD4 count is not a diagnostic marker of infection.","A":"They appear in sequence across about three weeks."},"t":"The marker timeline","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L15_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>The marker timeline</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Exposure last night","s":"A healthy man asks for an HIV test the morning after condomless sex with a partner of unknown HIV status. What is the best advice?","lec":"L15","sl":37,"c":"<b>Exposure last night</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Wait 72 hours, then test and decide about PEP","B":"Start PEP now; today's test is only a baseline","C":"Test today; start PEP only if the result is reactive","D":"Test for p24 today, as it is positive within hours","E":"Start PrEP now and retest him in 6 months"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"72 hours is the limit for starting PEP, not the time to wait.","B":"He is in the eclipse period, so no test can detect a new infection yet; PEP is for emergencies and must start within 72 hours (slide 37).","C":"A non-reactive test now means nothing — every marker is still negative.","D":"p24 appears after RNA, days to weeks later.","E":"PrEP is taken before exposure; after an exposure the tool is PEP."},"img":"slides_i/L15_37.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A healthy man had condomless sex with a partner of unknown HIV status last night and asks for an HIV test today. What is the best advice?","o":{"B":"A test today can only serve as a baseline, because he is in the eclipse period; start PEP now (within 72 h) and re-test later","D":"Test today; a negative 4th-generation test excludes infection","A":"Wait 6 months before doing anything","E":"Start PrEP instead of PEP, since the exposure has already happened","C":"Measure CD4 today to see whether he is infected"},"r":{"B":"Correct. That is her own scenario. The test can wait; post-exposure prophylaxis cannot.","D":"No marker is detectable during eclipse, so a negative test is meaningless here.","A":"The 72-hour PEP window would be lost.","E":"PrEP is taken BEFORE exposure; after an exposure the answer is PEP.","C":"CD4 does not diagnose infection."},"t":"Exposure last night","sl":33,"img":"slides_i/L15_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Exposure last night</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Three-test algorithm — discordant result","s":"In the national testing algorithm, a client's first (A1, 4th-generation) test is reactive, the second (A2) is reactive and the third (A3) is non-reactive. How is the result reported, and what happens next?","lec":"L15","sl":35,"c":"<b>Three-test algorithm — discordant result</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Negative; no further testing is needed","B":"Positive; start antiretroviral therapy today","C":"Negative; repeat only A1 in 6 months time","D":"Positive; confirm by repeating A1 only","E":"Inconclusive; repeat the algorithm in 2 weeks"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Two reactive tests cannot simply be called negative.","B":"A positive report needs all three tests reactive.","C":"The follow-up interval is 2 weeks, and the result is not negative.","D":"Repeating the most sensitive test cannot resolve a specificity question.","E":"Slides 35–36: any discordance among the three tests is reported inconclusive and the algorithm is repeated after 2 weeks (send NAT if a window-period infection is possible)."},"img":"slides_i/L15_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Screening (A1, 4th generation) is reactive, A2 is reactive, and A3 is non-reactive. How is this reported, and what next?","o":{"E":"Inconclusive — repeat the algorithm in 2 weeks, or send HIV NAT if the window period is possible","A":"HIV positive, since two of the three tests are reactive and the majority result is reported","C":"HIV negative, since the most specific test (A3) is negative and it overrides the others","B":"Positive, provided the CD4 count is below 500, which confirms the discordant results","D":"Repeat A1 twice more with the same kit and report whichever result appears most often"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Only A1+ A2+ A3+ may be reported as positive. Discordant results are inconclusive.","A":"A majority vote is not the rule. All three must agree.","C":"An early seroconverter can be A3-negative, so it is not a clean negative.","B":"CD4 is not part of the diagnostic algorithm.","D":"Repeating the same kit repeats the same error; the kits must use different principles."},"t":"Three-test algorithm","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L15_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Three-test algorithm</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Why the algorithm is ordered this way","s":"In the three-test algorithm, the first assay is the most sensitive 4th-generation test, and the next two are progressively more specific and use different antigens. What is the reasoning behind that order?","lec":"L15","sl":35,"c":"<b>Why the algorithm is ordered this way</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The first measures viral load; later ones measure CD4 count","B":"The first misses no infection; later ones remove false positives","C":"The first must confirm infection; later ones find missed cases","D":"The first detects antibody only; later ones detect viral RNA","E":"The first detects HIV-2 only; later ones detect HIV-1"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Viral load and CD4 are monitoring tests (slide 34), not the diagnostic algorithm.","B":"Sensitivity first (rule out — miss no one), specificity after (rule in — clear the screen's false positives), with different kits so their false positives don't coincide.","C":"Reversed roles.","D":"The first test is 4th generation, i.e. antigen + antibody.","E":"The algorithm detects both types."},"img":"slides_i/L15_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why does the HIV testing strategy put the most SENSITIVE test first and the most SPECIFIC tests after it?","o":{"B":"Screening must miss no one; the later specific tests remove the false positives the sensitive screen lets through","D":"The most specific tests are the cheapest, so they are kept for last to save cost","C":"Highly sensitive tests produce no false positives, so the later tests only confirm","E":"Highly specific tests detect infection earlier in the window period than sensitive ones","A":"The order does not matter, as long as any three different tests are reactive"},"r":{"B":"Correct. That is why a three-test strategy is recommended once national positivity falls below 5%: at low prevalence, false positives are a larger share of all positives.","D":"Cost is not the rationale on the slide.","C":"A highly sensitive screen does produce false positives.","E":"Timing depends on the analyte (RNA vs antigen vs antibody), not on specificity.","A":"The order is deliberate."},"t":"Why sensitivity first","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L15_35.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Why sensitivity first</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Patterns of progression","s":"Two newly infected adults are followed without treatment. Patient 1's viral set point is very high; patient 2's is barely detectable. Which prediction fits the lecture's three patterns of progression?","lec":"L15","sl":29,"c":"<b>Patterns of progression</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Patient 1 is likely to be a long-term non-progressor","B":"Both will reach AIDS in 5–10 years regardless","C":"Patient 1 may reach AIDS within about 2 years","D":"The set point shows which HIV type each carries","E":"Patient 2 may reach AIDS within about 2 years"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Non-progressors control the virus to a low level.","B":"The patterns differ precisely in speed.","C":"Slide 29: 2–10% are rapid progressors (~2 years), 85–90% typical (5–10 years), 2–5% long-term non-progressors (>10 years). A high set point (slide 25) predicts faster progression.","D":"HIV type is determined by sequencing or serology, not set point.","E":"A barely detectable set point fits a non-progressor or elite controller."},"img":"slides_i/L15_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Two newly diagnosed patients have very different baseline viral loads. Which statement is most accurate?","o":{"C":"The patient with the higher baseline viral load is more likely to be a rapid progressor","B":"Baseline viral load has no relation to progression","A":"Most infected people are long-term non-progressors","D":"Rapid progressors make up about 85–90% of infections","E":"Elite controllers progress to AIDS within 2 years"},"r":{"C":"Correct. She said this is why a baseline viral load is measured at diagnosis.","B":"The lecturer tied baseline RNA copies to the pace of progression.","A":"Most (85–90%) are TYPICAL progressors.","D":"Rapid progressors are only 2–10%.","E":"Elite controllers / long-term non-progressors stay well for more than 10 years."},"t":"Progression patterns","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L15_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Progression patterns</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Thai HIV subtype","s":"Virus from a Thai patient is sequenced: its gag and pol genes resemble clade A, while its env gene resembles clade E. How is this virus classified?","lec":"L15","sl":19,"c":"<b>Thai HIV subtype</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HIV-1 group N, a pure clade A virus of chimpanzees","B":"HIV-1 group M, the clade B of North America","C":"HIV-1 group O, a pure clade E virus from Cameroon","D":"HIV-1 group M, circulating recombinant form CRF01_AE","E":"HIV-2, related to the sooty mangabey virus"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Group N is rare; the mosaic genome shows recombination.","B":"Clade B predominates in North America and Europe, not Thailand (slide 20).","C":"Group O is the 'outlier' group; the virus is a recombinant, not pure E.","D":"Slide 19: 96% of HIV-1-infected Thais carry CRF01_AE (gag, pol = clade A; env = clade E); >90% of cases worldwide are group M.","E":"HIV-2 is West African and related to sooty mangabey viruses."},"img":"slides_i/L15_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about HIV types and subtypes is correct?","o":{"D":"Most Thai infections are HIV-1 CRF01_AE, a circulating recombinant form, whereas HIV-2 is largely confined to West Africa","B":"Thailand's epidemic is mainly HIV-2, which came from the sooty mangabey in West Africa","C":"Clade B predominates in Thailand, as it does across Africa and the rest of Asia","E":"HIV-1 and HIV-2 differ by less than 5%, so the same subtype names apply to both","A":"Clades are defined by the gag gene, so CRF01_AE has clade A gag and clade E pol"},"r":{"D":"Correct. CRF01_AE takes gag/pol from clade A and env from clade E.","B":"HIV-2 (sooty mangabey origin) is a West African virus.","C":"Clade B is North America/Europe; Africa and Asia are non-B.","E":"They differ by more than 50%.","A":"Clades are based on the ENV gene."},"t":"HIV-1 vs HIV-2 and Thailand","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L15_19.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HIV-1 vs HIV-2 and Thailand</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Transmission risk by route","s":"Five people each had a single exposure to an HIV-positive source. Using the lecture's route-risk comparison, whose risk of infection is highest?","lec":"L15","sl":21,"c":"<b>Transmission risk by route</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A drug user who shared a needle with an infected user","B":"A patient transfused with one unit of infected blood","C":"A nurse with a needlestick from an infected patient","D":"A partner after one act of insertive vaginal sex","E":"A partner after one act of receptive anal sex"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Needle-sharing is well under 1% per episode.","B":"Slide 21: risk differs with the route — transfusion of infected blood is by far the highest per exposure (~90%).","C":"Percutaneous needlestick is ~0.2–0.3%.","D":"Insertive vaginal sex is among the lowest listed.","E":"Receptive anal sex is the highest-risk SEXUAL route, but still ~1–2%."},"img":"slides_i/L15_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which exposure carries the HIGHEST per-exposure risk of HIV transmission among these options?","o":{"B":"Transfusion of HIV-infected blood","C":"A needlestick injury from an HIV-positive patient","D":"Receptive vaginal intercourse","A":"Insertive vaginal intercourse","E":"A mosquito that has fed on an HIV-positive person"},"r":{"B":"Correct. ~9,000 per 10,000: a whole unit of infected blood straight into a vein.","C":"About 30 per 10,000. Important to you as a clinician, but far below transfusion.","D":"About 10 per 10,000.","A":"About 5 per 10,000, the lowest on the table.","E":"HIV has no arthropod vector: insect bites do not transmit it."},"t":"Transmission risk","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L15_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Transmission risk</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Genital papules — identify and manage","s":"A 22-year-old has several smooth, pearly, 3-mm dome-shaped papules with a central dimple in the pubic area; pressing one expresses a white core. Which statement about the causative virus is correct?","lec":"L15","sl":11,"c":"<b>Genital papules — identify and manage</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A poxvirus treated with oral acyclovir","B":"A herpesvirus treated with oral acyclovir","C":"A papillomavirus that produces koilocytes","D":"A papillomavirus prevented by the quadrivalent vaccine","E":"A poxvirus with no specific antiviral or vaccine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Acyclovir is for herpesviruses, not poxviruses.","B":"Herpes gives painful vesicles and ulcers.","C":"Koilocytes are HPV's cytopathic change (slide 5).","D":"Genital warts are verrucous, not umbilicated.","E":"Umbilicated pearly papules = molluscum contagiosum (Poxviridae). Slide 11: no antiviral drug and no vaccine."},"img":"slides_i/L15_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 22-year-old has several smooth, pearly, 3-mm dome-shaped papules with a central dimple on the pubic area. Squeezing one releases a white curd-like core. What is the cause?","o":{"E":"Molluscum contagiosum virus, a poxvirus","D":"HPV types 6 and 11","B":"HSV-2","A":"HPV types 16 and 18","C":"Cytomegalovirus"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Pearly + umbilicated + a white core (molluscum bodies) = หูดข้าวสุก.","D":"HPV 6/11 give cauliflower/cockscomb warts (condyloma acuminatum).","B":"HSV gives painful grouped vesicles that ulcerate.","A":"16/18 are the oncogenic types, causing dysplasia and cancer, not papules.","C":"CMV does not cause this skin lesion."},"t":"Genital lesion discriminator","sl":6,"img":"slides_i/L15_6.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Genital lesion discriminator</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Molluscum as an epidemiological marker","s":"A provincial health office notices a sharp rise in genital molluscum contagiosum among young adults. Why does the lecture treat this as significant?","lec":"L15","sl":11,"c":"<b>Molluscum as an epidemiological marker</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It signals a new animal reservoir for the virus","B":"It is an epidemiological marker for STDs","C":"It predicts a rise in cervical cancer","D":"It shows the national vaccine is failing","E":"It indicates spreading resistance to acyclovir"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Disease is only in humans — there is no animal reservoir.","B":"Slide 11: MCV infects only humans, spreads by contact and sex, and is an 'epidemiology marker for STDs'.","C":"Cervical cancer is linked to high-risk HPV, not MCV.","D":"There is no MCV vaccine.","E":"MCV is not treated with acyclovir."},"img":"slides_i/L15_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about molluscum contagiosum virus is correct?","o":{"B":"It infects only humans, and a rise in genital molluscum has served as an epidemiological marker for STDs such as HIV","A":"It is an RNA virus whose genital lesions respond to a short course of acyclovir","C":"It is a papillomavirus type covered by Gardasil-9, so vaccination prevents it","D":"It has an animal reservoir in rodents, so outbreaks follow rodent exposure","E":"Its incubation period is 2–3 days, so lesions appear soon after the sexual contact"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Visible molluscum tracked the invisible spread of HIV in the population.","A":"It is a dsDNA poxvirus, with no antiviral and no vaccine.","C":"It is a poxvirus, not HPV.","D":"'Disease = only in human'.","E":"Incubation is 14–50 days (she said about 2 months)."},"t":"Molluscum — epidemiology","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L15_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Molluscum — epidemiology</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Neonatal HSV without lesions","s":"A woman with known genital herpes has no lesions at a vaginal delivery, yet her baby develops disseminated vesicles in the second week of life. How was the virus transmitted?","lec":"L15","sl":10,"c":"<b>Neonatal HSV without lesions</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Contact with the mother's active skin lesions","B":"Reactivation of latent virus in the baby's own ganglia","C":"Breastfeeding after delivery","D":"Transplacental spread earlier in pregnancy","E":"Subclinical viral shedding from the genital mucosa"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The mother had no lesions.","B":"Latency follows primary infection — the baby had none before birth.","C":"HSV is not a breast-milk virus in the lecture.","D":"Onset in week 2 after a vaginal birth fits acquisition at delivery.","E":"Slide 10: 'Subclinical viral shedding can occur in the absence of lesions → leads to unrecognised transmission to neonates and sexual partners (HSV-2)'."},"img":"slides_i/L15_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A woman with known genital herpes has no lesions at delivery, yet her newborn develops neonatal HSV. What explains the transmission?","o":{"E":"Subclinical (asymptomatic) viral shedding from the genital mucosa","A":"Transplacental spread in the first trimester","B":"Re-infection of the mother by a new strain during labour","D":"Transmission through breast milk only","C":"HSV cannot be transmitted in the absence of lesions"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The slide: shedding without lesions leads to unrecognised transmission to neonates and sexual partners.","A":"Neonatal HSV is typically acquired DURING delivery.","B":"Recurrence is reactivation of latent virus, not re-infection.","D":"Not the mechanism taught.","C":"That is exactly the misconception the slide corrects."},"t":"Genital herpes — recurrence","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L15_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Genital herpes — recurrence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Genital ulcers and HIV","s":"A man with frequent recurrences of painful genital ulcers is counselled that his risk of acquiring HIV is raised. Which explanation is correct?","lec":"L15","sl":9,"c":"<b>Genital ulcers and HIV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The ulcer breaks the mucosa and draws in CD4 cells","B":"HSV lowers his neutralising antibody to HIV","C":"HSV proteins bind HIV and carry it into neurons","D":"Antiviral treatment of herpes depletes his CD4 cells","E":"HSV integrates into the HIV provirus and activates it"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 9: HSV-related ulcers are associated with increased HIV transmission — broken epithelium plus inflammatory CD4 T cells and macrophages at the lesion.","B":"There is no such mechanism in the lecture.","C":"Neuronal latency is HSV's own biology, not a vehicle for HIV.","D":"Acyclovir does not deplete CD4 cells.","E":"HSV does not integrate."},"img":"slides_i/L15_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why is genital herpes a risk factor for acquiring HIV?","o":{"A":"The ulcer breaches the mucosa and is rich in CD4 T cells and macrophages, the cells HIV infects","C":"HSV carries HIV inside its own virion and integrates it into the host genome","D":"HSV co-infection makes HIV resistant to the antiretroviral drugs used in PEP","B":"The acyclovir used for herpes suppresses CD4 cells and lowers local immunity","E":"HSV and HIV share the same entry receptor, so one infection opens the door for the other"},"r":{"A":"Correct: a portal of entry plus target cells.","C":"HSV does not carry or integrate HIV.","D":"Not a mechanism.","B":"Acyclovir is not immunosuppressive.","E":"HSV and HIV use different receptors."},"t":"HSV ulcers and HIV","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L15_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HSV ulcers and HIV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Congenital CMV — outcome","s":"A woman who was seronegative before pregnancy has a mild, heterophile-negative mononucleosis-like illness in her first trimester, caused by the herpesvirus that forms owl's-eye inclusions. Which outcome is her baby most at risk of?","lec":"L15","sl":12,"c":"<b>Congenital CMV — outcome</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cataract with patent ductus arteriosus","B":"Limb hypoplasia with skin scarring","C":"Sensorineural hearing loss","D":"Recurrent laryngeal papillomas","E":"Hydrops from severe fetal anaemia"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cataract + PDA is congenital rubella (L12).","B":"Limb hypoplasia with scarring is congenital varicella.","C":"CMV is the most common infectious cause of congenital hearing loss, vision loss and mental retardation (slide 12); primary infection in a seronegative mother carries the greatest risk (slide 13).","D":"Laryngeal papillomas come from HPV acquired at birth.","E":"Hydrops from fetal anaemia is parvovirus B19."},"img":"slides_i/L15_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A woman who is CMV-seronegative before pregnancy acquires a primary CMV infection in the first trimester. What outcome is her baby at greatest risk of?","o":{"C":"Cytomegalic inclusion disease, with hearing loss, vision loss and mental retardation","A":"Heterophile-positive infectious mononucleosis","B":"No risk, because CMV cannot cross the placenta","E":"Congenital cataract, patent ductus and deafness as the classic triad","D":"Condyloma acuminatum at birth"},"r":{"C":"Correct. A seronegative mother has no IgG to protect the fetus. CMV is the commonest infectious cause of congenital hearing loss.","A":"Heterophile-positive mono is EBV.","B":"CMV is a TORCH agent and crosses the placenta.","E":"That triad is congenital RUBELLA.","D":"Not a CMV outcome."},"t":"CMV — host-dependent outcome","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L15_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>CMV — host-dependent outcome</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Heterophile-negative mononucleosis","s":"A previously healthy 30-year-old has 2 weeks of fever and fatigue, mild lymphadenopathy, only slight pharyngitis and atypical lymphocytes. The Monospot (heterophile antibody) test is negative. Which virus is most likely?","lec":"L15","sl":14,"c":"<b>Heterophile-negative mononucleosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Human herpesvirus 8","B":"Molluscum contagiosum virus","C":"Herpes simplex virus 2","D":"Cytomegalovirus","E":"Epstein-Barr virus"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"HHV-8 causes Kaposi's sarcoma.","B":"MCV causes umbilicated papules.","C":"HSV-2 causes genital vesicles and ulcers.","D":"Slide 14: in adults CMV causes a heterophile-NEGATIVE mononucleosis syndrome, like EBV but with less severe pharyngitis and lymphadenopathy.","E":"EBV mononucleosis is heterophile-positive, with marked pharyngitis."},"img":"slides_i/L15_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A previously healthy 30-year-old has 2 weeks of fever, fatigue and mild lymphadenopathy with atypical lymphocytes. The Monospot (heterophile antibody) test is negative. Which virus is the best fit?","o":{"D":"Cytomegalovirus","B":"Epstein–Barr virus","C":"HSV-2","A":"Molluscum contagiosum virus","E":"HPV type 16"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Heterophile-negative mono = CMV. She named the heterophile test as the lab tool that separates the two.","B":"EBV mono is heterophile-POSITIVE.","C":"Genital HSV does not give a mono syndrome.","A":"MCV causes skin papules only.","E":"HPV does not cause a febrile mono picture."},"t":"Heterophile-negative mononucleosis","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L15_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Heterophile-negative mononucleosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"CMV retinitis — drug and inclusion","s":"A kidney-transplant recipient on immunosuppression develops blurred vision, and fundoscopy shows haemorrhagic necrotising retinitis. Which drug is used, and which inclusion would biopsy of an infected organ show?","lec":"L15","sl":14,"c":"<b>CMV retinitis — drug and inclusion</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ganciclovir; Negri cytoplasmic inclusions","B":"Acyclovir; owl's-eye intranuclear inclusions","C":"No antiviral exists; koilocytes with halos","D":"Acyclovir; Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusions","E":"Ganciclovir; owl's-eye intranuclear inclusions"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Negri bodies are rabies.","B":"CMV lacks the thymidine kinase acyclovir needs; ganciclovir is the drug.","C":"CMV has an antiviral; koilocytes are HPV.","D":"Acyclovir and Cowdry A fit HSV/VZV.","E":"Slide 14: CMV in the immunocompromised causes retinitis, pneumonia, colitis; treatment ganciclovir; 'owl's eye' basophilic intranuclear inclusions."},"img":"slides_i/L15_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A kidney-transplant recipient on immunosuppression develops blurred vision; fundoscopy shows haemorrhagic retinal necrosis. What is the likely cause and drug?","o":{"E":"CMV retinitis — ganciclovir","C":"HSV keratitis — acyclovir","D":"EBV retinitis — rituximab","B":"CMV retinitis — acyclovir, the first-line drug for all herpesviruses","A":"Toxoplasma retinochoroiditis — oseltamivir"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Retinitis, pneumonia and colitis are the common three in the immunocompromised, and ganciclovir is the CMV drug.","C":"HSV causes keratitis of the cornea, not retinal necrosis in a transplant.","D":"Not taught.","B":"Acyclovir is for HSV/VZV. CMV lacks the thymidine kinase that activates it.","A":"Oseltamivir is an influenza drug."},"t":"CMV in the immunocompromised","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L15_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>CMV in the immunocompromised</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Transmission routes of the STD viruses","s":"Which pairing of a sexually transmitted virus with a transmission route stated in the lecture is correct?","lec":"L15","sl":5,"c":"<b>Transmission routes of the STD viruses</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HPV — neonatal transmission from the mother","B":"HIV — transmission by sharing eating utensils","C":"CMV — transmission only by sexual contact","D":"Molluscum — transmission from animal hosts","E":"HSV — transmission only when lesions are visible"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 5: HPV is transmitted from skin lesions by close contact, sexually and neonatally.","B":"HIV is not transmitted by casual contact such as utensils.","C":"CMV also spreads by blood and organ donation, and congenitally (slide 12).","D":"MCV causes disease only in humans (slide 11).","E":"HSV also spreads by subclinical shedding without lesions (slide 10)."},"img":"slides_i/L15_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing of TORCH agent and its usual route to the baby is correct?","o":{"A":"HSV — mainly during delivery through the infected birth canal","C":"CMV — only after birth through breast milk","B":"Rubella — only during delivery","E":"Toxoplasma — only after birth","D":"HIV — only across the placenta"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The H of TORCH is typically acquired intrapartum, often from asymptomatic shedding.","C":"CMV crosses the placenta; congenital CMV is the major concern.","B":"Rubella causes CRS by transplacental infection.","E":"Toxoplasma is transplacental (tachyzoites).","D":"HIV can use all three routes, including breastfeeding."},"t":"TORCH routes","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L15_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>TORCH routes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Vaccine vs drug across the STD viruses","s":"Of the five sexually transmitted viruses taught in detail (HPV, HSV, MCV, HIV, CMV), which one has a licensed vaccine but no antiviral drug?","lec":"L15","sl":7,"c":"<b>Vaccine vs drug across the STD viruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"HPV","B":"HSV","C":"MCV","D":"CMV","E":"HIV"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 7: HPV — no antiviral drug; three recombinant vaccines (Cervarix, Gardasil-4, Gardasil-9).","B":"HSV: acyclovir, no licensed vaccine (slide 10).","C":"MCV: neither drug nor vaccine (slide 11).","D":"CMV: ganciclovir, no licensed vaccine (slide 14).","E":"HIV: antiretrovirals, no licensed vaccine (slide 37)."},"img":"slides_i/L15_7.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Among the sexually transmitted viruses in this lecture, which one can be prevented by a licensed vaccine?","o":{"A":"HPV","B":"HSV-2","D":"HIV","E":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Molluscum contagiosum virus"},"r":{"A":"Correct: Cervarix, Gardasil-4 and Gardasil-9. She urged vaccinating both sexes.","B":"No licensed HSV vaccine.","D":"No licensed HIV vaccine; prevention is safe sex, PrEP and PEP.","E":"No licensed CMV vaccine.","C":"No drug and no vaccine."},"t":"STDs with no vaccine","sl":7,"img":"slides_i/L15_7.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>STDs with no vaccine</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"PEP vs PrEP","s":"A laboratory worker had a needlestick from an HIV-positive sample 30 hours ago. Separately, a couple in which only one partner has HIV wants ongoing protection for the other. Which advice is correct for each?","lec":"L15","sl":37,"c":"<b>PEP vs PrEP</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Worker: PrEP now; couple: PEP after each exposure","B":"Worker: PEP only if positive at 3 months; couple: PrEP","C":"Worker: PEP now; couple: HIV vaccine for the negative partner","D":"Worker: PEP now; couple: daily PrEP for the negative partner","E":"Worker: too late for PEP; couple: daily PrEP"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Reversed: PrEP is before exposure, PEP after.","B":"Waiting to see seroconversion defeats the purpose of PEP.","C":"There is no licensed HIV vaccine.","D":"PEP is an emergency measure and must start within 72 hours (30 h is still in time); PrEP, taken consistently, cuts sexual transmission risk by ~99% (slide 37).","E":"The window for PEP is 72 hours, not 24."},"img":"slides_i/L15_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement about HIV prophylaxis is correct?","o":{"D":"PEP should start as early as possible, ideally within 24 h and no later than 72 h, on a TDF + 3TC/FTC backbone","A":"PEP can be started up to 2 weeks after exposure with full effect","E":"PrEP is taken only after a high-risk exposure","B":"PEP uses a single drug, to limit side effects","C":"PrEP gives no protection even when taken consistently"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Two drugs work; three are preferred.","A":"The window is 72 h, because the reservoir forms within days.","E":"That describes PEP; PrEP is taken BEFORE exposure.","B":"PEP is a combination.","C":"PrEP cuts sexual transmission by ~99% if taken consistently."},"t":"PrEP vs PEP","sl":37,"img":"slides_i/L15_37.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>PrEP vs PEP</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Co-receptor use","s":"Virus isolated early in an HIV infection mainly uses CCR5 and infects macrophages; late in the disease, isolates increasingly use CXCR4 and infect T cells. Changes in which viral protein underlie this switch?","lec":"L15","sl":17,"c":"<b>Co-receptor use</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"gp120, which binds the co-receptor","B":"p24, the group-specific capsid protein","C":"Integrase, which chooses the target cell","D":"Protease, which cleaves the co-receptor","E":"Reverse transcriptase, which binds CXCR4"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Slide 17: gp120 interacts with CD4 and the chemokine co-receptors CCR5 and CXCR4 for attachment and entry — and its gene mutates rapidly.","B":"p24 is an internal core antigen.","C":"Integrase acts after entry.","D":"Viral protease cleaves viral polyproteins.","E":"RT is internal and acts after entry."},"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"An AIDS patient develops fever and blurred vision from disseminated CMV. The CD4 count is most likely:","o":{"A":"Below 50/mm³","C":"Above 500/mm³","D":"Between 200 and 500/mm³","E":"Exactly 350/mm³","B":"CD4 count has no relation to CMV disease"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Disseminated CMV, MAC and primary CNS lymphoma sit in the <50 row. She said she does not stress the table, so this is a supporting item.","C":"Above 500: ARS, candida vaginitis, PGL.","D":"200–500: zoster, TB, oral hairy leukoplakia, Kaposi's sarcoma.","E":"Not a threshold in the table.","B":"The whole table is organised by CD4."},"t":"HIV complications by CD4","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L15_30.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>HIV complications by CD4</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233841838},{"id":"I|L15|28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q64","t":"Earliest HIV test","s":"Which laboratory test detects HIV infection EARLIEST after exposure?","lec":"L15","sl":33,"c":"<b>Earliest HIV test</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q64 — HIV patient; which test gives the earliest result (early detect stage)? Keyed HIV RNA.</span>","o":{"C":"HIV RNA","B":"p24 antigen","E":"HIV antibody","A":"CD4 count","D":"Western blot"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. RNA by NAT turns positive at ~day 10, which is first on the slide-33 timeline.","B":"p24 is the runner-up (~day 15), and the usual wrong pick.","E":"Antibody appears after about three weeks.","A":"CD4 count is a staging/monitoring test.","D":"Western blot detects antibody, so it is later still."},"img":"slides_i/L15_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L15|29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q65","t":"Asymptomatic phase — pathogenesis","s":"A 28-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with HIV; she is asymptomatic with a low viral load. Which best describes the pathogenesis of her infection?","lec":"L15","sl":17,"c":"<b>Asymptomatic phase — pathogenesis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q65 — newly diagnosed, asymptomatic, low viral load; best description of pathogenesis. Keyed 'binding the viral envelope glycoprotein to CD4'.</span>","o":{"A":"Binding of the viral envelope glycoprotein to the CD4 receptor","C":"Direct infection and destruction of CD8 cells by the virus","B":"Integration of the viral genome leading to cell lysis","E":"An autoimmune response against host cells","D":"NK-cell activation leading to hyperinflammation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct (keyed). The basic event, gp120 binding CD4, continues at a level the immune system roughly balances during clinical latency.","C":"HIV targets CD4, not CD8.","B":"Integration does not lyse the cell; latently infected cells survive.","E":"Not the mechanism.","D":"Not the mechanism."},"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L15|30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q66","t":"Early ART — expected effect","s":"If HIV infection is treated early, what is the expected outcome?","lec":"L15","sl":37,"c":"<b>Early ART — expected effect</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q66 — what happens if HIV is treated early? ⚠️ The circulating student key highlights TWO options ('unsure'); the slide-supported answer is the CD4-rise option.</span>","o":{"D":"CD4 cells gradually rise and the risk of opportunistic infection falls","E":"The virus is eliminated within a few months","A":"Viral levels fall so far that treatment can be stopped","B":"CD4 function is stable but the count falls","C":"Progression to AIDS is faster"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. Treatment restores immunity; it cannot cure. ⚠️ The BM30 key also highlights option D with an 'unsure' note. D contradicts the goal of ART (restore CD4).","E":"The latent reservoir persists.","A":"Treatment is lifelong; rebound follows stopping.","B":"The goal and usual effect of ART is a RISING CD4 count.","C":"The opposite."},"img":"slides_i/L15_37.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L15|31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q4","t":"Monitoring on ART","s":"Which pair of QUANTITATIVE tests is used to monitor an HIV patient on antiviral therapy?","lec":"L15","sl":34,"c":"<b>Monitoring on ART</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Virology Formative Q4 — the quantitative tests for monitoring HIV patients on antiviral therapy. Keyed 'CD4 count and viral load'.</span>","o":{"A":"CD4 count and viral load","D":"Anti-HIV titre and CD4 count","B":"HIV antigen titre and CD4 count","E":"Anti-HIV titre and viral load","C":"HIV antigen titre and viral load"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct (keyed). Viral load shows whether the drugs work; CD4 shows immune recovery.","D":"Anti-HIV stays positive for life, so it cannot track treatment.","B":"Antigen titre is not the monitoring test.","E":"Antibody titre does not reflect treatment response.","C":"Antigen titre is not the monitoring test."},"img":"slides_i/L15_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L15|32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"virology Q8","t":"Which statement about HIV is correct","s":"Which statement about human immunodeficiency virus is CORRECT?","lec":"L15","sl":17,"c":"<b>Which statement about HIV is correct</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Virology Formative Q8 — which is CORRECT about HIV? Keyed 'antibodies detected in an infected person indicate the state of infection but not prevention'.</span>","o":{"C":"Antibodies detected in an infected person indicate infection, not protection","A":"Most infected people have no early symptoms and eliminate the virus later","E":"HIV is not transmitted through breastfeeding","B":"An HIV-infected mother who is asymptomatic cannot transmit the virus to her baby","D":"The virus most likely originated in bats"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct (keyed). Anti-p24 is a marker; anti-gp120 is neutralising but fails against quasispecies.","A":"HIV is not eliminated; the reservoir persists.","E":"Breastfeeding is a listed perinatal route.","B":"Asymptomatic mothers transmit too.","D":"HIV-1 came from chimpanzees/gorillas and HIV-2 from sooty mangabeys, both primates."},"img":"slides_i/L15_17.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L15|33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q65","t":"Low CD4 — next test","s":"A known HIV patient has a CD4 count of about 180/mm³. Which laboratory test should be ordered to guide management?","lec":"L15","sl":34,"c":"<b>Low CD4 — next test</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q65 — HIV patient with a low CD4 (~180); which laboratory test? Options included CD8 count, HIV antibody, HIV RNA.</span>","o":{"A":"Plasma HIV RNA (viral load)","B":"CD8 count","C":"HIV antibody test","E":"Heterophile antibody test","D":"Tzanck smear"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Initial evaluation = CD4 + plasma HIV RNA (+ resistance testing). The antibody test is already known positive.","B":"Not a monitoring test on the slide.","C":"He is already diagnosed; antibody stays positive and adds nothing.","E":"An EBV test.","D":"A herpes test."},"img":"slides_i/L15_34.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L15|34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q67","t":"Kaposi's sarcoma in AIDS","s":"A 50-year-old man with AIDS has purple nodules in the mouth and on the skin; biopsy shows Kaposi's sarcoma with detectable viral DNA. Which virus is responsible?","lec":"L15","sl":31,"c":"<b>Kaposi's sarcoma in AIDS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q67 · BM30 SUM1 Q55 · BM30 Virology Formative Q6 — AIDS patient with oral/cutaneous vascular nodules; Kaposi's sarcoma → which virus? Keyed HHV-8.</span>","o":{"B":"HHV-8","E":"HPV","D":"HTLV-1","C":"Hepatitis B virus","A":"Hepatitis C virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct: KSHV = HHV-8. The papers give the NUMBER, not the name.","E":"HPV → cervical, anal and oropharyngeal cancer.","D":"HTLV-1 → adult T-cell leukaemia.","C":"HBV → hepatocellular carcinoma.","A":"HCV → hepatocellular carcinoma."},"img":"slides_i/L15_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q55","BM30 virology Q6"]},{"id":"I|L15|35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q66","t":"CMV pneumonitis in HIV","s":"A 38-year-old HIV-positive woman has 2 days of increasing dyspnoea with bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Lung biopsy shows greatly enlarged cells with large intranuclear inclusions surrounded by a halo. Which organism?","lec":"L15","sl":14,"c":"<b>CMV pneumonitis in HIV</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q44 · BM29 SUM2 Q63 · BM30 SUM2 Q62 · BM31 SUM2 Q66 — HIV-positive patient, interstitial pneumonia, large cells with intranuclear inclusions. Keyed CMV.</span>","o":{"D":"Cytomegalovirus","E":"Varicella-zoster virus","A":"Epstein–Barr virus","B":"Herpes simplex virus","C":"Zika virus"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The owl's-eye intranuclear inclusion in a cytomegalic cell, with pneumonitis as one of the common three.","E":"VZV gives Cowdry A inclusions and multinucleated giant cells, not owl's eyes.","A":"EBV does not produce this inclusion.","B":"HSV: multinucleated giant cells and Cowdry A.","C":"Not a pneumonia agent in this setting."},"img":"slides_i/L15_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q62","BM29 Q63","BM28 Q44"]},{"id":"I|L15|36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q63","t":"Preventing mother-to-child HIV","s":"An HIV-positive woman in her first trimester asks how to protect her baby. Which plan is best?","lec":"L15","sl":21,"c":"<b>Preventing mother-to-child HIV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q63 — pregnant, HIV-positive, first trimester; what is the prevention? (short answer). L15 transcript: C-section when not suppressed; L18: same-day ART, TLD.</span>","o":{"E":"Start ART now and continue through delivery, with infant prophylaxis and avoidance of breastfeeding where safe alternatives exist","D":"Wait until the third trimester to start ART, because the drugs are unsafe in early pregnancy","B":"No intervention is needed, because an asymptomatic mother with a normal CD4 cannot transmit","C":"Give acyclovir from week 36 to suppress viral shedding before labour","A":"Advise termination, because no intervention reduces the risk of transmission"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct: cover all three perinatal routes (intrapartum, peripartum, breastfeeding). Most transmission happens around delivery.","D":"Delay lets the viral load stay high; ART starts as soon as possible.","B":"False. Asymptomatic mothers do transmit.","C":"Acyclovir is an HSV drug.","A":"Not a recommended intervention."},"img":"slides_i/L15_21.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L15|37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L15 s42","t":"NL — live vaccine in AIDS","s":"Which vaccine is contraindicated in a patient with HIV infection and clinical AIDS?","lec":"L15","sl":42,"c":"<b>NL — live vaccine in AIDS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>NL item printed on BM33 L15 slide 42 and answered in class — which vaccine is contraindicated in HIV with clinical AIDS?</span>","o":{"B":"BCG","E":"Hepatitis A vaccine","A":"Inactivated influenza vaccine","C":"Tetanus toxoid","D":"Inactivated JE vaccine"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. BCG is a LIVE attenuated vaccine and can disseminate when T-cell immunity is gone. She: 'separate out the live ones'.","E":"Inactivated, so safe.","A":"The injectable vaccine is inactivated, so safe.","C":"A toxoid, so safe.","D":"The inactivated JE vaccine is safe."},"img":"slides_i/L15_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L15|38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L15 s43","t":"NL — oral hairy leukoplakia","s":"A 40-year-old woman who has had HIV for 7 years has white, corrugated plaques on the lateral tongue that cannot be scraped off. The diagnosis is oral hairy leukoplakia. Which virus causes it?","lec":"L15","sl":43,"c":"<b>NL — oral hairy leukoplakia</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>NL item printed on BM33 L15 slide 43 and reviewed in L18 — 7 years of HIV, white corrugated plaques on the lateral tongue that cannot be scraped off.</span>","o":{"C":"EBV","A":"CMV","B":"HSV","D":"HHV-6","E":"HHV-8"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Oral hairy leukoplakia is EBV and is listed as an AIDS indicator.","A":"CMV → retinitis, pneumonitis, colitis.","B":"HSV → vesicles and ulcers.","D":"HHV-6 → roseola.","E":"HHV-8 → Kaposi's sarcoma. Also: thrush would SCRAPE OFF, which is the contrast with leukoplakia."},"img":"slides_i/L15_43.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L16|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Emerging vs re-emerging","s":"A country that had recorded almost no cases of a febrile illness with Koplik spots and a descending rash for twelve years reports 900 cases in six months. Routine childhood immunisation coverage there fell to 62% during a two-year political crisis. How does the lecture classify this event, and what drove it?","lec":"L16","sl":3,"c":"<b>Emerging vs re-emerging</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Re-emerging disease, driven by loss of population immunity","B":"Emerging disease, driven by expansion of the vector population","C":"Re-emerging disease, driven by antigenic shift in the virus","D":"Emerging disease, driven by spillover from a new animal reservoir","E":"Re-emerging disease, driven by climate change and urbanisation"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Koplik spots plus a descending rash identify measles, a virus recognised for centuries and previously controlled. A previously recognised agent rising again in incidence is a re-emerging infectious disease (slide 3), and slide 7 pairs measles specifically with 'declining vaccination coverage' — falling coverage means falling population immunity.","B":"Wrong on both counts. Measles spreads by the respiratory route with no vector at all; vector expansion is the driver slide 7 assigns to dengue.","C":"The classification is right but the driver is not. Antigenic shift requires a segmented genome and reassortment; measles has a single unsegmented (-)RNA genome and one serotype, which is why one vaccine has worked for decades.","D":"An emerging infectious disease is one newly appeared in a population or rapidly increasing in range. Measles is human-only with no animal reservoir, and nothing here is new.","E":"Climate change and urbanisation are on slide 7's list of re-emergence drivers, but they are the ones that act through vectors (dengue). The stem hands you the immunisation figure, which is the driver to use."},"img":"slides_i/L16_3.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Measles cases rise sharply in a country where the disease had been almost eliminated, after vaccination coverage fell. How is this classified, and why?","o":{"A":"Re-emerging — a previously recognised, controlled virus increasing again because population immunity fell","B":"Emerging — any rise in cases makes a virus emerging","D":"Re-emerging — because the measles virus has undergone antigenic shift","C":"Emerging — because measles has a new animal reservoir","E":"Neither — measles cannot re-emerge once it has a vaccine"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 7 pairs measles with 'declining vaccination coverage'. Old virus, new increase = REID.","B":"An EID is newly appeared or new to a region; a known, once-controlled virus returning is RE-emerging.","D":"Measles has one serotype and no segmented genome, so no shift.","C":"Measles is human-only.","E":"Loss of vaccine coverage is exactly how it re-emerges."},"t":"Emerging vs re-emerging","sl":3,"img":"slides_i/L16_3.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Emerging vs re-emerging</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Shared characteristics of EID and REID","s":"A national preparedness plan concentrates its virus surveillance budget on live-animal markets, poultry farms and wildlife traders rather than on hospitals. Which of the lecture's shared characteristics of emerging and re-emerging pathogens justifies that choice?","lec":"L16","sl":5,"c":"<b>Shared characteristics of EID and REID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fewer than 20% of them are able to infect more than one host species","B":"They rarely acquire mutations that improve infection of human cells","C":"They are mostly bacteria that have acquired resistance genes in animals","D":"Between 60 and 80% of them originate in animal sources","E":"They lose the potential for person-to-person spread after spillover"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The opposite of the slide: the ability to infect multiple host species is listed as a characteristic, which is exactly what lets a virus move between reservoir, amplifying host and human.","B":"Adaptation and evolution head the list: genetic change that enhances human infection or immune escape is the first characteristic on slide 5.","C":"Most emerging agents in this lecture are RNA viruses; their error-prone polymerases, not antibiotic resistance, drive emergence. Bacteria, parasites and fungi can emerge too, but they are not the majority.","D":"Correct. Slide 5 gives zoonotic origin as one of the four shared characteristics and puts the figure at 60-80%. If most emerging agents come out of animals, the animal-human interface is where you watch.","E":"Potential for human-to-human transmission is the fourth listed characteristic. Losing it is what stops most spillovers from becoming epidemics, but it is not a property of the group."},"img":"slides_i/L16_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement about emerging infectious diseases is correct?","o":{"D":"Most (about 60–80%) originate from animals and can infect several host species","A":"Most are caused by DNA viruses because DNA mutates fastest","C":"They never spread from person to person","B":"Only bacteria can be emerging pathogens","E":"About 5% originate from animals"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Zoonotic origin is one of the four shared characteristics.","A":"RNA viruses dominate, because their polymerases lack proofreading.","C":"Human-to-human potential is one of the four characteristics.","B":"Bacteria, parasites and fungi can all emerge.","E":"The figure on the slide is 60–80%."},"t":"Zoonotic share","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L16_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Zoonotic share</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Stages of viral emergence","s":"Three men who butchered a wild animal carcass in a newly logged forest are admitted with an undiagnosed febrile illness, and a novel virus is sequenced from all three. None of their 140 traced household and hospital contacts becomes infected over eight weeks. Using the lecture's pathway of emergence, which step has this virus not yet taken?","lec":"L16","sl":9,"c":"<b>Stages of viral emergence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Adaptation to sustained transmission between people","B":"Establishment of a reservoir species in a new geographic region","C":"Spillover from an animal reservoir into a human host","D":"Environmental change that increases human contact with animals","E":"Acquisition of a segmented genome allowing gene reassortment"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 9's chain runs environmental change, increased human-animal contact, spillover, viral adaptation, human-to-human transmission, emerging viral disease. Logging supplied the first two steps and the three cases are the spillover; zero secondary cases in 140 contacts means the adaptation step has not happened. Slide 10 states the rule outright: spillover is not the same as sustained human transmission.","B":"Not part of the lecture's pathway, and nothing suggests the reservoir species has moved anywhere.","C":"Three infected butchers are the spillover event itself.","D":"The logging in the stem is that step, already taken.","E":"Segmentation is a fixed property of a virus family, not a step a virus acquires. It is what allows reassortment in influenza, hantavirus and SFTS virus, and it is not required for emergence at all."},"img":"slides_i/L16_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A novel bat virus infects three villagers who butchered wild animals, but none of their contacts falls ill. What stage of emergence has the virus reached?","o":{"A":"Spillover without sustained human-to-human transmission, so it is not yet an emerging epidemic","E":"A pandemic, since the virus has already crossed into humans in more than one person","D":"Full adaptation, since a virus that can infect humans has completed its adaptation","B":"Re-emergence, since bats have carried the virus long before these cases appeared","C":"Eradication, since the contacts were spared and the chain of transmission has ended"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Spillover ≠ sustained transmission. The virus must still adapt and spread human to human.","E":"A pandemic needs global human-to-human spread.","D":"Infecting a human is spillover; adaptation is what enables onward spread.","B":"Re-emergence means a known human disease returning.","C":"The reservoir persists."},"t":"Order of emergence","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L16_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Order of emergence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Drivers of viral emergence","s":"An outbreak review lists five contributing circumstances: a reservoir dam that flooded rodent habitat, a fourfold rise in the settlement's population density over ten years, a virus whose polymerase makes about one error every 10,000 bases, a live-animal market trading wild species, and a district office that took six weeks to report the first cluster. Which of these is a host factor in the lecture's driver framework?","lec":"L16","sl":8,"c":"<b>Drivers of viral emergence</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The error rate of the virus's own polymerase","B":"The six-week delay in reporting the first cluster","C":"The flooding of rodent habitat behind the new dam","D":"The market trading wild animal species","E":"The rise in the settlement's population density"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"A viral factor: high mutation rate, recombination and reassortment, genetic adaptation, immune escape. The 1 in 10,000 figure is influenza's drift error rate from slide 18.","B":"A public-health factor: inadequate surveillance, low vaccination coverage, delayed outbreak recognition.","C":"An environmental factor: climate change, deforestation, habitat disruption and changes in vector distribution.","D":"A human factor: international travel, urbanisation, animal farming, wildlife trade, population displacement. This is the category students most often mistake for a host factor.","E":"Correct. Slide 8's host factors are population immunity, immunosuppression, human susceptibility and increased population density. Host factors describe the people, not the virus, the environment or the system."},"img":"slides_i/L16_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which of the following is classified as a HOST factor for viral emergence?","o":{"E":"Immunosuppression in the population","B":"Wildlife trade","D":"Deforestation","C":"High viral mutation rate","A":"Delayed outbreak recognition"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Host factors: population immunity, immunosuppression, susceptibility, density (and an ageing population).","B":"A HUMAN (behavioural) factor.","D":"An ENVIRONMENTAL factor.","C":"A VIRAL factor.","A":"A PUBLIC-HEALTH factor."},"t":"Driver categories","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L16_8.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Driver categories</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Why RNA viruses dominate emergence","s":"Every virus covered in this lecture has an RNA genome, and the lecturer used that to explain why RNA viruses supply most emerging human pathogens. Which property is the explanation she gave?","lec":"L16","sl":5,"c":"<b>Why RNA viruses dominate emergence</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"They encode only a single polyprotein, which antibody cannot recognise","B":"Their particles are naked and therefore survive in the environment","C":"Their genomes are integrated into the host chromosome and persist","D":"Their polymerases copy the genome without proofreading","E":"They replicate in the nucleus, where host enzymes correct their errors"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A single polyprotein is a picornavirus and flavivirus strategy, and it has nothing to do with antibody recognition or with emergence.","B":"Every virus in this lecture is enveloped, which makes them less, not more, robust outside the host.","C":"Integration is a retroviral property (HIV, HTLV-1). None of the eight viruses in this lecture integrates.","D":"Correct. Slide 17 makes the point for influenza and it generalises: an RNA polymerase lacking proofreading gives a high error rate, so the population of progeny contains variants, and a few of those may bind a new species' receptor or escape immunity. Rapid trial and error is what crosses a species barrier.","E":"Influenza does transcribe in the nucleus, but host repair enzymes do not proofread viral RNA; this reverses the actual mechanism."},"img":"slides_i/L16_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Every virus in this lecture is an RNA virus. What is the main reason RNA viruses dominate emerging infections?","o":{"D":"Their RNA polymerases lack proofreading, so high mutation rates let them adapt quickly to new hosts","E":"RNA viruses are always larger than DNA viruses, so they carry more host-range genes","C":"RNA viruses cannot infect animals, so every new RNA virus in humans must be emerging","B":"RNA viruses integrate into the host genome, so they persist and re-emerge repeatedly","A":"RNA viruses resist all immune responses, so population immunity never limits them"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Rapid trial and error supplies variants able to cross the species barrier; segmented ones can also reassort.","E":"Size is not the reason.","C":"Most emerging RNA viruses come from animals.","B":"Only retroviruses integrate.","A":"Not true."},"t":"Why RNA viruses emerge","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L16_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Why RNA viruses emerge</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Why only influenza A causes pandemics","s":"Influenza B circulates every winter and causes seasonal epidemics, but the lecture states that only influenza A has ever caused a pandemic. Which difference between the two types accounts for that?","lec":"L16","sl":14,"c":"<b>Why only influenza A causes pandemics</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Influenza B is copied by a polymerase that has proofreading activity","B":"Influenza B undergoes antigenic shift but never antigenic drift","C":"Influenza B has an unsegmented genome, so its genes cannot be exchanged","D":"Influenza A infects many animal species, influenza B essentially only humans","E":"Influenza A alone carries a neuraminidase on the surface of the virion"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"No influenza polymerase proofreads. That is why both types drift and the vaccine is reformulated annually.","B":"Exactly reversed, and impossible for influenza B: drift by point mutation is what it does, and shift is what it cannot do.","C":"Both types are segmented orthomyxoviruses. Segmentation is not the difference; the host range that supplies foreign segments is.","D":"Correct. Slides 14 and 15 give influenza A a broad animal host range with wild aquatic birds as the major reservoir. That animal pool is what supplies an H or N segment the human population has never met; without a second species there is no foreign segment to reassort in, so influenza B can drift but cannot shift into a novel subtype.","E":"Both types carry haemagglutinin and neuraminidase; only influenza A is divided into H and N subtypes (H1-H18, N1-N11)."},"img":"slides_i/L16_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why can influenza A cause pandemics while influenza B does not?","o":{"D":"Influenza A has a broad animal host range (aquatic birds, pigs), which supplies new H and N segments for reassortment","C":"Influenza A is a DNA virus, so it recombines with host DNA to form new subtypes","E":"Influenza B has no neuraminidase, so its progeny cannot be released from infected cells","B":"Influenza A cannot undergo antigenic drift, so any change in it must be a major shift","A":"Influenza B is not segmented whereas A is, so only A can swap gene segments"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Reservoir = wild aquatic birds. Mixing animal and human strains produces a novel subtype nobody is immune to.","C":"Both are (−)ssRNA orthomyxoviruses.","E":"Both have HA and NA.","B":"A undergoes both drift and shift.","A":"Both are segmented; the difference is the host range that supplies foreign segments."},"t":"Why only influenza A is pandemic","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L16_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Why only influenza A is pandemic</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Drift vs shift: mechanism and scale","s":"Two influenza events are compared. In March, a circulating H3N2 strain with a handful of amino-acid changes in its haemagglutinin causes a winter epidemic confined to one hemisphere. In June, a virus carrying a haemagglutinin segment of avian origin together with seven segments of a human strain spreads across four continents in three months. Which statement about the two events is correct?","lec":"L16","sl":18,"c":"<b>Drift vs shift: mechanism and scale</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The June virus arose by recombination within one continuous RNA molecule, unlike the March virus","B":"The June virus arose by reassortment, which can create a subtype nobody is immune to","C":"Both viruses arose by point mutation, differing only in how many changes accumulated","D":"Both viruses arose by reassortment, and the different scale reflects differing vaccine coverage","E":"The March virus arose by reassortment and the June virus by point mutation in its H gene"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Recombination joins sequence within a molecule and is not how influenza generates new subtypes. Influenza has eight separate segments, so the mechanism available to it is reassortment.","B":"Correct. Segments from two different parent viruses in one particle is reassortment, which is antigenic shift (slide 19): it generates a new H or N subtype, the population has no immunity to it, and the result is a large-scale outbreak or pandemic.","C":"The March event is point mutation, which is drift: minor antigenic change, insufficient to create a new subtype, outbreaks but not on a wide scale (slide 18). The June event is not point mutation at all.","D":"The March virus did not reassort, and the difference in scale comes from absent population immunity to a novel subtype, not from vaccine uptake.","E":"The two mechanisms are swapped. A handful of changes within one gene is drift; whole segments from two viruses is shift."},"img":"slides_i/L16_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A new influenza A subtype carrying an H segment never before seen in humans spreads worldwide within months. Which process produced it, and why did it spread so widely?","o":{"B":"Antigenic shift by reassortment — the population had no immunity to the new H","C":"Antigenic drift by point mutation — minor changes evade partial immunity","D":"Antigenic drift — drift always creates new subtypes","E":"Antigenic shift by point mutation in NA","A":"Recombination within a single unsegmented genome"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Shift = new H and/or N = pandemic potential. The definitions are in L10; here, know the epidemiological result.","C":"Drift explains SEASONAL epidemics, not a novel-subtype pandemic.","D":"Drift is 'not sufficient to generate new H and N subtypes'.","E":"Shift is reassortment of whole segments, not point mutation.","A":"Influenza is segmented; the mechanism is reassortment."},"t":"Drift vs shift outcome","sl":18,"img":"slides_i/L16_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Drift vs shift outcome</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Neuraminidase: function and subtypes","s":"An antiviral drug binds the active site of the influenza surface enzyme that cleaves sialic acid. What happens to the infected cell's progeny virions, and how many subtypes of that surface protein are recognised in influenza A?","lec":"L16","sl":16,"c":"<b>Neuraminidase: function and subtypes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"They cannot fuse their envelope with the endosome; 11 subtypes","B":"They are assembled but never uncoat on entry; 2 subtypes","C":"They remain bound to the cell surface; 11 subtypes","D":"They fail to transcribe their messenger RNA; 8 subtypes","E":"They cannot attach to respiratory epithelium; 18 subtypes"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Membrane fusion is mediated by haemagglutinin after endosomal acidification. The subtype count here is right for neuraminidase, which is why the number alone cannot settle this item.","B":"Uncoating is blocked by amantadine through the M2 ion channel, and influenza A is not divided into two subtypes of anything.","C":"Correct. The sialic-acid-cleaving enzyme is neuraminidase, the target of oseltamivir. Haemagglutinin binds the cell's sialic acid, so without neuraminidase the new virions stay tethered to the cell that made them and cannot spread. Slide 16 gives N1-N11, eleven subtypes.","D":"Transcription is the target of the endonuclease inhibitor baloxavir. Eight is the number of genome segments, not of a surface protein.","E":"Attachment is haemagglutinin's job, and there are 18 H subtypes (H1-H18). This option describes blocking the wrong protein."},"img":"slides_i/L16_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"How many haemagglutinin and neuraminidase subtypes of influenza A are recognised, and what does each protein do?","o":{"C":"18 H (attachment to sialic-acid receptors) and 11 N (release of new virions)","A":"11 H (release) and 18 N (attachment)","D":"3 H and 2 N, both for attachment","B":"16 H and 9 N, both for uncoating","E":"Subtypes are defined by the M2 protein"},"r":{"C":"Correct. H binds; N releases, which is why oseltamivir (an N inhibitor) blocks exit.","A":"The numbers and functions are swapped.","D":"Wrong numbers.","B":"Wrong numbers and function.","E":"Subtype = H + N."},"t":"H and N","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L16_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>H and N</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Influenza pandemic timeline","s":"The lecture's timeline of influenza A outbreaks shows each pandemic subtype replacing the one circulating before it. Which statement about that sequence is correct?","lec":"L16","sl":21,"c":"<b>Influenza pandemic timeline</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The 1957 Asian pandemic virus (H2N2) was replaced in 1968 by H3N2","B":"The 1968 Hong Kong pandemic virus (H1N1) replaced the H2N2 of 1957","C":"The 1918 Spanish pandemic virus (H2N2) circulated until 1957","D":"The 1977 Children's pandemic virus (H5N1) co-circulated with H3N2","E":"The 2009 pandemic virus (H3N2) replaced the H1N1 circulating from 1977"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 21 labels the 1957 Asian pandemic H2N2 and marks 'replacement of H2N2 with H3N2' at the 1968 Hong Kong pandemic.","B":"Hong Kong 1968 was H3N2. H1N1 is the 1918 and 2009 subtype.","C":"The 1918 Spanish pandemic was H1N1, with 50-100 million deaths; H1N1 then circulated until the 1957 replacement.","D":"The 1977 Children's pandemic was H1N1. H5N1 appears on the timeline in 1997 as avian influenza, not as a pandemic subtype.","E":"The 2009 pandemic virus was a swine-origin H1N1, shown on the slide as 'replacement of H1N1/77 with H1N1/09'."},"img":"slides_i/L16_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of influenza pandemic and subtype is correct?","o":{"A":"1918 Spanish flu — H1N1, the deadliest (50–100 million deaths)","E":"1968 Hong Kong flu — H1N1","B":"1957 Asian flu — H5N1","D":"2009 pandemic — H3N2","C":"1918 Spanish flu — H2N2"},"r":{"A":"Correct. She stressed Spanish flu, H1N1, as the one with the most deaths.","E":"Hong Kong 1968 = H3N2.","B":"Asian 1957 = H2N2.","D":"2009 = H1N1pdm (swine origin).","C":"1918 = H1N1."},"t":"Pandemic history","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L16_21.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Pandemic history</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Influenza management in a high-risk patient","s":"A 70-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes attends 24 hours after the onset of fever, myalgia and cough during the peak of the influenza season. A rapid antigen test performed at the counter is negative. Which course of action does the lecture support?","lec":"L16","sl":22,"c":"<b>Influenza management in a high-risk patient</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Send a nasopharyngeal swab for viral culture and treat once it grows","B":"Withhold antivirals and repeat the rapid antigen test in 48 hours","C":"Start oseltamivir now and send a nasopharyngeal swab for RT-PCR","D":"Start oseltamivir only if the molecular test is reported as positive","E":"Withhold antivirals because fewer than 48 hours of symptoms have passed"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Culture takes days and is not the preferred test; RT-PCR is. Treatment would be useless by the time it read out.","B":"Delays treatment past the window on the strength of the least sensitive test available.","C":"Correct. Slide 22 carries both rules this item needs: the rapid antigen test is fast but less sensitive, so a negative result in a symptomatic patient does not exclude influenza, and treatment must not be delayed while awaiting a test in high-risk or severe cases. Age and diabetes make her high-risk, and 24 hours is well inside the window.","D":"This is the delay the slide explicitly warns against for high-risk patients.","E":"Reverses the timing rule. Oseltamivir should be started as soon as possible and ideally within 48 hours, so 24 hours is the ideal moment rather than a reason to wait."},"img":"slides_i/L16_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 70-year-old with diabetes has had fever and cough for 24 h during flu season. A rapid influenza antigen test is negative. What is the best management?","o":{"C":"Start oseltamivir now and confirm by RT-PCR — the rapid antigen test is less sensitive","E":"Exclude influenza, because a negative rapid antigen test rules the diagnosis out","B":"Wait for viral culture before any treatment, because culture is the gold standard","A":"Start amantadine, because it is the first-line drug for influenza A in the elderly","D":"Give oseltamivir only after 72 h of symptoms, once the diagnosis is certain"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Two slide rules: rapid Ag is less sensitive, and do not delay treatment while awaiting tests in high-risk/severe cases.","E":"A negative rapid test does not rule flu out.","B":"Culture is slow and not needed to treat.","A":"Amantadine faces widespread resistance (L18).","D":"Start ASAP, ideally within 48 h."},"t":"Influenza diagnosis","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L16_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Influenza diagnosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"SARS-CoV zoonotic chain","s":"A betacoronavirus recognised in 2002-2003 caused severe lower respiratory disease, spread efficiently between people, produced major healthcare-associated outbreaks and killed roughly one in ten of the 8,098 people it infected. Bats were its ultimate source. Which animal did the lecture name as the intermediate host that carried it to humans?","lec":"L16","sl":27,"c":"<b>SARS-CoV zoonotic chain</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The field rodent","B":"The fruit bat","C":"The domestic pig","D":"The dromedary camel","E":"The civet"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Rodents are the reservoir of the hantaviruses, which cause renal or pulmonary syndromes rather than the picture described.","B":"Fruit bats are a reservoir, not an intermediate host, and the stem has already told you bats were the ultimate source. The question asks what stood between the bat and the human.","C":"Pigs are the amplifying host for Nipah virus and the classic mixing vessel for influenza reassortment, not for SARS-CoV.","D":"Dromedary camels are the natural reservoir of MERS-CoV, recognised a decade later in the Middle East.","E":"Correct. The epidemiology identifies SARS-CoV, and slide 28 gives its chain as bats to civets (chamot in Thai) to humans, followed by droplet, close-contact, fomite and healthcare-associated spread."},"img":"slides_i/L16_27.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"During the 2002–2003 SARS outbreak, which animal served as the intermediate host between bats and humans?","o":{"E":"Civet (ชะมด)","B":"Dromedary camel","A":"Pig","C":"Rodent","D":"Tick"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Bats (reservoir) → civets → humans, followed by healthcare-associated spread.","B":"Camel → MERS-CoV.","A":"Pig → Nipah (and swine flu).","C":"Rodent → hantavirus.","D":"Tick → SFTS."},"t":"SARS-CoV chain","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L16_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>SARS-CoV chain</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Coronavirus genome polarity","s":"One virus family in this lecture has a genome that host ribosomes can translate directly once the particle has uncoated, with no need for a polymerase carried inside the virion to make a readable copy first. Which of these viruses is it?","lec":"L16","sl":24,"c":"<b>Coronavirus genome polarity</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Influenza A virus","B":"SFTS virus","C":"MERS coronavirus","D":"Hantavirus","E":"Nipah virus"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"An enveloped, segmented ss(-)RNA orthomyxovirus; it must carry its own polymerase into the cell to transcribe positive-sense copies.","B":"An enveloped segmented ss(-)RNA phenuivirus (Dabie bandavirus). Along with influenza and hantavirus it is negative-sense and segmented.","C":"Correct. Slide 24 gives the coronaviruses an enveloped ss(+)RNA genome. Positive-sense RNA reads as messenger RNA, so the incoming genome is translated at once and the replicase is made from it. The three coronaviruses are the only positive-sense viruses in this lecture.","D":"An enveloped tri-segmented ss(-)RNA hantavirus.","E":"An enveloped ss(-)RNA paramyxovirus, so the same constraint applies."},"img":"slides_i/L16_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which virus in this lecture has a POSITIVE-sense RNA genome?","o":{"C":"SARS-CoV-2","B":"Influenza A","D":"Ebola virus","E":"Nipah virus","A":"SFTS virus"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Coronaviruses are the only (+)RNA viruses here, and the genome acts directly as mRNA.","B":"(−)ssRNA, segmented.","D":"(−)ssRNA, a filovirus.","E":"(−)ssRNA, a paramyxovirus.","A":"(−)ssRNA, segmented, a phenuivirus."},"t":"Coronavirus genome","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L16_24.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Coronavirus genome</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Comparing the three epidemic coronaviruses","s":"The lecture puts the three epidemic coronaviruses side by side with their year of first report, their case numbers and their deaths. Which statement comparing them is correct?","lec":"L16","sl":25,"c":"<b>Comparing the three epidemic coronaviruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"MERS-CoV was first reported in 2002, ten years before SARS-CoV was","B":"SARS-CoV-2 killed a larger proportion of those it infected than MERS-CoV","C":"SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV were both first reported in the Middle East","D":"SARS-CoV infected more people worldwide than SARS-CoV-2 ever did","E":"MERS-CoV killed a larger proportion of those it infected than SARS-CoV-2"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The years are swapped: SARS-CoV 2002, MERS-CoV 2012, SARS-CoV-2 2019.","B":"Reverses it. SARS-CoV-2 caused by far the most deaths precisely because it spread so widely, including from mild and presymptomatic cases, while its case-fatality is the lowest of the three.","C":"SARS-CoV emerged in southern China and SARS-CoV-2 in Wuhan; only MERS-CoV was first reported in the Middle East.","D":"SARS-CoV infected 8,098 people in 17 countries, a tiny fraction of the COVID-19 pandemic.","E":"Correct. Slide 25 gives MERS more than 2,500 cases with roughly 860 deaths, a case-fatality near 35%, against SARS-CoV-2's roughly 7 million deaths out of hundreds of millions infected, a far smaller proportion. Total deaths and case-fatality are different measures, and the lecture contrasts them deliberately."},"img":"slides_i/L16_25.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which statement comparing the epidemic coronaviruses is correct?","o":{"E":"MERS-CoV (first reported 2012) had the highest case-fatality, although SARS-CoV-2 caused by far the most deaths","B":"SARS-CoV was first reported in 2019 and MERS-CoV in 2002, both in East Asia","C":"MERS-CoV caused about 7 million deaths because camels spread it worldwide","D":"SARS-CoV-2 had the highest case-fatality as well as the most deaths of the three","A":"All three were first reported in the Middle East after contact with camels"},"r":{"E":"Correct. MERS ~860/2,500 (~35%) vs SARS ~10% (774/8,098). Case-fatality is not the same as total deaths.","B":"SARS = 2002–2003.","C":"~7 million is SARS-CoV-2.","D":"Lowest case-fatality, highest spread.","A":"SARS in China, MERS in the Middle East, SARS-CoV-2 in Wuhan."},"t":"The three epidemic coronaviruses","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L16_25.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>The three epidemic coronaviruses</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Antigen test vs RT-PCR","s":"A man with three days of fever and cough whose wife has laboratory-confirmed COVID-19 has a negative lateral-flow antigen test. The clinic sends a second sample for the test the lecture calls highly sensitive. Which property of that test explains why it may be positive when the strip was not?","lec":"L16","sl":38,"c":"<b>Antigen test vs RT-PCR</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It amplifies viral nucleic acid, so very little is needed","B":"It detects viral protein bound to a labelled antibody on a strip","C":"It detects antibody, which appears earlier than viral protein does","D":"It recovers infectious virus in cell culture within a few hours","E":"It compares IgG titres in paired sera taken two weeks apart"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 38 pairs RT-PCR with high sensitivity and the antigen test with speed and lower sensitivity. Amplification is the reason: PCR copies a scarce target until it is detectable, while a strip needs enough viral protein present in the sample to develop a visible line.","B":"That is the antigen test itself, the one that was already negative.","C":"Antibody appears days after infection and after viral protein, so serology is useless for confirming an acute case; the lecture uses serology for retrospective or convalescent diagnosis.","D":"Culture takes days, not hours, needs containment, and is not part of the routine COVID-19 algorithm.","E":"Paired sera 7-14 days apart with a fourfold rise is the serological principle, which answers a different question and far too late."},"img":"slides_i/L16_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A symptomatic household contact of a COVID-19 case has a negative antigen test (ATK). What is the best interpretation?","o":{"A":"The antigen test is less sensitive than RT-PCR, so a negative ATK does not exclude infection — repeat or confirm by RT-PCR","D":"COVID-19 is excluded","C":"The antigen test is more sensitive than PCR, so the result is definitive","E":"Only a viral culture can diagnose COVID-19","B":"A negative ATK proves immunity"},"r":{"A":"Correct. PCR amplifies (sensitivity); the strip needs enough protein (speed).","D":"A false negative is plausible.","C":"The opposite is true.","E":"Culture is not routine.","B":"Meaningless."},"t":"SARS-CoV-2 testing","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L16_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>SARS-CoV-2 testing</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Variants and vaccine efficacy","s":"Serum from people vaccinated against SARS-CoV-2 neutralises a variant carrying 32 changes in one structural protein far less well than it neutralises the original strain, and reinfections rise. Which explanation does the lecture give?","lec":"L16","sl":40,"c":"<b>Variants and vaccine efficacy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The nucleocapsid is the principal target of neutralising antibody","B":"The changes lie in the protein those antibodies bind","C":"The variant replicates in cells that circulating antibody cannot enter","D":"The variant has shed the envelope that the antibodies would have bound","E":"The polymerase has mutated so that antibodies no longer inhibit it"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The nucleocapsid protein is abundant and immunogenic but internal, so anti-N antibody does not neutralise. Spike is the neutralising target.","B":"Correct. The variant with 32 spike mutations is Omicron. Slides 40 and 41 show that mutations accumulating in the spike glycoprotein, the target of neutralising antibody and of every vaccine platform used, reduce antibody binding: antibody escape, hence reinfection risk and lower vaccine efficacy.","C":"Neutralising antibody works outside the cell, by blocking attachment and entry; intracellular access is not the issue.","D":"Coronaviruses are enveloped throughout their life cycle and cannot discard the envelope.","E":"The polymerase is inside the particle and is the target of remdesivir and molnupiravir, not of antibody. Keep this separate from the attenuating mutation that made Omicron clinically milder."},"img":"slides_i/L16_40.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why did new SARS-CoV-2 variants reduce vaccine efficacy?","o":{"B":"Mutations accumulated in the spike protein, the main target of neutralising antibody, allowing antibody escape","E":"The variants lost their envelope, so vaccine-induced antibody had nothing to bind","A":"The variants switched to a DNA genome, which the mRNA vaccines could not match","D":"Mutations in the N protein blocked PCR detection, so infections were missed","C":"The vaccines target the viral RdRp, which mutated and escaped the antibody"},"r":{"B":"Correct: from her group's own sequencing across the Vajira waves. Keep separate: attenuating mutation (Omicron milder, L10) vs spike escape.","E":"Not a mechanism.","A":"Impossible.","D":"Not the point of the slide.","C":"Vaccines target spike, not RdRp."},"t":"Variants and spike","sl":39,"img":"slides_i/L16_39.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Variants and spike</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Ebola exposure and route","s":"In an eastern province of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, three sisters who washed, dressed and carried the body of a brother who had died of an undiagnosed illness fall ill ten days later with fever, vomiting and bleeding gums. Which virus and route of infection fit best?","lec":"L16","sl":43,"c":"<b>Ebola exposure and route</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hantavirus, from inhaled aerosols of dried rodent excreta","B":"Nipah virus, from food contaminated with fruit-bat secretions","C":"SFTS virus, from fluid released by a tick crushed in the fingers","D":"MERS coronavirus, from close contact with secretions of camels","E":"Ebola virus, from contact with blood and body fluids"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Hantavirus needs rodent exposure such as sweeping an infested building, causes a renal or pulmonary syndrome, and spreads person-to-person only uncommonly.","B":"Nipah follows fruit bats, pigs or contaminated food and presents with encephalitis with or without respiratory disease.","C":"SFTS follows a tick bite or handling of an infected tick, with fever, leukopenia and thrombocytopenia, and is reported from East Asia and Thailand rather than central Africa.","D":"MERS follows camel contact in the Middle East and presents as pneumonia progressing to ARDS, not as a bleeding illness.","E":"Correct. Slide 44 names unsafe burial practices as one of the three high-risk situations, alongside caring for patients and healthcare exposure, because viral load is at its highest late in illness and at death. Ten days sits inside the 2-21 day incubation, and the DRC is the site of the 2026 outbreak on her map."},"img":"slides_i/L16_43.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Three relatives fall ill 10 days after washing and preparing the body of a man who died of a haemorrhagic fever in the DRC. Which virus and route are most likely?","o":{"E":"Ebola virus — direct contact with blood and body fluids during unsafe burial","B":"Hantavirus — inhalation of rodent excreta","C":"Dengue virus — Aedes bites at the funeral","A":"MERS-CoV — contact with camels","D":"Influenza — respiratory droplets from the corpse"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Viral load peaks late and at death; unsafe burial is a listed high-risk situation. DRC/Uganda 2026 = re-emergence.","B":"Rodent exposure, and person-to-person spread is uncommon.","C":"Vector-borne, not body-fluid.","A":"Wrong exposure and region.","D":"Not a haemorrhagic fever."},"t":"Ebola","sl":43,"img":"slides_i/L16_43.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Ebola</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Ebola laboratory picture","s":"A nurse who worked in an Ebola treatment unit is admitted on day 9 of illness with vomiting and profuse watery stools, and on day 11 she oozes from venepuncture sites. Which combination of laboratory findings does the lecture list for this disease?","lec":"L16","sl":48,"c":"<b>Ebola laboratory picture</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Low white cells, raised liver enzymes, normal platelet count","B":"Low platelets, raised liver enzymes, prolonged clotting time","C":"Normal platelets, raised bilirubin, prolonged clotting time","D":"High white cells, raised creatinine, normal liver enzymes","E":"Low platelets, rising haematocrit, normal clotting time"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Leukopenia with raised transaminases and a normal platelet count fits nothing here; SFTS lowers the platelets as well as the white cells.","B":"Correct. Slide 49's three other laboratory findings are thrombocytopenia, elevated liver enzymes and prolonged coagulation time. Low platelets plus a coagulopathy is why she bleeds from puncture sites.","C":"Ebola does cause hepatic dysfunction, but the lecture's listed triad starts with thrombocytopenia, and normal platelets would not explain the bleeding.","D":"A neutrophil leucocytosis with renal failure and normal liver enzymes points away from a viral haemorrhagic fever altogether.","E":"A rising haematocrit with preserved clotting is the dengue picture: plasma leakage rather than consumption of clotting factors."},"img":"slides_i/L16_48.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which laboratory picture fits Ebola virus disease?","o":{"B":"Thrombocytopenia, elevated liver enzymes and a prolonged coagulation time","E":"Rising haematocrit with normal platelets","D":"Isolated eosinophilia","C":"Heterophile-positive lymphocytosis","A":"Normal coagulation with hypercalcaemia"},"r":{"B":"Correct: the three findings on slide 49. They explain the bleeding.","E":"A rising haematocrit is the dengue plasma-leak marker.","D":"Eosinophilia points to helminths.","C":"That is EBV mono.","A":"Not described."},"t":"Ebola — labs and incubation","sl":48,"img":"slides_i/L16_48.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Ebola — labs and incubation</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Hantavirus prevention","s":"A 46-year-old farmer spent a morning sweeping out a grain store heavily soiled with rat droppings. Two weeks later he has fever, oliguria, a creatinine of 4.2 mg/dL and a platelet count of 70,000. The district team must advise the other workers. Which measure does the lecture give for preventing this infection?","lec":"L16","sl":52,"c":"<b>Hantavirus prevention</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Applying insect repellent to exposed skin during field work","B":"Boiling all drinking water drawn from the farm's storage tank","C":"Wearing gloves when handling raw milk and animal carcasses","D":"Wetting soiled surfaces before they are cleaned","E":"Sleeping under an insecticide-treated net during the harvest"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Repellent protects against biting arthropods: relevant to SFTS, dengue and Japanese encephalitis, useless against inhaled dust.","B":"Hantavirus is not waterborne. Boiling water would address enteric pathogens instead.","C":"Raw animal products are the MERS exposure to avoid, and this virus is not acquired by handling milk.","D":"Correct. Fever with acute kidney injury and thrombocytopenia after rodent exposure is haemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome. Humans are infected by inhaling aerosolised rodent urine, faeces and saliva, so slide 55's prevention is rodent control with safe cleaning procedures that avoid aerosolising excreta. Damping down before sweeping is exactly that.","E":"A bed net addresses night-biting mosquitoes, which have no part in hantavirus transmission."},"img":"slides_i/L16_52.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A farmer develops fever and acute kidney injury with thrombocytopenia two weeks after sweeping out a rat-infested grain store. Which virus, and which prevention?","o":{"D":"Hantavirus (HFRS) — rodent control and wet, non-aerosolising cleaning","C":"SFTS virus — tick repellent","B":"Nipah virus — avoid fruit-bat contact","A":"Dengue — mosquito nets","E":"Ebola — avoid unsafe burials"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Aerosolised rodent excreta; renal syndrome + thrombocytopenia = HFRS (Old World, Europe/Asia).","C":"Ticks, not rodents.","B":"Bats and pigs, with encephalitis.","A":"Mosquito-borne; no renal syndrome picture.","E":"Body fluids."},"t":"Hantavirus exposure","sl":51,"img":"slides_i/L16_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Hantavirus exposure</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"HFRS vs HPS","s":"Eight passengers who boarded an expedition ship in Ushuaia, Argentina develop fever and myalgia followed by rapidly progressive breathlessness; chest imaging shows pulmonary oedema and three of them die. A hantavirus is confirmed. Which syndrome is this, and which group of hantaviruses causes it?","lec":"L16","sl":51,"c":"<b>HFRS vs HPS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Haemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome, an Old World hantavirus","B":"Severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome, a tick-borne bandavirus","C":"Hantavirus pulmonary syndrome, a New World hantavirus","D":"Hantavirus pulmonary syndrome, an Old World hantavirus","E":"Haemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome, a New World hantavirus"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The pairing of syndrome and group is correct here, but the patients have lungs full of oedema, not acute kidney injury, and the source is South America rather than Europe or Asia.","B":"SFTS is a tick-borne phenuivirus of East Asia with fever, leukopenia and thrombocytopenia; the stem states a hantavirus was confirmed.","C":"Correct. Slide 51 assigns hantavirus pulmonary syndrome to the New World hantaviruses, and slide 53 places it in the Americas with respiratory failure and pulmonary oedema as its complication and typical finding. Ushuaia is in southern Argentina, so a New World virus fits the geography as well as the organ.","D":"This is the trap. The header of slide 53 prints HPS as Old World, which the lecturer corrected aloud in class as her own typing error. Slide 51 has it right: HPS is New World.","E":"Haemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome is the Old World disease, and nothing in this cluster is renal."},"img":"slides_i/L16_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing of hantavirus syndrome with region and organ is correct?","o":{"C":"HPS — New World (Americas) — lung: pulmonary oedema and respiratory failure","E":"HPS — Old World (Europe/Asia) — kidney","D":"HFRS — New World — lung","A":"HFRS — Americas — respiratory failure","B":"Both syndromes are defined by encephalitis"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Renal = Old World, Pulmonary = New World. The 2026 MV Hondius cruise-ship cluster (from Argentina) was pulmonary.","E":"⚠️ This is how slide 53's header reads. The lecturer said 'พี่เขียนผิด': HPS is NEW World.","D":"HFRS is Old World and renal.","A":"HFRS is renal.","B":"Neither is encephalitic; that is Nipah."},"t":"HFRS vs HPS","sl":51,"img":"slides_i/L16_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HFRS vs HPS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Nipah vs Japanese encephalitis","s":"On a Malaysian pig farm, eleven workers develop fever followed by confusion and seizures over three weeks; four also have cough and breathlessness, and a nurse who cared for two of them is admitted with the same illness. Japanese encephalitis is considered. Which feature of this outbreak argues most strongly against that diagnosis?","lec":"L16","sl":58,"c":"<b>Nipah vs Japanese encephalitis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The outbreak occurred in rural South-East Asia","B":"The nurse who cared for the patients fell ill","C":"Fever preceded the neurological signs by several days","D":"Several of the patients had seizures and altered consciousness","E":"The affected workers were in daily close contact with pigs"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Both circulate in this region; Japanese encephalitis is endemic across rural South-East Asia.","B":"Correct. Japanese encephalitis reaches people only through a Culex mosquito, and humans are dead-end hosts whose viraemia is too low to infect anyone else, so a carer cannot catch it from a patient. Slide 58 lists human-to-human spread through blood and secretions as one of Nipah's four routes, which is what a secondary case in a nurse indicates.","C":"A febrile prodrome before neurological signs is typical of both. The respiratory involvement in four workers is a second pointer to Nipah, but the secondary case in a carer is the decisive one.","D":"Encephalitis is the shared presentation of both viruses and cannot separate them.","E":"Pigs feature in both: they are the amplifying host for Japanese encephalitis as well as the source in the Malaysian Nipah outbreak, so this discriminates nothing."},"img":"slides_i/L16_58.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Pig farmers in Malaysia develop fever followed by confusion and seizures; some also have cough and dyspnoea, and a nurse caring for them falls ill. Which virus best fits?","o":{"B":"Nipah virus","C":"Japanese encephalitis virus","E":"West Nile virus","D":"Hantavirus","A":"Rabies virus"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Pigs + encephalitis + RESPIRATORY involvement + human-to-human spread = Nipah (first found in Malaysia, 1998).","C":"JE also involves pigs, but through Culex mosquitoes: humans are dead-end hosts, with no person-to-person spread and no respiratory picture. JE has a vaccine.","E":"West Nile is birds and Culex.","D":"Hantavirus is renal/pulmonary, not encephalitis.","A":"Rabies comes from an animal bite."},"t":"Nipah vs JE","sl":57,"img":"slides_i/L16_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Nipah vs JE</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Nipah transmission routes","s":"In a district of Bangladesh, several people who drank raw sap collected overnight in open clay pots tied to date-palm trunks develop encephalitis. Fruit bats are seen feeding at the pots at night. Which of the virus's listed routes of transmission does this outbreak illustrate?","lec":"L16","sl":58,"c":"<b>Nipah transmission routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Contaminated food reaching a human host","B":"An infected person transmitting through blood and secretions","C":"A tick transmitting after feeding on an infected animal","D":"An infected pig transmitting to a human handler","E":"A Culex mosquito transmitting from a viraemic pig"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 58 lists contaminated food to human as one of Nipah's four routes. Sap in an open pot is soiled overnight by the saliva and urine of feeding fruit bats, the natural reservoir, and drinking it unboiled delivers the virus. Food hygiene is on slide 60's prevention list for exactly this reason.","B":"A listed route, and the reason carers and nurses become secondary cases, but these people were infected before any of them was ill.","C":"Ticks transmit SFTS virus, which causes fever with thrombocytopenia rather than encephalitis.","D":"A listed route, and the mechanism of the 1998 Malaysian outbreak, but there are no pigs in this stem.","E":"Nipah is not vector-borne at all. Culex plus pigs is the Japanese encephalitis cycle."},"img":"slides_i/L16_58.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which is NOT a listed transmission route of Nipah virus?","o":{"A":"Aedes mosquito bite","E":"Contact with infected pigs","C":"Eating food contaminated by fruit-bat secretions","D":"Human-to-human through blood and secretions","B":"Direct contact with fruit bats"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Nipah is not vector-borne.","E":"A listed route: pigs are amplifying hosts.","C":"A listed route.","D":"A listed route.","B":"A listed route: fruit bats are the natural reservoir."},"t":"Nipah routes","sl":58,"img":"slides_i/L16_58.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Nipah routes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"SFTS identification","s":"A 58-year-old woman in north-eastern Thailand pulls engorged ticks off her dog with bare fingers each week. Nine days after the last time, she develops fever of 39.5 C, nausea and diarrhoea. Her white cell count is 2,100/uL, platelets 48,000/uL and alanine aminotransferase 180 U/L. Which virus is most likely?","lec":"L16","sl":62,"c":"<b>SFTS identification</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ebola virus","B":"Hantavirus","C":"Nipah virus","D":"Influenza A virus","E":"SFTS virus"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Ebola requires contact with the blood or body fluids of a case in an outbreak area in Africa, and its laboratory picture includes prolonged clotting rather than leukopenia.","B":"Hantavirus comes from aerosolised rodent excreta and causes a renal or a pulmonary syndrome; ticks play no part in it.","C":"Nipah comes from fruit bats, pigs or contaminated food and presents with encephalitis, often with respiratory disease.","D":"Influenza would not produce this degree of thrombocytopenia with raised transaminases, and there is no respiratory illness here.","E":"Correct. The exposure (ticks, and a dog as an amplifying host) and the laboratory triad of low white cells, low platelets and raised liver enzymes are severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome, caused by Dabie bandavirus. Nine days sits inside the 5-14 day incubation, and slide 62 records spread from China in 2009 to Korea, Japan and other Asian countries including Thailand."},"img":"slides_i/L16_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A Thai dog owner who crushes ticks off her pet with bare hands develops high fever, diarrhoea, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and raised liver enzymes. Which emerging virus is most likely?","o":{"E":"SFTS virus (Dabie bandavirus)","D":"Hantavirus","A":"Ebola virus","B":"Nipah virus","C":"MERS-CoV"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The name is the picture (fever + thrombocytopenia); ticks and pets; now reported in Thailand.","D":"Rodent excreta.","A":"Africa, body fluids.","B":"Bats/pigs, encephalitis.","C":"Camels, pneumonia."},"t":"SFTS","sl":62,"img":"slides_i/L16_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>SFTS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"SFTS differential diagnosis in Thailand","s":"A 40-year-old rice farmer in Loei has five days of fever, a platelet count of 60,000/uL and a white cell count of 2,800/uL. The team suspects the tick-borne phenuivirus that has recently been reported in Thailand, and sends blood for reverse-transcription PCR. Which set of illnesses does the lecture tell you to consider alongside it?","lec":"L16","sl":65,"c":"<b>SFTS differential diagnosis in Thailand</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Chikungunya, scrub typhus and typhoid fever","B":"Dengue, malaria and melioidosis","C":"Malaria, brucellosis and rickettsial spotted fever","D":"Leptospirosis, influenza and viral hepatitis A","E":"Dengue, chikungunya and leptospirosis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Scrub typhus and typhoid are plausible clinically and are not the three the slide names.","B":"Both malaria and melioidosis are real Thai causes of fever, but neither is on the lecture's list for this virus.","C":"A reasonable tropical fever list, but not the lecture's.","D":"Influenza and hepatitis A do not belong to the fever-with-low-platelets group the slide is separating.","E":"Correct. Slide 65 gives the Thai differential for SFTS as dengue, chikungunya, leptospirosis and other viral haemorrhagic or febrile illnesses. Each presents as fever with or without low platelets in a tropical setting; the exposure separates them, with ticks and pets pointing to SFTS, Aedes and an urban setting to dengue or chikungunya, and flood water or rice fields to leptospirosis."},"img":"slides_i/L16_65.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which set of diseases does the lecture list as differentials for SFTS in Thailand?","o":{"E":"Dengue, chikungunya and leptospirosis","C":"Measles, rubella and roseola","D":"Influenza, RSV and adenovirus","A":"HPV, HSV and molluscum","B":"Malaria only"},"r":{"E":"Correct: all fever ± low platelets in a tropical setting. Exposure separates them (tick vs Aedes vs flood water).","C":"Exanthems, not the listed differential.","D":"Respiratory viruses.","A":"STD viruses.","B":"Not the slide's list."},"t":"SFTS differential in Thailand","sl":65,"img":"slides_i/L16_65.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>SFTS differential in Thailand</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Fruit bats as reservoir","s":"A wildlife-health programme plans to sample fruit-bat colonies along a flight corridor in order to monitor the natural reservoir of emerging viruses. Using the lecture's summary of reservoirs, which two of its viruses would that sampling target?","lec":"L16","sl":43,"c":"<b>Fruit bats as reservoir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ebola virus and SFTS virus","B":"Nipah virus and Ebola virus","C":"SARS coronavirus and hantavirus","D":"MERS coronavirus and Nipah virus","E":"Nipah virus and hantavirus"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Ebola is right, but SFTS virus is maintained in ticks with dogs, cats and livestock as amplifying hosts.","B":"Correct. Slide 67 lists fruit bats as Nipah's reservoir and as Ebola's probable natural host, and slides 57 and 43 say the same. These are the two viruses in the lecture whose stated reservoir is the fruit bat.","C":"SARS-CoV traces back to bats through the civet, but the summary records its source as bats with an intermediate host rather than as fruit bats; and hantavirus is a rodent virus, which makes this pairing wrong in any reading.","D":"Nipah is right, but MERS-CoV's natural reservoir is the dromedary camel.","E":"Nipah is right, but hantaviruses live in rodents and reach people through aerosolised excreta."},"img":"slides_i/L16_43.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing of emerging virus and reservoir/source is INCORRECT?","o":{"B":"Hantavirus — fruit bats","E":"MERS-CoV — dromedary camels","A":"Nipah virus — fruit bats","C":"SFTS virus — ticks and animals","D":"Influenza A — wild aquatic birds"},"r":{"B":"Correct choice (it is the wrong pairing): hantavirus = RODENTS.","E":"Correct pairing.","A":"Correct pairing.","C":"Correct pairing.","D":"Correct pairing."},"t":"Exposure → virus","sl":67,"img":"slides_i/L16_67.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Exposure → virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Syndrome of the paramyxovirus","s":"A referral hospital admits five patients in one week: one with respiratory distress after working with camels, one with acute renal failure after clearing out a rat-infested barn, one with encephalitis after slaughtering pigs, one with a bleeding illness after attending a funeral, and one with fever and leukopenia after a tick bite. Which admission was caused by the paramyxovirus in this lecture?","lec":"L16","sl":57,"c":"<b>Syndrome of the paramyxovirus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The patient with encephalitis after slaughtering pigs","B":"The patient with respiratory distress after working with camels","C":"The patient with a bleeding illness after the funeral","D":"The patient with fever and leukopenia after the tick bite","E":"The patient with renal failure after clearing out the barn"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 57 places Nipah virus in the Paramyxoviridae, and slide 68 gives its major clinical syndrome as encephalitis with or without respiratory disease. Pigs are its amplifying host, so this is the admission to pick.","B":"Camel contact with respiratory distress is MERS-CoV, family Coronaviridae, giving severe pneumonia and ARDS.","C":"A bleeding illness after a funeral is Ebola virus, family Filoviridae, whose syndrome is viral haemorrhagic fever.","D":"A tick bite with fever and leukopenia is SFTS virus, family Phenuiviridae, giving fever with thrombocytopenia and multiorgan dysfunction.","E":"Rodent exposure with renal failure is hantavirus, family Hantaviridae, with a tri-segmented negative-sense genome."},"img":"slides_i/L16_57.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which emerging virus in this lecture is the one characterised by ENCEPHALITIS?","o":{"A":"Nipah virus","B":"MERS-CoV","E":"Hantavirus","D":"SFTS virus","C":"Influenza A"},"r":{"A":"Correct: encephalitis ± respiratory disease (slide 68).","B":"Severe pneumonia/ARDS.","E":"HPS/HFRS.","D":"Fever + thrombocytopenia + multiorgan dysfunction. Neurological signs occur in severe cases, but it is not the defining syndrome.","C":"Acute respiratory disease."},"t":"Syndrome → virus","sl":68,"img":"slides_i/L16_68.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Syndrome → virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Segmented genomes and reassortment","s":"A containment laboratory co-infects cell cultures with two distinct strains of each virus in this lecture and screens the progeny for particles that carry whole genes inherited from both parent strains. For which three viruses could such progeny be recovered?","lec":"L16","sl":15,"c":"<b>Segmented genomes and reassortment</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Hantavirus, SFTS virus and MERS coronavirus","B":"Ebola virus, Nipah virus and SFTS virus","C":"Influenza A, hantavirus and SFTS virus","D":"Influenza A, Nipah virus and Ebola virus","E":"Influenza A, hantavirus and SARS coronavirus"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Coronaviruses have one continuous positive-sense genome; they can recombine within a molecule but cannot swap segments.","B":"None of these three is segmented except SFTS virus.","C":"Correct. Exchanging whole genes between co-infecting strains is reassortment, and it requires a segmented genome. The three segmented viruses here are influenza A (slide 15), hantavirus, which is tri-segmented (slide 51), and SFTS virus (slide 62). Only influenza also has the broad multi-host range that turns reassortment into pandemic antigenic shift.","D":"Nipah and Ebola have single unsegmented negative-sense genomes, so neither can reassort.","E":"Influenza and hantavirus are segmented, but SARS-CoV is not."},"img":"slides_i/L16_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which set of viruses from this lecture all have SEGMENTED genomes?","o":{"C":"Influenza, hantavirus and SFTS virus","B":"Ebola, Nipah and SARS-CoV-2","E":"MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV and influenza","D":"Nipah, hantavirus and Ebola","A":"None of the viruses in the lecture are segmented"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Segmented = capable of reassortment. Only influenza has the broad multi-host mixing that makes shift a pandemic engine.","B":"All non-segmented.","E":"Coronaviruses are not segmented.","D":"Nipah and Ebola are not segmented.","A":"Three are."},"t":"Segmented genomes","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L16_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Segmented genomes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Treatment of MERS","s":"A 61-year-old man returns from two months working on a farm outside Riyadh with fever and breathlessness. RT-PCR on a lower respiratory specimen confirms the betacoronavirus first identified in 2012. Which treatment does the lecture give for this infection?","lec":"L16","sl":31,"c":"<b>Treatment of MERS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Remdesivir given for five days","B":"Nirmatrelvir combined with ritonavir","C":"Intensive supportive care alone","D":"Oseltamivir started within 48 hours","E":"Molnupiravir given for five days"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"An RNA-polymerase inhibitor listed for SARS-CoV-2, not for MERS.","B":"Listed on slide 38 for SARS-CoV-2 only. Ritonavir is there to inhibit CYP3A4 and boost nirmatrelvir.","C":"Correct. Slide 34 states that MERS treatment is supportive care and that there is no licensed specific antiviral therapy, and there is no vaccine. The lecturer's own rule is that a which-drug question about MERS, Nipah, hantavirus, SFTS or Ebola is a trick: supportive care is the answer.","D":"A neuraminidase inhibitor, so it is useless against a coronavirus; the 48-hour rule belongs to influenza.","E":"Also listed for SARS-CoV-2 only. Note the deliberate parallel: the lecture gives specific antivirals to influenza and SARS-CoV-2 and to no other virus in this lecture."},"img":"slides_i/L16_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient is confirmed to have MERS. Which treatment does the lecture recommend?","o":{"C":"Supportive care — there is no licensed specific antiviral","B":"Oseltamivir","A":"Acyclovir","D":"Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir, as for SARS-CoV-2","E":"Tecovirimat"},"r":{"C":"Correct (slide 34). A 'which drug?' stem for MERS, Nipah, hanta, SFTS or Ebola is a trick: the answer is supportive care.","B":"A neuraminidase inhibitor for influenza.","A":"For herpesviruses.","D":"Listed only for SARS-CoV-2.","E":"For orthopoxviruses."},"t":"Which have a specific treatment","sl":22,"img":"slides_i/L16_22.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Which have a specific treatment</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Incubation period and contact monitoring","s":"A health worker is splashed in the eye with blood while caring for a patient in a filovirus treatment unit. She is well and is entered into a monitoring programme. Over which range of days after exposure does the lecture say symptoms could appear?","lec":"L16","sl":48,"c":"<b>Incubation period and contact monitoring</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"7 to 28 days","B":"3 to 14 days","C":"2 to 10 days","D":"2 to 21 days","E":"5 to 14 days"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Not a figure given for any virus in this lecture; 28 days is the duration of HIV post-exposure prophylaxis, which belongs to L15.","B":"The incubation period of Nipah virus (slide 59).","C":"The incubation period of SARS-CoV (slide 29).","D":"Correct. Slide 48 gives Ebola an incubation period of 2 to 21 days, the longest in the lecture, which is why exposed contacts are followed for a full 21 days before they are cleared.","E":"The incubation period of SFTS virus (slide 64)."},"img":"slides_i/L16_48.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Which emerging virus has the LONGEST maximum incubation period, the basis of 21-day contact monitoring?","o":{"D":"Ebola virus","C":"SARS-CoV","A":"Nipah virus","E":"SFTS virus","B":"Influenza A"},"r":{"D":"Correct: 2–21 days.","C":"2–10 days.","A":"3–14 days.","E":"5–14 days.","B":"Short, a few days."},"t":"Incubation periods","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L16_29.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Incubation periods</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233361046},{"id":"I|L16|28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q74","t":"Camel → MERS","s":"A man develops severe pneumonia after close contact with camels. Which virus is most likely?","lec":"L16","sl":31,"c":"<b>Camel → MERS</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q74 — pneumonia after contact with camels; options Zika, hanta, MERS, SARS, Nipah.</span>","o":{"B":"MERS-CoV","C":"SARS-CoV","D":"Hantavirus","E":"Nipah virus","A":"Zika virus"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. The natural reservoir is the dromedary (one-humped) camel.","C":"Bats → civets.","D":"Rodents.","E":"Fruit bats, pigs.","A":"Aedes mosquitoes."},"img":"slides_i/L16_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L16|29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L16 s69","t":"Quiz — definition","s":"Which of the following best defines an emerging virus?","lec":"L16","sl":69,"c":"<b>Quiz — definition</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's own quiz, slide 69, answered in class (→ C): which best defines an emerging virus?</span>","o":{"A":"A virus newly appearing in a population or increasing in incidence","C":"A virus that only infects animals","B":"A virus that suddenly disappears from human populations","E":"A virus that cannot mutate","D":"A virus that only spreads through vectors"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct: 'newly appeared OR rapidly increasing in incidence or geographic range'.","C":"Emerging viruses infect humans, most after spillover.","B":"The opposite.","E":"Emerging viruses mutate readily.","D":"Vectors are one route among many."},"img":"slides_i/L16_69.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L16|30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L16 s70","t":"Quiz — filovirus","s":"A healthcare worker develops fever, diarrhoea and haemorrhage after caring for patients in an outbreak of viral haemorrhagic fever. The suspected virus is a filovirus. Which virus is most likely?","lec":"L16","sl":70,"c":"<b>Quiz — filovirus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's own quiz, slide 70, answered in class (→ Ebola).</span>","o":{"D":"Ebola virus","C":"Zika virus","A":"Dengue virus","B":"Hantavirus","E":"Influenza virus"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct: filovirus + healthcare worker (body fluids) + haemorrhagic fever.","C":"A flavivirus, not a haemorrhagic fever of caregivers.","A":"A flavivirus, mosquito-borne.","B":"A hantavirus, from rodents.","E":"An orthomyxovirus."},"img":"slides_i/L16_70.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L16|31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L16 s71","t":"Quiz — antigenic drift","s":"Antigenic drift in influenza viruses leads to:","lec":"L16","sl":71,"c":"<b>Quiz — antigenic drift</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's own quiz, slide 71, answered in class (→ A): antigenic drift leads to …</span>","o":{"B":"Small mutations causing minor antigenic changes","D":"Complete replacement of viral genome segments","E":"Elimination of viral replication","A":"Resistance to antiviral drugs only","C":"Development of entirely new subtypes"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Drift = point mutations = seasonal epidemics.","D":"Segment replacement is SHIFT.","E":"Not a result of drift.","A":"Drift changes antigenicity, not only drug susceptibility.","C":"New subtypes come from shift."},"img":"slides_i/L16_71.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L17|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"When to test at all","s":"A 22-year-old presents on day 3 of fever with a maculopapular rash. He is a sporadic case with no known contacts. The team cannot tell clinically whether this is measles, which would be managed at home with supportive care and vitamin A, or dengue, which would need admission to watch for plasma leakage and shock. Which of the lecture's indications for laboratory diagnosis does this case fit?","lec":"L17","sl":5,"c":"<b>When to test at all</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Management and prognosis depend on the correct diagnosis","B":"An epidemic spread of viral disease has to be traced","C":"Protective antibody has to be measured after recovery","D":"The clinical picture is typical of one particular virus","E":"A specific antiviral drug has to be selected for treatment"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 5 lists three indications: to treat with antivirals, an epidemic, and when treatment and prognosis depend on the correct diagnosis. Her own example was exactly this: fever + rash could be measles (supportive care) or dengue (watch for leakage and shock), so the answer changes management.","B":"A real indication, but the stem makes him a sporadic case with no contacts, so there is no epidemic to trace.","C":"Not one of the three indications; protective antibody (neutralisation test) is a research/vaccine question, not a reason to test an acutely ill patient.","D":"The opposite of an indication: a typical clinical picture is why most viral infections do NOT need lab confirmation (slide 5, first line).","E":"A real indication, but neither measles nor dengue has a specific antiviral in this course; the decision here is about admission and monitoring, not drug choice."},"img":"slides_i/L17_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","old":{"s":"Most viral infections are diagnosed clinically. In which situation is laboratory confirmation MOST justified?","o":{"A":"An immunocompromised patient with severe influenza-like illness who may need a specific antiviral","D":"A healthy adult with a 2-day common cold","C":"A child with a mild, self-limiting viral gastroenteritis that is improving","B":"Every patient with a fever, as a routine","E":"A patient who recovered from a cold last month"},"r":{"A":"Correct: 'to treat with antiviral drugs'. The other two reasons are an epidemic and a case where treatment or prognosis depends on the exact virus.","D":"The result would change nothing.","C":"Self-limiting, with supportive care only.","B":"Routine testing is not the principle.","E":"The episode is over."},"t":"When to test at all","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L17_5.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>When to test at all</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Correct time","s":"A 40-year-old with an influenza-like illness is first swabbed on day 12 of illness. Real-time RT-PCR of the nasopharyngeal swab, sent in VTM at 4 °C, is negative, yet his IgG titre later rises four-fold between paired sera. What is the most likely reason the RT-PCR missed the virus?","lec":"L17","sl":8,"c":"<b>Correct time</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The swab was sent in transport medium rather than in a dry tube","B":"The swab was taken after the peak of viral shedding had passed","C":"Real-time PCR is less sensitive than isolation in cell culture","D":"The swab was chilled at 4 °C rather than frozen before transport","E":"A nasopharyngeal swab is the wrong specimen for a respiratory virus"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"VTM is correct practice: it preserves infectivity, prevents drying and stops bacteria/fungi. A dry swab would be worse.","B":"Correct. 'Correct time' (slide 8) = the acute phase, when symptoms are clear and viral load peaks (2–3 days after onset, L11). By day 12 the virus has largely been cleared while antibody is rising, which is exactly what the four-fold IgG rise shows.","C":"Reversed: PCR has high sensitivity (slide 26); cell culture has poor sensitivity (slide 30).","D":"4 °C is the correct transport temperature; freezing is what destroys the virion (ice crystals).","E":"Throat/nasal swab and nasopharyngeal aspirate are the listed respiratory specimens (slide 9)."},"img":"slides_i/L17_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"'Correct time' is the first rule of specimen collection. For detecting the virus itself, what does it mean?","o":{"B":"Collect during the acute phase, when symptoms are clear and the viral load is at its peak","A":"Collect at 08:00 every morning","E":"Collect after recovery, when antibody is highest","D":"Collect during the incubation period, before symptoms","C":"Timing does not matter for PCR"},"r":{"B":"Correct: her own clarification ('not 8 o'clock'). Links to the L11 item on the best phase to test.","A":"She joked about exactly this misreading.","E":"Virus is cleared by then.","D":"Viral load is still low and symptoms are absent.","C":"Even PCR fails once the virus is gone."},"t":"Correct time","sl":8,"img":"slides_i/L17_8.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Correct time</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Specimen by site","s":"A 10-month-old is admitted in the cool season with profuse watery diarrhoea and vomiting. The ward wants a viral antigen test today, but the infant has not passed any stool since admission several hours ago. Which specimen best follows the lecture's collection rules?","lec":"L17","sl":9,"c":"<b>Specimen by site</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Clotted blood for serum","B":"Rectal swab","C":"Throat swab in VTM","D":"Catheter urine sample","E":"Nasopharyngeal aspirate"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Blood is for blood-borne infections and for antibody (indirect) testing, not for direct antigen detection of an enteric virus.","B":"Correct. Step 1: watery diarrhoea in an infant = viral gastroenteritis (e.g. rotavirus) → enteric specimen. Step 2: enteric = stool OR rectal swab (slide 9); she said the rectal swab is for when the patient cannot pass stool. Rule: collect from the likely site of infection (quiz slide 38).","C":"A respiratory specimen, whatever the medium it is sent in.","D":"Urine is the urinary-tract specimen (slide 9).","E":"Respiratory specimen; the virus is replicating in the gut."},"img":"slides_i/L17_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing of suspected infection and specimen is INCORRECT?","o":{"B":"Viral gastroenteritis — nasopharyngeal swab","D":"Viral meningitis — cerebrospinal fluid","A":"Vesicular rash — swab or scraping of the vesicle base","E":"Viral conjunctivitis — conjunctival swab","C":"Lower respiratory tract infection — sputum"},"r":{"B":"Correct choice (it is the wrong pairing). Enteric infection → stool, or a rectal swab if the patient cannot pass stool. Collect from the site of disease.","D":"Correct pairing.","A":"Correct pairing.","E":"Correct pairing.","C":"Correct pairing."},"t":"Specimen by site","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L17_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Specimen by site</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Transport temperature","s":"A nasopharyngeal swab for viral isolation was placed in VTM, shielded from light, and frozen overnight at −20 °C 'to keep it fresh' before transport. The cell culture shows no CPE, although the patient's paired sera later show a four-fold IgG rise. Which handling step most likely caused the negative culture?","lec":"L17","sl":10,"c":"<b>Transport temperature</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Placing the swab in VTM instead of sending it dry","B":"Shielding the specimen from light during transport","C":"Sampling the nasopharynx instead of drawing blood","D":"Freezing the specimen instead of holding it at 4 °C","E":"Using a medium that contains antibiotics and antifungals"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"VTM is correct: it preserves infectivity and prevents drying (slide 10). Drying inactivates viruses.","B":"Correct practice: irradiation (light) is one of the physical factors that inactivate viruses, so protecting from light is on the slide.","C":"The nasopharynx is the right site for a respiratory virus; blood would be the wrong site.","D":"Correct. Slide 10: keep at 4 °C and transport immediately — do not freeze. She stopped on this trap ('don't be kind and freeze it'): ice crystals damage the virion, so culture, which needs live virus, fails.","E":"Correct practice: VTM's antibiotics and antifungals stop bacterial and fungal overgrowth, one of its three jobs (BM29)."},"img":"slides_i/L17_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A nasopharyngeal swab for viral testing cannot reach the lab for several hours. How should it be held?","o":{"D":"At 4 °C in viral transport medium, protected from light — not frozen, because ice crystals destroy the virion","A":"Frozen at −20 °C, because freezing preserves the virus best during a delay","E":"At room temperature on the bench, because cold damages the viral envelope","C":"Dry, with no medium, because liquid encourages bacterial contamination","B":"In formalin, because fixing the specimen stops the virus from degrading"},"r":{"D":"Correct. She stressed the trap: 'don't be kind and freeze it'.","A":"Freezing forms ice crystals that damage the virus.","E":"Heat and irradiation inactivate viruses.","C":"Drying kills the virus; VTM prevents it.","B":"Formalin inactivates the virus, which defeats culture."},"t":"Transport temperature","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L17_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Transport temperature</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Direct vs indirect","s":"A hospital uses two dengue ELISA kits. In kit 1 the well is coated with anti-NS1 antibody and the patient's serum is added. In kit 2 the well is coated with dengue antigen, the patient's serum is added, and then an enzyme-labelled anti-human IgM. How is each kit classified in the lecture's scheme?","lec":"L17","sl":13,"c":"<b>Direct vs indirect</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Kit 1 indirect examination; kit 2 direct examination","B":"Kit 1 direct examination; kit 2 indirect examination","C":"Kit 1 direct examination; kit 2 direct examination","D":"Kit 1 direct examination; kit 2 viral isolation","E":"Kit 1 indirect examination; kit 2 indirect examination"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Reversed: antigen capture looks for the virus (direct); antibody detection looks for the host's response (indirect).","B":"Correct. Decide by what the kit looks for. Kit 1 captures viral antigen (NS1) = the virus itself = direct (slide 13: antigen detection by ELISA). Kit 2 detects the patient's IgM = the host's response = indirect serology (slides 27, 37).","C":"ELISA is not automatically direct; when antigen is on the well and patient antibody is measured, it is serology.","D":"Viral isolation means growing the virus (animal, egg, cell culture); no ELISA grows virus.","E":"ELISA is not automatically serology; an antibody-coated well capturing NS1 is antigen detection."},"img":"slides_i/L17_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which of the following is an INDIRECT method of viral diagnosis?","o":{"B":"Haemagglutination inhibition test for antibody","C":"RT-PCR for viral RNA","D":"Immunofluorescence for viral antigen in cells","A":"Electron microscopy of stool","E":"Haemagglutination assay for virus particles"},"r":{"B":"Correct. HI detects the patient's ANTIBODY, i.e. the effect of the virus, not the virus itself.","C":"Direct: nucleic acid.","D":"Direct: antigen.","A":"Direct: particles.","E":"Direct: HA detects the virus's agglutinating protein."},"t":"Direct vs indirect","sl":12,"img":"slides_i/L17_12.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Direct vs indirect</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Specific inclusions","s":"A 35-year-old farmer dies after four days of agitation, hypersalivation and throat spasms whenever he tries to drink; a stray dog bit him two months earlier. To confirm the cause of death by light microscopy, which specimen and finding are expected?","lec":"L17","sl":9,"c":"<b>Specific inclusions</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Brain tissue — eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons","B":"Vesicle scraping — multinucleated giant cells from the lesion base","C":"Lung tissue — enlarged cells with intranuclear owl's-eye inclusions","D":"Cerebrospinal fluid — multinucleated giant cells with moulded nuclei","E":"Brain tissue — enlarged cells with intranuclear owl's-eye inclusions"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Dog bite + hydrophobia = rabies. CNS specimen = brain biopsy, which for rabies is taken post-mortem (her comment on slide 9). The specific inclusion is the Negri body: an eosinophilic inclusion in the cytoplasm of neurons (slide 15; one of the two she said to memorise).","B":"The Tzanck preparation of a herpes vesicle (slide 16): non-specific and the wrong disease.","C":"CMV pneumonitis in the immunocompromised (owl's eye); wrong organ and wrong virus.","D":"Multinucleated giant cells (Tzanck) are non-specific herpesvirus changes of skin/mucosal lesions; CSF is not where they are sought.","E":"Owl's eye = CMV, intranuclear, in enlarged cells (slide 15) — the other specific inclusion, wrong virus."},"img":"slides_i/L17_9.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A brain section from a patient who died of encephalitis after a dog bite shows pink cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons. What are they, and what do they indicate?","o":{"A":"Negri bodies — rabies virus","C":"Owl's eye inclusions — cytomegalovirus","B":"Multinucleated giant cells — any herpesvirus","E":"Koilocytes — HPV","D":"Molluscum bodies — poxvirus"},"r":{"A":"Correct: one of the two inclusions she said to memorise as SPECIFIC. Brain biopsy for rabies is post-mortem.","C":"Intranuclear, in enlarged cells, not neurons.","B":"Non-specific, and not cytoplasmic neuronal inclusions.","E":"Squamous epithelium, not neurons.","D":"Skin papules."},"t":"Specific inclusions","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L17_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Specific inclusions</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Electron microscopy","s":"During a cruise-ship outbreak of vomiting and watery diarrhoea, stool from 10 patients is examined both by electron microscopy and by RT-PCR. RT-PCR is positive in all 10, but EM finds virus particles in only 6. Which limitation of EM listed in the lecture best explains the discrepancy?","lec":"L17","sl":17,"c":"<b>Electron microscopy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its false positives arising from contamination","B":"Its need for live virus that is still replicating","C":"Its inability to resolve non-enveloped virions","D":"Its reliance on antibody labelling of the antigen","E":"Its poor sensitivity for particles in a specimen"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Contamination false positives are PCR's limitation (slide 26), and a false positive would not explain EM missing cases.","B":"EM sees particles whether or not they are infectious; needing live virus is a property of isolation.","C":"Gastroenteritis viruses (rotavirus, norovirus, adenovirus) are non-enveloped and are exactly what EM was used to see.","D":"Antibody labelling is immunofluorescence/ELISA, not EM.","E":"Correct. Slide 17: EM's problems are expensive equipment and maintenance, the need for an experienced observer, and poor sensitivity. Particles must be abundant to be seen in the fields scanned, so low-load stools are missed. That is why it has been replaced by antigen and molecular tests, even in its old niche of viral gastroenteritis."},"img":"slides_i/L17_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why is electron microscopy now rarely used in routine viral diagnosis?","o":{"E":"It is expensive, needs an experienced observer and has poor sensitivity, and antigen/molecular tests do the job better","C":"It cannot visualise virus particles, only the inclusion bodies they produce","D":"It can only detect bacteria, because viruses are below its resolution","A":"It is the most sensitive method available, but too slow for routine use","B":"It is reserved for HIV, the only virus with a recognisable EM shape"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Its niche was viral gastroenteritis, where the viruses are hard to culture.","C":"Visualising particles is exactly what it does.","D":"Wrong.","A":"Its sensitivity is POOR.","B":"Wrong."},"t":"Electron microscopy","sl":17,"img":"slides_i/L17_17.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Electron microscopy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Reading an HA titre","s":"Throat-wash samples from two patients with influenza-like illness are serially doubled from 1:2 to 1:256 and mixed with red cells. Patient P shows a diffuse pink mat from 1:2 to 1:32 and compact buttons from 1:64 onward; patient Q shows a mat from 1:2 to 1:128 and a button only at 1:256. Which reading is correct?","lec":"L17","sl":21,"c":"<b>Reading an HA titre</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Titres 32 (P) and 128 (Q); Q carries more virus","B":"Titres 64 (P) and 256 (Q); P carries more antibody","C":"Titres 64 (P) and 256 (Q); Q carries more virus","D":"Titres 32 (P) and 128 (Q); Q carries more antibody","E":"Titres 32 (P) and 128 (Q); P carries more virus"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The HA assay detects virus (its haemagglutinin cross-links the red cells into a mat = positive; slide 21). HA titre = the highest dilution still showing a mat: P = 32, Q = 128. The sample that can be diluted further and still agglutinate contains more virus. Same skill as BM30 lab Q19 ('who has more virus?').","B":"Wrong endpoint and wrong analyte: buttons in HA mean no virus, and HA does not measure antibody.","C":"Off by one well: 64 and 256 are the first button wells, i.e. the first negative dilutions, not the titre.","D":"HA measures virus, not antibody; antibody is measured by HI (the reading flips).","E":"Inverted: a higher HA titre means more virus, so Q has more."},"img":"slides_i/L17_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which virus agglutinates red cells through a true surface HAEMAGGLUTININ?","o":{"A":"Influenza virus","C":"Rotavirus","E":"HIV","D":"Hepatitis B virus","B":"Norovirus"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Rubella and dengue also agglutinate RBCs, but through other envelope proteins; only influenza has a true haemagglutinin.","C":"Not an HA-assay virus in this lecture.","E":"Not an HA-assay virus.","D":"Not an HA-assay virus.","B":"Not an HA-assay virus."},"t":"Which viruses haemagglutinate","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L17_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Which viruses haemagglutinate</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"PCR vs RT-PCR","s":"Two specimens reach the molecular lab. Specimen 1 is CSF from an adult with fever, confusion and temporal-lobe changes on MRI who has had recurrent lip vesicles for years. Specimen 2 is a throat swab from an unvaccinated child with fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and a rash that began at the hairline and spread downward. Which plan is correct?","lec":"L17","sl":24,"c":"<b>PCR vs RT-PCR</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Specimen 1: RT-PCR on an RNA genome; specimen 2: RT-PCR on an RNA genome","B":"Specimen 1: PCR on a DNA genome; specimen 2: RT-PCR on an RNA genome","C":"Specimen 1: PCR on a DNA genome; specimen 2: PCR on a DNA genome","D":"Specimen 1: RT-PCR on an RNA genome; specimen 2: PCR on a DNA genome","E":"Specimen 1: PCR on a DNA genome; specimen 2: RT-PCR on a DNA genome"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"HSV has a DNA genome; plain PCR is enough.","B":"Correct. Specimen 1 = HSV encephalitis (herpesvirus, dsDNA) → PCR amplifies DNA directly. Specimen 2 = measles (CCCP + rash from the hairline down; paramyxovirus, RNA) → RT-PCR: reverse transcriptase first converts RNA to cDNA, which is then amplified (slide 24).","C":"Plain PCR (Taq) cannot amplify an RNA genome; measles needs the reverse-transcription step.","D":"Swapped: HSV is a DNA virus, measles an RNA virus.","E":"The planted error from the method-matching items (BM29/BM30 Q50/BM32 Q78): 'RT' means the starting template is RNA, never DNA."},"img":"slides_i/L17_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which test is appropriate to detect measles virus in a throat swab, and why?","o":{"B":"RT-PCR — measles is an RNA virus, so its RNA must first be reverse-transcribed into cDNA","C":"Conventional PCR — it amplifies RNA directly","E":"RT-PCR — because it detects viral DNA","A":"Western blot for viral RNA","D":"Electron microscopy, the most sensitive option"},"r":{"B":"Correct. 'RT' tells you the template is RNA.","C":"Taq polymerase needs DNA; RNA must be converted first.","E":"The planted error in the method-matching items: RT-PCR detects RNA, not DNA.","A":"Western blot detects antibody to proteins.","D":"EM has poor sensitivity."},"t":"PCR vs RT-PCR","sl":24,"img":"slides_i/L17_24.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>PCR vs RT-PCR</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Reading Ct","s":"Real-time RT-PCR for influenza uses a cut-off of Ct 35. Patient K crosses the threshold at Ct 16, patient L at Ct 29 and patient M at Ct 37; patient N shows no signal by cycle 40. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L17","sl":25,"c":"<b>Reading Ct</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"M and N are positive; N carries the most virus","B":"K and L are positive; L carries the most virus","C":"K and L are positive; K carries the most virus","D":"K, L and M are positive; K carries the most virus","E":"L and M are positive; M carries the most virus"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Reads the rule backwards: no signal by cycle 40 is negative, and N has the least (undetectable) template. Same skill as BM28 Q60 and BM30 lab Q22.","B":"Inverted: a higher Ct means less starting virus.","C":"Correct. Positive = Ct below the cut-off (K 16, L 29). Ct is the cycle at which fluorescence crosses the threshold; the more starting template, the fewer cycles needed, so low Ct = more virus (her spoken tell). K has the most virus.","D":"M's Ct is above the cut-off, so M is negative.","E":"M (37) is above the cut-off = negative, and a high Ct means little template."},"img":"slides_i/L17_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Two respiratory samples are run by real-time RT-PCR. Sample X crosses the threshold at Ct 18, sample Y at Ct 32 (cut-off 38). Which statement is correct?","o":{"C":"Both are positive, and X contains much more virus than Y","B":"Y contains more virus, because its Ct is higher","E":"Only Y is positive, because it is closer to the cut-off","D":"Both are negative, because a positive needs Ct > 38","A":"Ct values say nothing about viral quantity"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Fewer cycles to cross = more starting template. Positive = Ct BELOW the cut-off. BM30 lab Q22 (Ct 15 vs cut-off 20 → positive) is in L14.","B":"Inverted: Ct is inversely related to load.","E":"Both are below the cut-off.","D":"The rule is the reverse.","A":"qPCR is quantitative."},"t":"Reading Ct","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L17_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Reading Ct</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"PCR limitations","s":"After a week spent amplifying a dengue plasmid standard, a lab's conventional PCR starts giving bands at the dengue position for several afebrile staff with no history of dengue. Fresh samples from the same people, run with new reagents in a separate room, are all negative. What is the best explanation?","lec":"L17","sl":26,"c":"<b>PCR limitations</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Carry-over contamination of the reactions with amplified product","B":"Residual viral nucleic acid persisting from an old, cleared infection","C":"Too few cycles to separate a positive from a negative sample","D":"Cross-reacting antibodies in the samples binding to the primers","E":"Poor specificity of PCR primers for viral sequences in general"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 26: PCR's limitations are expertise/expensive equipment and potential false positives owing to contamination. The amplification that makes PCR so sensitive also amplifies stray product from earlier runs; a clean repeat in a separate area turning negative confirms it.","B":"Residual nucleic acid would still be present in fresh samples from the same people, and they have no history of infection.","C":"Too few cycles would cause false negatives, not false positives.","D":"PCR detects nucleic acid; antibodies do not bind primers.","E":"PCR's advantage on the slide is high sensitivity AND specificity."},"img":"slides_i/L17_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A PCR lab reports several unexpected positives from patients with no symptoms. What is the most likely technical explanation?","o":{"A":"Contamination with amplified product, causing false positives","C":"PCR has low sensitivity","B":"PCR cannot detect DNA viruses","E":"The specimens were stored at 4 °C","D":"PCR detects antibody rather than virus"},"r":{"A":"Correct: the limitation on the slide. The same amplification that makes PCR sensitive amplifies stray molecules.","C":"Sensitivity is high.","B":"Plain PCR is for DNA.","E":"4 °C is correct storage.","D":"PCR detects nucleic acid."},"t":"PCR limitations","sl":26,"img":"slides_i/L17_26.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>PCR limitations</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Gold standard — proving live virus","s":"A man who recovered from a severe respiratory viral illness still has a positive real-time RT-PCR on a throat swab on day 20. Infection control must know whether he is still shedding live, infectious virus. Which method answers that question, paired with its recognised drawback?","lec":"L17","sl":28,"c":"<b>Gold standard — proving live virus</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Electron microscopy of the swab — costly, with poor sensitivity","B":"Antigen-detection ELISA — poor sensitivity and poor specificity","C":"Repeat real-time RT-PCR — costly, with false positives from contamination","D":"Paired-sera IgG serology — needs a second sample 7–14 days later","E":"Viral isolation in cell culture — slow, and many viruses will not grow"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Drawbacks correct (slide 17), but seeing particles does not prove they are infectious.","B":"Drawback correct (slide 22), but antigen is a viral protein, present whether or not the virus can still infect.","C":"Drawbacks correct (slide 26), but PCR detects nucleic acid, which persists from dead virus; it cannot tell live from dead.","D":"Paired sera prove a recent infection happened (host response), not that virus is still being shed. Serology is the most widely used method, isolation the gold standard — don't swap them.","E":"Correct. Only growth of the virus proves it is alive and infectious — that is why viral isolation is the gold standard and is used to confirm direct examination and serology (slide 28). Its drawbacks: needs facilities, time-consuming, and in cell culture poor sensitivity, contamination and many viruses will not grow (slide 30)."},"img":"slides_i/L17_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing is correct?","o":{"E":"Gold standard — viral isolation; most widely used — serology","B":"Gold standard — serology; most widely used — viral isolation","A":"Gold standard — electron microscopy; most widely used — cell culture","C":"Gold standard — rapid strip test; most widely used — PRNT","D":"Gold standard — CFT; most widely used — HA"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Isolation proves live infectious virus but is slow and costly, so routine diagnosis relies on serology (and now PCR).","B":"Swapped.","A":"EM has poor sensitivity.","C":"No.","D":"No."},"t":"Gold standard vs most used","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L17_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Gold standard vs most used</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Animal model","s":"A dog that bit a child became aggressive, drooled excessively and died within a week. The reference lab wants to isolate the virus from its brain for strain typing. Which isolation system does the lecture name as the method of choice for this virus, paired with a drawback that applies to it?","lec":"L17","sl":29,"c":"<b>Animal model</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Animal inoculation — the animal may harbour latent viruses of its own","B":"Embryonated egg — the host's own immunity may interfere with growth","C":"Embryonated egg — the inoculation site differs from virus to virus","D":"Animal inoculation — pocks on the membranes are slow to appear","E":"Cell culture — many viruses fail to grow in the cultured cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Aggressive, drooling dog = rabies. Slide 29: the animal model is the method of choice for isolating rabies virus, flaviviruses and togaviruses. Its drawbacks: immunity may interfere with viral growth, animals often harbour latent viruses, not suitable for all viruses.","B":"Immunity interfering is the animal model's drawback, not the egg's.","C":"A real drawback, but of the embryonated egg, which is not the method of choice for rabies.","D":"Right system, wrong read-out: pocks are the visible lesions of the embryonated egg; animals are read by clinical signs.","E":"A real drawback of cell culture (slide 30), but not the lecture's method of choice for rabies."},"img":"slides_i/L17_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which is a genuine drawback of isolating a virus in an animal model?","o":{"A":"The animal may already harbour latent viruses, so the lesion seen may not be caused by the inoculated virus","E":"Animals cannot be infected by any human virus, so no growth is ever seen","B":"Animal inoculation is the fastest method, so its speed hides slow infections","D":"It is the method of choice for HIV, so other viruses cannot be tested","C":"Animal immunity always enhances viral growth, which exaggerates the lesions"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Also listed: the animal's immunity may clear the virus, and it is not suitable for all viruses. It remains the method of choice for rabies, flaviviruses and togaviruses.","E":"Several grow in animals.","B":"It is slow.","D":"Not listed.","C":"Immunity INTERFERES."},"t":"Animal model","sl":29,"img":"slides_i/L17_29.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Animal model</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"CPE in culture","s":"A nasopharyngeal aspirate from a 6-month-old with bronchiolitis is inoculated onto a cell monolayer. On day 4 the monolayer shows large multinucleated cells formed by fusion of neighbouring cells. What does this observation, on its own, allow the laboratory to report?","lec":"L17","sl":30,"c":"<b>CPE in culture</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The presence of neutralising antibody in the sample","B":"A definitive identification of the virus species","C":"The infectious titre of the specimen in PFU/mL","D":"That the isolate is a non-enveloped virus","E":"A presumptive identification of the isolate"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Neutralising antibody is measured by the neutralisation test (slide 35), not by seeing CPE after inoculating a patient specimen.","B":"CPE is presumptive only; confirmation needs antigen, genome or neutralisation testing.","C":"A titre needs a quantitative method: counting plaques at known dilutions (plaque assay, slide 31).","D":"Syncytia come from viral fusion proteins in an envelope; this points toward an enveloped virus, not a naked one.","E":"Correct. Fused multinucleated cells = syncytia, one of the CPEs on slide 30 (typical of fusing enveloped viruses such as RSV and measles). The slide says CPE 'helps in presumptive identification of virus isolates' — it varies with the virus and the host cell and can be non-specific, so it is not proof of species."},"img":"slides_i/L17_30.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Three days after inoculating cultured cells with a respiratory sample, large multinucleated cells appear where cells have fused together. What is this called?","o":{"E":"Syncytium formation — a cytopathic effect","A":"A plaque-forming unit count","D":"Haemagglutination","B":"Complement fixation","C":"Koilocytosis"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The practical exam shows CPE photos and asks what the CPE is and which virus causes it (BM31 lab Q19–20). Syncytia point to fusing viruses such as RSV and measles.","A":"A count, not a morphology.","D":"Agglutination of RBCs.","B":"A serological test.","C":"HPV's squamous-cell change."},"t":"CPE in culture","sl":30,"img":"slides_i/L17_30.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>CPE in culture</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Plaque assay — comparing stocks","s":"Two virus stocks are titrated by plaque assay using 100 µL of each dilution per well. Stock X gives 40 plaques at 10⁻³ and 4 plaques at 10⁻⁴. Stock Y gives 8 plaques at 10⁻⁵ and none at 10⁻⁶. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L17","sl":31,"c":"<b>Plaque assay — comparing stocks</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Y has the higher titre, 8 × 10⁵ PFU/mL","B":"Y has the higher titre, 8 × 10⁶ PFU/mL","C":"X has the higher titre, 4 × 10⁶ PFU/mL","D":"X has the higher titre, 4 × 10⁵ PFU/mL","E":"Y has the higher titre, 8 × 10⁷ PFU/mL"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Forgot to convert per well (100 µL) to per mL (× 10) — the classic BM28 Q59 slip.","B":"Correct. Plaques × dilution factor = PFU per 100 µL. X: 40 × 10³ = 4 × 10⁴ (and 4 × 10⁴ = 4 × 10⁴) → × 10 for 1 mL = 4 × 10⁵ PFU/mL. Y: 8 × 10⁵ per 100 µL → 8 × 10⁶ PFU/mL. Y is 20-fold higher. BM30 lab Q24 asked exactly this: which virus grows better — the one still making plaques at the higher dilution.","C":"Wrong stock and wrong power: X is 4 × 10⁵, and Y is higher.","D":"The 'more plaques' trap: X has more plaques only because it was counted at a much lower dilution. Its titre is the lower one.","E":"Applied the volume factor twice (× 100 instead of × 10)."},"img":"slides_i/L17_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"In a plaque assay, why is a semi-solid overlay poured over the infected monolayer?","o":{"B":"It limits spread to neighbouring cells, so each infectious unit forms a separate, countable plaque","A":"It feeds the virus with nutrients, so that more plaques form in each well","C":"It contains antibiotics that kill bacteria contaminating the sample","D":"It contains a dye that stains the plaques red so they can be counted","E":"It captures antibody in the sample so that neutralisation can be read"},"r":{"B":"Correct. One plaque ≈ one infectious unit, which gives a quantitative titre in PFU/mL.","A":"The overlay restricts rather than feeds.","C":"That is a VTM job.","D":"Staining is a separate step.","E":"Antibody measurement is PRNT."},"t":"Plaque assay principle","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/L17_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Plaque assay principle</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Paired sera","s":"Five adults with fever and rash each have IgG titres against the suspected virus measured at presentation and again 10 days later. Which patient has serological evidence of a current infection?","lec":"L17","sl":32,"c":"<b>Paired sera</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Patient P: 80 → 160","B":"Patient Q: 20 → 40","C":"Patient R: 64 → 256","D":"Patient T: 320 → 80","E":"Patient S: 640 → 640"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Only two-fold (one doubling step) — within the error of a doubling-dilution test, not diagnostic.","B":"Also only two-fold; a low titre that doubles is not a significant rise.","C":"Correct. Slide 32: a rising IgG titre between acute and convalescent sera of ≥4-fold = current infection; she said the sera are taken 7–14 days apart and gave 40 → 160 as the example. 64 → 256 is exactly four-fold (two doubling steps). BM28 Q15 keyed the same pattern (128 → 512).","D":"A falling titre shows an older infection waning, not one happening now.","E":"A high but stable titre = past infection or vaccination; it does not date the infection."},"img":"slides_i/L17_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient's IgG titre against a virus is 1:40 in the acute phase and 1:320 two weeks later. How is this interpreted?","o":{"C":"Recent infection — an eight-fold rise exceeds the required four-fold rise","A":"Past infection only, since IgG is present","E":"Not interpretable without IgM","B":"Vaccination, since IgG rose","D":"Negative, because a single IgG titre cannot be interpreted"},"r":{"C":"Correct. ≥4-fold between acute and convalescent sera = the immune system is answering an infection happening now.","A":"A single IgG could be past; a RISE is current.","E":"A 4-fold IgG rise is itself diagnostic.","B":"A two-week rise tracks the illness, not an old vaccine.","D":"There are two titres here, i.e. paired sera."},"t":"Paired sera","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L17_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Paired sera</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Reading an HI titre","s":"A pregnant woman exposed to a child with a rash has her serum tested by haemagglutination inhibition against rubella antigen. Doubling serum dilutions from 1:8 to 1:512 show a compact red button from 1:8 to 1:64 and a diffuse pink mat from 1:128 onward. How is the result read?","lec":"L17","sl":21,"c":"<b>Reading an HI titre</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Virus present, with an HA titre of 64","B":"Antibody absent, as a button marks a negative well","C":"Antibody present, with an HI titre of 64","D":"Virus present, with an HA titre of 128","E":"Antibody present, with an HI titre of 128"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"HI measures antibody in serum, not virus; an HA titre is read from a virus sample.","B":"The HA rule applied to HI. In HI (and CFT) the button/intact cells = positive.","C":"Correct. In HI the serum is mixed with the virus first; if antibody is present it blocks the haemagglutinin, so the red cells cannot be cross-linked and settle into a button = positive (slide 33). The titre is the highest serum dilution still inhibiting: 1:64. 'The reading flips' — in HA a button is negative, in HI it is positive.","D":"Wrong analyte and wrong endpoint.","E":"1:128 is the first mat well, where antibody is too dilute to inhibit — the first negative dilution."},"img":"slides_i/L17_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"In a haemagglutination-INHIBITION test for influenza antibody, a well shows a compact red button at the bottom. How is it read?","o":{"C":"Positive — antibody in the serum blocked the haemagglutinin, so the red cells could not be cross-linked","A":"Negative — in any haemagglutination test a button means nothing is present","D":"Positive for virus particles, which settled into a button in the serum","E":"Invalid — HI wells cannot form buttons, so the well must be repeated","B":"Positive for complement fixation, which prevented the cells from lysing"},"r":{"C":"Correct. 'The reading flips': in HA a button = no virus; in HI a button = antibody present.","A":"True in HA, false in HI.","D":"HI detects antibody.","E":"The button is the positive endpoint.","B":"A different test."},"t":"HA vs HI reading","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L17_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HA vs HI reading</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"CFT reading","s":"Acute and convalescent sera from a patient with a febrile illness are tested by complement fixation. After the indicator sheep red cells are added, the acute-serum wells turn clear red with no cell pellet, whereas the convalescent-serum wells show intact cells settled into a pellet up to 1:64. How are the two sera read?","lec":"L17","sl":34,"c":"<b>CFT reading</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Antibody present in both sera, higher in the convalescent serum","B":"Antibody absent in both sera, with lysis in the acute wells","C":"Antibody present in both sera, higher in the acute serum","D":"Antibody absent in the acute serum, present in the convalescent serum","E":"Antibody present in the acute serum, absent in the convalescent serum"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Lysis in the acute wells means no antibody there, not a lower level.","B":"The convalescent wells show intact cells, which is a positive CFT.","C":"The acute wells lysed, so the acute serum had no detectable antibody.","D":"Correct. Slide 34: with antibody, the Ag–Ab complex fixes (uses up) complement, so the sensitised RBCs are not lysed and settle as a pellet = positive (convalescent). Without antibody, complement stays free and lyses the indicator cells = clear red fluid = negative (acute). Negative → positive between paired sera = seroconversion, i.e. a current infection.","E":"Reads CFT like an HA well; in CFT lysis = negative and intact cells = positive."},"img":"slides_i/L17_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"In a complement fixation test, the indicator sheep red cells are completely LYSED. What does this mean?","o":{"D":"Negative — no antibody formed a complex, so complement stayed free to lyse the indicator cells","E":"Positive — lysis shows that antibody in the serum attacked the red cells","B":"Invalid — lysis in a CFT well always means the reagents have expired","C":"The patient has haemolytic anaemia, which lysed the indicator cells","A":"The virus haemagglutinated the cells, which then burst in the well"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Complement can do only one job. If an Ag–Ab complex used it up (positive), nothing is left to burst the indicator cells.","E":"Reversed: in CFT, intact cells = positive.","B":"Lysis is the negative endpoint.","C":"Irrelevant.","A":"A different test."},"t":"CFT reading","sl":34,"img":"slides_i/L17_34.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>CFT reading</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Neutralisation titre","s":"A fixed dose of virus is mixed with doubling dilutions of a patient's serum from 1:10 to 1:160, and each mixture is added to a cell monolayer. The monolayers stay intact at 1:10, 1:20 and 1:40; cytopathic effect appears at 1:80 and 1:160. What is the neutralising antibody titre?","lec":"L17","sl":35,"c":"<b>Neutralisation titre</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"1:160","B":"1:40","C":"1:20","D":"1:80","E":"1:10"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The most dilute well, and it shows CPE.","B":"Correct. Slide 35: neutralising antibody titre = the highest serum dilution that can still neutralise the virus infection, seen as absence of CPE (viable cells = +) or reduction of plaques. 1:40 is the last dilution with intact cells.","C":"Protected, but 1:40 is a higher dilution that still protects.","D":"The first dilution showing CPE — antibody is too dilute to neutralise here, so it is the first negative well.","E":"Protected, but not the highest protecting dilution."},"img":"slides_i/L17_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"After a vaccine trial, investigators want the level of FUNCTIONAL protective antibody. Which method?","o":{"B":"Neutralisation test (PRNT)","D":"Plaque assay","C":"RT-PCR","A":"Rapid antigen test","E":"Electron microscopy"},"r":{"B":"Correct: the only serology that measures whether antibody STOPS infection.","D":"Counts VIRUS, not antibody.","C":"Detects viral RNA.","A":"Detects viral protein.","E":"Sees particles."},"t":"Which test measures protection","sl":35,"img":"slides_i/L17_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Which test measures protection</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Rapid strip validity","s":"A 20-year-old on day 5 of fever with a positive tourniquet test is tested with a dengue IgM/IgG immunochromatographic strip. Coloured lines appear at the M and G positions, but no line appears at the C position. How should the result be reported?","lec":"L17","sl":36,"c":"<b>Rapid strip validity</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Invalid; repeat the test on a new strip","B":"Valid; a current primary dengue infection","C":"Valid; a past dengue infection with none now","D":"Valid; negative, as the C position is blank","E":"Valid; a current secondary dengue infection"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The control (C) line must appear on every strip, whatever the sample contains (slide 36 figure: every result, including negative, shows C). No C line = invalid, so the M and G lines cannot be interpreted; repeat.","B":"Primary infection would be IgM alone on a valid strip.","C":"Past infection would be IgG alone on a valid strip.","D":"A negative strip shows the C line only; a blank C line means the test failed, not that it is negative.","E":"IgM + IgG would suggest secondary infection on a valid strip — the tempting read, but this strip is not valid."},"img":"slides_i/L17_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A rapid IgM/IgG strip shows a line at the IgM position but NO control line. How is it reported?","o":{"A":"Invalid — repeat with a new strip","C":"Positive for acute infection","B":"Positive for past infection","E":"Negative","D":"Positive, since the control line is optional"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The control line must appear whatever the sample contains; without it, nothing on the strip can be trusted.","C":"Not without a valid control.","B":"No.","E":"Not negative either: invalid.","D":"It is mandatory."},"t":"Rapid strip validity","sl":36,"img":"slides_i/L17_36.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Rapid strip validity</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Method for the purpose","s":"A manufacturer must state how many infectious units of a live attenuated measles vaccine are present in each millilitre of a new batch before release. Which laboratory method gives this number directly?","lec":"L17","sl":31,"c":"<b>Method for the purpose</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Plaque assay on a cell monolayer","B":"Antigen-capture ELISA with a standard","C":"Real-time RT-PCR for genome copies","D":"Plaque reduction neutralisation test","E":"Haemagglutination assay with red cells"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 31: the plaque assay is the quantitative assay of infectious virus — each plaque is a region of destruction started by one infectious unit, counted and reported as PFU/mL.","B":"Measures viral protein, which is present whether or not particles are infectious.","C":"Counts genome copies, including those in dead or defective particles, so it does not give infectious units.","D":"PRNT measures neutralising antibody in a serum (slide 35), not how much virus a stock contains.","E":"HA gives a titre of agglutinating particles, not of infectious virus."},"img":"slides_i/L17_31.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of question and method is INCORRECT?","o":{"A":"'How much infectious virus is in this stock?' — neutralisation test","E":"'Is there viral RNA in this acute throat swab?' — RT-PCR","D":"'Did this patient have a recent infection?' — ≥4-fold IgG rise in paired sera","C":"'Does this serum block infection?' — PRNT","B":"'Ultimate confirmation of a live virus' — viral isolation"},"r":{"A":"Correct choice (it is the wrong pairing). Infectious-virus titre is the PLAQUE ASSAY (PFU/mL); the neutralisation test measures ANTIBODY.","E":"Correct pairing.","D":"Correct pairing.","C":"Correct pairing.","B":"Correct pairing."},"t":"Method for the purpose","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L17_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Method for the purpose</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233365541},{"id":"I|L17|21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q78","t":"Method → what it detects","s":"Which pairing of laboratory method and what it detects is correct?","lec":"L17","sl":13,"c":"<b>Method → what it detects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q78 · BM30 SUM1 Q50 · BM29 SUM1 — match method and result; keyed 'light microscope – histological morphology'. The planted error is 'RT-PCR – viral DNA'.</span>","o":{"D":"Light microscope — histological morphology (e.g. giant cells, inclusions)","B":"RT-PCR — viral DNA","E":"ELISA — HA titre","A":"Electron microscope — multinucleated giant cells","C":"Haemagglutination assay — antibody"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct (keyed), in three cohorts.","B":"RT = RNA template.","E":"ELISA detects antigen or antibody; HA titre comes from the HA assay.","A":"EM sees virus particles.","C":"The HA assay detects VIRUS; antibody is HI."},"img":"slides_i/L17_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q50","BM29 Q-methods"]},{"id":"I|L17|22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q51","t":"HI after influenza vaccination","s":"After influenza vaccination, which test determines whether antibody against the vaccine strain has risen?","lec":"L17","sl":33,"c":"<b>HI after influenza vaccination</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q51 — after influenza vaccination, which test shows whether antibody has risen? Keyed HI.</span>","o":{"D":"Haemagglutination inhibition (HI) assay","E":"Haemagglutination assay","A":"Immunofluorescence for viral antigen","B":"Immunochromatography for viral antigen","C":"RT-PCR"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct (keyed). HI detects antibody that blocks the haemagglutinin.","E":"HA detects virus, not antibody.","A":"Antigen detection.","B":"Antigen detection as worded.","C":"Detects viral RNA."},"img":"slides_i/L17_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L17|23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q80","t":"Rubella neutralising antibody","s":"A 25-year-old woman has low-grade fever, sore throat and rash; rubella is suspected. Which method detects SPECIFIC NEUTRALISING antibody for the diagnosis, and what is its positive endpoint?","lec":"L17","sl":33,"c":"<b>Rubella neutralising antibody</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q80 — rubella suspected; which method detects specific neutralising antibody? · BM28 Q14 — HI endpoint for a positive result = no lattice formation.</span>","o":{"E":"Haemagglutination inhibition — no lattice formation on the red cells","C":"Haemagglutination — lattice formation","A":"ELISA — haemolysis","B":"Immunochromatography — no control line","D":"Immunofluorescence — lattice and haemolysis"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Antibody neutralises the virus's agglutinating protein, so the cells button. BM28 Q14 keyed exactly this endpoint.","C":"Detects virus.","A":"ELISA gives colour, not haemolysis.","B":"Means invalid.","D":"Not an endpoint."},"img":"slides_i/L17_33.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q14"]},{"id":"I|L17|24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q49","t":"Specimen handling","s":"Which statement about specimen collection for viral diagnosis is MOST correct?","lec":"L17","sl":8,"c":"<b>Specimen handling</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q49 — which statement about specimen collection is most correct? Keyed from the เฉลย highlight: urine in a closed, dry, clean container.</span>","o":{"C":"Urine should be collected in a tightly closed, dry, clean container","D":"Stool specimens must be placed in viral transport medium","E":"Nasal specimens can be kept at room temperature until tested","A":"Specimens should be frozen at once to preserve the virus","B":"Specimens are best collected after symptoms resolve"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct (keyed): 'proper containers'.","D":"VTM is for swabs.","E":"Keep at 4 °C.","A":"Do not freeze.","B":"Collect in the acute phase."},"img":"slides_i/L17_8.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L17|25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q-VTM","t":"What VTM does","s":"Which is NOT a function of viral transport medium?","lec":"L17","sl":10,"c":"<b>What VTM does</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM29 SUM1 (VTM item; the key lists the three functions) — keep in VTM to preserve infectivity, prevent drying and stop bacterial/fungal growth.</span>","o":{"D":"Supporting the growth and multiplication of the virus during transport","B":"Preserving viral infectivity within the specimen","C":"Preventing the specimen from drying","A":"Stopping the growth of bacteria and fungi","E":"Buffering the pH around the virus"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct choice. She said VTM is not a growth medium: it only PRESERVES. Viruses cannot multiply without living cells.","B":"A real function (protein).","C":"A real function.","A":"A real function (antibiotics + antifungals).","E":"A real function (buffer)."},"img":"slides_i/L17_10.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L17|26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q59","t":"Plaque titre calculation","s":"A plaque assay uses 100 µL of each dilution per well. Plaques: 10⁻¹ → 10; 10⁻² → 1; 10⁻³ to 10⁻⁵ → 0. What is the virus titre?","lec":"L17","sl":31,"c":"<b>Plaque titre calculation</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q59 — plaque assay, 100 µL per well; 10⁻¹ → 10 PFU, 10⁻² → 1 PFU, rest 0. Keyed 1,000 PFU/mL.</span>","o":{"D":"1,000 PFU/mL","A":"100 PFU/mL","E":"10 PFU/mL","B":"10,000 PFU/mL","C":"11 PFU/mL"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct (keyed). 10 × 10¹ = 100 and 1 × 10² = 100; the mean is 100 PFU per 100 µL → × 10 = 1,000 PFU/mL.","A":"Forgot to convert 100 µL to 1 mL.","E":"Ignored the dilution factor.","B":"Converted twice.","C":"Just added the counts."},"img":"slides_i/L17_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L17|27","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q61","t":"PRNT50 comparison","s":"Volunteer 1 (CoronaVac × 2) has a PRNT₅₀ titre of 160; volunteer 2 (AstraZeneca × 2) has 320. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L17","sl":35,"c":"<b>PRNT50 comparison</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q61 — PRNT50: CoronaVac × 2 → 160, AstraZeneca × 2 → 320; who has higher immunity? Keyed AZ.</span>","o":{"C":"Volunteer 2 has more neutralising antibody — the serum still halves plaques at a two-fold greater dilution","E":"Volunteer 1 has more antibody, because a lower titre means stronger serum","B":"They are identical, since both are above 100","D":"PRNT₅₀ measures virus, not antibody","A":"Titre cannot be compared between people"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct (keyed). Higher titre = more antibody.","E":"Inverted.","B":"A 2-fold difference is real in a doubling-dilution series.","D":"It measures neutralising ANTIBODY.","A":"Comparison is exactly its use."},"img":"slides_i/L17_35.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L17|28","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q27","t":"PRNT50 from a table","s":"Virus control well = 100 PFU. Serum dilutions give 1:50 → 5 · 1:100 → 20 · 1:200 → 45 · 1:400 → 70 PFU. What is the PRNT₅₀ titre?","lec":"L17","sl":35,"c":"<b>PRNT50 from a table</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 lab Q27 — find the neutralising antibody level from a plaque-reduction table (virus control well vs serum dilutions). Numbers here are re-stated.</span>","o":{"C":"1:200","E":"1:400","B":"1:100","D":"1:50","A":"Cannot be determined"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. Reduction = 95%, 80%, 55%, 30%. The highest dilution still ≥50% is 1:200.","E":"Only 30% reduction.","B":"Qualifies, but 1:200 is a higher dilution that still qualifies.","D":"Qualifies, but is not the highest.","A":"It can be."},"img":"slides_i/L17_35.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L17|29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L17 s38","t":"Quiz — specimen principle","s":"Which principle is most important when selecting a clinical specimen for DIRECT detection of a viral infection?","lec":"L17","sl":38,"c":"<b>Quiz — specimen principle</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's NEW quiz, slide 38, answered in class (→ D): most important principle when selecting a specimen for direct detection.</span>","o":{"E":"Collect specimens from the likely site of infection","B":"Always collect blood regardless of the virus","C":"Collect the specimen only after symptoms resolve","A":"Collect from a site where the virus cannot replicate","D":"Use the same specimen type for all viral infections"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct: correct SITE.","B":"Wrong for most local infections.","C":"The virus has gone by then.","A":"The opposite.","D":"The specimen must follow the disease site."},"img":"slides_i/L17_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L17|30","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L17 s39","t":"Quiz — neonate of HIV mother","s":"Which is the appropriate laboratory diagnosis for a neonate whose mother is HIV-infected?","lec":"L17","sl":39,"c":"<b>Quiz — neonate of HIV mother</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's quiz, slide 39, answered in class (→ viral genome detection); a student asked 'why not ELISA?' → maternal IgG crosses the placenta.</span>","o":{"E":"Viral genome detection","C":"ELISA for antibody detection","A":"Cell culture","B":"Neutralisation test","D":"Complement fixation test"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct. Only the virus itself tells you about the BABY.","C":"The trap: maternal IgG crosses the placenta, so every baby is antibody-positive.","A":"Slow and not the diagnostic approach.","B":"Antibody-based.","D":"Antibody-based."},"img":"slides_i/L17_39.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L17|31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L17 s40","t":"Quiz — protective antibody","s":"A researcher wants to determine whether antibodies in a patient's serum can specifically prevent infection by a particular virus. Which method is most appropriate?","lec":"L17","sl":40,"c":"<b>Quiz — protective antibody</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>The lecturer's NEW quiz, slide 40, answered in class (→ neutralisation test).</span>","o":{"B":"Neutralisation test","A":"PCR","D":"Antigen detection assay","E":"Plaque assay","C":"Western blot"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct: it measures function (blocking infection of cells).","A":"Detects nucleic acid.","D":"Detects viral protein.","E":"Counts infectious virus.","C":"Shows binding, not protection."},"img":"slides_i/L17_40.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"RT inhibitor activation","s":"A 30-year-old man starts TDF/3TC/EFV. His tutor asks how each of the three reverse-transcriptase blockers becomes active inside his CD4 T cells. Which answer is correct?","lec":"L18","sl":7,"c":"<b>RT inhibitor activation</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Tenofovir needs three host-kinase steps, lamivudine three, efavirenz none","B":"Tenofovir needs two host-kinase steps, lamivudine three, efavirenz three","C":"Tenofovir needs three host-kinase steps, lamivudine two, efavirenz one","D":"Tenofovir needs two host-kinase steps, lamivudine three, efavirenz none","E":"Tenofovir needs one viral-kinase step, lamivudine three, efavirenz none"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Treats tenofovir as if it were a nucleoside. Its built-in phosphate saves one step: it is converted to the diphosphate form.","B":"Efavirenz is not a nucleotide mimic. NNRTIs do not require activation by cellular enzymes (slide 14).","C":"Reversed for tenofovir and lamivudine, and efavirenz needs no activation at all.","D":"Correct. Tenofovir is a nucleoTide (an adenosine monophosphate analogue that already carries one phosphate), so host enzymes add two more to make tenofovir-diphosphate (slide 13). Lamivudine is a nucleoSide and needs three host-kinase phosphorylations (slide 10). Efavirenz is an NNRTI and needs none; it binds an allosteric site (slide 14). Her mnemonic: NNRTI = No phosphorylation + Non-active site.","E":"Viral-kinase activation belongs to the anti-herpes drugs (HSV/VZV thymidine kinase for acyclovir, CMV UL97 for ganciclovir). All HIV drugs are activated, if at all, by host kinases."},"img":"slides_i/L18_7.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the three classes of HIV reverse-transcriptase inhibitor?","o":{"D":"NRTIs need three phosphorylations, the NtRTI tenofovir needs one more, and NNRTIs need none because they bind an allosteric site","A":"NNRTIs need three phosphorylations by viral thymidine kinase","B":"All three classes are incorporated into viral DNA as chain terminators","E":"Tenofovir is an NNRTI that binds an allosteric site","C":"NRTIs bind the enzyme non-competitively at a site away from the active site"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Her mnemonic: NNRTI = No phosphorylation needed + a Non-active site.","A":"NNRTIs need no activation, and viral TK is a herpes enzyme.","B":"NNRTIs are not incorporated; they jam the enzyme allosterically.","E":"Tenofovir is the one NUCLEOTIDE RTI.","C":"That describes NNRTIs."},"t":"RT inhibitor classes","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/L18_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>RT inhibitor classes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Older NRTI mitochondrial toxicity","s":"A man has taken stavudine + lamivudine + nevirapine for eight years. He now has burning pain in both feet, epigastric pain with a raised serum amylase, and hollow cheeks with thin limbs. Which drug and mechanism best account for all three problems?","lec":"L18","sl":10,"c":"<b>Older NRTI mitochondrial toxicity</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Lamivudine — inhibition of mitochondrial DNA polymerase","B":"Stavudine — suppression of all bone-marrow cell lines","C":"Nevirapine — induction of hepatic CYP enzymes","D":"Nevirapine — immune-mediated hypersensitivity reaction","E":"Stavudine — inhibition of mitochondrial DNA polymerase"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The right mechanism attached to the wrong drug. Lamivudine is the 'relatively clean' NRTI in her table; the neuropathy, pancreatitis and lipoatrophy triad is named for stavudine, didanosine and zalcitabine.","B":"Marrow suppression is the zidovudine (AZT → anaemia) signature, and it would not explain neuropathy, pancreatitis or fat loss.","C":"NNRTIs do affect CYP enzymes, but that causes drug interactions, not this toxicity triad.","D":"Nevirapine's hypersensitivity is rash/SJS plus hepatotoxicity, which comes early. It does not cause neuropathy, pancreatitis or fat loss.","E":"Correct. Slide 10: zalcitabine, didanosine and stavudine inhibit mitochondrial DNA polymerase → peripheral neuropathy, pancreatitis and lipoatrophy (the hollow cheeks). There is no PI in this regimen, so the fat loss is not PI lipodystrophy."},"img":"slides_i/L18_10.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient on a long-term NRTI-based regimen presents with malaise, an enlarged fatty liver and a raised serum lactate. What is the mechanism?","o":{"E":"Mitochondrial toxicity: NRTIs also inhibit mitochondrial DNA polymerase, causing lactic acidosis and hepatic steatosis","B":"An allergic hepatitis caused by the ritonavir booster included in the regimen","C":"CYP3A4 induction by the NRTI, which generates a toxic hepatic metabolite","A":"Integrase inhibition in hepatocytes, which blocks their DNA repair","D":"Direct HIV infection of hepatocytes, which the drug makes more likely"},"r":{"E":"Correct: the class-wide NRTI toxicity on slide 10.","B":"Ritonavir is a PI booster, not an NRTI.","C":"Not the NRTI mechanism.","A":"Hepatocytes have no integrase target.","D":"Not a drug effect."},"t":"NRTI class toxicity","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L18_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>NRTI class toxicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Zidovudine — infant prophylaxis","s":"A mother with HIV took TLD throughout pregnancy. Her newborn is given the oral antiretroviral the lecturer named for infant prophylaxis. At 5 weeks the baby is pale: Hb 8.5 g/dL, MCV raised, neutrophils low. Which description fits the drug responsible?","lec":"L18","sl":11,"c":"<b>Zidovudine — infant prophylaxis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"A cytidine analogue that terminates the growing viral DNA chain","B":"An adenosine nucleotide analogue that stops viral DNA synthesis","C":"A thymidine analogue that terminates the growing viral DNA chain","D":"A guanosine analogue that terminates the growing viral DNA chain","E":"An integrase inhibitor that blocks strand transfer into host DNA"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cytidine analogues are lamivudine and emtricitabine, both well tolerated with no marrow signature.","B":"Tenofovir, which the mother took. Its toxicity is kidney and bone, not marrow.","C":"Correct. The infant drug is zidovudine (AZT). It is a thymidine analogue NRTI whose bone-marrow toxicity gives macrocytic anaemia and neutropenia ('AZT → Anaemia'). She said the mother now takes the same TLD as everyone else, and the infant gets AZT.","D":"Abacavir, the drug with HLA-B*57:01 hypersensitivity.","E":"Dolutegravir, the third drug of TLD. It is not the infant drug and does not suppress the marrow."},"img":"slides_i/L18_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A man on an older HIV regimen has an Hb of 8 g/dL with an MCV of 112 fL, neutropenia, and darkening of his nails and palms. Which drug is responsible?","o":{"C":"Zidovudine (AZT)","B":"Tenofovir","D":"Dolutegravir","A":"Efavirenz","E":"Atazanavir"},"r":{"C":"Correct. 'AZT → Anaemia': marrow suppression gives MACROCYTIC anaemia, plus hyperpigmentation.","B":"Kidney and bone.","D":"Insomnia, weight gain, cation chelation.","A":"CNS effects.","E":"Unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, kidney stones."},"t":"Zidovudine","sl":11,"img":"slides_i/L18_11.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Zidovudine</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"TDF toxicity in HIV–HBV","s":"A woman with HIV and chronic hepatitis B has taken TLD for 6 years. She now has glycosuria with a normal blood glucose, phosphaturia and a low bone mineral density. Both viruses are suppressed. What is the best regimen change?","lec":"L18","sl":13,"c":"<b>TDF toxicity in HIV–HBV</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Change to TAF/FTC/DTG","B":"Change to TDF/3TC/DRV/r","C":"Change to TDF/FTC/EFV","D":"Change to AZT/3TC/DTG","E":"Add low-dose ritonavir to TLD"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Proximal tubulopathy (Fanconi) plus bone loss = TDF ('Toxic to kidney + Decreases bone'). TAF delivers the same tenofovir-diphosphate with much less renal and bone toxicity, and FTC plus TAF keep treating HBV.","B":"Still contains TDF; changing the third drug does not help the kidney or bone.","C":"Still contains TDF, the cause.","D":"Removes tenofovir, leaving lamivudine alone against HBV. Lamivudine has a low resistance barrier (~20% resistance), and AZT adds anaemia.","E":"Ritonavir boosts PIs, not tenofovir, and it would not remove the kidney toxicity."},"img":"slides_i/L18_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient on TDF/3TC/DTG develops glycosuria with normal blood glucose, phosphaturia and low bone density. What is the best adjustment?","o":{"A":"Switch TDF to TAF, which delivers the same active drug with much less renal and bone toxicity","C":"Stop dolutegravir, which causes Fanconi syndrome","B":"Add ritonavir to boost tenofovir","E":"Switch lamivudine to emtricitabine","D":"Continue unchanged; these are expected and harmless"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Proximal tubulopathy (Fanconi) + bone loss = TDF. TAF gives far lower plasma levels.","C":"DTG does not cause Fanconi.","B":"Would raise levels, and ritonavir is a PI booster.","E":"3TC and FTC are interchangeable and not the cause.","D":"Renal and bone damage must be acted on."},"t":"Tenofovir toxicity","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/L18_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Tenofovir toxicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Abacavir hypersensitivity","s":"A man with HIV and chronic kidney disease is started on the NRTI that is metabolised by alcohol dehydrogenase and needs no renal dose adjustment. On day 10 he has fever, rash, vomiting and malaise. What is the right action now, and what should have been done beforehand?","lec":"L18","sl":10,"c":"<b>Abacavir hypersensitivity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Stop it permanently; HLA-B*57:01 screening before the first dose","B":"Continue and monitor; renal function tests before the first dose","C":"Stop it, then restart at half dose; HLA-B*57:01 screening beforehand","D":"Continue with an antihistamine; HLA-B27 screening before the first dose","E":"Stop it permanently; G6PD testing before the first dose"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The NRTI needing no renal adjustment is abacavir (slide 10). Its potentially fatal hypersensitivity is linked to HLA-B*57:01. 'ABC = Allergy Before Continuing': if a reaction occurs, stop it and never rechallenge.","B":"A hypersensitivity reaction to abacavir is never continued, and a renal test was not the relevant precaution for a drug that needs no renal adjustment.","C":"Rechallenge is exactly what must never be done; a re-exposure reaction can be fatal.","D":"Wrong allele (HLA-B27 is ankylosing spondylitis) and wrong action.","E":"Right action, wrong test. G6PD matters for oxidant drugs such as primaquine, not abacavir."},"img":"slides_i/L18_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Before starting abacavir, which test is required, and why?","o":{"A":"HLA-B*57:01, because carriers risk a potentially fatal hypersensitivity reaction","C":"HLA-B27, because of ankylosing spondylitis risk","B":"G6PD, because of haemolysis","D":"CYP2D6 genotype, because of efavirenz levels","E":"No test is needed"},"r":{"A":"Correct. If a reaction occurs: STOP and NEVER rechallenge. Abacavir is also the NRTI that needs no renal dose adjustment.","C":"Wrong allele.","B":"Relevant to primaquine/dapsone, not abacavir.","D":"Not the slide's point.","E":"The slide says testing may be required before initiation."},"t":"Abacavir","sl":10,"img":"slides_i/L18_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Abacavir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"3TC + FTC duplication","s":"A man with HIV–HBV co-infection takes TAF/FTC/DTG. Worried about his hepatitis B, a doctor adds lamivudine 'for extra HBV cover'. Why is this addition wrong?","lec":"L18","sl":9,"c":"<b>3TC + FTC duplication</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Lamivudine and TAF compete for viral thymidine kinase activation","B":"Lamivudine chelates dolutegravir in the gut and lowers absorption","C":"Emtricitabine is an NNRTI, so adding an NRTI breaks the 2 + 1 rule","D":"Lamivudine has no activity against HBV, so it adds only toxicity","E":"Emtricitabine is a cytidine analogue that acts like lamivudine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"HIV drugs use host kinases; viral thymidine kinase is a herpes enzyme.","B":"Chelation is dolutegravir binding polyvalent cations (Ca, Fe, antacids), not lamivudine.","C":"Emtricitabine is an NRTI (cytidine analogue), not an NNRTI.","D":"Lamivudine is active against both HIV and HBV (its weakness is a low resistance barrier).","E":"Correct. FTC and 3TC are pharmacologically similar cytidine analogues: her table says do not use them together, and slide 9 says avoid two drugs of the same nucleoside analogue. FTC and TAF already cover HBV."},"img":"slides_i/L18_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A prescription lists tenofovir + lamivudine + emtricitabine + dolutegravir. What is wrong with it?","o":{"E":"Lamivudine and emtricitabine are near-identical cytidine analogues and must not be given together","D":"Dolutegravir cannot be combined with tenofovir because they share a transporter","A":"Tenofovir must always be boosted with ritonavir to reach effective levels","B":"Nothing — four drugs are better than three for preventing resistance","C":"Emtricitabine has no activity against HIV, so it adds only toxicity"},"r":{"E":"Correct. The backbone is TDF/TAF + ONE of 3TC or FTC.","D":"TDF + 3TC + DTG is TLD, the first-line regimen.","A":"Boosting is for PIs.","B":"Duplication adds toxicity and shared resistance, not potency.","C":"FTC is an active NRTI."},"t":"3TC vs FTC","sl":9,"img":"slides_i/L18_9.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>3TC vs FTC</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"NNRTI class properties","s":"Three weeks after starting an older HIV regimen, a woman develops a widespread blistering rash with mouth ulcers (Stevens–Johnson syndrome) and raised transaminases. Which statement about the drug class responsible is correct?","lec":"L18","sl":14,"c":"<b>NNRTI class properties</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"It has no CYP-mediated interactions, so it is easy to combine","B":"It causes no marrow toxicity and no cross-resistance with NRTIs","C":"It needs three host-kinase phosphorylations before it can act","D":"It is built into proviral DNA and terminates the growing chain","E":"It is given with low-dose ritonavir to overcome poor absorption"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The opposite: NNRTIs are both CYP inducers and inhibitors, with many interactions (slide 14).","B":"Correct. Rash/SJS plus hepatotoxicity = nevirapine (her table), an NNRTI. Slide 14 lists the NNRTI advantages: no adverse effect on bone marrow and no cross-resistance with NRTIs.","C":"That is the NRTI activation. NNRTIs need no activation by cellular enzymes.","D":"Chain termination is the NRTI mechanism; NNRTIs bind an allosteric site and are not incorporated.","E":"Ritonavir boosting belongs to the protease inhibitors (ATV/r, DRV/r, LPV/r)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which is an ADVANTAGE of NNRTIs over NRTIs listed in the lecture?","o":{"B":"No bone-marrow toxicity and no cross-resistance with NRTIs","A":"No drug interactions at all","D":"They never cause rash","E":"They are safe at any stage of pregnancy","C":"They need no partner drugs"},"r":{"B":"Correct.","A":"They are CYP inducers and inhibitors, with many interactions.","D":"Rash and SJS are key disadvantages (nevirapine).","E":"Efavirenz <8 weeks → neural tube defect.","C":"Always part of a combination."},"t":"NNRTI profile","sl":14,"img":"slides_i/L18_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>NNRTI profile</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Rilpivirine needs gastric acid","s":"A man whose HIV was suppressed for two years on TDF/FTC/rilpivirine returns with a viral load of 8,000 copies/mL. He reports perfect adherence. Which recently started medicine most likely explains the rebound?","lec":"L18","sl":32,"c":"<b>Rilpivirine needs gastric acid</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Inhaled fluticasone for asthma","B":"Omeprazole for reflux","C":"Sildenafil for erectile dysfunction","D":"Metformin for diabetes","E":"Ergotamine for migraine"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Fluticasone is the victim of ritonavir's CYP3A4 inhibition (Cushing's), not a cause of HIV rebound.","B":"Correct. Rilpivirine requires gastric acidity to be absorbed, so proton-pump inhibitors are contraindicated ('RPV = Requires food + Requires low pH'). Less absorption → low levels → virological failure.","C":"Sildenafil levels are raised by ritonavir-boosted PIs (slide 32); it does not lower antiretroviral levels.","D":"Metformin interacts with dolutegravir (DTG raises metformin exposure), and it does not lower rilpivirine.","E":"Ergotamine plus a boosted PI causes ergotism (slide 32); it does not reduce rilpivirine."},"img":"slides_i/L18_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A patient on rilpivirine starts omeprazole for reflux, and his viral load rebounds. Why?","o":{"B":"Rilpivirine needs an acidic stomach to be absorbed, so a proton-pump inhibitor cuts its levels","A":"Omeprazole activates HIV integrase","E":"Rilpivirine causes reflux resistance","C":"Omeprazole is a CYP3A4 inhibitor that destroys HIV","D":"Rilpivirine must be taken on an empty stomach"},"r":{"B":"Correct. PPIs are contraindicated with rilpivirine. Take it WITH food.","A":"Nonsense.","E":"Nonsense.","C":"Not the mechanism.","D":"The opposite: with food."},"t":"Rilpivirine","sl":1002,"img":"slides_i/L18_1002.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Rilpivirine</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Darunavir — preferred PI","s":"A patient's virus becomes resistant on first-line therapy, and a boosted protease inhibitor is chosen for the new regimen. According to the 2025 guideline taught, which PI is preferred, and for what reason?","lec":"L18","sl":16,"c":"<b>Darunavir — preferred PI</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Darunavir, because of its high genetic barrier to resistance","B":"Indinavir, because it needs no booster to reach good levels","C":"Ritonavir, because it is the most potent CYP3A4 inhibitor","D":"Lopinavir, because it has the longest record of clinical use","E":"Atazanavir, because it spares glucose and fat metabolism"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 17: darunavir is the preferred PI in the 2025 guideline (WHO/IAS–USA), with a high genetic barrier to resistance, effective in treatment-experienced patients. She said darunavir 'overtook' atazanavir.","B":"All PIs have poor oral bioavailability and are CYP3A4 substrates; indinavir is not the preferred PI.","C":"The reason is true, but that is why ritonavir is used at low dose as a booster, not as the preferred anti-HIV PI.","D":"Lopinavir/r is the older boosted PI with diarrhoea and hypertriglyceridaemia; recognise it, do not prefer it.","E":"The reason is true (slide 16: metabolic effects 'except atazanavir'), but atazanavir is no longer the preferred PI; darunavir has the better tolerability and resistance profile."},"img":"slides_i/L18_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"An HIV patient on long-term ART develops hyperglycaemia, high triglycerides, a buffalo hump and wasting of the face and limbs. Which class is responsible, and which member is the exception?","o":{"A":"Protease inhibitors; atazanavir has the least metabolic effect","E":"NRTIs; tenofovir is the exception","B":"INSTIs; dolutegravir is the exception","D":"NNRTIs; efavirenz is the exception","C":"Entry inhibitors; maraviroc is the exception"},"r":{"A":"Correct. 'PI disturbs metabolism'; atazanavir spares glucose and fat.","E":"Not the metabolic class.","B":"DTG: weight gain, but not this syndrome.","D":"CNS effects.","C":"She did not emphasise entry inhibitors."},"t":"PI metabolic effects","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L18_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>PI metabolic effects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Atazanavir — bilirubin and stones","s":"A man with HIV and type 2 diabetes is switched to the boosted PI that least disturbs glucose and fat metabolism. Two months later he is mildly jaundiced: unconjugated bilirubin 3.4 mg/dL, ALT and ALP normal. A week later he has renal colic from a stone. What is the best interpretation?","lec":"L18","sl":16,"c":"<b>Atazanavir — bilirubin and stones</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Fanconi syndrome from a tenofovir backbone","B":"Cholestatic hepatitis caused by the ritonavir booster","C":"Expected adverse effects of his new antiretroviral","D":"Acute hepatitis B flare after the regimen switch","E":"Haemolysis caused by the ritonavir booster"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Fanconi syndrome (TDF) causes glycosuria and phosphaturia, not unconjugated jaundice.","B":"Cholestasis would raise ALP and conjugated bilirubin; here ALP is normal and the bilirubin is unconjugated.","C":"Correct. The PI that spares glucose and fat is atazanavir (slide 16, 'except atazanavir'). Her table: unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia/jaundice and nephrolithiasis ('ATV = Affects bilirubin'). It is benign, with normal liver enzymes.","D":"A hepatitis flare would raise ALT and give conjugated plus unconjugated bilirubin.","E":"Ritonavir is a CYP3A4 booster; haemolysis is not its described effect (haemolytic anaemia is ribavirin)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A well patient on ATV/r becomes mildly jaundiced with an isolated UNCONJUGATED hyperbilirubinaemia and normal liver enzymes. What is the explanation?","o":{"C":"An expected atazanavir effect (inhibited bilirubin conjugation)","B":"Acute hepatitis B reactivation","A":"Ritonavir-induced haemolysis","D":"Zidovudine-induced marrow failure","E":"Nevirapine hepatotoxicity"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Benign, Gilbert-like; it also forms kidney stones.","B":"Enzymes are normal and the bilirubin is unconjugated.","A":"Not described.","D":"Not in the regimen.","E":"Not in the regimen."},"t":"Atazanavir","sl":16,"img":"slides_i/L18_16.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Atazanavir</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Protease inhibitor mechanism","s":"In a cell-culture experiment, HIV-infected T cells treated with drug X still bud virions, but the particles contain uncleaved gag-pol polyprotein, have no condensed core and cannot infect new cells. Which drug is X?","lec":"L18","sl":6,"c":"<b>Protease inhibitor mechanism</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Darunavir","B":"Rilpivirine","C":"Zidovudine","D":"Enfuvirtide","E":"Dolutegravir"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. PIs block HIV aspartyl protease cleavage of the gag/gag-pol polyprotein, so virions bud but stay immature and non-infectious (her memory concept). Darunavir is the preferred PI (-navir).","B":"An NNRTI: it blocks reverse transcription before any provirus exists.","C":"An NRTI: chain termination of viral DNA, an early step.","D":"A gp41 fusion inhibitor: it blocks entry into new cells, not virion maturation.","E":"An INSTI: it acts earlier, blocking integration, so infected cells would not produce virions at all."},"img":"slides_i/L18_6.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Why is low-dose ritonavir added to darunavir?","o":{"A":"Ritonavir strongly inhibits CYP3A4, slowing darunavir's metabolism and raising its levels","D":"Ritonavir is the main antiviral, and darunavir only reduces side effects","E":"Ritonavir induces CYP3A4 to lower darunavir toxicity","C":"Ritonavir inhibits integrase","B":"Ritonavir protects the kidneys from darunavir"},"r":{"A":"Correct. 'RTV = booster'. The same logic applies to nirmatrelvir/ritonavir (Paxlovid).","D":"Reversed.","E":"It INHIBITS CYP3A4.","C":"It is a PI.","B":"Not its role."},"t":"Ritonavir as booster","sl":15,"img":"slides_i/L18_15.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Ritonavir as booster</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Ritonavir–steroid interaction","s":"A 30-year-old woman whose asthma is controlled on an inhaled corticosteroid starts TDF/FTC plus a boosted protease inhibitor. Six weeks later she has a moon face, central obesity and purple striae. Which mechanism explains this?","lec":"L18","sl":15,"c":"<b>Ritonavir–steroid interaction</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Ritonavir inhibits CYP3A4, so the steroid accumulates in the blood","B":"Tenofovir damages the proximal tubule, so cortisol is retained","C":"Emtricitabine blocks mitochondrial polymerase in adipose tissue","D":"The steroid induces CYP3A4, so the booster is cleared faster","E":"Ritonavir induces CYP3A4, so a toxic steroid metabolite builds up"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slide 32: boosted PIs raise fluticasone ~350-fold → iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome. Ritonavir is the most potent P450 inhibitor (slide 15). She said you must know what the patient already takes before starting ART.","B":"Tenofovir tubulopathy causes phosphaturia and bone loss, not cortisol retention.","C":"Mitochondrial toxicity causes lipoatrophy (fat loss), and it belongs to older NRTIs such as stavudine.","D":"Direction reversed; the steroid is the victim, not the perpetrator, and HIV rebound would not cause Cushing's.","E":"Ritonavir inhibits, not induces, CYP3A4; the parent steroid accumulates."},"img":"slides_i/L18_15.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"An HIV patient on a ritonavir-boosted regimen uses an inhaled fluticasone inhaler for asthma and develops a moon face and central obesity. What happened?","o":{"A":"Ritonavir inhibited CYP3A4, raising fluticasone levels ~350-fold and causing iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome","D":"Fluticasone induced ritonavir metabolism, lowering levels and causing HIV rebound","B":"PI lipodystrophy alone explains it; the inhaled steroid is irrelevant here","C":"Fluticasone blocked the HIV protease and caused a steroid-like syndrome","E":"Ritonavir caused adrenal insufficiency, which presents with a moon face"},"r":{"A":"Correct (slide 32). The same mechanism gives ERGOTISM with ergotamine.","D":"The direction is reversed.","B":"The steroid interaction is the named cause on the slide.","C":"Nonsense.","E":"Cushingoid, not insufficiency."},"t":"Ritonavir interactions","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/L18_32.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Ritonavir interactions</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Dolutegravir chelation","s":"A pregnant woman with HIV takes the first-line Thai single tablet each morning together with her prenatal iron and calcium tablets. At 20 weeks her viral load, previously undetectable, is 900 copies/mL, although adherence is good. What is the best advice?","lec":"L18","sl":23,"c":"<b>Dolutegravir chelation</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Add low-dose ritonavir to boost the whole regimen","B":"Switch the lamivudine in the tablet to emtricitabine","C":"Take the minerals several hours apart from the ARV pill","D":"Stop the iron and calcium until after she delivers","E":"Move the ARV tablet to bedtime and take it on an empty stomach"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Ritonavir boosts PIs; dolutegravir does not need a booster (elvitegravir is the INSTI that needs cobicistat).","B":"3TC and FTC are interchangeable; neither is the problem.","C":"Correct. The tablet is TLD, which is also used in pregnancy. Dolutegravir chelates polyvalent cations → ↓ absorption with antacids, iron and calcium ('DTG = Don't Take with minerals Together'). Separating the doses fixes it without stopping her supplements.","D":"Her supplements are needed in pregnancy; the interaction is solved by spacing the doses, not by stopping them. (Taking the tablet with food is the rilpivirine rule.)","E":"Bedtime on an empty stomach is the efavirenz instruction (for its CNS effects)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_23.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient on TLD (tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir) takes calcium and iron supplements at the same time as his pill and develops low-level viraemia. What is the cause?","o":{"C":"Dolutegravir chelates polyvalent cations, so co-administered calcium and iron reduce its absorption","A":"Iron inhibits reverse transcriptase, so the NRTI backbone stops working","E":"Calcium induces CYP3A4, which speeds up dolutegravir metabolism","D":"Lamivudine binds iron in the gut, so the backbone is not absorbed","B":"Tenofovir is destroyed by calcium in the stomach before absorption"},"r":{"C":"Correct: separate the doses. DTG also raises metformin levels.","A":"No.","E":"No.","D":"Not the slide's point.","B":"No."},"t":"INSTIs","sl":19,"img":"slides_i/L18_19.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>INSTIs</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Occupational PEP","s":"A nurse sustains a deep needlestick injury from a patient with uncontrolled HIV and reaches the clinic 10 hours later. What should she receive?","lec":"L18","sl":28,"c":"<b>Occupational PEP</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"TDF/FTC plus dolutegravir, started now and taken for 6 months","B":"TDF/FTC plus dolutegravir, started now and taken for 28 days","C":"TDF/FTC alone, started now and taken for 28 days","D":"Cabotegravir injection now, repeated after 2 months","E":"Zidovudine alone, started now and taken for 28 days"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right drugs, wrong duration: PEP is 28 days.","B":"Correct. Slide 31: occupational PEP as soon as possible (no later than 72 h), taken for 28 days, with (TDF/FTC or TAF/FTC) + (DTG or BIC or DRV/r).","C":"PEP uses a three-drug regimen: a two-drug backbone plus a third agent. Two-drug TDF/FTC alone is oral PrEP.","D":"Cabotegravir every 2 months is injectable PrEP, for people with ongoing risk, not post-exposure treatment.","E":"Zidovudine monotherapy is an obsolete approach; monotherapy is never used now."},"img":"slides_i/L18_28.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which is the recommended first-line regimen for a newly diagnosed Thai adult with HIV (2026)?","o":{"B":"TDF + lamivudine + dolutegravir as one fixed-dose tablet daily (TLD)","A":"Zidovudine monotherapy","E":"Two protease inhibitors without a backbone","D":"Lamivudine + emtricitabine + efavirenz","C":"Maraviroc + enfuvirtide"},"r":{"B":"Correct. 2 + 1 in a single tablet, which aids adherence. The alternative is TAF/FTC/DTG.","A":"Monotherapy breeds resistance.","E":"Not a valid structure.","D":"Two same-slot NRTIs.","C":"Entry inhibitors are second-line."},"t":"First-line regimen","sl":21,"img":"slides_i/L18_21.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>First-line regimen</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"When to start ART","s":"HIV infection was confirmed this morning in an asymptomatic 25-year-old man; CD4 650 cells/µL, HBsAg negative, no other illness. Which plan follows the current guideline taught?","lec":"L18","sl":20,"c":"<b>When to start ART</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Defer ART, because his CD4 count is still above 350 cells/µL","B":"Defer ART, because none of the 2012 DHHS start criteria is met","C":"Defer ART, because HBV co-infection is the trigger for early treatment","D":"Start ART now, because early treatment lowers complications and spread","E":"Defer ART, because he has no HIV-related symptoms yet"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The old CD4 < 350 threshold is superseded.","B":"The DHHS 2012 table (CD4 < 350, HBV co-infection, pregnancy, symptoms) is shown only as history; it is superseded.","C":"HBV co-infection was one of the old 2012 triggers; now everyone starts.","D":"Correct. WHO since 2015: same-day ART once HIV is diagnosed, regardless of CD4. Earlier treatment lowers viral load faster, reduces complications and cuts transmission (slide 20).","E":"Symptoms are no longer required."},"img":"slides_i/L18_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"HIV infection is confirmed today in an asymptomatic 25-year-old with a CD4 count of 650. When should ART start?","o":{"D":"Today — same-day ART, regardless of the CD4 count","B":"Only when the CD4 count falls below 350","E":"Only when symptoms appear","C":"After 6 months of observation","A":"Only if he is co-infected with HBV"},"r":{"D":"Correct: 'confirmed → start the same day'. Earlier ART means faster viral suppression, fewer complications and less transmission.","B":"The superseded 2012 threshold.","E":"Superseded.","C":"No.","A":"HBV co-infection was an old trigger; now everyone starts."},"t":"When to start ART","sl":20,"img":"slides_i/L18_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>When to start ART</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Oral PrEP lead-in","s":"A woman whose husband has HIV (not yet suppressed) starts daily oral TDF/FTC today. She asks from when vaginal sex will be protected. What did the lecturer advise?","lec":"L18","sl":27,"c":"<b>Oral PrEP lead-in</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"About 2 hours after the first tablet","B":"After about 4 days of daily tablets","C":"After about 6 days of daily tablets","D":"After a 2-1-1 double loading dose","E":"After about 28 days of daily tablets"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Oral PrEP does not protect within hours; tissue levels take days.","B":"4 days is the figure she gave for men.","C":"Correct (spoken in class). Protective drug levels take at least about 6 days in women and 4 days in men, because the drug builds up in vaginal tissue more slowly.","D":"On-demand 2-1-1 dosing is for men who have sex with men (slide 28), not for a woman.","E":"28 days is the PEP duration (slide 31)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_27.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A woman in a serodiscordant relationship starts daily oral TDF/FTC PrEP today and asks when she will be protected during vaginal sex. What is the best answer?","o":{"C":"After at least about 6 days of daily dosing, when drug levels in genital tissue become protective","B":"Immediately after the first tablet, because oral PrEP acts within hours","E":"Only after 6 months of daily tablets, when full protection is reached","D":"Never — oral PrEP protects men only, so women need the vaginal ring","A":"After 1 day, since tissue levels build at the same rate as in men"},"r":{"C":"Correct: she said at least 4 days for men and 6 days for women. On-demand 2-1-1 is for MSM.","B":"Tissue levels take days to build.","E":"Too long.","D":"PrEP protects women too.","A":"Men need at least 4 days, and women longer."},"t":"PrEP lead-in","sl":27,"img":"slides_i/L18_27.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>PrEP lead-in</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Injectable PrEP","s":"A 22-year-old man who has sex with men wants PrEP but keeps forgetting daily pills, and he wants as few injection visits as possible, ideally two a year. Which drug fits, and what is its class?","lec":"L18","sl":18,"c":"<b>Injectable PrEP</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Cabotegravir, an integrase inhibitor","B":"Cabotegravir, a capsid inhibitor","C":"Enfuvirtide, a fusion inhibitor","D":"Lenacapavir, an integrase inhibitor","E":"Lenacapavir, a capsid inhibitor"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Correct class for cabotegravir (-tegravir = INSTI), but it is given IM every 2 months, six visits a year.","B":"Cabotegravir is an INSTI, given every 2 months.","C":"Enfuvirtide is a subcutaneous gp41 fusion inhibitor used as second-line treatment, not PrEP.","D":"Lenacapavir is not an integrase inhibitor; it is the one class you cannot guess from the name.","E":"Correct. Lenacapavir: subcutaneous every 6 months (~96–100%), a first-in-class HIV capsid inhibitor."},"img":"slides_i/L18_18.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing of injectable PrEP drug, class and interval is correct?","o":{"E":"Lenacapavir — HIV capsid inhibitor — subcutaneous every 6 months","C":"Cabotegravir — protease inhibitor — every 6 months","B":"Lenacapavir — integrase inhibitor — monthly","D":"Cabotegravir — NNRTI — daily injection","A":"Dapivirine — capsid inhibitor — every 2 months"},"r":{"E":"Correct: twice a year. Cabotegravir = INSTI ('-tegravir'), IM every 2 months.","C":"Cabotegravir is an INSTI, given every 2 months.","B":"Lenacapavir is a capsid inhibitor, given every 6 months.","D":"No.","A":"Dapivirine is an NNRTI vaginal ring (L15)."},"t":"Injectable PrEP","sl":28,"img":"slides_i/L18_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Injectable PrEP</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Efavirenz in early pregnancy","s":"A woman with HIV takes an older single tablet whose third drug gave her vivid dreams and dizziness when she started it, so she takes it at bedtime. She discovers she is 5 weeks pregnant. Which fetal risk did the lecture link to that drug?","lec":"L18","sl":14,"c":"<b>Efavirenz in early pregnancy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Sensorineural deafness","B":"Neural tube defect","C":"Macrocytic anaemia","D":"Congenital cataract","E":"Limb hypoplasia"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"A congenital CMV/rubella feature.","B":"Correct. Vivid dreams + dizziness, taken at bedtime = efavirenz ('EFV = Effects on dreams'). Slide 14: efavirenz < 8 weeks of pregnancy → neural tube defect. The current regimen for pregnancy is TLD.","C":"Marrow toxicity is the zidovudine signature (the infant prophylaxis drug), not efavirenz.","D":"A congenital rubella feature, not a drug effect.","E":"Limb hypoplasia is a congenital varicella feature."},"img":"slides_i/L18_14.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"A pregnant woman is newly diagnosed with HIV at 14 weeks. Which management matches the current guideline taught?","o":{"B":"Start TLD the same day, continue through delivery, and give the newborn zidovudine prophylaxis","A":"Wait until after delivery to start ART","E":"Use a completely separate pregnancy-only regimen of stavudine","D":"Give acyclovir from 36 weeks","C":"Start efavirenz monotherapy"},"r":{"B":"Correct. She said pregnancy now uses the same regimen, so there is less to memorise. AZT is the infant drug.","A":"Most transmission is intrapartum, so suppress early.","E":"No longer separate; stavudine is obsolete.","D":"A herpes drug.","C":"Monotherapy is never used."},"t":"HIV in pregnancy","sl":25,"img":"slides_i/L18_25.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>HIV in pregnancy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Acyclovir selectivity","s":"Acyclovir is added to three cultures: (1) uninfected fibroblasts, (2) fibroblasts infected with HSV-1, (3) fibroblasts infected with CMV. Its triphosphate reaches high levels in only one culture. Which culture, and why?","lec":"L18","sl":38,"c":"<b>Acyclovir selectivity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Culture 2, because HSV DNA polymerase adds the first phosphate","B":"Culture 3, because CMV DNA polymerase binds the drug most tightly","C":"Culture 2, because HSV thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate","D":"Culture 3, because CMV UL97 kinase adds the first phosphate","E":"Culture 1, because host kinases work fastest in uninfected cells"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The polymerase is the target of the triphosphate, not the enzyme that phosphorylates the drug.","B":"Polymerase specificity is the second level of selectivity, but the drug must first be phosphorylated, which does not happen well in CMV-infected cells.","C":"Correct. Acyclovir is monophosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase (viral TK >> cellular kinases), then host kinases complete the triphosphate. That kinase specificity is why uninfected cells are spared (slide 44). 'Acyclovir needs viral TK.'","D":"UL97 is the CMV enzyme for ganciclovir; CMV lacks thymidine kinase, which is why ganciclovir, not acyclovir, is the CMV drug.","E":"In uninfected cells the drug stays inactive; cellular kinases barely touch it."},"img":"slides_i/L18_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why does acyclovir have so little toxicity to uninfected cells?","o":{"C":"Its first phosphorylation needs viral thymidine kinase, present only in infected cells, and its triphosphate prefers viral DNA polymerase","B":"It is activated by host kinases equally in all cells but is excreted quickly","E":"It acts only in the cytoplasm, where uninfected cells have no DNA synthesis","A":"It is a protease inhibitor, and human cells have no equivalent protease","D":"It is excreted before reaching most cells, so exposure is very low"},"r":{"C":"Correct: two levels of selectivity, kinase and polymerase (slide 44). 'Acyclovir needs viral TK.'","B":"The FIRST step is viral-specific.","E":"It acts on DNA replication in the nucleus.","A":"It is a guanosine analogue.","D":"Nonsense."},"t":"Acyclovir selectivity","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L18_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Acyclovir selectivity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"CMV prophylaxis — valganciclovir","s":"A seronegative kidney-transplant recipient receives an organ from a donor seropositive for the herpesvirus that classically causes retinitis in AIDS. He needs oral prophylaxis. Which drug is used, and which enzyme starts its activation?","lec":"L18","sl":42,"c":"<b>CMV prophylaxis — valganciclovir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Valacyclovir — viral UL97 kinase","B":"Foscarnet — host-cell kinases","C":"Valganciclovir — viral thymidine kinase","D":"Valganciclovir — viral UL97 kinase","E":"Valacyclovir — viral thymidine kinase"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Wrong drug: the val-prodrug of acyclovir is not the CMV drug.","B":"Foscarnet is IV only, and as a pyrophosphate analogue it needs no phosphorylation at all ('Fails to need phosphorylation').","C":"Right drug, wrong enzyme: thymidine kinase activates ganciclovir in HSV, but CMV uses UL97.","D":"Correct. Retinitis in AIDS = CMV → ganciclovir family. Valganciclovir is the oral L-valyl prodrug used for CMV prophylaxis in transplant patients (slide 45), and in CMV the first phosphorylation is done by UL97 (slide 42).","E":"Valacyclovir is the oral prodrug of acyclovir for HSV/VZV; CMV lacks thymidine kinase."},"img":"slides_i/L18_42.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Why is ganciclovir, not acyclovir, the drug for CMV retinitis?","o":{"D":"CMV lacks thymidine kinase but its UL97 kinase phosphorylates ganciclovir, which is 8–20× more active against CMV","A":"Acyclovir cannot cross into the eye at any dose, so retinitis cannot be reached","C":"Ganciclovir is a neuraminidase inhibitor that blocks CMV release","B":"Acyclovir is too toxic to the bone marrow for use in AIDS patients","E":"CMV is an RNA virus, so a guanosine analogue like acyclovir cannot act"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Match the drug to the virus: ACV → HSV/VZV, GCV → CMV.","A":"Not the reason taught.","C":"Wrong class.","B":"It is GANCICLOVIR that is marrow-toxic ('Ganciclovir → Granulocytopenia').","E":"CMV is a DNA herpesvirus."},"t":"Why CMV needs ganciclovir","sl":38,"img":"slides_i/L18_38.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Why CMV needs ganciclovir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Acyclovir crystalluria","s":"A 74-year-old man with fever, confusion and a temporal-lobe lesion on MRI is started on high-dose IV therapy with the drug of choice for the suspected viral encephalitis. He is drinking poorly. By day 3 his creatinine has doubled. What is the mechanism, and how is it prevented?","lec":"L18","sl":41,"c":"<b>Acyclovir crystalluria</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Proximal tubulopathy — switch to the alafenamide prodrug","B":"Allergic interstitial nephritis — HLA-B*57:01 screening first","C":"Pigment nephropathy from haemolysis — erythropoietin support","D":"Drug crystals in the renal tubules — IV fluids and renal dosing","E":"Tubular calcium and magnesium wasting — electrolyte replacement"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Tenofovir disoproxil → switch to TAF.","B":"HLA-B*57:01 screening is for abacavir hypersensitivity.","C":"Haemolytic anaemia is ribavirin, and erythropoietin is not a way to protect the kidney.","D":"Correct. Temporal-lobe encephalitis = HSV → IV acyclovir, which reaches the CSF. Her table: crystalluria/AKI, especially with high-dose IV use or dehydration → hydrate and adjust the dose to renal function.","E":"Electrolyte wasting (↓Ca, ↓Mg, ↓K) is foscarnet."},"img":"slides_i/L18_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"An elderly dehydrated patient receiving high-dose IV acyclovir for HSV encephalitis develops a rising creatinine. What is the mechanism, and how is it prevented?","o":{"D":"Acyclovir crystals precipitate in the renal tubules — prevent with hydration and renal dose adjustment","C":"Marrow suppression lowering renal perfusion — prevent with G-CSF","A":"Proximal tubulopathy (Fanconi syndrome) — prevent by switching to TAF","E":"Lactic acidosis from mitochondrial toxicity — prevent with thiamine","B":"Hypersensitivity nephritis — prevent by testing HLA-B*57:01 first"},"r":{"D":"Correct: crystalluria. Acyclovir crosses the blood–brain barrier, which is why it treats HSV encephalitis.","C":"That is ganciclovir's signature.","A":"Tenofovir.","E":"NRTIs.","B":"Abacavir."},"t":"Acyclovir toxicity","sl":41,"img":"slides_i/L18_41.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Acyclovir toxicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Foscarnet for TK-negative HSV","s":"A man with advanced AIDS has genital HSV ulcers that keep enlarging despite high-dose acyclovir; the isolate lacks thymidine kinase. Which drug still acts, and what must be monitored?","lec":"L18","sl":41,"c":"<b>Foscarnet for TK-negative HSV</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Foscarnet — full blood count for neutropenia","B":"Foscarnet — serum calcium, magnesium and creatinine","C":"Famciclovir — full blood count for neutropenia","D":"Valacyclovir — serum calcium, magnesium and creatinine","E":"Ganciclovir — full blood count for neutropenia"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Right drug, wrong monitoring: foscarnet is not the marrow-suppressing CMV drug; that is ganciclovir (the NL 2024 trap).","B":"Correct. Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate analogue that inhibits viral DNA polymerase directly, with no TK/UL97 activation ('FOS = Fails to need phosphorylation'). Toxicity: nephrotoxicity, ↓Ca²⁺/↓Mg²⁺/↓K⁺, seizures.","C":"Famciclovir → penciclovir, which is also activated by viral TK.","D":"Valacyclovir becomes acyclovir, which still needs TK.","E":"In HSV, ganciclovir's first phosphorylation is also by TK (slide 42), so it fails too."},"img":"slides_i/L18_41.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"An AIDS patient's genital HSV no longer responds to acyclovir because of a thymidine-kinase mutation. Which drug still works, and why?","o":{"B":"Foscarnet — it inhibits viral DNA polymerase directly without needing activation by TK","C":"Valacyclovir — its better absorption overcomes TK loss","E":"Famciclovir — it is activated by host kinases only","A":"Ganciclovir — UL97 replaces TK in HSV","D":"Oseltamivir"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Watch the kidneys and electrolytes (↓Ca/Mg/K → seizures).","C":"Still acyclovir, so it still needs TK.","E":"Penciclovir also needs viral TK.","A":"In HSV, ganciclovir also needs TK; UL97 is CMV's.","D":"An influenza drug."},"t":"Foscarnet","sl":45,"img":"slides_i/L18_45.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Foscarnet</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Anti-influenza sites of action","s":"Influenza A-infected cells are treated with three drugs in separate wells. Drug X leaves progeny virions clumped on the cell surface; drug Y stops new viral mRNA being made in the nucleus; drug Z traps the viral genome inside the endosome. Which set is correct?","lec":"L18","sl":49,"c":"<b>Anti-influenza sites of action</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"X oseltamivir; Y amantadine; Z baloxavir","B":"X amantadine; Y oseltamivir; Z baloxavir","C":"X oseltamivir; Y baloxavir; Z amantadine","D":"X baloxavir; Y amantadine; Z oseltamivir","E":"X zanamivir; Y oseltamivir; Z amantadine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"X is right, but Y and Z are swapped.","B":"Amantadine does not cause clumping at the surface; oseltamivir does not stop mRNA synthesis.","C":"Correct. Her summary line: neuraminidase → release (oseltamivir: progeny aggregate on the membrane); PA endonuclease → blocks viral mRNA transcription (baloxavir); M2 → uncoating (amantadine: no endosomal acidification, RNA not released to the cytosol).","D":"Every drug is in the wrong well.","E":"X is acceptable (zanamivir is also a neuraminidase inhibitor), but oseltamivir does not block mRNA transcription."},"img":"slides_i/L18_49.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which pairing of anti-influenza drug and blocked step is correct?","o":{"C":"Amantadine — uncoating (M2 proton channel)","A":"Oseltamivir — uncoating","D":"Baloxavir — release of new virions","B":"Zanamivir — viral RNA polymerase","E":"Favipiravir — M2 channel"},"r":{"C":"Correct. M2 blocked → no endosomal acidification → no uncoating.","A":"Oseltamivir blocks RELEASE (neuraminidase).","D":"Baloxavir blocks the PA endonuclease (transcription).","B":"Zanamivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor.","E":"Favipiravir inhibits the polymerase."},"t":"Anti-influenza targets","sl":49,"img":"slides_i/L18_49.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Anti-influenza targets</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Influenza drug choice — asthma + pregnancy","s":"A 29-year-old woman with asthma, 24 weeks pregnant, has PCR-confirmed influenza 20 hours after symptom onset. Which antiviral is the appropriate choice?","lec":"L18","sl":51,"c":"<b>Influenza drug choice — asthma + pregnancy</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Oral amantadine","B":"Oral favipiravir","C":"Oral baloxavir","D":"Inhaled zanamivir","E":"Oral oseltamivir"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Amantadine is teratogenic too, and resistance is widespread (all H3N2).","B":"Favipiravir: teratogenic, do not use in pregnancy (slide 53).","C":"Baloxavir is on the same 'do not use in pregnancy' slide (53), and it is second-line.","D":"Inhaled zanamivir can cause bronchospasm, especially in asthma/COPD.","E":"Correct. It is the only option not excluded: oral, covers influenza A and B, best given within 30–36 h, and its toxicity is GI upset. It is the first-line flu drug."},"img":"slides_i/L18_51.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 30-year-old with asthma has confirmed influenza A within 24 h of onset. Which neuraminidase inhibitor is preferred, and why?","o":{"E":"Oral oseltamivir, because inhaled zanamivir can cause bronchospasm in asthma","D":"Inhaled zanamivir, because it is safest in asthma","B":"Amantadine, because it is first-line","C":"No treatment, because oseltamivir works only on influenza B","A":"Acyclovir"},"r":{"E":"Correct: the route decides the toxicity. Swallowed → GI upset; inhaled → airways.","D":"The opposite.","B":"Widespread resistance (all H3N2).","C":"Oseltamivir covers A and B.","A":"A herpes drug."},"t":"Oseltamivir vs zanamivir","sl":52,"img":"slides_i/L18_52.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Oseltamivir vs zanamivir</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"Chronic HBV after lamivudine failure","s":"A man has been HBsAg-positive for 3 years, with high HBV DNA and moderate inflammation on biopsy. Earlier treatment with lamivudine failed because of resistance mutations. Which drug does the lecture prefer now?","lec":"L18","sl":58,"c":"<b>Chronic HBV after lamivudine failure</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Lamivudine","B":"Tenofovir","C":"Entecavir","D":"Emtricitabine","E":"Telbivudine"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The drug that already failed. Slide 60: ~20% resistance and ~80% relapse after stopping; resistance is not overcome by giving it again.","B":"Correct. Slide 60: tenofovir is preferred if there is a history of lamivudine resistance. She called tenofovir 'number 1' for HBV.","C":"A preferred first-line HBV drug, but its high resistance barrier applies to nucleoside-naïve patients (her table); after lamivudine resistance tenofovir is preferred.","D":"A cytidine analogue like lamivudine: active against HBV but with the same resistance problem.","E":"An alternative drug whose use is limited by resistance."},"img":"slides_i/L18_58.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient has been HBsAg-positive for 9 months with high HBV DNA and active liver inflammation. Which drug is preferred, and why is lamivudine not first choice?","o":{"B":"Tenofovir (or entecavir) — lamivudine has a low resistance barrier and frequent relapse after stopping","A":"Lamivudine — it has the highest resistance barrier of the HBV drugs","D":"Oseltamivir — HBV release depends on a neuraminidase-like enzyme","C":"No treatment — chronic HBV resolves by itself in about 90% of cases","E":"Ribavirin monotherapy — it is the standard nucleoside for hepatitis"},"r":{"B":"Correct. Chronic = HBsAg >6 months. She called tenofovir 'number 1'.","A":"Reversed.","D":"No.","C":"Chronic HBV risks cirrhosis and HCC.","E":"Not an HBV drug."},"t":"Chronic HBV — when and what","sl":58,"img":"slides_i/L18_58.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Chronic HBV — when and what</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Interferon-α adverse effects","s":"A 28-year-old with compensated chronic hepatitis B chooses a finite course of a weekly injection that raises MHC class I on hepatocytes and activates NK cells. Which problem is most likely to appear during the course?","lec":"L18","sl":62,"c":"<b>Interferon-α adverse effects</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia","B":"Glycosuria with phosphaturia and osteomalacia","C":"Crystalluria with a rising creatinine","D":"Symptomatic bradycardia with amiodarone","E":"Leukopenia with new thyroid autoimmunity"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Atazanavir.","B":"Tenofovir disoproxil (Fanconi, bone).","C":"High-dose IV acyclovir.","D":"The sofosbuvir–amiodarone interaction.","E":"Correct. The drug is peginterferon-α. Her table: flu-like symptoms, bone-marrow suppression, psychiatric effects, autoimmune/thyroid disorders. It is contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis."},"img":"slides_i/L18_62.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which patient with chronic viral hepatitis should NOT receive pegylated interferon-α?","o":{"E":"A patient with decompensated cirrhosis","B":"A patient with compensated chronic hepatitis B","C":"A patient with chronic hepatitis C and normal liver function","D":"A young patient wanting a finite treatment course","A":"A patient with an HBV DNA of 10⁵ copies/mL"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Interferon boosts the immune attack on the liver, which a decompensated liver cannot survive.","B":"An appropriate candidate.","C":"An appropriate candidate.","D":"A finite course is one of its advantages.","A":"High viral load is an indication, not a contraindication."},"t":"Interferon-α","sl":62,"img":"slides_i/L18_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Interferon-α</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"HCV NS5A inhibitor","s":"A patient with chronic hepatitis C of unknown genotype is prescribed a pangenotypic two-drug tablet. One component is a uridine nucleotide prodrug. What does the other component inhibit?","lec":"L18","sl":64,"c":"<b>HCV NS5A inhibitor</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"The viral main protease Mpro","B":"The NS5B RNA polymerase","C":"The NS3/4A serine protease","D":"The viral reverse transcriptase","E":"The NS5A replication complex"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Mpro is the SARS-CoV-2 protease targeted by nirmatrelvir.","B":"That is sofosbuvir itself (-buvir).","C":"-previr drugs such as simeprevir.","D":"HCV is a (+)RNA flavivirus with no reverse transcriptase.","E":"Correct. The pangenotypic tablet is SOF/VEL. Sofosbuvir is the uridine nucleotide prodrug (NS5B), so the other drug is velpatasvir: -asvir = NS5A replication complex."},"img":"slides_i/L18_64.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which pairing of HCV direct-acting antiviral and target is correct?","o":{"E":"Velpatasvir — NS5A replication complex","B":"Sofosbuvir — NS3/4A protease","D":"Simeprevir — NS5B polymerase","C":"Ledipasvir — NS3/4A protease","A":"Ribavirin — NS5A"},"r":{"E":"Correct. -asvir = NS5A.","B":"-buvir = NS5B polymerase.","D":"-previr = protease.","C":"-asvir = NS5A.","A":"Ribavirin is a guanosine analogue with an uncertain mechanism."},"t":"HCV DAA targets","sl":64,"img":"slides_i/L18_64.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HCV DAA targets</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"HCV with decompensated cirrhosis","s":"A 58-year-old with chronic HCV infection has ascites, jaundice, a low albumin and a prolonged INR. Which regimen does the lecture recommend?","lec":"L18","sl":62,"c":"<b>HCV with decompensated cirrhosis</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Peginterferon-α with ribavirin","B":"Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir without ribavirin","C":"Entecavir with ribavirin","D":"Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir with ribavirin","E":"Peginterferon-α without ribavirin"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The legacy PR regimen; interferon is contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis.","B":"For chronic HCV without cirrhosis.","C":"Entecavir is an HBV drug.","D":"Correct. Ascites + jaundice + low albumin + high INR = decompensated cirrhosis. Slide 66 and her rule: no cirrhosis = SOF/VEL (2 drugs); decompensated cirrhosis = SOF/VEL + ribavirin (3).","E":"Interferon is contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis; alone, it is the acute-HCV option."},"img":"slides_i/L18_62.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A patient with chronic hepatitis C has DECOMPENSATED cirrhosis. Which regimen does the lecture recommend?","o":{"D":"Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir plus ribavirin","A":"Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir alone","C":"Pegylated interferon alone","E":"Tenofovir","B":"Oseltamivir"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Her rule: no cirrhosis = 2 drugs, decompensated = 3.","A":"For chronic HCV WITHOUT cirrhosis.","C":"Interferon is contraindicated in decompensation.","E":"An HBV/HIV drug.","B":"An influenza drug."},"t":"HCV regimen by liver status","sl":65,"img":"slides_i/L18_65.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>HCV regimen by liver status</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"COVID-19 antiviral order","s":"A 76-year-old man with diabetes has mild COVID-19 on day 2 of symptoms. He is on rifampicin-based tuberculosis treatment that cannot be interrupted. Which antiviral does the lecture's order of preference point to?","lec":"L18","sl":32,"c":"<b>COVID-19 antiviral order</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Baloxavir, an oral endonuclease inhibitor","B":"Molnupiravir, an oral RdRp mutagen","C":"Oseltamivir, an oral neuraminidase inhibitor","D":"Remdesivir, an intravenous RdRp inhibitor","E":"Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir, an oral Mpro inhibitor"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"An influenza PA endonuclease inhibitor.","B":"Third in the order, behind remdesivir.","C":"An influenza neuraminidase inhibitor.","D":"Correct. He qualifies for treatment (mild disease + risk factors, within 5–7 days). The first choice, nirmatrelvir/ritonavir, is ruled out by its major CYP3A interactions (rifampicin induces CYP3A and cuts PI levels, slide 32), so the next in her order is IV remdesivir.","E":"First choice normally, but ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitors should not be co-administered with rifampicin."},"img":"slides_i/L18_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 72-year-old with diabetes has mild COVID-19 on day 2 and takes no interacting drugs. Which is the preferred antiviral?","o":{"D":"Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir (Paxlovid), an oral main-protease inhibitor with a CYP3A4 booster","E":"Molnupiravir as first choice","B":"Oseltamivir","C":"No antiviral, because COVID drugs are only for ventilated patients","A":"Acyclovir"},"r":{"D":"Correct: first line. Remdesivir (IV) is second, molnupiravir third. Treat the risk group ('608') within 5–7 days.","E":"Molnupiravir is third.","B":"Influenza.","C":"The target group is MILD disease with risk factors.","A":"Herpes."},"t":"COVID drug order","sl":73,"img":"slides_i/L18_73.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>COVID drug order</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"Molnupiravir in pregnancy","s":"A woman at 9 weeks of pregnancy has mild COVID-19 with risk factors. A pharmacist refuses to dispense one of the oral COVID-19 drugs because of the way it works. Which mechanism concerned the pharmacist?","lec":"L18","sl":74,"c":"<b>Molnupiravir in pregnancy</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Incorporation into viral RNA causing lethal mutagenesis","B":"Blockade of the cap-dependent viral endonuclease","C":"Delayed RNA chain elongation by the RdRp","D":"Inhibition of the SARS-CoV-2 main protease","E":"Inhibition of hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Molnupiravir (oral) is incorporated by RdRp and causes excessive mutation (lethal mutagenesis), which raises the embryotoxic/teratogenic concern. She said it is banned in pregnancy.","B":"Baloxavir, an influenza drug (itself not for pregnancy, but not a COVID drug).","C":"Remdesivir, which is IV and not flagged as teratogenic.","D":"Nirmatrelvir, not flagged as teratogenic.","E":"Ritonavir's booster action: an interaction concern, not a teratogenic one."},"img":"slides_i/L18_74.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A pregnant woman at 10 weeks has mild COVID-19 with risk factors. Which antiviral must be AVOIDED?","o":{"A":"Molnupiravir","B":"Remdesivir","C":"Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir after checking interactions","D":"Supportive care","E":"Paracetamol"},"r":{"A":"Correct. Lethal mutagenesis gives an embryotoxic/teratogenic concern; she said it is banned in pregnancy. The same warning covers favipiravir, baloxavir, ribavirin and amantadine.","B":"Not flagged as teratogenic on the slide.","C":"Not flagged as teratogenic, though interactions must be checked.","D":"Always appropriate.","E":"Not an antiviral."},"t":"Teratogenic antivirals","sl":53,"img":"slides_i/L18_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Teratogenic antivirals</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Ganciclovir + zidovudine marrow toxicity","s":"A man with AIDS on an older AZT/3TC/EFV regimen develops floaters and retinal haemorrhages, and IV induction with the prototype drug for this infection is started. Two weeks later his neutrophils are 400/µL and Hb 7.5 g/dL; creatinine is unchanged. What is the best explanation?","lec":"L18","sl":11,"c":"<b>Ganciclovir + zidovudine marrow toxicity</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Loss of viral UL97 letting host marrow cells activate the drug","B":"Efavirenz converting the retinitis drug to a marrow-toxic metabolite","C":"Additive marrow suppression by zidovudine and the retinitis drug","D":"Crystal nephropathy from the retinitis drug lowering erythropoietin","E":"Lamivudine chelating iron and blocking red-cell production"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"UL97 loss causes ganciclovir resistance, not toxicity.","B":"No such interaction was taught; efavirenz's signature is CNS effects.","C":"Correct. Retinitis in AIDS = CMV → ganciclovir ('Ganciclovir → Granulocytopenia'). Her table warns of additive marrow toxicity with other myelosuppressive drugs, and zidovudine causes anaemia and neutropenia.","D":"Creatinine is unchanged, so no nephropathy; and it would not explain the neutropenia.","E":"Chelation of minerals is dolutegravir, and lamivudine is the clean NRTI."},"img":"slides_i/L18_11.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which antiviral is associated with BOTH dose-limiting marrow suppression AND crystalluria requiring hydration?","o":{"C":"Ganciclovir","A":"Tenofovir disoproxil","B":"Oseltamivir","D":"Dolutegravir","E":"Foscarnet"},"r":{"C":"Correct (slide 43). That is why the NL 2024 item (cytopenias + rising creatinine) is ganciclovir.","A":"Kidney and bone, but not marrow.","B":"GI effects.","D":"Mild.","E":"Kidney + electrolytes, but NOT marrow."},"t":"Kidney vs marrow","sl":43,"img":"slides_i/L18_43.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Kidney vs marrow</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233844095},{"id":"I|L18|31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L18 s77","t":"NL — CMV drug toxicity","s":"A 40-year-old man with HIV is being treated for CMV infection. CBC: Hb 9 g/dL, low WBC, platelets 100,000/mm³. After starting treatment his creatinine rises from 0.9 to 1.8 mg/dL. Which drug is most likely responsible?","lec":"L18","sl":77,"c":"<b>NL — CMV drug toxicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>NL1 2024 item printed on BM33 L18 slide 77, answered in class (→ ganciclovir).</span>","o":{"D":"Ganciclovir","C":"Foscarnet","B":"Acyclovir","A":"Ketoconazole","E":"Oseltamivir"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. The CMV drug with marrow suppression ('Granulocytopenia') plus crystalluria.","C":"The trap: nephrotoxic and anti-CMV, but it does not suppress the marrow.","B":"Not the CMV drug.","A":"An antifungal.","E":"An influenza drug."},"img":"slides_i/L18_77.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L18 s78","t":"NL — HIV patient with lip blisters","s":"A 20-year-old HIV patient develops mucous-membrane blisters of both lips. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","lec":"L18","sl":78,"c":"<b>NL — HIV patient with lip blisters</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>NL1 2023 item printed on BM33 L18 slide 78, answered in class (→ acyclovir): 'don't be fooled'.</span>","o":{"B":"Acyclovir","D":"Lamivudine","E":"Ritonavir","A":"Tenofovir","C":"Ribavirin"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Herpes labialis → acyclovir. Treat the lesion, not the background HIV.","D":"An ARV.","E":"A PI booster.","A":"An ARV/HBV drug.","C":"HCV/RSV."},"img":"slides_i/L18_78.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L18 s79","t":"NL — zoster drug mechanism","s":"A 45-year-old woman has a severe burning sensation over the abdomen for 12 hours, then vesicles in the T10 dermatome. What is the mechanism of the drug used to treat her?","lec":"L18","sl":79,"c":"<b>NL — zoster drug mechanism</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>NL item printed on BM33 L18 slide 79, answered in class (→ inhibit DNA replication): 'you have to think in several layers'.</span>","o":{"B":"Inhibits DNA replication","A":"Inhibits reverse transcriptase","E":"Inhibits protein synthesis","D":"Blocks viral entry","C":"Blocks capsid formation"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Zoster (VZV) → acyclovir → viral DNA polymerase inhibition / chain termination.","A":"HIV/HBV drugs.","E":"Not an antiviral mechanism taught.","D":"Maraviroc/enfuvirtide (HIV).","C":"Lenacapavir (HIV)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_79.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM33","n":"L18 s80","t":"Oseltamivir — step blocked","s":"A 60-year-old woman with high fever, cough and sore throat is given oseltamivir. Which process of the virus does it inhibit?","lec":"L18","sl":80,"c":"<b>Oseltamivir — step blocked</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>NL1 item printed on BM33 L18 slide 80, answered in class (→ release from host cell) · BM30 SUM1 Q68 — the drug that blocks influenza virus exit (keyed oseltamivir).</span>","o":{"A":"Release from the host cell","B":"Release into the host cell","E":"Maturation","D":"Virion assembly","C":"Reverse transcription"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Neuraminidase inhibition leaves progeny stuck and aggregated on the cell surface.","B":"Her warning: 'release TO/into' vs 'release FROM'.","E":"PIs block maturation (HIV).","D":"Not the NA step.","C":"HIV/HBV."},"img":"slides_i/L18_80.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM30 Q68"]},{"id":"I|L18|35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q92","t":"Efavirenz CNS effects","s":"A 44-year-old treated for HIV experiences confusion, insomnia and nightmares. Which drug is responsible?","lec":"L18","sl":14,"c":"<b>Efavirenz CNS effects</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q92 · BM28 Q22 — HIV patient on an antiviral with confusion/dizziness, insomnia and nightmares; which drug? Keyed efavirenz.</span>","o":{"B":"Efavirenz","E":"Emtricitabine","C":"Raltegravir","D":"Atazanavir","A":"Tenofovir"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. 'EFV = Effects on dreams'; take it at bedtime on an empty stomach.","E":"Well tolerated.","C":"Headache, nausea.","D":"Bilirubin, stones.","A":"Kidney, bone."},"img":"slides_i/L18_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q22"]},{"id":"I|L18|36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q35","t":"Efavirenz mechanism","s":"An HIV patient develops dizziness, insomnia and nightmares from his antiviral. What is the mechanism of that drug?","lec":"L18","sl":14,"c":"<b>Efavirenz mechanism</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q35 — HIV patient with dizziness, insomnia, nightmares due to an antiviral; what is its mechanism? Keyed allosteric (non-nucleoside-site) inhibition of RT.</span>","o":{"E":"It binds reverse transcriptase at a site away from the nucleoside-binding site, inhibiting it allosterically","D":"It is incorporated into DNA as a chain terminator after triple phosphorylation","A":"It blocks HIV protease cleavage of gag-pol","B":"It blocks strand transfer by integrase","C":"It blocks CCR5"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct (keyed): an NNRTI, non-competitive, needing no activation.","D":"NRTIs.","A":"PIs.","B":"INSTIs.","C":"Maraviroc."},"img":"slides_i/L18_14.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q69","t":"PI lipodystrophy","s":"Which antiretroviral regimen is associated with lipodystrophy?","lec":"L18","sl":16,"c":"<b>PI lipodystrophy</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q69 (keyed the darunavir–ritonavir regimen) · BM32 SUM1 Q86 (PI adverse effect: skin rash / lipodystrophy / nephrolithiasis).</span>","o":{"E":"Darunavir – ritonavir – tenofovir – emtricitabine","C":"Atazanavir – emtricitabine – ritonavir – tenofovir","A":"Efavirenz – tenofovir – emtricitabine","D":"Raltegravir – tenofovir – emtricitabine","B":"Tenofovir – emtricitabine – zidovudine"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct (keyed). A boosted PI. Of the two PI options, atazanavir is the one the slide EXEMPTS from the metabolic/fat effects.","C":"The trap: a PI regimen too, but atazanavir is 'except'.","A":"NNRTI.","D":"INSTI.","B":"NRTIs only."},"img":"slides_i/L18_16.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM32 Q86"]},{"id":"I|L18|38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q68","t":"Protease inhibitor recognition","s":"Which of the following drugs is an HIV protease inhibitor?","lec":"L18","sl":5,"c":"<b>Protease inhibitor recognition</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q68 — which drug is a protease inhibitor? (tenofovir, efavirenz, raltegravir, atazanavir, zidovudine).</span>","o":{"C":"Atazanavir","E":"Tenofovir","A":"Efavirenz","B":"Raltegravir","D":"Zidovudine"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct. -navir = PI.","E":"NtRTI.","A":"NNRTI (-vir- in the middle).","B":"INSTI (-tegravir).","D":"NRTI (-vudine)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q85","t":"Chain terminator","s":"An HIV patient receives a drug that inhibits reverse transcriptase by causing DNA chain termination. Which drug?","lec":"L18","sl":10,"c":"<b>Chain terminator</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q85 — HIV patient on a drug that inhibits reverse transcriptase causing chain termination (nevirapine, ribavirin, efavirenz, raltegravir, zidovudine).</span>","o":{"B":"Zidovudine","D":"Nevirapine","C":"Efavirenz","A":"Raltegravir","E":"Ribavirin"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. An NRTI is incorporated and terminates the chain.","D":"An NNRTI: allosteric, not incorporated.","C":"An NNRTI.","A":"An INSTI.","E":"Not an HIV drug."},"img":"slides_i/L18_10.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q83","t":"One drug for HIV + HBV","s":"A 35-year-old with HIV is found to be co-infected with hepatitis B. Which single drug is most effective against both?","lec":"L18","sl":25,"c":"<b>One drug for HIV + HBV</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 Virology Formative Q9 (keyed tenofovir) · BM31 SUM1 Q69 · BM32 SUM1 Q83 (HBsAg-positive — which drug?).</span>","o":{"C":"Tenofovir","D":"Zidovudine","E":"Sofosbuvir","B":"Acyclovir","A":"Amantadine"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct: both viruses use reverse transcriptase. 3TC and FTC also cover both.","D":"HIV only.","E":"HCV.","B":"Herpes.","A":"Influenza A."},"img":"slides_i/L18_25.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM31 Q69","BM30 virology Q9"]},{"id":"I|L18|41","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q23","t":"Ribavirin toxicity","s":"A patient on an antiviral develops haemolytic anaemia. Which drug is most likely responsible?","lec":"L18","sl":67,"c":"<b>Ribavirin toxicity</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q23 — an antiviral causing haemolytic anaemia; keyed ribavirin.</span>","o":{"A":"Ribavirin","D":"Oseltamivir","B":"Acyclovir","C":"Dolutegravir","E":"Sofosbuvir"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct (keyed). It is also teratogenic, and is used for HCV (with IFN or added to SOF/VEL) and RSV.","D":"GI effects.","B":"Crystalluria.","C":"Insomnia, weight gain.","E":"Well tolerated (amiodarone bradycardia)."},"img":"slides_i/L18_67.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|42","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q67","t":"Sofosbuvir mechanism","s":"A 34-year-old man with hepatitis C is treated with sofosbuvir. What is its mechanism?","lec":"L18","sl":69,"c":"<b>Sofosbuvir mechanism</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q67 — 34-year-old with hepatitis C on sofosbuvir; mechanism? (short answer).</span>","o":{"A":"Its triphosphate inhibits the NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase, terminating HCV RNA synthesis","D":"It inhibits the NS3/4A serine protease that cleaves the HCV polyprotein","E":"It inhibits the NS5A replication complex that assembles new virions","B":"It induces an antiviral state in hepatocytes via the interferon receptor","C":"It blocks HCV reverse transcriptase, like tenofovir does for HBV"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. -buvir = NS5B. It is pan-genotypic.","D":"-previr.","E":"-asvir (velpatasvir).","B":"Interferon.","C":"HCV has no RT; it is a (+)RNA flavivirus."},"img":"slides_i/L18_69.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|43","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q67","t":"Herpes labialis — drug mechanism","s":"A 32-year-old man with herpes labialis is given an antiviral. Which process does the drug inhibit?","lec":"L18","sl":40,"c":"<b>Herpes labialis — drug mechanism</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM30 SUM1 Q67 — herpes labialis treated with an antiviral; what does it do? Keyed 'inhibition of DNA and RNA synthesis' (viral DNA synthesis).</span>","o":{"D":"Viral nucleic-acid (DNA) synthesis","E":"Viral entry into the host cell","B":"Viral assembly and maturation","C":"Release from the host cell","A":"It stimulates the immune response"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct (keyed as 'inhibition of DNA and RNA synthesis'). Acyclovir-triphosphate blocks viral DNA polymerase.","E":"Entry inhibitors are HIV drugs.","B":"PIs (HIV).","C":"Neuraminidase inhibitors (flu).","A":"Interferon."},"img":"slides_i/L18_40.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|L18|44","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q82","t":"Resistant influenza","s":"A patient's influenza virus is resistant to oseltamivir, zanamivir and amantadine. Which drug should be given?","lec":"L18","sl":53,"c":"<b>Resistant influenza</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q82 — flu-like illness resistant to oseltamivir, zanamivir AND amantadine; which drug? (oseltamivir, amantadine, favipiravir, zanamivir).</span>","o":{"D":"Favipiravir","A":"Oseltamivir at double dose","B":"Amantadine","E":"Zanamivir","C":"Acyclovir"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct. A polymerase inhibitor with a different target, and second line for oseltamivir resistance. Not in pregnancy.","A":"Resistance is not overcome by dose.","B":"Resistant.","E":"Resistant.","C":"Herpes."},"img":"slides_i/L18_53.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L18|45","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"Q88","t":"ART timing with mpox","s":"A patient newly diagnosed with HIV also has active mpox. According to WHO 2025 recommendations, when should ART be started?","lec":"L18","sl":20,"c":"<b>ART timing with mpox</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM32 SUM1 Q88 — newly diagnosed HIV with concurrent mpox; appropriate time to start ART (WHO 2025). No key in the archive; the answer follows the same-day ART principle on slide 20.</span>","o":{"A":"Within 7 days, ideally the same day","D":"Only after the mpox has completely resolved","C":"Only after the mpox antiviral course is finished","E":"Only once the CD4 count is ≤200 cells/µL","B":"Delay 4 weeks to avoid IRIS"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"The recalled paper has no key; this option follows the same-day-ART principle taught on slide 20. Mpox is more severe with uncontrolled HIV, so waiting harms.","D":"Delay leaves HIV uncontrolled.","C":"Same objection.","E":"CD4 thresholds are superseded.","B":"Not the recommendation for mpox."},"img":"slides_i/L18_20.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|L18|46","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q24","t":"Regimen in pregnancy","s":"Which statement about antiretroviral therapy for an HIV-positive pregnant woman is correct according to the current guideline taught?","lec":"L18","sl":25,"c":"<b>Regimen in pregnancy</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 Q24 — which antiviral regimen is correct (zidovudine/lamivudine…; the stem is only partly recalled). The BM28 key's answer is the OLD pregnancy backbone; the 2026 lecture uses the same TLD regimen for everyone.</span>","o":{"E":"She takes the same first-line TLD regimen as other adults, started same-day and continued through delivery","C":"She must take zidovudine monotherapy, the only drug proven safe in pregnancy","D":"ART is withheld until after delivery to avoid any drug exposure to the fetus","A":"Efavirenz is preferred in the first 8 weeks, as it is safest in organogenesis","B":"Only PrEP drugs may be used, because full ART regimens are teratogenic"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct for 2026. Older answer keys (BM28) used zidovudine + lamivudine as a separate pregnancy backbone, so know both, and answer by the current lecture.","C":"Monotherapy is never used.","D":"Most transmission is intrapartum; treat early.","A":"Efavirenz <8 weeks carries a neural tube defect risk.","B":"Nonsense."},"img":"slides_i/L18_25.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|0","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"Larval morphology","s":"A saline wet mount of stool passed 30 minutes earlier is examined at ×40. A motile larva with a bulbous oesophagus is found. The buccal cavity is hard to judge at this power, but a large genital primordium is obvious in the mid-body. What is the infective stage of this worm?","lec":"LAB","sl":28,"c":"<b>Larval morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Filariform larva with a notched tail","B":"Rhabditiform larva with a short buccal cavity","C":"Rhabditiform larva with a long buccal cavity","D":"Embryonated egg containing a coiled larva","E":"Filariform larva with a pointed tail"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Step 1: a rhabditiform (bulbous-oesophagus) larva with a LARGE genital primordium in FRESH stool is Strongyloides stercoralis (Lab 1 p.28, p.31). The lab talk says the genital primordium, not the buccal cavity, is what you can actually judge at ×40. Step 2: its infective stage is the filariform larva, and the Strongyloides filariform larva has a NOTCHED tail (p.29, p.30, p.32).","B":"This is the stage on the slide: the Strongyloides rhabditiform larva. It is the diagnostic stage in fresh faeces (p.30), not the infective stage.","C":"Long buccal cavity + small genital primordium = hookworm rhabditiform larva (p.24). It appears in stool only when the specimen has stood long enough for the eggs to hatch, and it is not infective either.","D":"An embryonated egg is the infective stage of Ascaris, Trichuris and Enterobius (p.17, p.21, p.39). Strongyloides has no egg stage in stool.","E":"The pointed-tail filariform larva is the hookworm infective stage (p.25). It is the right stage type for the wrong worm."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_28.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Under the microscope you must separate a hookworm rhabditiform larva from a Strongyloides rhabditiform larva. Which pair of features does it?","o":{"A":"Hookworm: LONG buccal cavity, SMALL genital primordium. Strongyloides: SHORT buccal cavity, LARGE genital primordium","C":"Hookworm: short buccal cavity, large genital primordium. Strongyloides: long buccal cavity, small genital primordium","D":"Hookworm has a notched tail; Strongyloides has a pointed tail","E":"Hookworm has a filariform oesophagus; Strongyloides has a rhabditiform oesophagus","B":"They are morphologically identical and are separated only by culture"},"r":{"A":"Correct, and this is the single most examinable comparison in Lab 1. Memory hook: the worm that BITES the gut wall has the LONG mouth.","C":"Exactly reversed.","D":"Tail shape separates the FILARIFORM larvae, not the rhabditiform ones — and it is the other way round: Strongyloides has the NOTCHED tail, hookworm the POINTED tail.","E":"Both rhabditiform larvae have a rhabditiform (bulbous) oesophagus; that is what the name means.","B":"They are routinely separated on these two features."},"t":"Larval morphology","sl":31,"img":"slides_i/LAB_31.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Larval morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Acanthamoeba","s":"A demonstration slide prepared from a corneal-scraping culture shows 15 µm uninucleate cysts. Each cyst has a double wall: a wrinkled outer layer and a smooth, polygonal inner layer. On the lab sheet, which stage of this organism is written as its infective stage?","lec":"LAB","sl":2011,"c":"<b>Acanthamoeba</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Promastigote with a free anterior flagellum","B":"Mature cyst containing four nuclei","C":"Double-walled cyst with a wrinkled ectocyst","D":"Mature cyst containing eight nuclei","E":"Trophozoite bearing spine-like acanthopodia"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The promastigote is the infective stage of Leishmania, which the female sand fly injects (p.21).","B":"A mature quadrinucleate cyst is the infective stage of Entamoeba histolytica and Giardia (p.7, p.18). Acanthamoeba cysts have one nucleus.","C":"This is the stage on the slide. The talk labels it diagnostic, not infective.","D":"The eight-nucleate mature cyst belongs to Entamoeba coli, the non-pathogenic amoeba (p.9).","E":"Correct. Step 1: a double-walled cyst with a wrinkled ectocyst and a polygonal endocyst, 10–20 µm, uninucleate = Acanthamoeba spp. (Lab 3 p.11). Step 2: unlike the intestinal protozoa, the Lab 3 talk marks the TROPHOZOITE (with acanthopodia) as the infective stage. Both stages are diagnostic (p.11). It enters through the eye, nose or skin, never through the stomach, so it does not need a cyst."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_2011.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which filariform larva has a NOTCHED tail?","o":{"E":"Strongyloides stercoralis","A":"Hookworm","B":"Trichinella spiralis","D":"Wuchereria bancrofti","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},"r":{"E":"Correct. Strongyloides filariform larva = notched (forked) tail; hookworm filariform larva = pointed tail. Both have a filariform (elongated) oesophagus.","A":"Hookworm's filariform larva has a POINTED tail.","B":"Trichinella's larva is coiled inside a nurse cell in muscle, not a free filariform larva.","D":"Wuchereria's blood stage is a sheathed microfilaria, not a filariform larva.","C":"Ascaris is diagnosed on eggs; its larvae are not a lab specimen here."},"t":"Larval morphology","sl":32,"img":"slides_i/LAB_32.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Larval morphology</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"Microfilariae","s":"A stained night blood film shows a sheathed microfilaria lying in kinked, angular folds. Its body nuclei overlap one another, and two isolated nuclei sit in the tip of the tail. According to the Lab 1 talk, which mosquito genera are the intermediate hosts of this species?","lec":"LAB","sl":53,"c":"<b>Microfilariae</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Mansonia and Anopheles","B":"Aedes and Anopheles","C":"Culex, Mansonia, Anopheles and Aedes","D":"Culex and Mansonia","E":"Culex and Aedes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Step 1: kinked body + crowded, overlapping nuclei + terminal nuclei in the tail = Brugia malayi (p.54). Step 2: the Lab 1 learning-guidance table lists Mansonia spp. and Anopheles spp. as the intermediate hosts of B. malayi (p.55). The same mosquitoes are also its vectors.","B":"Anopheles is right, but Aedes is a W. bancrofti host only.","C":"These four genera are the intermediate hosts of Wuchereria bancrofti (p.55). The W. bancrofti microfilaria has graceful curves, separate nuclei and NO terminal nuclei (p.53).","D":"Mansonia is right, but Culex is a W. bancrofti host only.","E":"Culex and Aedes both carry W. bancrofti, but neither is listed for B. malayi."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"Which feature separates the microfilaria of Brugia malayi from that of Wuchereria bancrofti on a stained blood film?","o":{"A":"B. malayi has TERMINAL nuclei extending into the tail tip and a kinked, irregular body; W. bancrofti has a smoothly curved body with no terminal nuclei","B":"W. bancrofti has terminal nuclei; B. malayi has none","C":"B. malayi has no sheath; W. bancrofti has a sheath","D":"W. bancrofti is unsheathed and B. malayi is sheathed","E":"They are identical and are separated only by the vector"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The worksheet describes W. bancrofti as 'ลำตัวโค้งสวยงาม' — gracefully curved — and B. malayi as 'ลำตัวโค้งแบบขดงอ', kinked. The terminal nuclei are the decisive feature.","B":"Reversed.","C":"BOTH are sheathed.","D":"Both are sheathed.","E":"They are separated on morphology in the lab, which is the point of the specimen."},"t":"Microfilariae","sl":53,"img":"slides_i/LAB_53.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Microfilariae</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Ascaris eggs","s":"In freshly passed stool a round egg of about 55 µm is found. Its shell is thick and smooth with no bumpy outer coat, it is colourless rather than bile-stained, and the inside holds a single undivided cell mass. What is this egg, and is it an infective stage?","lec":"LAB","sl":14,"c":"<b>Ascaris eggs</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Unembryonated hookworm egg; infective: no","B":"Decorticated Ascaris egg; infective: no","C":"Unfertilized Ascaris egg; infective: no","D":"Embryonated Enterobius egg; infective: yes","E":"Decorticated Ascaris egg; infective: yes"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The hookworm egg is also colourless, but its shell is VERY THIN, with a clear space around 4–8 cells (p.23). A thick shell around a single cell mass rules it out. This is the classic decorticated-vs-hookworm trap.","B":"Correct. Step 1: round or oval, colourless, with the chitinous shell intact but no outer albuminous layer = the decorticated Ascaris egg (p.15). Step 2: it holds a single cell mass, so it is unembryonated. Only the embryonated egg is infective, after 2–3 weeks in soil (p.16, p.17).","C":"The unfertilized egg is elongated and irregular, 78–105 µm, with a thin shell of two layers and a mass of refractile granules (p.14). A round 55 µm egg with one cell mass does not fit.","D":"The Enterobius egg is D-shaped (plano-convex), colourless and contains a fully developed larva (p.38). It is usually found on Scotch tape, not in stool.","E":"The identification is right but the infectivity is wrong. A decorticated egg straight from stool is not embryonated, so it is diagnostic only (p.17)."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_14.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Four forms of the Ascaris egg are demonstrated. Which description matches the DECORTICATED egg?","o":{"B":"Round or oval, COLOURLESS, with no outer albuminous layer","D":"Oval to round, brown-yellow, with a thick mamillated albuminous coat","A":"Oval and irregular, 78–105 µm, brown-yellow with a thin albuminous layer","E":"Round, containing a coiled larva, and infective","C":"Barrel-shaped with bipolar mucoid plugs"},"r":{"B":"Correct. 'Decorticated' means the outer albuminous coat has been lost, which also removes the bile staining — hence colourless.","D":"That is the FERTILIZED egg, the classic form.","A":"That is the UNFERTILIZED egg — larger, more irregular, and with a thinner coat.","E":"That is the EMBRYONATED egg, which is the infective stage after two to three weeks in soil.","C":"That is Trichuris trichiura."},"t":"Ascaris eggs","sl":13,"img":"slides_i/LAB_13.webp","tier":"MustKnow","c":"<b>Ascaris eggs</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Paragonimus","s":"A concentrated specimen shows an operculated, unembryonated egg measuring 90 × 55 µm. The shell is thick, the egg is asymmetrical with a clearly visible operculum, and the end opposite the operculum is thickened. The bench also asks for the second intermediate host. What do you write?","lec":"LAB","sl":1006,"c":"<b>Paragonimus</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Freshwater crab or crayfish","B":"Water caltrop and water chestnut","C":"Cyclops copepod","D":"Cyprinoid freshwater fish","E":"Freshwater snail"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Step 1: 68–118 µm, unembryonated, thick asymmetrical shell, obvious operculum and a thickened posterior end = Paragonimus spp. egg (Lab 2 p.6). Step 2: its second intermediate hosts are the freshwater crab, the mountain crab and the crayfish, which carry the metacercaria (p.7).","B":"Water plants carry the metacercariae of Fasciolopsis buski (p.9). The large intestinal fluke egg is 135–190 µm, with a thin smooth wall and a small indistinct operculum (p.8).","C":"Cyclops is the first intermediate host of Gnathostoma (Lab 1 p.43). Paragonimus has no copepod stage.","D":"Cyprinoid fish are the second intermediate host of Opisthorchis viverrini (p.5), whose egg is only 25 × 12 µm and embryonated.","E":"A freshwater snail is Paragonimus's FIRST intermediate host (p.7). The question asks for the second."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1006.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"What is the specific key point of the Opisthorchis viverrini egg as the lab worksheet describes it?","o":{"A":"25 × 12 µm, 'electric light bulb' appearance, musk-melon surface texture, embryonated, with a shoulder and a terminal knob","E":"58–76 × 40–51 µm, unembryonated, with an operculum and a terminal knob","B":"140 × 85 µm with a faint operculum and a smooth thin shell","D":"Non-operculated with a lateral spine","C":"Barrel-shaped with bipolar mucoid plugs"},"r":{"A":"Correct — and the phrases 'electric light bulb' and 'musk-melon texture' are the worksheet's own wording, which is what a lab short-answer marks.","E":"That is Diphyllobothrium latum — also operculated with a knob, but more than twice the size and unembryonated. Measuring is what separates them.","B":"That is the large intestinal fluke group (Fasciolopsis, Fasciola, Echinostoma).","D":"That is Schistosoma mansoni.","C":"That is Trichuris trichiura."},"t":"Opisthorchis","sl":1004,"img":"slides_i/LAB_1004.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Opisthorchis</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Small intestinal flukes","s":"FECT shows an operculated, embryonated egg of 28 × 12 µm with an electric-light-bulb outline. At ×40 there is no prominent shoulder around the operculum, and the posterior end shows only a faint knob. What is written as the report?","lec":"LAB","sl":1004,"c":"<b>Small intestinal flukes</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Opisthorchis viverrini egg","B":"Diphyllobothrium latum egg","C":"Small intestinal fluke egg","D":"Clonorchis sinensis egg","E":"Large intestinal fluke egg"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"O. viverrini is the look-alike, but its egg has a PROMINENT shoulder around the operculum and a clear posterior knob, with a musk-melon surface (p.4). The lecturer calls shoulder + knob the key discrimination.","B":"D. latum also has an operculum and a small knob, but its egg is 58–76 × 40–51 µm, unembryonated and thin-shelled (p.23).","C":"Correct. Step 1: the size and light-bulb outline place it in the O. viverrini / small intestinal fluke group (25 × 12 vs 22–32 × 11–12 µm; the sizes overlap). Step 2: shoulder not prominent and knob faint or absent = small intestinal fluke. The slide's own instruction is to report it as 'small intestinal fluke egg' (Lab 2 p.10).","D":"The Clonorchis egg is morphologically similar to O. viverrini, including the shoulder (p.4), so the missing shoulder rules it out too.","E":"The large intestinal fluke egg (Fasciolopsis, Fasciola, Echinostoma) is 135–190 µm and unembryonated (p.8)."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1004.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"A 25 µm operculated egg is seen. How do you decide between Opisthorchis viverrini and a small intestinal fluke, and what do you report?","o":{"C":"Shoulder and knob PROMINENT = Opisthorchis; both faint or absent = report 'small intestinal fluke egg'","D":"Shoulder and knob absent = Opisthorchis; prominent = small intestinal fluke","E":"Size alone decides — Opisthorchis is twice as large","B":"Only PCR can separate them","A":"They are reported together as 'liver fluke egg'"},"r":{"C":"Correct. This is the ONE egg discrimination the lecturer expects you to make on morphology alone. The small intestinal fluke egg is 22–32 x 11–12 micrometres, also with an 'electric light bulb' shape, but with a faint or absent shoulder and knob.","D":"Reversed.","E":"They overlap in size — 25 x 12 versus 22–32 x 11–12 micrometres — which is exactly why the shoulder and knob are needed.","B":"Light microscopy is the taught method.","A":"The small intestinal fluke egg is reported under its own name, which is what makes this discrimination worth marks."},"t":"Small intestinal flukes","sl":1010,"img":"slides_i/LAB_1010.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Small intestinal flukes</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Schistosoma","s":"An oval, non-operculated egg of 150 × 60 µm is found in a stool concentrate. It contains a ciliated larva, and a sharp spine projects from one side of the shell. What is the infective stage of this parasite, and how does it enter a human?","lec":"LAB","sl":1011,"c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Embryonated egg swallowed in untreated river water","B":"Miracidium entering skin in fresh water","C":"Metacercaria eaten in raw freshwater or mountain crab","D":"Forked-tail cercaria entering skin in fresh water","E":"Metacercaria eaten on raw water caltrop or chestnut"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"No fluke egg is infective when swallowed. The egg is the diagnostic stage.","B":"The miracidium hatches from the egg and infects the SNAIL, not the human. This is the classic stage-swap trap.","C":"This is Paragonimus, acquired from raw crab containing metacercariae (p.7).","D":"Correct. Step 1: non-operculated, embryonated, 110–175 µm, with a LATERAL spine = Schistosoma mansoni (Lab 2 p.11). Schistosomes are the only non-operculated flukes. Step 2: the infective stage is the forked-tail cercaria, which penetrates the skin in water (water-borne transmission) (p.12). Schistosomes have no metacercaria.","E":"This is Fasciolopsis buski, whose metacercariae encyst on water plants (p.9)."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Which Schistosoma egg has a LATERAL spine, and how do the other three differ?","o":{"D":"S. mansoni has a lateral spine; S. haematobium has a terminal spine; S. japonicum (70–100 µm) and S. mekongi (51–73 µm) have a small lateral knob and differ by size","C":"S. haematobium has a lateral spine; S. mansoni has a terminal spine","E":"All four have terminal spines and differ only in size","B":"S. japonicum has the lateral spine; S. mansoni has a knob","A":"Schistosoma eggs are operculated and have no spine"},"r":{"D":"Correct. Spine position first, then size for the japonicum/mekongi pair — and mekongi is the Mekong-basin species, which is the geographical hint.","C":"Reversed — and S. haematobium is the URINE specimen, found by filtration.","E":"Only haematobium's spine is terminal.","B":"S. japonicum has a small lateral KNOB, not a spine.","A":"Schistosoma eggs are the one NON-operculated fluke group, and they are embryonated when passed."},"t":"Schistosoma","sl":1011,"img":"slides_i/LAB_1011.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Schistosoma</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Taenia","s":"A gravid proglottid passed in a patient's stool is injected with Indian ink and shows 18 lateral uterine branches on each side. Which statement about this tapeworm is correct?","lec":"LAB","sl":1018,"c":"<b>Taenia</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Its egg is told from the pork tapeworm egg by its radial striations","B":"Its egg is operculated with a small knob at the opposite end","C":"Its scolex carries four suckers but no rostellum or hooks","D":"Its larva in muscle has an invaginated scolex bearing hooks","E":"Its scolex carries four suckers and a rostellum with hooks"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Both Taenia eggs are radially striated and identical (round, 31–43 µm, six-hooked oncosphere), so an egg can only be reported as 'Taenia spp.' (p.21).","B":"An operculum with a small knob describes the Diphyllobothrium latum egg (p.23). Taenia eggs are not operculated.","C":"Correct. Step 1: uterine branches >15 = Taenia saginata (Lab 2 p.19). A bare '18' or 'more than 15' scores zero; write 'uterine branches >15'. Step 2: the T. saginata scolex has four suckers and is unarmed, with no rostellum or hooks (p.18).","D":"An invaginated scolex WITH hooks is Cysticercus cellulosae, the T. solium larva. Cysticercus bovis (T. saginata) has an invaginated scolex without hooks (p.20).","E":"A rostellum with hooks is the armed scolex of T. solium (p.18), which has fewer than 15 uterine branches (p.19)."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_1018.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"Two Taenia scolices are demonstrated. Which description is correct?","o":{"C":"T. solium has a rostellum with hooks; T. saginata has four suckers and NO rostellum or hooks","D":"T. saginata has a rostellum with hooks; T. solium has none","B":"Both have rostellar hooks and are separated by sucker number","A":"Neither has a rostellum; both are separated by the uterine branch count only","E":"T. solium has six suckers; T. saginata has four"},"r":{"C":"Correct, and it is the fastest discriminator on an adult specimen. Memory hook: pork (solium) is 'armed' with hooks; beef (saginata) is 'unarmed'. The uterine-branch count (<15 vs >15) is the other method, done on a gravid proglottid with Indian ink.","D":"Reversed.","B":"Both have four suckers; only solium has hooks.","A":"T. solium does have a rostellum.","E":"All cyclophyllidean cestodes have four suckers."},"t":"Taenia scolex","sl":1018,"img":"slides_i/LAB_1018.webp","tier":"HighYield","c":"<b>Taenia scolex</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Entamoeba cysts","s":"A permanent-stained stool smear is examined under the ×100 oil objective. A round cyst spans 12 eyepiece divisions and contains four nuclei, each with a centrally placed karyosome and regular peripheral chromatin, plus a cigar-shaped chromatoid body. Identify the organism and stage, and state whether it is infective.","lec":"LAB","sl":2007,"c":"<b>Entamoeba cysts</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica, mature cyst; infective: yes","B":"Entamoeba coli, immature cyst; infective: no","C":"Entamoeba coli, mature cyst; infective: yes","D":"Entamoeba histolytica, immature cyst; infective: no","E":"Giardia intestinalis, mature cyst; infective: yes"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Step 1: at ×100 oil, one division = 1 µm, so the cyst is 12 µm (E. histolytica cyst 10–15 µm). A central karyosome, regular peripheral chromatin and cigar-shaped chromatoid bodies = E. histolytica (Lab 3 p.7). Step 2: four nuclei = MATURE cyst, which is the infective stage (p.7). The lecturer gives zero for 'cyst' alone: write 'mature cyst (4 nuclei)'.","B":"E. coli cysts can carry four nuclei on the way to eight, but they have ECCENTRIC karyosomes, irregular peripheral chromatin and thin splinter-ended chromatoid bodies (p.9). If you misread 12 divisions as ~30 µm (the ×40 conversion), the size seems to fit E. coli (10–35 µm), which is the point of the trap.","C":"The mature E. coli cyst has eight nuclei (p.9), and the nuclear detail here is wrong for E. coli anyway.","D":"Four nuclei is the mature count for E. histolytica. The immature cyst has one or two nuclei and is not infective.","E":"The Giardia cyst is oval, 8–19 × 7–10 µm, and is recognised by axonemes and two median bodies, not by chromatoid bars (p.18)."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_2007.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"The adult Angiostrongylus cantonensis specimen is described by a distinctive pattern. What is it and what causes it?","o":{"A":"A 'barber's pole' appearance — the red blood-filled intestine spirals around the white uterus","E":"A 'barber's pole' appearance — alternating bands of pigmented cuticle","B":"A 'whip-like' shape with a narrow anterior and thick posterior","D":"A 'cephalic bulb with hooklets' at the anterior end","C":"A coiled larva inside a nurse cell"},"r":{"A":"Correct. The intestine is red because it is filled with blood; the uterus is white; the two interwind, giving the barber's-pole stripe. It is the key point for this specimen.","E":"The stripe is internal anatomy showing through, not cuticular pigment.","B":"Whip-like is Trichuris trichiura.","D":"The cephalic bulb with hooklets is Gnathostoma spinigerum.","C":"That is Trichinella spiralis."},"t":"Angiostrongylus","sl":45,"img":"slides_i/LAB_45.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Angiostrongylus</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Plasmodium ovale","s":"An unknown thin blood film shows infected red cells that are enlarged and oval, many with ragged, fimbriated edges and pink stippling. A mature schizont holds 8 merozoites. Which species, with a correct pair of reasons, should be written on the lab sheet?","lec":"LAB","sl":3015,"c":"<b>Plasmodium ovale</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"P. ovale: normal-sized RBC; band-form growing trophozoite","B":"P. vivax: enlarged RBC with Schüffner's dots; more than 12 merozoites in the mature schizont","C":"P. ovale: enlarged, oval fimbriated RBC; 6–12 merozoites in the mature schizont","D":"P. falciparum: normal-sized RBC; more than 12 merozoites in a grape-like cluster","E":"P. vivax: enlarged RBC; amoeboid growing trophozoite"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"The species is right but both reasons are wrong. P. ovale enlarges the RBC, and the band form belongs to P. malariae (p.30). The lab marks the reasons as well as the name.","B":"P. vivax is the enlarged-RBC look-alike, but its mature schizont has more than 12 (~16) merozoites (p.23) and its RBC is not fimbriated. The 8 merozoites here exclude it.","C":"Correct. Question 1 of the malaria matrix, 'is the RBC enlarged?', leaves P. vivax or P. ovale (both have Schüffner's dots). Question 2: an oval, fimbriated (comet-shaped) RBC and fewer than 12 (6–12) merozoites = P. ovale (Lab 4 p.15, p.26–27). Both reasons are ones you saw on the slide, which is what the unknown-slide sheet requires.","D":"P. falciparum leaves the RBC normal-sized, and its schizont has more than 12 (~24) merozoites (p.19). Both findings contradict the film.","E":"An amoeboid trophozoite is the P. vivax key point (p.22), but the stem gives fimbriated oval cells and 8 merozoites, which point away from vivax."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_3015.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"How do the adult and third-stage larva of Gnathostoma spinigerum differ on the demonstration slide?","o":{"C":"Both have a cephalic bulb, but the adult has 8 rows of cephalic hooklets and the L3 larva has 4","B":"Only the adult has a cephalic bulb; the larva has none","D":"Only the larva has hooklets; the adult has suckers","A":"The adult has 4 rows and the larva has 8","E":"Neither has hooklets; they are separated by size alone"},"r":{"C":"Correct. Cephalic bulb in both, and the ROW COUNT is what the worksheet asks for.","B":"Both have the bulb.","D":"Suckers belong to the flatworms, not to a nematode.","A":"Reversed.","E":"The hooklet rows are the specific key point."},"t":"Gnathostoma","sl":42,"img":"slides_i/LAB_42.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Gnathostoma</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Stool examination","s":"A team screening a village for Opisthorchis viverrini needs a stool method clearly more sensitive than the direct simple smear, but the site has no centrifuge and very little equipment. Which method fits, and which limitation must the team accept?","lec":"LAB","sl":5,"c":"<b>Stool examination</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Direct simple smear; it detects roughly 10–20% of infections","B":"FECT; formalin kills trophozoites, so motility can no longer be seen","C":"Kato-Katz; it misses light infections unless a centrifuge concentrates eggs","D":"Kato-Katz; the thick smear takes time to clear and is hard to read","E":"FECT; it needs a centrifuge plus formalin and ethyl acetate reagents"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"This is the direct smear's real limitation (10–20% sensitivity), which is why the stem rules it out. Its advantages are that it is easy, convenient and fast.","B":"Concentration does kill organisms, but that is not the point here: FECT is already excluded because it needs a centrifuge.","C":"Kato-Katz uses no centrifuge at all. It uses a measured, sieved stool sample cleared under glycerol-soaked cellophane, so this limitation is invented.","D":"Correct. Step 1: the constraints exclude FECT (needs a centrifuge and more equipment) and the direct smear (too insensitive), which leaves Kato-Katz. The lecturer's worksheet table gives its advantages as little equipment + high sensitivity (68.3% for O. viverrini vs 12.4% for the direct smear). Step 2: its limitations are that the thick smear takes time to clear and is hard to read. If it is left too long, the clearing also destroys thin-shelled eggs.","E":"This is a true limitation of FECT, and it is exactly why FECT cannot be used at this site. FECT is the most sensitive method (91%), but it is the wrong choice here."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_5.webp","tier":"MustKnow","old":{"s":"What is the advantage of the concentration technique over a direct simple smear?","o":{"D":"It examines a far larger volume of stool, so it detects light infections that a direct smear misses","B":"It preserves motility, which a direct smear destroys","C":"It allows the species to be identified where a direct smear cannot","E":"It is quicker and needs no centrifuge","A":"It is the only method that shows trophozoites"},"r":{"D":"Correct — sensitivity is the whole point. A direct simple smear for Opisthorchis viverrini is only about 12% sensitive, which is why 'formalin ethyl-acetate concentration' beats 'direct simple smear' whenever both appear in an option list.","B":"The opposite: concentration KILLS the organisms, so motility is a direct-wet-mount observation only.","C":"Species identification is a morphology question, and both methods show morphology.","E":"Concentration is slower and does need a centrifuge — its advantage is sensitivity, not speed.","A":"Trophozoites are best seen on a fresh direct wet mount, because concentration destroys them."},"t":"Stool examination","sl":5,"img":"slides_i/LAB_5.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Stool examination</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Mosquito ID","s":"A female mosquito on the entomology bench has palpi much shorter than the proboscis and a trilobed scutellum. Its wings carry broad scales in a mixed dark-and-pale pattern. Which breeding place and egg form belong to this genus?","lec":"LAB","sl":4005,"c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>","o":{"A":"Clean streams, rice fields and hoof prints; single boat-shaped eggs with floats","B":"Ponds thick with water plants; eggs in a sunflower-shaped cluster","C":"Clean water in household jars and tyres; single oval eggs","D":"Clean water in household jars and tyres; eggs stuck together in a raft","E":"Dirty, low-oxygen drains and ditches; eggs stuck together in a raft"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Clean natural water + boat-shaped eggs with floats = Anopheles spp. (p.6), but Anopheles palpi are as long as the proboscis and its scutellum is rounded (p.5).","B":"Correct. Step 1: short palpi + trilobed scutellum = culicine. Broad wing scales in alternating dark and pale patches = Mansonia spp. (Lab 5 worksheet p.6). Step 2: Mansonia breeds in water with dense aquatic plants (water lettuce, duckweed, water-hyacinth roots), and its eggs are laid in a sunflower-shaped cluster (p.6). It is the vector of Brugia malayi.","C":"Clean household containers + single oval (rugby-ball) eggs = Aedes spp. (p.6). Aedes is recognised by snow-white scales and a pointed abdomen tip, not broad mixed wing scales.","D":"This mixes the Aedes breeding site with the Culex egg raft. Swapping Culex and Aedes is the planted error in these items.","E":"Dirty, low-oxygen water + egg raft = Culex spp. (p.6). Culex is plain brown with a blunt abdomen tip."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4005.webp","tier":"HighYield","old":{"s":"On the Lab 5 worksheet you must state which genus is Anopheline and which are Culicine. Which grouping is correct?","o":{"B":"Anopheline = Anopheles only; Culicine = Culex, Aedes and Mansonia","D":"Anopheline = Anopheles and Culex; Culicine = Aedes and Mansonia","C":"Anopheline = Aedes only; Culicine = the other three","E":"Anopheline = Mansonia only; Culicine = the other three","A":"All four genera are Culicine"},"r":{"B":"Correct. One genus against three — which is why step one of the identification (palpi as long as the proboscis?) splits 1 from 3.","D":"Culex is a culicine.","C":"Aedes is a culicine.","E":"Mansonia is a culicine.","A":"Anopheles is the anopheline."},"t":"Mosquito ID","sl":4005,"img":"slides_i/LAB_4005.webp","tier":"Supporting","c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>Authored for this block &mdash; not from a past paper.</span>"},"v2t":1790233928721},{"id":"I|LAB|12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"lab Q","t":"Trichuris","s":"A lab specimen shows a yellow-brown, barrel-shaped egg with a mucoid plug at each end. Give the scientific name, the stage, and the key identification.","lec":"LAB","sl":20,"c":"<b>Trichuris</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab — barrel-shaped egg with bipolar mucoid plug</span>","o":{"D":"Trichuris trichiura · unembryonated egg · barrel-shaped with bipolar mucoid plugs, yellow-brown","E":"Capillaria philippinensis · unembryonated egg · peanut-shaped with flattened bipolar plugs","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides · fertilized egg · thick mamillated albuminous coat","A":"Enterobius vermicularis · embryonated egg · flattened on one side","B":"Hookworm · unembryonated egg · thin shell with 4–8 cells"},"a":"D","r":{"D":"Correct, and this is the BM28 answer key's exact wording for all three parts.","E":"The designed near-miss: Capillaria is also bipolar, but PEANUT-shaped with FLATTENED plugs and a striated shell, and it is 36–45 µm rather than 49–65 µm.","C":"Ascaris eggs are oval to round with a bumpy outer coat, not barrel-shaped.","A":"Enterobius eggs are D-shaped (plano-convex) and colourless.","B":"Hookworm eggs are transparent ovals with a segmented interior."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_20.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q23","t":"Cryptosporidium","s":"A lab specimen 5 µm across is shown. Give the scientific name, the stage, the key point and the staining technique.","lec":"LAB","sl":3001,"c":"<b>Cryptosporidium</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab Q23 — 5 µm round organism, stain and stage asked</span>","o":{"C":"Cryptosporidium spp. · sporulated (mature) oocyst containing 4 sporozoites · round, 4–6 µm · modified acid-fast stain","B":"Cyclospora cayetanensis · unsporulated oocyst · round, 8–12 µm · modified acid-fast stain","D":"Microsporidia · spore · belt-like, 1–2 µm · gram-chromotrope stain","A":"Cystoisospora belli · unsporulated oocyst · ellipsoidal, 25 × 15 µm · modified acid-fast stain","E":"Entamoeba histolytica · mature cyst · 10–15 µm with 4 nuclei · iodine stain"},"a":"C","r":{"C":"Correct on all four parts, and the size is what gives it away — 5 µm sits inside Cryptosporidium's 4–6 µm range and nowhere near the others.","B":"Cyclospora is roughly twice the size.","D":"Microsporidia is 1–2 µm and is never acid-fast.","A":"Cystoisospora is five times the size and ellipsoidal.","E":"An Entamoeba cyst is two to three times the size and is not acid-fast."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_3001.webp","tier":"MustKnow"},{"id":"I|LAB|14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q19","t":"Mosquito ID","s":"A mosquito specimen is shown with an egg that is boat-shaped with lateral floats. Give the genus and its medical importance.","lec":"LAB","sl":4006,"c":"<b>Mosquito ID</b> &middot; Must know<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab Q19 and Q20 — Anopheles vs Aedes, with medical importance and breeding place</span>","o":{"E":"Anopheles spp. — vector of malaria and of lymphatic filariasis","C":"Aedes spp. — vector of dengue virus, chikungunya and Zika virus","B":"Culex spp. — vector of Japanese B encephalitis","D":"Mansonia spp. — vector of Brugia malayi","A":"Anopheles spp. — causes malaria"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both parts. The answer key's own note explains how the two genera are separated on the specimen: Anopheles eggs carry floats and are laid singly, while Aedes eggs are oval/rugby-ball shaped.","C":"Aedes eggs are oval without floats, and the companion question asks where Aedes breeds — the answer is water containers around the house.","B":"Culex lays a raft, not single eggs with floats.","D":"Mansonia lays a sunflower-shaped cluster attached to plant roots.","A":"⚠ Wrong FORM, even though the genus is right. The mosquito does not CAUSE malaria — it is a vector. The lecturer marks this as incorrect."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4006.webp","tier":"MustKnow","also":["BM28 Q20"]},{"id":"I|LAB|15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q-tick","t":"Hard tick","s":"A demonstration specimen has four pairs of legs, a dorsal shield and a capitulum visible from above. What is it, and what is its medical importance?","lec":"LAB","sl":4007,"c":"<b>Hard tick</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 lab · BM30 — scutum, visible capitulum, 4 pairs of legs</span>","o":{"B":"Hard tick — vector of Lyme disease and Rocky Mountain spotted fever","C":"Soft tick — vector of tick-borne relapsing fever","D":"Chigger mite — vector of scrub typhus","E":"Sarcoptes scabiei — causes scabiasis","A":"Demodex spp. — causes folliculitis"},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Scutum present + capitulum visible from above = Ixodidae, the hard ticks.","C":"Soft ticks have NO scutum and their capitulum is not visible from above.","D":"The chigger mite also has a scutum — but it is the LARVA, with only THREE pairs of legs, and it is 0.1–0.3 mm rather than 2–10 mm.","E":"Sarcoptes is a burrowing mite without a scutum of this kind.","A":"Demodex is cylindrical with bud-like legs and lives inside follicles."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4007.webp","tier":"HighYield","also":["BM30 Q38"]},{"id":"I|LAB|16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q24","t":"Plasmodium","s":"A thin blood film shows a trophozoite lying as a band across a normal-sized red cell. Which species, and what are the two key points?","lec":"LAB","sl":3001,"c":"<b>Plasmodium</b> &middot; High yield<br><span class='cite'>BM28 lab Q24 — Plasmodium malariae trophozoite, two key points</span>","o":{"E":"Plasmodium malariae — BAND-form trophozoite, and the infected red cell is NOT enlarged","A":"Plasmodium vivax — amoeboid trophozoite in an enlarged red cell with Schüffner's dots","C":"Plasmodium ovale — comet-shaped, fimbriated red cell","B":"Plasmodium falciparum — ring form with double chromatin, multiple infection of one cell","D":"Plasmodium knowlesi — indistinguishable from P. malariae on morphology"},"a":"E","r":{"E":"Correct on both counts, and the pairing matters: the band form IS the key point, and 'red cell not enlarged' is what separates it from vivax and ovale.","A":"P. vivax enlarges the cell and shows an amoeboid parasite with pink dots.","C":"P. ovale gives a fimbriated, comet-shaped red cell.","B":"P. falciparum gives double-chromatin rings and banana gametocytes.","D":"P. knowlesi does resemble P. malariae, but the taught answer for a band form is P. malariae — and knowlesi has a 24-hour cycle rather than 72."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_3001.webp","tier":"HighYield"},{"id":"I|LAB|17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q26","t":"Scabies","s":"A child bitten between the fingers has a skin biopsy showing a mite with four pairs of legs. What is it?","lec":"LAB","sl":4007,"c":"<b>Scabies</b> &middot; Supporting<br><span class='cite'>BM31 SUM1 Q26 · BM30 — four-legged-pair mite from a finger-web biopsy</span>","o":{"B":"Sarcoptes scabiei","A":"Pthirus pubis","D":"Pediculus humanus capitis","E":"Pediculus humanus corporis","C":"Demodex spp."},"a":"B","r":{"B":"Correct. Four pairs of legs makes it an arachnid, and the finger-web site with burrows is classical scabies.","A":"Lice are INSECTS with three pairs of legs, and the pubic louse is found on pubic hair and eyelashes.","D":"Also an insect, and found on the scalp.","E":"Also an insect, and found in clothing seams.","C":"Demodex has four pairs of legs too — but they are stubby and bud-like, the body is cylindrical, and it lives inside hair follicles rather than burrowing between the fingers."},"img":"slides_i/LAB_4007.webp","tier":"Supporting","also":["BM30 Q-scab"]}];
window.META_I={"lecOrder":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","LAB"],"lecName":{"L1":"Intestinal Nematodes","L2":"Blood & Tissue Nematodes","L3":"Cestodes","L4":"Trematodes","L5":"Amoeba, Ciliate, Flagellate","L6":"Intestinal Sporozoa","L7":"Toxoplasma & Malaria","L8":"Medical Entomology","L9":"Antiparasitic Drugs","L10":"Introduction to Virology","L11":"Viral Epidemiology & Pathogenesis","L12":"Viral Exanthems","L13":"Oncogenic Viruses","L14":"Arbovirus","L15":"HIV & STDs","L16":"Emerging Viruses","L17":"Lab Diagnosis of Viral Infections","L18":"Antiviral Drugs","LAB":"Labs 1–5"},"lecShort":{"L1":"L1","L2":"L2","L3":"L3","L4":"L4","L5":"L5","L6":"L6","L7":"L7","L8":"L8","L9":"L9","L10":"L10","L11":"L11","L12":"L12","L13":"L13","L14":"L14","L15":"L15","L16":"L16","L17":"L17","L18":"L18","LAB":"Labs"},"yearOrder":["BM33","BM32","BM31","BM30","BM29","BM28","LS54"],"yearMeta":{"BM33":["BM33 · 2569 (lecturer)","Quiz and NL-exam slides the BM33 lecturers printed in their own decks and answered in class"],"BM32":["BM32 · 2568","BM32 Infectious summative 1 · SUM2 lecture & lab"],"BM31":["BM31 · 2567","BM31 SUM1/SUM2 lecture & lab, with the BM32-written answer keys"],"BM30":["BM30 · 2566","BM30 Lec+Lab Summative I & II (2566) + the two 2566 formatives"],"BM29":["BM29 · 2565","BM29 summative 1 & 2 with เฉลย"],"BM28":["BM28 · 2564","BM28 Summative I & II (2564) + the Practice paper"],"LS54":["หลักสูตร 54 · 2557–2560","Ax_Infectious question bank, cohorts 57–60"]},"authorMeta":["Authored","Exam-grade items written for this block — one clinical decision per stem, one planted near-miss."],"built":"Infectious Summative I · BM33 L1-L18 + Labs 1-5","lecBlurb":"19 buckets, L1 → L18 plus the labs","authBlurb":"NOT past papers — exam-grade items written for this block","aboutTitle":"Infectious Summative I — question trainer","aboutBody":"General Principles of Infectious Diseases <b>1007105</b>, Summative I. Every past-paper item names the <b>cohort and question number</b> it came from, and the BM33 source slide is shown on the answer card.<br><br>The past-paper layer was re-harvested from <b>all 31 recalled papers</b>: 162 cited items carrying 263 cohort appearances across BM28–BM32 and the หลักสูตร 54 bank. The BM30 เฉลย keys its answers by DOCX <i>highlight</i>, which the plain-text extracts had dropped — recovering it is what took the past-paper count from 72 to 162.<br><br>⚠️ <b>Two circulating student keys are wrong</b>, and the cards say so rather than quietly correcting them. BM30 Q63 keys <i>Rubella</i> for the infant whose rash appears <i>after</i> the fever subsides — it is roseola (HHV-6/7). BM29 Q13 keys <i>trophozoite, schizont, gametocyte</i> for P. falciparum in blood; BM30's key and the BM33 deck both say <b>trophozoite and gametocyte only</b>, because schizonts sequester.<br><br><b>Scope:</b> Summative I examines <b>L1–L18</b> — there is <b>no L19</b> (the professor announced there is no L19). L15–L18 carry the virology past papers on HIV, antivirals and laboratory diagnosis, plus the quiz and NL slides the lecturers printed and answered in class."};
